KANT_TR['reflm/kemp'] = {"reflm:3489":["Expresses *propositiones tavtologicas* (tautological propositions).",[]],"reflm:3490":["Tedious pastime is a *contradictio in adiecto* (contradiction in the adjunct).\nEntertaining pastime is tautology.",[]],"reflm:3491":["visible darkness",[]],"reflm:3492":["Above and \n*pia fraus* (pious fraud)",[]],"reflm:3493":["In the margin on the right \nApparent contradictions in the government of the world. In mysteries. |\nIn riddles. In witty ideas. Which are resolved. A book would be shorter, etc. etc. In the writing of a great man the contradictions are held to be merely apparent.",[]],"reflm:3494":["*identitas et contradictio explicita et implicita* (identity and contradiction, explicit and implicit).\nOn M § 13 »patens«:\n*indemonstrata* (undemonstrated).\nOn M § 13 »latens«:\n*demonstrata* (demonstrated).",[]],"reflm:3495":["*pia fraus* (pious fraud).",[]],"reflm:3496":["*latens* (concealed) is *pia fraus* (pious fraud).",[]],"reflm:3497":["Also tautology.",[]],"reflm:3498":["*Ratio est aliud qvam rationatum* (the ground is something other than the grounded).\n*Nihil continet rationem in se ipso* (nothing contains the ground in itself).\n*Prius, posterius. Antecedens et conseqvens* (prior, posterior. Antecedent and consequent).",[]],"reflm:3499":["*Oppositio Logica, realis* (logical, real opposition).",[]],"reflm:3500":["In the margin on the right \nThe real ground of actuality is the efficient cause.",[]],"reflm:3501":["*Connexa sunt, qvorum uno posito ponitur aliud* (those are connected, of which, when one is posited, the other is posited).",[]],"reflm:3502":["*Qvo non posito non ponitur aliud, est conditio* (that, if which is not posited, the other is not posited, is a condition).",[]],"reflm:3503":["*posito rationato non ponitur, sed reqviritur ratio* (when the grounded is posited, the ground is not posited, but is required).",[]],"reflm:3504":["*ratio vel analytica* (logical) *vel synthetica* (real ground) (the ground is either analytic [logical] or synthetic [real ground]).",[]],"reflm:3505":["*qvo posito necessario ponitur aliud, est ratio* (that, when which is posited something else is necessarily posited, is the ground).",[]],"reflm:3506":["*non rationato posito non ponitur ratio* (when the grounded is not posited, the ground is not posited).",[]],"reflm:3507":["*Qvo posito* (if I posit it) *ponitur aliud* (s determinately) *secundum regulam, est ratio* (that, when which is posited [if I posit it], something else is posited [s determinately] according to a rule, is the ground).\n*a rationato ad rationem non valet conseqventia* (from the grounded to the ground the inference does not hold).\n*conditio: qvo non posito non ponitur aliud* (condition: that, if which is not posited, the other is not posited).",[]],"reflm:3508":["ideal and real.",[]],"reflm:3509":["*nexus etiam est coordinatorum, non semper subordinatorum* (connection is also of the coordinated, not always of the subordinated).",[]],"reflm:3510":["Something, nothing, no thing.",[]],"reflm:3511":["In the margin on the left \nOn possibility. On the something. On the thing.",[]],"reflm:3512":["in a certain, in every respect (*respectu*).",[]],"reflm:3513":["*connexa* (g *inuicem*) *vel per consensum vel oppositionem* (connected [g mutually] either through agreement or through opposition).",[]],"reflm:3514":["*In serie* (g *coordinatorum et*) *subordinatorum* (in the series [g of the coordinated and] of the subordinated).",[]],"reflm:3515":["*Ratio primitiva et subalterna* (primitive and subaltern ground).",[]],"reflm:3516":["*ratio primitiva vel subalterna* (primitive or subaltern ground).",[]],"reflm:3517":["*ratio qvae ipsa est rationatum* (a ground which is itself a grounded).",[]],"reflm:3518":["On condition in general — the limiting.",[]],"reflm:3519":["Above and \n*ratio vel sufficit rationato vel [sibim] etiam sibimet ipsi; si prior: est secundum qvid sufficiens, si posterior: est simpliciter* (the ground suffices either for the grounded or also for itself; if the former, it is sufficient in a certain respect [*secundum qvid*]; if the latter, it is sufficient absolutely [*simpliciter*]).",[]],"reflm:3520":["*praedicatum logicum* (logical predicate). *Determinat:* (it determines:)",[]],"reflm:3521":["He who is a man is determined in respect of learnedness, but not as man. That is, not the logical subject man, but the real, namely that which is thought through the concept of humanity.",[]],"reflm:3522":["*in qvo est respectus rationis ad rationatum, dicitur determinans* (that in which there is a relation of the ground to the grounded is called determining).\n*per se indeterminatum determinatur ab alio* (what is in itself indeterminate is determined by another).",[]],"reflm:3523":["*praedicatum logicum* (logical predicate) or determination.",[]],"reflm:3524":["See p. 12.\nOn M § 36 »realitas — negatio«:\n*per se, non relative ad alium conceptum, qvae est logica, non metaphysica sive transscendentalis* (in itself, not relatively to another concept, which is logical, not metaphysical or transcendental).",[]],"reflm:3525":["Absolute and respective concepts.\nSuch as those of ground and of consequences are respective. Only the respective can be made distinct.",[]],"reflm:3526":["A book in the library\n*deest* — *negatio* (negation): is lacking. What is not in its place. (g *abest*: what is not in its time.)\n*deficit* — *qvando deest, qvod esse debebat, h.e. negatio perfectioni opposita* (when that is lacking which ought to be, that is, a negation opposed to perfection) (g is wanting).\n*abest* — when [it] one knows where it otherwise is (g is not present).",[]],"reflm:3527":["This is a logical *respectus* (relation).\n(s real.)",[]],"reflm:3528":["The essence of a thing (adjectivally).\nA being: *ens*.",[]],"reflm:3529":["peculiar\nFurther from σ-ψ:\nare those whose concepts can be made reciprocal with the subjects.",[]],"reflm:3530":["From the assertoric to the problematic, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:3531":["ψ−χ?? M 16.\nFrom the denial of the assertoric (g antecedent) not to the denial of the problematic.",[]],"reflm:3532":["*nullo* (none)\nBetween § 59 and § 60 stand the words:\nfor if the major proposition is true, then nothing at all can follow from a particular.",[]],"reflm:3533":["That is, from the concept neither of thoroughgoing determination nor of a reality can one infer to existence.",[]],"reflm:3534":["»omni, non tollitur possibilitas« cannot be said; what does not exist at all need not be possible in every respect.",[]],"reflm:3535":["*qvod continet realitatem* (what contains reality)",[]],"reflm:3536":["Thing and its predicates",[]],"reflm:3537":["chimera, fantasy, ideal.",[]],"reflm:3538":["*determinatio entis, quatenus qvid sit positum vel qvoties positum sit aliqvid, spectatur* (determination of a being, in so far as what is posited, or how often something is posited, is considered).",[]],"reflm:3539":["The difference of one thing from another through the multiplicity of the homogeneous that is contained in both [ihm] is magnitude. The difference of the heterogeneous is quality.",[]],"reflm:3540":["*qvod possibile est per unum aliqvoties positum, est qvantum* (what is possible through one posited several times is a quantum).",[]],"reflm:3541":["better *quanta*; that something is a quantum can be known absolutely, but how great it is (*qvantitas*) only relatively.",[]],"reflm:3542":["*quantum* and *quantitas* (quantum and quantity).",[]],"reflm:3543":["In the margin on the right, \nTaken substantively.\nThe adjectival is hypothetical.\nFurther likewise in the margin on the right, above l. 3, next to M § 74 »Quicquid — est«, probably also to »unitas categorica«:\nquantitative and qualitative",[]],"reflm:3544":["That of which there are not several of the same kind is unique.",[]],"reflm:3545":["Nonsense in which there is method.",[]],"reflm:3546":["On »Sectio VII. Perfectum.«, or \n*perfectio entis in genere est omnitudo realitatis* (the perfection of a being in general is the allness of reality).",[]],"reflm:3547":["*consentiunt vel logice, qvatenus non contradicunt, vel realiter, qvatenus sibi non realiter opponuntur* (they agree either logically, in so far as they do not contradict, or really, in so far as they are not really opposed to one another).",[]],"reflm:3548":["On »Sectio VII«:\n*perfectio* (perfection) taken adjectivally or substantively. Likewise unity, truth, possibility.",[]],"reflm:3549":["*Omnimode contingens* (contingent in every way)",[]],"reflm:3550":["Something is contingent either to a thing (that is, it is not necessary to it), e.g. composition to the elements, or breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3551":["There are contingent essences, namely the essences of all contingent things, e.g. motions.",[]],"reflm:3552":["Unconditioned necessity is, in respect of both the outer and the inner Best breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3553":["Absolutely necessary.",[]],"reflm:3554":["Either in respect of one determination or of all: 1. the opposite is internally or absolutely not thinkable, that is, there cannot be more necessary beings.\nFurther:\nthat is, under the condition under which something belongs necessarily to a thing, this thing cannot be determined in another way; but it can be subjected to another condition. E.g. a bell cannot be otherwise than [inn] internally hollow and open; but the same metal can nevertheless be melted into a bar.",[]],"reflm:3555":["After the opening word of M § 125 Kant wants inserted:\n*perdurabilis* (enduring)",[]],"reflm:3556":["*subiectum mutatur, determinationes variantur, status* (the subject is changed, the determinations vary, the state) breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3557":["Beside and \nWhether matter, motion, is reality.\nWhether vice is *realitas* (reality) or *negatio* (negation).\nNegations in objects are limits.\n*realitas affecta negationibus* (reality affected by negations).\nMultiplicity of reality and degree.\nSpace and time have no degree.\nExtensive, protensive and intensive magnitude.",[]],"reflm:3558":["*opposito realis* (real opposition)",[]],"reflm:3559":["In the margin below, \n*limitatio* (limitation).",[]],"reflm:3560":["Occamists (g Thomists) nominalists. Scotists realists.",[]],"reflm:3561":["nominalists and realists.",[]],"reflm:3562":["not 2/3 + 3/4 = 1. On the 21 quarters of the city of Paris.",[]],"reflm:3563":["Kant changes the definition at the beginning of M § 157 so that it reads:\n*Compositum prorsus idem cum suis partibus est totum* (a composite wholly the same with its parts is a whole).",[]],"reflm:3564":["*Qvod non existere potest nisi vel ut pars vel ut rationatum vel ut accidens* (what can exist only either as a part or as a grounded or as an accident).",[]],"reflm:3565":["*totalitas vel qvanti vel compositi qva talis. Pars totius vel qvanti vel compositi* (totality, either of a quantum or of a composite as such. Part of a whole, either of a quantum or of a composite).",[]],"reflm:3566":["*qvantum qvantitatis — — indeterminatae* (a quantum of indeterminate quantity — —).\n*determinata est qvantitas* (quantity is determinate).\n[*partes assignabiles sunt* ([the] parts are assignable)]\n*qvantum dabile est, qvod [totum] ut completum potest esse actuale* (a quantum is givable which, [as a whole], as completed, can be actual). *partes assignabiles sunt, qvae in se possunt clare cognosci* (parts are assignable which can be clearly known in themselves). [*cuius partes* ([that] whose parts)] *in qvo partium assignabilium multitudo est indeterminata (cuius partes nisi per totum omnimode dari non possunt), est continuum* (that in which the multitude of assignable parts is indeterminate [whose parts cannot be given in every way except through the whole] is a continuum).",[]],"reflm:3567":["*Falsum est; nam qvod est nihilo majus, illius pars nihilo aequale; ergo nihilum est pars alicujus, qvod repugnat* (it is false; for what is greater than nothing, a part of it is equal to nothing; therefore nothing is a part of something, which is contradictory).",[]],"reflm:3568":["*Absolute* (that is, *respectu qvodam*) *possibile* (absolutely [that is, in a certain respect] possible).\n*In certo respectu etiam possibile* (also possible in a certain respect).",[]],"reflm:3569":["*stricte determinat, late* (strictly it determines, broadly).\n*latitudinarius, qvi admittit exceptiones* (latitudinarian: one who admits exceptions).",[]],"reflm:3570":["*qvo magis ad universalitatem accedit et caret restrictionibus* (the more it approaches universality and lacks restrictions).",[]],"reflm:3571":["The intensive magnitude of laws rests on necessity, that is, on the lack of exceptions. *Nulla regula sine exceptione, sed lex est absque exceptione* (no rule without exception, but a law is without exception).",[]],"reflm:3572":["*substratum*, *stabile* (substrate, the abiding).",[]],"reflm:3573":["*Subiectum reale est substantia* (the real subject is substance).",[]],"reflm:3574":["*accidens potest esse subiectum logicum* (an accident can be a logical subject).",[]],"reflm:3575":["*Phaenomenon substantiatum* (substantiated appearance)",[]],"reflm:3576":["*Si haec vera essent, status nullius entis esset immutabilis* (if these were true, the state of no being would be immutable).",[]],"reflm:3577":["On the heading »Sectio VIII. Status«:\nWhether it is one and the same to say: a thing has changed, or: its state (at most the inner) has changed.",[]],"reflm:3578":["Strictly it is not a *modus* (mode) but the substance that is changed. For that which is changed remains; the change is only the alternation of its determinations.",[]],"reflm:3579":["On the last two sentences of M § 210:\nThe [Menschl] body (s the lifeless) works [wirkt]; the living (g makes) acts [handelt] (s *operatur*); the free does [thut] (s *facit*).",[]],"reflm:3580":["*substantia substantiam actuans* (g *producens*) *creat*∗ (a substance actuating [g producing] a substance creates).\n(g *durationis causa conservat* [for the sake of duration it conserves].)\n*substantia substantiae actrici complementum addens concurrit* (a substance adding a complement to an acting substance concurs).\n∗(s *causa ortus substantiae creat* [the cause of the origin of a substance creates].)",[]],"reflm:3581":["If all suffering of the substance is properly the determination of the activity of the suffering subject, then no substance can be determined internally, even by God, to other and greater accidents than are possible from its nature; otherwise another fundamental force and substantiality would be produced in it, and thus it would be re-created, and there would not be the identity of the subject.",[]],"reflm:3582":["The inner possibility of a force (s of acting) is the capacity [Vermögen]. E.g. someone can have a great capacity for science, but as yet has no force of acuteness, etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:3583":["The internally sufficient ground for it is force; if it is also externally sufficient, it is living force; if it is externally insufficient, it is dead force. Dead force on account of the hindrance is *conatus* (endeavour).",[]],"reflm:3584":["Capacity and force,\n*potentia* — *actus* (potency — act).\nForce acts, capacity does not.",[]],"reflm:3585":["The inner principle of the possibility of acting is the faculty*, e.g. a machine**.\n*(g The inner determining ground of the faculty for acting is endeavour (*conatus*). The faculty together with an endeavour to act is force. Force, so far as it is inwardly sufficient for the actuality of the action but without effect, is dead force; this always proves a hindrance. (s Force sufficient both inwardly and outwardly is living force.))\n**(s The faculty together with a determining ground of it (e.g. an applied lever) is force. Force (that is, faculty and its determining ground together) [is brings] is of itself sufficient for the actuality of the action. Thus *posita vi* (g *per se*) *ponitur actio* (once the force is posited, the action is posited, g of itself). But another [ground] can conflict with the determining ground [of the] in the force. [This] The state of a force in this conflict is endeavour (*conatus*), and that which contains the ground of the fact that the action does not result is the hindrance. Every *conatus* is an action to which something works in opposition. Hence it is dead force. Living force is not greater than the *conatus*, but brings forth a desired effect.\nNB. For the *conatus* a time is required, if it is to become living force. For if the support were taken from a weight and gravity were not to continue to act, the single impression would give no motion.)",[]],"reflm:3586":["To act is entitled: to contain the sufficient ground of the *accidens*.\nThe possibility of the action is the faculty.\nThe inwardly sufficient ground of the action is the force.\nIt is dead if [outwardly] a ground of the opposite is present.\nThe opposed ground is *impedimentum* (hindrance); if it is another substance: *resistentia* (resistance).",[]],"reflm:3587":["Concordant forces: one of them adds to the other something of the same kind, e.g. the sun's attraction on the moon. Unconnected [forces]: here the accession is 0. Opposed [forces]: –a; they take away just as much, so that the difference between the addition and the taking away is twice the amount of the *accessorium*. Hence the danger of diminution is always greater than the gain from the addition.",[]],"reflm:3588":["Substance, so far as it contains the sufficient ground of a determinate *accidens*, acts; so far as it contains the ground of accidents in general, it has a force. This is either inwardly sufficient or insufficient. In the latter case it is a mere faculty; in the former it is either outwardly insufficient, i.e. a *conatus*, or outwardly sufficient: *vis viva* (living force).\nWith every dead force another, opposed force is also dead.",[]],"reflm:3589":["Where there is force, there is also action (in lifeless beings), but not therefore at once effect, because an inner or outer resistance [a] is the counter-action of a force which cancels the effect of the former. In living beings faculty and force are distinguished; in lifeless ones, e.g., a rope has as much faculty to bear a load hung upon it as it exercises actual force of cohesion.",[]],"reflm:3590":["The real relation of a substance, as cause, to [its] the [there] effect possible through it is entitled force. This [relation], before the effect, is entitled *conatus*; without the effect, because a resistance takes place, *vis mortua* (dead force).",[]],"reflm:3591":["At the top of the page:\nBecause every *nexus* is *logicus* or *realis* (logical or real), so is breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3592":["*ortus est existentia, qvae seqvitur non-existentiam.* (Origination is existence which follows non-existence.)",[]],"reflm:3593":["Arising and passing away, beginning and ceasing. The latter applies to *accidentia*.",[]],"reflm:3594":["*Substantia phaenomenon est perdurabile.* (Substance as appearance is the abiding.)",[]],"reflm:3595":["Above the closing word of M § 233:\n*Compositum qvantitative, qvalitative dictum.* (A composite, spoken of quantitatively, [or] qualitatively.)",[]],"reflm:3596":["*simile* of the bow.",[]],"reflm:3597":["Tensed.",[]],"reflm:3598":["*Spatium et tempus habent terminos.* (Space and time have boundaries.)",[]],"reflm:3599":["Identity and diversity belong to the relation of comparison.",[]],"reflm:3600":["*Generice, specifice et numerice identica.* (Identical generically, specifically and numerically.)",[]],"reflm:3601":["wholly the same (*totaliter eadem*) —\nthe same in number (*numero idem*)\n(g *congruentia*) (g as regards the internal (*qvoad interna*) —\nin diverse relations)\nas regards quantity (*qvoad quantitatem*) —\nthey are also so as regards quality, because only the homogeneous can be equal,\nas regards quality (*qvoad qvalitatem*) —\nthey can also be so as regards quantity, and then they would be congruent.",[]],"reflm:3602":["The *terminus* of space is distinguished from the *limites*.\nA spherical surface is not properly terminated anywhere, but is terminating.",[]],"reflm:3603":["Because the boundary is a ground of limits, it is no part, since it adds nothing to the magnitude but rather cuts off; but the cutting off belongs to both [things] that are cut off; hence the boundary is common.",[]],"reflm:3604":["The quality of the boundary of the extended is figure.",[]],"reflm:3605":["The community of boundaries. Two points do not touch one another; hence they are *distant*.",[]],"reflm:3606":["Two points always lie in a line; three points [in a surface and 4 in a corporeal space.], through which no common straight line can be drawn, lie in a surface, and four points, of which no straight line can pass through three, lie in corporeal space. Moreover, from these data two bodies are possible, namely in two opposed regions.\nThis means as much as the following. If one point is given, then at a given distance another point can be given in infinitely many places (g i.e. it lies on a spherical surface). If two points are given, the third point lies in a circle*. If three are given, the fourth point lies in two places on opposite sides**. Every position of points can be determined geometrically from certain data, but the last position of the fourth point only in relation to our body.\nOne must therefore know the whole corporeal space, in order to draw a line from a given point with a given distance; for this can be drawn only to a determinate point in the surface.\n*(g the third point is determined, but not its place.)\n**(g for then no rotation is possible.)",[]],"reflm:3607":["*Continua* have not only limits but also boundaries.",[]],"reflm:3608":["The mathematical concepts are unity, plurality, number, infinity, (the relations) [equality, inequality], composition of a magnitude from another once or several times, or from its parts, or also from many magnitudes *per synthesin vel positivam vel negativam* (by synthesis, either positive or negative); hence the idea of equality, inequality and relations. The objects of mathematics are space, time, motion, or any other ground, estimated either as a *totum* (whole) or as a ground, the former through the many that is in it, the latter through what is posited by it.",[]],"reflm:3609":["If the quality of a magnitude [magnitudes of] is of such a kind that the action [the *coordinatio*] of raising one of them, which has a certain extensive magnitude, intensively to the second degree is a multiplication, then there are no more than 3 *dimensiones*. But where the magnitude |\ngrows not intensively, by multiplying, but extensively, by adding, there is only one *dimensio*. Of things that are simultaneous one can represent the generation of the multitude from unity as in flux.",[]],"reflm:3610":["It is remarkable that with point, line and surface, when they move (g perpendicularly), the motions of the elements do not coincide, but with the body they do. Nor is it necessary that it happen perpendicularly, so long as it is not in the direction in which the motion of the previous elements would meet. The question is: why then do they meet at the fourth dimension, namely that after the generation of a body? Is the ground of this not perhaps to be found in combinations in general?",[]],"reflm:3611":["*qvasi qvies esset accidens.* (as if rest were an accident.)",[]],"reflm:3612":["Only in *phaenomenis* (appearances).",[]],"reflm:3613":["*ut quantitas* (as quantity)\nbut he forgot to strike out the second word of the text, »continuatio«, which of course must fall away with the new definition.",[]],"reflm:3614":["*omnibus partibus temporis* (in all parts of time)",[]],"reflm:3615":["*ens a se, non mutatur.* (A being of itself is not changed.) For it is in no determination through others, hence not to something else.",[]],"reflm:3616":["*respectus* and *relation*; the former of comparison, the latter of influences.",[]],"reflm:3617":["*Accidens, qvatenus est causa, non dicitur efficiens.* (An accident, so far as it is a cause, is not called efficient.)\n*Substantia, qvatenus ipsius accidens est causa, itidem non est efficiens.* (A substance, so far as its accident is the cause, likewise is not efficient.)",[]],"reflm:3618":["*vel efficiens vel conditio sine qva non* (either efficient [cause] or condition without which not)",[]],"reflm:3619":["Buffon seeks *nexus effectivos* (efficient connections), others final ones. Epicurus [seeks] the former in organisation.",[]],"reflm:3620":["To the closing sentence of M § 332:\nThe causality in the cause *non est nobilior effectu* (is not nobler than the effect).",[]],"reflm:3621":["Estimable (worthy of estimation)\nInestimable (not capable of estimation),\njust as *unverbesserlich* [incorrigible, or unimprovable] has two senses.",[]],"reflm:3622":["objective",[]],"reflm:3623":["Idea. Archetype. Pattern. Example. Similarity, analogy are different.",[]],"reflm:3624":["*universalitas absoluta conceptus.* (absolute universality of the concept.)\n*universalitas absoluta totius, h.e. rerum universitas metaphysica.* (absolute universality of the whole, i.e. the metaphysical universe of things.) *haec probaret, unicum tantum esse mundum, causam esse summam.* (This would prove that there is only one world, and that there is a supreme cause.)",[]],"reflm:3625":["Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:3626":["*casus respectivus. absolutus.* (relative case. absolute.)",[]],"reflm:3627":["Nature is here taken only adjectivally.",[]],"reflm:3628":["To the heading »Mundus optimus«:\nThe world as *summum bonum derivativum* (derived highest good).",[]],"reflm:3629":["[Every constraint is either a] All necessitation (g is either a necessitation) against the will (g or with the will,) either aesthetic or physical, a necessitation of the will itself breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3630":[") M 277'.\nThe action, so far as it so arises from the faculty of desire that I can become conscious that I am not determined to it breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3631":["Separation of the soul from the body.",[]],"reflm:3632":["*remotio nexus vel virtualis vel localis.* (removal of the connection, either virtual or local.)",[]],"reflm:3633":["*Separatio animae a corpore est vel localis vel virtualis.* (The separation of the soul from the body is either local or virtual.)",[]],"reflm:3634":["Beside the second-last sentence of M § 784:\nMetempsychosis of Michelangelo, Galileo and Newton. (from Nelli in Florence.)",[]],"reflm:3635":["Internal predicate of God\nexternal\n{\nindependence; dependence of all things on him",[]],"reflm:3636":["Logical perfection (*sufficientia*, sufficiency) or practical.",[]],"reflm:3637":["*nexus cum contingentibus* (connection with contingent things)",[]],"reflm:3638":["He is not changeable.\nHis existence is not changeable; [it is] abiding.",[]],"reflm:3639":["Because things subsist through God, it seems as if they subsisted in him.\n*Non est anima Mundi.* (He is not the soul of the world.)",[]],"reflm:3640":["*Causatum maximum* [the greatest effect]. The highest created good.",[]],"reflm:3641":["As an active being he has nature.",[]],"reflm:3642":["On the underlined word »theologia«:\n*ectypa* [derived, copied]: from mundane affections. Adapted *per reductionem* [by reduction]: *analogica* [analogical].",[]],"reflm:3643":["The knowledge of the good (g that is, of what is good in the whole (in every actual relation)), derived from the idea of the whole as a ground of choice, is wisdom.\nWhat is to be good in every respect must be derived from the idea of the most perfect whole.\n(g Prudence is the knowledge of the means to ends that are not entirely in our power, and so with regard to the opinion and freedom of other human beings.)\nThe wisdom of man consists in this, that he does without the ends that are not in his power.",[]],"reflm:3644":["Wisdom is directed to the good.\nPrudence to the useful.",[]],"reflm:3645":["The faculty of knowing the relation of all things* to the highest good** is wisdom; the faculty of knowing their relation to the (g good in appearance), the particular good, is prudence (not in God).\n(s The adequacy of knowledge to the highest good, and to its possibility, is either merely theoretical, that is, science, or moral, that is, wisdom.)\n*(s The faculty of knowing the [means] best means to it is artistic wisdom (*Kunstweisheit*); the faculty of knowing the end together with the will for it is moral wisdom.)\n**(s Either to the highest good itself or to the derived highest good. The former is the ground of the latter, but only in the divine understanding.)",[]],"reflm:3646":[") M 359.\nWisdom in general is the perfection of knowledge, so far as it is adequate to the whole of all ends (either the physical or the moral whole).",[]],"reflm:3647":["Wisdom is the (g subjective) principle of the unity of all ends [a pr] according to reason, and consequently is happiness united with morality.",[]],"reflm:3648":["The highest good has two parts. Which is the condition, and which the conditioned? Morality or happiness?",[]],"reflm:3649":["Whether we can fathom the ends of divine wisdom in nature, and whether that is physicotheology? — He alone is holy, blessed, wise.",[]],"reflm:3650":["The divine understanding derives all knowledge of the particular from the whole of all possibility, and every particular end from the whole of all ends. Everything from itself.",[]],"reflm:3651":["M 359':\n[Kl] Skill (*Geschicklichkeit*) is the faculty of reason in respect of the means to any ends whatever; prudence, in respect of the sum of all subjective ends.\nWisdom is the faculty of determining every particular end through the whole of all objective ends. |\nM 359: |\nHence »a man« and »wise« is a contradiction, except in morality, as a model.",[]],"reflm:3652":["It is all one whether many or few means lead to the end, provided the end is attained. Superfluous means are blameworthy in men only because they hinder their other ends. But since the sufficient ground of the means lies solely in the end, there can be nothing more in the former than is required for the latter.",[]],"reflm:3653":["As means in and for themselves (g in execution) there can surely never be more than is in the end; that is, there is not more in the cause than in the effect. But as means in the intention there can, through error, be more than is required for the end. Yet much can exist besides what fulfils the whole end, and the law of parsimony is not for God.",[]],"reflm:3654":["The commensurateness of means.",[]],"reflm:3655":["Not everything is ends or means, or at least not everything is good in the consequences of certain causes.",[]],"reflm:3656":["Divine wisdom is the ground of the agreement of his choice with itself, without exceptions or collision, in accordance with one plan.",[]],"reflm:3657":["The parsimony of the divine decree, in choosing only that which is useful as a means to the highest good represented, is no property of divine wisdom. It is the property of the powerless, who, if he does in any case more than contributes to some benefit, neglects other benefit.",[]],"reflm:3658":["The *voluntas obiective antecedens* [objectively antecedent will] is that whose [obiect] motive is what in objects is universal.\nThe *voluntas obiective consequens* [objectively consequent will] is that whose motive is what in objects is individual.",[]],"reflm:3659":["The best will determines [perfectionem] particular volition *in omnitudine* [in its entirety], that is, according to human manner of speaking, in the universal, in so far as this is restricted by particular conditions.",[]],"reflm:3660":["The divine will, so far as it is directed to what the object has in common with others [nicht ge] that he does not will, is entitled *antecedens*; so far as it is directed to that whereby the object is distinguished from all others, it is entitled *consequens*.",[]],"reflm:3661":["*Voluntas antecedens* is the will that is directed to a general concept of an object (as undetermined); *consequens*, the will directed to the determinate concept of the object.",[]],"reflm:3662":["The object of the highest intelligence is the highest good: in respect of knowledge, as the good known; in respect of will, as author.\nThe highest good in the whole, and also the relation of each individual thing to it:\n1. the relation to the general concept of the good: *voluntas antecedens*.\n2. the relation to the thoroughly determinate knowledge of this world: *voluntas consequens*.",[]],"reflm:3663":["One could still ask whether reward is a matter of justice or of kindness (*Gütigkeit*).",[]],"reflm:3664":["On M § 907 Kant has made a sign after the words »bonum morale«, underlined by him, to which the same sign in the right margin before the word\n*meritum*\ncorresponds, which evidently is to take the place of the two words of the text. For Kant adds:\nFor whether *bonum morale* [moral good] in general is merely [Weishei] wise kindness.",[]],"reflm:3665":["*Praemia* [rewards] are either *munera* [gifts]: *praemia gratuita* [free rewards]\n—\n—\n—\nor *mercedes* [wages]: — — *debita* [owed].\n*Moralia* [moral]: of wisdom,\n*pragmatica* [pragmatic]: of prudence.",[]],"reflm:3666":["Punitive justice is distinguished from vindictive justice (*Rachgerechtigkeit*). The former is exercised by one whose right has been injured, the latter by one whose person has been injured.",[]],"reflm:3667":["God does not punish the offence against his person (*laesae maiestatis* [of injured majesty]) but, because man knows God only by analogy with men, the wickedness which man displays, [in that] he would be capable of it [sie] in relation to other men. [Hence] Otherwise we should lose ourselves in the infinity of the crime.",[]],"reflm:3668":["Punitive justice belongs to wisdom.",[]],"reflm:3669":["On the opening words of M § 910:\n*distributiva vel vindicativa* [distributive or vindictive]",[]],"reflm:3670":["*Iustitia distributiva* [distributive justice] is not instrumental but final.",[]],"reflm:3671":["Kant alters the first sentence of M § 910 so that it reads:\nThe justice of God is not *remuneratoria* [remunerative] but *punitiva* [punitive]; it is either *pragmatica* [pragmatic]: that of the ruler, or *moralis* [moral]: that of the judge, *quia peccatum est* [because there is sin].",[]],"reflm:3672":["Whether there is a *crimen laesae majestatis divinae* [crime of injured divine majesty].",[]],"reflm:3673":["All these properties are the moral [which], primarily on the ground that God demands the inner part of the human heart [conformable] to him and in agreement with his presence.",[]],"reflm:3674":["He demands forbearance among men in punishing.\nKindness it is that defers the punishment of the offender for the sake of his improvement.",[]],"reflm:3675":["He punishes, according to the order of nature in the whole, only after a long time.\nHe punishes only in conformity with the infinite progress.",[]],"reflm:3676":["He [wills] loves impartiality in the judgment of men.\nHe has no favourites, e.g. the Jewish people.",[]],"reflm:3677":["Man cannot always derive from general providence the particular good which God dispenses. He is therefore entitled to regard it as a particular providence and to thank God for it, yet also to believe that in a manner unknown to him it is nevertheless general.",[]],"reflm:3678":["He administers equity; that is, before his tribunal a transgression of equity is a violation of strict right.",[]],"reflm:3679":["He judges from the standpoint of a man, who is a limited being. Man can regard much as a preference which God grants him above others.",[]],"reflm:3680":["He demands sincerity, which in regard to God is commonly much lacking, since men affect praises and devotions which they do not feel, profess from fear to believe much, and busy themselves with flattery.\n(s God is open in the revelations necessary to us; he is not concealed, not enigmatic and ambiguous. He does not lead us into temptation.)",[]],"reflm:3681":["He wills truthfulness among men.\n(s One must not say of God what is entirely identical with the predicates through which we think his moral perfections.)",[]],"reflm:3682":["*beatus* [blessed]. Self-sufficiency in well-being.",[]],"reflm:3683":["He is above the [nature] world, as its originator (nature) and as its lawgiver (freedom).",[]],"reflm:3684":["*finis absqve motivis* [end without motives].",[]],"reflm:3685":["*actuatio mundi et regimen* [actuation and government of the world].",[]],"reflm:3686":["On the last two sentences of M § 950:\nThe preservation of the form of the world, conformable to the final end, in time is entitled gubernation: administration.",[]],"reflm:3687":["Not *influxus*, which is [actio] modification, but *actuatio* of substance.",[]],"reflm:3688":["A continued beginning is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3689":["*Concursus* [concurrence] to nature or to freedom.\n*Providentia* [providence] with respect to laws (determining them) is general; with respect to events it is special.",[]],"reflm:3690":["He does not concur in the formal element of actions. He is not the author of evil according to the ordinary direction.",[]],"reflm:3691":["Permission (*Erlaubnis*). Toleration (*Zulassung*) is not permission.",[]],"reflm:3692":["Lawgiver in the beginning,\nRuler in the duration,\nJudge at the end; properly, in the infinity of the progress.",[]],"reflm:3693":["One who is not subject to the power of another.",[]],"reflm:3694":["397. E I 689.\nIs he who binds the creature in accordance with an absolute will, without taking into consideration * their advantage in the laws. Thus there are despotic and patriotic laws.\n*(g to take into consideration the agreement of their own will, governed by reason; that is, treating them as machines.)",[]],"reflm:3695":["The will that is determined, with regard to the universal grounds [of it] of willing a certain action, is *antecedens* (antecedent). The will that is determined with regard to the *individuum* is *consequens* (consequent).",[]],"reflm:3696":["Here God is represented as if he decided upon everything possible, which part he takes out for his choice.",[]],"reflm:3697":["A decree (*decretum*) is a consequent will (*voluntas consequens*), therefore with respect to the individual (*respectu individui*).\nAn antecedent will (*voluntas antecedens*) is one whose impulsive cause (*caussa impulsiva*) is only something general, which is common to all objects of choice (*arbitrii*).\n[No] decree concerning the founding of this world is a decree of the individual, therefore it is not universal. There is no universal decree. But there is a decree that is universal in the sense of the whole (*universum*) or total, differing not from a [partial] particular one, but from a partial one.",[]],"reflm:3698":["is the highest good of the world (*summum bonum mundi*), insofar as it is opposed to the highest good of the individual.",[]],"reflm:3699":["Divine free choice (*Willkühr*) is never absolutely unconditioned, but perhaps it is unconditioned with regard to the moral quality of the person, as despotism; for God's intention could after all be directed to the best of the world as a whole [and t] and this could be the condition.* God cannot sacrifice a (g rational) creature to the whole of the world, but he can well permit that it sacrifice itself.\n*(g Here it is assumed that God uses the rational creature merely as a means to the perfection of the whole. But there is no moral worth in a world where rational beings are used merely as means.)",[]],"reflm:3700":["Whether the divine will is in itself determined by motives (*motiven*), or is thought by us merely according to this analogy?",[]],"reflm:3701":["Fate.",[]],"reflm:3702":["as it were as the end of the will (*tanquam fine voluntatis*)",[]],"reflm:3703":["You ask: [is] who is happier in the world, the virtuous or the vicious? If one examines it, there will always be mixed in with the advantages of the [virtuous] wicked something that the [vicious] virtuous does not desire, and on account of which he would not wish to exchange his condition for the other's; thus he is in fact more content with himself than one thinks. The ills that befall the virtuous do not properly affect virtue, but are common to all. If the universal laws were to be restricted only to the relation of the pious and the godless, then tell me: who then are the pious? Will not one hold this person deserving of punishment, and another hold someone else |\nto be so, and would God's justice then be recognized by all? Bread is the reward not of virtue but of diligence. If finally you give man everything good, tell me: are you then content, do you not always desire more, and could God find a goal for your wishes? The true prize of virtue is the inner tranquillity of the soul; the other goods overthrow or corrupt it. Learning, posthumous fame, wealth: none of them has the true good in itself. Thus virtue alone makes true happiness, which finds in abundance as well as in want, in weeping as well as in gaiety, something that satisfies it. Since virtue therefore finds no lack, wishing counts for nothing.\nSelf-love, when it is combined with the love of God and of one's neighbour, constitutes the happiness of men. The greater the love, the further extended: the greater the happiness. God begins his love with the whole and extends it to the parts; human love, however, begins with itself and spreads little by little over the whole. The earth smiles upon such a man from all sides, and the Godhead itself sees its image in his soul.",[]],"reflm:3704":["P. I:\nOutline of optimism.\nOptimism is that doctrinal system [which, from the presup|position of a most good, wise and almighty original being] the [presence of] evils of the world [with the concept] seeks to justify from the presupposition of an infinitely perfect, good and almighty original being, [to reconcile] in that one convinces oneself that [of such nothing but that] which, notwithstanding all apparent contradictions, has been chosen by this infinitely perfect being must nevertheless be the best among all that is possible. It ascribes the presence of evil not to the choice of divine good pleasure but to the unavoidable necessity of the essential defects of finite things, [which] which have been brought into the plan of creation through the decree of permission, without fault on His part. Yet these defects are so turned by His wisdom and goodness to the best of the whole that the displeasure which the sight of them taken singly can arouse is completely made good in the whole, through the compensation which the divine goodness knows how to arrange. Thus this world is the best among all that were possible through divine power, and that which piecemeal could be better [the without] might not through the [text breaks off].\n[Herr von Leibnitz] If one examines closely the outline which Leibnitz gives of his principles, one will find that this idea expresses his opinion as exactly as possible. One need only consider his division into necessary or metaphysical evils and contingent or hypothetically physical and hypothetically moral evils. One must consider his distinction between the antecedent and the consequent will, of which the first strives to exclude all evil, [all without] while the second includes it in His plan as the unavoidable consequence of the [finitude] eternal nature of things. Then one will be convinced of the [correctness] truthfulness of our portrayal. |\nLeibnitz represents the goodness of God, which spreads without measure, as the pull of a stream which drives everything contained within it to movement with equal force, but carries along more slowly than the others the heavy cargo ships, which possess more natural force of inertia than those of lesser mass.\nP. II:\nHe represents God as ready to reveal Himself, with the whole infinity of [His] all [His] attributes, in the works of creation, and as having a true displeasure at the evils whose sight so greatly disturbs the mind of the well-disposed. But He lets them hope, on the strength of the goodness, wisdom and power of this Lord, sufficiently known from other undeniable grounds, that the defects will be compensated in connection with advantages, and that, however much the evils may finally disfigure the [plan] best plan, [it] they could nevertheless [not] not have been prevented in the whole without a still greater deviation. To be sure, freely acting beings could have avoided many an evil action, to the great pleasure of God. But there was an unavoidable necessity to choose the lesser of two evils, one of which is the lack of freedom, the other the lack of the morally best; and there were also in the best plan other obstacles which could move God, from concern for still greater absurdities, to omit [to] the arrangement of certain moving causes which could have prevented some kinds of evil. In short: it was not possible otherwise; there had to be evil. Thanks be to eternal wisdom that it permitted the least, and has carried everything in the whole to its glory in the most splendid manner. It is true, I think, that we do not yet see where the compensation is properly to turn out, which is to indemnify the whole for its |\npartial defects. But [are you not assured] are you not assured that God (g thus) is [so] as (g how He) must be, in order that we may expect everything good from Him? In this case you can expect nothing other than, in the sequel, a complete satisfaction, or at least a complete vindication of the divine justice and goodness.\nP. III:\nComparison of Pope's doctrinal system with optimism, and the superiority of the former.\nLeibnitz admitted that the deviations and defects which, as true evils, cause distress to the well-disposed, were really such; but he reserved the right to excuse the permission of them by the highest wisdom, (g which he recognized on other grounds, [on account of]). Thus the attributes of God were secured only for the benefit of those who have insight and docility enough to give assent to the metaphysical proofs of the divine existence. The remaining part, namely those who, [on account of] on beholding the world, would gladly recognize the trace of God, is left in distress. Pope chose a way which, for making the beautiful proof of God intelligible to all men, [is] is the most suitable of all possible ways, and which, and this constitutes the very perfection of his system, (g even) subjects all possibility to the dominion of an all-sufficient original being. Under this being things can have no other attributes, not even those which are called essentially necessary, than such as agree together perfectly in the ex|pression\nof His perfection. He goes through creation piece by piece, chiefly where it seems most to lack agreement; yet he shows that each thing which we should gladly wish away from the plan of greatest perfection is, even considered by itself, good, and that one need not (g beforehand) have a favourable prejudice about the wisdom of the ordering being, in order to win assent for it. The essential and necessary determinations of things, the universal laws, which are set in relation to one another in a harmonious plan by no [foreign union forced upon them] (g constrained) unification, will dispose themselves as it were of their own accord toward the attainment of perfect ends. Self-love, which has in view only its own pleasure and which evidently seems to be the cause of the moral disorder we observe, is the origin of that beautiful [attribute] agreement which we admire. [Wealth can itself] Everything that benefits itself finds itself under the necessity of being useful to others at the same time. The universal bonds which link the whole together in an unexplored |\nP. IV: |\nmanner bring it about that individual advantages always relate to the advantage of other things, and indeed by a natural consequence. Thus a universal law of nature establishes that love [through the drives] which preserves the whole, and indeed through such moving causes as naturally also bring forth that very evil whose sources we should gladly see destroyed.\nWhen one sees the essential constitutions in so universal a natural harmony, can one suppose that what so fits itself to an excellent whole is at the same time the cause of so evil [text breaks off].\nOn the universal perfection of the constitution of the world in the physical and moral sense.\nThe foremost rule of the perfection of the world is that it be complete in the highest degree, that everything be there that is possible, and that neither in the chain of beings nor in the manifoldness of their variation anything be lacking that is capable of existence at all; for there is for the world in general no greater defect than that nothing should be in some one of its parts. Hence the [revelation] field of the revelation of the divine power embraces all kinds of finite things and [through] with a kind of richness extends even to defects, and loses itself only through all the stages of diminution, from the highest degrees of perfection down to nothing.\nLet us, on this presupposition, listen with contempt to the complaints of those to whom heaven has not, in their opinion, allotted an agreeable portion of perfections. I should like, says one, to have less darkness in the understanding and less vehemence in the sensible inclinations, if only I were so fortunate as to have [inclination to sin] no other inclination than that for virtue. If most people considered that, when they wish for themselves such attributes as presuppose an alteration of their nature, this means just as much as desiring that they themselves should not be at all, and that in their place another being of the demanded constitution should exist, |\nthey would go into themselves; for self-love has an abhorrence of annihilation, and is more content with the state, such as it may have been allotted to it, than that it [text breaks off] with the side.",[]],"reflm:3705":["S. I:\nDefects of Optimism.\nLeibnitz was right to entitle his system a Theodicy, or defence of the good cause of God. For in fact it is nothing other than a justification of God against the imputation that he may perhaps be the author of evil. It proceeds by assuring us that, so far as lies in him, all is good, and that it is at least not his fault if [all] not every thing turns out as perfect as, by the wish of the upright, it ought to turn out.\nThe faults of this doctrine are far too serious for one to rest content with it. Leibnitz [represents] represents the rules of perfection (g which has an end in view) as conflicting with one another in execution. He regards the exceptions as necessary defects, and finds the action befitting the highest wisdom to consist in its making its choice on the side of the best, just as a seafarer sacrifices part of his wares in order to save the ship and the rest. It is true that the wisdom and goodness of God here triumph over all reproaches. But what is one to say of the infinity (g and) the independence? From what causes can the essential determinations of things have that discord which makes the perfections incompatible in combination, although each of them taken singly would increase God's good pleasure? What kind of [strange] inscrutable conflict is this between the general will of God, which aims solely at the good, and the (g metaphysical) necessity [of the | metaphysical properties], which will not accommodate itself to that will with complete agreement (g without exceptions)? Suppose that evils, by I know not what kind of necessary fatality, extort from God his permission of them, without having aroused in him [on their account] any good pleasure. Then they place this most blessed being in a certain kind of displeasure, which, though it can be somewhat mitigated by the vindication of his innocence, cannot be quelled. If everything were good in the whole, or were still good in the parts, then the sight from every side would unfailingly be the source of a true pleasure. Why then must it be so arranged that everything in the parts |\nS. II: |\nis disagreeable, merely in order to arouse good pleasure in the whole? If God abhors vices and torments, [why] if he does not desire them but merely permits them: why then was it necessary that they must exist, [since they] even supposing that they cannot be excluded without making room for still greater defects? This excuse serves indeed to acquit God of guilt. But it will never resolve the weighty doubt why the [necessary property] essential necessity has something which conflicts with [God against his] the general will of God, and extorts from him permission, without having won his good pleasure. The whole fault lies in this: Leibnitz places the plan of the best world partly in a kind of independence of the will of God, and partly in a dependence upon it. [All] All possibility is spread out before God. God sees, weighs and examines it; by the determinations inherent in the possibilities he is inclined to one side in accordance with their (g particular) perfections, and to the other side in accordance with what they make up in combination; this comparison occasions his decree. [The world is properly not so because God wills to have it so, but because it cannot be done to make it otherwise. |\nThe independence of the eternal natures takes precedence. The dependence consists only in the plan, in which God strives to order them as well, according to the rules of the best, as their essential determinations permit.]\nThe essence of the world is not [good merely because] solely so because God wills to have it so [and], but because it was not possible otherwise, only ei— [the text] breaks off, but leaves room for 7 lines.\nS. III:\nThe second principal fault of [the doctrine of (Lehrb)] Optimism is that the evils and incongruities perceived in the world are excused only on the presupposition of God's existence. One must therefore (g first) believe that there is an infinitely good and infinitely perfect being, before one can assure oneself that the world, which is assumed to be his work, is beautiful and regular. Yet the general agreement [and] of the arrangements of the world, if it can be known in and for itself, furnishes the finest proof of the existence of God and of the universal dependence of all things upon him. The surest and easiest proof, then, of the actuality of an all-sufficient, infinitely [infinite being] good and infinitely wise being, a proof which is derived from the consideration of the excellent arrangements that the world everywhere displays, is enfeebled by the system of Herr v. Leibnitz. It seems to me that an Epicurus would answer anyone who built upon this proof as follows: If the agreement which you perceive in the world seems to you to prove an ordering wisdom as its author, then you must admit that [that part] the world, for the greater part, does not depend upon this wisdom, since everywhere, in more than half of it, it contains incongruities and repugnant deviations. I do not accept your evasion, that of the wisdom which has perhaps ordered some parts to prudent ends it is to be presumed that it has likewise concealed proofs of its oversight in the remaining parts. I prefer, then, to conclude: if [ihr] the wise supreme cause has not been able to bring all things into a plan of harmonious beauty, then at least not all things were, in respect of their properties, subject to its good pleasure. And the eternal fate, which so greatly restricts the [purpose] power [of this Godhead] of the potent cause [only to make room for (nur Platz zu einzu)] and extorts from it consent to gross evils, thereby robs it of all-sufficiency |\nS. IV: |\nand [in that it weighs it against the rest, under (indem sie sie mit den übrigen wiegt, unter)] subjects it to that same necessity.",[]],"reflm:3706":["P. I:\nIf existence could be counted (g also) among the various predicates that may belong to a thing, then certainly no proof could be demanded that would be more cogent and at the same time easier to grasp, for demonstrating the existence of God, than the Cartesian. For among all possible things there is one in which [all possible (g the highest) reality that is to be met with in one] all the realities are to be met with that can be together in one being. [Among these] Among these realities, that is, genuinely positive predicates, existence also belongs; consequently existence belongs [irgend: in any case] to the most real of all beings by reason of its inner possibility. It is also vain to object to this that [all reality only in thought with one another] such a possible thing contains existence only in the understanding, that is, is posited only as the thing itself is posited in thought, but not [in the real understanding (im Realverstande)] outside thought. For in that way we should have to say of all the predicates that belong to a [thing] possible thing that they do not belong to it in fact but are only posited in it in thought. The latter does indeed hold when one arbitrarily combines with a concept something that is not necessarily posited by it. For example, wings are posited in thought to a horse in order to make a Pegasus; hence [the winged horse] [irgend: any] wings belong to a horse only in thought. Where, on the other hand, the connection |\nof a predicate |\nP. II: |\nwith a thing is not arbitrary but is bound up with it through the essence of the things themselves, there the predicate does not belong to it because we think it in it; rather it is necessary to think it in it, because it belongs to it in itself. Hence I cannot say that the equality of its angles to two right angles belongs to a triangle only in thought; it belongs to it in itself. Nor does this prevent me from merely thinking such a possible thing; for [ich] it is in itself always something, even if no one thinks it, and the predicate belongs to it in itself, even if no one connects it with it. It is the same with existence, if it could be regarded (g as) a predicate of things. For among all that is possible it would necessarily belong to that thing [that is, that thing will necessarily exist] in which all reality is; that is, [einem] a most real being will necessarily exist, and its possibility would include actuality. Further, if, apart from my thought or that of any thing whatever, existence [no predicate of the] did not belong to the most perfect being, the thought of this being in general would be false. For if the thought is correct, it can represent no predicates other than those which are to be found in the thing even without this thought.\nP. III:\nThe question here is not whether more is posited (g in general through an actual thing) than through a non-actual one, but whether more predicates are posited in it. Now [in a kind good one as such, in so far as he is regarded as existing] in the goodness of God, in so far as it is regarded as existing, nothing more is posited than in the goodness that lies in his possibility. The difference consists only in this, that in the first case the |\nthing itself, together with what is contained in it, is posited, whereas in the second case the *respectus* (relations) of this manifold to one another are posited.\nThe most essential difficulty is probably this: that our usage of language, in so far as it seems to express our most exact idea, always designates existence as a predicate, which has the look of arising from the immediate constitution of our concept. I say, by way of an existing thing: existence belongs to a thing [ich] etc. I consider the existence of things separately and distinguish it from possibility. This character of our judgments well deserves to be weighed. It is not philosophizing merely to seek to show that something is a delusion, a deception of the understanding; one must also learn to see how such a deception is possible. This illusion is itself a [warhaf] real appearance (Erscheinung) in the nature of our mind (Geist), and I must either be able to explain it, or I have cause to fear that my judgment, which [this] declares one or another opinion to be a delusion, may itself be such an illusion.\nP. IV:\nI now ask: if existence is no predicate of things, how can I make use of the expression 'existence' at all? For this distinguishes something in the thing from the thing itself, which can be attributed to it. For example, [This] to a certain thing actuality belongs. Since one and the same thing can be posited in different ways, this positing itself seems to be a mark of the difference, which one could attribute to a thing as a predicate or separate from it. But it is certain that this difference depends merely on how this thing is posited with everything that belongs to it, and not on what is posited in it. According to the difference in the way the thing is posited, something different is also posited thereby: namely, through its predicates in relation to the subject, mere relations and |\nthe possibility, [through the] through the thing, in so far as it is posited absolutely, the thing itself. [But since that through which we] We do not, therefore, distinguish the possible from the actual by a predicate which belongs more to the one than to the other.\nThe concept of an existing thing can never be changed into a judgment of which the thing would be the subject and existence the predicate.\nRather, something existing must be the subject, and everything that belongs to it must be its predicates.",[]],"reflm:3707":["S. I:\nPreparation\nOn the certainty and uncertainty of knowledge in general.\nUncertainty is either an uncertainty of the things [Sachen] or of insight [Einsicht]. The objects of knowledge are in themselves certainly what they are, yet [so] one may ascribe uncertainty to these things in so far as nothing is settled about the remainder, which one seeks, from that [Din] which one knows of them and which is therefore given. This holds however perfectly one may know these data. Suppose that in a mixture of three different metals (g whose specific gravity is given, besides) the weight of the whole lump and its hydrostatic relation to water are given. One may know these data as one will, [so] but it always remains uncertain in what proportion [unter] they are mixed with one another. The reason is that the items known are in themselves of such a kind that this proportion remains undetermined by them. |\nThis kind of uncertainty, which one might call the objective, must be met with in the knowledge of every mode of knowledge [erkenntnisses] that is limited. He who does not know everything must leave something (g in respect of its constitution) undecided among the items of his knowledge, however he may institute comparisons with this knowledge. [Es kommt nur d]\nNow if one recognises [diese Er] that, from certain data which one knows, certain other items [Best] are undetermined, then [ist] to that extent no error can find a place in our knowledge, and for that reason it is not (g objectively) uncertain. If the apparent diameter of a star is known but its distance unknown, it remains uncertain what the true magnitude of the star is, although [woferne man nur nicht weiter ge] no error can arise from this uncertainty alone. Likewise, if in observation |\nS. II: |\nthe angle is once known to be such that an error of two seconds cannot be noticed, then everything that depends, more or less, on a magnitude of this size is undetermined by the observation. If one recognises this to be so, no error can to that extent take place.\nUncertainty consists in the possibility of erring, that is, of passing a judgment which is false. All the grounds of this possibility are therefore either negative or positive. That is to say, they consist either in the lack of grounds for a (g certain) true [Erkentniße] judgment, or in the presence of positive grounds for judging, which judgments are nevertheless not in conformity with the constitution of the things. The former ground is not in itself sufficient for understanding the possibility of error. For from the fact that I do not know certain things it does not yet follow that I can pass a false judgment. Suppose I recognise that I would have to know these items in order to judge. Then, if I find that I do not know them, I would not be willing to judge and would be safeguarded against all error. (g In the case of) the astronomer who knows of a comet the apparent diameter but not the [entfernung] parallax, it is to that extent objectively uncertain how distant and how large it is. But it is impossible for him to err in this matter so long as |\nhe is conscious that he cannot judge. Nor is this [Ungewishei] ignorance of certain given items, even then, a ground of the possibility of erring in itself, if the man is not at all conscious of this indeterminacy. For so long as there is no ground that induces him to judge [so] in a matter about which he does not know enough, he is safeguarded against all judgment. Hence the common man is safeguarded against many errors. He is unconcerned about most of the things after which the learned strive so eagerly. Even when he hears the questions that are raised, most of them never come into his thoughts. Thus nothing is there to induce him to judge. |\nS. III: |\nWe see, accordingly, that uncertainty in the subjective sense (g still) presupposes a certain ground [ist] for passing judgments. [This is so] when to this there is added uncertainty in the objective sense, of which, however, one is not conscious.\nHence, whatever the impulse to pass a judgment, error is impossible if the consciousness of objective uncertainty is added to it, as may be seen in geometry. The greater the desire to judge, or the habit of judging [is, the greater the possibility of error]. We have cited objective uncertainty only in so far as it is taken together with the inner grounds for judging, in order to make the subjective possibility of erring comprehensible. But even if this desire is the same, the grounds of distinction between the true and the false are nevertheless either concealed and, relatively to the agreements which a false mode of knowledge has with a true one, small, or they are not. (Since we infer from analogy, many agreements can be a ground of errors, e.g. the reason [vernunft] of animals.) In the case where one has many, but not all, grounds of distinction, error is possible only through an ignorance of the indeterminacy of the knowledge derived from these grounds, together with the inclination to judge.\nCertainty must be possible in philosophical knowledge just as well as in mathematical knowledge. As regards the degree required for conviction [überzeugung], it must be sufficient. One cannot say that one certainty is greater |\nthan another; nothing is ever more certain than that which is certain. As to the kind, however, this does not concern certainty but clearness.\n(s All uncertainty is a possibility of erring. To err is to pass a false judgment. The possibility of erring is under either a potential or an actual condition. The first is understood to mean that one could err if one wished to judge. The second means that, from actual grounds, there is also a conditioned possibility of judging in cases where it is nevertheless possible to err. The potential possibility of erring rests on the constitution of the things. That is, from what one knows of them it is not determined what belongs to them or not. The actual condition of uncertainty is the ground for judging, in which a potential uncertainty nevertheless lies. This ground for judging [liegt entweder in der] lies in the [Mang] apparent similarity of the procedure in a false mode of knowledge with that in a true one. Thus, first: where I do not notice that something has been omitted among the marks of the thing, nothing has been omitted. Second: things that are similar in certain respects are [supposed to be] similar in others. Third: what belongs to many belongs to all.\nIn the manner of designating: signs of [einerley] similar things are regarded as one and the same.)\n(s The uncertainty of that for which various causes are possible. Existence of bodies.)\nOn pp. I and II Kant had left a margin of about 4 cm, across the whole width of the page, below the text. On p. I he later filled this free space with additions, and, as the content shows, still within phase γ. The additions show slight differences in hand and ink. They are printed in the probable order of their composition.\n(s Means of mathematics: to take the mean [medium] from many [observations].)\n(s Provisional judgments.)\n(s On philosophical opinions and hypotheses,\non the observation of the grounds why an opinion is uncertain.)\n(s Uncertainty is of two kinds: either one finds in the thing itself no sufficient grounds of assent, or, notwithstanding all one's conviction, one is apprehensive of erring, from the expectation of similar cases, in calculating.)\n(s Uncertainty is either a possibility of erring on the condition that one would judge about that for which no data are given, or a conditioned possibility of judging in that for which one has no data.)\n(s Immediately certain propositions: before one makes a concept distinct, by what means, however, one begins this.)\n(s Immediately certain proposition: many thinkers cannot produce one thought.)\n(s An immediately certain proposition can exist only when the subject cannot be thought without the predicate, not when it merely seems that one cannot deny it.)",[]],"reflm:3708":["Only rational concepts can be explained [erklären]; empirical concepts never. The concept of the latter serves only to designate the subject of synthetic judgments, but never for analysis.",[]],"reflm:3709":["Prolegomena Metaphysicorum [Prolegomena of the Metaphysicians].\nIn our total knowledge some modes of knowledge must lie at the foundation of others. |\nMany concepts have other concepts at their foundation, and many judgments have other judgments. At the foundation of the commonest concept of a clock lie the concepts of time, of motion, and of measurement. Whoever utters the word friendship relies upon the concepts of love, of uprightness, etc., etc. It is the same with judgments. Whoever says that envy is a vice is secretly grounding himself on many hidden judgments: that love of one's neighbour is a duty, that whatever is opposed to a duty is a vice, that envy is a form of ill-will and is opposed to love, etc., etc. The fundamental concepts can be entitled *notiones fundamentales*, and the fundamental judgments *judicia fundamentalia*. Those fundamental concepts which do not in turn presuppose others are entitled *notiones primitivae* (first fundamental concepts), and judgments of this kind *judicia primitiva* (first fundamental judgments). But something can be regarded as a first either absolutely or in relation to something else. Something is a *cognitio absolute primitiva* in so far as no other knowledge at all lies at its foundation; but it is *respective primitiva* in so far as it [einer gewißen vernünftigen Absicht nicht ge] either does not lie within the powers of a subject to know its ground, or is not in keeping with a certain rational purpose. At the foundation of the concept of good and evil [überein] there lies much that philosophers also know clearly; but the common man certainly cannot penetrate so far into his concepts, and for him they are *notiones primitivae*. It is just so in common knowledge with the concepts of space and of time. Some of these fundamental concepts may be *notiones primitivae* in regard to human understanding as a whole, although *in sensu absoluto* they may be only *derivativae*, e.g. being simultaneous, being in succession, etc. These one may entitle *notiones [absolute] primitivas in sensu subjectivo*. Whether they are so also *in sensu objectivo* man cannot determine. In the same way *judicia in sensu subjectivo prima* are such in regard to some men, e.g. proverbs, or in regard to all, e.g. the *principium contradictionis*.\nIf we now entitle all modes of knowledge that lie at the foundation of others *principia*, then there are *respectus* in regard to — [the text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3710":["All (g first) principles are either formal or material. The former contain the ground of how the concepts in a judgment are to be considered in relation. The latter contain the *medius terminus* by means of which they are to be considered together in this relation.\n1. The manner in which judgments are to be compared is either by affirmation or by negation. An affirmative judgment represents that a predicate is identical with the subject (is its mark). A negative judgment represents that the predicate contradicts the subject (is the opposite of its mark). The universal formal principle of the former can therefore be only this, which indicates the essence of affirmation: *cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum* [to every subject there belongs a predicate identical with it]; and that of the second the one which contains the essence of a negation: [cuilibet subjecto] *nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum* [to no subject does there belong a predicate opposed to it]. The first is the principle of identity, the second that of contradiction. From these principles alone nothing at all can be proved. For to prove is to judge mediately, or to infer. Every inference of reason, however, is a judgment by means of an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia*, *medius terminus*). But the propositions named contain no *medius terminus* for other propositions. All propositions that stand immediately under these two are unprovable; all that stand mediately under them are provable.\n2. Universal judgments which contain the *medius terminus* for others are the material grounds of these; and, when they stand immediately under the two highest *principia formalia*, they are material first principles. E.g. 'A body is composite' stands immediately under the principle of identity and contains the *medius terminus* for the proposition 'A body is changeable'; e.g. 'Everything composite is changeable, a body is composite, therefore it is,' etc., etc. On the other hand the proposition 'No space is without places' stands immediately under the principle of contradiction, etc.:",[]],"reflm:3711":["*Nihil negativum* is quite and altogether nothing. non-A is also nothing and in itself not thinkable, whereas everything else can be thought. Now were the *nihil negativum* merely the nothing of a certain concept, it would be A–A\n=0, and so the same as a privation; hence the *nihil negativum* is P–P = 0×P.",[]],"reflm:3712":["The mark of the absolutely necessary being cannot be the contingent in the existence of other things, for then this necessity would be only *hypothetica antecedentis*. It is therefore not to be found in this, that it is regarded as the last ground of everything that is, but in this, that it is a ground of everything whatsoever, of what is as well as of what is possible. For since possibility in general is certainly necessary, that which contains the ground [of it] is then also necessary.",[]],"reflm:3713":["On and\nThe determination of a being (s of anything), in so far as it can be considered *what* is posited, is *qualitas* [quality].\nThe determination of a being (s of anything), in so far as it can be considered *how many times* something is posited, is *quantitas* [quantity].\nA one in which there is quantity is a *quantum*.\nThe positing of the same thing made several times is *multitudo* [multitude].\n(s absolute unity is not a *quantum*.)\nIn every *quantum* there is multitude, and that in which there is multitude is a *quantum*.\nTo cognise a multitude distinctly by successively adding one to one is *numerare* [to number].\nA multitude distinctly cognised by numbering is *numerus* [number].\nA *quantum* in which the number of times the one is posited is in itself indeterminate (s which comes about in that which is nothing but the mere possibility of coordination) is a *continuum*.\nA *quantum* in which the number of times the one is posited is in itself determinate is a *discretum*.",[]],"reflm:3714":["Every *caussatum* is in itself contingent; the question is whether every absolutely contingent thing is a *caussatum* of another. The answer is this: contingency can be understood only through the fact that the existence of a thing, without its being a *caussatum*, is undetermined.",[]],"reflm:3715":["*Arbitrium* is either *liberum* or *brutum*. In the former one is conscious of the internal and external *contingentia* of the action.",[]],"reflm:3716":["P. I:\nA rational knowledge which has no other principles than empirical concepts can only be a critique. The universal (e.g. the touching) can be understood only from the particular, and the universal rules can only be abstracted from the particular rules of practice. The doctrine of the probable stands in the same case.\nTruth has objective marks. But the certainty that these objective marks are to be met with in each case can be only subjective, that is, the agreement of knowledge with itself.\nA universal concept is empirical if it can be understood only [as] one knows its *concretum*. E.g. no concept of water can be obtained through any number of definitions, [nor] unless one has seen it. Above all, all concepts of beauty, of simplicity, of the ridiculous are comprehensible only in their *concreto*. Hence here too the rules cannot give rise to the practice; it is the practice that makes the rules.\n––––––––––\nIn judging of the moral the question is not whether it is agreeable but whether it is good. We do indeed recognise therein a con|nection with happiness, as regards possibility, but it does not rest upon it. The principles of moral judgment are not to be grounds of the inclination to the good, [i.e.] for these belong to feeling. The most vicious man, however small the moral inclination to the good may be in comparison, nevertheless holds it to be the best. The principles of this judgment are to be established.\n––––––––––\nAll sciences and arts relate either to the culture of sound reason or not. In the latter case there is also a complete lack of taste. The age of sound reason and of the arts and sciences is only the age of honour; this is found only in republics and monarchies, hence not under the feudal constitution, where far too great an inequality prevails.\n(g The principle of identity and of contradiction are for reason what space and time are for the senses. Universally: the principle of agreement (whether of sameness or of difference). The principle of highest reality is parallel to the sum total of all sensations.)\nPerfection is what constitutes the worth of the person; happiness, what constitutes the worth of the condition [Zustand].\n(The principle of sufficient reason is grounded on this, that everything can be cancelled in and for itself; hence, in order to posit something, something else must be posited with which this cancellation conflicts. This connection, however, is either according to laws of reason or according to laws of the senses.)\nThis principle, however, means only that nothing can be known through reason without a ground.\n––––––––––\n(g If we wish to posit something through reason, we need a ground. That is, positing *a priori* is through a ground, unless it be intuition.)\nIn the proposition 'everything that is has a ground' there lie two things: 1. something else; 2. that whereby the consequence is determined. The former is the *dependens*, the latter the *contingens*.\nP. II:\nAll ideas of metaphysics are analytic, except those of space, time and force.\nAnalysis rests on the principles of identity and of contradiction. But all these and other principles are grounded on this, that the understanding posits nothing absolutely, but only so far as it is constrained thereto by a condition, either analytic or synthetic.\nThe idea of the possible. The idea of all, or of a whole.\nMetaphysics is not a philosophy concerning objects, for these can be given only through the senses, but concerning the subject, namely its laws of reason.\nWe have laws for the use of our reason *a posteriori*; these cannot be applied to concepts, but only to objects of experience.\nMetaphysics therefore treats only the subject dogmatically, but the object, in respect of synthetic judgments, problematically.\nAll judgments are either logical, which enunciate the relative [*respective*] being, or real, which enunciate the absolute. All being in [sich] itself (position) is an existence either relative (g *hypothetice*) or *categorice*.\nUniversality is understood either synthetically, and then holds only of the cases of experience in which the synthesis is made; or analytically, and then holds according to reason of concepts.\nTo the determination of the boundaries of reason there belongs something positive, namely to show, in the first place, the extent of the modes of rational knowledge; and something negative, namely the limits [Schranken]; and finally also the quality of the boundaries, as it were their figure.\nModes of knowledge are of two kinds: either those which relate to objects that are given, or those which relate to the concepts of the form in which reason considers every object. The latter are merely subjective, and they alone can have a universality of reason.\nIn synthetic judgments I represent an identity for the sake of the predicate, but not the predicate for the sake of the identity.\nThe fundamental concepts of analysis are: possibility, impossibility, necessity, contingency, unity, etc., etc.; those of synthesis: space, time and force.\nNot all *conceptus superiores* (higher concepts) are *abstracti*, but only those which arise analytically *a posteriori*; those *a priori* are not *abstracti*. These *abstracti* are empirical or rational.",[]],"reflm:3717":["27:\nP. I:\nThe principle of the form of all experiences is space and time.\nThe principle of the form of all judgments of pure reason: identity and contradiction.\nThe principle of the form of all judgments of reason *a posteriori*: ground and force.\nComparison, derivation, taking together.\nThe possible is contradistinguished: 1. from the [Wiederst], whose concept contains a conflict; 2. from the determined or true; 3. from the actual.\nIn all empirical judgments the fundamental formula is: [everything] in every thing that which contains the ground of one sensation is also the same subject that contains the ground of the other. The synthesis is one either of taking together or of derivation.\nAll necessity and contingency that we can represent to ourselves is conditioned. The unconditioned is thought only problematically. Neither the absolutely contingent (e.g. a free action) nor the absolutely necessary can be thought.\nEvery relation is either of connection, or of comparison, or of taking together.\nAll connection (in the real sense): space, time and force.\nAll definitions are grounded on the concept of the essence; hence we can define nothing but pure concepts of reason and arbitrary concepts.\nAll abstract modes of knowledge have their first grounds *a posteriori*.\nThe universal judgment which has not arisen through abstraction *a posteriori*, and hence empirically, is not abstract but a pure judgment of reason.\nIn metaphysics the grounds of knowledge must be taken *a priori*, although the objects are given *a posteriori*.\nIf the grounds [von dem] concerning that which must be are taken from that |\nwhich happens, they are *a posteriori*. The synthesis is twofold: of the coordinated and of the subordinated.\nThe synthesis of reason or of experience.\nP. II:\nBesides the principle of sufficient reason this also holds: all analysis [is posits a synt] conversely includes the possibility of a synthesis. Accordingly, in every series of subordinated things there must be a first, since otherwise no complete synthesis, and hence also no analysis, would take place.\nThe synthetic principle of the understanding is — [the text] breaks off.\nMathematical analysis is always a philosophical synthesis, in that I think the whole before the parts; but if I think the parts before the whole, it is a mathematical synthesis [in der]. All synthesis rests on coordination and takes place through understanding. Philosophical analysis (*analysis philosophica*), on the other hand, rests on subordination and takes place through reason. The ground is no part of the consequence, nor conversely.\nIn all images of things I coordinate nothing but immediate marks and partial representations.\nab represents the act of coordination, ac that of subordination.\nThe soul has three dimensions: sense and its clearness; understanding and its form as well as matter (i.e. extensiveness) of coordination; and reason, that\nis, height of subordination.\nWhether it may not be the same with the relations to feeling?\nSince all negations serve only to prevent errors and to demonstrate non-knowledge, metaphysics is a very useful science, not in so far as it extends knowledge, but in that it prevents errors. One learns what Socrates knew.\nTaste is directed to appearances and not to sensation, consequently to the constitution of the object in so far as it is an object of the senses; and it is therefore, as a mode of knowledge, subject to universally valid rules.\nThe sensible is a perfection, and confusion is not essential to it; distinctness of coordination can take place.\n–––––––––––\nMathematics shows the greatest dignity of human reason, but metaphysics shows its limits and its proper determination [Bestimmung].\nIt belongs altogether to everything beautiful that I need for it only understanding, and as little reason as possible; for it is to please in appearance. Hence coordination.\n–––––––––––\nRemark: that the rules of the beautiful are given through experience, because one cannot think a beautiful shape of a rational being without a human figure.",[]],"reflm:3718":["After »dunckele« (l. 32) Kant inserts:\nindeed clear, but still in part confused.",[]],"reflm:3719":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nBeside »ebenso nothwendig — doch ich muß« (l. 15-18):\nIf the real consequence were contained in the real ground and were posited through it according to the rule of identity, it would always be simultaneous with it. All alterations are therefore possible only through the real relation of grounds to their consequences, and the logical grounds are therefore distinguished from the real ground not through the limits of my knowledge but in themselves.",[]],"reflm:3720":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nAfter »widersprechen« (l. 38) Kant inserts:\nbut may well conflict [widerstreiten]: namely, that the one cancels the consequence of the other. Contradiction is the putting together of two contradictorily, i.e. logically, opposed predicates in one subject, and its result is *nihil negativum* (negative nothing): a and not-a at once. Real conflict [Widerstreit] is the combination of two real grounds in one subject, of which the one cancels the consequence of the other, and its result is =0, which is not a *nihil negativum*.",[]],"reflm:3721":["ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?)\nBeside l. 25-29 (»widerstreiten — princip.«) Kant has made a bracket ({) and has added to the left of »zwar — entweder« (l. 26-30):\nThat both positions can be removed does not yet constitute any [contradiction] conflict, and a contradiction is not present here at all; rather, conflict is possible only because, besides the consequence of a=x and the consequence of b=y, a third, namely 0, is possible when both are taken together.",[]],"reflm:3722":["Not-possible =0P. Impossible –P. In the former I need only not think anything, as with *blitri* [sic; a nonsense word]; in the latter I must cancel the possibility. For this an actual thought is required. But if it were only the cancelling of a possibility, it would again be 0.",[]],"reflm:3723":["One must distinguish between the possible, the not-possible and the impossible. If A is thought, it is possible; if nothing besides non-A is thought (which happens in the signification of an empty term), it is not-possible; if possibility is cancelled, it is impossible. Impossibility is negative possibility.",[]],"reflm:3724":["On and \nPossibility is respective positing (*positio respectiva*) in conformity with the principle of contradiction. Truth (affirmative) is respective positing according to the principle of identity. Existence is absolute positing (*positio absoluta*). From respective positing |\nto absolute positing the inference does not hold. But from respective positing taken generally to absolute positing the inference does hold, since, unless something were absolutely posited, no relation at all would be possible.",[]],"reflm:3725":["Absolute necessity is either logical, on account of the principle of contradiction, or real, not on account of the principle of contradiction.\nThe former is the necessity of judgments, or the necessity of the relation of predicate and subject.\nThe latter is the necessity of beings. 1. God is almighty. 2. God is. The latter cannot be known through the contradiction of its opposite (g in se). The opposite of existing is not-being. But not-being alone does not contradict itself. Existence is not a predicate; therefore its opposite is not a predicate opposed to anything.",[]],"reflm:3726":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum«:\nThere is no simple accident, because there is no simple action, but it is always a *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:3727":["On the heading »Simplex et compositum« and \nQuantity is either that of a composite or such intensively, i.e. degree. A greater degree is not the whole of the lesser ones, but a ground (*ratio*), i.e. a double degree, which can be the cause of two equal degrees; but in order that grounds of an effect which equals the effects of two substances taken together may reside in the same |\nsubject, there is required 1. a ground for each effect, 2. a principle of uniting these grounds. E.g. an understanding that understands twice as much.\nWhatever is composite, since before composition whatever is given is simple, necessarily consists of simples; but not on that account is every *quantum* composite and a whole.",[]],"reflm:3728":["We can think through reason (g total) absolute contingency just as little as absolute necessity; rather, experience shows it in that which happens.",[]],"reflm:3729":["There is among the substances of the universe a commerce (*commercium*) through established harmony; this is either harmony internally established (physical influx, *influxus physicus*) or externally established; the latter is either harmony externally pre-established (*harmonia praestabilita*) or externally established occasionally.",[]],"reflm:3730":["If each of the existing substances were necessary, no connection would obtain among them, [si] i.e. if there were plainly no God, the variety of all substances would not constitute a world. If of some substances one God, of others another, were the common creator, several worlds [would be] possible, not a single one. If there is one author of all, all that is caused by him necessarily constitutes a single whole.\nThe system by which, because several substances exist, their relation and commerce seem necessarily to follow, is physical influx.\nThe system by which, because all depend on one, commerce and universal harmony are necessary, is pre-established harmony. [In this all influx]\nThe system by which, because all depend on one, commerce is [not yet necessar] merely possible and is in itself contingent, so that every mutual dependence is established arbitrarily, is occasionalism.\nHere, however, it is to be noted that Leibniz's pre-established harmony is a system that feigns for itself the contingency of the commerce of substances, even with one author posited, and a correspondence of the one with the other instituted arbitrarily. In true harmony, however, all harmonic acts occur according to a rule; in pre-established harmony and in occasionalism [su] however [are] the divine act inducing harmony is nothing but singular, and the difference between these consists only in time.",[]],"reflm:3731":["The question why something is presupposes the thought that it is possible that something also not be. This thought, however, [is grounded] |\ngoes [either] either to the existence of substances or to their relations. As regards the latter, that kind of composition which could not [itself] be understood from the necessary existence of the substances is a proof of the contingent [existence] existence [of the same] of this composition. As regards the existence of substance itself, however, the question why it is rests on the inner possibility of thinking its non-being.\nThere are only three kinds of concepts of things: first, an absolute one, from the inner determinations; second, a respective one, from the relation to possibility; third, a relative one, from the relation to the actuality of other things. From the first concept no absolute necessity can be proved, for every thing with all its inner predicates can be cancelled without contradiction. From the third also none, because this is only a *necessitas hypothetica conseqventiae* (hypothetical necessity of the consequence). So there remains the second: that possibility itself stands in such a relation to actuality that it includes it.\nThe absolute necessity of a thing must be capable of being known without any determinate condition. [This condition is]\nHow do we know absolute possibility? Data for it must always be given to us.\nWhen I say: a certain predicate is impossible for a thing, I say: it [wiede] does not belong to it, i.e. it contradicts it, though not what I think in it, but what belongs to it. (It is a question whether I can say: it contradicts the remainder of a thing, apart from its opposite.) What is impossible, therefore, contradicts something; what is absolutely impossible contradicts everything, that is, the contradiction rests on no specially determinate condition.",[]],"reflm:3732":["A concept of whose possibility nothing is established, once there is removed the condition under which alone one may judge its possibility, is pro|blematic.\nThus the absolute necessity of a being, the simple element of a body, an action determined by no ground.",[]],"reflm:3733":["(g The thing) which contains the ground of the inner possibility of things is absolutely necessary. Conversely, (g the thing) which is absolutely necessary contains the ground of the (g inner) possibility of things; (g for) even if things were inwardly necessary without it, there would be no impossibility at all in its not being. (g Or: since the opposite of a thing [ein Ding] cannot contradict itself, it must conflict with some other.)\nWhat contains the ground of one inner possibility contains the ground of all. For let us suppose that something possible still remained over. This would not agree with some possible; that is, it would contradict something possible; but what contradicts any possible is impossible. (g The necessary being therefore contains the ground of all possibility.)\nIn all possibility the formal and the material are distinguished. The ground of the first is the *principium contradictionis*; of the latter, some existence which contains the ground of reality (*realitas*).\nAccordingly the most real being contains the ground of all possibility (s not a particular ground of the inner possibility of things and another not) and is absolutely necessary.\nThe necessary being is all-sufficient (s the most perfect *transscendentaliter*); that is, the most real as a ground.\nThe necessary being is unique. For if we posit several, each of them could be removed while all the others remained. But since, when something necessary is removed, |\nthe impossible arises, it would be possible for something possible to be impossible. Moreover, each of them would be a consequence of the others: *regressus in infinitum curvilineus*.\nThe necessary being is simple. For if a whole is necessary, its parts must be so too; but then there would be many necessary beings.\nThe world, therefore, is not a necessary being.\nThe necessary being is the highest good. For among all possibles, *bonitas* is a reality without which all existence is equal in worth to nothing. Hence it contains the ground of all good. The good, however, consists in the relation of things (g to beings) which have modes of knowledge and feeling, [den] and to a being which on that account contains the ground of things.\nThe necessary being has the most perfect understanding and will.\nThe necessary being is therefore a person, which contains the ground of all existence through understanding and will. That is, it is a God.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Erkenntnisse' = 'modes of knowledge'. 'Verstand' = 'understanding'.\n- First paragraph, last (g) sentence: Kant's 'ein Ding' is bracketed in the text; I read 'the opposite of a thing' and supplied 'Or:' to show it is an alternative ground.\n- 'Dem Werthe nach dem nichts gleich ist' smoothed to 'equal in worth to nothing'.\n- Stray '[den]' left untranslated; the text is probably corrupt there.\n- Sentence on uniqueness broken by | and carried over where it stands.",[]],"reflm:3734":["*Vnicitas* (uniqueness) follows either from supreme reality or from absolute necessity.\nEither necessary existence is inferred from the concept of the most real, or the greatest reality from the absolute necessity of existing.\n[Maxima] Each principle is drawn either from experience or from mere concepts. The former obtains if 1. from the great perfection of the author of the world, and then absolute necessity, is inferred, 2. if it is inferred — breaks off?\n. . . . . .\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . infers\nEither from contingent existence to the necessary, or from perfect existence to the most perfect.\n===NOTES===\n- 'bricht ab?' is the editor's remark; rendered 'breaks off?' and kept in the text.\n- The Latin of the second sentence of the 'prius obtinet' clause is incomplete; I translated only what is there.\n- 'schließt' stands alone after the dots (probably the start of a German sentence); rendered 'infers'.",[]],"reflm:3735":["logical necessity,\nreal necessity.\n(s Impossibility.\nin the logical sense,\nin the real sense.)\n===NOTES===\n- A bare list of headings; 'im logischen Verstande' rendered 'in the logical sense' (not 'understanding').",[]],"reflm:3736":["On the column title of M 333: »Exsistentia Dei«:\nPrimarily the question presents itself for inquiry: what is the ground of absolutely necessary existence, or in what way can the opposite of some existence be absolutely necessary.\n1. The existence of contingent things, as caused, does not constitute the principle of absolute necessity; rather this is the ultimate ground of the possibility of the existence of many contingent things.\n2. No other existence at all contains the ground of absolute necessity; for of contingent things this has already been said, and the question is now about the condition of necessary existence.\n3. Therefore the proper mark of necessary existence is to be found in its relation to possibility, which likewise is necessary.\n4. Impossibility consists in contradiction; for it [erit] is properly present if you [nege] posit what was to be denied, or vice versa. E.g. it is impossible that a triangle be [parallelo] quadrilateral. Which also runs thus: a triangle is not quadrilateral, which expresses a contradiction. For let A be not B; then A, which is B, would be B not B, which is impossible and contradictory. |\n5. The opposite of no existence contradicts itself. Contradiction is between subject and predicate; existence is not a predicate; therefore non-existence contradicts no subject in itself.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Zudem Columnentitel' is the editor's locus note, rendered 'On the column title'.\n- Deleted/inserted words ([erit], [nege], [parallelo]) kept in brackets as in the Academy text.\n- Item 5 as written: 'oppositum nullius existentiae sibi contradicit'. The sense seems to require 'does not contradict itself'; I translated it as written and flag a possible slip (or a missing 'non').",[]],"reflm:3737":["The question is whether God's power is greater than his decree (*Ratschluss*), and, since the former extends to all things [possible in nexu], whether the latter does not extend to them also; in that case everything possible is actual. But if it seems that much that is possible is not actual, we represent to ourselves as possible that whose conditions are nevertheless impossible.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Ratschlus' (sic) = 'decree'; 'conditionen' rendered 'conditions'.\n- The bracketed phrase '[in nexu mögliche]' is an insertion in the Academy text; kept in brackets, with the Latin 'in nexu' (in connection) left in italics-free form.",[]],"reflm:3738":["All analytic judgments teach what is in the concepts, but thought confusedly; the synthetic, what is to be thought as combined with the concept. In all judgments the concept of the subject is something (g a) that I think in the object x, and the predicate is regarded as a mark of a in the [synthetic] analytic judgments, or of x in the synthetic.\n|–a—|—x——|\nAll analytic judgments are rational and conversely. All synthetic judgments are empirical and conversely. The first rational principles [or] of matter [or prior ones of form] are [iud] elementary principles; synthetic principles, if they were at the same time rational, would be called *axiomata*; but since there are none such, the analogues of the rational in mathematics are so called. In philosophy there are no synthetic principles except *a posteriori*, i.e. empirically, and no analytic principles except *a priori*, i.e. elementary propositions, both material.\nI can compare notions by thinking their relation either according to the rule of the understanding (*intellectus*): empirically (g and synthetically), or of reason: rationally and analytically, or according to the rules of the analogue of reason, i.e. |\nof imagination and wit (*ingenium*). To this Crusius ascribes many of his principles.\nLocke seems to have caught a scent of the difference between synthetic and analytic judgments in his inquiry into man.\n===NOTES===\n- The diagram line is carried over unchanged.\n- 'Principia rationalia prima [vel] materiae [vel formae priora]' is obscure and the brackets leave it doubtful; translated literally. '[iud]' is an abandoned start of 'iudicia', left as is.\n- 'Disqvisitione hominis' rendered 'inquiry into man' (presumably the Essay concerning Human Understanding).",[]],"reflm:3739":["If I deny something of a subject, this happens in accordance with the principle of contradiction. This denial together with the affirmation is impossible.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Satz des Wiederspruchs' = 'principle of contradiction'. The second sentence is elliptical; 'together with the affirmation' = of the same predicate.",[]],"reflm:3740":["Something [is] the opposite of nothing (*nihili*)\n{\nnegative, privative, or negative.\n===NOTES===\n- Fragmentary: 'Aliqvid oppositum nihili' has no verb, and the brace governs a list ending with a repeated 'negativo'; 'sive' suggests a variant reading. Doubtful; the 'is' is supplied in brackets.",[]],"reflm:3741":["Material principles of judgments are notions\nformal\n*principium identitatis*\nor of contradiction\nMaterial principles of syllogisms are notions\nformal\n*dictum de omni et nullo*\n(s The logical principle of judgments. The real principle of concepts.)\n===NOTES===\n- A schematic note; the layout is kept as in the source. 'Formal' and 'or of contradiction' are left elliptical.",[]],"reflm:3742":["(g There are material, and there are also formal, fundamental concepts.)\n*Principia analytica, synthetica.*\n===NOTES===\n- 'Grundbegriffe' = 'fundamental concepts'.",[]],"reflm:3743":["All mathematical concepts are synthetic.\n===NOTES===\n- None beyond 'Begriff' = 'concept'.",[]],"reflm:3744":["There are synthetic propositions from experience, hence *principia prima synthetica*; of this kind also are (g the definitions of mathematics) the *axiomata* of mathematics concerning space.\n*Principia rationalia* cannot be synthetic at all.\nAll empirical propositions are synthetic and conversely.\nAll rational propositions are analytic.\n===NOTES===\n- 'sind' for 'sein' in the second sentence (apparently a slip) was smoothed to 'be'.\n- 'Sätze' = 'propositions'.",[]],"reflm:3745":["From the principle of identity and of contradiction there follow not merely propositions, but necessary ones.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Satz der identitaet und des Wiederspruch' (sic) rendered as the principles of identity and of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3746":["The *principia formalia* are only the first grounds of analytic or rational judgments.\n===NOTES===\n- None.",[]],"reflm:3747":["All principles of human knowledge are either formal or material. The former contain merely the relation of concepts in judgments: either the logical or the metaphysical. The latter [contain the relation] of things, and are synthetic. (s Either [they contain the relation] of the analytic, and are entitled logical, and hold for every understanding, and are objective (only not conversely); or of the synthetic, and are entitled real, and, because they are asserted universally without the senses, they are either principles of the form of the understanding or of sensibility [and hold in both ca—]; in the first case they are merely subjective laws. In the second they are objective only under the hypothesis of sensibility; but if the hypothesis already lies in the subject, they are objective.)\n===NOTES===\n- 'Diese der Dinge' is elliptical; 'contain the relation' supplied in brackets. The (s) passage is restated with the same supplement.\n- '[und gelten in beyden fä]' is an abandoned fragment (probably 'Fällen', cases); rendered '[and hold in both ca—]'.\n- 'Heissen' = 'are entitled'. 'Ohne Sinne' rendered 'without the senses'.\n- The last sentence is doubtful as it stands (the final 'objective' may be a slip for 'subjective'); translated as written.",[]],"reflm:3748":["Metaphysical fundamental concepts. Elementary concepts. Systematic fundamental concepts.\n===NOTES===\n- A list of headings only.",[]],"reflm:3749":["One can combine concepts with one another so as to make from them a larger concept (synthetic); or one can think concepts as combined with one another, in order to know what is in them.\nThe concepts of causes are synthetic and therefore empirical.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Zu erkennen' rendered 'to know'. 'Begriffe der Ursachen' = 'concepts of causes'; Kant's wording kept.",[]],"reflm:3750":["All *principia primitiva* are either elementary propositions and ana|lytic\nor *axiomata* and are synthetic. Difference of an analytic and a synthetic proposition in general. The rational are analytic, the empirical synthetic, likewise the mathematical.\n===NOTES===\n- The | falls within the word 'analytisch' and is kept there.\n- 'Imgleichen mathematische' is elliptical: presumably 'the mathematical [are] synthetic'; I have kept the ellipsis.",[]],"reflm:3751":["Not to posit and to remove (*tollere*) are different.\nOf predicates contradictorily opposed, to posit both at once is impossible by the *principium contradictionis*.\nOf predicates contradictorily opposed, to remove both is impossible by the *principium exclusi medii*.\nOf predicates contradictorily opposed, to think neither of something is possible: a rule of abstract notions, which are indeterminate. That this latter is possible is clear from this, that, if they are not material, there is no contradiction.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Aufheben' rendered 'remove', matching the Latin *tollere*.\n- 'Qvod posterius sit possibile' taken to refer to the third case (thinking neither); 'if they are not material' is obscure (the antecedent of 'they' is uncertain) and is left as written.",[]],"reflm:3752":["[respectus]\nWhoever thinks a ground thinks also the consequence, and conversely: it is a concept of a relation (*respectus*). Since no simpler *respectus* can be thought, the concept of it too is inexplicable. Yet one can by a judgment prevent ground and consequences from being regarded as wholly one and the same, so that it runs: *posita ratione ponitur aliud*; or: *ratio et rationatum sunt diversa*.\n===NOTES===\n- The first line '[respectus]' is a bracketed heading in the Academy text and is kept.\n- 'Folgen' (plural, after 'Grund') kept plural; 'Begriff' = 'concept'.",[]],"reflm:3753":["*Ratio* is either of positing or of removing.\n(g *Respectus* is either logical: of concepts (of identity),\nor real: of things (really diverse).)\nThe *respectus* of a positing *ratio* is *nexus*, of a removing one is *oppositio*.\nThe *respectus* of a logical *ratio*, whether positing or removing, is analytic — rational;\nthe *respectus* of a real *ratio*, whether positing or removing, is synthetic — empirical.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Realis' and 'realiter diversi' kept as Kant's Latin parallel to 'real'. The brace/parenthesis marks are carried over exactly.",[]],"reflm:3754":["A relation (*respectus*) is either of connection (*nexus*) or of opposition, and each is either logical or real.\nThe former is on account of identity [post] or contradiction, the latter not through these.\n(s The logical ground (*ratio*) and the logical consequent (*rationatum*) are always simultaneous, real ones one after another.)\n(In logical opposition, when one is posited the other is removed.)\n(In real opposition, when one is posited the consequent of the other is removed.)\n(s In the former the connection is knowable analytically, in the latter synthetically. The former rationally, the latter empirically.)\nIn the former one or the other is a negation;\nin the latter both are positive.\nIn the former (s with both posited) the consequent is a negative nothing (*nihil negativum*) (s or the impossible);\nin the latter (s with both posited) the consequent is a privative nothing (*nihil privativum*) = 0.",[]],"reflm:3755":["The relation of the logical ground to the consequent is a judgment.\nThe relation of the real ground is a concept.\nVarious [Grundb] primitive concepts of real grounds.\nThe possibility of a real connection in primitive grounds cannot be understood rationally.",[]],"reflm:3756":["The logical [aspect] of possibility is contradistinguished from truth, the real [aspect] of possibility from actuality.\nThe relation of identity and contradiction, thought negatively, i.e. where one concept is not identical with another and yet does not conflict with it, is the logical or formal [aspect] of mere possibility. The real [aspect] of possibility is at the same time its material [aspect], and our concept of it extends as far as the simple in our sensations, and likewise as far as the primitive relations (*respectus reales*) which we come to know through experience.\nThe logical [aspect] of possibility is a relation of comparison, the logical [aspect] of truth a relation of connection.\nKnowledge of the logical relation is rational, [zweyte] the real is empirical.\nGround and consequent are always distinct, but either (g only) logically, where the consequent is only a partial concept of the ground (and consequently, where it is wholly the same as the ground, it is also no consequent), or really, where it is not thought as belonging to the ground as a partial concept but as wholly different from it.\nAll connection of the logical ground with the consequent is an affirmative judgment, in which the predicate is the consequent and the subject the ground. In real connection this is never so; although the consequent can be a predicate of the cause, yet none is a predicate of the ground in it. Logical connection and conflict can be known analytically, and so rationally; the real can be known only empirically. Since everything empirical teaches only what is customarily wont to be with or after one another (except in the properties of space), the question arises whether we |\ndo not, in this counter-relation, distinguish the ground from the consequent by means of time.",[]],"reflm:3757":["That whose position is necessarily (according to universal laws) [etwa] connected with the position of something else is entitled its ground.",[]],"reflm:3758":["The possible, so far as it is a consequent (*rationatum*), is connected a priori; so far as it is a ground (*ratio*), it is connected a posteriori. What is neither ground nor consequent is plainly unconnected. The series of all possibles subordinated to one another has a terminus a priori and a posteriori, and cannot [esse] again be connected, *osse* [sic], with others.",[]],"reflm:3759":["Several connected things make a series. A series of [connexorum] coordinated things can be contained under the idea of all, a series of subordinated things cannot. The little word 'simul' (at once) comprehends all.\nThe first grounds in the series of coordinated parts of a body are simples.",[]],"reflm:3760":["Reality and negation are distinctions between what is posited or negative absolutely, or meta|\nphysically, and that which is called such only logically, in which there is only a relation either of position or of negation.",[]],"reflm:3761":["A universal and merely possible concept is not completely determined (*omnimode determinatum*). But an individual concept is, and can nevertheless be merely possible.\nExistence cannot be a predicate, for otherwise a thing as existing would be known only through a judgment and by means of the understanding. But as it is, we know the existence of things through sensation.",[]],"reflm:3762":["The respective position of the predicates of a being toward one another, in conformity with the principle of contradiction, contains less in itself than the absolute position of the same predicates together; therefore the inference from the former to the latter is not valid.",[]],"reflm:3763":["Fictions can be possible [in sensu absolu]; but if one occupies oneself merely with their possibility, there arises from it an ideal way of thinking.",[]],"reflm:3764":["(s What has quantity is a quantum.)\nQuantity is the repeated position of the same. It is pure when what is posited is not asked about at all, but is something in general, as in arithmetic. It is quantity in concreto when what is posited is determined, i.e. when some quality is given as one.",[]],"reflm:3765":["(s Unity is conditioned or unconditioned.)\nPerhaps by the four sections one meant only to say that the concepts of the one, the true, the perfect can be applied to all things in general.\n'Every thing is not many' signifies transcendental unity.\n'Every thing contains much that is nevertheless one with one*': the metaphysical (g can be greater or lesser unity).\n'Every thing is true,' i.e. it cannot be denied among all possible concepts and agrees with other things: transcendental.\n'Every thing contains truths': metaphysical, the principle of elementary propositions. (g Some things indeed contain possibility, but not enough metaphysical truth, i.e. conditioned possibility.)\n'Every thing is perfect in what it is': transcendental.\n'Every thing has reality': metaphysical; it is comparatively perfect.\n[Das] The absolute unity, truth and perfection consists in the possibility which comprehends sufficiency in itself, possibility in every respect and all reality.\n*(s In the circle there is more unity than in the polygon. The unity of the conic sections.)",[]],"reflm:3766":["Unity is either absolute (g categorical) (s numerical), which is opposed to multitude, or hypothetical, so far as it belongs to several things conjoined\n|\nunity either by coordinating the whole or by subordinating: unity of the ground (*unitas rationis*) and also unity of the consequent (*unitas rationati*) (in machines, in purpose).",[]],"reflm:3767":["All necessity is either logical or real. The former is known rationally, the latter empirically. The opposite of the latter is entitled impossible because it never occurs; the opposite of the former never occurs because it is impossible.",[]],"reflm:3768":["That whose opposite is possible cannot on that account be called contingent, if by the opposite is understood something else, which is non-A. For, besides a substance whose determination is A, there can be another which is determined by non-A; e.g. thought is not contingent to a man because another man can also be without thought; [aut] at the least this contingency is only logical, not real, because in this man it is perhaps necessary. Nor is a substance contingent whose determinations can succeed one another.",[]],"reflm:3769":["Opposite the figure of M § 109:\nThe possibility of the same thing without its existence is not the same as the possibility of its non-being. I can represent to myself comets that are not; but to represent that the sun is not could be hypothetically impossible.",[]],"reflm:3770":["(g Absolute) possibility can be known only from the *nexus* with everything possible. E.g. whether in a triangle the square of one side can be equal to that of the other two cannot be known from a triangle |\nin general, but from its concept as connected with the right angle.",[]],"reflm:3771":["A thing exists necessarily whose opposite, as regards existence, is impossible. Yet the opposite of its determinations is possible. For one says of changeable things that the same things successively have all sorts of determinations. If the opposite of the determinations were at the same time the opposite of the thing, no thing would be changeable. Indeed, even (g in) a changed thing these very changes can be necessary, for the logical opposite of a determination consists in this, that in place of A, in the same time, non-A takes place; but if non-A follows upon A, then this is [potentialiter] *disparatum*, (the disparate), *non oppositum* (not an opposite).\nThe alternation of a thing, or change, is what agrees very well [mit] together and where both necessarily follow upon one another. Only the thing must not cease to be, since then there would also be no succession, and consequently the real opposite.",[]],"reflm:3772":["Reality is (g its concept is) το *esse* (being);\nNegation is\nτο *non esse* (not-being).",[]],"reflm:3773":["Negation is a predicate. A being (*ens*) is the subject. A merely negative being would be something in which there is nothing.",[]],"reflm:3774":["Opposite and \nReality is either relatively positing or relatively removing. Diversity is only from relation.\nThe negation of one of the opposites is the position of the other.",[]],"reflm:3775":["A being (s transcendentally) maximal is the most real (*realissimum*), and in general one in which there is not synthetic or extensive quantity but intensive; for in the former, synthesis is never terminated. (s Every minimum or maximum is such only relatively. E.g. the diameter, the circumference, with respect to the area.) (s Comparative quantity of the universe: *universitas*.)",[]],"reflm:3776":["Opposite and\nConcepts (g absolute) of the maximum and minimum in mathematical (g and ideal) synthesis are deceptive concepts; but in the real synthesis of qualities they are possible and are equated with totality (*omnitudo*); but in repeating the same, no absolute totality is given.",[]],"reflm:3777":["The several things in a being are either to be counted or to be weighed.\nThe minimum of the ground is the moment (*momentum*).",[]],"reflm:3778":["Negative determinations are not accidents.",[]],"reflm:3779":["The empirical concept of substance is identity amid all variations.",[]],"reflm:3780":["Inherence is not an accident.",[]],"reflm:3781":["In any accident there is considered, first, the relation of real dependence on the [subiecto] substance; second, the logical relation as of predicate to subject. If only the former is present, the consequent does not on that account inhere in the ground, as Spinoza wrongly held of the world in relation to God. If only the latter is present, the subject will be only logical, not a substance, and the predicate a determination, not an accident.",[]],"reflm:3782":["All properties that the poets personify are *phaenomena substantiata* (substantiated appearances).",[]],"reflm:3783":["The accidents (*accidentia*) are not particular things inhering in the subject (*subiectum*) but predicates of a subject, i.e. ways in which the subject exists. The concept of inherence is a logical expedient, because in it all things can be represented in the form of predicates.",[]],"reflm:3784":["Whether [s] an accident is something accessory to the substance (g as colour is) and properly inheres in it, or only the positive form of the substance itself and its actual determination? E.g. motion inhering in a body.",[]],"reflm:3785":["Three things are to be thought: substance, accident, and the relation (*respectus*) of each. This relation is either that of the accident, and is inherence; or that of the substance, and this again is either that of the potential ground and is force (*vis*), or that of the actuating ground and is action (*actio*).",[]],"reflm:3786":["Just as in logic subject, predicate and relation are distinguished, and hence the [predicate] relation is not a predicate, so also in real things. Force is not an accident but a relation of the substance, as ground, [relation] toward the accidents.",[]],"reflm:3787":["The relation (*respectus*) of a part and of its complement (*complementum*) to the whole must be [mutual] reciprocal (*mutuus*) and homogeneous (*homogeneus*). Hence a consequence cannot be a part of the ground and belong with the ground, as a part, to the same whole. The thought is not a part of the human being but his consequence.",[]],"reflm:3788":["The mode of composition is not the essence of the composite but of the composition.\nComposition is form. The parts are matter.",[]],"reflm:3789":["The question is whether in a substantial composite (*compositum substantiale*) there is no substance but only substances, and whether only the plural of it is possible. A synthetic whole (*totum syntheticum*) is one whose [The] composition, as regards possibility, is grounded on the parts, which can be thought even without [diese: these] any composition at all. An analytic whole (*totum analyticum*) is one whose parts, as regards their possibility, already presuppose the composition in the whole. Space and time (*spatium et tempus*) are analytic wholes, bodies synthetic. A composite of substances is a synthetic whole. An analytic whole is neither composed of substances nor of accidents, but is a whole of possible relations.",[]],"reflm:3790":["Either space contains the ground of the possibility of the compresence of many substances and of their relations, or these contain the ground of the possibility of space.",[]],"reflm:3791":["Division is either logical, insofar as various things are regarded as contained under some higher notion; or mathematical, insofar as several things are distinguished as parts of the same whole (ideal division); or physical, insofar as one acts upon the parts in such a way that the real nexus by which they constitute the same whole is removed (real division).\n(s A monad (*monas*) is not mathematically divisible, since the several things discernible in it are not parts but *rationata* (things grounded).\nLogical division is distinguished from analysis; the latter is division of a notion [pr] and metaphysical; the former is division of the sphere of a notion, and the less a notion is metaphysically indivisible, the greater the sphere.)",[]],"reflm:3792":["A moment (*momentum*) is an element of the ground (*ratio*). Therefore the moment of free descent on an inclined plane is different. Moments in the deed (*in facto*).",[]],"reflm:3793":["(s The quantity of one as a whole is regarded extensively.\nThe quantity of one as ground is regarded intensively.)\n[Die W] There is more heat in a kettle full of boiling water than in a spoonful of it, but not greater heat. Two asses draw a wagon with more speed, but not with greater speed.\nIf many single things cannot be so combined that they give a greater degree — — breaks off.\nMore virtue, greater virtue; more welfare, greater welfare.",[]],"reflm:3794":["Infinity relates to the absence of an end (*limes*, limit), either in adding, and then it is the greatest, or in |\ntaking away, and then it is not for that reason the greatest. The former is, as it were, like a line to which none can be added; the second like one from which a certain line can never be so taken away that it reaches the end.",[]],"reflm:3795":["It is not yet established whether the greatest reality in a being is compossible simultaneously or in a successive series.",[]],"reflm:3796":["Extended, impenetrable, inert: this is matter (*materia*).",[]],"reflm:3797":["Opposite and \nThe existence of a being which all duration follows is the beginning (*initium*).\nThe existence of a being which follows all duration is the end (*finis*).\nBeginning and end are the limits of duration, the one *a parte ante*, the other *a parte post*. Beginning and end are instants (*instantia*).",[]],"reflm:3798":["Useful is what is mediately agreeable, i.e. is a |\nmeans to happiness; for the pleasures are the elements of happiness, whereas perfection consists of others.",[]],"reflm:3799":["If the concept of the world signified the whole of all possible things, namely those that are possible in connection with the sufficient ground, it would be more fruitful.\nOn cosmology. 1. The *commercium* (community) of substances.",[]],"reflm:3800":["The question is twofold: whether to a given world another can be surrogated, i.e. substituted, or whether another can be added over and above a given world. The former is permissible through contingency (unless it be supposed that the world must have a totality simply as such); the latter is not permissible, because they would be joined in time, either in the same part of it [or] in different parts.\n*(s *alius* (another) is either *per oppositionem* or *per additionem*.)",[]],"reflm:3801":["121. E II 1736.\nM 121':\nThe mathematical law of continuity (*lex continuitatis*) is: space and time are continua.\nThe physical law of continuity is: just as between any points placed outside one another in a simultaneous series a continuous series of points lies between, so also between two successive states a continuous series of states lies between, [just as] also in diversity a continuous series of degrees of diversity. E.g. the direction of a motion is not changed except through the continuous interposition of intervening degrees of direction. So, perhaps, the state of the mind too.\nThe cosmological law of continuity is: between any different species lie all possible intermediate degrees, and there is a continuous series of degrees of diversity.\nThe logical law of continuity.\n(s No succession of two states is immediate; otherwise a moment would be common to them: a contradiction of opposed simultaneous things (*simultaneorum oppositorum contradictio*).)",[]],"reflm:3802":["Cause and caused do not belong to the same whole, because then the cause would suffer externally from itself; therefore the cause of the world is an extramundane being.",[]],"reflm:3803":["Every Spinozist is an egoist. The question is whether every egoist is necessarily a Spinozist.",[]],"reflm:3804":["The absolute perfection of the world does not consist in the multitude, order or variety of substances, but in the greatest [sense] and purest sense of pleasure. Hence the rules of perfection of the so-called physico-mechanical kind do not collide with the pneumatic, since no perfection is present in the former.",[]],"reflm:3805":["Rules of perfection of the world of spirits and of the natural world never stand in collision, for the latter has no absolute perfection.",[]],"reflm:3806":[") (ι1?) M 147'. E II 768.\nThe difference between ideal and real influence (*influxus idealis, realis*) makes no different system, but is merely a correctly determined concept of influence in general. But since all influence is ideal, it can be concluded from this that it is not otherwise possible to think a harmony (*harmonia*) of substances in their self-activity than insofar as they depend on one [being].*\nSpace and time are the first relations that all things thereby acquire, and the first grounds of the possibility of a world-whole.\n(g The theory of ideal influence is not [a ground of] a particular system, but a ground for the abolition of physical influence (*influxus physicus*). Then it must [still the possibility] be shown how a harmony under the condition of real influence is possible. Then the possibility [of an influence] of a pre-established harmony (*harmonia praestabilita*), or rather its necessity, must be shown.)",[]],"reflm:3807":["Rational beings in other worlds (s theurgy). Astral spirits (*spiritus astrales*). Angels. Influence on the spirit of man. Summoning of departed souls or of those still living, hauntings.\nPoltergeists. (s idol. Fetish. Talisman. Amulet. Sympathy.)\nRübezahl. (g goblin.) Incubi, succubi. Delrio.\nChangeling. Vampire. (s Subterranean beings.)\nWater-sprites. Little mountain-men. Ghost-seeing.\nSummoning. Pact. Magic. Obsession. Exorcism. Ventriloquists.",[]],"reflm:3808":["The original being (the [first] independent), highest, supreme If. (Ground.) The being of all beings (of all). The supreme ground of everything.\nThat it is no consequence, hence a first,\nThat it is a (g first) ground,\nThat it is a ground of everything.",[]],"reflm:3809":["The possibility of a thing is twofold: either 1, that what is there be combined in a certain way according to laws which already hold in them by virtue of their own properties. E.g. if wood is there, a house of wood is possible, or also that an effect flow from forces. 2, The possibility where nothing is there; for then there is no matter for anything, and so the concept of no thing can arise either. Thus the concept of possibility is indeed logically prior to actuality, according to given concepts, but really it does not precede everything actual. Thus [that which] is a being which contains the matter for all possibility, and in which possibility is to be distinguished from actuality, is just as absurd as [it would be] if, when one takes space for an object, one could not say that it is possible.",[]],"reflm:3810":["Possibility is seen logically only through [pr] judgments in which there is no combination, and also no conflict.",[]],"reflm:3811":["All negations are limits. Limits in general are possible only through the unlimited. Accordingly the unlimited is that where|by everything else is possible. The possibility of things *ascendendo* comes about through comparison; *descendendo* it is seen through derivation.",[]],"reflm:3812":["In the Wolffian proof one can indeed recognise the independence, but not the (g inner) necessity; for his existence is necessary only for the sake of the world.\nFrom the necessity he infers the *omnitudo realitatum* (the totality of realities). — — If this is correct, [that] he had no need to substruct an experience.\nIt is strange that one should want to prove the existence of an absolutely necessary being from something other than the necessary. For if one understands what absolutely necessary means, and recognises that the concept is something, the proof is accomplished. If one does not know this, [the argument] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3813":["It is to be proved: 1. that an absolutely necessary being is possible; 2. that it is the *realissimum* (the most real being). The absolute necessity of the thing must be derived from concepts [and not from] and not from its relation to something else existing.\nIt does not consist in this, that the opposite contradicts itself.\nNo opposite of existence contradicts itself; only [the opposite] of propositions does.\nIt must therefore be necessary in connection with the possible; not as a consequence, but as a ground.\nPossibility is a respective position. Much can indeed be posited as to its possibility although it is not posited absolutely; but without that, nothing at all exists, [and] there is no possibility.\n(g All simple possibilities contain the material ground of possibility; their possibility does not rest on the principle of contradiction.)",[]],"reflm:3814":["Beside the number of M § 815:\nAll judgments are logical or real. The latter concern existence, and, if they concern absolute necessity, cannot be known through the principle of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3815":["God contains the material ground of all possibility, and in this there lies at the same time, according to the *principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction), the formal ground of the same.\nThat God contains the ground of the essence of things is not a *perfectio operativa* (operative perfection), but a *perfectio qviescens* (quiescent perfection).\nDivine all-sufficiency is either *omnisufficientia interna* or *externa* (internal or external all-sufficiency).",[]],"reflm:3816":["God is represented [first] as the being of highest perfection, as a ground, namely as a ground of all perfections, in the ontological sense, and secondly in the cosmotheological sense.",[]],"reflm:3817":["According to the analogy of empirical concepts [the text] breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3818":["Analogy of one born blind with regard to colours and tones. Knowledge of the properties of God is, with regard to the moral, dogmatic and positive; with regard to the theoretical it is [analo] critical and negative. The chief thing is that one not go astray. Hence the theoretical knowledge which we require is very simple.",[]],"reflm:3819":["Moral properties. The goodness of the Creator, the holiness of the Lawgiver, the justice of the Judge. } }\n}All-sufficiency of duration, of power, of [omniscience according to] knowledge. Eternity, omnipotence, omniscience.",[]],"reflm:3820":["That there is a primordial being [*Urwesen*].\nWhether there is more than one primordial being?",[]],"reflm:3821":["That there can be only one most perfect being is clear.",[]],"reflm:3822":["The supreme cause is the author of the world through freedom.\nThe world is a being from another, but not from many, rather from one; not through nature, but through freedom; therefore the cause of the world is an author and God. But that God is at the same time all-sufficient and unique cannot be inferred from this. [*mundus est ens ab alio, sed non a pluribus, sed ab uno; non per naturam, sed per libertatem; ergo causa mundi est auctor et Deus; qvod Deus autem sit simul omnisufficiens et vnicus, inde concludi non potest.*]",[]],"reflm:3823":["Whether there are *dii comparativi* (comparative gods), whose right with respect to men is absolute and the subjection of men natural. This is denied. For there is not given |\nnatural subjection, except under the will of one who cannot be obligated, therefore not under a creature.",[]],"reflm:3824":["Independence and omnisufficiency are the two *principia cognoscitiva* (principles of knowledge) for determining the properties of God.",[]],"reflm:3825":["*Cognitio prototypa* (archetypal knowledge): the cause of things.\n*Cognitio ectypa* (ectypal knowledge): the thing caused.",[]],"reflm:3826":["God knows everything in knowing himself.\nMan knows himself in knowing other things.",[]],"reflm:3827":["One might ask whether all willing also presupposes a representation of the good in the object, and whether he who wills the highest good could not will still more than is good; the last will. If the ground of willing does not require a *causa movens* (moving cause) in the object, but rests on the nature of the subject, then [the text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3828":["The motive of God is related to ours only analogically; no ground of his action lies in the object; rather, because he |\nregards himself as the cause, and as the most perfect cause, he is the author of the best.",[]],"reflm:3829":["The concepts of substances, in so far as they are abstracted, are only respective: hence bodies are substances in so far as they are the subjects of the inherence of their accidents and inhere in no other known subject; but they would not be entitled substances in so far as they in turn have another subject [seyn], of which they are merely the effects.",[]],"reflm:3830":["That will with which a smaller (g known) good agrees more than a greater one is a worse will. The proof is here conducted *a posteriori* that no better world is possible. In the case [that] of an infinite series of better worlds, no best will could be possible. Indeed, every possible will would be infinitely evil. It is the proof of optimism under the hypothesis that there is a God; more evident than even the proof of the divine existence. Indeed, it is still more needful, or rather one has need of the idea of God only for this. Only the idea of the *maximum* can teach me what I have to expect from God.\nIt is a question whether even a single world only is possible.\n(s One cannot represent to oneself any existence (consistent with reason) which would be equally possible with non-being. This is also to be regarded as a difficulty for the rational concept of freedom.)",[]],"reflm:3831":["*Vtrum possibile sit, creare substantiam spontaneam aut dati gradus spontaneitatis.* [Whether it is possible to create a spontaneous substance, or one of a given degree of spontaneity.]",[]],"reflm:3832":["At the very top of the page:\nOne will sooner say: infinite space is in God, than: God is in infinite space.",[]],"reflm:3833":["*Naturalismus* (naturalism) is either of method or dogmatic; the latter is either of conservation or of origin, and this is atheism. Naturalism of method is either propaedeutic or universal; the former is that of Epicurus and quite rational, the latter is atheism, negatively so called. [*Naturalismus est vel methodi vel naturalismus dogmaticus; posterior est vel conservationis vel ortus, hic autem est atheismus. Naturalismus methodi est vel propaedeuticae vel vniversalis; prior est epicuri et admodum rationalis, posterior est atheismus negative talis.*]",[]],"reflm:3834":["Synthesis is either philosophical or mathematical, and so likewise is analysis; philosophical synthesis is connection through a ground.",[]],"reflm:3835":["Nothing, in respect of concept or of existence.",[]],"reflm:3836":["In the inner margin:\nMatter and form belong to impossibility, but not to the non-possible.",[]],"reflm:3837":["It is just the opposite when an explanation includes a circle, as compared with its involving a *contradictio* (contradiction).\nIn regard to what is necessary we do not ask why it is: why a triangle has three sides; but we do ask why there are three sides.",[]],"reflm:3838":["Contingent is that whose non-being is possible. Being is here taken irrespective of time, whether it be present, past or future. Non-being is the total abolition, and so [abolition] from all time. Changes themselves can be necessary; and if they are contingent, the persistence of the thing is possible.\nThe opposite of a predicate at another time [bedeutet] proves that the subject in and for itself is undetermined in respect of it. It does not prove that it is possible that the determination did not exist in the previous time. Changes themselves, if they are necessary, since none of the successive determinations takes place without the opposite of the previous one being at the same time, prove not so much contingency as limitations, i.e. the nothing which follows the something with equal steps.",[]],"reflm:3839":["That magnitude which can be the ground of another only through diminution or detachment [of itself] is *extensiva* (extensive). That which is a ground without diminution is *intensiva* (intensive).\nThe magnitude of matter and form.\nThe degrees of possibility.",[]],"reflm:3840":["The elapsed infinite which has a beginning is impos|sible.\nIf everything future is a merely mathematical repetition, future infinity is to be regarded as possible. But if the future is determined from the previous or otherwise, it makes up a whole which is thoroughly determined through some ground, and its infinity is no more comprehensible than that of past and present time.",[]],"reflm:3841":["There is no necessary completeness of possible coordination, hence also no (g necessary) limits of a whole in which the quality of the units is undetermined. (g All limits are conditions of a whole of contingent magnitude.) Hence every whole is determined only contingently in respect of limits, and the possibility of still more parts is the void. Hence, because elapsed time makes up a whole, it has limits, before which a time precedes. The future, which makes up no whole, has no limits. The (g multitude of) things in present time makes up a whole which has limits, and the world is therefore in absolute empty space. On the other hand, every series of subordinated things necessarily has a limit *a priori*, and the *universitas* (totality) extends even to all possible members, not merely actual ones, and the first of things can be seen into by reason.",[]],"reflm:3842":["In the concept of all together, there must be in the thing itself a special ground for taking all together. If now each member is thought synthetically in relation to the others, then the present-past time is that which takes them all together, since it is itself a whole. [In the analytic] If the parts are given analytically out of the whole, then the *completudo* (completeness) of the analysis with the breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3843":["The relation of cause to effect is not a relation of identity; consequently there is neither similarity nor equality between cause and effect, but *conformitas* (conformity). From the effect the cause is to be known and also named. *talis est notio causae qva talis, qvalis oritur e notione causati* (such is the notion of a cause as such as arises from the notion of the thing caused).\nWe know every thing in the world only as cause, but in the cause only the causality of the effect, that is, only the effects, and so not the thing itself and its determinations, through which [they] it produces the effect.",[]],"reflm:3844":["*Effectus plenus* (the full effect) is measured not in a *series subordinatorum* but in a *series coordinatorum*; that is, not through the series of effects arising from the effects, e.g. the force of a ball through the series of balls which it can move mediately. From this one sees that it is always only the same effect which continues in time, though under different conditions. Thus the effect which arose immediately continues always, and neither increases nor diminishes.",[]],"reflm:3845":["102'. E II 719.\nTo the concluding word of M § 333 Kant adds: *aliqva*, and then continues on p. 102':\nBut the question is: what do we know sooner? Do we know sooner that something is an effect, and so has a cause, or that something is a cause, and so has an effect? The former. For that |\nsomething which happens is an effect is posited by reason only *per aliud*; we know this *nexus* only *a posteriori*. If we were to know causes beforehand, the *nexus* would be known *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:3846":["Immediate goodness (*bonitas*) is for the understanding the greatest, chiefly because it is necessary and universal; mediate usefulness is smaller, because it pleases only contingently and under certain circumstances.",[]],"reflm:3847":["If many causes agree together in one effect, they are connected with one another *a posteriori*. From this one infers that they are so connected also *a priori*, i.e. that there is a common cause of them. Are breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3848":["For reason it is not the data but the form that is the essential.",[]],"reflm:3849":["The concept of matter also refers to something passive; hence the substantial forms are active forces: entelechies. Spirits in their republic cannot be entitled the matter of the whole, because they are connected not passively but self-actively, and constitute their whole.",[]],"reflm:3850":["*forma dat esse rei* (form gives being to the thing). For the essential of a thing can be known only through reason; but all the matter of knowledge must be given through the senses; therefore the essence of things, so far as they are known through reason, is [merely] the form.",[]],"reflm:3851":["Matter is at all times regarded as passive, and form depends on activity; likewise all activity has reference to nothing further than form. Yet corporeal matter, as substance, is according to Leibniz an entelechy, i.e. something active. Our knowledge is, as regards the data, merely passive, and the form rests on self-activity.",[]],"reflm:3852":["*Forma dat esse rei* (form gives being to the thing). *nam per intellectum solummodo formam cognoscimus. Materia continet data, e qvibus varia oriri possunt diversimode determinando; ideoque determinatum esse alicuius rei pendet a forma. Forma est differentia specifica.* (For through the understanding we know only the form. Matter contains the data from which various things can arise by being determined in different ways; and therefore the determinate being of any thing depends on form. Form is the specific difference.)",[]],"reflm:3853":["On the first sentence of M § 381 (10718f.):\nFor otherwise all members of the series would be *caussata* (things caused) of another member of the series. [Thus wü] Thus there would be a member of the series which would be something other than all the members of it.",[]],"reflm:3854":["If the present (our) world is the only existing one, then it is also the most perfect whole from a single original being. But if there are several worlds, then one of them must indeed be the most perfect among all possible worlds, but perhaps not ours.\nThe idea of the most perfect world is as necessary to us as that of the most perfect cause; indeed the latter is properly necessary to be assumed for the sake of the former.",[]],"reflm:3855":["Life is the faculty of beginning a state (one's own or another's) from an inner principle. The former is not a complete life, since that whose state is changeable itself requires something external as its cause. Bodies do indeed have an inner principle for acting on one another (e.g. cohesion), and also for preserving a state imparted from without, but not for beginning one of themselves. Thus all alteration, all origin, proves a first beginning and consequently freedom. [Thus] But since the beginning can be the first only comparatively, namely according to mechanical laws, e.g. when a carrion excites the dog, motion begins in him which was not generated by the smell according to mechanical laws, but was caused by the arousing of appetite. In animals, however, this is just as much an external necessitation as in machines; hence they are called *automata spiritualia* (spiritual automata). But in man the chain of determining causes is in every case cut off, and therefore one also distinguishes |\nthe immaterial, as a principle of life, from the material. In man the spirit is free and wills the good; the animal is an automaton. If this spirit were always effective only in the animal element, and did not alternate with its forces, we should find more proofs of freedom.\n(s *libertas est independentia a necessitatione externa.*\n*libertas est vel originaria vel derivativa* [*haec vel*]. *Arbitrium autem est vel sensitivum vel intellectuale; illud est vel brutum vel liberum.*)\n[Freedom is independence of external necessitation. Freedom is either original or derivative. Choice (*arbitrium*), again, is either sensitive or intellectual; the former is either brute or free.]",[]],"reflm:3856":["To be determined is, in the case of freedom, quite admissible, but not passively: neither through the manner in which objects affect, nor through a supreme producing cause. I can say: at this moment I am free (g *liber aut devinctus*) and unbound to do what pleases me; and yet it is unavoidably necessary that I act thus. It is a law of self-activity which makes the opposite impossible. Can one also, in respect of the (g moral) evil, be determined in just the same way by free resolve? No! One can be determined to it only passively, or not at all, because the pure will always remains, and so cannot be bound at all, but its activity does not always exert itself.",[]],"reflm:3857":["A substance acts freely when, without being externally determined, it produces something which was not there before; and this freedom is opposed to inner or outer natural necessity. It acts from free choice (*Willkür*) so far as the causality of the action lies in its discretion, which is not passive. The difficulties affect only the first idea of |\nfreedom, and it is incomprehensible in the necessary being as well as in the contingent, but on different grounds, since the former can begin nothing, the latter can begin nothing first. The first degree of (g independence is) self-activity [is]: that of a substance in general; the second degree: independence in acting of all external determining causes; the third degree: independence of its own nature.\nThus the negative is properly incomprehensible; the positive, that of the motives, is comprehensible.",[]],"reflm:3858":["In all actions the ground, the causality, lies (g as regards matter) in nature; as regards form, however, it lies either merely in nature, e.g. attraction, or in another faculty which inwardly directs the forces of nature. The latter is freedom.",[]],"reflm:3859":["Freedom is (g a) practically necessary fundamental concept.\nThe first necessary is not to be comprehended, because there is no ground of it; the first contingent also is not [of] to be comprehended, because a necessitating ground must be there. The former, because it is necessary although it has no ground; the latter, because it is contingent although it has one.",[]],"reflm:3860":["For freedom there is required, 1. *spontaneitas simpliciter talis* (spontaneity simply as such) (*automaton*) (g *independentia a causis subiective necessitantibus* (stimulis) [independence of causes subjectively necessitating (stimuli)]), in order that [das arb was von dem subiect] the determinations may be attributed to the subject as its action; 2. the faculty of the *arbitrium*\n|\n*intellectuale* (intellectual choice), in order that it may be imputed to him as a *factum* (deed). Since everything that happens according to the laws of the faculty of desire presupposes a liking, the *complacentia* (satisfaction) which is independent of subjective necessitation must be intellectual, and the former accordingly presupposes the latter.\nThe greatest difficulty lies in this: how a subjectively unconditioned *Willkür* can be thought (g *est obiective hypotheticum* [is objectively hypothetical]) in (g according to) the *nexus causarum efficientium sive determinantium* (connection of efficient or determining causes), or, if one departs from this, how the imputability of actions is possible.",[]],"reflm:3861":["On aversion [*aversation*].\nThe natural and practical possibility [has] is the same — breaks off.\nThe first question is: how the causality [of a] of freedom is to be conceived, whether it breaks off.\nIn a contingent (g acting) being the causality can be either necessitated or dependent.\nWhether the generation (g genesis) of the action is determined *aliunde* (from elsewhere) or is *spontanea* (spontaneous).",[]],"reflm:3862":["One often says: The man has talents, and he has no bad heart either. He could become useful. It lies only in the willing. One would think that this circumstance could easily be changed; yet it does not happen (g negligent). If he now has a bad heart as well, will it then still be able to happen?\nIn bad men is the will bad, or is it good but weak? The means of making the will good are moral; those of making it strong are pragmatic, when one seeks to practise resolution, steadfastness in purpose, resignation. In some it seems not possible to bring forth a good will (although there is correct power of judgment, i.e. a preliminary will); in others, not a strong will.",[]],"reflm:3863":["(g sensuality)\nFreedom from brutality (*spontaneitas practice talis*, practical spontaneity as such)\nFreedom from fatality (*transscendentalis*, transcendental)\n*actus arbitrii vel originarii vel derivativi* (an act of choice, either original or derivative).",[]],"reflm:3864":["Arbitrary liking [Belieben] is a desire without any necessity of an efficient cause; consequently a pure liking can be necessary for the sake of the *bonitas* (goodness) of the thing and is yet free from inclination, and breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3865":["Freedom is properly a faculty [Vermögen] of subordinating all actions of choice [willkührliche Handlungen] to the motives of reason.",[]],"reflm:3866":["The *stimuli* [move] do not necessitate our choice [Willkühr] objectively, i.e. what one ought to do is independent of all sensible stimuli.",[]],"reflm:3867":["No one counts among freedom the faculty of being able to desire what is abominable (evil).\nWe have, then, sensible modes of knowledge [Erkentnisse], sensible pleasure and displeasure, and sensible desires. The faculty of acting according to motives of reason is freedom. The possibility of consciously willing what is disapproved by reason is the weak will; the [incli] skill [Fertigkeit] of willing the evil is the evil will.",[]],"reflm:3868":["The faculty of actively willing the good that is known and that lies in our power is freedom; but [not] the faculty of willing the evil that is known, and the prevention of which lies in our power, does not belong to it with equal necessity. This is also not properly a faculty but a possibility of suffering. Evil actions do indeed stand under freedom, but do not come about through it.",[]],"reflm:3869":["The true ends with regard to things are subordinated to the idea of the good; but with regard to the person, the idea of what is personally good is subordinated to the end — not the end which the person has, but that for which the nature of the person is designed, and which one knows through observation.\nOf all things there is a twofold ground of their nature: the cause and the end. The end seems just as much a ground of existence, namely from the worth of the existence, as is the efficient cause.",[]],"reflm:3870":["M 283':\n(s Pathological is what accords with the determination of the power [Kraft] by impressions.)\n(s Practical is everything that belongs to action according to rules, or everything that is connected with rules that determine actions. *Necessitatio est vel pathologica vel practica* (necessitation is either pathological or practical); |\nM 283: |\nthe latter can be objective, the former not.)\nM 283':\nSomething is considered practical in general so far as it is weighed according to the laws of free choice [Willkühr]. If it happens according to the rules of good choice, it is moral; hence practical and moral |\npossible or necessary. To practical philosophy belong only understanding and a physical feeling; to moral philosophy, understanding and a moral feeling. A human race would be possible even without the latter.",[]],"reflm:3871":["Opposite M § 723 (13638-1371), probably\nAs regards the inner necessity [of the distinction] in the faculty of desire, we find ourselves compelled not only by feeling (*stimuli*) to desire something; we also find ourselves compelled [our these our desires] to put ourselves in the place of a being that is subject to no such desires or feeling, and to regard our actions themselves from merely universal concepts of approval [Wohlgefallen]; and we feel ourselves compelled to look upon ourselves with abhorrence or with approval. Since now this abhorrence itself bears upon desire, it yields a law superior to desire.\nWhy are we compelled [to take] the abhorrence of — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3872":["The free will is as it were isolated. Nothing external determines it; it is active without suffering. The *motiva* (motives) are only objects that harmonize with the inner law of its activity.* The good moves only a good will, i.e. it is only the effect [according], which is possible according to its laws. Whether it be determined by moving causes or by *causis efficientibus* (efficient causes) (of the Deity), the subject is in either case hung upon a foreign cause by means of a chain, and its actions are only derived, the good as well as the evil.\nFreedom consists in the faculty of being able to act independently of external determining grounds, according to intellectual choice [intellectuale Willkühr]. [Of it] Under this stands all sensibility. Hence |\nwe think even hasty acts, or a series of obscure representations that are the causes of errors, as subject to our choice. The actions that occur according to mere rules of sensibility. In man we must distinguish the animal, i.e. man according to the laws of sensibility, and the spirit, according to laws of reason. His choice as animal is in fact always determined by *stimuli*; but his will is nevertheless free, so far as his reason is capable of altering these determinations of choice. Why reason at times fails to do so, and man does not act according to the intellectual will, is unknown. Hence it comes about that man, considered as spirit, blames himself as animal, is in contradiction and conflict with himself, wishes himself other inclinations, but often loses himself in sensibility. One spiritual use is to order the animal sensations and to direct means according to animal ends. The other is to consider the ends themselves intellectually [*intellectualiter*].\nHow is it possible that one can blame oneself, if the self is not, so to speak, twofold? For otherwise one can pass no other judgment upon oneself than what is taken from oneself and also agrees with oneself.\n*(s Not the objective but subjective grounds necessitate actually [*actualiter*].)",[]],"reflm:3873":["The (g contradictory) opposite of the [thing] thing is to be distinguished from the opposite of its relation [Verhältnis]; the latter is the opposite of the judgment.\nE.g. a divisible body; its opposite is: *Corpus* ×0 + *divisibile* ×0 = 0, *nihil privativum* (privative nothing). This opposite never contradicts itself.",[]],"reflm:3874":["Space and time are mere possibilities, whose opposite, i.e. no space and no time, is impossible. (There is no space, there is no time.) Now space and time are grounded on something actual; therefore something actual is absolutely necessary.",[]],"reflm:3875":["When the absolute necessity of something is known, its existence is known.\nAbsolute necessity is not knowable externally through what is caused, but internally from its idea.\nAn existent, by the removal of which all possibility is removed, is absolutely necessary.\nIf some (g internal) possibility is removed, all possibility is removed; for, with that removed, the absolutely impossible is thought; but if there were still some things thinkable, it would be possible for something to be impossible. Otherwise: If some existent were absolutely necessary, another existent likewise, and so |\non: one of the existents could be removed with the existence of the others preserved, and thus possibility would extend only so far as some existence touches.\nOn the notion of possibility in which one can abstract from existence, in relation to the possibility in which one cannot abstract from it, e.g. space, time.\nImpossibility is either of repugnance [*repugnantiae*] or of negation [*negationis*]; the former obtains always if the relation of certain given notions is impossible; the latter in so far as there is nothing given to be thought, i.e. nothing possible.",[]],"reflm:3876":["That the possibility of something does not contain the ground of its existence. Nor its existence that of its possibility. But that in the necessary, possibility and existence are the same. That nothing contains the ground of itself.",[]],"reflm:3877":["The cause of substance through necessity of nature is emanative (system of emanation).\nThe cause of substance through freedom is a creator.\nThe absolute necessity of the substances of the universe with the contingency and dependence of state breaks off?",[]],"reflm:3878":["Goodness [Gütigkeit] is the divine will in relation to the condition (g the possible happiness) of the rational creature. Holiness is the divine will as a ground of the rules of the human. Justice is the (g wise) goodness of the divine will in relation to the prescription of holiness, *virtualis* (virtual).",[]],"reflm:3879":["The world cannot be compared in respect of time with any being outside the world. It has a beginning, but not, in regard to God, an origin; rather a dependence. I can compare the duration of the world with its parts as an hour with minutes, but not with any thing that is distinct from the world. Any thing in the world might well have existed earlier, but not the world itself.",[]],"reflm:3880":["The world, in respect of what in it is the work of God, is the best among all possible worlds. For this is the [practical] representation of the greatest practical perfection of God. The representation of the greatest perfection of the world on this side includes within itself, as an identical proposition, all the hopes that I can form from the existence of God. And therefore this proposition is also a principal proposition (*Hauptsatz*). But on the side of freely acting beings one cannot assert the same. Were one to say that God has the power to make the free beings act as it pleases him, this would be contrary to their freedom. Or were one to say that he foresaw what they would do in all circumstances, and chose the best *conditiones*, then this foresight would prove the necessary connection of the actions with these conditions, which are posited by God.",[]],"reflm:3881":["In so far as the events of the world are supposed to spring from its nature, they all happen according to [common] general laws, because nature [without] (provided no miracle occurs) always remains the same, and the changes are therefore in accordance with a common *principium*. Now all things of the world, each of which has its particular |\nnature, are [dependent] on one another, and one state is dependent on another. No event, therefore, happens merely in relation to one state, but in relation to all the states of the thing, that is, to its nature, and at the same time according to the laws of the nature of other connected things. For example, a man perishes at sea according to laws of the nature of the water, the winds and the shore, and not merely in relation to his moral constitution. Now it is impossible that the events, if each is intended singly, should [of] coincide by chance with all the things of the world, for example a man's wicked deed and the winds, each of which happens according to the order of nature of its subject. They could then not follow from nature, nor according to one and the same laws.\nTo the opinion of some [of the] that God directs his governance not [before] to individuals but to the general, one is accustomed to reply that the general is thought only through abstraction from the *individua*. On this it is to be noted that the general is either the logically general ([or] the common mark of many) or the really general. [The former.] For example, suppose a machine is constructed for many single effects, like Vaucanson's duck. Then I can say that [its] the artist's provision is directed to the general of the animal actions, that is, to the logically general, or to all of them. But if the construction of the machine is made in such a way that it is designed not at all for any single [effect] but indeterminately for a ground of all of them, then the construction is a general real ground. Thus a barometer is a general measurer of the air, and the feet of a jumper are adapted not to the single dances but to walking and dancing in general.",[]],"reflm:3882":["It is one thing to concede a particular providence, and another to discern its marks in a particular case, or to understand the particular rules for the application of the goodness and wisdom of God. He who professes to recognise a particular divine purpose in an event asserts that he discerns the grounds of the divine decrees, through which the event had necessarily to turn out thus and not otherwise. But in that case this same insight would also have to suffice for predicting the event from the known rules of the divine decrees. In the same way he has no insight into the causes of eclipses [who does not] in so far as they are determined to a certain point of time, if he cannot announce them beforehand.",[]],"reflm:3883":["A ground (*ratio*) is that through whose concept (that is, a universal one) (g not according to laws of appearances) something else is posited, and by which, accordingly, something else is universally determined. From the grounded (*rationatum*) (g predicate) to the ground (g subject) the inference (*consequentia*) does not hold, except indeterminately or particularly.",[]],"reflm:3884":["The fundamental concepts are not determinations, and hence also not the essential constituents.\nThe logical essence is hypothetical or absolute. The former is what constitutes the whole (g primitive) concept that we have of the thing; the second is what expresses the possible concept.\nThe real essence signifies the subject that is unchanged and determinable. Everything that exists has a real essence which always remains, and the rest are determinations.",[]],"reflm:3885":["From being to being possible (*ab esse ad posse*) the inference holds, and it is empirical. What is possible, concluded through the principle of contradiction, is known rationally. We know the possibility of a real connection (*nexus realis*) empirically.\nFrom not being to not being possible (*a non esse ad non posse*) the inference does not hold, but it is nevertheless an imperfect empirical conclusion.",[]],"reflm:3886":["Whether magnitude is not something that is relative in itself, in relation to what is the greatest and contains the data for everything possible, e.g. the triangle in infinite space. [Da] This is absolute magnitude, which we know by the fact that something can still be greater, and not by the fact that it can be diminished, i.e. contains parts. The latter is relative magnitude.",[]],"reflm:3887":["Perfections simply (*schlechthin*) are [necessary agreements], that which accords together for the inner worth of things. Hence only rational beings can have perfections; e.g. understanding is distinguished from beauty and happiness.\n*In sensu respectivo* (in a relative sense) [either the perfection of the], when nothing is lacking to a thing either with respect to something positive in general or with respect to a determinate concept: form of perfection.\n(g The [inner] worth itself is the *bonitas* (goodness); it depends on the matter of perfection.)",[]],"reflm:3888":["What is inwardly necessary is absolutely necessary (*omnimode*); but we cannot see the inner necessity, owing to the omission of the determinate conditions. Our concept of necessity, however, also admits that of a ground, and indeed of a general ground, and this too is what is understood in the idea of thoroughgoing necessity. But this is only a concept of relation in the subordination of ideas, and logically conditional; it serves only to distinguish what is necessary in every respect (*omnimode*) from what is such only in a restricted way (*restrictive*).",[]],"reflm:3889":["*Realitas in sensu substantivo* (reality in the substantive sense) is a perfection in the metaphysical sense.\nThe highest reality does not consist in everything's being in it, but in everything's being through it as a ground. For the maximum of reality is not possible synthetically or through coordination; lesser degrees are possible only through limitation of the greatest possible. Now the highest reality is that which cannot be limited. Hence that which is the measure of all things, and in which the reality of all things lies, is only the consequence of the *ens summum* (the highest being).",[]],"reflm:3890":["Every general concept contains what is common to many things |\n(g wherein many are one and the same), and is indeterminate with regard to the differences.\n[Through it one can think several things, and it contains many under it.]\nA particular concept is more determined, namely with regard to the differences. (g On the thoroughgoingly determined.)\nIt contains the particulars under it and is *latior* (wider); the particulars are *conceptus angustiores* (narrower concepts). It is entitled *superior* in regard to the particulars, which are *inferiores*.\nIt [contains less in itself] is contained in the lower ones and contains less in itself.\nLogical subordination. *Conceptus summus* (highest concept) (g least determined), *infimus* (lowest). Most determined (g singular). *Sphaera conceptus* (sphere of a concept). *Logica divisio* (logical division).\n*Progressus per synthesin, regressus per analysin* (progress by synthesis, regress by analysis).\nIn the series of logical subordination there is a member that has higher ones above it and lower ones within it: *conceptus communis* [subord:] (s *subalternus*). If it is considered only in its *superior*, it is represented *in abstracto*; in its *inferior*, *in concreto*; most of all *in abstracto* or *in individuo*.\nThe *conceptus communis* [inferior] (s *subalternus*) is, in regard to its *inferior*, a *genus*; in regard to its *superior*, a *species*. A *genus* which is a *species* in regard to its *superior* is *subalternum*; one which is not: *summum*. A *species* which contains only *individua* under it is a *species infima*. *Continuitas formarum logicarum* (continuity of logical forms). Between two concepts logically subordinated to one another there is always a middle one.\n*Subordinatio realis* (real subordination). *Omnitudo realis* (real allness). In place of the determinations of the general concept, limitation of the *sphaera* of the highest ground, *negationes*. In logical divisions one limits the *sphaera*; in real determinations one limits the reality. The concept that is (g logically) *inferior* contains fewer determinations; the one that is really *inferior* contains fewer *positiones*, until the *conceptus infimus* |\nis *realitas evanescens* (vanishing reality) or pure *negatio*. Nevertheless the *subordinatio realis* too holds only as regards possibility, and is therefore metaphysics; the causal subordination in existence is entitled physics.\n*Omnitudo realis* [signifies] is opposed to particular *negatio*.",[]],"reflm:3891":["Origin is a moment on which the whole existence follows; ceasing to be (end) is the moment which follows upon the whole existence. By this it is shown, first, that origin is an existence, and secondly, a *terminus* of duration.\nA whole springs from something when the existence of the parts precedes the form, and from nothing when nothing of the parts precedes the whole.",[]],"reflm:3892":["Whether there is an absolute space or an absolute time (*spatium absolutum*, *tempus absolutum*) would amount to asking whether, between two things in space, everything [else] lying between them could be annihilated and yet the determinate empty gap would remain; and whether, if for a whole year [between the beginning] motions and alterations in general were to cease, the following year could not begin, so that an empty year would have elapsed in between. We do not resolve these difficulties, but answer our opponents by *retorsion* (turning the argument against them), since their conception has precisely these same difficulties.\nThe ground or principle (*ratio sive principium*) of a state is time, and indeed of the connection of all that belongs to one and the same state.",[]],"reflm:3893":["It is absolutely impossible for a thing to be in many places at once, but not for it to be in them successively.\nIt is only conditionally [necessarily] impossible for many substances to be in one and the same place, namely those that are impenetrable.",[]],"reflm:3894":["Cosmology is either empirical (physics and psychology) or rational.\nThe latter considers either the material, i.e. [subiect] constitutive elements (matter and the immaterial), or the form. The second is treated here.\nRational [empirical] cosmology belongs to metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:3895":["Whether the *ens realissimum* (the most real being) is at the same time absolutely perfect (g the *summum bonum*) is a question; for that it is most perfect *respective sive externe* (relatively or externally) is certain. Internal perfection (*bonitas*) presupposes the feeling of pleasure.",[]],"reflm:3896":["*Nihil* (nothing) in the logical sense is opposed to some thing; in the metaphysical sense it is opposed to the thinkable (*cogitabile*). The former is *nihil* only relatively such (none of those things that are contained under some notion). The latter is the opposite of those things that are contained under any notion whatever.",[]],"reflm:3897":["A ground (*ratio*) is sufficient either among coordinates (s i.e. *ratio completa*, the complete ground) or among subordinates.\nIn the case of the former one no longer asks the 'why' of the consequence; in the case of the latter, of the ground.\n( s This stands under no higher ground; that is no part of a larger ground. The terminus of subordinated grounds is the *ratio primitiva* (primitive ground). The terminus of coordinated grounds is *completudo* (completeness). )\n(s The whole of the series or of the ground.)",[]],"reflm:3898":["In complex concepts the inference from possibility to actuality (*a posse ad esse*) does not hold, but in simple ones it does.\nFrom the form of possibility one cannot infer to actuality, but one can from the matter.",[]],"reflm:3899":["All unity is either of comparison or of connection. The first is so far as something is the same as many others. The second, so far as many are combined in one ground.\nThe unity of taking together (addition) is mathematical; or that of derivation: this is philosophical.",[]],"reflm:3900":["Everything is necessary, either conditionally (g i.e. through a ground) or unconditionally.\n[Everything] Among all grounds there must be a first ground; this is absolutely necessary.",[]],"reflm:3901":["The synthesis of reality consists merely in the separation from the |\none greatest by negations, because negations presuppose reality; consequently the distinction of the [gan] composite and the simple lies therein.",[]],"reflm:3902":["Besides substance and accident one can still think something positive, namely the *respectus* (relation). Every *respectus* (g of these) is threefold: that of substance (empirical): space; that of the accidents (*accidentia*): time; that of substance to the accidents: force.",[]],"reflm:3903":["Substance alone is a thing or [something] object.",[]],"reflm:3904":["A state (*status*) is the coexistence of the substantial with accidents.\nThe substantial (*substantiale*) is immutable; for since it contains the first ground of the accidents, and hence of the changes, if it were changed this change would be an accident, not something substantial. The first ground, moreover, is immutable for this reason: since there is no other ground of change, it would change itself, i.e. *a* and not-*a* would be determined at once in the same being.",[]],"reflm:3905":["On M 369:\nThe whole of the world must not be confounded with the whole state of it. |\nFor the accidents are not parts of the world. Consequently I can say: the world arises gradually, in that one part is created after another.",[]],"reflm:3906":["( s Necessity and contingency in the world. Nature (s with ground) and freedom, and the perfection of both: happiness and morality. Blind necessity, blind contingency. Fortuity. Supreme principle of the rationally necessary, natural necessity [of the rationally contingent] and freedom; both are contained in the supreme being. )( s Nature and freedom, destiny and chance. { Connection. *Saltus* (leap). Order. *)\n*( (g this concerns events, consequently what happens in the world; for the origin of it is no event.) )\nAll events of the world happen either through blind necessity:\n*bruta necessitas*;\n*Fatum*, or through destiny — decree — counsel of the supreme cause;\n*per libertatem*, or through freedom of the acting beings in the world;\n*casus*, or not through destiny or freedom;\n*fortuitum*, or from no cause at all.\nBlind necessity (s — The rational)\nPredetermined. Decree.\nThrough freedom (s Contingency.)\nWithout will — — — Chance\nBlind fortuity. (s Natural chance.)\n*Nihil per saltum, casum et fatum accidit* (nothing happens through a leap, chance or fate).\n( s In the series of everything contingent the first is the free action. In the series of everything necessary the first is absolute necessity. Limits. )\n(s *Qualitas mundi* (quality of the world); *mundus non constat vacuo* (the world does not consist of void).)\n(s Every transition is through all possible intermediate members.)\n(s That which is in itself contingent, yet happens necessarily [is] without ground, is blind necessity.\nThat which is in itself dependent, which [is] happens without any [ground] necessity, is blind chance.)\n(s Necessity in the world without any ground is the blind.\nContingency in the world without any ground is the blind.)",[]],"reflm:3907":["( s (g negative: One does not speak of divine reason.) Understanding. No sensibility. (*non affectus*, not affected.) Intuitive, not discursive: extends to the possible and the actual with equal strength (g sees everything in itself). No difficulty regarding future time. (g *Perfectiones quiescentes et operativae* (quiescent and operative perfections).) [Will] Pleasure and displeasure from itself, blessedness. Will. Freedom. Independence of incentives (g analogue of a motive); yet no *absolutum decretum* (absolute decree), but conformable to the goodness (*bonitas*) of the object. Moral motive: justice. Benevolent purpose. )\nAll the great properties that I assert of God from the arbitrary idea of him are only expositions of the hypothesis that I adopt. But those that I draw from the work (these, however, are only practically perfect) apply to a real being given through actual documents.\n1. Existence of the original being [of God] (*ens originarium*): against atheists (s dogmatic and sceptical). Existence of the supreme intelligence [of God]: against deists.\n(s Real condition of all possibility. Necessity. Immutability. Impassibility. Immateriality. Infinity.)\n[2. *Originaria* (original), against Epicureans] (Existence of the supreme person: against fatalists.)\n3. Immutable and impassible: against anthropomorphists (g because his concept is purely intellectual).\n4. Substantiality: against Spinoza.\n(s Not a single substance (g simple): against Spinoza (so that the world would inhere in him as an accident); not a single substance (g composite) as the sum of all, i.e. το παν (the All) [as if God were an accident inhering in the world]. God is a being outside the world (*ens extramundanum*).)\n5. That he is a being outside the world: against the Stoics, because they maintained a world-soul.\n6. Unity: against polytheists.\n7. Highest perfection (s highest good): against Manichaeans.\n8. Simplicity. (s Infinity: against anthropomorphists.)\n9. Eternity. (s 10. Causality in respect of the world.)\n10. Omnipresence. (s Being outside the world (not world-soul): against the Stoics.)\n11. Omnipotence. (s Intelligence: against deists.)\na. Personality: against deists, through intellectual concepts of consciousness of oneself, not so far as we are affected, but as we act. (s The transcendental, natural and moral theology. God as holy.)",[]],"reflm:3908":["(s a)\na: Against the Manichaeans.\n(s a 2,)\n3. Omnisufficiency (*omnisufficientia*). As cause of the universe, also of the possibles.\n4. Immutability.\n5. Impassibility (s Spirituality: against deists; theism, but not anthropomorphism.)\n(s c)\n6. Unity. (s Holiness: against the heathens.)\n(s d)\n7. Simplicity.\n8. Immateriality.\n9. Intelligence. Living God.\n(s a 3. Infinity [ads] internal — relative; omnisufficiency [respectu]. The conditions are 9 and 10.)",[]],"reflm:3909":["Transcendental concept of God.\nGod as the highest being. (s Defence against atheism.)\nMetaphysical-psychological:*\nGod as the highest intelligence. (s Defence against deism, which properly attacks anthropomorphism.)\nMoral:\nGod as the highest good. (s Defence against Epicureanism.)\n(s He cannot be a rightful lawgiver of morals unless he is at the same time the lawgiver of nature, and indeed in its whole completeness. For if he does not have destiny wholly in his power, he cannot command absolutely. But for this he must be creator; this is necessary for speculation, but not for religion.)\nα. *Moralitas* (morality) against the Epicureans. (s Moral theology, theological morals.)\n*(s We can know God only psychologically, by analogy with the powers of nature, which contain the complete causality of perfection. For we have no data of God, but only data of his relation to the world; hence we know in him only s breaks off.)",[]],"reflm:3910":["Of God I know only the condition of his sufficient relation to the world, and this relation is indeed similar to that of the things of the world, because he is the principle of the series; but these attributes are not similar to the attributes of the things of the world. We can quite well ignore that which has no such relation. For it does not concern us.",[]],"reflm:3911":["God is perfect through his nature, but he is good through his will. The ideal of a good will is realised in God or through God.\nWhat is good, its existence has a worth. Perfection contains the *completudinem* [completeness] of the *datorum* [data] for something, from whose worth we abstract.",[]],"reflm:3912":["The world has a beginning, that is, a state which is not a consequence of another state: *terminum a priori*; *non est aeternus a parte ante*. For if every state of the world were a consequence of another state of the world, then all states of the world would have another state of the world before them; hence a state of the world would be distinguished from all states [of the world], which is a *contradictorium*.\nFrom the beginning of the world up to the present time no eternity has elapsed: *mundus non creatus est ab aeterno*.\nThe world could not have been created earlier; but we might well have existed later.\nWe cannot represent distinctly how the causality of a necessary cause begins. For we always think to ourselves that it had not been there before. But at the beginning of all things we must indeed think a relation of it *a parte post*, but none *a parte ante*, and so no relation of the world to a time that had previously elapsed.",[]],"reflm:3913":["[consensus vel opposit]\nAll combination [Verbindung] is either one of combination or of comparison. The former is either of agreement or of conflict.\nThat of combination is either analytic (of the part with the whole, and so also with itself) and is logical; or synthetic: of the parts with one another, of the [parts] combined unities with one another.",[]],"reflm:3914":["Nothing in [human soul] metaphysics is empirical, except what concerns the general laws of thinking in the human soul.\nHence the synthetic principles too hold not as rational but as subjectively universal; and it is not possible for the understanding to begin from concepts and merely compare the concepts analytically, unless some rules of synthesis lie at the foundation.",[]],"reflm:3915":["[The relation of magnitudes is the ground of an ideal combination, in that I from a]\nThere are comparisons which are not connections, e.g. of identity, diversity. These are ideal connections or conflicts.\nComparisons of magnitude.",[]],"reflm:3916":["The comparison of concepts according to the law of the imagination is not to be wholly rejected. Things which are always found combined with one another give rise to the presumption that they are combined according to the law of the understanding. Hence the inference from induction. According to the law of wit, the inference from analogy.",[]],"reflm:3917":["(s All knowledge has two *termini*, *a priori* and *a posteriori*; hence *principia prima* [first principles] *a priori* or *a posteriori*. [If ab] The *principia absolute prima a priori* are all indemonstrable; hence metaphysics contains only *principia comparative prima*.)\n(s All pure ideas of reason are ideas of reflection (*discursivae* and no *intuitus*, as Plato asserted). Hence objects too are not represented by them, but only laws for comparing the concepts which have been given to us through the senses.)\nAll knowledge has grounds, and among these a first. The first are either those which in the natural course come first in origination, or those [out of which] into which they ultimately resolve themselves and from which they can again be composed: *a priori* or *a posteriori*. Grammar: empirical, rational.\n(s empirical or intellectual principles)\nThe first grounds of knowledge, reckoned from behind, are experiences.\nThe science of arriving at the highest grounds *a priori* is metaphysics. This science is analytic [auflösend]. Fundamental concepts, fundamental judgments.\nMethod of nature.\nConditional indispensability of this science in the case of higher judgments of reason.\nHistory. Names.\nOrder of the disciplines.\n( g Grounds of one's own insights\nof others [Eins] testimonies, proverbs )",[]],"reflm:3918":["The territory of metaphysics. Parts.\nIts function determines its worth. It is not the satisfac|tion\nof the desire for knowledge, but the determination of boundaries, partly positive, partly negative.\nIt is a science of the ends of our knowledge. Comparison with mathematics.\n[history. Aristotle.]",[]],"reflm:3919":["(s The understanding combines concepts because one includes the other; the imagination, because one accompanies the other; wit, because one is similar to the other. With negative judgments it is just the same; e.g. because we combine contact with the immediate effect, we deny attraction.)\nThe law of the understanding is put into practice only with a great admixture of the law of the imagination.\nIt is not enough to have grounds for a piece of knowledge; one must also accustom oneself to it slowly. (Friction.) It is not enough to remove grounds; one must also for a time resist habit, and gradually hold back that same former judgment even without grounds.",[]],"reflm:3920":["With all judgments of the understanding the matter stands as follows. (If (g any) something x [which] through a representation a known [is, is compared with another concept b, either that it the representation b] can be, then a is a mark of something x, [but] the knowledge of x through a is a concept. Thus extension, motion, ignorance etc. etc. is a mark of something x.) If something x, which is known through a representation a, is compared with another concept (g b), either in that it includes or excludes this, |\nthen this relation is in the judgment. This judgment is (g therefore) either the knowledge of agreement or of conflict, so that in the [concept of] thing x, which I know through the concept a, either b is contained as a partial concept, and thus x, [also] which is known through a, can also be known through b; or [through] x the concept of b is cancelled.\nIn all judgments the matter and the form are to be considered. The former are the concepts of the subject (y+a)\n= x and of the predicate b; second, the form, which among the logicians is called the concept of connection (copula). (One can represent every concept to oneself in relation with all others; those which it includes stand toward it in the relation of affirmation, those which it excludes, in that of negation.) x is called the possible concept of a thing, a the representation through which I think it. All form of judgments is either affirmation or negation. The first represents the relation in which the concept of the thing y+a includes the mark b and so is identical with it as to its part. Negation consists in this, that the concept of the thing y+a is the opposite of the mark b, and negation consists in the representation of the cancelling.",[]],"reflm:3921":["The predicate is not a part (g concept) of the subject, but a representation of the [whole] subject through a partial concept. The understanding always knows something through a clear or obscure judgment, in that it resolves something into its predicates. All [signs] our concepts are marks drawn from sensation. Sensation itself is no object of the understanding, but rather its marks; hence, e.g., the concept of man is nothing other than the representation of something which has the predicates into which we can resolve the concept of a man. Hence in every judgment the subject is in general Something = x, which, known under the mark a, is compared with another mark. Hence it is also no wonder that we know no subject |\nbefore all predicates, except merely the I, which nevertheless is no concept but an [sensation] intuition. Hence through the understanding we know in bodies not the proper subjects, but the predicates of extension, solidity, rest, motion etc. The reason is: through our senses only the relations of things can be revealed, and we can represent the absolute or subject only from ourselves. The idea of substance comes properly from the *repraesentatio sui ipsius* [representation of oneself], so far as we represent that something is distinct from us, and predicates cannot be thought without a subject and without a last subject, [hinzu k] the constant predicates are then together called the subject.\nThrough a predicate I do not represent to myself a part of the thing or have a concept of the part, but represent the object itself to myself and have of it a partial concept; hence designation by mathematical signs is also impossible. Let y+s be the thing itself, which is represented under the concept s, and p its predicate; then y+s–p would = 0, and consequently y+s = p.",[]],"reflm:3922":["M III:\nMaterial principles seem to be these: what [arises] happens must have a ground. Every successive series has a beginning*. Propositions of this kind implicate themselves; for since the beginning is an arising or has happened, there would, so far as it is concerned, again have to be a ground. The idea of freedom indicates an arising without a preceding determining ground. The nature of our understanding brings it with it that, according to its rule, nothing (g contingent) is thinkable without connection with grounds, and that a consequence (in time) without a ground, and a happening of something without connection with its ground, cannot be thought, because the understanding would then be wholly useless.\nM IV:\n*(g and every series of subordinated things has a first. †)\n†(s It is hard to think of a beginning in the series of all things, to which everything else succeeds, other than as creation. Equally it is impossible to think an elapsed infinite series. The idea of applying 'all' to an infinite series seems to be false, and yet the necessity of the *causa prima* (first cause) rests upon it, since otherwise everything would be *causatum* (something caused).)\nM III:\nAnother synthetic principle is: what thinks is only a simple subject. Every thing must (not absolutely, but relatively to another) be at some time, either simultaneously or successively.\nIt does not follow that what must be judged according to the laws of our understanding is true, if it concerns such things as our understanding, by the constitution of its use, is not determined to judge.\n[There would be no philosophy] We borrow from corporeal appearances the law of sufficient reason; but if we wish to make it universal [so] and apply it to things that are exalted above the idea of our understanding, we confound the idea of what is for us absolutely unintelligible with inner impossibility.",[]],"reflm:3923":["Some principles are analytic and concern the formal aspect of distinctness in our knowledge. Some are synthetic and concern the material aspect, such as the arithmetical, geometrical and chronological principles, likewise the empirical. But there are still principles that concern the use of reason in synthesis in general. The nature of our reason, however, has this law, that it knows things not immediately but mediately; hence it can expect everything that happens only in accordance with a ground, and what is determined by no other ground is for it unreasonable.\n'Matter is lifeless' is a rational and not an empirical judgment, [it] because one also [assumes but] perceives much life in matter, yet distinguishes this from the material properties. By contrast, 'matter has attractive forces' is empirical.",[]],"reflm:3924":["From the imperfect idea of substance it follows that one can hardly represent to oneself as a distinct substance that which is and acts merely through a foreign force. Spinozism.",[]],"reflm:3925":["Synthesis is either successive [or], that is, of the subordinated, or side by side, of the coordinated. From the latter arise the judgments about the whole and its parts.",[]],"reflm:3926":["How do empirical and synthetic judgments become universal?\nHave we not, besides the *principia formalia* (formal principles) of rational propositions, perhaps also *formalia* of the synthetic and empirical? Likewise, are there not just as many *principia formalia* of real connection as of logical connection?",[]],"reflm:3927":["Of empirical fundamental concepts, obtained through abstraction, there are countless ones that cannot be resolved further into their elements, although one can show the determination in its cause. The rational fundamental concepts, in which alone the sensed properties of things can be explained, are, for outer objects: space, time, motion. For the [äusse] inner: A 1. Immediate representation of the present, the past, the future. 2. Comparison, distinction and sameness. 3. Relation (logical) of connection and of conflict. 4. Consciousness, judgments, inferences. B 1. Feeling. Pleasure, displeasure. 2. In relation to the judgment of the understanding or of the senses. C. Desire, etc. etc. [Fundamental concepts common to both sensations] Through the nature of the understanding, not *abstrahendo* (by abstracting) but *iudicando* (by judging), fundamental concepts of synthesis arise: existence, possibility, unity, substance, accident, relation, [der lo] *respectus realis*, *logicus*, necessary, contingent. Whole, a part. Simple, composite, ground, consequence, force, cause.",[]],"reflm:3928":["The *principia* of the form of all analytic judgments (g of the existential judgments of things absolutely, or of predicates relatively) are the principle of identity and of contradiction, and [the subjective] the proofs are conducted by showing, through analysis of the given concept, the identity or opposition with the predicate. The *principia* of the form of synthetic judgments are: whatever is at all times combined with a known part of the possible concept of a thing belongs with it, as a part, to this concept, etc. etc. The proposition 'Every body is impenetrable' is analytic, because [the b] not only can body not be thought without impenetrability, but it can be thought solely through impenetrability; this mark belongs as *pars* (part) to the *notio* (notion) of body. But 'Every body is inert' is a synthetic proposition; for inertia is *compars* (co-part) with the concept [of the b] of that which is thought under the expression 'body', in a whole concept that is necessarily combined with those partial concepts which belong to the notion of body. If one had the whole concept, of which the notions of subject and predicate are *compartes*, the synthetic judgments would be transformed into analytic. The question is how far something arbitrary is involved here.\nSo much for the *nexus logicus* (logical connection). As regards the *nexus realis* (real connection), its material principles are experiences; the formal principles are: everything that happens has a determining ground, and secondly: everything has a first ground. These principles are both synthetic, the former for the use of our reason, the latter for the limit (*terminus*) of this use. For according to the former we always look, in the series of causes determining one another, to the higher grounds, and according to the latter we concede that this series is bounded. But it is just as impossible to represent a series of subordinated grounds that has no |\nbeginning as to conceive how it begins. Meanwhile the proposition 'everything that happens has a determining ground', which makes an infinite series necessary, is the *principium* of the form of all our judgments of reason about real connection. But the proposition 'every series of subordinated things and every successive series has a first' is a synthetic proposition that is abstracted more from the limits of our understanding than from the object of knowledge.\nThe first in the series of causes is always free choice [Willkühr]. That this has no determining ground is an empirical proposition, but to that extent uncertain; yet its certainty depends on this, that otherwise there could be no first at all.\nOn the possibility of change, that is, of arising and ceasing in general.",[]],"reflm:3929":["Strictly, the representation of all things is the representation of our own state and the relation of one representation to another according to our inner laws. The impossibility of separating concepts, or of combining them without any laws of our understanding, is merely subjective; so too is the possibility. We know the possibility of free actions only empirically; in the rational sense it conflicts with the laws of using our understanding. *Analysis sine termino* (analysis without limit) [is] makes synthesis impossible. Hence, according to the laws in the use of our understanding, we posit a ground in everything that happens, but [according to] for just that reason synthesis is impossible (just this applied to the analysis of body *sine termino*, where no synthesis is impossible). Of the first relations according to the laws of our understanding no further ground can be given. (g Complete analysis (*analysis completa*) led [in all actions] from the manifold to unity: in the series of successives to the principle, in simultaneous things to the monad.)\nThe (synthetic) axioms (*axiomata*) of philosophy relate solely to the relation, which can be known only subjectively, according to the laws of our understanding.",[]],"reflm:3930":["Some concepts are abstracted from sensations, others merely from the law of the understanding, to compare abstracted concepts, [and] to combine or to separate them. The origin of the latter is in the understanding, that of the former in the senses. All concepts of such a kind are entitled pure concepts of the understanding, *conceptus intellectus puri*. We can indeed set these activities of the understanding in motion only on the occasion of sensible sensations, and become conscious of certain concepts of the universal relations of abstracted ideas in accordance with laws of the understanding; and so Locke's rule also holds here, that without sensible sensation no idea becomes clear in us. The *notiones rationales* arise, to be sure, by means of the sensations, and can also be thought only in application to the ideas abstracted from them, but they do not lie in them and are not abstracted from them. Thus in [mathem] geometry we do not borrow the idea of space from the sensation of extended beings, although we can make this concept clear only on the occasion of the [corporeal] sensation of corporeal things. Hence the idea of space is a *notio intellectus puri*, which can be applied to the abstracted idea of mountains and of casks.\nThe [science] philosophy [of everything that] of the concepts of the *intellectus purus* is metaphysics. It is related to the rest of philosophy as *mathesis pura* to *mathesis applicata*. The concepts of existence (g reality), of possibility, of necessity, of ground, of unity and plurality, of the whole and the parts (g all, none), of the composite and the simple, of space, of time, of change, (g of motion,) of substance and accident, of force and action, and everything that belongs to ontology proper, stand in relation to the rest of metaphysics as (g general) arithmetic stands in *mathesis pura*.",[]],"reflm:3931":["The first principles of empirical philosophy are propositions of experience which are universal, and from which one descends to the lower ones. Those of rational philosophy are pure grounds of reason. In empirical philosophy one proceeds analytically, in rational philosophy synthetically.\nOf synthetic principles we can give grounds *in sensu subiectivo*, but not *in sensu obiectivo*. Only of abstracted concepts can we give grounds through *analysis obiectiva*, but of rational concepts only through *analysis subiectiva*. Or rather: of rational propositions we can give grounds only through *analysis subiectiva*. E.g. of the proposition that a being exists necessarily, namely because our thoughts of a possibility without any actuality are [contradictory] void, since all possibility must be borrowed from something actual.\nMetaphysics proper, or pure philosophy, consists of ontology.",[]],"reflm:3932":["The ideas and rules of reason are also used in the relation of empirical concepts, and this is their natural and correct use; but they are then grounded on *iudicia empirica primitiva*, which are universal only through induction. But just these judgments of reason, so far as they are pure, are supposed to be universal in themselves. *Philosophia pura* is not, as *mathesis pura* is, certain in itself.",[]],"reflm:3933":["In all identity of concepts [the not] two concepts agree in one, i.e. [one concept is common to both. In every judgment] one [mark] concept belongs to two; in every judgment two concepts belong to one thing. The thing that I think through the concept A, I think also through the concept B | is a judgment (g of connection). The concept that I think in A, I think also in B: is a judgment of comparison.\nIn identity I compare two subjects that have one and the same predicate.\nIn unity I compare two predicates or concepts that have one subject.",[]],"reflm:3934":["To the inconceivability of the divine attributes belongs not only his presence in all places of space at once, but also at all points of time at once, i.e. that he can represent to himself an eternity as a whole.",[]],"reflm:3935":["The proposition: all events have consequences (each one), and all together have in turn consequences, are not the same.\nAll rational synthetic propositions are subjective, and conversely only the analytic are objective. *Principia identitatis et contradictionis*.\nThe synthesis of reason (g rational) or of experience (g empirical).\nThe first is either of coordination: whole and parts, number and unit, or of subordination: ground and consequence.\n[Empirical] The second, of coordination according to space and time.",[]],"reflm:3936":["Everything is considered either *distributive* or *collective*. The former indicates a universal concept so far as it is a mark common to the lower ones; the second indicates the taking together of | many. Since the collection of all cannot always be carried out, the *omnitudo collectiva* is at times merely imagined.\nThe *principia subiectiva*, when they are weighed objectively, contradict one another. E.g. Everything has a ground contradicts this proposition: nothing is necessary through itself, etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:3937":["One is to think something as absolutely necessary, because everything that is, is necessary, but not everything can be hypothetically necessary. Yet one cannot think anything absolutely necessary. One must think the world as bounded, but one cannot think the boundaries either.\nThose synthetic propositions which by their nature do not [through] cancel the same conditions of comprehensibility which they posit, hold objectively, as of the *commercium substantiarum*.",[]],"reflm:3938":["Besides the determinations without which objects cannot be, there are in our reason further conditions without which we cannot think certain objects through reason, even if these conditions are not determinations of the objects themselves. These conditions are therefore subjective, and their concepts signify nothing in the object. All synthetic judgments of pure reason are accordingly subjective, and the concepts themselves signify relations of the actions of reason to itself.",[]],"reflm:3939":["Logic treats of the objective laws of reason, that is, of how it ought to proceed. Metaphysics treats of the subjective laws of pure reason, of how it proceeds. In both, reason is the object, for the (g rational) form is in every science, of philosophy and of mathematics. That reason wants to treat, according to its laws, of the laws according to which it ought to think, brings it about that it makes use of rules which it first wants to invent. Hence come the tautologies and the rules which are without practice.",[]],"reflm:3940":["In all knowledge of reason only relations are to be considered, and these are either given (sensibly or through the nature of reason) or invented. But we can invent no relations, (g of) whose possibility we can be convinced, except as to magnitude, by repetition, in the science of number. To invent in regard to the quality of the relations, and therefore to invent (g first) concepts of reason, is just as impossible as to invent first sensations of the senses.",[]],"reflm:3941":["Metaphysical concepts relate 1. merely to the relation of coordination: (g *absolutum et relativum*, whole, part, *continuum*, *discretum*) (g many) some, all (g the first, the last, a single one); 2. or of subordination in the logical sense: universal or particular; 3. to subordination in the real sense: ground, consequence, cause, effect. From this arises the concept of the first cause, of the last consequence, of the cause of all, of some. 4. to existence: necessary, contingent (g possible); 5. Substance. (g subject, predicate) simple, composite, *actio*, *passio* (g *vis*, *receptivitas*), *spontanea*, [*coacta*] *iners*. (g Whole of substances. World.)\nTime and space.\n| Duration, moment, eternity, beginning, end. Change, persistence. | Place, extension, point. | (g Space and time:) Motion, rest. Omnipresence.\nEffect in space: filling, force, mass.\nEffect in time: change, creation, annihilation.",[]],"reflm:3942":["A mode of knowledge is true which agrees with the constitution of the object. Because the representation of outer objects is possible only through the idea of space, all the axioms of space, and what is derived from them, agree with the object, as do all relations of concepts according to the rule of identity. For the ideas then agree with one another. But since the metaphysical concepts of ground, substance, etc. are not properly representations of objects, inasmuch as even the most perfect sense could sense these in no thing, and since without these relations things altogether, though not through our reason, can be represented, these concepts are not objective; hence in their axioms everything is subjective. Therefore, if they are falsely regarded (g as) objective, neither truth nor falsity holds of them. In general, if one wishes to find the concept of cause, one will find, apart from the relations of connection according to ideas of time, no explanation that does not involve a circle, and it appears to be nothing else.\nThe surest proof that they are not objective is that they stand in evident contradiction.",[]],"reflm:3943":["(g Metaphysics is the ground of the form of all philosophy.)\nThe utility of metaphysics [first in the] can best be seen in its actual (g natural) employment.\nThis utility is positive, in increasing insights;\nnegative, in preventing false metaphysics;\nin both respects the insight is objective, when one seeks thereby to acquire the manifold objects of knowledge, or subjective, when one seeks to know the limits of knowledge.\nIt is noble. The subjective insight can be certain, although the objective is uncertain.",[]],"reflm:3944":["The logical form of our modes of knowledge is to be distinguished from the metaphysical; the first is analysis, the second synthesis.",[]],"reflm:3945":["We judge rationally about all objects of outer and inner sense, in that we [the laws of] give a ground of them through universal laws of appearances. The first possible laws of appearances cannot be established through experience; their first grounds rest rather on pure reason: *physica pura et pnevmatologia* (pure physics and pneumatology).",[]],"reflm:3946":["M XXXXIII:\nAll pure philosophy is either logical or metaphysical. The former [judges] contains only the subordination of concepts under the sphere of others, either immediately, in judgments, or mediately, in inferences. [This] It leaves, however, the concepts themselves, which can be subordinated to one another, undetermined, and does not settle which predicates belong to things according to laws of pure reason. Hence to know the first predicates of things through pure reason is a matter for metaphysics, and consequently so too is the finding of the first fundamental concepts with which we judge through pure reason, and of the principles.\nM XXXXIV:\n(s Metaphysics is concerned with modes of knowledge solely through reason, logic with all of them, even the empirical. All concepts of reason are universal; logic shows only the relation of the universal to the particular in general. Metaphysics, however, shows the origin of universal concepts, to which all knowledge must be traced back if appearances are to be transformed into concepts.)\nM XXXXIII:\nMetaphysics is therefore a science of the fundamental concepts and principles of human reason, and not of human knowledge in general, in which there is much that is empirical and sensible; logic is a science of the relations of universal concepts and propositions in general. Logic borrows concepts and propositions from metaphysics or from some empirical knowledge, and teaches how to make use of them.\nLogic contains the concept of the universal, metaphysics universal concepts of reason (s the former deals with the relation of universal concepts to one another, the second indicates the universal |\nconcepts under which [other things] the concepts of reason of things are contained; the former is logical theory, for logical practice is tautological unless knowledge of the subject is presupposed); logic [has] propositions which express the relation of the universal to the particular, without predicate and subject, metaphysics, however, universal propositions. Metaphysics contains the elements of which all rational knowledge is composed.\nM XXXXIV:\nLogic leaves the particular nature of human reason undetermined and is valid for any reason whatever; metaphysics shows the universal concepts which flow from the nature of human reason, and their particular laws.",[]],"reflm:3947":["All rational knowledge contains either the first grounds of that which is an object of merely pure knowledge, or the first grounds of appearance. Modes of knowledge that are valid for everyone and rest on mere concepts are dogmatic and objective.",[]],"reflm:3948":["If the question were whether new discoveries are still to be hoped for in the empirical sciences, there is no doubt of it, since more experiences give more and new modes of knowledge. If the question is whether new discoveries are to be expected in the science of pure reason, it is clear that in mathematics, logic and pure morals many are possible, because, according to the rule of identity, consequences [will] be able to be inferred from universal concepts in all sorts of arbitrarily proposed combinations. But with metaphysics it is quite otherwise. Here the question is not what flows from the universal to the particular, by the rule of identity, in arbitrarily assumed |\nrelations, but which relations actually lie at the ground of universal rules in the first place. Now since these relations are given neither through the senses in experience, nor through the understanding in an intuitive and singular concept, nothing further can be done than to analyse one's confused ideas. From this, however, only a science of the subject can arise. Since no object is given here, we should also be able to discover nothing about it by this cutting up and analysis of our ideas.",[]],"reflm:3949":["Under the logical rules stand mathematics and morals (*purae*, pure); under the metaphysical rules stands the pure knowledge of what falls within outer and inner sense, and consequently the first rational grounds of outer and inner appearances. In both one must consider that which is most universal in outer and inner sense according to its first rational grounds.\nThus there is no philosophy of pure reason other than logic and *metaphysica*.\nThe fundamental concept of logic is that of the universal, in so far as it contains something under itself or not; in accordance with this relation things are thought, but in it they are not represented. The concept of species or genus is not given, but is only the relation according to which *conceptus dabiles* (concepts that can be given) can be compared.\nLogic gives no fundamental concepts of reason at all, but [only] those of laws, according to which we [ourselves] make modes of knowledge in general distinct through one another. It therefore contains the rules without which we cannot obtain distinct knowledge of things; metaphysics, however, contains the rules without which objects cannot be known by us at all. The logical propositions are [arbi] rules which we use arbitrarily, as means, to make modes of knowledge distinct by comparison. Metaphysics shows the marks [Merkmale] which are given through the nature of reason (logic only the use of marks in general).",[]],"reflm:3950":["Synthetic [propositions] concepts are space, time and number. [and are objective.]\nIf the concepts are synthetic, but the propositions analytic, i.e. according to the rule of identity, then they are objective.\nBut if concepts are subjective, i.e. concepts of no object at all, being neither of a given condition of the representation of objects (space and time) nor [of] arbitrary concepts of quantity, the judgments are nevertheless objective and universally valid, i.e. either universally true or false, [but are] if their form is analytic. But if [then] their form is synthetic, they are subjective, and hold objectively only as rules of appearances, consequently as empirical judgments.",[]],"reflm:3951":["Dogmatic *philosophia pura* (pure philosophy) contains two disciplines.\n1. The rules of the universally valid use of the understanding;\n2. The rules of the universally valid use of the free will.\nIn both, judgment proceeds according to the *nexus logicus* (logical connection) from no data of experience; but in the *physiologia transscendentalis* (transcendental physiology) the data from experience are so given, according to the relations of space and time, that the most universal concept of the object of all outer and inner sensations is taken and its ground sought.",[]],"reflm:3952":["Metaphysics is a science of the laws of pure human reason, and is therefore subjective. Objective pure philosophy |\nhas either analytic *principia* (principles) without any axioms of experience, or synthetic ones. The former [consists] rests on universal judgments according to the rule of identity and on the subordination of the particular under the universal. These are, therefore, the universally valid rules of understanding and will, and consequently *Logica et Moralis pura* (pure logic and pure morals). The second has as its object the most universal element of outer and inner sense, and so contains the pure rational grounds to which all the natural sciences of outer and inner sense can be brought.",[]],"reflm:3953":["The principles of space are objective; but whether a thing is in space or not is subjective, because the concept of space is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3954":["A principal error arises from this, when one confounds [compares] the *genus* with all the *individua* and regards as a consequence according to concepts what is only a lawful appearance for immediate intuition. Hence the ground is nothing objective. What we call a (g real) ground is only a concomitant appearance for the intuitive concept. And what we call reason, and likewise all its pure concepts, in which we infer from the universal to the particular, have their reality, especially in respect of their fundamental concepts, only in that they lie in things and can be abstracted from them. Accordingly our reason is only a supplement to the lack of *intuitus* (intuition).\nIf, then, I say that a principle is subjective, i.e. that it contains the conditions under which alone we can judge through our reason according to laws of experience, this does not mean that our reason must assume this law in the objects; for it does not bear at all upon the same [dieselbe: antecedent unclear]; one can therefore say of it neither that it is false nor that it is true.",[]],"reflm:3955":["All modes of knowledge are either empirical, so far as they presuppose sensations, or pure modes of knowledge, so far as they have no sensation as their ground. The latter, namely the pure modes of knowledge, are either [singular] *conceptus singulares* (singular concepts) and are entitled *intuitus puri* (pure intuitions), or universal [concepts] and are pure concepts of reason. The empirical modes of knowledge are sensation, appearance and empirical concept. From the first the matter of all knowledge is to be derived; the second adds the form of intuition; the third brings both under a universal concept.",[]],"reflm:3956":["Immediate judgments of experience, according to relations of space and time, must be distinguished from reflected judgments, according to relations of reason.",[]],"reflm:3957":["M XXXXVII:\nAll human modes of knowledge can be divided into two main kinds: 1. those which spring from the senses [or] and are entitled empirical; 2. those which are not acquired through the senses [in the] at all, but have their ground in the constant nature of the [faculty of knowledge] thinking power of the soul, and can be entitled pure representations. Since all materials for thinking must necessarily be given through our senses, the matter of our entire knowledge is empirical. For this very reason all pure concepts must relate solely to the form of the modes of knowledge. Now we have a twofold form of the modes of knowledge: the intuitive and the rational form. The first is found only in the immediate knowledge of individual things, the second in universal representations; the first I shall entitle intuitive concepts, the second concepts of reason. Now in all empirical knowledge we can look, first, |\nsolely to the matter, and this consists in sensation; second, to the form of appearance [and this consists]; third, to the form of reason in concepts. The form of appearances rests solely upon space and time, and these concepts do not spring from any senses or sensation, but rest on the nature of the mind, in accordance with which the different sensations can be represented under such relations. Hence, if all sensation of the senses is set aside, [the concept] of space and time is a pure concept of intuition; and since it contains everything that the understanding can know in experiences, it is a concept of the understanding; and although the appearances are empirical, it is nevertheless intellectual. In the same way, sensations and appearances made universal are not pure but empirical concepts of reason; if, however, we leave out every effect of the senses, the concepts are pure concepts of reason, such as: possible, substance, etc. Hence all pure concepts are either intellectual and intuitive, or rational and [discursive] reflecting concepts. |\nM XXXXVIII: |\nFurther, all modes of knowledge are either given or invented. The matter of knowledge cannot be invented, hence only the form, and in the form only the repetition; thus all invention of reason relates only to mathematics; the form, on the other hand, which is given in geometry, is space.\nM XXXXVII:\n(g Since one can describe only the form of experience, it belongs to the understanding.)\nM XXXXVIII:\nA science of pure reason is possible and also necessary. It is, however, either philosophy or mathematics; the former considers what there is, and reason alone considers what *respectus* (relation) there is; the latter considers how many times the same thing is. Space is an object for philosophy as well as for mathematics. The judgments about space which are intuitive are not yet mathematical, nor yet philosophical. Both judge space not\n|\n*a priori* [through the Ver-] from principles of space which reason knows, but from principles *a posteriori*, that is, intuitively and singly, though not empirically. The philosophy of pure reason is either dogmatic, and then its object is given through that which all the senses have most in common, and, as it were, through the *genus* of the senses: so, first, the power of representation and the faculty of desire, as the objects of inner sense; 2. space and time, and thereby general mechanics, as the most general [object] of outer senses; hence dogmatic pure philosophy is theoretical logic, theoretical morals and general natural science. Second, it is critical, and therefore subjective. It is zetetic, sceptical, problematic.",[]],"reflm:3958":["All modes of knowledge [from experience are either] from experience (g empirical) belong either to sensation and contain the matter of empirical knowledge, or to appearance and contain at the same time the form, or to the concept and contain [what many objects] the universal of different sensations or appearances. Sensation represents individual objects, so far as they affect the senses, e.g. red, black, sweet, hard, warm, etc., [which] consequently [is] only the matter of empirical knowledge. The form of objects is thought according to space and time. The form of empirical knowledge is that of coordination, that of rational knowledge is that of subordination.\nLet all matter of knowledge be left out, and consequently everything that affects the senses; there still remains the empirical form of appearances. Let this also be left out, and the rational form remains; |\nand modes of knowledge of the first kind are pure concepts of intuitions, those of the second pure concepts of reason.",[]],"reflm:3959":["Metaphysics contains nothing but ontology*, which is falsely regarded as a science of things in general *quoad praedicata universalia et disiunctiva* (as regards universal and disjunctive predicates).\n(g for there is no matter, and consequently the object is a something in general.)",[]],"reflm:3960":["All modes of knowledge are either sensible or modes of knowledge of reason. The former are either of sensation [text breaks off].",[]],"reflm:3961":["All (g modes of knowledge from) experience are entitled empirical and are either [representations of sensation or of appearances, senses or] sensations or appearances or concepts. In the first, everything is given through sense [in the second] and is merely the matter for knowledge; the second contains within it the sensations together with the form of space and time; the third [contains] the sensations or appearances made universal through reason.\nIf we leave out the last two acts, sensation remains. If we leave this out, there remain pure concepts: 1. of the understanding: of coordination; 2. of reason: of subordination.",[]],"reflm:3962":["All concepts become universal through abstraction, but they do not all ori|ginate\nfrom it; *non subtrahendo a sensibus oriuntur, sed abstrahendo* (they arise not by subtracting from the senses, but by abstracting).",[]],"reflm:3963":["Modes of knowledge of reason are either given in the senses as to their matter and have only the form of reason, e.g. universal concepts; or they express the form of reason itself. The former are empirical, the latter *notiones purae* (pure notions).",[]],"reflm:3964":["Metaphysics is a critique of pure reason and not a doctrine. Logic is the doctrine of 1. pure reason, 2. mixed reason. The application of pure reason to objects that are given through experience occurs only with empirical concepts, where we can infer from the universal to the particular.\nThe science of a mode of knowledge considered subjectively is critique; considered objectively, it is doctrine.\nThere are certain universal concepts, given through the nature of reason, in accordance with which others (g and their relation) are thought. [There are, however, others] E.g. subject and predicate. These are metaphysical. There are representations which are given through the senses and are made universal through reason.",[]],"reflm:3965":["There are pure concepts of intuitions, arbitrary concepts of invention, and universal concepts of reason.\nA concept which cannot be regarded as an impression of the senses is pure.\nPrinciples:\n[1. What we do not through intuitions] 1. We know through judgments everything that we do not know through intuitions.\n2. All judgments of our reason are mediate.",[]],"reflm:3966":["The logical essence is the subjective fundamental concept and does not hold for all; it is also changeable. The real essence is objective. The former relates to the meaning of a word, which indeed wears down gradually and becomes uniform through usage.",[]],"reflm:3967":["Reason feels the need of a real ground and yet cannot think it according to its own laws; from this it is to be seen that this concept is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3968":["All connection is either of subordination or of coordination [the former is either of]. The unity, when a consequence is subordinated to many grounds, is unity *a priori*, e.g. in the utility of things. The second: when something is the ground of many things, or when many consequences spring from one ground.\nIt is remarkable that when many grounds agree in one consequence, we also think a common ground for them, e.g. the many things serving the utility of man, the many members of an animal.",[]],"reflm:3969":["Our reason contains nothing but *relationes* (relations). Now if these are not given through the relations of space and time in experience, nor through repetition and the composition of the one out of many in pure mathematics, then they are not relations which concern objects, but only relations of our concepts according to the laws of our reason.",[]],"reflm:3970":["Intuitive concepts of the senses, concepts of the understanding of abstraction, concepts of reason of the [relation] relation, which universal modes of knowledge have.\nThe relations of the senses are space and time; those of the understanding are the universal and the particular, and consequently also all judgments and the rules of all inferences of reason. Those of reason are ground and consequence, namely objective: the ground of things (real ground).\nThe relation of identity and difference is logical (g likewise the relation of the universal and the particular); [all] that of repetition is mathematical; the remaining ones are metaphysical, namely the real relations.\nMetaphysics is the critique of human reason; logic is its universal doctrine. The former is subjective and problematic, the latter objective and dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:3971":["That is true which holds for every mode of knowledge, and therefore not merely under the condition of the reason that judges mediately. Accordingly that is true the opposite of which contradicts itself, since the opposite cannot even once fall within the senses. But that something is without ground indicates only a subjective condition, and the object could fall within the senses even without this condition.",[]],"reflm:3972":["The concept of ground (g of consequence) contains not merely that something which is there is accompanied by something else, but in addition that this relation is universal and necessary; for where such a thing (g b) is, there is such a ground a, and where an [ground] a is, there is the consequence b. Now all real grounds, and even the possibility of them, can be known only *a posteriori*; these, however, show indeed a constant |\naccompaniment, but no universality of connection; consequently the concept of ground is not objective.",[]],"reflm:3973":["No other merely arbitrary concepts of pure reason can arise in us than those that arise through repetition, and consequently those of numbers and magnitude. All [other] concepts of reason that contain some other relation require that this relation be given through experience or through the nature of understanding and reason; empirical relations, however, presuppose a fundamental concept of relation. The absolute [can] and the simple cannot be invented.",[]],"reflm:3974":["In the use of pure reason a peculiar confusion arises from the mixing up of these two concepts. Everything (*singuli*) has a ground; but everything taken together cannot have a ground, so something is without ground. The first is a serviceable rule of reason for the explanation of appearances; the second is a consequence of the *synthesis completa* (complete synthesis), which is impossible for our understanding. Likewise: everything that is to be posited through reason is necessary; but everything is necessary under its *hypothesis*.\nAll concepts are either sensible concepts or concepts of reason. The former are either of sensation or of appearance; these have space and time as the ground of their form. The latter cannot be found through any *analysis* of experience, although all experience is coordinated with them, and they are pure concepts of reason if through them no object of experience is thought; but if the latter is the case, they are empirical concepts. For example, a *genus* is a pure concept, but a stone in general, or the *genus* of stone, is an empirical one.\nThe rational science of the rules of judging objectively, that is, of all [concepts] judgments and inferences, so far as they arise *per analysin* (by analysis), is logic. The rational science of [analytic] synthetic modes of knowledge and judgments is metaphysics.\nSpace is no concept of reason, but metaphysics seeks the concept of reason for it.",[]],"reflm:3975":["Since through reason, that is, through the knowledge which is not sensation, only concepts arise, through which it remains undetermined whether the thing is posited or not, something further is required which constrains us to posit something. Hence the concept of a ground has a subjective origin, and the expression: something is posited through another thing or for the sake of another, signifies objectively only: things accompany one another or follow upon one another.\nThe idea of ground (g cause) arises from experiences.\nTo know through reason that something is not, or that something is not possible, is one and the same; that something is, or that something is necessary, is one and the same. On the other hand everything appears as contingent of which I indeed know through the senses, but not through reason, that it is.\nThe only concepts of pure reason through whose relation truths can be discovered according to the rule of identity are magnitudes; but these too are arbitrary inventions.",[]],"reflm:3976":["According to the subjective law of reason it is necessary to assume a first action, from which all the rest follows; but it is just as necessary to assume a ground in general of every action, and therefore no first.\nThere are pure fundamental concepts of intuition or of reflection; the former are the principles of appearance, the latter of insight; the former show coordination, the latter subordination. Since everything is represented in time, all our concepts of reason are nevertheless always at the same time thought under the condition of the *phaenomenon*; the conditions of the |\nlatter do not agree with those of the former. In time no first is possible, and in the ground there is supposed to be one.\nIf the comprehensibility of a [action] thing were to be complete, we should have to have a first ground; but we cannot posit a first ground through reason either, and from this it follows that the comprehensibility of no absolute position is complete through human reason.\nThe simple concepts of reason, indeed all simple concepts, are subjective; the objective ones consist in the agreement of knowledge with itself and are therefore composite. Hence the concept of the first is subjective, for it contains the concept of everything.\nAll real relations (space and time excepted) are given through experience (g according to the relations of space and time) and can therefore serve for no propositions of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:3977":["All truth consists in the necessary agreement of a mode of knowledge with itself. If the knowledge which is to agree with itself constitutes the form of appearance itself, for example space and time, then every judgment is [subjec] objective and either true or false. But if the knowledge concerns merely a law of human reason by which we compare concepts, then it is not objective at all, and therefore neither true nor false. Hence ground and consequence are not a property of things given through mere reason at all, but are given only through experience. It is, however, a law of reason to look to this relation; all universal rules of reason concerning cause and effect have no validity of objects at all.",[]],"reflm:3978":["Every objective concept is either an archetype (*archetypon*) or |\na copy (*Ectypon*). The former occurs when the concept of the object is thought arbitrarily, or at least when without it no concept of objects [mo] and no experience is possible. The latter occurs when the object is given through the senses. The pure concepts of reason, which are given through the nature of reason, are not copies, and secondly not archetypes either, for they are not thought arbitrarily, but contain the ground of the possibility of all arbitrary ideas of reason. They are also universal concepts; consequently they do not contain the form of experiences, and without them immediate [representations] ideas of experience are always possible; consequently they are only concepts of conditions, without which a mode of knowledge through our reason is impossible, and therefore not objective.\nAll modes of knowledge of reason have the logical form, but the metaphysical form only those [in] which presuppose real fundamental concepts or principles of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:3979":["M XXXXI:\nA dogmatic science of pure reason has arbitrary ideas as its ground, and it thinks their relations in logical form. But no relations can be thought arbitrarily except those of repetition, that is, of numbers, and consequently the science is mathematics; but one can also seek the rule of the power of choice (*Willkühr*) itself, so far as it is to be universally valid, and this is morals.\nWhere the concepts are given neither through experience nor through the nature of reason, there the power of choice is their ground, either as to form, when no matter (g object) is given at all (the arbitrary form of concepts, however, is only the mathematical), |\nM XXXXII: |\nor the (g pure) power of choice (g in general) is itself the matter (g the |\nobject) and form for the concept, and the judgments are in both cases passed according to logical form. In mathematics it runs: I will think these relations to myself; what rules follow from them according to the logical form. In morals: I will think the power of choice itself in relations; what rules hold for it in general. Thus mathematics is the [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:3980":["If I wish to understand something in nature, I must not go outside nature with my explanation. But if I wish to understand the whole of nature, I must be outside its limits.",[]],"reflm:3981":["Pure concepts of reason have no *exemplaria* [exemplars]; they are themselves the archetypes [Urbilder]. But the concepts of our pure reason have this reason itself as their archetype, and are therefore subjective and not objective.",[]],"reflm:3982":["The logical laws of reason contain the rules in accordance with which concepts are subordinated, the one to the sphere of the other; this is entitled logical subordination. The metaphysical laws are those in accordance with which concepts are *realiter* [really] subordinated to one another.",[]],"reflm:3983":["All concepts are either archetypes, which [concep-] are grounds of the determinations that belong to objects, and through which the object itself is determined among all possible [objects]; or they are copies [Nachbilder], which |\nare consequences of the determinations of things. Since all concepts of experience are possible only through space and time, so breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3984":["Everything that happens, that is, what we are compelled [through] before experience to know as happening, has a ground; but what one wills to happen has no further ground. For every object that is determined through choice [Willkühr] has no further ground why it is thought thus and not otherwise than this choice. Since something first originates through the will, the question can just as little be raised in the case of arbitrary ideas which are a ground of actions as in the case of arbitrary speculative ideas, breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3985":["We can posit nothing through reason without subordinating it to something else. We cannot think any subordination without the ground being thought as antecedent [*antecedens*], and hence the whole series being thought *per synthesin a priori completam* [through a complete synthesis *a priori*].\nNo synthesis is *completa* [complete] save that which is completed by advancing successively [*successive progrediendo*] in a time. If, then, a whole is given, we posit that, in regressing [*regrediendo*], a *terminus* [limit] will be found from which the complete synthesis begins; and consequently that a whole is neither infinitely divisible nor infinitely composite. But infinite synthesis [*synthesis infinita*] (eternity) never yields a whole.",[]],"reflm:3986":["One can assume that the motion of a body is merely the successive presence in space |\nof a great efficacy of impenetrability [*impenetrabilitas*]. On this view the substance does not change its place; rather this effect of impenetrability succeeds itself in different places one after another, as in the case of sound the waves of air. One can also assume that there are no substances at all in space, but only a greater or lesser efficacy of a single supreme cause in different places of space. From this it would follow that matter would be infinitely divisible.",[]],"reflm:3987":["The concept of God is the only idea which is thoroughly determined through reason, and which is also given as the supreme ground of all determination.",[]],"reflm:3988":["[In our judgments we attend either only to the idea of a thing alone and to that through which we think it, or to its relation to others.]\nWe have two kinds of concepts: those that can arise in us through the presence of the thing; and those [which] through which the understanding represents to itself the relation of these concepts to the laws of its own thinking. To the latter belong the concept of ground, |\nof possibility, of existence. Hence the principles concerning the former are objective, those concerning the latter subjective.\nMetaphysics is a science of insight into the relation of human reason to the first properties of things.\nAll rational fundamental concepts are concepts of form; the empirical are *principia* of matter. The former are merely subjective, that is, abstracted from the [action] laws of our thinking; the latter are objective, abstracted from the representation itself through which the object is represented. The understanding is applied to experiences only in accordance with laws of the understanding; but the abstracted idea of the relation of the representation of sensation in general, according to laws of the understanding, constitutes the pure rational concept. The understanding proceeds according to a natural law when it thinks one and many. This understanding, applied to the sensation of a body [abstrac-], does not abstract from the body the idea of a whole, but from itself.\nPure philosophy [*philosophia pura*] concerned with rational concepts is only subjective and never synthetic; the *principia* of applied philosophy at home [*philosophiae applicatae domestica*] are the first laws of experience in combination with the *principia rationalia* [rational principles], so far as these relate to the *empirica* [empirical]. The proposition 'nothing comes from nothing' and the proposition 'everything has a ground' have the same fault, that they are expressed rationally, although they hold only empirically.\nThe idea of an undetermined freedom cannot be thought at all in accordance with the laws of our understanding; it is not, however, on that account false.\nBy what do we recognise the plurality and unity of substances? The representation of the I is really a unity; but is difference of places a proof of plurality?\nIf one merely observes breaks off.",[]],"reflm:3989":["A–A=0 signifies an empty representation, for what is negating is not |\nempty; whether, however, an empty representation signifies as much as something impossible, is open to question.",[]],"reflm:3990":["If I first posit something and afterwards cancel it, the result is 0 (g *rei*); if I posit and cancel at one and the same time, the result is [0 *cogitationis*\n. . . 0], the impossible. The impossible does not signify merely a *conceptus inanis* [empty concept], in which nothing at all is thought; for it is possible to think nothing at all.",[]],"reflm:3991":["Through the *principium contradictionis* [principle of contradiction] what is known is not the possibility of things, but that of their predicates, or of their relation.",[]],"reflm:3992":["Affirmative judgments serve for knowledge of the identity of a thing with others, negative judgments for distinction. Hence tautological propositions are ridiculous.\nReason contains nothing but *respectivae notiones* [relative notions]; the senses, since they know without reflection, must give something absolute.\nWe must here take great care that reason judges concerning itself according to its own laws, and consequently not always objectively.",[]],"reflm:3993":["From the nature of our understanding flows the idea of limits, that is, of the first and the last.",[]],"reflm:3994":["There are two concepts which are properly not predicates of things, but through which the things themselves are thought with all their predicates.",[]],"reflm:3995":["In metaphysics, as in optics, we must take care that combinations or separations of concepts, the ground of which is subjective, are not held to be objective.",[]],"reflm:3996":["There are two principles of the objective laws of reason, and these are analytic; there are two principles that are subjective, and these are synthetic. The latter are principles of every [use] real use of reason: they are the rules according to which we ought to use reason in all real relations, even in dealing with the senses. These are falsely regarded as principles of pure reason, which are objective.",[]],"reflm:3997":["Over and \n*Conceptus ponens* [positing concept]: A; *tollens* [cancelling]: non A; [*deficiens* [deficient]\n= A×0] *deficiens*\n=0; *conceptus inanis* [empty concept]: to which no object corresponds, e.g. *vis quaedam ficta* [some fictitious force]. *Conceptus implicans* [self-contradictory concept] = A non A (non A + non A). *Ens ponens*: A; *ens tollens*: –A; *ens deficiens*: A–A=0. *Realiter oppositum sibi non contradictorium, et impossibile diversum est a non possibili* [that which is really opposed to itself is not contradictory, and the impossible is different from the not-possible].",[]],"reflm:3998":["A proposition can be immediately certain; but its immediacy can be uncertain (g hidden), as in: a whole is greater than a part.\nThere are concepts which are problematic for reason, others which are dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:3999":["Because we form rational concepts only through judgments, and in these the thing is represented through predicates, we represent to ourselves an object of thought, and its being or not-being specially as a predicate; if we abstract from this [and the], there still remains an object of thought, which, so far as it does not cancel itself, is something possible.\nThe possibility of judgments is contradistinguished from truth, and the possibility of things from actuality.\nThe contradictions of pure reason arise from this, that it does not, like empirical philosophy, draw the universal propositions from particular ones, but judges universally from (g pure) concepts, and yet nevertheless requires a *terminus* [a priori] (g of subordination or coordination) which contradicts the universality.\nThrough reason we can completely know only the possibility of judgments, not that of things, since the latter requires the presupposition of the possibility of matter, which must be given through the senses and therefore *a posteriori*. As regards matter, the understanding cannot even invent anything impossible. The falsity of the form in the synthesis can be established by nothing but the conflict of the consequences with other certain modes of knowledge.\nPossibility is only a form, but the possible contains matter also.",[]],"reflm:4000":["Some things hold objectively under an arbitrary condition, others subjectively under a condition given (g through the understanding). These given conditions are appearances (space and time lie at the basis every|where\n). Hence, although one of two propositions, 'There is a first cause' or 'There is none', would have to be true if the concepts of ground and cause were objective, yet since neither of the two is objective, both are true at once as subjective laws.",[]],"reflm:4001":["It is hard to represent how the consequence follows upon the ground, if this relation is not merely a *phaenomenon* [appearance].",[]],"reflm:4002":["Where there are nothing but real connections [Realverknüpfungen], there is no 0 and also no change. All changes in the world therefore spring from real oppositions [Realoppositionen]; the final effect of these is therefore 0.",[]],"reflm:4003":["Impossibility of propositions and impossibility of judgments. If I include the condition of impossibility in the concept of the thing, then it is absolute impossibility according to concepts or according to experience.",[]],"reflm:4004":["Every concept is possible in which there lies a predicate that is considered in the subject and does not contradict it. But not every synthesis in which there is no contradiction is possible; that is to say, real relations are not seen through the principle of contradiction.",[]],"reflm:4005":["Through reason we can know only conditioned possibility. The absolute possibility that we know through reason |\ntakes place in accordance with the *principium contradictionis* [principle of contradiction] and concerns propositions into which we resolve the concepts.\nAll absolute possibility must otherwise be given through experience; what is, is possible in itself.",[]],"reflm:4006":["In the case of all elementary representations we can borrow possibility only from actuality, because the former is given to thought only through the latter.\nIf something under certain conditions always happens in one and the same way, and yet has in these conditions no natural ground, then it must have its ground in a purpose or in freedom; e.g. when the game [Spiel] always falls to one side. Freedom is therefore a first ground, and is not included among what happens.",[]],"reflm:4007":["Of everything that is, we must know a ground, if we wish to know through reason that it is*; hence we cannot know the absolutely necessary. In everything subordinate we must assume a first, which is therefore necessary in itself**; hence there is a conflict of subjective laws.\n*(s Hence it is a subjective rule of reason. 2. Everything that (g contingent) is an object of experience, (happens), has a ground: this is an objective rule of [senses] experience.)\n**(s A principle which we assume only because otherwise we could not see something a priori.)",[]],"reflm:4008":["Everything that (g comes to be) happens is through something; this is similar to the proposition: Everything that comes to be is from something. From nothing comes nothing, that is, substance cannot come to be. This only indicates that our understanding is valid only *in consequentia* [in the sphere of consequence], and that in all explanations of nature we must assume that substance always is. Arising and perishing, coming to be and ceasing, are also bound to time and hold only of phenomena.\nIt is also a law of our reason that the supreme cause (one or many) always is, and can neither arise nor perish. This also includes in itself the relation of time.",[]],"reflm:4009":["In this demonstration, 1. the word nothing in the logical sense (which signifies as much as the universal negation in the judgment, and affects the copula of a universal judgment) is confounded with the nothing, namely the removal [remotion] of things. E.g. no thing has a ground; nothing has a ground; and: the nothing is the ground of some things.\nThe author has done the same with the *definitio* of the *minimum* [definition of the minimum].",[]],"reflm:4010":["Everything has a highest logical ground\nand a last logical consequence.\n(g The former is the most general concept, which stands under none; the latter is the singular, under which none is contained.)",[]],"reflm:4011":["It is a necessary hypothesis of sound reason, and so holds |\nonly in the world, and is also the principle of the use of reason in the world. 'Everything has a first ground' is likewise such a principle, because it begins a posteriori; but to begin a priori from the first ground, and to posit it itself, is an act of original reason.",[]],"reflm:4012":["The *principium rationis* [principle of the ground], likewise the *principium rationati* [principle of the grounded], is a rule of sound reason and is therefore restricted to objects of experience. The limits of the world of sense are therefore also the limits of its use. If one goes further and wishes to make their validity universal, one errs and brings it into science.",[]],"reflm:4013":["Everything that is has a ground: this is a merely subjective principle of reason and has no reference to the possibility of appearances. 'What happens has a ground' means that, in regard to it, I am passive and observe it. But what I do voluntarily is not, for me, something that happens. (s It is a conditional proposition of the reality of our |\nrepresentations, which is opposed to their nullity, namely to fantasies. If the representation of an object is not to be [given] made by us but to be given, then it must be posited according to [all] a universal rule under certain conditions, whereby the relation of this object to the representation, and the existence of the same, is valid also for others. It is quite otherwise when the representation is made by us (through freedom); for then we are immediately conscious of its reality; that is, a state in us which we ourselves make can be known as a true state, even without a ground.\nSomething for [wovor] to know, to understand, to see into, to comprehend. We know that which is given to us, for [wovor] something; that is, we give its representations an object through the function of the understanding.\nWhen, where, why. Accidents and composition are not things.)",[]],"reflm:4014":["Everything that arises has a ground;\neverything that perishes has also a positive ground.",[]],"reflm:4015":["'All' [Alles] signifies either sufficiency (g of derivation) a priori, or the completeness of the taking-together a posteriori; e.g. we can say something in general of all motion, but we cannot know all motion together.",[]],"reflm:4016":["Knowledge of the logical essence is acquired merely analytically, that of the real essence synthetically. The former is the first subjective fundamental concept, the latter the first objective. In *conceptibus arbitrariis qua talibus* [in arbitrary concepts as such] the subjective and the objective are one and the same.",[]],"reflm:4017":["Logical predicates are concepts through which I can know or posit certain things. Accordingly all concepts are predicates; but they signify either things or their positing. The former is a real predicate, the latter only a logical one.\nConcepts are indeterminate in regard to many predicates, but things are not. Hence thoroughgoing determination also attaches to actuality, and is found more in existence than in possibility. This follows also from the fact that through possibility I posit merely the concept, through actuality the thing. If God takes existence from a thing, he takes from it no predicate, but the thing itself (s though not the possibility, or [its] the concept of the thing). Whoever denies existence removes the thing with all its predicates.\nExistence can indeed be a logical, but never a real predicate of a thing.",[]],"reflm:4018":["Existential propositions express how we are to think things, not what [they are]; that is to say, absolutely, not relatively.",[]],"reflm:4019":["An object is given in so far as it is thoroughly determined. If it is thoroughly given through its concept, it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:4020":["Existence [Daseyn] applies only to things; actuality [Wirklichkeit] applies also to their actions.",[]],"reflm:4021":["We can represent nothing to ourselves as possible except what has been given in the senses, both in its *data* and in its real relations. Our possibilities are therefore merely modifications of that whose universal ground is given through experience.",[]],"reflm:4022":["The proposition that a possible necessarily exists means as much as *sublata omni actualitate tollitur possibilitas* [if all actuality is taken away, possibility is taken away]; whether this latter proposition is true must be investigated.",[]],"reflm:4023":["If every thing [is] only *in nexu omnimodo* [in complete connection] with all the determinations of the supreme cause, and therefore also with its whole effect, while the whole effect of that being is only a single one: then everything that does not actually exist is also not possible *in totum* [as a whole].",[]],"reflm:4024":["Magnitude is known either through composition or through the limitation of a given All. The first always requires time.\nWhy is mathematics capable of such evident propositions and of such correctness? The origin of its concepts.\nOur faculty [Vermögen] of counting is (g in itself) unrestricted; if it is [kein] not limited, it is indeterminate; for it cannot be taken as a whole. But in the *realissimum* the *omnitudo* [allness] precedes the limits.",[]],"reflm:4025":["Something is considered *transcendentaliter* [transcendentally] when it is weighed in relation to its (s own) essence* as the consequence; *metaphysice* [metaphysically], when the essence is considered, in regard to its consequences, as a ground.\n*(s If it is considered in respect to the essence of things in general, it is entitled metaphysical.)",[]],"reflm:4026":["Transcendental unity.\nMetaphysical unity, truth and perfection. Agreement of one, as a ground, with the manifold; of the manifold with one, as consequence; and of the manifold among themselves.",[]],"reflm:4027":["Transcendental unity is logical, in relation to the essence of the thing.\nMetaphysical unity is real, in relation to possibility in general.\nThe former is the unity of the taking-together, wherein something does not contradict itself.\nThe latter is the unity of connection, wherein the many is a ground of One, and the unity is not arbitrary.",[]],"reflm:4028":["All finite things are defective as things in general (not transcendentally). One thing does indeed supplement the imperfection of another; but because it is another thing, it makes good the imperfection only in the whole.\nThe perfection of a thing relative to a certain concept, or [relative to] in regard to the concept of a thing in general, is the *completudo rationis* (completeness of ground); the former is the relative perfection, the latter the metaphysical.\nGoodness (*bonitas*) is the ground of perfection, *unum ad qvod consentitur* (the one to which assent is given).\nThe goodness of human beings is the moral. Relative goodness is that which is only a means to [absol] something else which pleases.",[]],"reflm:4029":["Perfection taken substantively or adjectively [betr].\nIn the latter case it is neither a quality nor a quantity nor a relation, but only the *modus* (mode) of a determination; *perfectio substantive sumta* (perfection taken substantively) is a determination in general, in so far as this mode belongs to it; in regard to *entitas* (entity), reality is that to which the mode of perfection belongs.",[]],"reflm:4030":["Necessity is bound up with every categorical position of reason.\nSomething is posited through concepts.\n*Necessarium et contingens* (the necessary and the contingent) is either absolute or hypothetical.\nLogical necessity or real necessity. The latter is absolute or hypothetical.\nNominal concept through omission of the condition.\n*oppositum entis sibi non contradicit* (the opposite of a being does not contradict itself). *Conceptus terminator* (limiting concept).",[]],"reflm:4031":["Law of reason, not of the senses:\nEverything that is, is necessary, either absolutely or conditionally.\nAll necessity is conditioned, but either under the condition of the inner determinations of a thing (of that which is inward in the thing) or under outer conditions.",[]],"reflm:4032":["Everything that happens is contingent in itself; but since it must nevertheless be necessary by virtue of the above, it is necessary through an extraneous ground.",[]],"reflm:4033":["All (g absolute) necessity is either of judgments or of things. The first, as the logical, is always a conditioned necessity of the predicate. The necessity of things which we can know is always conditioned; for in itself we can always remove any thing, since, in affirming nothing, we also contradict nothing through the negation. The concept of the necessary is nevertheless, in the first place, a concept given through reason, because through it alone something is determined. Absolute necessity is a limiting concept, because without it there would be no completeness in the series of the contingent. |\nThis limiting concept, however, is itself problematic and cannot be known a priori through reason, because it is a *conceptus terminator* (limiting concept); nor can it be understood a posteriori, for that is not to understand, nor [can it be known] immediately; it is therefore problematic.\nThe supreme principle of everything contingent is the absolutely necessary, and the supreme principle (g of the possibility) of origination is freedom. It is thought that, in regard to a freely acting being, every state that is in its power constitutes a *terminus a priori* (a term a priori) which is not connected with the preceding ones by a natural link. It is itself no link of a greater chain, at least not as regards activity.",[]],"reflm:4034":["The actuality of what is *omnimode contingens* (contingent in every respect; free action) is as little to be comprehended as that of what is *omnimode necessarium* (necessary in every respect). [The latter] The latter, because, if the *necessitas* is mediate, the first necessity is not to be understood; the former, because [if the contingency is only relative] the existence of such a thing is not to be comprehended.",[]],"reflm:4035":["All necessity is a necessity of judgments or of things. In the former, if I wish to think the opposite, I retain the subject and remove its identical predicate; in the latter, if I wish to think the opposite, I remove the thing with all its predicates. Hence in the former case I can know the (g absolute) necessity from the principle of contradiction, but in the latter case I cannot.",[]],"reflm:4036":["Not necessity, but contingency is incomprehensible to rea|son.\nThis idea of contingency is either logical (in the case of general concepts which are undetermined in regard to particular determinations) or consists in this, that I can remove the thing; this possibility, however, proves too much.",[]],"reflm:4037":["Inwardly contingent is what is contingent when one abstracts from all conditions; absolutely contingent is what (g either has no conditions or) is contingent even with all conditions, because they are the conditions.\nAbsolutely necessary is what is necessary even under necessary conditions and is therefore outwardly necessary. Inner or outer necessity makes no difference in this concept, but only in the conditions of it.\nNothing is absolutely necessary except what is necessarily connected with something inwardly necessary. Absolutely contingent is what is contingent under all conditions; such a thing one must deny.",[]],"reflm:4038":["Above and at the column heading »Necessarium et contingens«:\nAbsolute necessity is more than hypothetical.\nAbsolute contingency is less than hypothetical,\nbecause absolute possibility is the smallest.\n(g The *omnimoda contingentia* (contingency in every respect))",[]],"reflm:4039":["The concept of an absolutely necessary [being] is a *conceptus terminator* (limiting concept) (because we must regard everything contingent as necessary through a ground, and finally the condition must fall away); and since the con|dition\nof intelligibility falls away, it is not to be understood according to the laws of reason, inasmuch as, in the series of possible insights, it is something in relation to the consequences and borders on nothing in regard to the grounds. The concept of freedom is also a limiting concept of an absolute beginning. (g Likewise the concept of the simple and of the first in general. All laws of the understanding serve for comprehensibility in the series i breaks off.)\nThe concept of substance is also a *conceptus terminator*. Likewise the limit of the world. But who is *conceptus comprehensor* (comprehending concept).\nPossibility without actuality rests on the difference between the self-active representation through concept and the passive representation through sensation, since the former remains even if the latter has been entirely removed. In general the possibility of the non-actual is only something that is thought by a certain general concept.\nAll *conceptus terminatores* are taken from possibility *per synthesin* (through synthesis) and always require a first. Hence *a parte inferiori vel posteriori* (on the lower or later side) there is no *conceptus terminator*. The *terminus* (term) of the series is the first member of it, but the *conceptus terminator* is the concept through which a first of the series is possible. Of this concept, which is to be perfectly comprehensible through itself, [yet can] there must be thought such properties as enable it to be the principle of the series, in general and without a limitation to be taken from elsewhere, and so as an unlimited being by itself.\nMorality also has its own *termini a priori* and *a posteriori*: a lawgiver, and a reward and punishment according to spiritual laws, hence another world.",[]],"reflm:4040":["The two propositions: a state is possible in which a certain deter|mination\nis not, and: it is possible that a certain state should not be at all, are different. The opposite of a state is therefore possible either as a particular state or as the non-being of the given one. In general the removal of what is there is not the same as non-being.\nTo be necessarily and to endure necessarily are different concepts. The necessary being cannot be compared with time at all; therefore it can in time neither persist with the same determinations nor change them [noch] or vary in regard to them.\nWhat I call unalterable is nevertheless considered under the hypothesis of time; accordingly the *intellectualia* (intellectual things) are neither alterable nor unalterable. The beginning of sensibility is self-contradictory for this reason, that the sensible is then considered as bordering on the intellectual.",[]],"reflm:4041":["Contingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place; [zuf] alterable: [dessen] that which is possible in connection with its opposite.\nIn all alteration there are: 1. *oppositae determinationes, qvatenus eidem competunt* (opposite determinations, in so far as they belong to the same thing). 2. *successio earundem* (succession of the same). The possibility of *mutatio* (change) is not to be known from mere contingency. For because it is possible that instead of one predicate another should be, it is not yet to be known from this that the subject has the opposites one after the other. The relation of the real ground to the consequence seems at the same time to contain the idea of succession; for [in de] the consequence is here also something other than the ground, and the two can hardly be distinguished otherwise than through *prius et posterius* (prior and posterior)[erkann]. Whether a simple substance can alter itself in itself? and how alteration is possible through the *compraesentia* (co-presence) and |\nthe *nexus* (connection) of several? All alterations can be thought only in time.",[]],"reflm:4042":["(s Whether a concept has objective reality?)\n*realitas* (reality) taken adjectively is opposed to *idealitas* (ideality) and signifies [relation] the objective; e.g. realists in regard to bodies, or idealists.\nLikewise nature taken adjectively signifies something other than nature simply. (s Magnitudes, and the magnitude of a thing.)",[]],"reflm:4043":["Negation is either the *rationatum* (consequence) of a removing ground or of a deficient one*; the former is privation. The latter cannot properly be called a *rationatum*, because nothing is posited in it.\n*(s The latter can still be thought as a vanishing ground, and thus specifically the same as the *ratio ponens* (positing ground); e.g. a point an infinitely small space, rest as vanishing motion.)",[]],"reflm:4044":["Opposite and \nIn so far as a being is not the most perfect, certain defects are necessary in order that certain perfections may be obtained. E.g. abstraction for the sake of distinctness. A certain degree of cowardice, laziness and ignorance is very necessary to human nature.",[]],"reflm:4045":["Whether realities can all be of one kind and be measured by more and less.",[]],"reflm:4046":["The *omnitudo collectiva* (collective allness) (g in one) or totality rests on the *positio simultanea* (simultaneous positing). From the [omnitudine] *multitudo distributiva* I can infer to the *unitas collectiva*, but not from the *omnitudo*, because the progression is infinite and not complete. Time is infinite with respect to potential simultaneity. Hence we represent space to ourselves as actually infinite.",[]],"reflm:4047":["The whole is either of subordination: a series (sequence), or of coordination: an aggregate (heap).\nThe whole, in so far as its parts are homogeneous, is a magnitude; but they can also be heterogeneous, and in so far it is not a magnitude.\nThat whole of which no part is an absolute unity is infinitely divisible.",[]],"reflm:4048":["The ground of the unity of coordination: whether it rests upon the unity of subordination [and whether not sel], in so far as they all flow immediately from one ground.\nThe matter of the whole is the subject of the *coniunctio* (relation), i.e. the parts. The form [is that of] the *coniunctio coordinationis*. The possibility of this form is known either *per intellectum*: through space and time, or *per rationem*: through a common ground or cause.",[]],"reflm:4049":["Consequences too are connected with their grounds, but not as parts in a whole. The connection of the parts with the whole is [zu] according to the rule of identity, whereas that of the parts with one another is not according to it.\nOften one has first the idea of the whole or of dispertibility; e.g. a universal concept in respect of the classes, space in respect of its parts.\n*Totum ideale* or *reale*; *prius* either logical or arbitrary.",[]],"reflm:4050":["*Quantitas extensiva* or *intensiva*. Degree.\nThat which in respect of some quantum can be thought only intensively, namely as ground. E.g. heat, talent, God.\n(s A greater degree of heat cannot be compounded from smaller ones. But the calorific force of rays is infinite and is limited only by the inertia of matter. Hence by combining feeble rays of light (through rarefaction) the intensity of heat can increase.)",[]],"reflm:4051":["»Prima matheseos intensorum principia« [First principles of the mathematics of intensive magnitudes].\n*Maximum* and *minimum*: deceptive concepts.",[]],"reflm:4052":["Because logical subjects can in turn be predicates, we are |\nunder laws of reason compelled to think a last subject; this is substance.",[]],"reflm:4053":["Are substances really distinct from accidents? Is it not the substance itself that I can think through all its predicates, and does the least remain over by which it could be distinguished from its predicates? Only the succession of some things, while others remain the same, seems to confirm the distinction.",[]],"reflm:4054":["The concept of substance contains, besides the idea of the subject, the concept of persistence in that which succeeds one another, and of sameness in this succession, which for that reason are called changes of one and the same thing. But since [so wohl] all accidents are variable and the substantial is not known at all, the persistence of the *substantiale* is assumed *precario* (on sufferance). Even consciousness can flow. Especially if everything endures only through divine power.",[]],"reflm:4055":["The word predicate indicates no new distinction among things; hence every thing, or the concept of it, can have something predicated of it. E.g. rational beings are spirits. Thus [wenn] nothing can exist without being a predicate of other things. Man is a predicate of a rational inhabitant of the earth.\nThis logical relation can be transformed into a real one if [es] something exists in another, not in the possible concept of another, and indeed as in its real ground. For a part is in the whole on logical grounds, because the whole is something other than its parts only in part; otherwise all the parts would lie in one whole (not in the |\nplace, so to speak, for all these parts) and the whole would thus still be something other than all the parts together.",[]],"reflm:4056":["That every substance has a power is an identical proposition. For substance is properly the subject that contains the ground of the accidents and of the effects; consequently the concept of substance arises from the necessity of the power of a subject.",[]],"reflm:4057":["Under and \nThat whose representation is properly [that by which we ourselves something] the predicate of the representation of another thing is its determination; and if it is something positive in itself, it attaches to others (inherence).",[]],"reflm:4058":["60. E II 1058.\nThe concept of substance depends on inner sense. Likewise the phenomena teach us only concomitance or consequence, hence not the composition or connection (*nexus*) and the ground, but only phenomena of them. Thus the axioms of phenomena (*axiomata phaenomenorum*) are not to be regarded as intellectual. E.g. everything that happens certainly has a determining ground and follows upon something else according to a determinate law; but that is no rule for the actual actions which we perceive in ourselves.",[]],"reflm:4059":["All successions are changes of the same persisting sub|ject.\nSubstances do not flow, but their states (*status*) do; so reason demands, so experience shows; whence this agreement of experience with reason?",[]],"reflm:4060":["If one and the same subject x is to be successively a and not-a, the subject must not be altered; otherwise x would first be a and then y not-a.\nThe substantial is unalterable. For in the succession of the accidents it is always the same, and in origination and perishing (*ortus et interitus*) it is not altered.",[]],"reflm:4061":["In every composite all composition can be removed, but not in every quantum. In all parts of space parts can be indicated, but not in any part of space can all parts be indicated.",[]],"reflm:4062":["In every whole [partes] several things are coordinated, not subordinated; therefore ground and consequent do not make up a whole.",[]],"reflm:4063":["Every whole is composed either of substances or of accidents or of relations; since relations are either of substances or of accidents, the latter will be time, the former space.",[]],"reflm:4064":["That in a composition by which it is distinguished from every other is the *modus compositionis* (mode of composition).",[]],"reflm:4065":["That every substantial composite consists of simple parts does not mean that by analysis of the concept of the composite one arrives at simple parts, but that, according to the law of human knowledge, the matter of the composite, i.e. the simple, precedes the form. It therefore proves nothing objectively.",[]],"reflm:4066":["Every substantial composite consists of simple substances. For since the parts are substances, their existence is subsistence, and they can exist even though they are not composed with one another; therefore all composition can be removed, in such a way that nevertheless all the parts of such a composite remain. But when all composition is removed, the parts that remain are simple.\nThis proof goes only to the *compositum substantiale*, [welches] and its possibility can be thought *a priori*, i.e. synthetically, because then the uncomposed parts (*partes non compositae*) constitute the principles of the synthesis through which the idea of the composite arises. But an accidental composite (*compositum accidentale*) is not always of this kind. Time and space have no parts that could subsist even without all composition [seyn].",[]],"reflm:4067":["A [Einheit] multiplicity we cannot think without (g absolute) unity, |\nif the [data so wohl] whole multiplicity is to be thought through reason. But if it is given as a phenomenon, the analysis can go on without limit (*absque termino*).",[]],"reflm:4068":["Composition is an accident. Because the substantial is thought prior to all accidents, the parts are thought prior to all connection.",[]],"reflm:4069":["Opposite and \nThings that exist in one and the same time are simultaneous; those that exist in different times are successive.",[]],"reflm:4070":["Time is everywhere, or the parts of space are simultaneous. Newton.\nSpace is at all times: this means that time extends over all concepts, outer as well as inner sensations, but not that the concept of space extends over time.\nThe shortest way is not that which goes through the smallest space, but that which can be traversed in the shortest time; just as the shortest book is that through which I can grasp the object in the smallest time.",[]],"reflm:4071":["[All] The concept of time is a single concept. For all [parts of] the different times are one after another, and consequently are contained in one whole of time. No two years can exist at once. Time has only one dimension, but existence measured by time has two.\n one after another\nat once\nSpace is nothing actual, but a possibility which has its ground in something actual. The possibility of the changes of things is grounded, first, on their connection (for a single thing alone would not change), and second, on their dependence upon another; for a being whose existence does not depend on another could not change without ceasing to be.\nOne cannot think any space, e.g. a cubic foot, without an outer space that surrounds it, and thus no space without [its being] contained in the whole. Likewise no two spaces without a determinate distance and position relatively to one another.",[]],"reflm:4072":["The concepts of the understanding are reflected representations, and they also have their elements of reflection; space is an intuitive representation.",[]],"reflm:4073":["*Conceptus* (concepts) are either *intuitivi* (intuitive) or [*rationales*] *reflexi* (reflected).\nThe former are either *intuitus sensitivi* (sensible intuitions) or *intuitus puri* (pure intuitions), according as either the matter, or the form alone, of sensible representation is present in them. Reflected concepts are again either empirical or pure; the former are universal concepts containing matter given by the senses, the latter containing only form |\n[conc] containing. [priores Priores sunt intellect] Space and time are *conceptus intellectus puri* (pure concepts of the understanding). Metaphysical notions are *conceptus rationis purae* (concepts of pure reason).\nSpace is either determining (possible) or determined (actual).",[]],"reflm:4074":["Above, beside and \nThe persistence of the existence of all things in one is the *omnipraesentia* (omnipresence), space.\nThe persistence of all the existence of each thing in one is conservation, time.\nIf the whole existence of a thing depends on another, it is of contingent nature; if only its beginning depends on it, then is [be] the duration of its state contingent.",[]],"reflm:4075":["If all existence depends on one, both *extensive* and *protensive* [the text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4076":["The whole of existence that is possible in general is entitled eternity. The [existence] eternal existence [with], which is properly only the [limit] continuous limitation of eternity, is in time, as it were, a continuous beginning in one *respectu* (respect) and a continuous beginning in the other. It is related as motion through a line to the extension in this line.",[]],"reflm:4077":["Time and space precede things: that is quite |\nnatural. Both, that is, are subjective conditions [under] under which alone objects can be given to the senses. Taken objectively, this would be absurd. Hence the difficulty about the place of the world and the time before the world. Yet in absolute time no place is determined without actual things; hence absolute time can furnish no ground for the explanation of the *phaenomena* (appearances).",[]],"reflm:4078":["That space is a mere *phaenomenon* (appearance) and something subjective, and not a representation of things, is to be seen from this: that relations are represented in it which are yet no effects, but merely grounds of the possibility of effects, and these grounds are themselves not things.",[]],"reflm:4079":["Perhaps the eternity of the world is a consequence of its infinity as a *quantum simultaneum* (simultaneous quantity); for from a limited number of causes the effects must come to an end, whereas from an infinite number the combinations and consequences are infinite.\nThe difficulty of representing a *quantum simultaneum* as infinite rests on the nature of the human understanding, which can think a *totum* (whole), as regards its possibility, only synthetically, that is, *successive addendo unum uni* (by successively adding one to one). But the synthesis which is to go on to infinity is never complete. On the other hand, a *infinitum successivum* (successive infinite) can well be thought, precisely because the synthesis has no *termini* (limits). But just |\nfor that reason it is also impossible, according to the nature of our understanding, to think this successive series as whole and completed in the idea of a being.",[]],"reflm:4080":["Opposite the column heading »Finitum et infinitum«.\nInfinitely great is that which in the synthesis of *partes dabiles aeqvales* (equal assignable parts) has no last. That which in analysis has no last is infinitely divisible.",[]],"reflm:4081":["(g These propositions show how one confuses the subjective rule with the objective. It follows only that we could not distinguish such things, [but not] or that the [things] concepts would not much belong to them.)\nOn M § 270, first sentence:\nFor they would be distinguished only by places (g and times); hence they would really be only the very same thing at different times and places. For the substance itself must be presupposed prior to all relation; and if it is the same, it is one and the same thing.\nThe difference of [things] places constitutes no difference of the things themselves, but presupposes it; that is, [it is the differ] a thing stands in relation to itself when it is in relation with something that is one and the same with it.",[]],"reflm:4082":["For in that case they would be distinguished only by magnitude, that is, one would be wholly equal and similar to a part of the other, *qvod contra prius* (which is contrary to the former).* The following proposition underlies this.\n*\n(g Likewise, since a smaller magnitude can be changed into a greater, if (g the lack) of identity rested only on this circumstance, they could become wholly congruent.)",[]],"reflm:4083":["For equal things must be *homogenea* (homogeneous), and hence *totaliter aeqvalia* (totally equal) things must be *totaliter homogenea* (totally homogeneous). (s *totaliter aeqvalia* here signifies equal in every relation.)",[]],"reflm:4084":["*Qvies* (rest) is the lasting presence of a body in the same place.\nIf in a given place the body did not act, change of place would not be a succession of action. The force of change is not properly thinkable [but rather force], but it is necessary that changeable accidents be thought beforehand, whose actuation or removal requires force. Therefore the *vis motrix* (moving force) is the reason on account of which some unknown force is present successively in different places. But if the force of presence in any place be again called moving, the same is explained by the same. It is therefore evident that in bodies we know nothing but determinations of space and time, and do not know the absolute marks of the subjects intellectually.",[]],"reflm:4085":["The idea of the world is not arbitrary. For just as I must think a |\npart as finite which is no longer a whole, so I must think a whole which is no part.",[]],"reflm:4086":["* There exists a world, i.e. a whole which is not a part of any actual (possible) whole. From this it can be inferred:\n1. The world is a *compositum substantiale* (composite of substances). Hence it does not consist of necessary beings. Nor do its parts hang together through themselves. [After] Therefore, because they are through one cause. Space is the *phaenomenon* of the existence of all things through one. Infinity. The *commercium* of substances is possible thereby.\n2. Because it has its limit (s of subordination and of coordination) a priori, it has an origin. Also a beginning? The beginning of the world is considered only in respect of the world, but not in respect of something outside the world.\n(g 3. Of the order of nature. 4. Ladder of beings. Texture, figure, mixture.)\nThe whole of the world and of its state.\nIt depends on the supreme cause, but is not in *commercio* with it. There are no further worlds outside this one, although [others] are possible in place of this one, because there is only one cause. This holds *respective* to the things themselves; but *respective* to the supreme cause only one is hypothetically possible.\nWe are therefore to infer, from a world of which it is undetermined how great and how perfect it is, a supreme cause which is infinitely |\ngreater than the ground of proof of its existence. From this it is evident that the existence of this supreme and sole cause, and its all-sufficiency, must already be known otherwise, and that the existence of the world can only give proof of the existence of a necessary [being: the German reads \"eines nothwendigen\", and the noun seems to be missing].\nThe composition of a *totum substantiarum* (whole of substances) is a proof of contingency;\nThe limitation of each thing is a proof of contingency.\nThe alteration of each thing is a proof of contingency.\nWhether it may not be inferred from space, as the ground of the possibility of the *commercium* of substances according to laws of sensibility, to the intuition of a cause valid for all things that can stand in *commercio*. And from infinite time, as the ground of the possibility of the duration of things, to the eternity and necessity of the cause.\n(g A *compositum* which is no *totum absolute tale* is possible, *tanquam pars* (as a part), only through the limitation of the *totum*; hence everything that holds of a thing only as a part presupposes the concept of the whole.)\n*(g In every *compositum actuale* no part is completely determined as by all the rest together; consequently the thoroughgoing determination proceeds from all coordinated substances to one, and so it is always a part of a *totum absolutum*, i.e. of a world. But in a *compositum potentiale* every part is completely determined, because the rest are added to it and have no influence on the existence of the former; hence the series [therefore not com] is not necessarily complete. But if in a series of things following upon one another there is a last, there is also a first.)",[]],"reflm:4087":["A link in the chain is dependent; and that on which others depend |\nand which is itself independent is the uppermost link. According to the laws of gravity this one too must depend, because the lower members are a ground of the sinking of the upper ones. But in itself a thing with its consequences could be without any foreign ground.",[]],"reflm:4088":["Relation of the world to empty intuition outside the world.\nUnity or plurality of the world.\n- -\n- -\nContingency or necessity. Dependence.\n- -\n- -\nFiniteness or infinity.\nConnection (g contingency) and unconnectedness.\n(s Composition.* Community.)\nMetaphysical perfection or imperfection.\n(s Natural and supernatural.)\n(s Egoist)\n(s *Partes materiales et immateriales* (material and immaterial parts).)\n(s *partes repraesentativae* (representative parts): *vtrum simplices? vtrum spatium repleant?* (whether simple? whether they fill space?))\nTogether with the sensitive. Of eternity, of immensity, of the beginning, of *continuitas*.\n*(s Systems of the *commercium* of substances. If the cause of the harmony is external and merely arbitrary, then the state of the one does not necessarily agree with that of the other, but everything depends on arbitrary choice; hence there is no necessary ground, valid universally, for the relation of things; hence they make up no whole; or the harmony does not follow sufficiently from the things, and so is no *influxus*.)",[]],"reflm:4089":["[*Multitudo Complexus*]\n*Subordinatorum multitudo est series. Series est vel sensitive vel intellectualiter subordinatorum.* (The multitude of things subordinated is a series. A series is of things subordinated either sensitively or intellectually.)",[]],"reflm:4090":["Above, beside and \nThe impossibility of a *regressus* in a series of successions (not of things caused) comes from this, that the series is regarded as an absolute, as completed, and that we, who can determine magnitudes only successively, can never know such a series as [fully] complete. This, however, does not prove its impossibility.\nBut whether it is a *causatum* of another is not asked thereby at all.",[]],"reflm:4091":["Blind necessity is opposed by absolute (s natural) (rational) [necessity].\nTo blind chance is opposed freedom (intellectual).\n(s both are again contrary to the order which makes modes of knowledge from experience possible, and hence are impossible in regard to the objects of the same.)",[]],"reflm:4092":["In the series of marks subordinated to one another no two are the nearest; all differences are divisible to infinity.",[]],"reflm:4093":["All change occurs in a time. For between the two |\nmoments of the one state and of the following there is a time; hence the change has occurred in an infinite multitude of moments between the two states. Let us suppose that the substance persisted for a time in the same state under certain conditions: then it will persist therein eternally under the same conditions.\nBetween two states there are two moments, and between two moments there is a time, and in this time the *subiectum* is always in another state.",[]],"reflm:4094":["Egoism can be refuted only by showing that no change is possible in a single substance alone.\nThe idealist regards all his external (g actual) representations as *spontaneas* (spontaneous). The egoist [as if also none external] [so regards] all possible external [representations].",[]],"reflm:4095":["Essence (*Wesen*) is the first logical ground of the inner determinations;\nNature is the first real ground of the inner determinations.\nThe first: the ground of what is [constantly] necessary in a thing;\nThe second: the ground of what is possible through a thing.\n(s 1. That whereby a thing is possible.\n2. What is possible through a thing.\nWe know nature through experience.)",[]],"reflm:4096":["The real essence is nature.",[]],"reflm:4097":["The necessary of the concept constitutes the essence.\nThe necessary of actuality constitutes nature. It is the permanent.\nNature is the *principium* (principle) of contingencies.\nThrough experience we know nature sooner than essence, e.g. the hardness of steel. Essence and nature are both permanent.\nThe first of that which a thing is, is entitled essence.\nThe first of that which happens through a thing is entitled nature. The essence of bodies consists in impenetrable extension; to the nature of bodies belongs *inertia*. Knowledge of nature is grounded on experience, or at least on a contingent relation of the thing itself.",[]],"reflm:4098":["Progression.\nThe infinity of the series as such is possible, but not the infinity of the *aggregatum*. The former is an infinite possibility (of additions), the latter an infinite (actual) taking-together.",[]],"reflm:4099":["The greatest [magnitude] of an *aggregatum* is impossible; for it would be an *appositio* (addition) to which none further would be possible.",[]],"reflm:4100":["The incompetence of reason to set limits to sensibility itself, even under the condition of this intuition, both as to extension and as to division, and of both in respect of time and space. Reason can set limits to the empirical in regard to the rational [e.g. if one the] and can restrict empiricism, e.g. [as to the claims] that everything is extended, that before every series in the world another always precedes and that there is no first origin; but it cannot restrict sensualism.",[]],"reflm:4101":["In the *harmonia generaliter stabilita* (harmony generally established) the dancing-master, for example, is at the same time also fitted for walking instead of dancing; but in the *harmonia praestabilita* (pre-established harmony) there is no [ground] general ground at all, but every determination is individual.",[]],"reflm:4102":["Opposite and \n*effectus vel naturae vel libertatis.* (effect either of nature or of freedom.)\n*actio vel naturalis vel libera.* (action either natural or free.)",[]],"reflm:4103":["What is in different places admits of no knowledge through an [outer] inner sense, and hence of no selfhood.",[]],"reflm:4104":["*Systema emanationis* (from God or from the parents); *systema creationis*.\nMaterialism: *systema* of [propagation] natural generation, psychological epigenesis.\n(s Bad disposition or unruliness, e.g. violent heat.)\nWhether everything in the character of the child depends solely on the man or on the woman. The system of the *ovulorum* presupposes that, even if the woman had had another man, she would nevertheless have borne the same children; that of the *animalculorum* presupposes that, if the man had had another wife, he would have got the same children. This is very practical; for in the first case a [father] man has to look very much to the constitution of the woman and her race, whereas in the second he need not look to this at all, and only the woman has to look to the race of the man. According to epigenesis one must look to both: 1. on account of the alternative, secondly on account of the mixture. With free pre-existence, to neither of the two.\nBefore birth was the soul a pure spirit? Was it in a spiritual life? Has it already lived on the earth, or does it live in two worlds at once, the pneumatic and the mechanical?",[]],"reflm:4105":["The perpetual duration of substances, i.e. the equal age of each with the whole world, cannot so much be proved as that it must lie at the ground of the method of philosophising.",[]],"reflm:4106":["All proofs concerning the immortality of souls* must be so conducted that they hold not merely for some but for all (g and not merely for a brief existence after death but for an eternal duration). They cannot, therefore, be inferred from the necessity that the world must at all times have spectators who connect its epochs, since it does not yet follow from this that we, who have once begun to live, must live always.\n1. Not from divine justice. For, first, that would hold only of some, whose virtue and vice have not been sufficiently requited in this world. And, secondly, it would prove only a brief existence, which would cease after the requital.\n2. Not from the recognised immortal nature of the soul. Here the grounds of the contrary are preponderant.\n(s Not from empirical but from rational grounds.)\n3. Only from analogy with other things in nature. Human capacities have no correct proportion to the station [assigned] in this world.\n*(s Firstly, they are derived either from the nature of the soul by empirical principles, or from concepts *a priori* of the thinking being in general. The first is psychological, the second properly metaphysical;\nor secondly, they are teleological, either from the analogy of physical ends or of moral ends. According to the order of nature.)",[]],"reflm:4107":["Life cannot arise from connection with the lifeless; rather, this must diminish a degree of life. Birth can therefore be no beginning of life in general, but only of animal life, and death the end of the same.",[]],"reflm:4108":["[Is the soul in general always in this world of sense or]\nThe transition [is] either into another world or [into other relations with] a region of this one.\nThe first question: Is the soul after death a pure spirit, or still the soul of an animal? The answer rests on the decision of the question whether the soul is still, according to the present law of sensibility, in connection with the world, and so also with the corporeal world. Some sensibility will probably remain. [compar]\nSecond question. Is the other world another region of the world of sense, or is it different in form? Answer: [In respect of substance] objectively there can be only one world, for all substances except the supreme cause constitute a whole; but [in respect of form] subjectively, i.e. in the manner in which the subject represents them, there can be another world. And here it is to be presumed that sensibility diminishes, and that the transition from the *mundus sensibilis* (world of sense) into the *intelligibilis* (intelligible world) therefore proceeds *per approximationem*. The *intuitus* (intuition) is comparatively intellectual, the [mehr deu] more inner sense grows.\nThe *mundus vere intelligibilis* is the *mundus moralis*. The principles of its form hold for everyone, and from them one can infer God as the *causa mere intelligibilis*; but this *mundus intelligibilis* is not an object of intuition but of reflection. The intuition of God would at the same time give an *intuitum intellectualem* of the world.\nThose who assume an *intuitus mere intellectualis* which begins naturally after death maintain that the soul after death sees itself in the other world and does not pass over into it (g departure of the soul); that it belongs at all times to the *mundus immaterialis* as the true substance; that the corporeal world is only a certain sensible | appearance of the world of spirits; that the actions here are symbols of the actual character in the intelligible world; and that the virtuous man does not pass over into heaven but only sees himself in it.\n3. If the other world is this very same world of sense, though in a different relation, then the soul has at all times a body: either an ordinary body, perceptible [*aspectabel*] according to the laws of (g ordinary) sensation, which is *metempsychosis*; or one that is visible only through the arbitrary will of the departed soul: *apparitiones*, *spectra* (apparitions, spectres), either through external or internal influence. *Prodigia*.",[]],"reflm:4109":["Hope or fear of the other world.\nA man who has the intention of wickedness and knavery (g unless he fears God and another world) I cannot convince of the existence of both; I can only strike this fear into him, and so push into his mind sufficient grounds of the possibility, and speculative refutations or counter-grounds of the opposite possibility; but I cannot bring it about either that he knows there is another world or that he believes it. A man must have an immediate intention of being honest, if only he did not stake his whole happiness thereby. For then he will wish for, hope for and believe in the [Gl] other world and a wise government [of it], and that unchangeably and without being shaken.",[]],"reflm:4110":["The analogical proof of the hope of the other world is excellent in this, that it does not concern itself with the difficulties of speculative explanations, nor engage in theories and modes of explanation which one [not] hard to defend, but infers by the same law by which one in physics, where the object conceals itself as regards its inner constitution |, infers from what shows itself externally to what does not directly reveal itself.",[]],"reflm:4111":["It is excellent that on this earth the course of the world does not harmonise with the moral laws; for otherwise no man would himself know whether he acted from prudence or from morality, and purely moral motives could not be felt.",[]],"reflm:4112":["Blessedness [Seeligkeit] is a perfect tranquil gratification, contentment in the consciousness of one's state as a good one. *Saumseelig* [dilatory], *redseelig* [talkative] signify an activity prevailing in a tranquil state of mind.\nFortune [Glück] is a contingent acquisition of the agreeable. Happiness [Glückseeligkeit] is contentment with fortune.",[]],"reflm:4113":["S. I:\n1. Through speculation.\na. (g The natural) Original being [Urwesen]. Supreme and eternal cause. Substratum. Einos. (g Subordinate gods, tutelary gods.)\n(g c. Freely acting original being. Life alone can make the first beginning.)\nb. The first mover (g the [architect] author). |\nThe supreme spirit. Living God.\nWorld-soul. Spinozism.\n2. From morality. There must be an inwardly present and universal judge.\nThe holy, benevolent and just being.\n––––––––––\nThe greatest perfection: 1. As [an] (g the highest) ideal of all that is good, like a principle of the understanding.\n2. As a principle of method, for judging from ends in physics.\n––––––––––\nThe unity of the whole [All] of perfection, because it is only through limitation of the reality which is given to us that we know the manifold of beings.\n––––––––––\nThe idea of God is therefore\n1. The necessary (g principle) logical ideal,\n2. The necessary hypothesis of the natural order,\n3. —\n— —\n— —\n—\nmoral\n—.\nThe necessary being, the first, the highest, the being of all beings, the most perfect being, the all-sufficient.\nTotality [Allheit] is something determinate in itself. It is something absolute. The greatest is determined only through its being the greatest among possible things (and hence the whole [All]); otherwise it is something relative.\nOriginal magnitude is not thought through synthesis. All derived magnitude is only comparative, namely in relation to a measure.\n––––––––––\n*Theologia est vel rationalis vel revelata (non est empirica), rationalis philosophica vel sensus communis (maxime colenda), philosophica vel transscendentalis vel naturalis (physicotheologia).* [Theology is either rational or revealed (it is not empirical); the rational is philosophical or of common sense (to be cultivated above all); the philosophical is transcendental or natural (physico-theology).] One. Omnipotent. Eternal. Omnipresent. Simple. Unchangeable. *Impassibel* [impassible].\nOriginal being. The highest intelligence. God.\nS. II:\nThe concept of God is once for all there; one must [investigate what] develop it genetically from its use, seeking out not the meaning which one actually connects with it, but the purpose that lies at the basis of all these concepts. |\nIn all modes of knowledge which arise long before science and give occasion to it — [the text] breaks off.\n1. Fear. Sorcery. Spirits. Priests.\n2. Supreme cause.\n––––––––––\n(s At the basis of all our concepts of things lies the concept of reality. A thing, something. [This] The manifold realities [make up the U] must be given to us through the senses; reality therefore constitutes, as it were, the matter of all possibility. The whole [All] of reality [comprises] therefore comprehends the possibility of things collectively, and is (g the concept of) a single [thing]. The differences of things rest, then, on the determination of the whole of reality; namely [on the position and the ...] only on the difference in the limitation of the whole of reality. Negations are therefore limits, and cannot be thought otherwise than in that in which there are no negations at all. [The concept of the highest reality is therefore a ground...] With regard to the concept of the whole of reality, the real in a thing is the (g transcendental) matter, the negations the transcendental form. The highest reality is therefore the substratum [of] our rational knowledge of all possibility, in which, either or through which, all possibility is given.)",[]],"reflm:4114":["The concept of God is either a mere ideal of reason (g in relation to everything possible) or is given in relation to the world alone. In the latter case it is a concept inferred a posteriori, in the former a concept given a priori. Insofar as it has been inferred in relation to the world, it is needed either only as a practical ground or as a ground of theory.",[]],"reflm:4115":["Changeability in no way proves contingency; the former is the *possibilitas statuum diversorum* (possibility of different states), the latter the *possibilitas oppositorum* (possibility of opposites); the former is a *determinatio secundum leges sensitivi in cognitione* (a determination according to laws of the sensitive in knowledge). Through reason we have no marks whatever of contingency, any more than of necessity, other than the conditioned. The absolute is unknown in all respects.",[]],"reflm:4116":["The proposition that a universal author is necessary says as much as this: that it is comprehensible of itself, and its contrary absurd in itself, that a being full of universal reason, of the highest power and of the best will should exist; and that, on the other hand, it is obscure and not conceivable that many things which change among one another should exist, or even that no thing at all should exist.",[]],"reflm:4117":["The inference from contingent things to a necessary being says only this: that the existence of changeable things is not (g completely) comprehensible unless a being is assumed at which the question 'Why?' ceases, [it] that is, which is necessary of itself. But nothing is comprehensible without conditions, any more than necessary existence is; just as subaltern necessity is not comprehensible without first conditions. This shows that the inference from changeable things to a necessary [being] does not prove that such a being exists necessarily, but that such a concept, if it is possible, is requisite for the explanation; and of this being it can again be said that its necessity cannot be comprehended without a condition under which it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:4118":["The existence of God is [the condi] bound up with the highest maxims in the employment of our reason, in respect of the speculative as well as the practical.",[]],"reflm:4119":["Over, beside and [the text breaks off]\nThe possibility of a thing is only the reality or negation in respect to a certain particular concept. Reality given absolutely [schlechthin] is actuality. All reality must be given absolutely, and so some actuality precedes all possibility, just as space is not something possible but the ground of all possible figures.",[]],"reflm:4120":["At the lower margin:\nWe see through mere concepts that all realities are there, and that the possibilities of things are given through their combination and limitation.\nThis *omnitudo realitatum* [totality of realities] is in a single being [because otherwise]. Insofar as this is to be a cause of something, it is contingent; but of something contingent, something cannot be the cause from itself otherwise than through the will.",[]],"reflm:4121":["The moment is existence without any duration. Eternity is existence with all duration.",[]],"reflm:4122":["Arising, passing away and being changed occur only in time. Of what is not in time none of these can be said.\nChange is not an intellectual predicate at all. Hence it is not things but their *phaenomena* [phenomena] that change; but this change is itself a *phaenomenon*. (g Things in themselves are constant, and the changes are appearances of their limits.) What corresponds to the changes in *intellectualibus* [in intellectual matters] we do not know.\nIt is quite a question whether the concept of substance, which indicates the permanence of something amid the change of determinations, is not a concept valid only among *phaenomenis* [phenomena]; for intellectually the substance taken together with its determinations cannot be anything other than one and the same, and inherence cannot be a particular relation of something in the thing to the subject itself.\nTime too is eternal, that is, the *finita* [finite things], compared with one another through representations, that is, insofar as one cognizes the other, are all in time.",[]],"reflm:4123":["Philosophical knowledge of magnitudes determines them from the idea of the *omnitudo* [totality] (g *totum absolutum* [absolute whole]) [through] *limitando* [by limiting], and consequently the *omnisufficiens* [all-sufficient] through the *oppositum* [opposite] of all limitations.\nMathematical knowledge does not begin from the absolute whole but from the relative, and determines the whole from the parts.\nThe *modus cognoscendi* [mode of knowing] also differs. Mathematics proceeds *per constructionem conceptus secundum intuitionem sensitivam* [by construction of the concept according to sensible intuition]; philosophy *per conceptus secundum cognitionem discursivam* [through concepts according to discursive knowledge].\nIn the concept of God the sensible condition must be left out. The concept of his magnitude is: as *conceptus definitor, terminator et comprehensor* [a concept that defines, bounds and comprehends], to infer from the universal to the particular, or from the whole to the part. One must leave out the limitations (which are hindrances).",[]],"reflm:4124":["*Cognitio dei non est ectypa: mutuata ab obiectis, sed prototypa: principium obiectorum.* [The knowledge of God is not ectypal, borrowed from objects, but prototypal: the principle of objects.]",[]],"reflm:4125":["A necessary being can be the cause of contingent things only through freedom, for from the necessity of its nature there would [arise] be only that existence which is in it.\nThe independence of the divine will, hence without sensibility, and therefore the greatest freedom.\nIts goodness [*bonitas*].\nAll-sufficiency, or omnipotence.\nInner necessity.",[]],"reflm:4126":["Because God represents the highest good as determined from his own (g free) power, he wills.",[]],"reflm:4127":["(g Free) (g Rational will) (g free)\nHis will is original. Almighty. Holy. Wise. Benevolent. Just. Independent. Because he is pleased with himself as a (g fruitful) ground, [the] therefore the greatest perfection is actual through him. He is not satisfied by things.",[]],"reflm:4128":["No inclination. He needs nothing. His perfection overflows with consciousness. That is his willing.\nIn transcendental theology it must be shown that we really think this through transcendental representations, e.g. that the original being has an understanding, on account of the possibility of things through ideas, [and] order; that it has a will, on account of the efficient cause according to ideas; that the contingent is not determined through the nature of a being necessary in itself, hence is determined through freedom; [and] that a first beginning is possible only through freedom.",[]],"reflm:4129":["Everything that exists outside God exists only through his will, and nothing through the necessity of his nature except what is in him.\nEverything that exists exists either through a physical cause or according to laws of freedom. We cannot think a beginning without a freedom.",[]],"reflm:4130":["The will of God proceeds from the whole to the part (s from the highest good, the good in the whole, to each single thing).\nThis is the wise will.",[]],"reflm:4131":["*Voluntas* (will), whose [*motiva*] (motives) are not *stimuli* (stimuli) but the *complacentia* (g *acquiescentia*) (satisfaction, (g acquiescence)) of the cause with itself. The *motiva* are subjectively intellectual.",[]],"reflm:4132":["Wise decrees are benevolent and just.\nThe immediate desire for the happiness of others is benevolence.\nInsofar as this accords with worthiness (good conduct), it is justice.",[]],"reflm:4133":["Because the will of God proceeds from the universal to the particular, it is always just; the human will proceeds from the particular to the particular.\n*Iustitia passiva* (passive justice): by virtue of which someone does what he owes; *activa* (active): by virtue of which he constrains others to do what they owe. The benevolence that is exercised in proportion to conduct belongs to the *activa*.",[]],"reflm:4134":["That God is the cause of the world through his nature can be assumed, although he is not its author.\nThat God is the author of the world, i.e. that it rests on his decree, can be assumed, although he is not the creator, i.e. has not produced it in time.\nA beginning in the world can be thought, but not a beginning of the world, because for that an imagined time is required. For the world is to be not merely through another, but to come to be, having not been before.\nNor can the *actus* (act) of creation be thought, i.e. an *actus* that begins together with its effect; for in necessary beings nothing begins.\nEven if one assumed this as possible, no motive of freedom can be thought for choosing in absolute time.\nThe world is everlasting, not because it is in the whole of absolute time, but because this finds no place at all; [but because] there is no time before the world, the world is at all times.\nEternity is an unrestricted existence of a thing. [Zuf] Hence only the necessary being is eternal. What is in time is everlasting, but not eternal; for in every time it either has been, or still is, or at least will be.",[]],"reflm:4135":["God created the world from eternity (g i.e. his decree is eternal *a parte ante et post* (as regards before and after), or properly without before and after; but this decree is entitled creation with regard to the beginning of the world, and preservation with regard to its continuance), but did not thereby create an eternal world. (g In the former case eternity belongs to the divine action (s causality), in the latter to its product, which cannot have such a determination.) For the eternity of God is his duration outside every condition of time, whereas the eternity of the world is in time. The duration of the world therefore cannot be brought into any common measure with the duration [of the world] (s of God). (s The phenomenon of a thing is a product of our sensibility. God is the author of things in themselves.)",[]],"reflm:4136":["What is formed is mere matter. What forms itself is a plant [Gewächs]. What forms other things in the world is an animal. Stones are not called creatures [Geschöpfe], because they do not have their form from creation, but plants are. Animals are called creatures [Creaturen]. Beings that act freely are not called creatures.",[]],"reflm:4137":["One substance of the world cannot be the creator of another, because otherwise it would depend on itself (g in its whole existence) (*ob commercium*, on account of community). (g For things that stand in community can stand in community only through that which makes their existence possible as externally dependent.) The *substantia creatrix est extramundana* (creating substance is extramundane).\nThe creator of one substance is at the same time the creator of all, because he places all the rest in dependence on it. (g There is no community without a common principle.)\nCreation is a unity, i.e. several substances did not come, one after another, to be added to those created. For otherwise we should have no rule or unity for the use of our understanding, if e.g. in the growth of a tree substances were to arise in addition; hence the *principium stabile perpetuum* (stable, perpetual principle) must also be *invariabile* (invariable) with regard to quantity.",[]],"reflm:4138":["(g Evil lies only in freedom.)\nNature is good in the whole. Secondly, it is good in every particular order that has its laws, e.g. even the faults of the female sex, which are natural.\nBut contingency, i.e. the event of nature insofar as it is made to deviate from one of the rules of order through the influence of others, hence insofar as it lies not under a particular rule of predetermination but merely under that of possibility, can be evil.",[]],"reflm:4139":["If I could assume a most perfect world without a wise author, then everything of importance in morals could be understood without the existence of God.",[]],"reflm:4140":["To fear God: to do his commandments from obligation. To love God: to do his commandments gladly.\n}\nVeneration.",[]],"reflm:4141":["God proves his honour through the exercise of his moral attributes, i.e. through actions on account of which he is worthy of veneration and adoration, not of high praise. We do not always venerate him whom we love, nor him whom we fear, but [only when we do both] together. *Adoratio* (adoration) is love, fear and admiration together.",[]],"reflm:4142":["Creation (beginning) springs from freedom.",[]],"reflm:4143":["The end of creation is always a connection of the world with the divine highest delight, but not as its ground, rather as its consequence. Human beings too depend in part on their end, even in moral matters. But if they could feel their own moral perfection even without exercising it, the satisfaction would not arise through the action, but the action from the satisfaction.\nThe highest happiness [aus d] under the condition, in creatures, that lets them regard it as universally good, is the final end. Hence [it is] the highest happiness in accord with morality.\nHence our actions are never directed to the satisfaction of the divine desire. The ends of actions therefore lie not in God but in creatures; hence the honour of God is not the end but the [motive] ground of good actions.",[]],"reflm:4144":["*Omnipraesentia* (omnipresence) not (g itself) in space, not *localis* (local) but *virtualis* (virtual).",[]],"reflm:4145":["Not (g as) world-soul, which reciprocally also suffers.\n*Spatium sensorium omnipraesentiae divinae* (space the sensorium of divine omnipresence): Newton.\n*Intima praesentia fanatice repraesentatur* (intimate presence is represented fanatically).",[]],"reflm:4146":["(s System of pure reason. Not mathematics.)\nMetaphysics is a philosophy of pure reason.\n—\n— —\n— —\n— —\nof the form.\n—\n— —\n— —\n— —\nof the subject and not of the object.\n(s is the Critique of pure reason.)\n— — of those [Ges] conditions under which alone human pure reason can judge.\n(s *Conceptus vel empirici vel puri* (concepts are either empirical or pure))",[]],"reflm:4147":["One can never infer from below upwards (for reason infers only from the universal to the particular), but only by means of higher rules, under which many particular cases are subsumed, to a subordinate law.\n(s One must regard the principles as heterogeneous (rational).)",[]],"reflm:4148":["Criticism is a science not of the production but of the judging of certain things according to rules of perfection; hence metaphysics is a science of judging modes of knowledge from pure reason. Besides logic, which is a [organon] propaedeutic science of all learning, rational learning [Vernunftgelehrsamkeit] contains one critical part, of experience and reason, and two dogmatic parts; the first is metaphysics, the second mathematics and morals in pure reason, and physics and psychology in the empirical science of reason.",[]],"reflm:4149":["*Philosophia transscendentalis* (transcendental philosophy).\n*Omnitudo* (allness) is (s disjunctive)\neither distributive.\n*Vniversalitas* (universality).*\nor collective.\n*Vniversitas* (totality).\n*Omnitudo collectiva* is either of coordination or of subordination [et]; the limit of the former is the world, the limit of the latter is the *ens primum* (first being).\nHence 1. Ontology 2. Cosmology 3. Theology }\n}\n}\n} transcendental { s\n{\n{\n{pure philosophy applied to nature in general or to this nature. }\n}\n}\n}\n*(g distributive is logical. Each one.\ncollective: real. All together.\nEvery [man is] body is movable; not: all together are movable.\n[Al] Every alteration has an end; not: all together have an end.)",[]],"reflm:4150":["All philosophy is either empirical or *pura* (g *intellectualis*).\nThe first is that whose principles are borrowed from experience. Hence *psychologia et physica*.\nPure philosophy (*philosophia pura*) is either *theoretica* or *practica*.\nThe *theoretica* has either no object of the senses at all as its object: *transscendentalis*; or it has as its object the universal of all objects of the senses: *metaphysica propria*. The latter consists of the *physiologia, mechanica, pnevmatologia, theologia naturalis*.",[]],"reflm:4151":["*Qvaestio Stolpiana*: From the attributes which are common to any things whatever that constitute this universe (but no others are common, or rather are known as common, save that they constitute [this] universe), to demonstrate that they are not necessary but created by a necessary being.\n––––––––––\nProf. Pestel, Secretary of the Stolp Institute, before 1 July 1771.",[]],"reflm:4152":["That ontology is nothing other than a transcendental logic (g subjective), while applied metaphysics is merely negative, so that nothing remains but morals, whose data the human will supplies and whose formal principles are analytic.",[]],"reflm:4153":["The synthetic judgments of pure reason are clear not through themselves but only in their application *in conseqventia*, and therefore cannot be used *in antecedentia*.",[]],"reflm:4154":["Since in the sensible there are matter and form, should there not also be matter and form in the intellectual?\nThe form of sensibility is twofold: space and time.\nThe form of reason is twofold: logical and real.\nuniversal and particular\n| ground and consequence",[]],"reflm:4155":["The metaphysical concepts are, first, absolute: possibility and existence. Secondly, respective [relative].\na. Unity and plurality. *omnitudo* and *particularitas*. [Infin]\nb. Limits. The first, the last, *infinitum*, *finitum*.\nc. Connection: of coordination. Whole and part, simple and composite.\nof subordination:\n1. subject and predicate.\n2. ground and consequence.",[]],"reflm:4156":["We cannot think any contingent being that is not conditionally necessary, and we must in the end terminate this subordination in a being that is unconditionally necessary, although of this absolute necessity we have no insight of reason, since otherwise we should know its existence immediately in itself. The *ens necessarium* is a concept given by reason and its limit, but not found within that limit, and is therefore problematic.\nWe can think nothing that happens without its being contingent, and indeed [we cannot do so] without thinking an absolute contingency of the first ground in the coming-to-be [zu d] [so daß]. The first beginning occurs only through freedom.\nThus according to rules of (g pure) reason nothing is absolutely contingent, and the supreme causality is necessary; but according to rules of reason which has concepts of appearance [erscheinungsbegriffe] as its ground, the causality of the first cause of what happens is contingent. According to rules of morality there must be authors, and not merely intermediate causes, of events; according to rules of [pure reason, metaphysics] causality there is only one author, who however is outside the world.",[]],"reflm:4157":["The actuality of (g contingent) things demands a necessary principle; coming-to-be demands the absolute contingency of causality. Thus [the concepts are laws of appearance to the laws of reason] reason cannot be used [a pr] *a priori* with the principles which, when applied to appearances, are correct.",[]],"reflm:4158":["A subject can be represented only through its predicates, and predicates only in their subject. Hence the necessity of representing substances to ourselves, which is more a subjective necessity of the laws of our understanding than an objective one.\nAll judgments arise through the understanding's attending to its own actions, or to representations in general. If these are representations of sensation and of their relations, the judgments are empirical and correct; if they are representations [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4159":["Of arbitrary [and abstracted] concepts many definitions can be given, that is, explanations from which [all] entirely the same things can follow [be derived]; and for that very reason [from] different proofs of one and the same proposition. But of independent concepts of reason only [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4160":["The adverbs and prepositions, likewise (I, thou), further the |\npluperfect etc., are inexplicable; in this consists the philosophy of languages.\nPiccard: *isagoge in lectionem aristotelis*.",[]],"reflm:4161":["If it were a matter of names of sciences, we should distinguish *Physica, Metaphysica, Hyperphysica* and *hypophysica*. Namely: 1. the nature of actual things (g objects of the senses), from experience; 2. [the nature] of all possible things (g objects), from reason*; 3. that which depends upon the cause of nature among actual things; 4. what is actual without having its determining ground in any nature, that is, the by-chance [Ohngefehr] (g and *qvalitas occulta*) (s and blind necessity).\nThis division holds for [under] the determination of the sciences with regard to empirical knowledge.\n*(g We know through reason the things and their relation, or the relation of concepts. Morals.)",[]],"reflm:4162":["All knowledge has either empirical or rational principles; the latter are either logical or real. The logical take the first concepts of things and relations as they are given, |\nand consider only the subordination according to identity and contradiction; the second are first concepts and relations given through the nature of reason.",[]],"reflm:4163":["All sciences of (g pure) reason are either those which consider through pure reason the rules of universal knowledge in general, or those which consider the particular rules of pure reason itself. *Logica*. *Phaenomologia generalis* and *Noologia generalis* have as their aim merely the rules of universal [and not empirical] modes of knowledge, which are given through no experience. Noology applied to that which is given through experience, although not through grounds of experience, is theoretical: metaphysics; or practical: morals.",[]],"reflm:4164":["*Metaphysica est organon intellectus puri, primo critica* (Metaphysics is the organon of the pure understanding, in the first place a critique).\n[*Omnis dubitatio*] [Every doubt]\nScepticism is either dogmatic or critical; the former [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4165":["The terminus of coordination is the world, that of subordination is God; hence *cosmologia* and *theologia*. The logical *omnitudo* is real [*realis*] in ontology.",[]],"reflm:4166":["The first part, on the first grounds of our pure knowledge of reason concerning all things (g the supreme *principium cognoscendi*). Ontology.\nThe second part, on the first ground of things: the supreme *principium essendi*. *Theologia naturalis*.\nOn the first principles of reason with regard to [objects] corporeal nature, that is, on the possibility of outer appearances: *pnevmatologia* or [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4167":["In empirical philosophy it is permitted to feign (*fingere licet*), because the possibility of the principles is empirically given; in pure philosophy it is not permitted to feign, because fictions would be fictions of pure reason, that is, relations given neither from the nature of reason nor by experience, and hence asserted of possibility in a wholly arbitrary fashion.",[]],"reflm:4168":["Metaphysics [is a science] has as its object either mere objects of pure reason or [also] objects of the senses, whose grounds and nature it comes to know not through the senses but through pure reason. The former is the general part, the latter the special part (*Metaphysica applicata*), and this consists of *pneumatologia* and *somatologia pura*. Before both of these the physiology of inner or outer sense must come, but not before *metaphysica universalis*,\n|\nnamely ontology and *theologia generalis*, of which the first considers the supreme principle of all modes of knowledge through pure reason, the second the supreme principle, to be known through pure reason, of all things. In *ontologia* everything is considered disjunctively, in *theologia naturalis* and *cosmologia* everything collectively.",[]],"reflm:4169":["Universality (g *omnitudo*) is either distributed or comprehensive universality, *distributiva* or *collectiva*. Of the latter we must first treat in general. (It has its limits or not.) The *collectiva* is either that of subordination or that of coordination. The latter gives the concept of the world; the former gives the concept of the original being, as the ground of all consequences subordinated to one another, so that among those which stand under one another no consequence is to be met with that does not stand under it.\nThe [univers] *omnitudo distributiva* is either *universaliter* or *disiunctive distributiva*. Ontology treats of the predicates of things which hold *universaliter* or *disiunctive* of all. Cosmology treats of all together that belong, as parts, to one and the same whole. *Theologia naturalis* treats of all together that belong, as consequences, to one ground.",[]],"reflm:4170":["An empty concept is one that (g relates to) no object [hat]; the negative, one that relates to the negation of an object; the impossible, one that cancels itself.",[]],"reflm:4171":["Logical cancelling is as much as: not positing; real cancelling is just as much as: removing what is there. The former is *negatio*, the latter *ablatio*.",[]],"reflm:4172":["The principles of pure reason are of two kinds: 1. those from concepts given through pure reason but confused, *per analysin*, e.g. substance, whole, necessary, etc.; 2. those from concepts given through reason *per synthesin*. The concepts given through reason before all experience, although abstracted on the occasion of experience from the laws of reason, e.g. that of ground, are forms impressed upon the ordering understanding for the use of experiences.",[]],"reflm:4173":["What lies in the concept of a necessary being and can be drawn from it *per analysin* is little; what is connected with it *per synthesin*, that is, what is required for a necessary being, is much, but can be known only *per principia synthetica*.",[]],"reflm:4174":["Everything that happens is in the series of successions [des] and is represented therein. But nothing can be represented in absolute time. Things are represented in a sequence only in so far as a real connection of them is conceived, whereby one member draws the other after it. Hence nothing in a series can be known as actual if the transition to it from what precedes is not necessary according to a universal law, that is, without a ground, although one does not know it.\nThe relations of things are in general always represented through real connections, and time is only the form of appearance [Erscheinung], in which things connected in this way are intuited. Time does not bring forth representations of connected things.",[]],"reflm:4175":["The thinking of negation is properly a thinking of the removal of a true position, which might otherwise creep into the understanding. A visible eclipse of the moon is possible, but not Milton's visible darkness.",[]],"reflm:4176":["In the case of essence there is sufficiency, so far as coordination is concerned, and [subordination, as to] precision (g exactness of measure), so far as subordination is concerned, the purpose. *Reductio ad minimos terminos* (reduction to the least terms).",[]],"reflm:4177":["Strictly, only substances exist. Accidents are only predicates of existing substances. The predicate itself is nothing existing.",[]],"reflm:4178":["Necessity is either (g as) that of the *rationatum* (what is grounded) or as that of the ground, that is, of independence;\nNecessity is either absolute, i.e. necessary in every respect,\nor hypothetical: necessary only in a certain respect.\nUnqualified contingency belongs to that whose opposite is possible in every respect: mere chance, the haphazard. Contingentia[omnimod] (contingency) unqualified in external respect is freedom.\nA first beginning can be thought only through freedom. Motion is by its nature internally contingent, but, *dato qvodam nexu externo* (given a certain external connection), always necessary; freedom is the reverse.",[]],"reflm:4179":["Two propositions that stand in direct opposition to one another: if something is, then it is necessary through something else (*ratio intelligendi in serie data*, ground of understanding in a given series); if something necessary is, then there is an absolutely necessary being (*ratio complete intelligendi seriem, h.e. terminandi*, ground of completely understanding the series, i.e. of terminating it). According to the first there is nothing absolutely necessary; according to the second there is. The cause is that possibility can be thought without actuality, and yet not without any [actuality at all].",[]],"reflm:4180":["The hypothetically necessary, without an absolutely necessary principle, allows a long use of reason, so long as one does not think of the limit, and is therefore better suited to the understanding than mere haphazard. But if one denies [the absolutely necessary] (*atheismus assertorius*, assertoric atheism), this use of reason is again rendered useless, for the whole chain is contingent, since it demands something outside itself that does not hang further on another link, and this is denied. The concept of the absolutely necessary is therefore an essential condition of the use of our reason, but only a presupposition, not a product of it. For I leave out every condition that is finite; what remains can no longer be known *a priori*, that is, be had insight into. I seize upon a higher hold and find myself in empty space, which is infinite and through itself makes everything possible and bounds it. But this concept of a limit we can, in relation to the things subordinated to it, know sufficiently, namely what is required for it to hold all things through itself. So it is also with freedom, the first beginning of arising. We know well what flows from freedom and from its presupposition, and we also need to presuppose it; but no one can comprehend the arising of a free action, because it is the beginning of all arising. I am a substance; in me the predicates terminate, and I am myself none. This self I know with certainty as the *terminus* of my imputations. But I am contingent in all respects and am not the independent in respect of existence, though I am in respect of inherence, etc. etc. That without presupposing which I cannot use my reason (necessity) is, in relation to me, the condition of my reason; but that without knowing which I cannot use my reason completely is merely a limit for reason.",[]],"reflm:4181":["From succession one cannot yet infer contingency; for the state that follows is no *contradictorie oppositum* (contradictory opposite) of the previous one, and consequently (g from this) it cannot be known that its opposite is possible.\n[absolute] (g internal, inwardly) unchangeable means either: what by itself alone can never change; but what cannot change even in any connection is absolutely unchangeable. The internally unchangeable does not present for reason such a difficulty as the internally changeable.",[]],"reflm:4182":["Reality is either comparative or absolute.\nThe reality of phenomena is not absolute, e.g. impenetrability.\nBetween realities and negations of phenomena (g non) there is given a third, sensual, but intellectual. (s between realities of phenomena there is given a third = 0.)\n(s The intellectual is that whose concept is an act.)",[]],"reflm:4183":["Not every *quantum* (quantity) is on that account a *compositum* (composite), nor even every *totum* (whole), although it is *divisibile* (divisible); but the division is rather a distinguishing than a separating; hence it is also not possible through composition of what has been separated. *Qvantum continuum non est divisibile* [omne] (a continuous quantity is not [every one] divisible). The magnitude of an (g intensive) ground proves no composition out of smaller ones [ausser], but the magnitude of a whole does.\nWhat can be divided only through limitation is not possible through composition; hence not space.\n(s The earth is not composed of countries but is divided into them.\n*materia*: parts,\n*forma*: *conjunctio*. Coordination, not subordination.\n*Qvantitas*, *totalitas*.)",[]],"reflm:4184":["Opposite the column heading:\nIncomprehensible is the first of things (*necessarium*) and the first of states (*initium primum*: freedom). Likewise the last, that is, the outermost of things in the synthesis, which breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4185":["Several things conjoined make a *compositum*. All things conjoined make a *totum*. Therefore not every *compositum* (e.g. eternity) is a *totum*, although every *totum* is a *compositum*. The word *omnia* (all) is taken either generically or singly. We assert of all numbers generically in arithmetic, and we think all straight lines generically in geometry, but [sin] to think all and each singly is not permitted.\nIn the rational notion of a composite the *terminus* of analysis is the simple, and the synthetic *terminus* is the whole. The former is the *terminus a priori*, the latter *a posteriori*.\nIn order that all things be thought distributively, it is necessary that unities be posited at the same time; and therefore eternity is not a *totum*.\nIn any whole, therefore, there are to be thought: matter, form and totality.",[]],"reflm:4186":["Time is the first condition of the possibility of a whole, for coordination requires simultaneity and subordination succession.",[]],"reflm:4187":["Things that are simultaneous are connected through time just as (g those) that are after one another. But time is one only; therefore it is the possibility of the coordination of many, depending on one. The coordination of contradictory opposites is succession, that of non-opposites is coexistence.\nThe reason by which the existence of many pertains to one, namely that they are simultaneous, lies in this, that [all be through] grounds produce one state of the world; and therefore simultaneity pertains to the unity of the world and bears witness to a common cause. (Time is everywhere.) That changes are possible (in which the idea of succession in time consists) rests upon — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4188":["Space is not an empirical concept: 1. only through it are experiences possible; 2. it is no object of the senses; 3. the immediate principles concerning it do not have the contingency and particularity of empirical propositions, and are also not appeals to experience. It |\nis also not a concept of reason: 1. it is not a general but a single concept; hence all spaces are parts of a single one, and the principles [are not] cognized through immediate intuiting.",[]],"reflm:4189":["In the case of time there is the relation of things to one another in respect of existence in general, whether they are simultaneous or successive, and the magnitude of their duration. They all appear as in one whole of existence, yet of vanishing magnitude in coming to be and passing away at once. In the case of space there is the relation of things in respect of their community: all as in one whole, which makes all [possible] community possible.\nBecause we cognize by intuition not merely the space of the object that touches our senses, but the whole of space, space must not merely arise from the actual touching of the senses, but must precede it. Yet since it is nevertheless represented as something actual, it will be the effect of the feeling of omnipresence, and time the effect of the dependence of all things in existence on One, just as space is the consequence thereof for the community of things.",[]],"reflm:4190":["All existence in time is contingent. For it is a perpetual vanishing and beginning; and from the fact that a thing exists, it does not follow that it will exist. Necessary existence is the only complete existence.\nExistence in time is to necessary existence as, in space, in respect of extension, an infinite line is to the motion of a point in it, [the present].",[]],"reflm:4191":["M 72:\nOf space the rules are: 1. that, since it is only the condition of the appearance [*Erscheinung*] of outer things, it must not be regarded as the condition of the existence of the things themselves, unless the things themselves must be mere appearances; 2. that the predicates of space must not be regarded as predicates of things, but of their appearances, and consequently the propositions which express the possibility of things do not hold of space; 3. that space is something wholly true |\nM 72': |\nwith regard to outer appearances, because it is the condition of them.",[]],"reflm:4192":["Infinity is not an objective, determinate concept of a magnitude in relation to others, but subjectively a surpassing of a magnitude beyond all [magnitudes] that can be assigned by us [*angebliche*], though not beyond all that can be assigned by every understanding.",[]],"reflm:4193":["Above, beside and\nunder the things, there are the following kinds of the greatest:\n1. the concept which contains everything [under itself] in its sphere (under itself),\n2. that which comprehends everything in itself as a whole,\n3. that which comprehends everything under itself as a ground.\nThe widest concept, the greatest whole, the greatest ground. Logical. Mathematical. Metaphysical: all intellectual sciences.",[]],"reflm:4194":["Beside and\nPotential infinity, actual infinity. Why the past too is reckoned with the actual.",[]],"reflm:4195":["Under and\nIn the infinite the difficulty is to reconcile the totality with the impossibility of a *synthesis completae* [complete synthesis]. Consequently the difficulty is subjective. On the other hand, the *potentialiter infinitum* [the potentially infinite] (*infinitum coordinationis potentialis*) is quite comprehensible, but without totality.",[]],"reflm:4196":["The greatest concept, the greatest intuition, the greatest thing are all given; and through their limitation we generate the logical, the sensible and the real possibility of all things.",[]],"reflm:4197":["The mathematically infinite is without end according to laws of sensible estimation; the metaphysically infinite is without end in general, i.e. without limits.\n*Maximum vel respective tale* [the greatest, or such relatively], e.g. the ideal, *vel absolute qvoad realitatem* [or absolutely as regards reality].",[]],"reflm:4198":["To and\nThe infinite is the greatest in the absolute sense; that which contains everything. The second is the same as the third, if the thing is regarded as a ground. The third signifies most. Through reason the greatest can be seen only by means of the *consummatum* [the completed].",[]],"reflm:4199":["That two things cannot be in one place is not an objective proposition; rather, we call [two] things different only because they are in different places, and in one and the same place we have, through outer sense, no ground for distinguishing different things.\nThe being of a thing in a place can be expressed thus: the place is the ground of something, which means as much as: to be in a place is as much as to act externally in a certain relation. Because in absolute space no relation of a thing to others is to be met with, a thing can be met with in no absolute place (without relation of the acts to others). But in empty space, which is not filled.",[]],"reflm:4200":["1. That [no] (g all) relation in space is [one-sided] (g relative and never absolute); e.g. no absolute place, no absolute rest or motion, but motion and rest must at all times be related to some other body;\n2. what follows from this: namely, that all motion and rest is reciprocal and equal, and that no body rests relatively to another which is moved in relation to it; [that therefore effect and that these motions are opposed]*\n3. That [what the production], if they are thus combined, a common rest follows therefrom, i.e. that action and reaction are equal to one another.\n*(g That no motion can be generated without producing just as much in the opposite direction (in the mover), because a body moves toward another only as much as this |\nmoves toward it, or [as much as] another toward itself as it toward the other, and because they mutually recede from or approach one another. [The] Both bodies taken together will therefore, with regard to the [place] space in relation to which they were considered before the generated motion, remain in one and the same state. I.e. if they were at rest before the communication, their system will be at rest, and if they were moving, their system will move.)",[]],"reflm:4201":["(s the absolutely complete substantial whole [*totum substantiale*] is the world.)\n(g contingency, i.e. relation to a cause, flows from composition.)\nTo the (g notion) of the world belong 1. the material element: [subjects] a plurality of substances. An egoistic world [*mundus egoisticus*] is not a world. 2. The formal element, or the (g real) nexus of substances, insofar as they constitute a whole (g coordination (not subordination under their cause)). 3. [determination] a negatively determined relation: that it is not a part of another whole. Or the totality. In every whole there is *totalitas* [absoluta] *respectiva* [relative totality]; in the world, *totalitas absoluta* [absolute totality]. The earth [is] has *totalitas*; and if one takes all its parts together, one has *totalitas respectiva*; but it is itself still a part, and so it has no *totalitas absoluta*.\nThe *terminus* [limit] of coordination *per analysin* [through analysis], i.e. *a priori*, is the simple; the *terminus* of the same *per synthesin* [through synthesis], i.e. *a posteriori*, is the world. The *terminus* of subordination *a priori* is the supreme cause, *a posteriori* the totality of the consequences. Reason recognises its insight as incomplete if it is not enclosed within *termini*. The necessity of the *termini*, however, is not objective. But reason also cannot conceive how a *terminus* is to find a place, in coordination as well as in subordination.\nThings which are connected only as [mutually] subordinated do not always admit of totality, unless they can be logically coordinated by reckoning.\n(s The completeness of a composite as such is totality, and this is either absolute or hypothetical; the former either with respect to all possible wholes or with respect to some actual whole.)",[]],"reflm:4202":["The complete is a *quantum* [magnitude] which is not a part of another.",[]],"reflm:4203":["In any (g substantial) whole there is a reciprocal dependence of states among the parts, not a dependence [under] as regards the actuality of the substance. For cause and effect are not co-parts of one and the same whole, because the nexus of subordination differs from the nexus of coordination. The dependence of states, however, consists in this, that the conditions of the inherence of accidents inhere reciprocally in the substances.",[]],"reflm:4204":["The real nexus of coordination (g real) is reciprocal; that of the subordination of substances as such is not reciprocal.",[]],"reflm:4205":["The supreme and all-sufficient cause is that which is neither subordinated nor coordinated to any other.\n(s *independentia* and *completudo*)",[]],"reflm:4206":["The coordination according to which all [Best] accidents of a thing make up the whole of its state is a consequence of subordination under one, namely the substantial. The coordination according to which all substances make up one whole is the subordination under a first being [*ens primum*].",[]],"reflm:4207":["Opposite and\nSpace and time are formal grounds of the possibility of a world. Space, time and force alone are capable of magnitude in the world.\n1. From the composition of the world: contingency, and beginning or eternity; [2. From space] likewise magnitude, infinity.\n2. Alteration of state. Order of nature. Gradation.\nThe supreme ground of connection is also the formal ground of the possibility of *commercium* [community]. Expressed sensibly, it is space. But space is presumably only the sensible intuition, which has as its ground the immediate consciousness (intellectual intuition), though this cannot be found in it [in space] by any analysis.",[]],"reflm:4208":["Effects are symbols of causes; hence space (through which things are actually represented as necessarily connected by means of a common ground) is a *symbolum* [symbol] of divine omnipresence, or the phenomenon of divine [intuitions] causality. In space possibility is not distinguished from actuality.\nAll sensible representation is a *symbolum* of the intellectual. All (g sensible) representation of the relation of the effect to its cause is the *symbolum*.",[]],"reflm:4209":["An infinite past series of appearances would presuppose an infinite duration of sensibility, and hence an appearance whose representation would in turn be an appearance.",[]],"reflm:4210":["In time the world is either from eternity (at all time *a parte priori*) or not from eternity, yet in time (in a part of time *a priori*). Both are false. For the part of time outside of or before the world [is], or rather absolute time, is indeed a condition of actual things, but not of non-actual things, in which their *positus* [positing] with regard to what precedes would be determined.\nIt is not possible to represent to oneself an infinite world. Now the world (*phaenomenon*) and time are only something in the representation. If I wished to say: whether an infinite world is not possible in itself, then the world of sense is nothing in itself. As far as I am willing to go (g back), the world is finite; to know the world back into the infinite is impossible; hence the world of sense, as far as I go, is always finite. But is the world therefore to be measured *a parte priori*?",[]],"reflm:4211":["M 121':\n*continuum formarum* (continuum of forms).\nThere is a continuity among the species (*species*); that is, there is no species proximate to another such that others could not be placed between them, and so also no lowest species (*species infima*). We have lowest species only because we do not know the small differences. E.g. gold, of which we know no [further] species; likewise with rye and plants. Perhaps they were at first placed very [from one another] according to the law of continuity, and were separated only through mixing.\n*Non datur species infima* (there is no lowest species) can mean either: no lowest species is possible, or: it is not actual. E.g. the germs that lie in man can produce a creature perfectly similar to an ape.\nM 121:\nDifferent species argue (*arguiren*) a difference in that which belongs to substance; but difference of states makes no difference of things, and so none of species either.",[]],"reflm:4212":["If everything exists through One, then the thoroughgoing determination of each thing is a limitation of the whole of the world, just as every measured space is a limitation of infinite space. That is: the whole does not arise from the composition of the act through which parts are produced, for thereby a *quantum discretum* (discrete quantity) would result; rather each part is possible only through the limitation of the power that is in the whole. But one product of nature is connected with another only through the limitation of the universal force which produces them |\nboth. Hence [also only] because in the universal sufficient force lies the ground of all the species that are possible between one product and another, [they are possible] only by means of all the intermediate products, of which there are infinitely many, contained between one species and another.",[]],"reflm:4213":["In the metaphysical sense, things are of one kind when transition from the one to the other is possible [only] through alteration of magnitude, whether in a property or in the relation of two. Things of different kind, on the other hand, are those whose rule is already different.",[]],"reflm:4214":["139. E II 1354. 1355.\nM 139':\nThe first (g [innerl]) inner ground of everything that belongs to the actuality of a thing is its nature.\nAn effect has its ground either in the nature or in the freedom of the cause. In the first case the opposite is physically impossible, in the second practically impossible.\nThe first ground of the contingent is not in the nature of any thing.\nEverything that happens through the nature of a thing has an antecedent |\nM 139: |\nground and is not the first. The [Zus] thoroughgoing determination of that which belongs to the inner side of existence, according to subjective laws: nature; according to objective laws: freedom.",[]],"reflm:4215":["Given space, the possibility of the compresence of many (*compraesentia plurium*) is given; but the compresence of many is not possible unless a common principle is posited as existing; therefore from given space one can conclude to some first being. But space is unique; therefore one concludes to a unique first cause. Likewise it is necessary, insofar as it is mere possibility; therefore one concludes to a necessary [being].",[]],"reflm:4216":["Every harmony is established either generally or singularly; the former requires that something be a substantial whole.\nGenerally established [harmony] is established either externally or internally; the latter is physical influx (*influxus physicus*). Singularly established [harmony] is either occasional or pre-established (*occasionaliter vel praestabilita*).\n[In universo est] As regards the special commerce of mind and body, the difficulty arises from this: that the substance which we know through inner sense is wholly unknown [as to] its being an object of outer sense, and so no force is given as a general principle of external presence [can]; yet it is not permitted to invent one. This difficulty, however, is also found [in] with regard to the actions of bodies, whose principle is established only from experience, not by insight of reason.\nSpace is a singular idea; hence it is established that the possibility of the compresence of many rests on the existence of a singular being.",[]],"reflm:4217":["148. E II 1693. 784.\nM 148':\nThe cause of substance is creative, the cause of form architectonic. Therefore the extramundane being is not only the architect but the creator of the world.\nThe world [est non est par] is a whole which is not an actual part of another whole; if it is also not a part of any possible world, it is the greatest whole. The rational cause of the world is its author (*auctor*). An author is not an architect. etc etc. The author of the world is neither coordinated nor subordinated to anyone. God.\nThings which are not harmonious of themselves, but agree through external and arbitrary contrivance, are in apparent commerce, i.e. sympathetic. [Qvorum natura] Things which are conjoined from a general principle, so that their common nature is the ground of their mutual dependence, agree physically or naturally. Physical influx*, however, is either originary or derivative (s subalternate). The former would have place if the formal ground is domestic to the substances; the latter if it is foreign. But the cause foreign to the world is an extramundane being. The commerce of the substances of the world therefore takes place through derivative influx, or is natural commerce on account of common dependence.\nThe nature of the universe as such (since the universal nature is indeed the nature of all substances) consists in conjunction and completeness (*completudo*) (on which account it is called *rerum universitas*, the universality of things); hence breaks off.\nM 148:\n*(s originarily a substance cannot influence a substance, except that which it sustains, because substances do not suffer from others [a qvibus non], except in such a way that the passion is at the same time action.)",[]],"reflm:4218":["(g If there is a will (*Willkühr*) in God, it is a free will.)\nDivine freedom is very well to be comprehended. Spontaneity and subjective necessity through intellectual motives (*necessitas subiectiva per motiva intellectualia*) (which never necessitate subjectively, since they do not consist in the manner in which the subject is affected) are in him most perfect. Here there is no combination of necessity with contingency. But in the case of human freedom: 1. a series of determining grounds of each free action is necessary for the comprehensibility of its origin, but [at the same time] spontaneity simply as such (*spontaneitas simpliciter talis*) is then impossible in a contingent thing, on account of the [highest determ] external determining grounds; but on the other hand, [if one it a first beginning with the] for a first beginning unconditioned contingency or independent freedom is necessary.",[]],"reflm:4219":["The difficulty of comprehending human freedom lies in this: that the subject is dependent and yet is to act independently of other beings.\nThe necessary being cannot begin to act; the contingent can make no first beginning.",[]],"reflm:4220":["Freedom is properly only the self-activity of which one is conscious. When something occurs to one, this is an act of self-activity; but in this one is conscious not of one's activity but of the |\neffect. The expression: I think (this object), already indicates that I am not passive with regard to the representation, that it is to be ascribed to me, [and] that the opposite depends upon myself.",[]],"reflm:4221":["Freedom is the faculty of originally producing and effecting something. But how originary causality (*causalitas originaria*) and the faculty of originally effecting (*facultas originarie efficiendi*) can obtain in a derivative being (*ens derivativum*) is not to be comprehended at all.",[]],"reflm:4222":["Our *arbitrium* (power of choice) is not sensitive, but the acts of choice (*actus arbitrii*) are either sensitive or intellectual. For *arbitrium* is not inclination, but choice from inclination or from reason.",[]],"reflm:4223":["Practically that is nothing which I cannot perceive at all*. Now the transcendental necessitation cannot be perceived by me; I feel nothing but the law, a force sufficient thereto, and the liking; therefore the practical is never breaks off.\n*(g For that does not come into account among the motives. Shall I, on account of this predetermination, be indifferent with regard to the aims of life, or proceed otherwise? It is one thing to speculate and another to think practically; the former is for explaining, the latter for acting.)",[]],"reflm:4224":["Pathological necessitation is internal and agrees with spontaneity, but it is nevertheless the condition of a possible external necessitation. Intellectual necessitation is founded not on passive but |\nactive conditions; this is therefore not opposed to freedom, because it is a liking that is not independent [of us] but made by ourselves. If we have the nearest causes, we need not know the connection with the most remote, nor how we come by this force which we have. The laws of motion are always the same, whatever their cause may be.",[]],"reflm:4225":["10\nSince freedom is a complete self-activity of the will, without being determined by stimuli (*stimulos*) or by anything else that affects the subject, all that matters in its case is the certainty of personality: namely, that it is conscious (g of itself) that it acts from its own choice [Willkühr], [it acts] that the will is active and not passive, neither through stimuli nor through alien impressions. Otherwise I should have to say: I am driven or moved to act thus or thus, which means as much as: I am not acting but suffering. If God governs the determinations of choice, then he acts; if the charms of things determine it necessarily, then they compel; in both cases the action springs not from me, but I am only the means of another cause.\nIn the world of sense nothing is comprehensible save what is made necessary (*necessitirt*) by preceding grounds. The actions of free choice are *phaenomena*; but their connection with a self-active subject and with (g the faculty of) reason is intellectual; accordingly the determination of free choice cannot be subjected to the *leges sensitivae* [laws of sensibility].\nThe question whether freedom is possible is perhaps the same as |\nthe question whether (g man) is a true person, and whether the I is possible in a being of external determinations.\nThe I is an inexplicable representation. It is an intuition that is unchangeable.",[]],"reflm:4226":["(g Freedom does not consist in this, that the opposite might have pleased us, but only in this, that our pleasure was not passively compelled.)\nThe *arbitrium sensitivum* [sensitive choice] without consciousness is *brutum* [brute].\nThe *arbitrium* [choice] is determined to an action either actively or passively. In the first case it is nevertheless free, even though the *motiva* [motives] are objectively necessitating (*obiective necessitantia*). For it acts [without that] of necessity, not because its subject, in accordance with its passive property, sensibility, is affected by the objects. Human choice is never passively determined. The *arbitrium* that is merely actively determined is a good will. But whether (g although) a good will is in itself determined to good actions (g and free), even if the substance is *causatum alterius* [caused by another], yet the admixture with the sensitive amounts to the same as if the *rationes ad determinandum* [grounds for determining] were to become *incompletae* [incomplete]. Therefore the human will is determined from itself to no kind of actions. Its choice is thus indeed a free, but an undetermined choice (the divine is determined). The *arbitrium brutum* is determined *secundum rationes* [intellectuales] *sensitivas* [according to sensitive grounds], the divine *secundum intellectuales* [according to intellectual grounds], the human by neither. Its actions could all have occurred in accordance with reason. Hence it is free. But is there not then a determining ground, certainly not in the choice of man in general, yet in the circumstances and conditions? And if this is not so, whence do the actions actually come about? Answer: All the *stimuli* of sensitive choice cannot, after all, make the active in man into the passive. The higher choice still decides for itself; but why it decides at times on the side |\nof sensibility, at times on that of reason, of this no law can be given, because there is no constant law of the two powers [Kräfte].\nIn the case of an active principle (*principium*) the state has no influence at all on the subject in determining its action, neither the past nor the present state. The subject is at all times from itself the source of the actions. But why it acts thus and not otherwise. [The sentence breaks off here.]",[]],"reflm:4227":["Were human beings wholly intellectual, all their actions would be actively determined, yet free, and would be contingent only in respect of the changeable occasions. These actions, together with the rewards, could also be imputed to them, even though they were creatures of a higher being. For they would have to be regarded as self-active principles and as worthy objects of his goodness. Were they wholly sensible, their actions would be solely passively determined; nothing could be imputed to them, and they would be capable of no rewards and punishments. Now they are partly sensible, partly intellectual, yet in such a way that sensibility indeed cannot make the intellectual passive, but the intellectual, for its part, cannot overcome the actions otherwise than through a certain measure of preponderance over sensibility. Therefore man is determined neither actively nor passively; and since sensibility as well as the strength of reason depends on circumstances, his actions depend partly on the circumstances, partly on the use of his reason, and cannot be wholly imputed to him. If one takes it most strictly, he is free only [in respect of] the possibility of doing something good, in which freedom properly consists. But whether the action actually springs from this principle or from the sensitive depends on the conditions. It is as in a game, where every throw can win, regardless of the preceding and accompanying circumstances.",[]],"reflm:4228":["Through the consciousness of our personality we see ourselves in the intellectual world and find ourselves free. Through our dependence on impressions we see ourselves in the world of sense and find ourselves determined. Our intuitions of bodies all belong to the world of sense; accordingly experiences agree with the laws of the world of sense, [that is,] with laws of determining grounds. But our intellectual intuitions of the free will do not agree with the laws of the *phaenomena*.",[]],"reflm:4229":["Freedom cannot be divided. Man is either wholly free or not free at all, because he can either act from an active principle or depends on conditions.",[]],"reflm:4230":["292. E II 1284. I 356.\nM 292':\nThe pure doctrine of the soul, since it presupposes no principles (g fundamental propositions) from experience, can consist of no other parts than the following:\n1. [Erke] Absolute consideration of it from transcendental concepts: substance. Simple (g single). Contingent.* (g whether I or another in me effects the *actus* [acts] of choice, if I am a contingent being.) Finite. (s imperishable.)\n2. Comparison of it with other beings (g transcendental concepts) which we know through experience:\na, bodies in general. Immaterial;\nb,** other thinking natures of the world. Those that are beneath or alongside or above man (*bruta* [brutes], *incolae planetarum* [inhabitants of the planets], *angeli* [angels]).\n3. Connection of it with the body:\n(g first difficulty: how merely inwardly working forces can have outward consequences, [and] how merely outwardly working forces are to produce inward alterations. Second: how the union of an immaterial being with a material one is possible.)\na. Possibility of it. *Commercium animae et corporis* [commerce of soul and body]. Seat of the soul (g they cannot alter it). *Sensorium commune* [common sensorium]. (g *Ideae materiales* [material ideas]. *Materialismus* [subli] *vel subiectivus vel formalis* [materialism, either subjective or formal].) (s *Status animae in diverso statu hominis* [state of the soul in the different states of man]. *Sedes animae* [seat of the soul].) (s Principle of life. Freedom.)\nb. Beginning. Birth. Before birth.\nc. End. Death. After death (s *metempsychosis* [transmigration of souls]). (g Separation. Cessation of dependence on the body. Whether it is a new dependence? Place.) (g Resurrection. Sleep of the soul, ethereal body. *Metempsychosis*. Place immediately after death: in the grave, on the earth, in heaven and hell. Pneumatists.)\n*** *Anima est subiectum cogitans pendens a corpore (sentiens). Animus activum qvidem, sed adstrictum conditionibus corporis. Mens activum in et per se.* [The *anima* is a thinking subject dependent on the body (sensing). The *animus* is indeed active, but bound by conditions of the body. The *mens* is active in and through itself.]\n4. Connection with other spirits. Appearances [Erscheinungen]: inner, outer.\nM 292: |\n*(s [Its] self-activity (s freedom). My state is not necessary through my nature, but I am free. Higher powers of knowledge and of the will. Power over oneself, possibility of morality.)\nM 292: |\n**(s General condition of inner sense: consciousness. 2, Merely an object of inner sense: spirit.)\nM 292':\n***(g Soul. Mind [Gemüth]. Spirit. A pain strikes the soul through sensation; it is in the mind (g arises) through reflection, and in the spirit through disapproval of itself. †)\nM 292: |\n(† s In the one the sensation displeases, in the second the whole state, in the third the person to himself. The first feels ill, the second [is] esteems himself unhappy, the third worthy of unhappiness. (At times before the misfortune is yet actual.) )\n[Editorial note:] Above the Reflexion, between 46719-23, 4681-5, and in the margin at the left, the following s-additions further stand on M 292':\n(s 1. Soul as a thinking being: a. Belonging to the world of sense. b. To the intelligible [world]. 2. As dignity [würde].)\n(s *Existentia corporis contra idealismum* [existence of the body against idealism].)\n(s NB. I am substance, I am simple, etc. etc. The I *in sensu stricto* [in the strict sense] contains all this.)\n(s I, as object of inner sense, as thinking, am not an object of outer sense. Thoughts are not *phaenomena* in space.)\n(s *bruta* without inner sense, consciousness: analogues of me.\n*spiritus* – – outer – –\nmere animality, mere intelligence. Humanity (a priori).)",[]],"reflm:4231":["In theoretical philosophy the objects are either *obiecte rationis purae* [objects of pure reason] (s *et etiam principia intellectualia* [and also intellectual principles]): *philosophia transscendentalis* [transcendental philosophy]; or *obiecte sensuum* [objects of the senses] (s *sed principia* (g *vel*) *intellectualia vel* [*sens*] *empirica* [but intellectual or empirical principles]): *Natura* [nature]. *Physiologia*.\n*Physiologia* [is] either of inner sense: *Psychologia*;\nor of outer: *Physica*;\neach of the two either from rational or from empirical principles (*vtraque vel e principiis rationalibus vel empiricis*).",[]],"reflm:4232":["objects of pure reason and empirical principles according to *analogiam* (analogy), i.e. the law of a certain philosophical proportion, [or] from the given sensible world (*mundo sensibili dato*) either backwards among the antecedents to a term *a priori* (*principium*): God, or forwards among the consequences to a term *a posteriori* (*finem*): the world.\n(g 1. Where the intellectual is the first principle,\n2. Where it is the last consequence.)",[]],"reflm:4233":["Opposite 14116-18:\nFrom immateriality it follows that soul and body do not act on one another through contact, and indeed cannot even be present to one another through it; and that their community with matter must rest on that in matter which is no object of appearance, etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:4234":["Opposite 14119-21:\nStrictly, all proof of the simple nature of the soul comes down to this: that the soul is an immediate intuition of itself through the absolute unity I, which is the *singularis* (singular) of the acts of thinking! For the proof taken from this, that many thinking subjects cannot make up merely one thought, means just as |\nmuch as: the I is required for this, and thinking is always an act in the singular.",[]],"reflm:4235":["At the top of the page:\nThe soul in man is not only simple but also single [*einzeln*]. *anima sentiens, vegetativa, rationalis* (the sentient, the vegetative, the rational soul).",[]],"reflm:4236":["The whole difficulty rests on how a body can produce thoughts in the soul, and the soul, through its will, produce movements. Every active subject alters the inner state in the other, and this external relation is *influxus* (influx) or external action, whose *phaenomenon* (phenomenon) the [text breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4237":["No matter is living; consequently death, i.e. lifelessness, |\nwhich follows upon life, is no event that can befall the human body. Man has two kinds of life: 1. the animal, 2. the spiritual. The latter is personality, taken not merely *potentialiter* (potentially) but *actualiter* (actually). In the existence of the human soul there are to be considered: 1. the existence of the substance; 2. life in general (animal) as a soul; 3. personality, i.e. life as a human spirit. The question therefore is whether it is possible that the human soul, even without body, should be a particular person (at least it could then not become conscious of its outer state, since this requires that one sense oneself through the very same sense by which one perceives external things). This question does not ask whether certain interruptions of its personality (such as sleep) take place, but whether the soul could ever become conscious of itself without a body.",[]],"reflm:4238":["314. E II 1263.\nM 314':\nEither man (g long ago, before he was born) represented something great before his birth, or after (g his) death he will represent nothing at all. (g If his origin is to be ascribed to birth — —) Consider the contingency of births. |\nM 314: |\n(g If one wishes to dispute their contingency, then all bad kings are Pharaohs, since God, if he does not produce the wickedness, yet furthers it a little in order to prove his power.) |\nM 314': |\nIf man has a spiritual part which thinks even after the complete decay of the body, why should it not have thought rationally before it assumed the body? If matter had to help it to think, then matter will leave it without thought [and] when it is separated from it. But how was this spirit compelled to animate this matter? We do not know the world of spirits, nor how matter stands under its command. I do not know how philosophers can be so coy about an incomprehensibility.",[]],"reflm:4239":["315. E II 1306. 1268. 1264.\nM 315':\nIf one can infer from the nature of the grown man to his eternal duration, then the newborn man too must give ground for this hope. So too the embryo, the *ovulum* (egg), the *ovulum* of the *ovulum*. This claim to eternity cannot depend on the contingent connection with the body; for that perfection which cannot arise without connection with corporeal things cannot continue without it either. Hence human souls had a spiritual life even before the body; hence animal life cannot decide their eternal destiny.\nThe continuing life of the soul is distinct from its immortality. The first means that it will not die, perhaps by reason of particular divine arrangements; the second, that it cannot die naturally. The proofs of the first are moral, those of the second metaphysical. The enduring life of the soul consists not in the continuance of its substance or of its remaining powers, but of its personality. If it can no longer attain to consciousness, it is like a tree that has died and can no longer put forth sap.\nThe physical aspect of this life is of no significance, because it concerns only the contingent connection with the corporeal world, which is not our natural state; but the moral aspect, which can be found only in the soul in accordance with its spiritual nature, is bound up with the spiritual life, and because the moral belongs to the inner worth of the person, it is indelible, whereas good and ill fortune, belonging merely to the fleeting state, lose all worth after their short duration*. Hence we must esteem this life lightly. We should, however, have reason to esteem it highly if the physical aspect of it were the condition of the beginning of the metaphysical. The physical [*Leben*: life] aspect of life therefore remains a trifle; birth and death are the beginning and end of a scene in which only morality is of consequence, and indeed only in this way, that one [it] not act contrary to it.\nM 315:\n*(g To grieve over pain, above all past pain, is soft or foolish; but over transgressions, it is fitting and praiseworthy. The latter therefore belongs to our person, hence insofar as we are spiritual beings.)",[]],"reflm:4240":["316'. E II 1277. 1265.\nM 315':\nMatter is lifeless. This proposition attaches not merely to the concept of matter; rather, even if the parts of matter possessed life, then without a common principle which united the many intermediate causes, no unity of life and no determination according to a common choice [*Willkühr*] would be possible. The principle of life in man, the proper subject of his personality, so far as it animates matter, is entitled soul, and the life of man is an animal life. But if this principle of life can live by itself alone, this life would be a spiritual one. Since the lifeless can be no ground of life, but must rather be a hindrance to it, pure spiritual life will be the original and independent life; animal life, on the other hand, is derived and restricted. Birth is therefore not a beginning of life in general, but of animal life, and a degree of lifelessness, and the complete spiritual life begins after the death of the animal. The other world will present not other objects, but the same objects seen differently (namely *intellectualiter*, intellectually) and in other relations to us. And the knowledge of things through divine intuition, likewise |\nthe feeling of blessedness through it, is no longer the world but heaven.\nBut how has the soul been connected with this body? This is the same as asking: what is the beginning and cause of sensibility? On this footing the present life is only the appearance and image of the spiritual, and the vice which we practise here, |\nM 316': |\nwould, if our eyes were opened, present us in our bad character in the spiritual world, and the punishments here would be only weak copies of those there; death is nothing other than the end of sensibility. But is not vice perhaps itself only an effect of sensibility, and must it not finally cease with the end of all sensibility? Nothing can be imputed to a man save what belongs to his character and to what is peculiar to him. The vices which one man practises in preference to another do not always indicate a more vicious character; most of them are *delicta fortunae* (offences of fortune). Take away everything that does not stand in your power: how much can you then ascribe to yourselves? Meanwhile, in the state of sensibility the character may be vicious and therefore also punishable, which in the intellectual state is only [faulty] deficient, as always in a creature which from the part it knows wants to make applications that fit the whole.",[]],"reflm:4241":["The concepts of God (and of the universal subordination under the law of necessity), of freedom (of contingency and independence from the present world), and of the other world (the relation of our freedom and independent nature to God) are the three concepts of reason which have an inner importance, are connected with one another, and are the ground of the significance of the whole of metaphysics.\nAll modes of knowledge of reason, finally, have a practical reference. The concepts of freedom are, from both sides, problems for reason; but in respect of past actions we must assume contingency, of present actions necessity, and of future actions both at once, if we are to act at all.\n(s We have a natural instinct to direct our care, even without reflection, to [zuk] what will happen in the world after our death, just as the butterfly, before it dies, carefully secures and provides for its eggs. We become avaricious in old age and want our property to be well employed. We do not want people to think ill of us. We spare the dead, as if they could be harmed by it. We miss them in company. Food is brought to the dead. The miser likes (g that) his money should stay together after death. Through this prolongation everything in the world hangs together. Funeral rites of crude nations.)\n(s Civilised people take leave of life as of society, as if they supposed they would see it once more. They shrink from dying as poltrons, or from remaining in memory as base persons, or from offending those who are left behind and making them ill-disposed towards themselves.)",[]],"reflm:4242":["The concept of God is a concept of completion, either of speculation in respect of things, or of morality, and it arises from the following fundamental concepts. 1. A necessary being. 2. The supreme cause. 3. The all-sufficient being (instead of the most perfect, because |\nthe latter does not on that account contain everything). The first concept is sufficient to itself. The second completes the series of subordinates (*seriem subordinatorum*), the third that of coordinates (*coordinatorum*). One can infer from the one to the other. 1. The necessary being is the ground of everything, and therefore also the first cause. 2. The first cause is necessary, and therefore also the most perfect being. 3. The most perfect being is necessary and the first cause. The first concept ends all questions about existence.",[]],"reflm:4243":["The idea of God is occasioned: 1. by a need of the understanding (*vide supra*, see above); 2. by a need of the will. The latter arises from the lack of supreme grounds from which to derive one's fortunes in respect of happiness and one's verdict in respect of morality. The former [i.e. happiness] is the strongest incentive, and in this the incentive of averting evils is the most powerful. We require an object of worship from which, since our powers do not suffice, wishes, prayers and submissions can fetch our happiness. If the concept of a Deity is generated from this ground of physical needs, it is at first thought of as terrible. To this a benevolent *principium* is commonly set alongside.",[]],"reflm:4244":["Of the necessary being, the original being, the supreme and first of all beings.\nEvery thing is in itself thoroughly determined, but not every concept is thoroughly determined. In order, therefore, to know a thing completely it is not enough to know what it is; we must know also what it is not; for if this were omitted, it remains undetermined whether, |\nbeyond what is known in it, still more may not belong to it. Accordingly the possibility of a thing is thoroughly determined only through the whole of possibility of everything, and whoever wishes to know anything completely must know everything. Now of all opposed predicates the negative can be thought only through the *opposita realia* (real opposites) (what is to be removed I can know only in so far as I know that which is to be cancelled). Accordingly the possibility of all things is given through their being contained in the sum total of infinite reality, and through the limitation of that reality. There is therefore a universal *principium reale* of all possibilities, and the possibilities are only positions and limitations, effected with respect to a certain concept in the *omnitudo realitatum* (the all of realities). Since these limitations are thought only respectively, and the *ens illimitatum* (unlimited being) is laid down absolutely as ground, the *ens fundamentale* (fundamental being) is not itself something merely possible, but is what lies at the ground of everything possible. It is therefore posited absolutely, and all *respectus* (relations) are contained in it. Accordingly [the] Reality taken in general breaks off.\n(g The proposition, nothing comes from nothing, indicates that possibility cannot be thought absolutely by itself, but presupposes something that is [was]. That we can afterwards think each thing as merely possible comes from this, that once we have a determinate concept we do not ask: whence?, but, regarding it as given, cancel the thing itself, though yet not everything in general.)\nI assert: the proof of a single original being from the derivation of all possibilities is that which directs all the others in human understanding and dwells in each. The old proposition, nothing comes from nothing, means nothing other than that the possibilities must lie in something, and that things cannot find a place at all unless there is something by which they can be thought through determinations. Even in the works of men all possibilities are mere modifications |\nof something that is, at least of our concepts given somehow; and with every possibility we represent to ourselves its agreement with everything else, and consequently a certain unity of the thinkable.",[]],"reflm:4245":["All things seem to lie in an infinity; for they cannot be undetermined in respect of anything possible, and since, *quoad praedicata affirmantia* (as regards affirmative predicates), they are a part of this infinite, but *quoad negantia* (as regards negating predicates) are limited, they are (g they) necessarily in a thoroughgoing relation, and the complete representation of each thing must express this, determinately or indeterminately. Even in a number, e.g. 8, everything else is excluded from the infinite multitude, although in 10 strictly no other possibilities or realities are added in thought, but only the same act is repeated.\nAll finite things are therefore, in respect of their possibility, dependent upon a being of all beings, which contains all reality and is independent of all others; for in their thoroughgoing determination they refer themselves always to the highest reality, which is the [ground] original ground of their possibility.",[]],"reflm:4246":["Possibility belongs necessarily to things; thus the possibilities of things have no further ground; but a being is nevertheless necessary through which possibilities are given. In relation to it each possibility is only a limitation of the highest reality, and thoroughgoing possibility is strictly the relation to the highest reality in its determination. The original being itself cannot be regarded as possible, but is the *principium* of possibility. Even the possibility of all fictitious beings rests merely on limitations. The multiplicity of things rests on the manifoldness of the limitations, and what they have that is different are limits, |\nhence relations to an original being. Accordingly there are not many original beings. The necessity in the existence of this being needs no special proof. For that it is the ground of all possibility, and that this is only a derivation from it, already means as much as: nothing is possible without a *realissimum*; consequently this being itself does not belong among merely possible beings. For otherwise, if one wishes to make the possibilities by way of composition, a being in which there are many realities is not more possible than one in which there are few, and in the most real being, as the most composite, there is the greatest contingency.",[]],"reflm:4247":["Possibility consists precisely in the actuality of the reality in which, through limitations, all things are given; e.g. a triangle is possible through the actuality of space, and [it is] its possibility consists in this, that through limitations it lies in this space. Possibility is thus a derived concept, derived not from the actuality of this thing, but from [any] the actuality which contains the first *data* for things in general. In all limited things possibility (its concept) precedes actuality, because the reality given for things precedes the limitation of it. But not in the case of the original being.\n*conceptus terminator* (limiting concept).",[]],"reflm:4248":["The highest reality cannot be limited, since otherwise it would cease to be the ground of the possibility of all things. Just as space cannot properly be limited, but only the things in space.\nWe therefore know the original being and its necessity not from itself, that is, not from the concepts which we have of the inner predicates of a being in which existence is contained, but relatively, from its relation to our concepts of the possibility of all things. It is a limiting concept (*Grenzbegriff*) *a priori*. We assume it without contradiction, we need it for the completion of our reason, and we can make no well-grounded objections against it.",[]],"reflm:4249":["According to our concepts, the diversity of things rests upon the limitations of given realities. It cannot, however, be regarded as the limitations of diverse things, since this diversity in turn presupposes limitations. It arises, therefore, from the limitation of the thing, or of its consequences, [through in] whose concept all reality is contained. For the realities must be given, since without this one could not think them. The limitation is either that of abstraction or that of determination.\nThe inferences from this are given from the possibility of modes of knowledge through our reason, not from the things themselves. Consequently the argument *ad homines* holds.",[]],"reflm:4250":["This proof from mere concepts is not complete without confirmation by the empirical proof that the principles of reason agree with experience, although they are not borrowed from it. Conversely, an empirical argument without this proof of reason is not sufficient for a determinate concept of the original being.",[]],"reflm:4251":["If an object interests us in action, then [that quality] that judgment of it which we would pass upon it according to the natural laws of our reason is [although] even though the conditions of our judgment [nothing] contain no data of it, nevertheless practically certain (e.g. of God and of the other world) (g i.e. according to rules of prudence and morality).\n(Moral certainty is a mere probability, when a duty is concerned.)",[]],"reflm:4252":["The limited is regarded as contingent for this reason, that the opposite reality is necessarily presupposed in every possible limitation.",[]],"reflm:4253":["a. The proof: 1. That all possibility must be regarded as a determination of the highest reality, and presupposes such a being, rests on this: the *omnimoda determinatio* (thoroughgoing determination) is possible only through the *omnitudo* [of the prae] *metaphysica*; in this, however, the negative predicates presuppose the *realia*; and since every *ens limitatum* is possible only through the presupposition of a *reale*, everything presupposes a *realissimum*. This, however, is the necessary subordination of concepts according to the law of the human understanding.\n2. The absolute necessity of a thing is a limiting concept. It cannot be seen through contradiction. That which contains the matter for all possibility is its real ground and [its opposite] is necessary *realiter* (really).\nIn itself the necessity of a perfect being is no better to be seen than that of an imperfect one.\nb. The proof *a contingentia* [setzt vo] infers from concepts, namely that the limited cannot be necessary. Consequently it must |\npresuppose the necessity in the *realissimum*. The reason is this: the general concept of a being that has limits comprehends many sorts of things under it; but the unlimited is a *conceptus singularis*./\nc. The physico-theological proof infers from the incomplete knowledge of the perfection of the world to the highest perfection of God, and from there back to the greatest perfection of the world.\nd. The moral proof [infers] from the geometrical, but objective, necessity of actions under the moral laws, but also to the necessary assumption of a ruler.\nTo the [incompl.] negative proofs, namely that the world is not from itself, belongs that from the *primo motore* (prime mover).\nThat the *ens realissimum* or *summum* has understanding.",[]],"reflm:4254":["327. E II 1654. 1157.\nM 327':\nThe existence of God can be known only through the understanding, because its concept is a highest concept of the understanding, which is [not related to] not limited to objects of experience, but whose all-sufficiency (*omnisufficientia*) is intellectual.\nIt cannot, however, be known from an assumed, arbitrary concept, and so not in itself (unless it be through immediate intuitions).\nHence it can be known only in relation to the world, but to that which is valid for every world; for this alone is intellectual.\nThe intelligible world is: |\nM 327: |\nthat whose concept holds for every world; consequently it contains not physical laws but objective and moral ones.\nM 327':\nThe intellectual concept of the world is therefore the concept of perfection. The world of the understanding is therefore the moral world, and its laws hold for every world as objective laws of perfection.\nThus we can infer only from the necessity of the moral laws in this world, as in every other, to the original and universally valid ground of the essential ends of things, hence to the existence of the most perfect being. Its concept is that which makes us perfect, and is therefore practically certain. *Mundus intelligibilis* (the intelligible world) is the world of rational beings, considered according to objective laws of freedom.\nThe supreme principle of morality is the agreement of free actions with the original and universally valid laws of the power of choice (*Willkühr*), or with that which |\nM 327: |\nis essentially bound up with the ends in the world. Thus the concept of God is breaks off?",[]],"reflm:4255":["The proposition of the existence of God is an *hypothesis originaria* (g *tam naturaliter quam obiective*), both of the comprehensibility, the possibility and the actuality of things through the understanding (theoretical), and also as a principle of actions (practical). The proof proceeds through a *dilemma theoreticum et practicum*.",[]],"reflm:4256":["M 327':\nIf I deny the existence of God, I must regard myself either as a fool, if I want to be (or am) an honest man, or as a villain, if I want to be a prudent man. There are proofs *per deductionem contrarii ad absurditatem* or *turpitudinem* (by deduction of the contrary to absurdity or to baseness). Moral faith presupposes that the constraining breaks off.\nM 327:\n(s If one denies God, the virtuous man is a fool and the prudent man a rogue. Prudence and morality can be united in the practical only if I assume the existence of God.\nWithout this proposition the [laws] moral laws of the *mundus intelligibilis*, which yet command universally, would not hold for the present world, and what is universally true would be false in the particular case.)\nM 327':\n(g There are proofs *per deductionem contrarii ad absurdum logicum* and also such *per deductionem ad absurdum morale*: namely, that he who does not assume it is a [villain] (g fool, visionary,) deceiver. Both are\n|\nM 327: |\nnot genetic, but proofs of the necessity of assuming something.)",[]],"reflm:4257":["All negations are limits (g for they can be thought only through the *oppositum reale* (real opposite), just as every line, figure and body is thought only through limitation of space. But why not numbers? Because there the reality is regarded not as given but as arbitrarily invented). Everything limited is thought as enclosed in the greater, hence everything limited in something else. That which is distinguished from everything limited is unlimited; everything limited is therefore in the infinite, which is on that account infinite *realiter* (really) (without end) and all-sufficient.",[]],"reflm:4258":["Because [we] the [unbe-] that which we can limit need not be necessary in itself, the infinity, which contains as it were the |\nmatter for all possibility, is itself given through a higher ground. The *ens summum* is therefore *realissimum* as a ground.",[]],"reflm:4259":["The possibilities seem in general to signify nothing other than the relation, through the limits, in the given infinite as the matter.",[]],"reflm:4260":["Because all diversity of things is only a manifoldness of limits, owing to the relation which everything possible has to all reality, the concept of a highest reality, which is unique, lies at the ground of everything.",[]],"reflm:4261":["One can try to prove the existence of God either as a necessary *hypothesis* for the knowledge of the existence of other things, or as a *dogma a priori*. The latter is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4262":["The reason why we represent the (g necessary) existence of a most real being as more comprehensible to us than the necessity of a limited one is this: we can think only the limited things and their negative determinations, in that we limit the more real concept. Accordingly a greater possibility cannot be composed of smaller ones. And the general possibility is no aggregate of all particular possibilities; these are rather consequences of that. The limited thing is that which has some part of |\nall possible reality and is thereby distinguished from others. But something that distinguishes from everything possible is thinkable only through the whole. Duration and external relation (g or figure and magnitude) can be known only through limitation of infinite time and infinite space, e.g. the triangle.",[]],"reflm:4263":["In order that an *ens realissimum* [most real being] may be possible, it is not enough that the realities do not contradict one another. Among all possible subjects there must also be one that unites them all as a common ground.",[]],"reflm:4264":["All predicates of God concern either his existence or his attributes. The first are possibility, *omnitudo*, necessity, eternity, independence (relat:), all-sufficiency, omnipresence, etc., etc. The second are his omnipotence, understanding and will: the almighty, living God. To the understanding belong: 1. that it is the *prototypon* and that it is independent; 2. that it is directed originally to everything.",[]],"reflm:4265":["If an existence is to be inferred from mere concepts, the necessity of existence must be contained in the mere possibility.",[]],"reflm:4266":[") M 332'. E II 1664.\nIf a thing's necessary existence is to be inferred from its attributes, then either existence itself must be regarded as |\nan attribute, or the concept of absolute necessity must be regarded as one that is identical with the given attributes.",[]],"reflm:4267":["The physico-theological concepts of God yield only an idea of God's greatness that is relative to ourselves. This idea suffices practically, but not theoretically. God is wise, but we merely compare him, through our understanding, with our own. 'God is great' means just as much as: how small we are! The absolute concept of d breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4268":["(g We are to infer from the concept of God the necessity of another world; hence it must not be inferred from this one, since this one does not wholly agree with him.)\nThe physico-theological proof gives only an opinion of God's existence, namely a certainty of the existence of an intelligent author. A faith must still be added to this, in order to represent him as supremely perfect. From experience we do not infer a most perfect world (we rather assume it to be very imperfect, in order to prove the future world), but only an intelligent author. Of him we believe that he who can do so much will be able to do everything.\nThe transcendental proof, too, can be carried out only relatively to our *hypothesis subiectiva* of reason. Why should the practical proof not be capable of being carried out from the subjective hypothesis of the moral laws, since otherwise the *leges morales* would be *effectu vacuae* [void of effect], and the virtuous man a visionary (*phantast*), that is, one not in harmony with this world?",[]],"reflm:4269":["Eternity (infinite duration), as a *phaenomenon*, is infinite time. [existence in] Time is the measure of the duration |\nof finite things, so far as they appear. Existence in time is contingency in the appearance [for the non-being and its]. The existence of that which is eternal is not infinitely limited by non-being. It is like infinite time itself. Contingent existence, however, is in the following moment shortened by the preceding one, and rather changes its place in time than increases its duration therein. (s except only on the presupposition [of it]. What one has lived has not enlarged our existence, except only through the present consequences.) It is always the mere boundary of time, which is limited by an infinite negation of the past and the future.",[]],"reflm:4270":["God determines the knowledge of each thing from (g and in) the knowledge of the whole. His knowledge is therefore intuitive, and an idea. We collect ours from partial representations; [there] the whole precedes the part originally, in respect of possibility.",[]],"reflm:4271":["The world, represented sensibly, has no first, that is, no beginning; but through reason it has. That is, it has no first in time, but it has a first in the order of cause. The two have nothing in common with one another.\nIn the same way the world, represented sensibly in respect of magnitude, has no boundaries through empty space, but it does have limits of reality.",[]],"reflm:4272":["To *auctoribus coordinatis* [co-ordinate authors] a *beneficium divisum* [divided benefit] is imputed; to *auctoribus plene subordinatis* [fully subordinate authors] the same benefit is imputed to the *auctor primarius* [primary author] *pro indiviso* [as undivided]. Hence I cannot impute my happiness in different parts to the creature and to God. I may rather ascribe it directly to nature and to my own diligence, and so to divine providence by means of the order of nature. It is also irreverent to regard God as a mere *causa concurrens* [concurrent cause]; he is *solitaria* [sole], but [per remot] *superior*.",[]],"reflm:4273":["In every judgment we represent an analytic unity; among the parts of a whole, a synthetic unity. This analytic unity, however, is not always known *per analysin*.\nWhere this unity is not conceived in identity, there is made not a judgment but a comparison of two judgments through a common mark. E.g., Man is [rational] an animal, the horse is an animal.",[]],"reflm:4274":["Disjunctive propositions are comparisons of two propositions having one subject and two opposed predicates. If [also] the subjects are different while the predicates are opposed, they are propositions of distinction. Propositions of comparison have two subjects and one predicate.",[]],"reflm:4275":["Analysis of reason. *Principium contradictionis, identitatis*: gives objectively valid propositions.\nSynthesis of reason: various laws (*axiomata subreptitia*), subjectively valid propositions.\nThe conditions of our reason, which 1. cognizes objects only mediately and not through intuition; hence the *conditiones* by means |\nof which a knowledge of anything is possible to it, and the necessity of positing something *primitive* and without *conditiones*.\nIntuitions of the senses (g in respect of sensible form and matter) give synthetic propositions that are objective. Crusius explains the real principles of reason [for] according to the *systema praeformationis* (g from subjective *principia*); Locke, like Aristotle, according to the *influxus physicus*; Plato and Malebranche from the *intuitus intellectualis*; we according to *epigenesis*, from the use of the natural laws of reason.\n(g The common human understanding, *sensus veri et falsi*, is a *qualitas occulta*.)\nAntithesis: a method of reason for discovering the opposition of the subjective laws. If this, through the *vitium subreptionis*, is held to be objective, it is scepticism (*in sensu obiectivo*). If it is only a [critique of the subject] propaedeutic, it is the *methodus sceptica* for the determination of the subjective laws of reason. *Antithesis subiectiva*.",[]],"reflm:4276":["Pure sensibility. Pure reason. Mixed.\n*ubi, quando*.\nTranscendental philosophy.\nAesthetic is the philosophy of sensibility, whether of knowledge or of feeling.\nLogic, either general or [tran] specifically transcendental.\nCategories are the universal acts of reason by which we think an object in general (for the representations, appearances). Aristotle.\nPredicaments are *modi* of the categories.\n3 categories. Thesis, synthesis,\nhypothesis.*\n*coordinatio*, *subordinatio*\n(g as well in mere categorical judgments as in all three kinds of judgments)\n*Praedicamenta*.\nThesis: *Possibile, actuale, necessarium cum oppositis*.\n—\n—\nSynthesis: *Qvantitas*.\n10 categories of Aristotle. Pythagoras. 1. *Substantia, Accidens*, 2. *qvalitas*, 3. *qvantitas*, 4. *relatio*, 5. *actio*, 6. *passio*, 7. *qvando*, 8. *ubi*, 9. *situs*, 10. *habitus*.\n*Postpraedicamenta*: *oppositum, prius, simul, motus, habere*.\n*(g The acts of the understanding are either in respect of concepts, from whatever source they may be given, in their relation to one another through the understanding, even if the concepts and the ground of their comparison are given through the senses;\nor in respect of things, where the [object] understanding thinks an object in general, and the manner of positing something in general and its relations. The two differ only in this, that in the first the representations are posited, in the second the things are posited through the representations.)",[]],"reflm:4277":["In this world I can become happy only according to the laws of sensibility, and accordingly I depend upon the mechanical laws and upon the [animal] sensible arbitrary will (*Willkühr*) of men. (The latter might be in harmony, and then I too should be happy according to desert.)",[]],"reflm:4278":["Reason is bound by the law of thinking everything mediately, *regrediendo*; and yet in the progress it must think something originally.",[]],"reflm:4279":["All propositions predicate *per thesin* (g actual, possible, necessary) or *per synthesin* or *analysin*.\nThetic propositions have no predicate and can be known only in intuitions; or they express the immediate [representation] intuition of a thing.",[]],"reflm:4280":["All particular propositions are synthetic, but not all universal propositions are therefore analytic.\nIf a particular proposition is analytic, the particularity is an empty limitation.",[]],"reflm:4281":["Limitation differs from restriction in this, that the former concerns merely the universality, the latter the necessity of the condition. Reason can limit only through *restrictiones*.",[]],"reflm:4282":["On synthesis: that there are in metaphysics no (g free) hypotheses.\nOn method: one must not doubt any more what one has once been compelled to concede. In metaphysics one must certainly doubt when difficulties appear. The spirit of the whole is distinct from the spirit of trifles: subtle and great.",[]],"reflm:4283":["Concerning a principle, ask: 1. Is it synthetic or analytic? If it is the former:\n2. Is it derived from universal experience or a priori?\n3. Is it a principle of sensibility or of reason?\n4. Is it a ground of the possibility of appearances or of real concepts, *principium constitutivum* (constitutive principle)?\n5. If it is the latter: is it a *principium subiectivum* or *obiectivum* (subjective or objective principle)?\n6. If it is the former, the question is whether it is a *principium convenientiae* (principle of agreement) or a *principium concipiendi per se* (principle of conceiving through itself).",[]],"reflm:4284":["Metaphysics is not science, not erudition, but merely the understanding that knows itself; it is therefore only a correction of sound understanding and reason.\nErudition and wide reading are a means of making its doctrines practical through examples. [Die Wi andern Wi] It serves the other sciences as a boundary-determination and keeps man to his vocation [Bestimmung], as regards the use and the limits of his reason; it is logical self-knowledge. It is strangely bitter, because it beats down vain pride and takes away imagined knowledge; it makes our possessions more secure, but at the expense of the imagined ones, and prevents books from swelling.\nReligion gains the greatest profit from it. Through it religion is secured in everything that is moral in it, covered against fanaticism and unbelief, and freed from dependence upon scholastic subtlety. It brings it about that our actions can flow from the\n|\nsources of sound understanding, without having to consult the uncertain and ever changeable brooding of the schools.",[]],"reflm:4285":["Only that is entitled object which has a logical quality, e.g. subject, not relatively to other concepts but in itself. These objects [Gegenstände] are distinguished from appearances, which can indeed be brought into logical relations, but to which this logical quality does not belong absolutely. If a logical position runs parallel to an appearance, a position which goes to the data of the appearance and not to the relation of one representation to another, then this is objective, e.g. ground, cause.\nOnly thereby can we conceive appearances as corresponding to things, namely in that they are grounds of a possible and universally valid knowledge. But they can be so if they conform to the logical form.",[]],"reflm:4286":["1. What is an object?\n2. In what does the relation of a determination of the soul to something else consist?\n3. What is the ground of the agreement?\n4. By what do we distinguish what relates to the object and what to the subject?",[]],"reflm:4287":["Logical is what consists in comparison; real, what is posited in itself.",[]],"reflm:4288":["Possibility, actuality, and necessity, which arises from both, are not concepts of objects, nor predicates, but positions [Stellungen] of the object.\n1. Of the faculty of fiction. The object of fiction is possible. A fiction which cancels itself is no fiction. To every act of fiction it belongs that something be given. 2. The object of sense is actual. 3. The positing through reason is necessary. Hence the possibility of the sensible is thought in space and time. Numbers are the purest fictions, and their possibility is therefore possible through understanding quite without data; but not so the possibility of qualities, except only through combining and cancelling.",[]],"reflm:4289":["On the method, with regard to the certainty of the metaphysical axioms, 1. of appealing to the constant presupposition of them in all empirical cases and inferring from this their abstract universality.\n2. Because, with just the same right with which one would deny them, one would have to let the certainty of all other knowledge vanish.",[]],"reflm:4290":["Whether one cannot, through reason, invent something by analogy. Natural theology, because all realities do not contradict one another.\nThe question is why magnitudes are the only thing of which the |\nhuman understanding forms concepts out of itself, whose possibilities it sees for itself, and in whose relations it can arrive, through the principle of contradiction, at certainty and at infinite extension.",[]],"reflm:4291":["Metaphysics is not the mother of religion but its bulwark (g against false sophistry). It proves the existence of God not apodictically but *suppletorie* (by way of supplement), in that it replaces, through reason, the lack of morality in unbelief.",[]],"reflm:4292":["The conditions without which objects cannot be given are objective, although according to laws of sensibility. The conditions without which they (even if they have been given) cannot be known (understood) are objective. Those without which they cannot be comprehended [eingesehen] (known through reason)* are merely subjective; but these subjective conditions are objective with regard to the use of reason in experiences [cond] (*leges convenientiae*, laws of agreement).\n*(s possibility, necessity)",[]],"reflm:4293":["Philosophy has this peculiarity, that the universal thoughts can be set down first, whereas in mathematics they come last. Hence in meditation the general project comes first.",[]],"reflm:4294":["All sciences and arts teach skill; philo|sophy\nalone teaches the principles and rules for the use of all skill, according to the ultimate relations of understanding and of will, in that it fixes the object itself and its relation to man.",[]],"reflm:4295":["How the predicate lies in the subject belongs to metaphysics; how the [unt] subject stands under it, to logic.",[]],"reflm:4296":["5. E II 1055.\nM 5':\nThe proposition: everything has a ground, is a law of reason; but the law: everything that happens is constantly accompanied by something else, is a |\nM 5: |\nlaw of appearances.",[]],"reflm:4297":["Above and \nExternal possibility is greater than internal.\nHypothetical possibility is smaller than unconditioned possibility (absolute, in general, in every respect).\nWhat is possible in every respect is actual.",[]],"reflm:4298":["Possibility: agreement (*non repugnantia*, non-repugnance) with a rule; actuality: position simply; necessity: position according to a rule.\nThe first is thought without being given. The second is given without being thought. The third is given through being thought.",[]],"reflm:4299":["The relation (of an object) to perception (*perceptio*) is existence; to thought, possibility; to thought in so far as it determines existence, necessity.",[]],"reflm:4300":["A and not-A are *contradictorie opposita* (contradictory opposites); A and B are *disparata* (disparates). Between *contradictorie opposita* there is no third (that is, one which is neither A nor not-A).",[]],"reflm:4301":["Reality is *materia cognitionis* (matter of knowledge)*, whether it be used affirmatively or negatively.\n*(g [realities] are *constitutiva* of things, negations only of concepts.)\n(s Does the concept of the simple designate reality or negation?)",[]],"reflm:4302":["Above and \nThe object of the concept is possible, and therefore, through this concept, because it is universal, not thoroughly determined. The object of intuition is actual; the object of the intuition which is the ground of the concepts of it in general is necessary.\n(g Intuition itself is no object. Something can be a logical or a real object.)",[]],"reflm:4303":["On M § 54:\nThe *principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction) is the formal condition, but not the *principium* of all possibility.",[]],"reflm:4304":["Because without a concept we can think nothing, every object that we are to think must have a relation of agreement to the concept in general, that is, must be possible.\nAn object, in so far as it is given, is thoroughly determined, but not in so far as it is thought.",[]],"reflm:4305":["*Mutabilitas* (mutability) presupposes the existence of things in time, and therefore also *immutabilitas* (immutability). Hence, since absolute necessity is a concept of reason, the *ens necessarium* (necessary being) is neither *mutabile* nor *immutabile*. Thus *mutabilitas* or its opposite belongs only to *sensualia* (things of sense).",[]],"reflm:4306":["The inference from changeableness to contingency is a *metabasis eis allo genos* (a passing over into another kind), for I infer from a *sensitivum* to the intellectual.",[]],"reflm:4307":["In a stream without banks we can notice no change of our own position, nor of the position of the water itself.",[]],"reflm:4308":["First, the contingency of the state is not to be confounded with the contingency of the thing. The thing remains.\nSecondly, the changeableness of the state is not to be confounded with the contingency of the state.\nThirdly, changeableness is a proof of a sensible contingency of the state.",[]],"reflm:4309":["At the top of the margin \nThe whole play of changes seems to derive from real oppositions. Since one reality drives the thing out of another state, no motion can be generated without producing just as much [in] opposed motion; so there are two forces which bring about the continuous change of direction.",[]],"reflm:4310":["Only one being is metaphysically perfect; all others have perfections but are not perfect. Allness [Allheit] is lacking.\nOne sees, nevertheless, that realities are regarded as grounds of perfection and negations as defects, not merely as not-being, but as defects in totality.",[]],"reflm:4311":["What is opposed to totality is limitation.\nWhat is opposed to universality is restriction.",[]],"reflm:4312":["*Totum reale* (real whole): in which there is *nexus* (connection).*\n*Totum ideale* (ideal whole): in which there is *coniunctio* (conjunction).\n*(g if this is possible by the addition of parts, it is a *compositum* (composite).)",[]],"reflm:4313":["The existence of a thing, in so far as it is determined in time, is its state; the state is that either of coexisting or of successive determinations. An original being [Urwesen] is in no state.",[]],"reflm:4314":["The whole belongs to magnitude, namely to *omnitudo* [allness]. The *compositum* [composite] belongs to relation, and specifically to that of *coordinatio reciproca* [reciprocal coordination]. [It is] that which is possible through the (mutual) *coniunctio* [conjunction]. A *quantum* is not in itself a *compositum*, but its concept can arise *per compositionem idealem* [through ideal composition]. Hence we speak of *composita realia* [real composites]. The *compositum ex accidentibus* [composite of accidents] is collective; that *ex substantiis* [of substances] is an aggregate.",[]],"reflm:4315":["We can think of particular spaces only in so far as we cut something out of the universal space. Space precedes things; |\ntherefore it can be no predicate of things, but only a law of sensibility, which, as the condition of all possible appearances, certainly precedes all actual ones.",[]],"reflm:4316":["The predicate of space and of place does not belong to substance, but to the sensible representation. Bodily parts are comparative substances according to laws of sensibility, and *phaenomena substantiata*. The quantity of the substance in a body (not of its space) is to be judged from the magnitude of the effect at the same velocity. In respect of this there is nothing simple. The question now is whether, in respect of space, there is something simple, and a determinate alleged number.",[]],"reflm:4317":["All axioms in which the subject has no content of sensibility, and which yet restrict the subject universally through the condition of sensibility, are false. They are corrected [reducirt] when [to it:] the condition of sensibility is given back to the subject.",[]],"reflm:4318":["The all-concept [Allbegrif] of time is not a universality [Allgemeinheit].",[]],"reflm:4319":["On infinite space and time. Time contains the ground of |\nthe limits [Schranken] of the existence of things, space the ground of the limits of their presence, the highest being the ground of the limits of their reality. Hence all of these are infinite. Here the highest being is thought, as it were, as the matter for all things, and only sensibly.\nTime is that which comprehends the existence of all things, in which all things exist, and through which, therefore, to each its existence is determined relatively to others, as to when and how long.",[]],"reflm:4320":["Whether space and time empty of sensation, and likewise infinite space and time, are *phaenomena*. For they are not *noumena*.",[]],"reflm:4321":["Time is the condition of infinite *appositio*, space of infinite *aggregatio*. The two cannot be united, because *aggregatio*, i.e. totality, does not occur in an *appositio*.\nHere the subjective conditions do not agree with one another.",[]],"reflm:4322":["75. E II 621.\nM 75':\nLimits [Grenzen] [find] (g goal, end) are negations which exclude the greater possible addition [end]. Bounds [Schranken] are negations which [the grö the] exclude from the given greatest [d eini the remainder] everything except some part. Number is a multitude which has limits; a determinate magnitude in space and time arises from the limits of infinity. The former is always a whole number, the latter a fraction. In the former no diminutions are thought; the latter is possible only through them. Definitions arise analytically through the bounds of still greater concepts, and are set forth synthetically through the |\nlimits of the composition of the marks. |\nM 75: |\nA definition is like a geometrical figure, which encloses a space through the number and magnitude of its sides and through the manner of their composition. The space is the *definitum*. If the figure does not close, the definition is *latior suo definito* [wider than its *definitum*].\nThe lack of enlargement is the limit (it is opposed to a possible greater).\nThe lack arising from diminution is the bound (it is opposed to an actual greater).\nThe all [the multitude (determinate)]. Some [show] are, others are not: this indicates bounds.",[]],"reflm:4323":["All change is a succession of states; that of the inner state is a change of the thing, that of the outer state a changed relation.",[]],"reflm:4324":["On the limits of a world and of its state, according to laws of sensibility and of reason.",[]],"reflm:4325":["The connection of a thing with that which is not its cause, and which therefore is not a subordination, is always a connection of one part with another to form a whole. Accordingly the whole, which is itself no part, is connected with no thing as with its cause, which would therefore have to be outside it; nor is it so connected with any finite being, because this, |\nsince it is not grounded in itself, must in turn have the ground of its connection in another.",[]],"reflm:4326":["Under and \nChanges make up not parts of the world, but parts of its state.",[]],"reflm:4327":["Beside the conclusion of M § 358 and the beginning of M § 359:\nThat a mere *multitudo* [multitude] is not a world. That *finitudo* [finitude] is a consequence of composition.",[]],"reflm:4328":["At the margin below \nComposition proves contingency (g and dependence), for necessary beings cannot be composite.\nTotality proves dependence upon One.\nComposition also proves *finitudo* [finitude].",[]],"reflm:4329":["The world is finite, because it is a *totum* [whole] of beings that limit one another.",[]],"reflm:4330":["The world of sense is only a single one.\nEvery *totum* is *contingens* [contingent] in respect of its parts.",[]],"reflm:4331":["In time, i.e. in the sensible series or the series of the understanding, a first has a meaning, even if the series were infinite.",[]],"reflm:4332":["Infinity is a concept in respect of sensibility; it is indeed for the understanding, but not for reason. The world (g of the senses) is necessarily infinite, because the senses derive from themselves no *termini* [bounds], except those which consist precisely in this, that the mind is affected by other things.",[]],"reflm:4333":["That the will of man is free means as much as this: reason has a power over the will and over the other faculties and inclinations. For reason determines itself, and without it all the other faculties are determined according to the law of efficient causes and are externally necessary. Reason cannot be determined, i.e. affected; for then it would be sensibility and not reason.",[]],"reflm:4334":["On freedom.\nWe can regard an action of ours either as something |\nthat happens, i.e. as appearance, or as something that ought to happen, i.e. as an intuition of self-activity toward possible effects. In the first case it is subject [an] to the laws of determining grounds; in the second case it is an intellectual [action], in which the subject is not passive; here the *regula rationis determinantis* [rule of the determining ground] prevails only as a *principium [cognosc] comprehensionis*, or logical principle, i.e. that we cannot know it *a priori* (except in the case of a perfect understanding) otherwise than through determining grounds. The good understanding has determining grounds, but according to laws of the understanding, through intellectual *motiva* [motives]; the affected [understanding] surrenders itself to sensibility.\nSomething happens necessarily, i.e. according to the empirical concept of necessity, which nevertheless admits a contingency according to concepts of reason. The understanding must itself excite sensibility, so that the latter may determine the action; thus the action happens according to laws of sensibility and yet of the understanding. We also know only the nearest determining grounds of sensibility, and in accordance with these the action must always be capable of being explained.",[]],"reflm:4335":["It is true that all morality must aim at something useful. But it is not the utility, but the universality of it, that makes it morally good, namely that it is good only as a rule, and that without it no universal rule would obtain. Even in duties of obligation the action is not to be omitted, even if it were harmful, and in this precisely the obligation consists.",[]],"reflm:4336":["We cannot infer the actuality of freedom from experience. But we do have a concept of it, through our intellectual inner intuiting (not inner sense) of our activity, which can be moved by *motiva intellectualia* [intellectual motives], and through which |\npractical laws and rules of the good will are possible even in respect of ourselves. Freedom is therefore a necessary practical presupposition. It also does not contradict theoretical reason. For as appearances [one may the] the actions are always in the field of experience; as objective data they are in the field of reason, and are approved and disapproved. Sensibility here is under the laws of the understanding and yields [weicht] breaks off [bricht ab].",[]],"reflm:4337":["Contingently optional [beliebige] actions (freedom of men) are those that are determined by no rules. Necessarily optional actions (divine freedom) are those that are determined only according to the rule of the good power of choice [Willkür].",[]],"reflm:4338":["Freedom from all external necessitation of our power of choice (*Willkühr*) is clear through experience, and likewise the moving force of the intellectual grounds of the good; we cannot, in this respect, shift the blame onto any other beings. We can ascribe it to ourselves, even the good which God works in us. Morality and religion are therefore *in salvo* [safe].\nBut how does it stand with speculative philosophy as regards the possibility of this freedom? The proposition that everything which happens has a determining ground, i.e. something else by which it is necessitated, is the principle of the alterations of all passive substances (g of all appearances, or of that which is given a posteriori; but actions, in which something is given a priori, are not comprehended under it), [but] as of body, so also of the soul, so far as it is modified, i.e. in everything that is distinguished from the actions of freedom. So far this principle is objective. But as a *principium* of activities it cannot be objective, for in that case a first beginning must be possible; but in the states of a being, considered as passive, there is no first (this lies in |\nthe active). Freedom is to be a faculty of beginning a state first. Passive states are nothing but consequences, and belong necessarily to what precedes. In the present moment I can say: for me the whole series up to now is as nothing. I now begin my state as I will. The difficulty is therefore [only] (g not) *secundum* [rationes] *possibilitatem fiendi* (as regards the possibility of coming to be), but *cognoscendi* (as regards the possibility of knowing). One cannot see the possibility [of a] of freedom, because one cannot see a first beginning, neither necessity in existence in general, nor freedom in origination. For our understanding knows existence through experience, but reason has insight into it when it knows it a priori, i.e. through grounds (that is, what is not necessary according to identity, but is posited really); now of the first there are no grounds, and hence no insight through reason is possible. That there must be a first action (g *natura*) which underlies everything contingent, lies indeed in reason; but a first *tempore* (in time) is not to be comprehended at all, since time itself and what is in it does not depend upon reason. Further: that what is determined in its whole existence by another being must end in itself the determining ground of its actions, is not to be comprehended; but only for this reason, that it is not to be comprehended how it is a substance. Now this is no objection, but a subjective difficulty; for the I proves the end-point of the grounds of actions. 'I do this' [or does not show] does not mean: another effects this; and even when I say 'I suffer this', it nevertheless signifies the intuition [of my sel] of a subject [which] which is for itself and suffers.",[]],"reflm:4339":["Applied philosophy is that whose object is represented through an empirical concept; but an empirical concept, so far as through analysis it is the ground of the knowledge of the object, has no principles except rational ones.",[]],"reflm:4340":["All philosophy (g theoretical) is either empirical (g physiology, psychology or physics) or rational (g transcendental philosophy). Rational philosophy is either pure or applied.",[]],"reflm:4341":["All theoretical philosophy is either pure or applied. The former is that whose objects are given only through concepts of the pure understanding; applied philosophy is that to which objects are given through the senses. Applied philosophy is physiology, and this is either empirical or rational, each being of inner sense or of outer.",[]],"reflm:4342":["1. (g transcendental:) That the principle of life cannot die. That life does not depend on matter, although animal life does.\n2. Psychological: That the soul even now acts independently of the body.\n3. *Theologia moralis* (moral theology): Divine ends.\n4. Analogy.",[]],"reflm:4343":["The proof from analogy requires no experiences which we could not have, and, in default of such experiences, no arbitrarily assumed forces.\nIn the analogical proof we must seek out all the cases in which there is in us an incentive wholly distinct from the senses, and a force, indeed a vocation, to detach ourselves from them; where life does not reward the effort, or is too short for it; and where we are noble creatures and see our own lowliness. Hence man has in himself an ideal of perfection which he does not attain.\nThis proof applies to our nature, and therefore to all men; and likewise, because it concerns the higher faculties, to a gradual natural extension.",[]],"reflm:4344":["According to the laws by which we, with sound reason, have to infer the existence of certain things in the world, and their properties and laws, the existence of a supreme, wise being must necessarily be inferred, and the inference is therefore practically sufficient up to the point of responsibility. But as regards the condition of the comprehensibility of this supreme cause, whereby we would prove the strength of our reason a priori, and so according to logical rules of synthetic or dogmatic knowledge, this use of reason is precarious. Now no man can excuse himself on the ground that, because he has not been able to have insight into something a priori, he has also not accepted it; just as he likewise has no a priori insight into the attraction of gravity, and yet accepts it in his actions.\nDeism is that which recognises no other theology than the transcendental. God as a spiritual being (g intelligence), in distinction from the eternal and necessary nature, is entitled the living God.",[]],"reflm:4345":["That man has a faculty of ascending in modes of knowledge up to the Godhead means nothing more than that he has a faculty of completing his concepts and of producing an idea of the *maximum*.",[]],"reflm:4346":["The understanding of the original being is *intellectus originarius* (original understanding); consequently [it has] its understanding is the archetype of things, and it is the cause of things only by means of understanding, not through mere nature.",[]],"reflm:4347":["Not concepts but ideas (*archetypae*; ours: *ectypae*); that is, we know all perfection only so far as we represent it as in one being, which thereby becomes the supreme idea. In it everything which nature contains becomes a part of the perfection of the whole. The most perfect is only one. The manifoldnesses conceal from us the self-subsistent and original character of the archetype. The abstract and universal representation arises in this way, in that we leave out much and retain only marks. For us everything is appearance [apparentz]. Platonism. The divine understanding contains every perfection in the archetype, ours in rules.\nThe idea is the unity of knowledge from which the manifold, whether of knowledge or of the object, becomes possible. Here the whole of knowledge precedes the part, the universal precedes the particular; there knowledge precedes the possibility of |\nthings, as for example in the case of order and perfection. Everything intelligible is at the same time incomprehensible.\nThat the original being has understanding. Inferred from all-sufficiency, because without this the possibility of every kind of relation, e.g. order, whereby anything in general can be an object of the understanding, [not] would not be possible.\n* No discursive knowledge. Intuition. Not sensation, not appearance.\nNo abstraction.\n*(g No sensibility. Materially. None formally: confusion, obscurity.)\nOur idea is a discursive knowledge. Hence it is only *idea informata* (an informed idea).\nThe intuitive [idea] is *archetypa*.\nOur knowledge through the understanding is discursive. By it no immediate representations of objects are given, but such representations are only brought under rules, or that which gives occasion for a rule.",[]],"reflm:4348":["Our understanding can know nothing in the concrete (it knows merely the manner of sensibility to dispose according to rules) except what is in itself. *Now the good is that which relates necessarily to itself, or to the inner [side] of analogous beings, and so it has knowledge of this a priori. But God knows objects in themselves a priori. Hence they must be given through him, and his understanding is therefore *archetypus*.\n*(g Of that of which it is itself the free author, the understanding can have a priori knowledge; for only the cause through freedom is a cause through understanding.)",[]],"reflm:4349":["Knowledge is either sensitive or intellectual; the objects ei|ther\nsensible or intelligible. No other world than the sensible can be given to us; every *mundus physicus* (physical world) (g materially) is therefore *sensibilis*; only the *mundus moralis* (moral world) (g formally) is *intelligibilis*. For freedom is the only thing which is given a priori, and consists in this giving a priori; the rule of freedom a priori in a world in general constitutes the *forma mundi intelligibilis* (form of the intelligible world). This leads, according to [laws] grounds of freedom, to the presumption of the *intelligibilia* (intelligibles): God and a future world, in which everything (g nature) will be in accordance with the moral laws.\nThe *mundus intelligibilis* as an object of intuition is a mere (g indeterminate) idea; but as an object of the practical relation of our intelligence to the intelligences of the world in general, and to God as the practical original being of that world, it is a true concept and determinate idea: *Civitas dei* (the city of God).",[]],"reflm:4350":["The opinion that we must regard all evils as punishments, and indeed those which befall us already in childhood as punishments for the sins of the first parents.\nIs God the [origin of] author of evil.",[]],"reflm:4351":["Pains are nothing evil, that is, they can subsist together with the highest good in the world even without antecedent sin. They are means of exercising freedom and of culture.",[]],"reflm:4352":["*inanimata dicuntur res creatae, animata creaturae* (inanimate things are called created things, animate things creatures). A plant |\nis a divine work, a worm is a creature; for to bring forth life, and so something that is active of itself, is *creatio per eminentiam* (creation in the eminent sense).",[]],"reflm:4353":["We find in the instincts of animals proofs of divine wisdom, but not in the reason of men. Does this not show that our own reason, which is known to itself, but to which the animals are unknown, supplies in this matter the rules of inference?",[]],"reflm:4354":["Over and \nEvery creature stands under the universal and the particular divine providential care [Vorsorge], but not every event befalls it through particular direction; rather, chance often rules here. For providential care is *potentiale* direction, or oversight, in which all ends are taken into consideration. But God has not determined the destinies of man through individual purposes; he has left them to the order of nature. E.g. I have a fine stud: I do not arrange each mating, but leave them as I know they will pair according to the general order. Then there is here universal providential care, but not particular direction. For only thereby is man put in a position to guide his own destinies according to universal laws.",[]],"reflm:4355":["Over and \nProvidence [Vorsehung] goes either to the particular through the universal or to the universal through the particular. The first is the systematic.",[]],"reflm:4356":["A universal determination is a destiny [Schicksal] (e.g. natural death).\nA particular determination is a decree of fate [Verhängnis].\nAn event that is not determined beforehand at all is good or ill fortune.",[]],"reflm:4357":["On the diminution of a miracle through mere direction.\nOn the enthusiastic *concursus*, in relation to the holiness of the will; we must ourselves do good.\nGod and the physician. Ebb and flow. Preservation of kingdoms. Life and death.",[]],"reflm:4358":["He who will not submit to the laws is a free-spirit (*libertin*); he who will not submit to statutes [Satzungen], a free-thinker.",[]],"reflm:4359":["By means of the concept of the first no science can be explained. Every whole can be divided into two parts, of which one is the first relatively to the other, e.g. the first centuries. The absolutely first is only a single one; the relatively first is indeterminate.",[]],"reflm:4360":["*Metaphysica est logica intellectus puri* (metaphysics is the logic of the pure understanding), but to pure philosophy morals also belongs. Since all logic is dogmatic or critical, metaphysics is either dogmatic or critical. And this critique is not merely of propositions but of human reason, and consequently also of the opposite.",[]],"reflm:4361":["*divisio Philosophiae in theoreticam et practicam* (division of philosophy into the theoretical and the practical); *theoretica vel ex principiis empiricis vel rationalibus, prior physiologia* (the theoretical is either from empirical or from rational principles, the former being physiology).",[]],"reflm:4362":["*Metaphysica est philosophia theoretica* [pura] *intellectualis* (metaphysics is theoretical philosophy, [pure,] intellectual; *oppositum: empirica*, opposite: empirical), *et haec vel transscendentalis* (g *ens summum*, highest being) *vel applicata* (and this is either transcendental or applied); *posterior vel ad naturam sensus interni vel externi* (the latter either with regard to the nature of inner or outer sense)* *vel naturam universam* (or with regard to nature as a whole) (g *summum bonum*, highest good).\n*(g The question is: what can reason know without any experience (much, in mathematics), and what are the conditions, the objects and the limits. Pure reason is dogmatic in morals.)",[]],"reflm:4363":["All our knowledge can, as to kind, be distinguished into sensible knowledge and knowledge of reason.\nAs to relation (g of subordination): into *principia et principiata* (principles and what is derived from principles).\nOf coordination: as a part to the whole of human knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4364":["Theoretical philosophy is either [pura] rational or empirical. The latter is psychology and physics. The rational [is] has either no object at all from experience [or] and is entitled transcendental; or it has an object from experience, but principles from reason, and is entitled metaphysics (rational psychology and rational physics); finally, it has an object of reason, so far as this is known through concepts of experience *per analogiam* (by analogy).",[]],"reflm:4365":["Difference of knowledge in order: *a priori*. *a posteriori*.\n—\n—\n—\nin kind: empirical or rational.\nThe former are universal, the second singular.",[]],"reflm:4366":["Metaphysics is *philosophia pura* (pure philosophy). The form of all knowledge is merely rational, the matter sensitive; metaphysics is therefore the philosophy concerned with form.",[]],"reflm:4367":["Analysis and *synthesis negativa* (negative synthesis) are very different. A *divisio logica* (logical division) is a *synthesis negativa*: I do not divide up the concept, but classify it (*repartior*).",[]],"reflm:4368":["Our material concepts can never refer to anything other than what we have sensed; and thus our material principles also can be regarded only as laws of experience, and can never be more universal. But if one extends this manner of judging to the concepts of form, there results from it rather a law of subjective use.",[]],"reflm:4369":["The question is whether metaphysics deals with the objects that can be known through pure reason, or with the subject, namely the principles and laws in the use of pure reason. Since |\nwe can know all objects only through our subject, above all those which do not affect us, metaphysics is subjective.",[]],"reflm:4370":["By what do I recognise that canons of reason are not axioms, that is, that they can be used only *a posteriori* and *descendendo* (in descending), not *a priori* and *ascendendo* (in ascending)? For the subjective laws of reason are rules for its use in application, the objective laws, however, rules for its use in explanation.\nAll immediately certain propositions are either 1. basic formulae, or 2. *axiomata*, or 3. *canones*, or 4. elementary propositions of analysis, or 5. immediately certain propositions of synthesis. The first are the principles of identity and of contradiction. The second: [Nur grund] objective principles of synthesis, space and time. The third: objective principles of *synthesis qvalitativae* (qualitative synthesis). The fourth and fifth: material propositions contained immediately under the principles of form [and of] of synthesis as well as of analysis.\n[Axiomata] *Formulae primitivae* (primitive formulae) hold for every mode of knowledge.\nThe synthetic fundamental concepts of reason, just like the synthetic fundamental concepts of appearances, give occasion to axioms, which, however, both serve only for the use of reason *a posteriori*.\nThe use of the principles *a posteriori*, when one applies them *a priori*, is a knowledge of reason of objects *per analogiam* (by analogy), [either] e.g. of space, of pleasure and displeasure, from God.",[]],"reflm:4371":["The logical relation is merely a relation of subordination, that is, of the *sphaerarum conceptuum* (spheres of concepts), and the logical rational character |\nof concepts consists in their being universal [or] concepts or *intuitus* (intuitions).\nThe real relation is that of coordination [either according to rules of], and the rules of it are either those of reason or those of sensibility. To the first belong substance and accident, whereas the logical relation has only subject and predicate. Inherence is a *coordinatio*, agreement [convenientz] a *subordinatio logica*. The concept of ground. That of the whole, or rather of the composite. Possibility and existence are *positiones absolutae* (absolute positings), the former of the concept (something), the latter of the thing.\nThus all synthetic concepts are (*intellectualiter*, intellectually). Substance, *compositum* (composite) and *ratio* (ground).",[]],"reflm:4372":["All judgments [are] have a logical object, something in the logical sense, whether it be possible or impossible, =A.\nIn these judgments there is either no predicate or there is one.\nIn the first case, either:\nA is — — possible, or:\nA is — — actual;\nin the second case:\nA is B.\nIf A contains a subject with predicates, the first proposition can be expressed logically. E.g. A figure can be enclosed by three sides, i.e. a triangle is possible. But since this presupposes that a figure is possible (otherwise it is only a proposition that expresses the relation of two concepts and not absolute possibility), one sees well that possibilities must be given. The absence of contradiction does not prove [sinnl] the possibility of the thing and of the *data* (givens), but only of the connection. What, then, does the possibility of the thing itself signify? Logical possibility signifies the possibility of the relation of A and B, or of A and non-B. A triangle can be oblique-angled. Logical actuality is entitled truth.\nMetaphysical possibility signifies something objective, an object of thought. The non-possible is that of which the thought is empty. The impossible.\nEvery simple thought contains something possible, for one can invent nothing simple. It must accordingly be given through experience or through an inner law.",[]],"reflm:4373":["All truth consists in the agreement of all (g thoughts with the) laws of thinking, and hence with one another. That is the object for us, what it is and how far it is given to us mediately or immediately through experience. Independently of all experience there are no objects and also no laws of the understanding. (E.g. substance: that this concept is something must be inferred from the experience of the permanence of a certain subject under all circumstances.) We accordingly have concepts, 1. in order to explain *phaenomena*, 2. in order to see into the grounds of the morally good and evil. Everything that is given through these laws, e.g. knowledge of the highest good (God), is practically true. The knowledge of this same object breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4374":["Appearances of reason. Substance, spirit etc.: concepts of reason according to their form: genus, species; according to their matter: good and evil; for the universal in the matter of all sensation is pleasure or displeasure or the equilibrium: 0, *indifferentia*.",[]],"reflm:4375":["Of the objects of the senses we have only representations that are adequate to our *phaenomena*, and of the things themselves (through pure reason) [we have representations] as they are adequate to the laws of our understanding. |\nEven the concept of God is possible and necessary for us only in so far as it necessarily accords with the laws of the understanding which we necessarily follow in (g judging) the world. It is therefore still only appearance, namely of the understanding, which borrows its certainty from the necessity of confirming our moral principles, from which every ought must ultimately be derived. [Through] According to the laws of the understanding in physical appearances we arrive at the idea of a necessary cause; according to those in moral appearances we arrive at the idea of a perfect author. It is absurd to act without thinking a cause for every motion and a common cause for every combination; it is just as absurd to place moral rules above happiness without God. What are appearances of reason? (Universal modes of knowledge, determined subjectively.) Hence arises the contradiction of the principles, when one judges according to the rule of reason with regard to its use in the world, and afterwards wishes to judge *in abstracto*, apart from all such relation.",[]],"reflm:4376":["Besides the appearance, the being or not-being of it is also to be noted.",[]],"reflm:4377":["The laws of pure reason can contain only the ground of the possibility of objects relatively to the mind, in so far as they concern such objects whose essence consists in the relation to the soul, i.e. the morally good.",[]],"reflm:4378":["Just as sensibility is a faculty of ordering things according to rela|tions\nof space and time, so reason also is a law of the co-ordination of things, separated from the laws of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:4379":["The concepts and propositions of reason which are correct *in concreto* with regard to the objects of the world or of the senses, actual as well as possible, lead us, if we go back, to limits which cannot be comprehensible according to these very rules; for their usefulness holds only within these limits. Nevertheless we cannot [from] the [constitution of] knowledge of that which sets these limits [no other] refrain, except in so far as it serves, according to rules of our concrete reason, to explain and confirm what is within the limits. The rest is useless. Thus in the case of freedom the first is incomprehensible.",[]],"reflm:4380":["1. Substance and accident; 2. Unity and plurality; 3. Ground and consequence (cause, effect, combination and separation).",[]],"reflm:4381":["M LIII:\nFor a pure concept of reason, e.g. substance, the predicate impenetrable is far too narrow, and for a sensible concept (impenetrable) the pure concept of substance is too wide. In the former one must either take sensibility away from the |\nsubject or add it to the predicate; in the latter, conversely. *Praedicatum univocum* or *aequivocum* (univocal or equivocal predicate). In the first case it is called: |\nM LIV: |\nA thing in general, thought through understanding, stands under conditions of sensibility, i.e. is *phaenomenon*; in the second: an appearance is *noumenon*. E.g. the world of sense is an absolute whole. Here the predicate would have to be restricted thus: It is a whole that can be determined sensibly; or, with the affection left out of the subject: a world (*noumenon*) is a whole. The proposition: The soul is simple, is transcendent, and I must say either: I, who think the soul, am something simple, or: the soul is something that is not physically separable. In short: an intellectual predicate can belong to an object as *phaenomenon* only in so far as it is sensibly determinable.",[]],"reflm:4382":["There are empirical concepts through which an object is first given and which cannot be experimented upon but only analysed. That they want to think the empirical fundamental concepts (matter) etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:4383":["Above, beside and\nWhat is necessary under the (g subjective) conditions under which things appear to us is also necessary for things as appearances.\nWhat is necessary under the hypothesis which I arbitrarily invent is objectively necessary everywhere where the case of the hypothesis is given.\nBut what is said of things in general, regardless of the subject, and not under an arbitrary hypothesis, cannot be known through the agreement of thoughts with themselves.",[]],"reflm:4384":["In and\nMathematical synthesis is not [not] an analysis of what we think in the given concept.",[]],"reflm:4385":["The metaphysical concepts are: 1. Possible, 2. Being, (g 2b. Necessary,) 3. One posited with another (whole), 4. One in another (substance), 5. One through another (ground). The last three are real relations. The unity of the manifold: a. of the whole, b. the unity of the predicates in one subject, or c. of the consequences through one ground.",[]],"reflm:4386":["The supreme principle of human reason is either one that expresses the condition under which alone we can know things through our reason, or one under which alone things are possible; subjective, objective. The principles of acquisition, of genesis, or of the mere knowledge of possibility.",[]],"reflm:4387":["1. The matter for the true judgment. Possibility.\n2. The *est* (is). Existence.\n3. The connection through concept. Necessity.",[]],"reflm:4388":["*Lockii simplices ideae* (Locke's simple ideas): *cogitatio* (thinking), *Voluptas et taedium* (pleasure and weariness), *Existentia*, *unitas*, *Potentia*, *Successio*.",[]],"reflm:4389":["(s Something and nothing are formal concepts in metaphysics and signify merely the relation.)\nSomething and nothing in the logical sense is possible and impossible, i.e. that to which no concept at all belongs: not A, also not non-A, which there is derived through contradiction, but in metaphysics from comparison with the original being. [Something or nothing in the met] The possible is something or nothing in the metaphysical sense as reality or negation. The three absolute concepts, as the simplest, are: Possible (comes from indeterminate judgments), Existence (from affirmations), Necessary (from affirmation through concepts). Categories.",[]],"reflm:4390":["As regards matter, possibility must be given |\nthrough the senses. We know only hypothetical possibility and the absolute possibility of space and time.",[]],"reflm:4391":["Above, beside and\nLack (of existence) is = 0A. That something does not belong under possibility signifies an empty concept, i.e. a complete lack of concept; but that it is impossible, like a square circle, signifies the cancellation of possibility in general. For in a square circle it is not merely the possibility of this figure that is cancelled; for an empty concept conflicts with nothing possible, although it is itself nothing; but to be a circle and also square conflicts with the possibility of a circle and also of every other figure; hence what conflicts with possibility is impossible. But to conflict with possibility concerns not the matter but the form; consequently, however ............",[]],"reflm:4392":["Beside and\n*Nihil in sensu logico opponitur contrarie omnibus* (nothing in the logical sense is contrarily opposed to all things). Nothing logically means as much as none.",[]],"reflm:4393":["The rational fundamental concepts can be brought to a certain number, but not the elementary concepts. Fundamental concepts of form and fundamental concepts of matter.",[]],"reflm:4394":["All simple concepts are possible; impossibility lies only in composition.",[]],"reflm:4395":["There are material and formal fundamental concepts, just as there are material and formal fundamental principles.",[]],"reflm:4396":["What is posited in itself exists; what is cancelled in itself does not exist; what is neither the one nor the other is indeterminate.\nWhat is posited through its idea is necessary; what is thereby cancelled is impossible; what is neither the one nor the other is merely possible or in itself contingent.\nPossible is that whose concept is not empty, nor cancelled through itself. That it is not to be empty signifies, however, that something outside the thought, hence an actuality, corresponds to it.",[]],"reflm:4397":["In the concept of the understanding, ground signifies that from which, universally, the inference to something else holds. The possibility of this can indeed be seen in logical grounds, but not in real grounds. Experience, however, yields no true universality, since it yields no necessity. Now we take the application of the concept of a real ground solely from experience. Hence the principles can be universal only empirically, and have only an empirical meaning, namely that something else is always connected with something as its ground.",[]],"reflm:4398":["Inner possibility is the smallest, the absolute the greatest.\nAbsolutely possible is that which is possible *absque ulla hypothesi* (without any hypothesis).",[]],"reflm:4399":["A–A=0 signifies no concept at all, but not no thing at all, i.e. a removal (*remotion*) of the thing. For A signifies an affirmative concept, –A a negative one, and hence both together signify no concept at all. The impossible is therefore not a negative concept but no concept at all; but if A signifies a thing and –A its real opposite, then 0 = *privativum*. In all concepts something (real) is thought, and then the positing or removal (*actus intellectus: positio vel negatio*).",[]],"reflm:4400":["A thought without an affirming or denying object [the object] |\nrepresents something impossible, since we neither posit nor remove anything. (g or rather: it is an empty thought.)",[]],"reflm:4401":["In every negative judgment it is thought that the [predicate] subject is not contained under the predicate; whether on account of contradiction or not is undetermined.",[]],"reflm:4402":["Everything that is considered transcendentally is weighed with respect to the essence; in the metaphysical sense, however, it is considered absolutely and *universaliter* (universally). In the former the essence is the consequence of the *essentialia*; in the latter the essence, together with its *essentialia*, is the ground of consequences *in indefinitum*. Hence the former propositions are tautological, the latter pregnant. For in the former the *essentialia* are given only through the essence, and are in turn constituents of it.",[]],"reflm:4403":["The absolutely necessary is necessary in every respect, whereas the inwardly necessary is (g it) necessary in itself; consequently such a thing must at the same time include in itself the relation to everything, i.e. must be the hypothesis for everything.\nImpossibility is restricted possibility. The more conditions by which possibility is restricted, the smaller is the extent of this concept, until, if something is [all] in every respect impossible, it is in itself (g absolutely) impossible. Through mere concepts this can be seen only when something contradicts itself. [But the M]",[]],"reflm:4404":["Every *commercium* (community), and hence every *compositum* (composite), is as such contingent.",[]],"reflm:4405":["Absolute necessity consists in this, that something is 1. no consequence of another, 2. a ground of everything else.",[]],"reflm:4406":["From alteration the logical contingency with regard to concepts can be inferred, and likewise inner possibility, but not the contingency of the series. The determinations that succeed one another are contingent, but the series is necessary; still more is the thing necessary, since it remains while its determinations change.",[]],"reflm:4407":["In the same sense in which a thing is necessary, it is also unalterable. If a predicate belongs to it absolutely necessarily, it is also unalterable in respect of that predicate; if it belongs conditionally, |\nit is unalterable under this condition; but the thing itself is not therefore unalterable, only in respect of its predicates. In respect of existence no thing can be alterable, for not-being is no alteration.",[]],"reflm:4408":["The agreement of the concept of an *ens omnimode reale* (a being real in every way) with the synthetic conditions of thought [has] seems to present no difficulty, because between *realitates noumena* (noumenal realities) no conflict can be conceived, though it can well be between *realitates phaenomena* (phenomenal realities). The latter one expresses by effect in opposite direction.",[]],"reflm:4409":["Opposite and \nEach, all, the All. I have a penny for each poor man, but not for all the poor, still less for the All. The latter is *completum* (the complete). From this All the rest is derived.",[]],"reflm:4410":["That of which no totality is possible is necessarily incomplete; so is a multitude in general. (s [series.] progressions.)\nThere are three kinds of *respectus* (relation), hence also as many *correlata*, and so also as many *incompleta*: *rationatum*, *pars*, *praedicatum*.\nThe part is, as such, incomplete. (All things of the world are incomplete [therefore]; the *ens realissimum* is the only complete being, as a ground.) The unity of the whole is nevertheless a ground why something belongs to it as a *complementum*.",[]],"reflm:4411":["The magnitude of a whole is extensive.\nThe magnitude of a ground is intensive, or degree.\nThe beginning of a degree (minimum) is entitled moment.\n(s Magnitude of weight and degree of gravity.\nDegree of extension, of light and of warmth.\nMoment of warming.)\n(s Degree of reality. Generation of a reality from 0, i.e. moment of generation.)\n(s The magnitude of motion depends on time and space. The magnitude of velocity (in an instant) is the magnitude of the moment of motion, whereby, namely, a space can be traversed in a time.)",[]],"reflm:4412":["The difference between a logical and a real subject is this, that the former contains the logical ground for positing the predicate, the latter the real ground (something other and positive), and so is the cause, the |\n*accidens* however the effect. This relation of cause we draw from our own [effective] actions, and apply it to that which is abiding in the appearances of outer things. But in the end we find everything in the object to be *accidentia*. The first subject is therefore a something through which the *accidentia* are. Hence arise *iudicia synthetica* (synthetic judgments). E.g. something that I know through the abiding *accidentia* of impenetrability also contains those of attraction. The reality of the *accidens* we judge from the sensation of an object, which is distinguished from that of the subject.",[]],"reflm:4413":["Opposite the end of M § 195 and the paragraph number of M § 196:\nThe unity of the ground, the unity of the whole, the unity of the subject (in the simple).\nThe connection of many (different) things in one whole presupposes dependence on one ground, and flows from it.",[]],"reflm:4414":["One distinguishes the substantial from all its predicates. But a thing cannot be distinguished from all its predicates.",[]],"reflm:4415":["All (g real) relations are either of connection or of conflict. The first are either of one through another (*ratio et rationatum*), or to another (*partium ad totum*), or in one another (*accidens et substantia*). The unity in all this is the greatest connection (*subordinatio, coordinatio, involutio*). The limits are the first ground, the *omnitudo* (allness) of what is connected, and the last *subiectum*. A substance that contains the first ground of everything that is real, and so is |\nwithout dependence, without limitation and without another subject, constitutes the supreme principle of all. *Vltimam rerum lineam* etc. etc. The logically parallel to the *ens summum* is the *ens entium logicum* (*ens in genere*). It is the supreme subject, comprehends all things in its sphere, and is the supreme ground of explanation, presupposed in everything as that which expresses its possibility. Symbols are the attachment of a part to others ([inherence] *composita in toto*), the hanging of one link of the chain on others (dependence), the support of one thing upon another (*portitor*, the bearer) (inherence). To the first ground necessity is attached, to the universal infinity, to the [subst] self-subsistent — — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4416":["[The G] The *conceptus terminator* (terminating concept) of *inhaerentia* is the *substantiale*\n*dependentia* *ens primum*\ncomposition *simplex*.",[]],"reflm:4417":["*aggregatum plurium se invicem determinantium est compositum reale; non omne totum est compositum* (an aggregate of several things mutually determining one another is a real composite; not every whole is a composite). e.g. *series subordinatorum* (a series of subordinated things).\nIn every *compositum* there is unity of reciprocal action, which proves the community with a third as principle.",[]],"reflm:4418":["All unity is relative; whether an absolute unity is possible is a question.",[]],"reflm:4419":["A *compositum* of any kind of parts consists of parts that are not composed in the same way, but may well consist of parts that are composed of parts of another kind (for the latter is not the *contradictorie oppositum* of the former). E.g. a rod consists of feet which are not further composed of feet, but are composed of inches. If, then, the composition is removed (namely that of a certain kind), parts remain that are not composed in the same way, but not such as would be composed in no way at all.",[]],"reflm:4420":["*Qvaestio hic proprie est, utrum compositum reale possit cogitari tanqvam qvantum continuum sive tanqvam discretum; si sit [discretum] continuum, non foret nisi vis qvaedam in spatio vel tempore adstricta.* [The question here is properly whether a real composite can be thought as a continuous quantity or as a discrete one; if it is [discrete] continuous, it would be nothing but a certain force confined in space or time.]\nPerhaps that whole which is the *totum intelligibile*, whose *phaenomenon* is body, may consist of simple parts. But it does not follow that body does, because this whole does not exhibit simplicity, or its consequences, in space.\nThe necessity of simple parts rests on this, that they can also exist all together in a separated state. But total separation in space is the complete abolition of the relations of space. Hence a body consists of simple parts only in so far as I do not consider these in relations of space, and so not as an *extensum*.",[]],"reflm:4421":["Body is not a substantial composite (*compositum substantiale*), but a *phaenomenon substantiatum*. [Neither] and (g both) composition with regard to space and division are phenomena of conjunction and division. If the composite substance whose phenomenon is body is conceived as consisting of simples, its phenomenon does not on that account consist of such. Moreover, space contains the ground of the possibility of the phenomenon; whatever, therefore, is so present that its presence is an action in space, is bound by the conditions of space.",[]],"reflm:4422":["At the top of the page:\nBecause space is a *phaenomenon*, bodies are phenomena of the external presence of several substances [action]. Therefore, although all composition [can be removed] in the substantial whole, it cannot on that account be removed in the phenomenon of external presence. Since space contains the ground of the possibility of external presence, [possibility] the necessity of division extends no further in the composite than the concept of space admits. Suppose a simple to be present in an assigned part of space: space would limit it objectively, and so it would be something objective.",[]],"reflm:4423":["Opposite the conclusion of M § 244:\nThat changes are real means: we really represent to ourselves the determinations of things, according to our law of sensibility, in the succession of time. Changes are possible only in time; that is, the representation of them presupposes the sensible form of time.",[]],"reflm:4424":["Every quantum is either a *compositum*, that is, *syntheticum*, or a *quantum analyticum*. A *compositum* is that whose parts can exist before the |\ncomposition, and which comes to be only through composition and combination. Every *compositum* consists of *simplicia* and is *divisibile*.\nA *quantum indivisibile* is that which cannot arise *per compositionem minorum quantorum*. The magnitude of the power of the understanding is no *compositum*. Neither is a greater moral perfection. The smaller degrees arise only from the limitation of the greatest, and this is the first thing that is given in itself. Every such quantum is *continuum*. A *discretum* is composite.\n(g In all those magnitudes which cannot arise through the composition of the smaller, although these are contained in them, there is a *progressus continuus in infinitum*. For they [they] exist themselves only through a greatest, and are limited only by others which exist at the same time.)",[]],"reflm:4425":["*Spatium est quantum, sed non compositum.* For space does not arise through the positing of its parts; rather, the parts are possible only through space. The same holds of time. The parts can indeed be thought singly *abstrahendo a caeteris*, but not *removendo caetera*; and so they can well be discerned, but not separated, and the *divisio non est realis, sed logica*. Since all matter seems, as regards divisibility, to depend on the space which it fills, and to be divisible just as this space is, the question arises whether the divisibility of matter, like that of space, is not merely logical. [The words [weiß ob nicht] and [nur als] stand in the manuscript at this point.]",[]],"reflm:4426":["Speed does not arise through the mere addition of smaller degrees, |\nbut merely through the limitation of the greatest force. Hence the Leibnizian law of the square of the speed. For the tortoise A carries itself with one degree of force and speed, and carries the second, B, with just the same degree of force: =2. B, in pushing itself forward, also works against the tortoise A with one degree, and A therefore needs in the direction AB, in order to withstand the counteraction, a further degree of force: =3. B has, besides, in the direction AB its own speed: =4. The third, C, [requires] therefore presupposes, first, these 4; second, in A one degree more to carry it: =5; in B likewise to carry it: =6; in A a resistance arising from this: =7; further, in B a degree from the resistance, in that C pushes itself forward: =8; and finally one in C, with which it goes forward: =9. The forces, then, which [the one upon the other] each applies are as the odd numbers. A=1. A+B=3. A+B+C=/5=9.",[]],"reflm:4427":["The speed which a spring can give to bodies within the very same space of its extension increases to infinity. Hence every enduring force, e.g. a spring, that acts externally has infinite force. Every internally acting force is, moreover, infinite as regards time, because it [not as] (first, it extends to all bodies, however great) can act in the same degree, without diminution, through any time however long.",[]],"reflm:4428":["We can represent the finite *in concreto* to ourselves as possible only through the limitation of the infinite; e.g. a *spatium* of a certain figure. So too, since a thing is thought complete only [through] in this way: if it has all reality, everything is affirmed of it and hence all being is thought; and if it is an *ens limitatum*, something is posited in it, while all the remaining reality is indeed thought but denied. Thus the concept of the finite seems to arise from that of the infinite. [[laut] is the Academy's reading of a doubtful word.]",[]],"reflm:4429":["[The infinitely small] In what is infinitely divisible (*continuum*) there is a *terminus*, but no *finis divisionis*. But what is infinite in synthesis has neither *finis* nor *terminus*, and yet is a totality. In the case of the continuum the synthesis has no first beginning; in the case of the infinite it has no end.",[]],"reflm:4430":["*Infinitum reale est omnisufficiens* (in which there is *omnitudo*). *Infinitum non est mutabile*; all changes are limitations of existence.",[]],"reflm:4431":["For the concept of matter, extension and impenetrability suffice; for mere matter, *inertia* is further required. *Materia spontanea (viva)* is not mere matter. How do we come to (g presuppose) that impenetrable extension cannot be self-active except through a foreign, and consequently immaterial, *principium*? Does this come from our meeting with some *impenetrabile iners*, and hence suspecting in the *vivum* a further *principium vitae*?\n(s Resistance takes place only in so far as a body does not have a motion, but rather an endeavour to the contrary.)",[]],"reflm:4432":["The *extensum impenetrabile* is, as to its substance, matter; as to its form, body. To form belong the outer form (figure) and the inner structure, (s third, the mixture. Figure is an object of mathematics, structure of mechanics, mixture of chemistry, and forces of physics.)\nThe [mathematical] knowledge of corporeal nature is entitled, as *doctrin*, [Naturlehre] *Naturkunde*, as discipline, *Naturlehre*. *Naturlehre* is either rational (*Naturwissenschaft*) or empirical, [concerned with] *phaenomena* (*Naturbeschreibung*). *Naturwissenschaft* is either mathematical or philosophical. The former considers [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4433":["If we assume that the difference of all things consists merely in the diversity of magnitude, whether of the whole, or of the parts, or also of the motion of these parts, then there is a continuous connection between all their possible *species*: between rest and motion; between equilateral, isosceles and scalene triangles, and the conic sections; between a surface which moves infinitely little, and one which moves through an assignable magnitude and makes a body.\nThis connection can also be seen, at least as a law of the understanding, from the classification that has been attempted at all times. For there one presupposes that all things can be regarded as variations of one fundamental being. If this were not so, a necessary (g and determinate) manifold of what is contained under a concept could not be inferred from the concept through its closer determination. Or else one represents to oneself the unity of the cause, and each product as subordinated not immediately to it but one by means of another. For in the supreme cause everything must be [a single] homogeneous, and the heterogeneity must be a limitation not of the parts by the supreme cause itself but of one part by another. It is as if plants had arisen gradually from coarse matter, animals from these, and finally man from them, with gradual transition; so that the animal faculties, which consist in a very special relation to certain circumstances that may occur, |\nare gradually adapted to more of them, or to the more general, and finally wholly to the general, and are entitled reason. One can represent to oneself that all species of animals arose either by gradual increase of perfection, from the mineral onwards, *ascendendo*, or by the decline of a creature more perfect than man, in which original animal there lay the sources of all possible mechanical formation, which [breaks off] in its products.",[]],"reflm:4434":["The *saltus* [leap] is either in respect of degree (a sudden passage from a smaller state to a greater) or in respect of quality (where, after the series of grounds has ceased, a new one begins *spontaneo* [spontaneously]). There is in the world a continuity of the dynamical series, but not merely as sensible world; it holds also of the intelligible world. The former in particular then has a *hiatus*. In respect of the receptivity of the mind there is continuity; the *facultas* [faculty] has spontaneity.",[]],"reflm:4435":["Mere matter is *iners* [inert], or lifeless. Hence what lives among extended beings (animal) [is] has in itself matter and, besides, a special *principium* of life: the immaterial. (The inner principles of the *phaenomena*, which are known only through inner sense.) Hence the immaterial natures, and the concept of them, rest already upon the distinction between inner and outer sense.",[]],"reflm:4436":["The supreme cause is also the ground of the possibility of the changes of one and the same world. That is to say, the subject remains, although it exists differently; otherwise the subject would have to perish together with its state, and no other would arise. The cause of this possibility is therefore preservation, that is, the infinite duration of the supreme cause.",[]],"reflm:4437":["*Non mutantur nisi quae sustentantur ab alio* [Nothing is changed except what is sustained by another].\nBecause all things subsist in another, there is space; because all things subsist durably in another, there is time.\nIn change there is a unity of manifold states of one and the same subject; this unity rests upon the dependence of the substances upon One.\nWhat belongs to the same state of the world is simultaneous [*simultanea*]; what belongs to different or opposed states is successive [*successiva*].\nTime contains in itself the ground of the possible change of contingent states; for from contingency alone mutability does not follow.\nNot contingency but mutability proves existence from another; and because time is unique, [it proves] existence from one. Simultaneity too proves existence from one.",[]],"reflm:4438":["At the top of the page:\nThe *influxus* [influence] of the sustaining substance alone is originary.\nNo commerce of substances (reciprocal influence) is originary; that of all is derivative, in so far as they are sustained through another.",[]],"reflm:4439":["We know the course of nature through the senses, and [we know] the order of nature through the (s reason, the) [faculty of reason]; the greater the reason, the more order one discovers. Where reason is lacking, everything appears as chance (s or blind necessity). Nature is at all times a *principium* of order.\nThe order of nature is to be distinguished from the order according to rules of perfection (that is, of a good will, [an order] of how things would have to be if they were to be pleasing). [This is the objective order of the world.] The order of nature is throughout necessary for the moral order, and the most perfect world will attain this [ground] perfection in accordance with the order of nature, because only under this condition is it possible to use understanding.\n(s Freedom also stands under the order of nature.)",[]],"reflm:4440":["There are three kinds of [psych] physiological effects of the human soul.\n1. [sensation] mere representations, 2. combination, 3. comparison.\nThree species of knowledge [i.e.] and objective differences:\n1. Sensation, 2. form of appearance, 3. concept.\nThree kinds of use of reason:\n1. Inner sense, or intuition of oneself and of one's thoughts. 2. General representations and the relation of their spheres (logical use). 3. The form of *thesis* and *synthesis*. The use of pure reason contains either absolute concepts or concepts of relation.|\nThe former [are] either intuitive, that is, according to matter (good), or discursive, according to mere form (existence).\n(s Animals also have the *facultas diiudicandi* [faculty of discriminating] (*iudicium sensitivum*), but not [that] of *iudicandi* [judging] (*iudicium intellectuale*).",[]],"reflm:4441":["One can regard freedom as if it were the influence of a particular cause, namely of intelligence, which however does not determine alone, and again of sensibility as a particular cause. The first furnishes character as the ground of explanation; the second, the natural disposition or temperament. Although in every case, whatever the man may previously have done, the opposite is now possible (as in a game, where one has often hit the mark), yet this freedom is on the whole to be regarded as nature, and the actions are sufficiently (practically) determined from character and temperament.",[]],"reflm:4442":["The state after death is either a regeneration or a liberation; the former either terrestrial or supraterrestrial. Either a new animal life of a terrestrial or supraterrestrial kind, or a spiritual life. And this its relation [is] either in this world or in another world.\nSeat of the soul. Place after death. Separation from the body. Heaven, hell and earth. The former belongs to the world in both respects, but not to the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:4443":["The reason why we hope for another life is that the outcome may accord more with morality. This, however, is brought about by the disappearance of the one means of dissembling [*sich zu verstellen*] [and]; for by this good human beings will soon associate with one another, expel the wicked, and produce happiness among themselves. Not past vices, but the character implanted by them, will constitute the punishment of the future world.",[]],"reflm:4444":["334. E II 1719.\nM 334':\nThat magnitude through which alone the smaller are possible, the magnitude of the universal ground, can be compared with the magnitudes of the consequence, or with smaller magnitudes in general, only *per analogiam* [by analogy]; it cannot be regarded as the sum of smaller forces; they are not contained in it but posited through it. Hence the all-sufficient ground is a magnitude only *per analogiam*, that is, only through its relation to the effect.\n(s Proof that there is an *ens realissimum* [most real being], in which all things are given; that this is necessary; that it does not exist in time and space, yet is eternal, omnipresent, all-sufficient.)\n[Possibility, actuality]\nTranscendental predicates.\nUnity: that the concept of God is a *conceptus singularis* [singular concept], and that there can also be no other *conceptus singularis* of a being than that which contains *omnitudo* [allness]. For there is all only once. Some a is b, another is non-b. (g Polytheism, Manichaeism.)\nThis is proved by the fact that all beings that differ from one another do so through their limits; many beings are therefore always considered in the unlimited. This concept is practically necessary.\n[Simp] Substantiality. Spinoza. *Originaria*.\nSimplicity.\nAll-sufficiency.\n(g Immutability, *impassibilitas*)\nMagnitude. Infinity. *illimitatum*. (Eternity, omnipresence.)\nIn existence, in all time and in all places. Eternity and omnipresence.\nIn power.\n(s The difficulty is not how God is at all times, but how He is at no time; likewise place.)\nMetaphysical attributes. In relation to the determinations of the *Cosmologia generalis*.\n(s They are taken from causality in respect of things, because the causality of the contingent lies in freedom, and causality in general [lies] in an effect outside itself.)\n(s *systema Inhaerentiae*: of Spinoza; of emanation; and of creation.)\n(s *Substantia omnisufficiens intellectualis est Deus* [God is the all-sufficient intellectual substance].)\n(s Independence; *intuitus intellectualis* [intellectual intuition], *prototypon*; not discursive but intuitive; not reason, not number.)\nIs a spirit, not a world-soul. *Anthropomorphismus crassior* [the cruder anthropomorphism].\n1. Understanding. Omniscience. (s Prediction.)\n3. Will. Goodness. Holiness. Justice. Freedom and independence.\n2. Blessedness (s not well-being). *complacentia relativa non est sensus voluptatis* [relative complacency is not a feeling of pleasure].\nPhysico-theological predicates (s inner and outer).\nCreation (s causality in respect) of the world.* Of the matter, not merely according to form.\n(s The world is not in time, but the things of the world are in it. The world has no beginning, but a cause.)\n(s A time of creation cannot be thought.)\n(s That all things of the world are creatures, but not of one another.)\n(g Preservation) Government and providence. (s Not the cause of evil. *Concursus*.)\nFinal end. (s Ordinary and extraordinary direction.) The two propositions: the most perfect cause, and the best world with its corollary [*consectarium*] of the other life, are inseparably bound together.\nGeneral negative determinations. Analogy.\nM 334:\n*(s signifies *intellectualiter* [intellectually] only the free cause of the substances, *sensitive* [sensibly] the beginning of the duration of the world.)",[]],"reflm:4445":["(s Possibility of such a science. With regard to what is not intuitive.)\nProlegomena. Some sciences contain merely a use of the understanding in respect of various objects of curiosity and of advantage, and logic is the universal *organon* of these, namely the direction for its use.\nOther sciences have as their aim the determination of the understanding itself, namely both according to its capacity and according to its highest ends, or [they have as their aim] the objects to which the understanding and the will are restricted by the supreme laws of nature. Thus [is] all other sciences are organs of skill, at most of prudence; metaphysics is the organ of wisdom.\nThe use of metaphysics in respect of the theoretical is merely negative; it does not open up knowledge of things and is not dogmatic; for where should it get [its] the [Eigensch] knowledge of things without the senses? Mathematics makes arbitrary concepts of magnitudes as hypothetical conditions, from which results can follow and be drawn [A categorical science], by mere repetitions. But in the question what a thing is, we can invent no concepts and no relations either. They must refer to what is given, at least as |\ngrounds of their use. Metaphysics merely prevents the false use of reason, which steps beyond its limits and regards the *intellectualia* as objects, whereas they serve only for the *modus cognoscendi* (mode of knowing) of the *sensitive dabilium* and at most for the limitation of the same, in so far as reason would use the *sensitiva* beyond their limits. Pure reason is dogmatic only in respect of the objects of the will; in respect of [specu] speculation, however, it is (merely supervisory) cathartic. In metaphysics there are no hypotheses, because, first, the possibility of a supreme ground of reason would be assumed entirely without rule, and because metaphysics wants precisely to set limits to reason in its pure use.\nAristippus: our judgments about what is agreeable (g likewise all that express mere appearances) are at all times true, the objects being constituted as they may, because agreeableness signifies something that is in us; but the judgments: this or that object exists, [is] are uncertain, because the objects are distinct from us.",[]],"reflm:4446":["*Intellectualia* are either theoretical (g metaphysical) or practical cognitions (g moral).\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n[dogmatics] Aristotle (g Romans) Scholastics\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\nPlato Ramus\nRoughness of the ...\n(s Critique before doctrine)\nunderstanding.\n((s Ideas are intuitions in Plato.) s Method: dogmatic — sceptical — critical.\nDescartes\nHume )\n*Loci Metaphysici Aristotelis*\n*domus conceptuum intellectualium*.\n( s *ideae connatae; acquisitae: per sensus, per anticipationes.* )\n(s Natural or supernatural sources of the intellectual cognitions.)\nLocke. Subjective habituation. (s He took the *principia fiendi* for the *essendi*.)\n( s (s instead of analytic) *Physiologia intellectus*, where [its phae] the origin of its concepts is explained. Wolff has it not at all. )\nCrusius* *Methodus cognitionis praestabilitae.*\n*vel per epigenesin vel per praeformationem.*\n*(s is not to be used, because we do not know what God has opened up to us.)\n(s All have treated metaphysics dogmatically, not critically.)\n(s Criterion of truth: the holding-true.)",[]],"reflm:4447":["Plato gave the origin of the concepts of perfection well. But not that of the *notiones*. Plato an enthusiast, Aristotle an analyst. The objective and subjective principles of the D breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4448":["M VII:\nThe analytic concept of the thing contains what lies in it; the synthetic, what must be attributed to it, in so far as it is to be a certain thing.*\nM VIII:\n(* g *apparentia. conceptus. notio. idea.* The idea [is] that through which things are possible a priori according to the rules of a perfect will. Images. Concepts. Ideas. Plants. Origin: minerals. Ends. )",[]],"reflm:4449":["History of the differences between the *sensitiva* and the *intellectualia*.\nEgyptians. Pythagoras. Heraclitus (g Eleatic). Plato (g *ideae innatae*, innate ideas) and Pythagoras made the *intellectualia** into special objects of possible intuition; his school, the academic |\nphilosophers, exoterically sceptical and esoterically dogmatic. *Intuitus intellectualis* (intellectual intuition), from which everything is derived. Aristotle taught the sensitive (g categories), but did not remain with them. Epicurus: a philosopher of sensations. Aristippus: merely the feeling of the agreeable.\n*(s *intellectualia vel quoad obiecta vel formam cognitionis*)\n(s *intellectualia vel mystice vel logice talia*)\n(s the intellectual cognitions, but *circa obiecta sensuum*, *neque per notiones connatas*)\n( s *vel per intuitus vel conceptus intellectuales.* The latter either metaphysical\nLocke — Leibniz.\nor physiological\nAristotle and Epicurus. )",[]],"reflm:4450":["Aristotle erred in that he made in logic a division of the general concepts through which one can think things: this belongs to metaphysics. Logic has to do with concepts, whatever they may be, and treats only of their relation.",[]],"reflm:4451":["Theosophical and mystical principles.\nPythagoras, the Eleatic school: Parmenides, Plato treated of *intellectualia*; Plato *per ideas connatas et intuitivas*, the others *per discursivas*, but the *sensitiva* only as *apparentias*. Aristotle derived everything from the senses, yet treated of *intellectualia*, which he held to be *sensitiva*.",[]],"reflm:4452":["The true concepts of reason relate only to the relation of things in general. The objects are sensitive; only the use of reason in respect of them proceeds according to merely intellectual laws; if the objects are intellectual, this is enthusiastic.",[]],"reflm:4453":["The *quaestiones* of metaphysics are all raised by common reason and by our most important ends; it is no *organon* of science but of wisdom, and is useful negatively, in removing the hindrances that oppose themselves to the most important knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4454":["In the critique of metaphysics one can make use of two methods. The first is to examine the proofs and seek out their *paralogismi* or *petitiones principii*. The second is to oppose to a proof another, and one equally convincing, of the opposite. This latter method is the best. For since the errors of metaphysical inferences consist chiefly in this, that what holds merely of the conditions of sensible knowledge is enunciated of the object, a proof can seem so rigorous that one will hardly become aware of an error, which is best discovered by a *demonstratio oppositi*.",[]],"reflm:4455":["(g It is the discipline of pure reason. Aesthetic: critique of taste.)\nIdea of metaphysics: Is it a critique or a doctrine; is its procedure zetetic or dogmatic? The question is: what can one know through mere reason without any experience at all [without] (mathematics, morals)? What are the sources, the conditions and the limits? Transcendental philosophy is the critique of pure reason. Study of the subject, confusion of the subjective with the objective, prevention.",[]],"reflm:4456":["In transcendental philosophy *notiones* occur, but not ideas.",[]],"reflm:4457":["In the *metaphysica applicata* much is dogmatic. (g In the transcendental, everything is critical; can anything indeed be discovered through metaphysics? Yes, in respect of the subject, but not of the object.)\n[But] As critique it has utility. Even if religion and virtue are not grounded upon it but have other sources, it serves to remove the hindrances. Critique of science and *organon** of wisdom (g which depends more on doing without than on acquiring. Socrates).\n[The Qvaest] It is necessary; the *quaestiones* are set for it by sound reason and by moral concerns. It is indispensable.\n*(g a propaedeutic of the same; morals is an *organon*.)",[]],"reflm:4458":["Of metaphysics, as of an unknown country on the possession of which we are intent, we have first diligently investigated (g the situation and) the approaches. (It lies in the (g region) hemisphere of pure reason;) we have even drawn the outline of it, [showing] where this island of [Erkennt] knowledge [to the] is connected with the land of experience by bridges, or where it is separated from it by a deep sea; we have even drawn the outline of it, and know, as it were, its geography (g ichnography), but do not yet know what may be found in this country, which some have held to be uninhabitable for men, others have regarded as their actual settlement. After this general geography of this land of reason we wish to consider its general history.",[]],"reflm:4459":["M XI:\nUtility.\nWhat is that which gives the profound investigations of metaphysics their highest motive, and in what is the true importance of such a science to be placed?\n1. It is not immediate desire for knowledge that it satisfies; thus it is not important as a science.\n2. Nor is it important as an *organon* of other sciences, e.g. of natural science.\n3. Thus only as a *propaedeutic* to wisdom. But as such, in what do the chief questions consist which it resolves, or the important modes of knowledge of which it is to be the key? They are two: Is there a God, and is there a future life? The answering of these questions is in turn important in so far as it is a ground of our conduct and confirms the principles of life.\nBut if the principles of life (g other) have independent [rules] sources, |\nM XII: |\nIf no science is needed to support them, but only practically sufficient knowledge, and this is given, even without strict logical perfection, from the practical sources themselves, then this science can serve only for the security of the doctrine of wisdom against all incursions of a seeming reason; and this service is very great. Belief in God gives the metaphysical inferences *favorem utilitatis* (the favour of utility).\n(s Attack not on the things, but on the assertions: speculations.)",[]],"reflm:4460":["[Analytic part princ]\nGenetic part: sensibility and reason.\nZetetic:\na. Analysis, *principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction).\nb. Synthesis.\nc. Antithesis. Sceptical part.",[]],"reflm:4461":["There are two highest practical propositions. 1. What we must do in order to become worthy of happiness. 2. What ground we have [to believe] to believe, in a practically sufficient manner, that one is to become happy in so far as one is worthy of this happiness. Thus a practically sufficient ground of belief in God and in another world. (s All other sciences have a value only as a means to contingent ends (to despise which would be still more sublime); yet in so far as they cultivate (*excoliren*) the understanding, |\nwhich is the universal organon of all ends, they have a value which is greater than the end.)",[]],"reflm:4462":["The longing for happiness expresses the sum of all inclinations, [as is] in whatever they may consist; hence it is the first [long] or supreme desire. The matter and condition of everything (subjective).",[]],"reflm:4463":["The universal longing of men is to be happy; but a [limitation and] condition to which reason (not desire and sensibility) restricts this longing is: to be worthy of this happiness.\nIf now worthiness to be happy is the sole condition of the hope of becoming so, then the precepts of morality take precedence over those of [pru] prudent self-love. But if these laws are to receive their incentives through the hope of becoming happy, then it is necessary that one should at least be able to believe that the world stands under a wise government.",[]],"reflm:4464":["If metaphysics is so treated, it is a hindrance to no empirical science through intellectual fictions (*fictiones*); it safeguards morals |\nagainst false subtlety and promotes the practical; it is advantageous to the fine sciences and promotes inner knowledge of man.\n(s It is the demarcation of pure reason and the frontier-guard, to prevent it from confusing itself, by straying beyond its limits, and from disquieting religion and morals with its chimeras.)",[]],"reflm:4465":["In the sciences of pure reason the philosophy of the present time is more critical than dogmatic, [more] an investigation of the subject [than] and thereby of the possibility of thinking an object for oneself.",[]],"reflm:4466":["The critique of pure reason is a preliminary exercise for metaphysics in theoretical philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4467":["[Speculation] Rational erudition and rational wisdom: the first is speculative and [goes to] is a skill in resolving the questions of reason; the second is a determination of the value of these exercises of the understanding and of their limits. Philosophy contains the former; the philosopher, who contains the end of philosophy in himself, is the latter. He is not an artist of reason, but a lawgiver of human reason.",[]],"reflm:4468":["That reason requires discipline. That, if it is not trained but spreads its branches wildly, it bears blossoms without fruit. |\nThat therefore a master of discipline is needed (not a taskmaster), who governs it. That without this discipline it does not agree with religion and morals, takes the big word, and, since it does not know itself, confuses the sound understanding practised in experiences.",[]],"reflm:4469":["The sceptical method is the best and only one for driving back objections by retorsion. Does a universal doubt then arise from it? No; rather, the pretensions of pure reason in regard to the conditions of the possibility of all things are thereby driven back. Thereby all judgments of sound reason in regard to the world and the practical gain their great authority. Sound reason, or the practical, will never let itself be persuaded that there is no God, provided only that the subtle reason does not seek to wrest the precedence from it.",[]],"reflm:4470":["The first question is: how concepts can arise in us [of things] which have become known to us through no appearance of the things themselves, or propositions which no experience has taught us.",[]],"reflm:4471":["Many modes of knowledge belong to philosophy as means and as [versch] cultivations of our insight, but they do not belong to it as constituent parts. Philosophy itself is the science of determining, for all this use of reason, its ends and its limits [to] or circle. The former are a part of erudition, the latter of wisdom. A sage and a philosopher are, however, different things. The latter goes only to the knowledge and appraisal of the essential ends, the former to their exercise, and is practical. The philosophical mind and the spirit of philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4472":["Empirical (g ground) propositions are always synthetic. Universal ones are never empirical. Hence the former are always universal only *in tantum* (so far as they go). One makes new propositions from empirical ones *per analysin* (by analysis).",[]],"reflm:4473":["M XIX:\nThe question is how we can represent things to ourselves wholly *a priori*, i.e. independently of all experience (even implicitly), and how we can grasp principles which are borrowed from no experience, consequently *a priori*; how it comes about that objects correspond to what is merely a product of our isolating mind, and that these objects are subject to the laws which we prescribe to them. That there are such modes of knowledge *a priori* is taught by pure mathematics and metaphysics; but it is an investigation of importance to see the ground of their possibility. That a representation which is itself an effect of the object should correspond to it is easily [grasped] comprehended. But that something which is merely a birth of my brain should relate as representation to an object is not so clear. Further, that with an impression arising in me from objects another should be connected, and consequently that we connect one representation with another in accordance with experience, is also comprehensible. But that we can from ourselves* [connect others with representations can so] connect with the represented objects properties and predicates (g valid), although no experience has shown them to us as connected, is hard to see. To say that a higher being has already wisely implanted in us such concepts and principles is to undermine all philosophy. It must be sought in the nature of modes of knowledge in general, how a relation and connection is possible where only one of the related terms is given.\n*(s to be able to say something universally of objects without needing that they should disclose it to us;)\nM XX:\nModes of knowledge of experience are not mere impressions. We ourselves must think something in the case of the impressions, in order that they may arise. Thus there must be acts of knowledge which precede experience and by which it is possible. Likewise experiences never give truly universal modes of knowledge, because necessity is lacking to them. Now reason needs universal propositions for certain knowledge. Thus certain universal judgments must lie in it even before experience.",[]],"reflm:4474":["The experiences in which we first form transcendental concepts show us not their origin, but only the empirical condition of their arising. For since they are not drawn from experience, their source must — [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:4475":["Mathematics has no principles at all from experience, not even in its applications (*applicata*), but only objects of experience.",[]],"reflm:4476":["M XXV:\nThe category of [Hypothes] synthesis will probably be entitled: *substantia* (substance) — *Causatum* (what is caused) (g *et independens*), *Compositum* (the composite) (g *et simplex*).\nThe category of analysis: *Totale* (the total) (g *perfectum, completum*) *et* [*simplex*] *partiale*. (g [*Pars*] *Finitum et infinitum* [(*qvod non est pars*)].) (g *particularitas est infinita*.) (g *Vnum et plura*.)\nThe [Tes] idea of thesis: *Realitas* (reality).\n(s *qvae, qvalis, qvanta*.)\nOf synthesis: *Materia et forma* (matter and form).\nOf analysis: *qvantitas et qvalitas* (quantity and quality).\nIn analysis [also a composition] the whole is prior to the part; in synthesis the part is prior to the whole.\nM XXVI: |\n*idem et diversum* [(*qvantitas qvalitas*)]: *realitas, negatio consentiens et oppositum* (*forma affirmationis et negationis*) *externum et internum* (*qvantitas, qvalitas*) *absolutum et relativum*\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\nComparison\nConnection\nRelation\n{s {\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\nIn judgments the relation 1. of agreement, of fitting together.\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}",[]],"reflm:4477":["Analytic propositions can be proved from the *principium contradictionis* or *identitatis* (principle of contradiction or of identity), but synthetic propositions cannot. Whence, then, do we arrive at the latter? 1. Empirically. 2. Through pure intuition. 3. Through subjective conditions of the representation of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4478":["The *principium* (principle) of all synthetic judgments of pure reason (not of pure intuition) is: whatever contains the conditions without which an apprehension would be impossible is true. The *principium contradictionis* holds of all modes of knowledge in so far as they are regarded [auf] as merely possible. That is, what contradicts the concept which I could have of an object is false.",[]],"reflm:4479":["Formulae are not axioms. The former are logical, the latter metaphysical; the former speak only of predicates and subjects in general, the latter supply one.",[]],"reflm:4480":["No predicate belongs to a subject which contradicts an analytic predicate of that subject. No predicate which contradicts the synthetic predicate of a subject belongs to that subject at the same time with it.\nWith respect to synthetic predicates, therefore, every thing is changeable, and the synthesis can be thought as successive determination. The possibility of concepts rests solely on the principle of contradiction; that of synthesis rests on experience.",[]],"reflm:4481":["Because all our simple concepts must be given through the senses (g and cannot be invented), and their object therefore is actual, everything that we think is possible, except that the relation of the concepts may constitute the impossibility. This relation may be logical or real; in the first case it is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:4482":["When I deny, I think something — — affirm, — — —\n}\nBoth are something logically, but the first is really nothing, both\ntogether are logically nothing; the zero is logically something, really nothing; transcendentally nothing: where there are no data for thinking, and so neither affirmation nor denial. Impossibility, however, consists solely in contradiction. Hence it is impossible that the transcendental nothing should be possible.",[]],"reflm:4483":["In the outer margin, beginning beside the third sentence of M § 18:\nEven for impossibility something given is required. The first data are not known as possible *a priori*, but constitute the condition of all our judgments about possibility, so that only that is possible which agrees with the *a priori* conditions [der] of empirical knowledge. Logical possibility, the *principium contradictionis*, is not objective, but pertains to knowledge. We cannot conceive any possibility of intuition, of reality, of real relation, or of the necessary, except in so far as [es] the principles thereof have been given in experience.",[]],"reflm:4484":["The possibility of what cannot be determinately known *a priori* must be represented according to the analogy of nature, that is, of the empirical.",[]],"reflm:4485":["Change is given through experience and is a problem for reason.",[]],"reflm:4486":["*Opposita* (opposites) in succession do not contradict one another. Hence the *succedens* (what follows) is not a *contradictorie oppositum* (contradictory opposite) of the *antecedens* (what precedes); hence changes are no proof of the possibility of the *contradictorie oppositum*, and therefore also not of contingency.",[]],"reflm:4487":["Either substances or their state are changeable, but in these cases the change has different meanings; e.g. that the shape, the place, is changeable means that the substances are changeable in respect of both.",[]],"reflm:4488":["In the world we infer contingency not so much from change as rather from dependence upon certain conditions. Afterwards, when we take together everything that is necessary only under a condition, we infer the contingency of the whole: *primus motor* (the first mover).",[]],"reflm:4489":["Beside and \nOne time contains one state, another time its opposite: this is not *repugnans* (conflicting), but *oppositum potentiale* or *disparatum*.\nFrom merely sensible ideas of succession nothing intellectual can be inferred.",[]],"reflm:4490":["*Omnitudo collectiva est completudo. distributiva vniversalitas.* (Collective allness is completeness; distributive allness is universality.)\n[*Multitudo completa est omnitudo.*] Universality [Allgemeinheit] as against particularity (g exception).\n*Vnitas in coniunctione plurium completa est totalitas.* Allness [Allheit].\n(*Vnum completum est totum.*) *Completudo in coniunctione plurium: Totalitas. defectus.*\nIn the case of the whole there is a taking together: *coniunctio*; in the case of the *compositum*, a putting together.\nA number is not a *compositum*; hence a body is a *totum reale*, *non ideale*.",[]],"reflm:4491":["The problematic concept of absolute totality, of necessity.\nSuch concepts we can indeed think, but not determine and carry out.",[]],"reflm:4492":["*Conceptus substantiae est terminator non entium, sed statuum.* (The concept of substance is the limit-setter not of non-beings but of states.)\nThat lies in our reason; now comes its application to the senses. What is the proper subject, namely that which serves the manifold [prae] accidents and modifications, the former without distinction of time, the latter in relation to it, as one and the same fixed point?",[]],"reflm:4493":["58'. E II 585.\nM 58:\nOnly three kinds of *respectus reales* (real relations) are possible:\n1. of consequence to ground, *dependentiae ab una et causalitatis ab altera parte* (of dependence on the one side and of causality on the other);\n2. of the part to the [composite] whole;\n3. of the *accidens* to the substance.\nIn all (g) three unity arises: of subordination*, of coordination, and of inherence (g of many accidents in one subject. I is the intuition of a substance.)\nAll three have their limits:\n1. the *independens* and the *absolute necessarium*;**\n2. the *totalitas absoluta* (*completa*. [*Mundus*] *infinitum*), that is, a synthesis than which none [further] greater is possible;\n3. the substantial.\n[Du] The first concept indicates how things belong through one another, the second how they belong to one another, the third how they belong in one another. All three are *termini* (limits). The first [*a priori*] the *necessarium* and its *oppositum*: the absolute or *primum contingens* (*libertas*); the second the *universitas*: everything taken together, and its *oppositum*: no taking together, the *simplex*; the third substantiality and its *oppositum*: mere relation.\nNone of the three can be understood; the first, because the condition of necessity, or on the other hand all necessity, is lacking; the second, because the *terminus* of the synthesis is lacking, and in the third the predicates are lacking. |\nM 58': |\nAll these relations are only the realised logical relations of subject and predicate, of antecedent to consequent, and of the universality of the concept of the subject. Subject and predicate with *est* appended behind signifies *existere*. Identity: necessity. Or universality alone: necessity; particularity: contingency.\nM 58:\n*(g One can also think a subordinated division.)\n**(g It is not yet clear that the first ground (e.g. freedom) is something necessary.)",[]],"reflm:4494":["All outer appearances are *phaenomena substantiata* (substantiated phenomena), because we treat them as substances.",[]],"reflm:4495":["That something is a subject and not a predicate (of the impression, *apparentia*) of another we can know only through the active verb »I«, that is, through consciousness; hence *corpora*, *h.e. externe apparentia*, *sunt phaenomena substantiata*, *h.e. permanentia substrata ceteris phaenomenis* (bodies, that is, outer appearances, are substantiated phenomena, that is, permanent substrata of the other phenomena).",[]],"reflm:4496":["Around and \nThree principles. 1. [Every] In everything actual there is the relation of a substance to its accident (*inhaerentia*); 2. of ground to consequence (*dependentia*); 3. of the parts and of composition (*compositio*).\nThere are therefore three presuppositions: of the subject, of the ground, and of the parts; and three real modes: of *insitio* (inherence), of *subordinatio* and of *compositio*. Hence also three first principles: 1. subject, which is no predicate; 2. ground, which is no consequence; 3. unity, which is not (g in itself) composite.",[]],"reflm:4497":["The community of the boundary of extension is contact, or the impenetrability of the contact of matter.\nPoints cannot touch one another.",[]],"reflm:4498":["At the upper margin, above the column heading:\nAll composition in a body cannot be removed. |\nFor the body is possible only according to the determinations of space, that is, [the part] so that every constituent part of it occupies a space. All composition in a *phaenomenon substantiatum* cannot possibly be removed.",[]],"reflm:4499":["Above, beside and \nThe substantiality of the parts of a body is only relative; namely, each part exists without inhering in the other. In themselves, however, they are not substances, but the *phaenomena* of substances. The *phaenomenon* of any substance, if it consists in the filling of space, is possible only through the condition of space, that is, only according to laws of continuity, and not through composition out of the simple, but always as a magnitude [which] whose parts are all in turn magnitudes.",[]],"reflm:4500":["The opinion of Leibniz, that bodies are *phaenomena*, offers nothing other than this: that the idea of body is sensitive, its intellectual substratum being unknown, i.e. that it expresses only a concept which arises from the relation of unknown substances to the sensitive mode of cognizing. If this [concept] is held to be intellectual, it is called a *phaenomenon substantiatum*. If this is avoided, bodies as *phaenomena* [sunt in] do not consist of simples, i.e. in dividing according to the concept of space [non terminus] the division is without end. But when conceived intellectually, [body] consists of monads. This thesis, however, has no influence on the consequence, i.e. it is not a principle of empirical cognition, but merely indicates that the connection of the soul with bodies is different from its connection with simple substances in general, not objectively, but only according to the cognitive form. When the sensitive form is removed, the intellectual form remains through |\npre-established harmony (*harmonia praestabilita*). And therefore both generation and death are nothing but different representative states, and what requires explanation is not a connection with heterogeneous substances but the heterogeneity of the cognitive form. Space, however, is the formal [element] of *phaenomena*; if it is held to be the very real conception of the connection of substances, it is called a *phaenomenon intellectuatum*, namely if it is extended further than to the sensitive mode of cognizing.\nIf sensitive cognition is extended in its kind to infinity, it nevertheless remains sensitive, and bodies will never be found empirically to consist of simples. Therefore monads are of no use in physics, and in metaphysics are of negative use, namely that, by taking *phaenomena* for the real constitution of objects, sensitive axioms may not become, as it were, intellectual.",[]],"reflm:4501":["All composition can be undone; that is, whatever has been composed can also be separated. But then simple parts remain, if the composition was made of simple parts, or parts of magnitude [Größentheile], if it was made of such. The simple is opposed to magnitude, and the composite to the separated.\nAs regards, however, all the parts [des Zus] of magnitude, there is in the case of the *continuum* only an *omnitudo collectiva*; every magnitude contains the possibility of a composition, but is not on that account composite.",[]],"reflm:4502":["At the upper margin:\nIf the *phaenomena* of different substances (*qvae sunt in locis diversis*) are really many substances, then all matter consists of sim|ple\nparts. The multitude of these simple parts cannot be infinite; for otherwise, by their moving away from one another, they would increase their extension to infinity; and if there is a finite velocity between two of them, the velocity of the surface would be infinite. If they are not substances but only *phaenomena*, they do not consist of simple parts at all. Accordingly they are infinitely divisible, but no alleged part is actually infinite. If the multitude of parts makes up a finite number of simple substances — [the text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4503":["Time and space alone admit of limits, but not of totality. The first beginning and the outermost limit of the world are equally inconceivable. For the first is a being and a not-being at once, and both seem to indicate an absolute time and space, i.e. something that is bounded and yet contains nothing.",[]],"reflm:4504":["Time extends over everything and is the form of inner sense.",[]],"reflm:4505":["The doctrines of space and time have this positive [aspect], that the propositions which relate to them in respect of sensibility cannot be reformed by reason, because they are not things, and that they are quite |\ncertain and evident. But [they have] this negative [aspect], that they restrict the application [of reason] to the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:4506":["In regard to space: the place, the position, the situation, the region. The place is a point; the position is the relation of a place to the places of others; the — [text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4507":["Space is neither a thing itself, nor an actual real relation whereby one thing posits something in another; consequently it is not a concept of the understanding, for such a concept at least has some object. It does not, then, relate to the object but to the subject, and indeed not to sensation but to the form of the senses.",[]],"reflm:4508":["The axioms of space and time, in respect of sensibility, are certain and intuitive. The principles 'everything is somewhere and sometime', and those of shape and place, are mere conditions of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:4509":["All spaces are simultaneous and all times successive; in space a thing is not in many places [at once], but only in succession.",[]],"reflm:4510":["They are not acquired but innate intuitions. (s They are *conceptus originarii* [original concepts], not *derivativi* (by abstraction).)\nSensation\nAppearance\nConcept\nHardness\nShape\nSubstance\nOf body.\nThe play. Alteration without alterable things.",[]],"reflm:4511":["Beside and \nIs space prior to things? Certainly. For the law of coordination is prior to things and lies at their foundation.\nBut is space sensible [empfindbar] without things, or can it be noticed only through things? Yes [only through things]; hence empty space as an object of the senses, e.g. between planets, is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4512":["Under and \nPure space is merely the potential relation and is represented prior to things, but not as something actual. Empty space without filling is possible; but absolute space, as that in relation to which creatures stand in actual relation, is impossible. For no substance is present anywhere without acting, and indeed externally; but in absolute space there are no correlates.",[]],"reflm:4513":["Space and time as yet give nothing actual. Only sensation offers it. Hence the real understanding is an activity parallel to sensation.",[]],"reflm:4514":["According to reason, time is the *nexus* of coordination (*coordinatio*), but according to sensibility that of subordination (*subordinatio*). The first and the last can be assigned arbitrarily in space.",[]],"reflm:4515":["Space is the ground of the possibility of relations, indeed even of their necessity. Possibility and actuality are not distinguished in space and time. In both, the part is possible only through the whole. Both are so connected that space is at all times, i.e. necessarily. Time relates to being in general, space to external being. Both are the only given grounds of synthesis, without any inferences.",[]],"reflm:4516":["Time and space are the necessary in intuition. Time expresses the whole existence of things.",[]],"reflm:4517":["We cannot use the [Zeitwort] word *est* otherwise than as a Zeitwort [verb]. 'God is', as distinguished from 'will be'. The predicates of possible things can be used only *in praesenti*.",[]],"reflm:4518":["That time is the form of inner sense can be seen from this: that one can indeed have it in thought, but can never intuit it as something external, as one can extension. Substances are in space; their state (*accidentia*) is in time. All predicates have as copula: *est, fuit, erit*.",[]],"reflm:4519":["The synthetic propositions of space do not lie in the general concept of space, any more than the chemical empirical propositions about |\ngold lie in its general concept; they are drawn from the intuition of it, or found in the intuition of it.",[]],"reflm:4520":["The first cause of all existence is eternity, and the relation of the existence of contingent things to the all-comprehending existence is time. [The first] In so far as this cause of all existence is the cause of all things, it is omnipresence, and the relation of created things to it is space.\nHow does it come about that our sensibility contains determinations in which reason sees its own fundamental concepts? This is because the sensible form is possible through the same grounds which the understanding requires for comprehension.",[]],"reflm:4521":["The present concept of the world serves rather to bring into order the idea of a substantial whole and of an isolated whole than to prove thereby the existence of any determinate cause.",[]],"reflm:4522":["The world (g which) can be no part of another whole is the world in the transcendental sense. The world that is no part of an actual whole is the world in the physical sense. Since the [possible] possible is distinguished from the actual only in that in the |\nformer the conditions are not considered in thoroughgoing determination, an entire world which has no other conditions outside itself than in the absolutely necessary being must also have no other limits of actuality than the inner conditions of possibility. From a world in this sense one could infer a single cause and its sufficiency, and further the unity of this world. But from a world that is not the absolute universe no more can be inferred than from a *compositum substantiale* in general. Finally, from a mere *multitudo rerum finitarum* no more can be inferred than from a single *finitum*.\nThe concept of completion is the last in increase, and arises from the concept of all, in synthesis as well as in analysis. The limiting concept is what remains in the diminution of the *conditiones*: e.g. a ground that is no consequence; the absolutely necessary; the absolutely (regardless of all conditions) contingent, i.e. freedom.",[]],"reflm:4523":["Above and below the heading »Notio mundi«:\nPrivate happiness and religion must, in the case of man, be derived either from a universally valid inner principle or from a universally valid outer principle, if they are to agree.",[]],"reflm:4524":["Out of our concept of the world only the concept of the relation of each part of a *compositum substantiale* (substantial composite) to the whole is brought into order. We can think of no effects of a *substantia* or *compositum* except [under the presupposition] of universal rules of the nature of the substance itself or of its cause. In a whole, because the parts stand in *commercium* (community), the purpose directed to each part taken singly cannot subsist together with that directed to the others, unless their community |\nagrees with it; otherwise they have no community at all. The greater the number of substances, the harder it is to produce a concord out of single purposes. At last the unity in the concord of the substances must in general follow from the unity of the whole. The purpose directed to the whole, and the nature placed in it for that reason, can alone contain the supreme ground of the possibility of the agreement of the purposes directed to the parts. One cannot begin from the purposes directed to each part in order to produce a clock by putting them together. The whole must have a nature which contains universal laws, according to which alone it is possible for the [parts] purpose of the parts to be harmonious. Only the unity of the purpose directed to the whole can become the ground of the harmony of the purposes of the parts. Even in each single substance the occurrences must flow from it according to universal laws, and this is its nature. It is a substance solely by this, that the universal ground of the accidents lies in itself; otherwise it would always accord with another. Nature is the same thing in respect of real determinations and alterations as space is in respect of the *principium* of all possible relations according to the laws of sensibility.\n(g *commercium*. Community. *nexus reciprocus*.)",[]],"reflm:4525":["The world is the absolute whole of possible experience. We can very well think an absolute whole of the world, only not in space and time. The absolute whole in appearance is a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:4526":["At the very top of the page.\n*Relatio* (relation) is a determination of a being by which (g or) it can act on another or be acted on by it. A reciprocal active relation is one by which it can both act and be acted on. |\n*Nexus* (connection) is the relation of an actually acting being to a being acted on. In every whole there is *nexus*.",[]],"reflm:4527":["The universal is not always borrowed from the particular. The [math] geometrical properties are not borrowed from the particular determinations of the figures; rather the latter are borrowed from universal space. It is the same with the science of number. In the divine will everything is determined according to universal rules. And if I remove the universality of the ground in the *substantia*, it ceases to be a substance. This real universality is the derivation of the partial determinations from the whole as from a ground. It is then a standing *principium*. When the things are already given, knowledge proceeds from the particular to the universal. But from the ground through which the things are given, the inference proceeds from the universal to the particular, and in the case of the whole from the *universitas* to the *individuum*.",[]],"reflm:4528":["Series of the understanding. The totality of it requires a supreme cause.\nSeries of the senses. The series itself always requires something preceding.\nNothing happens in the world *per fatum*, *per casum*, *per saltum*. (g Rules of the) order of nature. (s *non existunt — non fiunt — non connectuntur per saltum*.)",[]],"reflm:4529":["The limits of appearance cannot appear. Hence |\nno finite world can be represented to the senses. If I stretch a hand out of the world, then to that extent the larger world appears; the remaining space does not appear, except in so far as [I] I put something into it. Hence appearance without an object (the empty) is not possible. But an infinite time *a parte ante* (backwards) is indeed necessary in appearance, and in the concept of reason mathematical magnitude has no place at all; yet in this the understanding cannot suffice for the complete exposition of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:4530":["The *saltus spurius* (spurious leap) is attraction at a distance. For here the *intermedia* are not necessarily *causae intermediae* (intermediate causes); for the *intermedia* are only places.",[]],"reflm:4531":["Two (g successive) states are never immediately connected with one another. For two states lie in two different points of time. But between two instants there is a time. In what states, then, was the thing during this time? [It is the] The states are the extremes of a line. There is therefore an infinite series of intermediate states. (But can I not consider two states as two successive series which are immediately connected with one another, as two lines are?) All this cannot be reconciled with the rational propositions; but the question is also not a rational one, but only this: what are the laws and conditions of appearance. Moreover, the difficulty arises not from the understanding but from another law of sensibility, namely that of regarding a succession as a series whose members can be counted through, and which therefore could never be infinite.",[]],"reflm:4532":["Various power and honour\nApportioned by gradation, the countless hosts,\nUnequally sated with the splendour of the light imparted,\nStand in long order from God to barren Nothing.",[]],"reflm:4533":["There are negative or positive oppositions; in the case of the latter [is] the connection of both makes a negative opposition, which is logical. There is continuity here; one has to find the *punctum flexus contrarii* (point of contrary flexure) between magnanimity and miserliness. A different object is a different direction.",[]],"reflm:4534":["On M § 393,\nSimple beings (as such) cannot be parts of the world of sense at all. For then they would be parts of the object of outer sense, that is, of the extended; but the extended does not consist of simple parts. Hence every principle of life must be reckoned to the intelligible, and so too the soul. Of the intelligible, however, one knows nothing but the relation of the appearances, of which it is the substratum, in the world of sense. What it may be outside that world (after death) is therefore absolutely unknowable. On this our ignorance is also grounded with regard to all organised beings and beings that organise matter, whose possibility, resting on a principle of life, cannot be comprehended at all. Simple beings have no place in the world.",[]],"reflm:4535":["At the very top of the page, opposite the column heading and M § 393:\nWhether the world is in empty space or not (g is not an empirical question); for no empirical conditions of determination are possible as to whether a space is empty or not (in empty space no outer experience would be possible either). Whether it is possible that all matter should be removed from space by annihilation is an intellectual question; whether by motion, a physical one, which presupposes whether [the impenetrabili] a body which is moved takes with it all the matter that fills the space, or whether one [part] is not immovable.",[]],"reflm:4536":["At the upper margin and beside and \nThe question whether bodies outside me are something actual is answered thus: bodies are not bodies (phenomena) outside my sensibility, and so they are only in the representing power of sentient beings. Whether something outside me corresponds to these appearances of theirs is a question about the cause of this appearance, and not about the existence of that itself which appears. This existence, as that of an object, is the representation of the connection with all appearances according to laws.",[]],"reflm:4537":["All *commercium* of substances is* a harmony of their activities. The ground of this harmony therefore cannot lie in one alone, but in the common ground of all; the *commercium* breaks off.\n*(s The state of different substances in so far as they flow into one another *mutuo* (reciprocally): *realis* or *idealis*.)",[]],"reflm:4538":["*sola non successio plurium existentium non ideo est simultaneitas.* (Mere non-succession of several existing things is not on that account simultaneity.)\nThe *commercium substantiarum* is either *absolutum* or *hypotheticum*: the unconditioned community or the conditioned. The former is such merely by their existing, or by means of a third. The former is a *systema arbitrarium*.\nThe *commercium* established hypothetically is always *a causa extramundana*; but either [per] as *ordo naturae* (of mutual dependence of substances *secundum regulas generales*) or [as] *supernaturaliter*, so that there is really no *commercium naturale*. The first is *influxus physicus*, the other *harmonia idealis vel praestabilita vel occasionalis*.",[]],"reflm:4539":["Beside and \n*Influxus crassior* (the cruder influence) is that according to which a substance flowing into another pours accidents into the other, so that they are not sustained by the patient but actuated by an alien force. Such a passion, however, could also be feigned in the system of occasional causes. From the impossibility of such a passion, and because accidents inhere only through a proper force, it can be concluded that substances cannot be in *commercium* except by means of the same sustaining cause.",[]],"reflm:4540":["*Intuitus* (intuition) is either through inner or through outer sense. Pure outer *intuitus* always has the idea of space annexed to it.",[]],"reflm:4541":["The universal grounds of sensible choice (*Willkühr*) are inclinations.\nThe universal grounds of rational choice are principles.\n[The concept of freedom is from] According to theoretical principles the concept of a hypothetically necessary choice, according to practical principles that of an absolutely independent choice, is the more probable.",[]],"reflm:4542":["The beginning of an action [wirken] must be encountered in the world; everything, therefore, that is not free has no first beginning. Whether a being whose *subsistentia* [subsistence] is *derivativa* [derivative] cannot be independent in its actions? *spontaneitas simplex* [simple spontaneity].",[]],"reflm:4543":["Blind is what one cannot have insight into.\nBlind necessity (fate) (g providence), blind chance.",[]],"reflm:4544":["Everything is necessary either in and for itself or externally. Contingency is only respective. But free actions of contingent substances are supposed to have neither of these two necessities.",[]],"reflm:4545":["At the bottom margin:\nThe concept of a conditionally necessary power of choice [Willkühr] is only a hypothesis of theory, and must be assumed in order to explain free actions as *phaenomena*. The concept of an unconditionally free power of choice is a *postulatum practicum* [practical postulate], which everyone actually assumes, and in which he contradicts himself when he demands of others a use of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4546":["At the top margin:\n*libertas est vel facultatis vel status* [freedom is either of the faculty or of the state] (dogs in a kennel); *itidem est vel interna vel externa; haec est independentia a cogentibus* [likewise it is either internal or external; the latter is independence from compelling things].\n(s *Voluntas non potest cogi, sed arbitrium; hoc autem non simpliciter, sed secundum qvid.* [The will cannot be compelled, but the power of choice can; and this not absolutely but in a certain respect.])",[]],"reflm:4547":["Beside and \n*libertas itaqve est vel arbitrii vel potestatis* [freedom, then, is either of the power of choice or of power]. *libertas arbitrii est interna et [propria] per se talis. libertas potestatis est externe talis. libertas arbitrii est practica [vel transsce] vel transscendentalis; prior est independentia a stimulis, posterior ab omni necessitatione externa. actus arbitrii simpliciter spontanei sunt originarii, secundum qvid sunt derivativi. Necessitatio arbitrii est vel per stimulos et est pathologica, vel per motiva et est practica.*\n*Spontaneitas arbitrii simpliciter talis in ente originario est necessitas actuum arbitrii independens ab omni externo, ergo est libertas.*",[]],"reflm:4548":["Beside and \nFreedom is twofold: either of the power of choice or of power [Gewalt]. The first is internal, the second merely external.\nFreedom of the power of choice (g *arbitrium liberum*) is *independentia a stimulis* [independence from stimuli] and is entitled practical freedom. Opposed to it stands the *arbitrium brutum necessitatum a stimulis* [brute power of choice, necessitated by stimuli]. (The *arbitrium liberum* [is either] has either *spontaneitatem practicam* or *transscendentalem*.)\nThe *necessitatio arbitrii bruti* is pathological, that of the *arbitrii liberi* is [intellectual] practical. The *causae impulsivae* [impelling causes] of the former are *stimuli*, those of the latter *motiva* [motives]. The *actus arbitrii liberi* are either *originarii* or *derivativi*; the former is transcendental, the latter merely practical freedom.",[]],"reflm:4549":["Over and \nFreedom consists in the (g subjective) independence of the power of choice from everything that flows in upon our senses, inwardly and outwardly. Right is the objective independence of our power of choice from that of others, i.e. the limitation of every power of choice through the condition of reciprocal agreement, and the necessity of the actions that flow from these conditions. The wisest and kindest man, who designed everything for our best, does not on that account have a right over us.\n(s One must also not transform the title of right into that of kindness and religion. The latter is to serve only for carrying out [what right establishes].)",[]],"reflm:4550":["Beside and.\nThat something pleases [gefällt oder] or gratifies a man in a preferential way is not his doing; but that he lets himself be pleased by it [sich belieben läßt] is to be imputed to himself.\nHence he who is malicious by natural temperament reproaches himself for it, because the blame falls not on what is innate but on the deficiency of the will.\nIt seems that a man must give account to the spirit [Geist] that is in him. He must therefore surely have a capacity to obey its command or not. The latter is freedom. The soul [Seele] senses, the mind [Gemüth] judges [a poster] or estimates *a posteriori*, the spirit *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4551":["In judging actions that have happened, [imputiren] we nevertheless presuppose that they all have their determining (though not (g still by others) determined) grounds, and we impute them even though they may flow from the innate character of the man; indeed, we impute them all the more, partly because one |\nyet knows that everyone has a higher power of choice, under which this character itself stands; partly because everyone yet acts according to his own liking and inclination, and thus not against his own liking, whether he does good or evil, and sees that, according to the rule of the higher will, punishments and rewards fit such a character, and so recognizes himself as worthy of them, since he can renounce himself, and hence also his inclinations.",[]],"reflm:4552":["Near M § 770:\nThe question is whether there is an organically forming nature (epigenesis), or only one that forms mechanically and chemically.\nIt seems that a spirit belongs to this, on account of the unity of the relation of all the parts, as regards their generation, to each single one. But is there not also in animals and plants a spiritual, enlivening being? In this way one would have to assume an enlivening spirit even in the first chaos, in order to explain the various animals which now can only propagate themselves.\nHere it breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4553":["It is not clear that the human soul could not be [through th] begotten from that of the parents, although it is a simple substance. No materialism is needed.",[]],"reflm:4554":["Over and \nFirst there is the question whether the human being, or merely [the same] the soul, will live after death, and wherein the identity of substance and |\nof person consists. [In the hum] Whether the future life depends merely on the body or on the soul. Resurrection. Second, if the problem concerns only the future life of the human soul: whether it merely lives after death (g contingently) or is immortal (g necessarily) (universality, eternity). The first by means of reason from the ends of God, the second from the nature of the soul: a. from particular observation, b. from the nature of a spirit in general.\nThe grounds of the problem are either explanation of the *phaenomena* (g speculative) (e.g. the appearance of spirits) or the concern of man: that of self-love or of morality.\nEpicurus held it necessary for philosophers to decide nothing about it, so that 1. the motives would be purely moral, 2. no superstitious pretence would produce false terrors and hopes. This rests on too high an opinion of humanity. [The Stoi] Socrates had considered the future state merely problematically. The Stoics in the beginning also abstracted from it. Without it [i.e. the future state] the order of nature does not agree with the moral order, and well-being and well-conduct are separated. The Christian law makes it the chief point of view of all motives of this life.",[]],"reflm:4555":["Life does not belong to mere matter. Life appropriate to a certain nature. Freedom. Animal life, spiritual life.",[]],"reflm:4556":["Over, beside and \nLife consists in the inner sufficiency of spontaneity for representations and desires; but under outer hindrances this substance can sleep, although it lives. The body does not contain the condition of the possibility of the life of the soul, or even of any of its |\nactivities, but is a hindrance, to the overcoming of which the soul is nevertheless bound. The disposition of the body is a diminution of this hindrance; hence thinking is the more free; breaks off.\n(g since we can conduct experiences only in the body, they will prove dependence rather than independence.)",[]],"reflm:4557":["Over and \nI cannot say: I know that there is another life; but: I believe it. This much I know, that no one can prove there is no other life. Nor can one say that one believes there is no other life; rather, one can only not believe that there is one. This indicates a still continuing want of insight rather than an insight into the contrary. The hope of another world is a necessary hypothesis of reason with regard to ends, and a necessary hypothesis of the heart with regard to morality. It is thus practically based on secure grounds, but theoretically obscure and uncertain. Beyond this world one can know only what is incumbent on us in this one.",[]],"reflm:4558":["He who recognizes morality as important, and therefore a holy law and a just judgment together with a kindly government, is easily convinced of the future. He to whom this seems to have only a conditional necessity cannot be convinced at all. For why should precisely the enjoyment of a life that amounts to mere enjoyment be continued after death?",[]],"reflm:4559":["That there should be a being after death which is conscious of all the deter|minations\nof our life and ascribes them to itself as past, does not yet prove that this being is I. Here it seems that consciousness must have been interrupted, or at least must be continued through the dull interval upon awakening. Inner sense must continue uninterrupted [although] (as regards sensation), although consciousness is interrupted as regards reflection. Thus the constant personality in this life, notwithstanding all changes, proves that the nature of the soul survives the body. Now the I cannot be a passive sensation. Consequently it is self-active and does not depend on the body.",[]],"reflm:4560":["Another world, either in the cosmographical or in the cosmological sense, according to region or according to kind. The latter either objectively or subjectively. The transition either gradual or sudden. Communion with this world or not. Ghosts.",[]],"reflm:4561":["Personality is either continuing (continuous) or interrupted. Sleep of the soul.",[]],"reflm:4562":["If in the sequel someone were to become conscious of himself, and also conscious of all such actions as we remember, would he on that account be I myself? Thus the identity of the person rests not on the agreement of the apperception but on its continuation, even if this should be among obscure representations.",[]],"reflm:4563":["*Mundus* [world] is either *aspectabilis* [viewable] or *concupiscibilis* [desirable]. If it is [sens] viewable as *sensibilis*, then so far it is also (g *sensitive*) *concupiscibilis*; if it is |\nconsidered as *intelligibilis*, then it is also *intellectualiter concupiscibilis*, not according to appearance and feelings but according to concepts. *Mundus moralis est intelligibilis qvoad perfectionem* [the moral world is intelligible as regards perfection], and is in union with the *sensibilis*, a union which is however dissolved by death. With the ceasing of (g present) sensibility we pass, to be sure, out of one *mundus aspectabilis* into another, but the *mundus intelligibilis* endures, both in respect of substance (*noumenon*) and in respect of the reciprocal determination of the intelligences.",[]],"reflm:4564":["*Theologia est vel philosophica vel revelata* [theology is either philosophical or revealed]; the former is either *transscendentalis* or *naturalis*, just as (practical) religion is either *supernaturalis* or *naturalis*. (s Theology is either *rationalis* or *revelata*; the former either *philosophica* or *communis*; the former either transcendental or natural. He who contends that there is none but the former is a *deista* [deist]; he who contends for the latter (a supreme intelligence) is a *theista* [theist]; he who admits none but the rational is also a *naturalista*.)\n(s Natural [theology] is either metaphysical or *physicotheologia*.)",[]],"reflm:4565":["Of a most real being it is not more comprehensible that it exists than of a limited being; but from its presupposition it is easier to derive limited beings. Hence there is here a neces|sity\nof assuming such a being, on account of the grounds of speculative reason. It is not easier to see the necessity of a wholly holy will[als die] and its connection with moral perfection than that of a will which is limited; but we need it for morality. In this way both proofs hold for the subject, which is also sufficient for the supreme ground of actions.",[]],"reflm:4566":["Unless a most perfect being is proved, I have no determinate concept of its properties. If it is not determined that there is only a single original being, I have no concept of their number or of their properties. Likewise with the best world, as the ground of the judgment about what awaits rational creatures in the world.",[]],"reflm:4567":["From the concept of no thing can we derive its necessity, but we can well derive the concept from the conditions of necessity; for necessity is morality.",[]],"reflm:4568":["329'. E II 1236. 1630.\nM 329:\nThe subjective conditions of human reason are the *postulata* [postulates] of its use and not *axiomata* [axioms].\n(g Absolutely necessary is the assumption of a thing in the subjective sense, without which I cannot, according to the law of [meiner] the universal human reason, have any concept of the possibilities of things in general.\nThat the sensitive conditions affect only the knowledge of the present world serves only to prevent us from setting dogmatic hindrances against the concepts of reason (which are necessary on other grounds with regard to God).)\nM 329':\n(g This necessity from subjective grounds of universal human reason shows itself also in this, that we can derive the origin of the contingent more easily from a will which acts and chooses in conformity with the knowledge of manifold possibles, because knowledge and will are what is truly active in us, and because it is everywhere necessary to regard ends as given, so that our actions may be in harmony with the world.)",[]],"reflm:4569":["Everything possible is considered in respect to everything possible (through either-or), and so lies as a member of the division in a whole which contains all the data for it. Possibility in every respect (absolute, simply possible) requires breaks off.\nThe transcendental proof is a necessary hypothesis of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:4570":["All our possibility presupposes data, [und die] either for all things with the indeterminateness of each, or a given principle of relation with the indeterminateness of a particular one, e.g. in space triangles are possible. The logical relation is the principle of contradiction; that of the *phaenomena* (appearances) is space and time; that of things and their real relations is forces. From the former one cannot infer to the latter. The principle of contradiction is usable only negatively, namely for recognizing the impossible through contradiction; positively, for the possible, it presupposes too much, although even so it is true.",[]],"reflm:4571":["Whether the Gospel has so improved theology?\nThe idea of the highest perfection and of a universal and immediate governor is needed from want, but is not necessary from reason.",[]],"reflm:4572":["The absolute proposition: if something is God, it exists, expresses absolute necessity. Hence I cannot say: if something is a sun, it exists. The absolute proposition which is to express necessary existence must be analytic; therefore: if something is absolutely necessary, it exists.",[]],"reflm:4573":["From the *data* [data] of experience we cannot infer the existence of any being, nor its properties, which in respect of magnitude is a mere ideal and surpasses all concepts of experience.",[]],"reflm:4574":["Everything can be possible only through one. For through many together there is not possible what is possible through one who unites their powers.\nThe proof from concepts is either of God or of the world in general; the first: from the concept of a most perfect [being] to derive the necessity; and the second: to derive that from this.",[]],"reflm:4575":["The positive concept of God is easy. The negative serves for correction. Analogy.",[]],"reflm:4576":["Our knowledge of God is for the most part negative, i.e. it serves to determine the prac|tical\nas well as theoretical limits of our theological knowledge, and also to guard against errors, both logically and practically harmful. Presumption, idle curiosity. For all our knowledge of God is only an investigation of what might belong in the ideal of the highest perfection. Now from our idea of the highest perfection we cannot derive everything that belongs to the thing itself; rather the material principles must be given through experience, and the form serves only to separate off what contradicts perfection.",[]],"reflm:4577":["M330:\n(g Transcendental theology.)\nThe concept which terminates all things is that of a necessary being; of an original being or first of all beings, the highest being, so far as it has no ground above itself; of the all-sufficient being or being of all beings, so far as it has no ground beside itself and nothing independent outside itself, and consequently of a single being. The question is whether one of these three concepts posits all the others. We need the first two concepts because we require a *terminus* of subordination. These questions are transcendental and concern merely the subordination of our concepts. The second question, concerning an origin of all things known *a priori* as to the constitution of the cause, whether it has understanding or not, is metaphysical and presupposes analogies with known causes. The concept of the original being |\nM 330': |\nrelates to essences and mere possibilities, that of the cause to actualities, and causality always refers to contingencies.\nM 330:\n(g Natural theology: a perfect spirit as to understanding and will. Namely in relation to nature.)",[]],"reflm:4578":["Cosmotheology or physicotheology. The latter is empirical.",[]],"reflm:4579":["God as the highest intelligence: fundamental concept of natural theology. Reference to our concerns and ends. *Conceptus terminator* [stator], *comprehensor*, *definitor*; the concept which determines the limits of all things; *omnium determinator* in subordination, coordination and conception, because reason proceeds only *a universali ad particularia* [from the universal to the particular].\n(g In itself only the all is sufficiently determined: *conprehensor*.) What belongs to the concept of God.",[]],"reflm:4580":["The transcendental concept is needed, not the transcendental proof; likewise that one should see that nothing heterodox can be asserted with ground. The necessity of the divine existence [entweder] as a necessary hypothesis either of the mere concepts of the possible or of the experiences in this world, likewise as a hypothesis of morality. Absolute necessity cannot be proved.\n(g The proof of the existence of God is not apodictic but hypothetical, *sub hypothesi logica* and [morali] *practica*.)\nThe hypothesis which is necessary in relation to the laws of pure reason is *originaria*; that which is necessary in relation to experience is *conditionalis sive relativa*. The first is necessary [nothwendig], the second needed [nöthig]; the former *originaria*, the latter *subsidiaria*, e.g. *suppletoria* for the explanation of the order in the world.",[]],"reflm:4581":["Everything possible stands in a thoroughgoing connection. To be possible properly means as much as: to agree [stimmen]. All possibility has data, but its form consists in agreement. Hence thoroughgoing possibility is properly an agreement with everything.",[]],"reflm:4582":["M 330:\nThe need of reason to know a highest being is the need of a necessary hypothesis for the use of reason: 1. of pure reason; 2. of empirical reason, both speculative; 3. of practical reason. (s Hence 1. transcendental theology; 2. natural theology: cosmotheology, physicotheology; 3. moral theology. All of this: 1. to determine the concept of the highest being, 2. to demonstrate its existence. |\nM 330': |\nAll this knowledge is a belief, as always when we go back to first causes. Transcendental theology alone is deistic; natural theology alone is anthropomorphistic; moral theology alone is not sufficiently secured against encroachments. Transcendental theology secures it against them.)",[]],"reflm:4583":["[On the existence of God, toward the end] *conceptus definitor*: that which determines everything through ends; *terminator*: that which begins; *comprehensor*: that which comprehends everything.",[]],"reflm:4584":["The practical ground of belief in God can be sufficient; but speculative grounds are nevertheless needed *in subsidium* [as auxiliary support], in order to secure the former against false sophistry.",[]],"reflm:4585":["1. Pathological origin of theology; 2. speculative, sophistical; 3. moral. There are correspondingly different religions. [The second is deism.] In all these cases God is only a necessary hypothesis for the use of reason, in respect of its own happiness, or of speculation, or of morality.",[]],"reflm:4586":["See p. 336. The contingency of nature and an *ens supramundanum* [supramundane being] make theology necessary.",[]],"reflm:4587":["At the upper margin,\nad p. 330. The idea of a highest being is [a] the necessary presupposition for the completion of our knowledge of actual things through reason, both as [the idea] of a first and necessary [being] and for the sake of all relations: as the being of all beings, the being that comprehends everything in itself, [so that] because only through it can anything determinate be known. We infer from the existence of God to the greatest perfection of the world, and from this to the existence of God. This proceeds as follows. We must assume a supreme understanding and will in order to explain the contingencies of the world, and both together with power and duration in the highest degree and in perfect unity, because without these nothing determinate can be known.",[]],"reflm:4588":["If one wishes to prove the existence of God from empirical grounds, the strange circle arises that we do not indeed venture to infer, from the perfections which we perceive in the world, that this world is the best among all possible worlds, but [infer] first that the | cause of it is the most perfect among all possible, and consequently the effect also.\nIf we wish to infer the [existence] properties of God from concepts, yet in such a way that we assume some existence through experience, then we infer from this existence to something necessary in itself, just as if this did not have to be clear of itself; and since it is not clear of itself through the concept we have of it, we nevertheless infer from this problematic concept to such properties as can belong to only one being, namely all-sufficiency. Now since the necessity of existence can be inferred only from conditions that can belong to one thing alone, the inference must also be reversible, and consequently one must be able to infer the existence of God from mere concepts, without an empirical *principium* [principle].",[]],"reflm:4589":["Above and\nWe have need of the concept of God as a supreme ground of things through freedom: first, to explain the origin of contingent beings, and second, to determine the final ends and hopes of rational beings. The former belongs to the series of speculation, the latter to that of practice. The former serves only for the beginning of nature. The occurrences of nature themselves must not be explained from it.",[]],"reflm:4590":["Above and\nInfinite reality is the *substratum* of all possibility, the universal foundation. If all negations are limitations, then no thing is possible except through another which it presupposes, with the exception of the *ens realissimum* [most real being]. [The infinite time of] The all-embracing time, the all-containing space, the all-sufficient thing.",[]],"reflm:4591":["At the outer margin\nAtheism.\n1. That there is no original being at all: Epicurus.\n2. That it is the world itself, or that the world is a modification of it.\n3. That it is the cause of things according to blind fatality.",[]],"reflm:4592":["Above, at the outer margin beside and\n* In *theologia naturalis* [natural theology] the *ens summum* [highest being] (g determined) is considered as the cause of nature (g of our world); consequently, since we know causality only through forces that are given to us in experience, and these cannot be found *proprie* [in the strict sense] in the highest being (anthropopathos), we shall be able to think of God only their relation to the effects, which the understanding knows, *proprie*, but the manner in which they act, *improprie* [in an improper sense]; and since we must set apart what is here borrowed from the nature of the things of the world and think their absolute negatively, we must think theoprepos [in a manner befitting God]; consequently in *theologia naturalis* we shall know God by analogy from nature in general. *theologia naturalis vel cosmologia vel physico-theologia* [natural theology, either cosmology or physico-theology].\nWe infer from this world to another, because it does not agree with the greatest perfection alone; and yet one wishes to infer from it to the existence of the most perfect being, and afterwards from this being and its perfections to the other world.\n*(g All proof of the existence of the *ens summum* is to be conducted only from pure concepts of reason, consequently *a priori* (Wolff). This proof breaks off.)",[]],"reflm:4593":["At the inner margin\nin natural theology God [is thought] as cause of this world, consequently only his relation [to it], and he himself according to analogy *per eminentiam* [by way of eminence].",[]],"reflm:4594":["At the inner margin\nCosmotheology is built on the proposition: the first cause of a contingent thing is its cause from freedom, not from the necessity of its nature; first mover, teleological.",[]],"reflm:4595":["Natural theology is either cosmotheology or physicotheology.",[]],"reflm:4596":["It is not so easy to prove the existence of God theoretically, but it is easy and natural to believe it.",[]],"reflm:4597":["Above and\nTo cosmotheology belongs the relation of God to the world in respect of causality. 1. [princ] *systema inhaerentiae vel emanationis vel liberae productionis* [system of inherence, or of emanation, or of free production]; the last either *tanqvam architecti vel tanqvam creatoris* [as of an architect or as of a creator]. 2. *praesentia vel commercii vel conservationis* [presence, either of commerce or of conservation]. The first is *anima mundi* [world-soul]. 3. *duratio* not in time, just as *praesentia* not in space and *creatio* not in time. Moral theology: *sapientia* (g *benignitas*) *et sanctitas*, *finis**, *summum bonum*, *Optimismus*. *Summa felicitas cum summa dignitate ipsius.* *Origo mali.* *decreta omnia non absoluta, sed hypothetica.* *Gubernatio* and the idea determining the government: *providentia*.\n*(g *non est impellens, sed complacentiae immanentis.*)",[]],"reflm:4598":["In natural science also the causes are put forward only as necessary |\nhypotheses, but not as absolutely necessary, because another cause of the same phenomenon would still be possible.",[]],"reflm:4599":["Prudence and morality cannot be connected except merely through the hypothesis of a third being, who is powerful and good and at the same time holy and just.",[]],"reflm:4600":["The causality of the whole series of the contingent, and hence of its coming to be and beginning, is not physical but practical, i.e. from freedom.\n(s Freedom is the supreme condition of the most real being as cause, and causality in it is through understanding and will.)\n(s *Primus motor ex libertate. intelligentia.*)",[]],"reflm:4601":["All composition and limitation is contingent; the cause of it is itself contingent and cannot consist in anything other than a choice.",[]],"reflm:4602":["The [empirical] cosmotheological proof has sufficiency as the principle of the empirical use of reason in respect of all possible order in the world, and also of the first beginning, according to the analogy of experiences.\nThereby only an analogue of our freedom is inferred.",[]],"reflm:4603":["Causality (g of things) according to the necessity of nature yields substantial unity of cause and effect, because the removal of the latter would remove the nature of the former, and hence its existence.",[]],"reflm:4604":["Analogy with the human understanding.\n*via reductionis* [the way of reduction]: to set aside all sensibility and its (g limiting conditions) limits.\nBut if we leave out the conditions of sensibility, all knowledge *in concreto* falls away; accordingly we can never think God *proprie* through knowledge borrowed from nature, but only *secundum analogiam* [according to analogy].\nThe idea breaks off. |\nThe two words stand to the right of the end of § 868; below them, written in pencil, the word: |\nPlato.",[]],"reflm:4605":["*Ens originarium non est natura originaria, sed intelligentia originaria* [the original being is not an original nature, but an original intelligence].",[]],"reflm:4606":["*theologia transscendentalis* [transcendental theology];\n*theologia naturalis** [natural theology]: determination through predicates of nature — psychological;\n*(g either from transcendental or from physical principles [*ex principiis transscendentalibus vel physicis*])\n*theologia moralis* [moral theology]: determination and conclusion from moral predicates and arguments.",[]],"reflm:4607":["At the very top of the page, but\n*Acqviescentia in semet ipso* [contentment in oneself] *est principium omnis caussalitatis entis summi et est beatitudo* [is the principle of all causality of the highest being, and is blessedness].",[]],"reflm:4608":["Moral theology: God as *summum bonum* [highest good], *legislator* [lawgiver] and *finis* [end].",[]],"reflm:4609":["The principle of the inner possibility of a [being] thing is the essence. This essence, according to (g the determination of) its causality, is nature. The condition of nature as a first cause of the contingent is freedom.",[]],"reflm:4610":["On the outer margin, beginning at the top above the page number, continuing down to\nEssence [Wesen]: God (g as) *summa possibilitas* (highest possibility).\nNature: God as *summa perfectio* (highest perfection).\nFreedom:\n*summum bonum* (highest good).\n*Perfectiones* (perfections) are faculties [*facultates*] for the highest end;\nthe good [*bonum*] is the intention of the highest end.\nThe *summum bonum* is the union of the highest happiness with worthiness, or with its objective receptivity, which is as great as it is practically determinable in universal happiness, i.e. as far as it is permitted to be happy through practical agreement with the universal principles of happiness.*\n*\n(g both in relation to the ends of human nature (g and) of men.)\nMoral perfection, in so far as it is the determination [Bestimmung] of the divine will or is determining with respect to creatures; i.e. [He is the *principium*] His will is just, and He wills that men be just. Moral theology is the right one, which makes good.\nGod in this theology is of threefold person or *qualitas*: as the (g holy) lawgiver, as the benevolent ruler and as the just judge.",[]],"reflm:4611":["Morality is the agreement of free choice [Willkür] with the end of humanity and of men in general, namely with the necessary conditions of the universal ends of humanity and of men.",[]],"reflm:4612":["Above and \nWe ought to have as much share in universal happiness as we contribute to it. Morality is itself comprehensible only from the idea of such a will.",[]],"reflm:4613":["Fortune and fate, in a word, the advantages [bestowed] by nature, can never have the worth that those have which accord with correct rules.",[]],"reflm:4614":["Opposite the concluding sentence of M § 893:\n*Libertas voluntatis* (freedom of the will) — *Perfectio* (perfection), either natural: omnipotence, wisdom (g [natural] artificial) [holiness], or moral: holiness, benevolence, justice = wisdom.",[]],"reflm:4615":["The holiness, benevolence and justice of the divine will. Sum: wisdom.",[]],"reflm:4616":["At the top of the page.\nThe world considered *intellectualiter* (intellectually) has no beginning; not because it has endured through an infinite time, for in that case it would be considered *sensitive* (sensitively), but because [it] in this respect is not considered in time at all.\nThe *mundus sensibilis* (sensible world) has no beginning, because a first beginning is impossible. He who considers the world *sensitive* recognizes no limits in it.",[]],"reflm:4617":["The world as beginning cannot be thought according to grounds of reason, and as without beginning cannot be thought according to grounds of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:4618":["Above and \nIf we look into the world as world of sense, we shall find no beginning in the *regressus* (regress) of appearances. But these appearances are not actual things; hence what is properly said is not: the world is (g in itself) without beginning, but: the appearance (in relation to us) has no beginning. In appearances there is no absolute limit to [comp] increase or decrease.\nThe world has an origin, but not as appearance; [it has it] as a being in itself.",[]],"reflm:4619":["Above and \nThe causal relation of the intellectual to the sensitive, and the determination [Bestimmung] of sensibility according to merely intellectual principles, or conversely, can by no means be insighted by us; e.g. the first ground of composition, the first action through freedom, the origin and beginning of the world.",[]],"reflm:4620":["Causality with respect to substance: creation and preservation;\nwith respect to (g the course of the world) the ordering: providence and government.",[]],"reflm:4621":["On the upper margin and on the outer side margin\nThe physico-theological concept seeks, for the contingent, the necessary as determinate; the moral concept seeks, for the necessary, the necessary, in order to give it reality [der]. It is not necessary to go back to the first ground in speculation. But for the sake of duties it is necessary to go back to the supreme lawgiver, ruler and judge.\nRational belief [Vernunftglaube], which is necessary, is the unavoidable hypothesis of a moral being.\nThe subjective grounds of the need of our reason, pure as well as applied, increase this obligation.",[]],"reflm:4622":["The good is the agreement with the order of nature and of the world by means of freedom. Hence God, by means of nature, is the author of the good and at the same time the one who hinders evil.",[]],"reflm:4623":["The moral *concursus* (concurrence) takes place through the removal of the hindrances to our inner morality and not through determination [Bestimmung] of our actions.",[]],"reflm:4624":["At the top of the page.\nGod is the author of the order of nature, [and] freely acting beings [stand] in many respects [in relation] to the course of nature.",[]],"reflm:4625":["Whether the Danube runs toward the east so that the Turks should not flood the empire, and the Falun mine was placed in the province of Dalecarlia because this is an industrious people?",[]],"reflm:4626":["The analysis of pure reason gives nothing but distinctness in the representations that we already have.\nSynthetic propositions go to the conditions of judgments through pure reason and are subjective. Hence pure reason cannot teach us to know objects otherwise than in application [to that which] to the senses.",[]],"reflm:4627":["Metaphysics has neither the utility in use nor the brilliance of invention and the strength of rational insight that mathematics has; but its end is a universal human end, and thereby it takes precedence over all theoretical branches of knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4628":["Metaphysics serves science only negatively, in keeping off the errors of pure reason; but it is no small service that it renders to the most sublime and dr [text breaks off]",[]],"reflm:4629":["The fundamental concepts of all our knowledge [Erkenntnis] are [quality (what is there), quantity (how many times)] 1. being in general (g *qvidditaet*), secondly: how something is, thirdly: how many times it is.\nThat whereby things are given is sensation; how they are given, pure intuitions.\nThrough the understanding either things are thought or only their sensible representations. Number requires space and time for its intuitive representation.\nThe logical form is precisely for the [concepts] representations of the understanding of a thing what space and time are for the appearances of it: namely, the former contain the places for ordering them. The representation [be], whereby we assign to an object its own logical place, is the real concept of the understanding, and pure; e.g. something that I can always use only as subject; something from which I must infer hypothetically to a *consequens* (consequent), etc. etc.\nBy our sensations getting a determinate place* in space and time, they acquire a function among appearances; but the place in space and in time is determined by the neighbourhood of other sensations in them; e.g. upon the state of my sensations, which have something in common with the preceding ones, there follows the other; the sensation of a resistance is at the same time connected in the same space with weight.\nThrough the determination of the logical place the representation acquires a function among concepts; e.g. *antecedens* (antecedent), *consequens*. Yet the sensitive function is the ground of the intellectual.\n*(g a determinate place is distinguished from an arbitrary one.)",[]],"reflm:4630":["Propositions can be known *a priori*, but their principles *a posteriori*.",[]],"reflm:4631":["(g Space is a *datum*.)\nThe logical actions are *actus* (acts) whereby we posit and order the *data* in relation to one another as representations of things. Through them the representations acquire logical functions. The real function [is] consists in the way in which we posit a representation in and for itself; it is therefore an action (*a priori*) which corresponds to each *datum* (*a posteriori*) and whereby the latter becomes a concept. These actions are the sources from which the logical are possible [sic]. From them arise all modes of knowledge, namely how we can grasp *data* and form for ourselves something that is entitled knowledge. In nature no *data* can present themselves to us except that, if one perceives laws in it, they correspond to the universal modes according to which we posit something, since otherwise no laws would be noticed, or no object at all, but only confused inner alterations. Since, then, we can represent objects only through our alterations, in so far as these have in themselves something conformable to our [Ge] rules of positing and sublating, the real functions are the ground of the possibility of the representation of things, and the logical functions the ground of the possibility of judgments, and consequently of modes of knowledge. For an object is entitled only that which [sic] [text breaks off]",[]],"reflm:4632":["Universality is a continuous action, which can afterwards be divided.",[]],"reflm:4633":["How can modes of knowledge be generated in us of which the objects have not yet presented themselves to us? Since the objects do not have to conform to our modes of knowledge, but these to the objects, it seems that the objects must at least be given to us beforehand in their rudiments (g according to) |\nbefore they can be thought. Hence the possibility of a (g every) knowledge *a priori*, which subsists of itself [its sources and its Best] constantly, without being drawn from the objects themselves [of the Ge], constitutes our first and most important question. It is a question [which even only its importance even only to understand already a] which, merely to have raised and well understood it, already has some merit in itself, namely in a part of philosophy which owes nothing to experience and the senses. There are indeed in fact [sciences modes of knowledge] even whole sciences of this kind. Pure mathematics, which flows wholly from pure sources *a priori*, without taking anything from experience among its grounds, has in this respect an incomparable progress and a good fortune rightly admired and at times perhaps envied. But there is no lack [also] (g on the other hand) of others which, while they likewise wish to be of pure origin, find themselves unceasingly entangled in contradictions. Hence it is well to investigate the sources of knowledge *a priori* [to] in general, without troubling ourselves about this distinction, and only afterwards to make it comprehensible.",[]],"reflm:4634":["M XXII:\nWe know any object only through predicates which we say or think of it. Before that, what is found in us of representations is to be reckoned only as materials, not as knowledge. Hence an object is only a something in general, which we think to ourselves through certain predicates that constitute its concept. In every judgment, accordingly, there are two predicates which we compare with one another. Of these, the one [through which to us the] which constitutes the given knowledge of the object is entitled the logical subject; the second, which is compared with it, is entitled the logical predicate. If I say: a body is divisible, this signifies as much as: something [which] x, which I know among the predicates that together con|stitute a concept of body,\nI also think through the predicate of divisibility. x  is one and the same with xb. Now a as well as b belongs to x. But |\nM XXIII: |\nin different ways: either b already lies in that which constitutes the concept a, and so can be found by analysis of it; or b belongs to x without being included in a and comprehended with it. In the first case the judgment is analytic, in the second synthetic. The case cited is that of an analytic judgment; but the proposition: Every body is heavy, is a *synthesis*; the one predicate is not involved in the subject but added to it. Now all analytic judgments can be seen *a priori*; and what can be known only *a posteriori* is synthetic. Hence genuinely empirical judgments are synthetic. But there are nevertheless judgments [which a] whose validity seems to stand fast *a priori*, and which yet are synthetic, e.g. Everything alterable has a cause. Whence do we arrive at these judgments? [through which have] Whence do we take it to associate with one concept another of the very same object which no observation and experience shows in it? Yet all genuine axioms are such synthetic propositions, e.g. between two points there can be only one straight line. On the other hand the proposition: [Every magnitude is itself When one Every part is small] Every magnitude is equal to itself, is an analytic proposition. The *principium* or norm of all analytic propositions is the proposition of contradiction and of identity. It (if I take the two together) is no axiom but a formula, that is, a universal model of analytic propositions; for it contains no *medium terminum*.\nWe have accordingly judgments *a posteriori* which are synthetic, but also judgments *a priori* which are nevertheless synthetic and for that reason cannot be derived from any experience, because they contain both a true universality, and hence necessity, and also comprise nothing but concepts which cannot have been drawn from experience. [Whence] Let these concepts inhere in us from wherever they will: whence do we take this connection of them? Are they revelations, prejudices, etc., etc.?\nIf certain concepts in us contain nothing other than that through which all experiences are possible on our side, they can be affirmed *a priori*, before experience and yet with complete validity, of everything that may ever come before us. They then hold, to be sure, not of things in general, but yet of everything that can ever be given to us through experience, because they contain the conditions through which these experiences are possible. Such propositions will therefore contain the condition of the possibility not of things but of experience. |\nM XXIV: |\nBut things which cannot be given to us through any experience are nothing for us; so we can very well use such propositions as universal in a practical regard, only not as principles of speculation about objects in general.\nIn order, then, to determine what these concepts are which must necessarily precede all experience, and through which alone experience is possible, which are therefore given *a priori* and also contain the ground of the judgments *a priori*, we must analyse an experience in general. In every experience there is something through which an object is given to us, and something through which it is thought. If we take the conditions, lying in the activities of the mind, through which alone it can be given, we can know something of the objects *a priori*. If we take that through which alone it can be thought, we can likewise know something *a priori* of all possible objects. For through this alone does something become for us an object, or a knowledge of it.\nWe shall investigate the former first. That through which an object (g of experience) is given to us is entitled appearance. The possibility of appearances, on the side of the human mind, is sensibility. In sensibility there is a matter, which is entitled sensation; and in regard to this and its diversity we are merely passive, and the manifoldness of the impressions brings it about that we find in ourselves *a priori* nothing which we could know *a priori* out of ourselves prior to the impressions. One can never in thought represent to oneself [a new] an impression of a new kind. |\nBut appearances also have a form, a ground lying in our subject, through which we order either the impressions themselves or that which corresponds to them, and give to each part of them its place. This can be nothing other than an activity, which, though naturally aroused by the impressions, can yet be known for itself.\n(When we posit something in space and in time, we act; when we posit it side by side and one after another, we connect. These actions are only means of bringing those positions about; but they can be taken separately. When we posit one and the same thing several times, or posit the one in the same action together with the other, this is a kind of action by which we posit something in accordance with the rule of appearances, wherein this positing must have its own particular rules, which are distinct from the conditions of form, according to how these are to be placed in regard to the appearance.)",[]],"reflm:4635":["Not every object is a thing. That in the representation through which it has its peculiar function represents [the thing itself] the object (real function); its function in judgment is the relative function, through which it has a multiple validity in regard to other representations. The first function concerns what lies in the representation; the second, what can be known through it.",[]],"reflm:4636":["Modes of knowledge *a priori* must relate not at all to determinate things (since these are not yet given), but to universal representations of |\nthings in general, and so to intuitions (g not sensations, for it is these through which something determinate is given) or to thoughts in general. (One can intuit something without thinking anything in it or under it.)\n(g All knowledge comes to us through thinking, that is, through concepts; they are not intuitions.)",[]],"reflm:4637":["That which belongs to our modes of knowledge, and through which the objects are [given] thought (for space is only the manner in which they are given to us), is either the thinking of an object in general: *possibile* [it may be given or not]; or how it can in general be given by ourselves: *qvantitas*; or how it must be given to us through experience.",[]],"reflm:4638":["All knowledge consists in judgments. The judgments may now be immediate or mediate (inferences of reason); [a determinate] thinking is entitled judging. Even concepts are predicates. Accordingly [modes of knowledge] concepts to which no object is given, but which are nevertheless to express the ways of thinking objects in general, will contain in themselves that which is thought in judgments relatively, of two concepts in respect of each other.\nUniversally valid concepts have a logical [function] place [the place] among the concepts. The place in a judgment is a logical function; it is — breaks off.\nThe determinate logical function of a representation in general is the [real] pure concept of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4639":["That the necessity of mere truths of reason involves a kind of existence is to be judged from the little word 'is'. E.g. one cannot say: all triangles will become three-cornered.",[]],"reflm:4640":["Cause and effect seem to be for [fancy] *praevision* (foresight), whole and part for fancy, substance and accident for the senses, possibility for the faculty of fiction, actuality for [intuition] sensation, magnitude for intuition, necessity for the w breaks off.\nIn all these cases the constant connection makes the ground of an abstract concept of the understanding. For those powers are connecting, and from this springs the possibility of judgments a priori and also of these concepts.\nEverything subjective relates finally to sensation, hence every objective to substance.\nBecause all form (g of the subjective) is grounded on this, that one determination is connected together with another, inherence is grounded on intuition, the connection of effect with cause on after-formation (*Nachbildung*), conversely [of cause with effect] on fore-formation (*Vorbildung*), and whole and part on imagination.",[]],"reflm:4641":["The distinct representation of that which one\n[sees] intuits or that which one thinks.\nsynthesis\nanalysis\nOne can still transform a sensible concept, or concept of appearance, into a concept of reason.\nDistinctness is opposed to indistinctness, order to confusion. [Through the understanding] Order depends on giving each partial representation its place; hence it is not merely the consciousness of this partial representation (distinctness), but also the [Zusam] co-ordination or subordination. Distinctness of the understanding depends on analysis (dissection) and acquires order through the logical places.",[]],"reflm:4642":["Everything that makes a law of our representations a priori, [every *actus*] hence the relation of the representations to a universal law (g established a priori), is the object. What is to be thought thereunder presents the thing as it is, i.e. as it holds for all appearances. But for that very reason it must be something determinable a priori, because only thereby can we think; for to think means nothing other than to determine the representations from the universal.\nThe concepts of the understanding express all *actus* of the powers of the mind, in so far as representations are possible according to their universal laws, and indeed [that which] this their possibility a priori.",[]],"reflm:4643":["Transcendental is every pure knowledge a priori, in which therefore no sensation is given.\n1. The transcendental Aesthetic.\n2. The transcendental Logic.\n3. The transcendental Critique.\n4. —\n—\n—\n—\nArchitectonic.\nWhat one knows only through inner experience, of that one cannot say the predicates of space (hence the soul is a spirit).",[]],"reflm:4644":["If one uses, of a thing in general, [what] predicates of space and time a priori, this is a *petitio phaenomenorum*.\nIf one uses, of things which one knows only under the appearance of space, universal [concepts] real concepts of the understanding (of the kind whereby the things themselves are given to the understanding, and whereby their quality, not their magnitude or possibility, is given), one commits a *petitio noumeni*. The former is *synthesis subrepticia*, the latter *analysis subrepticia*.\nQuantity does not represent a thing itself, but substance (*substantia*), cause (*causa*) and composite (*compositum*) well do.",[]],"reflm:4645":["In all modes of knowledge there are to be noted in the object\n1. the matter and the form of it, i.e. the quality.\n2. The difference in how it is given.\n3. The quantity.\nIn a judgment the matter is subject and predicate, but the form of each consists just in this, that the one is entitled subject, etc. etc.\nThe positing is the copula: *est*. The quantity concerns both, either in that the subject is a universal concept in itself, etc. etc.\nHere it is not the logical aspect of the judgment but the real aspect of the concepts that is considered. It is the matter of the concepts.\nTo the matter belong objective real data, e.g. cause, inherence, composition.\nMagnitude concerns only the division of the representation, as it can arise from repetition, but not the real composition and the origin from it out of a preceding simple.",[]],"reflm:4646":["We should have no concept of magnitude at all if we did not repeat one several times; hence time and its intuitive counterpart, space, are here wholly operative.\nMagnitude is for pure intuition in general, without data.\nQuality is for the difference of sensation in general.\nThe categories belonging thereunder relate to sensations of the relations, which are posited through the activity of the soul.\nPosition is for the faculty of fiction, the [Ans] sensation and the breaks off.\n(g Categories of quantity, quality and position.)",[]],"reflm:4647":["It belongs to the maxims of reason that even that which contains the most distinct relation to ends has nevertheless arisen according to the order of nature.\nMy onto-theological proof, which is correct in this, that according to the order of nature, under the same Creator, we become aware of relations to ends.",[]],"reflm:4648":["Across the inner margin:\nMotion is something that happens, hence belongs to actual appearances and not to the mere sensible form; it also presupposes something mov|able,\ni.e. changeable in respect of place, which cannot be known a priori, but presupposes empirical concepts, among them also concepts of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4649":["Across the inner margin:\nThe understanding represents things as they are, but not in *intuition*, rather in *reflexion*, i.e. not as they are given in general, but as they are thought. That which the appearances of the object for the same sense under itself breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4650":["Across the inner margin:\nM XXV:\nAll *axiomata univoca*, whether of sensibility or of reason, [are] can be correct.\nThe *axiomata aeqvivoca*, in which either the subject is intellectual and the predicate sensible, or conversely, are reduced, either by *interpolation* in the first case or by *restriction* in the second.\nM XXVI:\nThe rational axioms or postulates (*axiomata oder postulata rationalia*) can become true only in this way, that one does not merely limit them but restricts them to the conditions of knowledge a priori or of *comprehension*, so that there are properly no *axiomata intellectus puri* (axioms of the pure understanding) at all.",[]],"reflm:4651":["Across the inner margin:\nA learned journal ought properly to announce the progress of a science. Up to now this has not been able to hap|pen\nwith metaphysical works. Now it can be done, and indeed as regards completeness and distinctness as well as precision. Indeed, the completion of the science can also be announced, and that not after any very long time.",[]],"reflm:4652":["Across the inner margin:\nI doubted at first whether motion belongs to the transcendental Aesthetic. Now I see that, since it contains something in space that is moved, hence the change of something in respect of relations, it contains not mere sensibility but an intellectual concept.",[]],"reflm:4653":["Across the inner margin:\nOur understanding gives no materials by which we could know a new world, but forms, in order to arrange the present one accordingly.",[]],"reflm:4654":["In and \nThe *contrapositum* of the principle of contradiction is a subjective law. On thinkability, the supreme *criterium veritatis* (criterion of truth) of Crusius.",[]],"reflm:4655":["In and \nThe *principium identitatis* and *contradictionis* (principles of identity and of contradiction) are both called [principles] of contradiction. Indemonstrable judgments stand immediately under them; material principles in which the predicate is determined are indemonstrable.\nIs the principle of judgments and of their possibility.\nThe possibility of things requires a real ground.",[]],"reflm:4656":["In and \nThe most universal object is object in general,\nbeing and not-being, *aliqvid et nihil* (something and nothing).\n(s Something or nothing according to the concept: possible, impossible; or objectively: positive, negative.)\nThe possibility of the predicates or of things.\nEvery object is either possible or impossible. The former is affirming or denying. The — in the logical sense means: not to posit; in the real sense: to cancel the thing.",[]],"reflm:4657":["Simple concepts can be given only through experience; hence the possibility in their relation cannot be seen through reason. *Possibilitas synthetica*, *analytica* (synthetic, analytic possibility).",[]],"reflm:4658":["A necessary being, which is posited not merely under a con|dition but\nunder every possible condition, must through its existence have a relation to all possibility.\nIf a contingent thing is not, it is\n=0A. If a necessary thing is not, it is =0û, i.e. *nihil negativum*; through the *nihil negativum* everything is always cancelled d breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4659":["The common proof of the [existence of a] necessary existence of a most perfect being is that, if it did not exist, a perfection, namely existence, would be lacking to it; that it therefore already contains existence in its concept. I answer: if it did not exist, it would not be that something is lacking to the most perfect being, but the most perfect being would be wholly lacking. There can be no contradiction when the thing itself is removed and nothing remains. The most perfect possible [being] that does not exist is the most perfect among all that is merely possible.",[]],"reflm:4660":["ρ1? φ1?? M 31.\nThe necessity of a *principii* (the rule) or of a *principiati*. The first is the necessity of assumption in accordance with reason.",[]],"reflm:4661":["Beside and \nOnly the possibility of a necessary being need be demonstrated, and its existence is thereby also proved. For a necessary being |\nis (necessarily) there; that is its definition. For this one does not need reality. The latter is to serve only to represent necessity as possible. But that all reality should be is possible; that none should be is also possible.",[]],"reflm:4662":["Under and \nSubstances among appearances are only empirically necessary, i.e. they are connected with the whole of appearances in respect of time; but they are not absolutely necessary, because they can be removed together with all other appearances. Empty space and time.",[]],"reflm:4663":["It is possible that a thing which is at one time should not be at another time: this expresses changeableness.\n(s In the same time signifies contingency.)\nWhat is contingent is not on that account changeable; and what is changeable is not yet contingent.",[]],"reflm:4664":["It is (g a time) possible that something [at one time is] be. It is possible in just the same circumstances of time that it should not be; i.e. it is contingent under just the same hypotheses of time.",[]],"reflm:4665":["That in which there is a multitude determinate in itself is a *quantum discretum* (discrete quantum).\nThe representation of a determinate multitude is a *quantitas discreta*.",[]],"reflm:4666":["*De lege continui logica.* Living and dead force (g if they have different measure) cannot be distinguished by motion and rest, since rest is an infinitely small motion, or also an infinitely small velocity. If virtue and vice were distinguished only in degree, a certain degree, as the limit of both, would be virtue as well as vice. The limit of a magnitude can indeed be regarded as something and also as nothing — (significant); but the ground for positing something, however small, can never be regarded as a ground for removing it, since these differ in quality |\nor are *disparata*. If gravity were equal at all distances, the body would be heavy even in the centre.\nIs the limit of the matter of a body [also] determined by a leap, or does its density decrease imperceptibly up to a certain distance, where it vanishes? The continuous in change and in limits, beginning and end. Everything in a motion is, as it were, related to duration. All *quanta discreta* are numbers. Are magnitudes in nature like numbers? And are numbers something from which natural magnitudes are possible? Or, conversely, are numbers only divisions of magnitudes already given?\n*Lex continui aesthetica.*\n(s The *lex continui* means: two successive states always have something in common, namely their limit.\nGravity cannot be equal at all distances from the centre.)",[]],"reflm:4667":["All action and reaction of substances distinct from one another is possible only insofar as the many constitute a unity, without which the state of the one would not concern that of the other; and [the] two opposed determinations conflict with each other only insofar as they are found in one [subject]; if therefore many substances were not combined into a unity, the state of the one would never conflict with the state of the other.",[]],"reflm:4668":["If our representations are not effects of the objects, they prove their existence in no other way than by revelation.",[]],"reflm:4669":["On brief expressions which contain much sense. The white bear. We learn in youth to talk of him. The philosopher talks of the white bear when he talks of the state after death. Clergymen have written great books for and against concerning the white bear. And the frontispiece of such writings should always be the white bear. Further, the saying: let each one ride his hobby-horse along the streets of the town, etc. etc. That is understanding in a vehicle.\n(g We can let the theologians ride their hobby-horses of philology and criticism to their hearts' content; but it is intolerable that they should compel the commonwealth to mount up behind.)\n(g It is too bad to work oneself up over the white bear into the savagery of a bear.)\nM305:\n(g When two people quarrel over the white bear, I look on calmly and do not mix in.)",[]],"reflm:4670":["From the analogy of nature we prove the future life most beautifully. The ground of proof, however, consists in this, that we develop from ourselves faculties which exceed our present vocation [*Bestimmung*]. This is shown chiefly in the sciences; from this, then, it can already be seen that [our] the sciences do not harmonise with our present vocation and are premature developments, which nevertheless, in that they betray a talent that ought to have remained hidden, have at least the use of discovering our future vocation. Meanwhile the sciences, though they give this prospect, give no incentive. They belong to the play of the human race, but not to its ends.",[]],"reflm:4671":["Remark by Kant on the last page of the letter of his brother J. H. Kant of 3 July 1773 (2 X 140f.) in vol. II of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant, p. 492:\nAll morality consists in the derivation of actions from the idea of the subject, not from sensation. The idea is universally valid [and contains] both from the end (g *abstrahendo*) and from the relation to all (g *combinando*).\nThe sources of all knowledge from experience [lie in] are transcendental. They are (g inner) anticipations. [The]",[]],"reflm:4672":["Remark by Kant on the back (address side) of the letter from E. T. von Kortum of 18 Nov. 1773 (2 X 142f.) in the third Dorpat volume of letters, leaf 27ψ.\nTo the left of the address, crosswise:\nFirst there must be certain titles of thought, under which appearances in themselves are brought: e.g. whether they are regarded as magnitude, or as subject, or as ground, or as whole, or merely as reality (figure is no reality). On that account I shall not, in the appearance, regard as subject whatever I will, or as I will, either as subject or predicate; rather it is determined as subject |\nrespectively as ground. What logical function, then, is properly valid of one appearance in relation to the others, whether that of magnitude or of the subject, and so which function of judgments. For otherwise we can use logical functions at pleasure, without determining, or even perceiving, that the object is more suited to one than to another. Thus one can think an appearance without bringing it under a title of thought in general, and consequently without determining an object for it.\nTo the right of the address, crosswise:\nIn order that appearances may be represented as proper to certain rules, or as determined in accordance with them, it is required that they be represented as belonging under one or other function of the latter. Thereby they become, in a determinate manner, objects of thoughts; otherwise there is nothing in their relations (for sensations [lass] are not thoughts) that would make them thinkable for the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4673":["Lossow's of 28 April 1774 (2 X 167), in vol. III of the Dorpat collection of letters, leaves 6, 6υ, 7, 7υ.\nP. I. Above the salutation:\n[single (einig). That is, all representations make up in my inner state through the unity of intuition] For as intuition of a single subject [all ob- attain] all objects belong only to one breaks off?\nBetween the salutation and the letter, and between its first four lines:\nTogether with the sense, [unt-] under which they are presented to our mind breaks off?\n1. Time is single. [For there is a sub-] Which means as much as: I can intuit all objects only in myself and in the representations that are found in [it this] my |\nsingle subject [know, so far as only according to the form of inner intuition know immediately.] (g to intuit, and all possible objects of my intuition stand in relation to one another according to the particular form of this intuition.)\n[2. It is infinite]\n2. It is infinite, without first or last. For it is the [datum] condition of coordination through inner sense, which can be determined and limited only through the representations that are [ge-] posited according to it. The [mere form of things recep-] receptivity for the ordering together cannot limit itself.\n3. It is necessary, that is, it depends on no thing but lies at the ground of all; it is therefore [the first datum of possibility] the condition of inner intuitions, and lies at the ground of the possibility of all intuitions.\n4. In it all things and all states of things have their determinate place. For [only through the Ve-] they have (g must have), through the unity of inner sense, their determinate relation to all [possible th-] other putative objects of intuition.\n5. It precedes all actual things and can [a priori] itself therefore also [the Be-] be known a priori as condition of objects.\nBetween X2 16722 and 16725:\n(g Inner determinations are not in space.)\nWith these concepts we cannot get out of the world.\n1. If, then, a subject is a thing in general and the predicate is space and time, or a concept built upon them as condition, the |\njudgment is transcendent. (g Everything is somewhere and at some time.) (g I do not say that it is false; it merely does not conclude, *non liquet* (it is not clear). What is false, namely, is that anything universal and objective should follow from this subjective.)\n2. If the subject is given only through (g predicates of) inner sensation and the predicate contains a condition of outer sensibility, then it too is transcendent.\nP. II:\nRemark.\nSpace is nothing but the intuition of the mere form, even without given matter, and so pure intuition. It is a single representation, because of the unity of the subject (g and of the capacity) in which all representations of outer objects can be placed (g next to) one another. It is infinite, because in the capacity to receive there are no limits. It is necessary, because it is the first condition of the possibility of outer representations; consequently it is the ground of the capacity for outer representations, and we cannot represent the opposite to ourselves, since otherwise we should have to have a still [other] higher capacity. It is something actual, which does not depend on the existence of things; [but] for the capacity to intuit does not depend on the existence of things, and can therefore be known a priori.\nSpace is not an object of intuitions (an object or a determination of one), but intuition itself, which precedes all objects and [through which it] in which (g when) they are placed |\n, the appearance of them is possible. It is a pure intuition a priori. But how is such an intuition possible? [It is] It is nothing other than the consciousness of one's own receptivity to receive representations (impressions) of things in certain relations to one another.\nThe *spatium absolutum* (absolute space), this riddle of the philosophers, is something quite correct (g but not real, rather ideal); otherwise one could not say anything of it a priori, and indeed not through general concepts, but through properties which can be perceived in it through [inner War-] immediate apprehension. It is, however, nothing external, but the condition, subsisting in the mind itself, of the form of all outer representation. It is [not] nothing imagined (*ens imaginarium*, an imaginary being). For it is the sole actual condition of the representation of actual outer things. The order of things which are next to one another is not space; rather space is that which makes such an order, or better coordination, possible according to determinate conditions. If it is a mere general concept of order, then let one try how much can be derived from it, and how one is to arrive a priori at the necessity of such an order; for to borrow it a posteriori is, first, contrary to what is manifest, and secondly it would then have only the consequences of an observation, not those of a [Grundbe-] fundamental representation.\nSpace, as an outer representation, would also have to have something in the object through which it would be produced in the mind. For it would then be no representation a priori. But where there is as yet nothing at all, there is also no influence, and the mere form cannot be imparted to us through influence.\nThat there are things which correspond to sensibility must in general be known by the understanding; thus the ideality of space is nothing further than the distinction of sensibility, and of that which is posited through it, from the understanding and what is thought through it. Through ideality the actuality of bodies |\n(g of certain beings which correspond to it) and of certain properties is not denied; indeed it is thought in them in no other way than merely negatively.\nThe ideality of space does not abolish its reality in respect of bodies, that is, of all [sensible] outer objects of sensibility, and space does actually belong to them; it only distinguishes objects of the senses as such from things in themselves. To a thing in itself no space belongs (g as condition or determination), but every object of the outer senses is thought through the condition of space.\nP. III. Beneath X2 16733-35:\nThe propositions about the properties of absolute space and time seemed strange; one therefore either denied absolute time and made it an *abstractum* (something abstracted) or an *empiricum* (something empirical), or one [vorichtete] made time objective and made it a most real ideal, that is, a chimera.\nTo the right of X2 16734f., above and below lines 12-16:\nWe have no intuitions other than through the senses; hence no other concepts can inhere in the understanding than those which concern the disposition and the order among these intuitions. These concepts must contain the universal, and rules. The faculty of rules in abstracto: the learned understanding; in concreto: the sound understanding. The sound understanding has [therefore vielfelt] the advantage in all cases where the rule must be abstracted a posteriori from the cases; but where the rule has its origin altogether a priori, it finds no place at all.\nP. IV:\nSpace is the condition inhering in our sensibility with respect to all outer appearance, for it is the form of this capacity |\nof the mind to perceive things as outer. Hence bodies are representations which [necessarily on this con-] are possible only under this condition [and are themselves nothing], and so far space is certainly something real. [The ideality] Through a body, however, there is thought not an object of knowledge in general, but a thing as an object of outer sense. Space as predicate therefore refers not at all to a thing in itself, but only to it as the object of outer sense. It is the condition not of things but of the phenomenon [of] of [a] the things, and indeed [wer in respect of that through outer sense] of outer sense. Hereby sensibility is distinguished only from the understanding, through which something is represented not as it is given to us as an object of the senses, but is thought independently of that.\nVarious predicates of space, which were formerly regarded as objective, can now be explained by this concept in respect of their origin. 1. Space is single, because it is the form of the representations (g of all possible outer objects) in a single subject. 2. Space is infinite. For the capacity [of the thereto] to admit several impressions of outer things, or the receptivity, has in itself no limits. 3. Space is necessary; for it is that on which the possibility of the senses itself is grounded.\nThe representation of space is no fiction (Einbildung), nor one that relates merely to the subject (g all-embracing, Albefassend), but a [means] condition for representing outer things, and a means of ordering them. The order is in accordance with the inner form.\nThe omnipresence of space and the eternity of time. That it is present everywhere means that it is itself the condition of all presence, for through it presence is known.\nThe former means: that we can intuit no thing as present except somewhere in space.\nReason is the faculty of rules a priori.\nA rule a priori is a rule of reason [and this]. A rule from concepts in abstracto is a law. Reason is the faculty of laws. A rule of the construction of concepts is entitled a formula. A symbolic formula —\n[Reason considers the uncondi-] Concepts of reason are concepts valid unconditionally, hence the All, the first, the [un-] transcendent. Unconditioned necessity, the unconditioned *principium* (principle) (g the independent *principium*), the unconditioned (g unrestricted) all.\n––––––––––\nSpace and time contain the conditions of the rules of appearance; hence all categories, in respect of their application, lay them at the ground.\n––––––––––\nThe question whether space is something ideal (not imaginary) or real does not interest various sciences at all. In mathematics, mechanics and general physics no heed is paid to it; although Leibnitz as well as Newton (I name them here at the head of the other great names), the latter assuming its subsisting reality, the former its adhering reality, yet both, in the application to objects of the world, treat it as if both, space and time, [receptacles] were self-subsisting receptacles of things; and even though we have proved the ideality, it can make no difference in respect of such investigations. But where these answers become transcendent, there it sounds otherwise.\nDifferent things are in different places and conversely: the difference of places proves the difference of things (this is already a proposition that concerns the empirical understanding).\nEven if we cannot comprehend many propositions from the concepts of space and time, we must observe that they are not representations of reason but of intuition, and [that we the] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4674":["P. I:\nThe principles of appearance (g in general) are solely of the form, namely time.\nThe principle of the exposition of appearances is the ground of exposition in general of that which has been given. The exposition of that which is thought rests solely on consciousness. The exposition of that which is given, if one regards the matter as undetermined, rests on the ground of all relation and of the connection of representations (sensations). The connection is grounded (just as appearance rests not on mere [influence] sensation, but on inner principles of the form) not on mere appearance. It is a representation of the inner action of the mind, that of combining representations, not merely placing them beside one another in intuition, but making a whole as regards matter. Here, then, there is unity, not [of] by virtue of that in which, but that through which the manifold is brought into one, and hence universal validity. Hence what the relations of appearances rest upon is not forms but functions. The exposition of appearances is therefore the determination of the ground on which the connection of the sensations [derse] in them rests.\n––––––––––\nBy a general concept of a sensible datum, in which the reality and at the same time its relation to the sensible condition in general is indicated, we understand the action of determining an object sensibly according to such conditions. For example, \"what happens\" signifies the action of determining something in time as regards succession. Now x is this determinable, which contains the conditions of determination; a, however, signifies only the action of determining in general. It is therefore no wonder if in x, besides the action of deter|mining, there is still more contained, which is expressed by b (g in the concept a there is still more contained, which belongs to determining it in the mind, i.e. to knowing the way in which this is generated or specified in the mind, or what flows from its specification or is connected with it as condition). For example, in space, besides the general action of constructing a triangle, there is also the magnitude of the angles; and in inner sense, besides the designation of that [wa] in general which happens, there are also the conditions under which alone this happening (g as apprehension) can be determined in the mind. These conditions are found in x, in the case of intuition, through the construction of a, e.g. a triangle; but in a real concept they are found through [the subject] the concretum of the subject, in which the representation a is posited. Thus the relation which is thought through a is to be determined solely through the real condition of the subject, which consists in the (g function of) relative positing in general, and with regard to the datum a in particular. Since the subjective condition (g x) is to suffice for all these positions, the determination of a, i.e. b, must be a general action whereby the appearance of a is exposed, i.e. breaks off.\n(g Only [with regard to] relation do objectively synthetic propositions of appearance hold.)\nIn synthetic propositions what is properly represented is not the relation among the concepts immediately [but] (for that happens only in analytic propositions), but the relation in the [subject given] conditions of their concrete representation in the subject, be it intuition or appearance. This subject contains the conditions of the representation of everything of which we have concepts, and it is in its sensibility [the objects must] that the objective of these concepts must be determined. x always signifies the object of the concept a. But there can be no object except either of pure or of empirical intuiting. As to the latter |, the concept a can refer either to a given object of the senses x, or to conditions of sensibility under which an object [of the concept of], so far as it merely corresponds to the concept x, must be given, and under which alone it can be known as conformable to a.\nP. II:\nThis is a proof that space is a subjective condition. For since the propositions concerning it are synthetic and (g thereby objects) can be known a priori, this would be impossible if space were not a subjective condition of the representation of these objects.\nOn the other hand, the synthetic judgments of experience are known a posteriori, because they are directed immediately to given objects.\nBut if something is to be known a priori of things, not merely according to the form of appearance but in respect of their remaining constitution, etc. etc.\n––––––––––\n(g The x drops out. For it is to signify the object which is thought through a; but since b is merely compared with the concept a and is thereby already determined, the remainder in x is a matter of indifference. If a is considered adjectively, the proposition is not always universal.)\nb must be a determination of a, and not an analytic predicate. Analytic predicates are identical and tautological. Of analytic hypothetical judgments. Disjunction, dichotomy. (g The categorical are the foundation.) The concept of substance and accident yields in itself a synthesis, likewise cause and effect, and multitude in a real unity. [It follows] that nature, according to the various relations to inner sense, must throughout stand under one of these syntheses.\nx is therefore the determinable (object) which I think through the concept a, and b is its determination (g or the way of determining it). In mathematics x is the construction of a; in experience it is the concretum; in regard to an inherent representation or thought |in general, x is the function of thinking in general in the subject. There the (g real) concept a [determined] together with everything is determined: 1. through the subject; 2. in regard to succession, through the ground; 3. in regard to coexistence, through composition.\n(g [The condition whereby a obtains an object x is enunciated in b, but] x is the object. This can be given in construction a priori. In exposition, however (which is something quite different from observation, which has nothing connected a priori [with] with a), the conditions can be known a priori in the subject under which a /in general is related to an object, namely a real one. This object can be represented only according to its relations, and is nothing other than the subjective representation (of the subject) itself, but made universal; for I am the original of all objects. It is therefore the conjugation, as function, which makes the exponent of a rule.)\n––––––––––\nReality must be given (g in sensation). Magnitude we can construct (g according to intuition). The [real] real synthesis is not merely given to us in sensation, nor can it be constructed, yet it lies in appearance neither as intuition nor as sensation. For experience does give us substance, effect and cause, and whole, to know (although we cannot think the last a priori, namely how, of many things, each in turn determines all the others and is determined by them, and we can only take many together in thought). These three kinds of concepts relate to objects as appearances (possibility etc. etc. only as concepts a priori). In the case of magnitude I need no sensation, but only time; in the case of real synthesis I need sensation in general as well as time. (Threefold dimension of synthesis. [We can therefore] How then can we represent to ourselves [the] positions of the postulates of synthesis a priori? They are the three functions of apperception, which are met with in [all] the thinking of our state in general |and under which all appearance must fall for this reason, that in it no synthesis lies in itself unless the mind adds it, or makes it from the data of the appearance. The mind is therefore for itself the archetype of [the possibility of] such a synthesis, through original and not derived thinking.\n––––––––––\nConcepts give only the outline of objects, namely [that whereby one] that which is a sign of their representation. b is always compared with the object x by means of a, but x is not always considered merely in the concept a. In the latter case b refers either to the way in which the object a is given a priori (g objectively) in intuition, or a posteriori in experience, or a priori but [subjectively] in the subjective perception of apperception. This last refers only to cases of perception, and indeed to the synthesis in it, i.e. to the relation. Apperception is the perception of oneself as a thinking subject in general.\nApperception is the consciousness of thinking, i.e. of representations as they are posited in the mind. Here there are three exponents: 1. of the relation to the subject; 2. of the relation of sequence among one another; 3. of taking together. The determination of a in these moments of apperception is the subsumption under one of these acts of thinking. One (g knows it as determinable in itself and therefore objectively, namely) the concept a, when one brings it under one of these general actions of thinking, by means of which it comes under a rule. Such a proposition is a *principium* (principle) of the rule, and hence of the knowledge of appearance through the understanding. Through it the appearance is regarded as something objective, which [not] is thought in itself, independently of the singularity in which it was given.",[]],"reflm:4675":["Kant's remarks on Bertram's letter of 20 May 1775, folded into two octavo leaves (2 X 182). R I 21-26.\np. I (address page):\nThe same being can exist successively with opposed predicates. [The predicates so] Something is posited outside us only in so far as the representation of it constitutes permanence and a particular point of reference.\nIf my representation follows upon something, the object of it would not yet follow thereupon unless its representation were determined as a consequence by something; and this can never happen otherwise than according to a universal law. Or: it must be a universal law that all sequence is determined by something preceding; otherwise I should posit no sequence of objects for the sequence of representations. For to posit objects for my representations always requires that the representation be determined according to a universal law, since the object consists precisely in the universally valid point.\nIn the same way I should not represent anything as outside me [if] and so make appearance into experience (objective), if the representations did not relate to something parallel to my I, whereby I refer them from myself to another subject. So too if manifold representations did not determine one another according to a universal law. The three relations in the mind therefore require three analogies of appearance, in order to transform the [functions of the mind] subjective functions of the mind into objective ones and [its] them thereby into concepts of the understanding, which give reality to appearances.\nEverything that is simultaneous in reciprocal relations belongs to one whole: *contra vacuum* [*separans*] *interrumpens* (*vacuum* [*ter*] *terminans*); from this follows the continuity of the breaks off.\nAll this is grounded on conditions of experience; consequently it is not necessary, nor is it known as such, |\nbut they are *analoga* of axioms, which hold *a priori*, but [only] only as anticipations of the laws of experience in general.\nEverything that happens is connected *a priori*; everything that is simultaneous is connected *comitative*; everything that exists is connected *inhaesive*.\nAxioms have a primitive certainty, analogies a derivative, petitions an adopted certainty. [De] The derivative certainty [arises] from the nature of our thinking (g in general) [als] not as appearances but as actions of the subject, which, in so far as there is to be an object, must think in a substance, be determined through a ground, and be bound up with the whole of the power of representation. It is therefore derived from the subjective real condition of thinking in general. Everything that belongs to an aggregate (g objective) stands in reciprocal determination with one another, for otherwise it is only a subjective (g ideal) whole.\np. II (containing signature and date):\n(g Intuition\nThinking.\n*a priori*\nSensibility\nUnderstanding\nReason\nThe understanding therefore connects both extremes by combining the data *a posteriori* with conditions *a priori*, but yet only *in concreto*, consequently [in ei] to an empirical knowledge.)\nThe ideal or real subject\n—\n—\n—\n—\nSeries\n—\n—\n—\n—\nAggregate.\nThe former are only [*actus*] actions of the mind; the second is something [unte] in the objects in themselves in relation to thinking, without distinction of the subject.\nBoth are distinguished by the necessity of the relations, which flows from universality: the subject, which always lies at the ground; the member of the series, from which something determinate always follows; the manifold, of which each is determined by the rest and in turn determines them.\nThe aggregate, considered objectively, must have a common ground of unity, whereby the manifold depends on one another. (g The consequence is: much that agrees together has a common ground.)\n[See image below: successio aggregatio sustentatio]\nContinuity in space and time.\nOn the intellectuation of apprehension.\na and b can stand in relation by means of x in a threefold way:\nx\n/ \\\neither a:b or a:x:b or a+b=x.\nThe inner necessity of the appearance, when it is detached from everything subjective and regarded as determinable [under] through a universal rule (of appearances), is the objective. The objective is the ground of the agreement of appearances with one another.\n––––––––––\nIn all three unities necessity rules. Every aggregate is contingent; hence there must be something whereby its *respectus* become necessary. Everything that happens is contingent; hence its origin must be necessary. Everything that breaks off.\n––––––––––\nThe objective is the ground of the agreement of appearances. Hence a threefold agreement: 1. in a common subject, 2. in a [one] common beginning, 3. in a common whole.\np. III (letter page). Across the letter:\nThe difference of all our modes of knowledge [der] is according to matter (content, object) or according to form. As to the latter, it is intuition or concept. The former is of the object in so far as it is given, |\nthe latter in so far as it is thought. The faculty of intuition is sensibility, that of thinking is understanding (of thinking *a priori*, without the object's being given, reason). The understanding is therefore opposed to sensibility and to reason. The perfection of knowledge according to intuition is aesthetic, according to concepts logical. Intuition is either of the object (*apprehensio*) or of ourselves; the latter (*apperceptio*) extends to all modes of knowledge, including those of the understanding and of reason.\nTranscendental logic deals with modes of knowledge of the understanding as to content, but undetermined in regard to the manner in which objects are given.\nAbove the letter, parallel to it:\nThe condition of all apperception is the unity of the thinking subject. From this flows the connection (g of the manifold) according to a rule and in a whole, because the unity of the function must suffice [so wohl] for subordination as well as for coordination.\np. IV:\nKey. Cupboard.\nInkwell. Pen and knife. Paper. Writings. Books.\nSlippers. Boots. Fur coat. Cap. Night-trousers.\nNapkins. Tablecloth. Hand towel. Plates. Dish. Knife and fork. Salt cellar.\nBottle. Wine and beer glasses. Bottle of wine.\nTobacco. Pipes. Tea things. Tea. Sugar.\nBrush.\nOf concepts which yet cannot be determined *a priori*, that is, constructed.\n(g If x, which is the objective condition of a, is at the same time the subjective condition of b, there arises a synthetic proposition, which is true only *restrictive*. E.g. All existence belongs to a substance; everything that happens [in a series] under a member of the series; everything that is simultaneous in one whole ([dav] whose parts determine one another reciprocally). x, time, in which (g in so far as therein) what happens is determined, is the subjective condition for thinking it [ve] in the concept of the understanding only as [to think substance] a consequence from a ground. The subjective condition signifies the condition of the specification of a concept of the understanding corresponding to this relation. Principles of this kind are not axioms. There are no actual anticipations of appearance. One finds them confirmed by experiences, because [the] laws of experience become possible through them. Other appearances give no laws. They have no evidence, because it is not appearances but experiences that become possible through them. Synthesis of thinking and of appearance.)\nThe subjective conditions of appearance which can be known *a priori* are space and time: intuitions.\nThe subjective condition of empirical knowledge is apprehension in time in general, and therefore according to conditions of inner sense in general.\nThe subjective condition of rational knowledge (g is) construction [in time] through the condition of apprehension in general.\n––––––––––\n[Rules of Critique]\n[Laws of intuition. Analogies of nature]\nThe universal relation of sensibility to understanding and to reason is either that whereby they are given *a priori* [or the], and so the (g sensible) condition of intuition; secondly, the sensible condition of judgment in general upon what is given; finally, the sensible condition of the concept *a priori*. The rules (g *a priori*) which enunciate these conditions contain in general the relation of the subjective to the objective: either of the subjective whereby the objective is given, or of that whereby it is thought as given in general (as object), or is determined *a priori*.\nEverything that is given is thought under the universal conditions of apprehension. Thus the subjectively universal of apprehension is the condition of the objectively universal of intellection. Everything is thought *a priori* under the subjective condition of construction, although the latter is only problematic, that is, the condition is not given, but is nevertheless necessary for construction. To determine *a priori* is to construct.",[]],"reflm:4676":["P. I-II:\n(g In all reality there is the relation of substance to accident, in the case of what happens: of ground to consequent, etc. etc.)\nThe principle of identity and contradiction contains the comparison of two predicates a and b with x, but only [through] so that [x by means] the |\nconcept a of x is compared with b (substantively), and thus the x becomes useless. It is a principle of form, not of content, hence merely logical. It is a principle of analysis, from which nothing is known objectively. [It can In cat] It can be known in categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive form. If I refer both predicates to the x and thereby to one another, it is synthetic: no x which is learned is without science, for [it signifies] here it requires the limitation of time, namely: at the same time. The (g lack of) science does indeed contradict learnedness, but not the man x who is learned, except in so far as he is learned. Thus the contradiction is directed either to the concept a which I have of x, or to x, to which this concept does not necessarily attach. The synthetic validity of b and non b in respect of x, which can be thought through the concept a or non a, is entitled alternation [*wechsel*].\nBut if a cannot be separated from b in x, e.g. no x which is a body is indivisible, then it is to be seen that the x which is thought through a can never be thought through non a, that no [body] being which has the nature of a body can ever become incorporeal, and that a in itself is no predicate in respect of x but is a reciprocal concept [*Wechselbegriff*] with it, and so is valid substantively.\nDifference between the affirmative and the negative theses, and between the principles of affirmation and of negation, which are alike in content.\n––––––––––\nBut if a and b are not identical, whether they be used affirmatively or negatively, and x is not thought completely (g determined) through the concept of a, then a and b stand not in a logical but in a real relation (g something different) of combination, and consequently not of [identit] involution. Their relation, therefore, is determined not through their concepts in themselves, but by means of the x, of which a contains the designation. How are such syntheses possible? x must be a datum of sensibility, [which then through] in which a synthesis, i.e. a relation of coordination, takes place; for [a discursive] this |\ncontains more than is thought through its concept a, and is the representation of a *in concreto*. Now there are three cases in which a transcendental subject is sensible and furnishes a relation of concepts: either it is the intuition of a, or the appearance of a, or the appearance of a (g or empirical knowledge). In the first case [is] the relation of a:b follows from the construction of a=x. In the second [and third] from [the V Erf] [example of] [empirical object of a in d] the sensible condition of the intellection of a; in the third it is drawn from observation. The first two syntheses are *a priori* (all three are objective). For in the second case a [an experience-] signifies a universal sensible condition of perception, while x signifies the condition of the subject in general, in which the relation of all perceptions is determinable (for perceptions are not merely objective, but presuppose sensation, which has only subjective validity). Thus [das Ve] a will signify the universal of perception, and x the (g sensible) condition of the subject [in which] (*substratum*), in which this perception is to receive its place; consequently the condition of disposition. Finally b [will signify] the universal function of the mind for determining the place of a in x, and so the exponent of the relation of perceptions [in the mind to one another], and hence for determining their place according to a rule.\nP. III:\nWhen something is apprehended, it is taken up into the function of apperception. I am, I think, thoughts are in me. These are all of them relations which, though they give no rules of appearance, bring it about that all appearance is represented as contained under rules. The I makes the substratum into a rule in general, and apprehension refers every appearance to it.\nThree items are required for the origin of a rule: 1. x, as the datum for a rule (object of sensibility, or rather sensible real representation). 2. a, the *aptitudo* for the rule, or the condition through which it is in general referred to a rule. 3. b, the exponent of the rule.\nNow if a norm is to arise as a rule of appearances in general, or of experiences, e.g. Everything existing is in substance, then x is sensation in general, as the specif[ic mark] of reality. Through its being represented as reality it becomes the matter of a rule, or [it] becomes capable of sensation of a rule [*Empfindung einer Regel fähig*], and a is only a function of the apprehension of appearance as given in general. Since now everything must be given in time, which therefore contains everything in itself, b is [a function] an act of apperception; namely, [the] the consciousness of the subject which apperceives, as of that which is given in the whole of time, is necessarily bound up with it, for otherwise the sensation would not be represented as belonging to me.\n––––––––––\nTranscendental Thetic: of the establishment of the principles of pure reason. Antithetic: of the (g natural) use of these principles [in general]. For general logic also treats of the natural |\nuse [of them] in common understanding. The fundamental rules of the latter are abstracted from common understanding, though not borrowed and derived from it. The general rules or principles of thinking in general, however, without determinate objects, or without determination of knowledge from the relation to the objects, are at all times dialectical.\nP. IV:\nWe think everything through predicates, and so there is always a relation to x. In judgments, however, there is a relation of a:b, both of which refer to x: a and b in x, x by means of a:b, and finally a+b=x.\n––––––––––\nThe (g absolute) predicate in general is reality, and of what [it is predicated].\nDeterminate predicates (g relation-predicates), which are real, concern only relations. There are three of these, corresponding to the three relations in judgments.\nThe relation-predicates are transcendental; the relation of the predicates is logical.\nWhat does the relation-predicate express in the mind before action, and on what is its reference grounded, on the one side to sensibility, on the other to the logical, so that through the first it receives reality, through the second the form of thinking?\nIs the x the form of (g inner) sensibility, or the real of apprehension?",[]],"reflm:4677":["P. I:\nOnly by this, that the relation which is posited according to the conditions of intuition is assumed to be determinable according to a rule, [is from the] does the appearance relate to an object; otherwise it is merely an inner *affection* of the mind.\n––––––––––\nEverything that is thought as an object of intuition stands under a rule of *construction*.\nEverything that is thought as an object of perception stands under a rule of *apperception*, of self-perception.\n(g Experience in general. Either intuition or sensation.)\nThe appearance is made objective by being brought under a title of self-perception, as contained in it. And so the original relations of *apprehension* are the conditions [both] of the perception of the (g real) relations in the appearance; and just by saying that an appearance belongs under them, it is determined from the universal and is represented objectively, that is, thought. By representing it not as belonging [to] under the functions of self-sensation, but [as] by means of a single perception, it is entitled mere sensation. We can determine these [determine] a priori from the functions of perception, just as much with regard to the objective, that is, to the conditions [of the] which are independent of the particular relations of the senses, as with regard to the relations of space and time. The mind must have a capacity to apprehend, and the functions of this capacity are as necessary to perception as is the receptivity for appearances.\nIf we intuited intellectually, no titles of *apprehension* would be needed in order to represent an object to ourselves. The object would then not appear at all. As things are, [is has] the appearance must be subordinated [to a ground] to a function, through which the mind disposes of it, and indeed to a universal condition of it, since otherwise nothing universally valid could be found in it.\n––––––––––\nAll synthetic propositions have a condition of sensibility (an opening one), either for the intuition [or and Per] |\n*construction*  *exposition*\n*specification*\n(pure or empirical), or of thinking through the understanding, or of insight through reason.\nThe x therefore always contains the condition.\nEither an objective condition of the appearance or a subjective condition of the pure intuitions, both in judgments where the predicate is sensible;\nor an objective condition of the understanding with regard to [perception] *intellection*, or a subjective condition of reason with regard to *conception*, both in the case of intellectual predicates.\n(g In an analytic proposition, therefore, the subject is always taken substantively. The concept of being learned is contradictory to the concept of being unlearned.)\nP. II:\nAll appearances relate to the concept of an object which is valid for all of them together, e.g. appearances of a quadrilateral; they therefore stand under rules of judging, by which this concept can be determined (optical illusion). Perceptions are not appearances alone, that is, representations [of that] of the appearances, but [representations] of their existence; e.g. that reality is there, that it is successive, that it is (g together with) other reality simultaneous. Perception is the position in inner sense in general, and refers to sensation according to relations of *apperception*, of self-consciousness, according to which we become conscious of our own existence. All perception likewise stands under a rule of judging.\nPresumption is no anticipation, because it does not determine but only says that something is determinable according to a rule yet to be found, according to a certain given exponent. It therefore serves to attempt this de|termination\nand to expound the appearance, and is the *principium* of the judging of it. E.g. whatever happens has its ground in something preceding.",[]],"reflm:4678":["P. I:\nThat in the soul there lies a *principium* of disposition just as much as of *affection*. That appearances can have no other order, and cannot belong otherwise to the unity of the power of representation, than by conforming to the common *principium* of disposition. For every appearance with its thoroughgoing determination must yet have unity in the mind, and consequently be subject to such conditions as make the unity of representations possible. Only that which is required for the unity of representations belongs to the objective conditions. The unity of *apprehension* is necessarily bound up with the unity of the intuition, space and time, for without these the latter would yield no real representation.\nThe principles of *exposition* must be determined on the one side by the laws of *apprehension*, on the other by the unity of the faculty of understanding. They are the standard of observation and are not borrowed from perceptions, but from the ground of these in the whole. (g original, and abstracted from them.)\nPure thinking (a priori), however, in relation to experiences, that is, to objects of the senses, contains fundamental propositions which contain the origin of all experiences, that is, of that which is thoroughly determined for experiences.\nWe must expound concepts when we cannot construct them. Appearances we cannot construct, though we can intuitions. But we must have rules for their *exposition*. These rules are really rules of the appearance itself, but only in so far as the inner [content] of it is to be discovered in its resolution. The |\nrules, therefore, of the resolution of appearances are properly the conditions of *apprehension*, in so far as it passes from one [appearance] to another and conjugates them.\n[Perceptions stand under rules of intuitions, the conjugated under rules of]\nThe fundamental proposition: everything that is thought stands under a rule, for only by means of the rule is it an object of thinking.\n––––––––––\nSynthesis (g *principium* of) contains rules of thinking a priori, but in so far as it is determined with regard to objects. There is therefore in it: 1. pure thinking (g a) and the rule of it; 2. the condition of the object, that is, that under which something is given (g x) (or brought) to be thought as object; 3. the determination of the thought from this relation (g b)/.\nThe *principium* of analysis: a rule of thinking in general. The principles of thinking, in so far as they are restricted by the condition of the subject or determined with reference to the subject, are not fundamental propositions but restrictions. (1. On the possibility of empirical synthesis in general.) Knowledge is determined a priori with reference to an object, if 1.\n*construction*\nit goes to the condition through which an object is given, and the knowledge represents the object only through concepts of the appearance. 2. If it goes to [conditions of *apprehension* in general through which] the appearance, in so far as it contains the conditions for forming a concept of it, |\nP. II: |\n3. if it goes to *apprehension* in general, in so far as it contains the condition of the unity of *perception* as well as of *intellection*, that is, of the agreement of appearances with one another and with the unity of the mind, and consequently of *exposition*.\nThe [first go] second goes to appearances, in so far as they can be brought under titles of thinking, e.g. whatever is permanent is substance; the 1st to appearances in relation to one another a priori, e.g. a triangle has three angles; the 3rd to that which constitutes the thoroughgoing determination of the appearance.\nBesides these there are subjective *principia* of thinking and objective ones of thinking or determining according to rules a priori.\nOn synthetic propositions: all objects of the senses are in space and time.\nAll objects of experience are under a rule of sensibility.\nSensation\nIntuition\nAppearance\nConcept\nThe determinability of the object according to\nsensation: *perceptio positiva*,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nintuition: *construction*,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nappearance: disposition,\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\n—\nconcept: *comprehension*.\nIn an analytic proposition the x drops out entirely, because a, taken substantively, already stands with b in a determinate identical relation.\nBut in synthetic propositions the x is that in which a, and through the condition of a, b, is determined.\nIn (g Through the) *construction* x of the concept a (triangle) the equality of three angles etc. is determined at the same time in the triangle. Through the *specification* x of the concept a the relation b is determined at the same time in this a.\nIf I determine *specifice* [specifically] the coming-to-be in time, that is, a reality in the series of time, then time is indeed the condition in which, but the rule is the condition through which.\nIf x is the sensible condition under which a is specifically determined, then b is the universal function through which it is determined therein.",[]],"reflm:4679":["P. I:\nWe are conscious of ourselves and of our own actions, and of appearances, in so far as we become conscious of their apprehension, either by coordinating them with one another or by apprehending one sensation through another.\nWe should therefore not become conscious of appearances at all, if we did not [breaks off]\nAn object of the senses is only that which acts on my senses, and therefore is active and so is substance. Hence the category of substance is *principial*. Every beginning of a state of representation is always a transition from a previous one, for otherwise we should not perceive that the former had begun. Hence, since the same subject holds of the one object as of the other, and the boundary also is common to both, the succeeding state belongs to the preceding as that which determines it. In the unity of the mind a whole is possible only in that the mind alternately determines one partial representation from another, and all together are comprehended in one act which holds of all.\n––––––––––\nx:a = m:n\nIf a indicates the condition through which x is given, then the relation a:b follows according to the principle of contradiction, and holds universally, without restriction to simultaneity [des zugleich]. E.g. in every reality (it is given only through the act of the subject) there is a relation of substance to accident. x here signifies the subject, a the apprehension of the object. x:a is therefore the relation of the original act [breaks off]\n––––––––––\nMere apprehension already declares that behind the appearance there must be a substance, the cause of the composition; but observation and judgment must show which is the substance, etc. Where there is an action, there is substance, e.g. warmth in the case of light; but whether light is substance does not follow from the apprehension, but from the exposition of the appearance. That something is represented as having happened is enough for regarding it as an effect; for the apprehension of it is in fact an effect, which occurs in the mind, etc.\nThe intellectual functions therefore make the beginning in apprehension, but the specification gives us the rule for applying this concept. Hence determinate rules of synthesis can be given only through experience, but their universal norm [can be given] *a priori*.\nP. II:\nEmpirical intuition is appearance.\nAppearance of which one is conscious is perception.\nEvery perception must be brought under a title of the understanding, since otherwise it yields no concept and nothing is thought in it. By means of these concepts we make use of appearances, or rather the concepts indicate the manner in which we make use of appearances as the matter for thinking: 1. of intuition in general, for magnitude; 2. of sensation, in order to determine the real relation in the appearance. We say: the stone weighs, the wood falls (g the body moves), i.e. it acts, and is therefore substance. The field is prepared, the meadow dried out, the glass broken: these are effects, which relate to a cause. The wall is firm, the wax soft, the gold dense: these are connections in the composite. Without such concepts the appearances would all be separate and would not belong to one another. If they have like relations to one another in space or time, these relations are yet not determined from the objects of the appearances, but are only set side by side.\nExperience is a perception that is understood. We understand it, however, when we represent it to ourselves under titles of the understanding. Experience is a specification of the concepts of the understanding through given appearances. Appearances are the matter or substratum.\nExperiences are therefore possible only through the presupposition that all appearances belong under titles of the understanding; that is, in all mere intuition there is magnitude, in all appearance substance and accident, in their change cause and effect, and in their whole reciprocity [Wechselwirkung]. These propositions therefore hold of all objects of experience. The very same propositions hold also of the mind, in respect of the generation of its own representations, and are moments of the genesis. Under the titles of apperception, however, all appearances must be brought, so that they are constructed as much according to intuition [breaks off]\nThe conditions of subsumption under these concepts, however, are taken from the sensible relation, which stands in analogy with the act of the understanding and belongs to inner sense, of which apperception the [breaks off]\nWhence is it that what acts is regarded as if it were abiding, and as if only the actions, effects and compositions varied?",[]],"reflm:4680":["Everything that happens is represented, for the sake of determining its concept among appearances, i.e. with a view to the possibility of experience, as contained under a rule, the relation of which is expressed through a concept of the understanding. In the appearance x, therefore, in which a is a concept, there must be contained, besides what is thought through a, conditions of its specification, which make a rule necessary, the function of which is expressed by b. a cannot, when it happens, be specifically determined in time otherwise than by means of a rule. Therefore no experience of a can take place without a rule. Therefore the principle of sufficient reason is a principle of the rule of experience, namely for instituting such experience.\nThe proposition that whatever in time follows upon something follows, according to a rule, upon something else, or that in respect of its sequence a rule obtains, does not lie in the specification of the concept a of happening or of contingency; for there only the appearance is meant. (But happening is already an existence according to a rule of time.)\nThe ordering of appearances according to the relation of space and time requires a rule, just as appearance itself [requires] a form.",[]],"reflm:4681":["S. I:\nBefore an occurrence [Begebenheit] something must always precede (condition of perception).\nBefore an occurrence all sorts of things may precede, but among them there is one upon which it always follows.\nA reality always depends (g on a point of time and on that which determines it) on something accompanying, [what] through which the point of time is determined for it (condition of perception).\nThere are all sorts of accompanying things, but among them there is something that is always there.\n[An aggregate is a manifold in reciprocal relation, but among these must]\n(g In [the] respect of what is simultaneous there is always a taking-together [*Zusammennehmung*] (condition of perception).)\nIt can (g but with) all sorts of things be taken together; but [where the manifold reciprocally determines itself, this combination is objectively a] where something is to be regarded as objectively combined, there is a reciprocal determination of the manifold among its members.\nIf something were not always, that is, if there were not something [subsisting] permanent, *stabile*, there would be no fixed point, or determination of the point of time, and so no perception, that is, no *determinatio* of something in time.\nIf something did not constantly (g before an occurrence) precede, then among the many things that precede there would be nothing [what] to which what happens belongs in a series; it would have no determinate place in the series.\nThrough the rules of perception the [things] objects of the senses are determinable in time; in intuition they are given merely as appearances. According to those rules a quite different series is found from that in which the object was given.\nS. II:\n(g Nothing synthetic can be objectively valid except that which is the condition, that is, that through which it is given as object, or through which something that has been given is thought as object. An object is thought only so far as it stands under a rule of appearance, and it is the receptivity of the rule [makes that the ap] that makes the ap|pearance objective; thus it is not the appearances that stand under a rule, but the objects which lie at their ground. According to this rule they are exposited.)\nWithout [any] such rules of perception no experiences could be made, because these are the titles of appearances, just as [sensible concepts of the fo] sensible concepts are titles of intuitions.\nRule of the *praesumtiones* [presumptions], as appraisal [*Beurtheilung*] of appearances, for judgments that are to be determined provisionally.\n(g One can indeed see much, but understand nothing of what appears, unless it is brought under concepts of the understanding and by means of them into relation to a rule; this is the acceptance [*Annahme*] through the understanding.)\n(g The synthesis contains the relation of appearances not in perception but in the concept. That every relation in [concepts must] perception nevertheless still presupposes a relation in the concept shows that the mind contains in itself the universal [source] and sufficient source of synthesis, and that all appearances are exponible in it.)\n*Principia* of perception.\n[Rules] Principles of observation, or of the exposition of appearances in general.\nThey are *praesumtiones* of experience.\nAnalogies of the understanding.\nAxioms of intuitions, analogies of the understanding, *petitiones* [postulates] of reason.\nWe perceive something only through being conscious of our apprehension, and consequently of existence in our inner sense, [consequently] |\nhence as belonging to one of the three relations in the mind. All observation requires a rule.\nThe intellectual element in perception relates to the power [*Kraft*] of inner sense. [The principles of obs] The analogies of observation relate to thoroughgoing perception, or perception thoroughly determined.\nAll combinations are made by the mind, and the mind combines nothing objectively except what is necessarily determined from its correlate; otherwise the representations are indeed put together, but not connected, in perception indeed but not in the concept.\n––––––––––\nOnly that which is capable of constant principles in the mind do we entitle object. Appraisal must therefore precede objective judgments. For everything else, which does not admit such principles, is nothing for us, and cannot be perceived either, since perception requires a *coniugatio* [conjugation] according to a universal [either] ground.",[]],"reflm:4682":["S. I:\nThe concept of what happens is a determination of sensibility, but through the understanding, in that something is posited in the temporal sequence. Now this cannot take place otherwise than in relation to something preceding. Accordingly the rule that what happens is determined by something preceding says nothing other than (g that all this is determinable in the order of time) that the determination of a place of existence in time [of the re of et] must take place through the understanding, and hence according to a rule.\nReality is that through which something is an object of perception. \"In every reality there is a relation of the accident to the substance\" means |\nas much as this: the determination of an existence in time in general can take place only through something that is in all time.\nThe analogies of appearance mean as much as this: unless I determined every relation of appearances in time through a universal condition of relation, I should assign to no appearance its place.\nThe concepts of substance, ground and whole therefore serve only to assign to each [appearance] reality in appearance its place, in that |\nS. II: |\neach represents a function or [power of] dimension of time, in which the object that is perceived is to be determined, and experience is to be made out of appearance.",[]],"reflm:4683":["S. I:\n1.\nA synthetic proposition that holds of all things in general is false, and so is one in general whose subject is a pure concept of the understanding, unless it is meant to hold not objectively (g absolutely) but only under the subjective restriction of the use of reason.\nOnly the conditions of sensibility make synthesis possible: 1. of pure, 2. of empirical intuition (of outer and inner sense).\nFurther, of the empirical or rational use of my understanding. For only in the sensible condition of a does there lie something in which anyone can know, besides the concept of a, also b.\nS. II:\n2.\nAll synthetic propositions have a homogeneity, although it seems that one concept is intellectual, the other empirical. In exposition they are [empirical] homogeneous. One need only take, instead of the concept, its specification [and].\n*Concipere* [to conceive] means to make for oneself a concept of something a priori. The principles of conception [relate to subj] are either those of thinking in general, or of absolute positing, or of taking-together a priori. For the first, the sensible condition is the [recep] whole of sensibility; for the second, the whole of thinking in respect of a datum in general; for the third, the whole in itself, or totality.\n––––––––––\nThe understanding can determine nothing in sensibility except through a universal act [*Handlung*]. For example, occurrence [*Entstehen*] through a universal condition of sequence; existence through a subject of all existence; being-together through a universal unity.",[]],"reflm:4684":["P. I:\nHow can one know that a thing in general, which is not given to the senses at all, contains something more besides what one actually thinks through one's concept a? But because one time in which something happens cannot be distinguished from another, the sequence can be determined only through a rule of time. Hence in the sensible condition we can represent to ourselves something more than was thought in a, namely, in this time as a construction (of the triangle), every time in which, if the same member is given, the same correlatum is given. We therefore represent the object to ourselves through an analogue of construction, namely, that it can be constructed before inner sense. That is to say, just as something follows upon something else, so, whenever something happens, something else always follows upon it; or [we represent] that this representation is one of the universal acts of the determination of appearances, which for this reason give a rule |, just as a triangle is constructed only according to a rule and serves as a rule for all [triangles].\n––––––––––\nIn analytic judgments the predicate relates properly to the concept a; in synthetic judgments it relates to [the condition of] the object of the concept, because the predicate is not contained in the concept. But the object which corresponds to a concept has certain conditions for the execution of this concept, that is, for its *positio in concreto* (position in the concrete). (For every concept is a universal act, which presupposes a *substratum* in which the [object] representation of the object can be posited.) Now the condition of all concepts is [proper(ly)] sensible. Hence if the concept is also sensible but universal, it must be considered in its *concretum*, e.g. the triangle in its construction. If the concept signifies not pure intuition but empirical intuition, that is, experience, then the x contains the condition of the relative position (a) in space and time, that is, the condition of determining something universally therein.\nOtherwise appearances are determined through time, but in synthesis time is determined through an appearance, e.g. through that which exists or happens or is together. These are the most universal [determinations] of appearances, of which [the real the] reality is the matter.\nOn the intellectuation of appearance, e.g.: Something exists, something happens. This is indeed already an *intellectuale*, placed in the form of time. The something is reality (sensation); the happening is existence as sequence. By what, then, does the appearance become intellectual?\n––––––––––\nIn a synthetic judgment two pure concepts of reason can never stand in relation to each other, but only a pure concept of the understanding with a concept under sensible condition, whether of the appearance or of the representation a priori. Reason for this.\nThe sum of the laws of free actions which are naturally determined through the common power of choice [Willkühr] is right [Recht]. By Willkühr I understand the will determining with force.\n––––––––––\nWhence is it that the analogies of the understanding have no evidence? They are nevertheless constitutive, only not directly objective.\nP. II:\n*Psychologia rationalis qvoad praedicata animae humanae generalia* (rational psychology as to the general predicates of the human soul):\n1. *Qvoad subiectum* (as to the subject). [Sim] Substance. Unique in man. Simple. Immaterial.\n2. *Qvoad vires* (as to the powers). Representative power, not unique. Spontaneity, either practically or transcendentally absolute (freedom), either original or derivative.\n(g Choice [*arbitrium*] is either brute or free, and freedom is either original or derivative; freedom is opposed either to brute necessity or to fatality, the former in the practical sense, the latter in the transcendental sense; in the transcendental sense, therefore, it is either original or derivative.)\n3. *Qvoad statum* (as to the state) either of human life: commerce*) of body and soul; or of birth**); or of death.\n*(g either ideal or real)\n**(g either epigenesis or preformation)\nThe soul after death [is] either [contingently] lives by decree or from its own nature; the latter life is immortality.\nThe argument for immortality is either natural or moral. The former is either from rational principles (metaphysical) or from empirical principles (physio|logical).\nThe metaphysical argument is concluded from the principle of life joined with inert matter devoid of life: the principle of life is not deprived of life by separation from matter, but is freed from the impediments of life. Hence animal life ceases after death and spiritual life [or free] begins, as the soul is freed.\nThe physiological argument is either strict or analogical (g the latter is the best of all from human nature). The former cannot be given, either for or against, since we have no experiences of a soul separated from commerce with the body, because we think only in this commerce. Nor can one argue against it from the dependence of the soul on the body in this life, because we do not see what the body does to the soul when it is at the same time an impediment to it. It is as when someone is tied to a carriage: he cannot move himself unless he moves the carriage. If the wheels are well greased, the motion still proceeds freely, but they are not the causes of this motion, although, if they are braked by some cause internal to the carriage, they render the man immobile.\nThe moral argument is practically sufficient, though not apodictic. It is teleological, drawn from the wisdom, goodness and justice of God, in a word from the laws of providence, and is best suited to common sense.\n(g animal life — spiritual)\nThe state after death is either one of regeneration or of liberation; the former is either to earthly life (metempsychosis) or to heavenly life (corpusc.).\nLife after death is either that of a [soul] dreaming or of one waking.\nThe former: *Hypnopsychita* (g reviviscence, or) with personality or with the Lethean cup.",[]],"reflm:4685":["Inside I:\nThe real differences of the logical acts are not with ....... │\nThe absolute [aspect] of the acts of the understanding relates to the object, │ the relative to other representations, e.g. to the logical relation. │\nThe real differences of concepts are essential, the logical are arbitrary, e.g. substance is not an accident, but subject and predicate │ are so alternately, etc. │\nInside II:\nThe *qvidditas* is reality or existence through some predicate. And the difference │ among these [things] from others which have the same predicate is quality. │\nGuilielmi Tyrii historia belli sacri | cum continuatione Heroldi. Basel. 1549.",[]],"reflm:4686":["P. I:\nAll appearances stand in community, that is, nothing is in the void.\n––––––––––\nIn order to determine something we must have something determining; hence the first or complete determination is impossible a priori.\nThe dispute between the conditioned and the unconditioned.\nP. II:\nDifference of evidence.\nTopical. Place in the mind, places among one another.\nThe mind can become conscious of itself only through the appearances which correspond to its dynamic functions, and of the appearances only through [that which] its dynamic functions.\nAppearances, if they were seen through in their continuous connection, can only the dynamic functions [text] breaks off, the rest is cut away.",[]],"reflm:4687":["Objects are given either in appearance or in the idea, and are thought either in intuition or in the concept (*conceptus*). In the idea the object is given which is thereby merely possible. This is perfection. It cannot be borrowed from things; rather, things are perfect so far as they agree with the idea. Ideas are *repraesentationes archetypae* (archetypal representations), sensible concepts are *ectypae* (ectypal), and concepts of the understanding are *reflectentes* (reflecting).\nEither the conditions through which objects are given are those through which they are thought: [pr] *cognitio prototypa* (prototypal knowledge); or they are thought only through the fact that they are given: *ectypae*.",[]],"reflm:4688":["The absolute is the relation to thoroughgoing determination. Agreement is absolute possibility; position in every respect is actuality; actuality through possibility *in sensu absoluto* (in the absolute sense) is absolute necessity.",[]],"reflm:4689":["From a particular major *propositio* (premiss) one infers only to a possible conclusion, from a singular one to the actual conclusion which is contained in it, from a universal one to the necessity of the conclusion.\n(*Qvidditas*) *qvaeitas*, *qvalitas*, *qvantitas*.",[]],"reflm:4690":["The inner necessity of a being is impossible to think. For in itself, from what belongs internally to a thing, the removal of it together with all its predicates is always possible.\nBut relative [absolu(te)] necessity can well be thought, and necessity in every respect, as condition of the possibility of all things, is absolute necessity.\nIt is the necessity of a universal *substratum* of the manifold of concepts of things.",[]],"reflm:4691":["Beside, in and \nFirst, the alteration of the thing is the persistence of the entity [Sache]. The entity is altered in that its affections change.",[]],"reflm:4692":["Dependence proves inner contingency.",[]],"reflm:4693":["Alteration proves the contingency of the state;\nComing-to-be and passing-away prove the contingency of the thing.\nBoth prove only inner contingency, namely contingency in itself; not conditioned contingency, [that is] (g that) under the circumstances and [in] the conditions the opposite of what happens would also be possible.\nBecause we can never experience the contradictory opposite, absolute contingency, that is, in every respect, also cannot be inferred from experience. *omnimoda contingentia* (contingency in every respect) (g possibility). Freedom is that whose consequences are absolutely contingent.\nThe possibility of the *contrarie oppositum* (contrary opposite) does not prove contingency at another time. Rest, from a moving body. Not-being and the opposite are not one and the same.\nThe *contradictorie oppositum* (contradictory opposite), namely at the same time, (g as possible) is chance.\nThe *oppositum* at another time is *contrarie oppositum*.",[]],"reflm:4694":["*Omnitudo distributiva* [distributive allness] is universality (*universalitas*); the collective is totality (*totalitas*); absolute totality is *universitas*.",[]],"reflm:4695":["A composite in which there is completeness (*omnitudo partium*, allness of the parts) is a whole (*totum*), and this completeness is entitled *totalitas*. Taking together at once (*simul sumtio*).",[]],"reflm:4696":["The limits of [aggregation] composition: infinitely great, or no limits (g immeasurable); of decomposition: infinitely small. Extensive, protensive, intensive magnitude.",[]],"reflm:4697":["A magnitude in which each unit is itself a magnitude is continuous; consequently it contains in itself no absolute units, and therefore constitutes in itself no number [in itself] but only quantity (*Menge*) in general. Point, moment.\nOf the *maximum*. Of the greatest number. Of infinite multitude. Of boundaries (*Gränzen*) and limits (*Schranken*). Of finite measure.\nThe infinite of increase is possible. But the infinite of taking-together is not. *Infinitudo progressiva*, *collectiva*.",[]],"reflm:4698":["At the top of the page:\nTo know and determine the *quantitas phaenomenon* is taught by |\nmathematics; the *quanta noumena* only by philosophy. Allness, unrestrictedness. — Reality, degree, etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:4699":["Divisibility is not an accident; an accident is at all times positive, because it is an existence.\nBodies are *phaenomena substantiata*, not because they are composite but because they are outer appearances in which the first subject is lacking.\nThinking beings I can represent to myself only as substances, because I lend them my I.",[]],"reflm:4700":["At the top of the page,\nIn a judgment the singular proposition expresses unity, the particular plurality, and the universal *omnitudo* [allness]. A universal proposition expresses, through the common predicate, the connection of the many that stand under the universal concept of a subject, or rather the connection in the sphere of a concept. If the predicate is a *reciprocum* of the subject, then the subject [is] fills out the *sphaera* and designates a *totum*. The conditional proposition expresses the relation of ground to consequence; this (g a) ground is the ground either of subordinated consequences in a *sorites* or of coordinated ones |\nin a *copulativum*. Thirdly, that the predicate is in the subject as an accident expresses the connection of accidents in a substance.\nAll connection of the many is unity. Hence the many is connected in a substance, or through a ground, or into a whole.\nThat from two things one can be inferred from the other as its [consequence] predicate or as its consequence, or that from two things the unity of a composite can be inferred, experience must teach.\nWith regard to quantity just the same relations hold as with regard to quality. Position, *defectus* [lack] and privation have the signs +a, 0, and –a.",[]],"reflm:4701":["The magnitude of a substance is always known and measured by its persistence in its own state amid all grounds of change. If a body of air has much elasticity, the quantity of the substance is to be measured not by this elasticity but by the *inertia* with which it resists the expansion of the same, or any force that [it] moves its mass.",[]],"reflm:4702":["The proposition 'no substance perishes' means as much as this: all alterations are always related to a subject; hence no alteration or exchange of subjects can take place, since otherwise they would be only alterations. Hence these [alterations] are a thread that is always inseparably attached to certain points. The subjects are also entitled the standing, in regard to which one can know the rest as variable.",[]],"reflm:4703":["Opposite and \nNo substance can perish of itself. For this an action is required by which it is abolished, and it cannot abolish itself. Nor can any substance annihilate another (if this other does not exist through the former). For one substance can flow only into the state of the other, and indeed only into that which is common to their states.",[]],"reflm:4704":["The *principium generale commercii* [general principle of community] is: all influence in the world is partly the effect of the agent upon the patient, partly the counter-effect of the latter. The inner state and determination of the substance in this counter-effect is the action through which the accident of influence is actuated (apprehension). There is therefore [a] unity of the acting forces of different substances in every *commercium*.",[]],"reflm:4705":["A composite is either substantial or accidental (*compositum vel substantiale vel accidentale*).\nA composite of substances and accidents (*compositum ex substantiis et accidentibus*) is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4706":["Unity is either of composition or of decomposition; the former is numerical unity, the latter the simple; both are unity of mathematical synthesis.",[]],"reflm:4707":["The question is whether a given magnitude is infinite. The condition is that it be given. Now through the 'at once' (*zugleich*) the being given is indicated. On the other hand, it is not given in apprehension or in determination.",[]],"reflm:4708":["(s *Lex isonomiae*.)\nA first beginning is impossible. For beginning can be thought only according to laws of sensibility, hence only in a time occupied by objects of sensibility. Consequently a first beginning, before which no *phaenomenon* went before, cannot be thought. Therefore all generations are only alterations. But a first cause can well be thought, because this is merely something intellectual. The former [a first beginning] cannot therefore serve for the explanation of appearances: the cause of appearances must be in *commercium* with the world. From this it follows that nothing can arise without something being abolished in return, so that the sum of reality remains. For otherwise time itself (the absolute) would stand in relation to the alterations, and the cause of alteration would be outside time. The universe itself cannot move, neither in empty space, still less in a completely full space. For otherwise, in the first case, [space] would be an appearance whose correlate is no appearance; in the second, there would be a motion in no space at all. Hence every cause of motion is in community with the world, and none is possible except in so far as just as much is generated on the opposite side. A spirit that moved a matter absolutely would [determine] effect this motion of the universe. He who |\ncan move only in so far as he remains at rest in the sum is in union with matter.",[]],"reflm:4709":["Beside and \nIn appearance two things can be distinguished through the mere *relationes*, but not universally through the understanding. (s *Diversitas phaenomenon*.)",[]],"reflm:4710":["If completely similar things were exchanged from different places, everything would then be as before.\nAnalytic identity; synthetic [identity] of space and time.",[]],"reflm:4711":["The question is whether one can say that a thing is in two places at once, if [that] which is in one place is internally (g completely) identical with that in the other.\nSpace also makes possible real outer conflict, and by analogy inner conflict.",[]],"reflm:4712":["The question is whether two things can differ merely *numero*, or whether they must be throughout specifically *plura* in order to be more than one thing.",[]],"reflm:4713":["93'. E II 860. 861. 894.\nEverything that is, is necessary, either absolutely or conditionally*, hence *ut causatum alterius* [as the effect of another].\n*(s We therefore recognize also in all relations of the manifold in the composite a necessity, which must always be derived. Nothing can be originally contingent (g |\nM 93': |\nrather the reverse: every *causatum* is contingent in itself). |\nM 93: |\nThe only question is: what is contingent? and upon what does the *principium necessitatis* rest? Upon this, that otherwise there would be no determining grounds *a priori*.)\nThe *principium rationati* says only: everything that happens is necessary under a condition.\nEverything must be objectively determined *a priori*.\nEverything alterable has a beginning, hence arises, and is contingent and is *causatum alterius*.",[]],"reflm:4714":["Logical completeness (*completudo logica*) is *universalitas*, real completeness is *universitas*; likewise *communitas* and *communio*.",[]],"reflm:4715":["We have three [pre-]categories and their predicaments: first, of position (being and not being); second, of *respectus* [relation]; third, of *completu|dinis* [completeness].\nThe first is whether something is or is not; the second, what is there in relation to another or not; the third, how much of a thing is together, etc. *Qvaeitas*, *qvalitas*, *qvantitas propositionis*.",[]],"reflm:4716":["The proof of Leibniz, who takes the world intellectually, from the representative power (*Vorstellungskraft*) of the monads, is not bad, except that it proves more than it concludes; it leads to an idealism.",[]],"reflm:4717":["That in the series and aggregate of sensibility there is no beginning and no totality, i.e. no determinability with regard to the *omnitudo*, comes from this: the All here must suffice for determination according to conditions of sensibility, and hence for addition or progress without limits. But this is no judgment about the world in itself, in whose concept the All precedes, and from which each part alone is determinable. In this series there are not true things and true causes but only appearances; and [an] appearance in general must be endless, because only in it can the indeterminate action of sensible knowledge proceed endlessly.",[]],"reflm:4718":["The outer senses give me nothing but impressions; only through the |\ninner intuition (which, however, goes only to myself) can I know the object that underlies the impressions. The question now is whether that which we call body is a particular kind of being, distinct from thinking beings, or only a particular appearance of their reciprocal presence. For the latter I have no ground; but neither can I be refuted. This would be the mystical world.\n(s The physical or the mystical idealist; the former denies altogether objects of appearance; the latter assumes them, but ascribes to them other positive, fanatical determinations.)",[]],"reflm:4719":["The transition from reality to negation is continuous, that is, through infinite degrees of ever smaller difference down to vanishing. Thus the decomposition of a body through a continuous motion is also continuous, and from the place in the body (outermost monad) it cannot proceed immediately to a place outside the body, but only through all the degrees of the intervals between the two. The same holds of every change.\nMonads can therefore not be objects of an empirical synthesis of outer appearance.\nOf the whole of all (g possible) decomposition and composition no experience can be given.",[]],"reflm:4720":["Opposite the section heading, and likewise opposite M § 406f.:\nSpace has this peculiarity as compared with the concept of time, that |\nfirst, the concept of time, and hence the whole of sensibility, can be thought in the determinations of [space]; second, that a force which I posit anywhere in space, as the *principium* of appearances, does not remain merely a force in general, but through the relations of space to everything outer has determinate conditions, and so gives a determinate concept of the possibility of the object. Third, space is the ground of the possibility (g of the outer, hence) of the object, whereas time concerns only the state, and extends in general to [all] mere existence [without d] as the ground of the relations of things in their existence and the measure of their duration. Hence time is no ground of the possibility of things, least of all of substances. Hence positive *principia intellectualia* (intellectual principles) are possible for physics, but not for [cosmology] psychology; everything in the latter is negative in relation to pure natural science: 1. lack of parts; 2. no such *commercium* (community) as matter has; 3. no such origin and perishing as bodies have.",[]],"reflm:4721":["That a force is bound up with motion is not established from the motion, since in it no *accidens absolutum* (absolute accident) is to be found, but from the sensation that is found in motion, whether when we move or when motion strikes us.",[]],"reflm:4722":["Nature, simply or taken subjectively, is that which corresponds to the necessary being.\nThe object of all empirical modes of knowledge is nature.\nThe contingent all: nature.",[]],"reflm:4723":["Appearances are representations so far as we are affected. The representation of our (g free) self-activity is one in which we are not affected; consequently it is not appearance, but [ursp frey] apperception. Now the principle of sufficient reason holds only as a *principium* of the exposition of appearances, and consequently not as exposition of original intuitions.",[]],"reflm:4724":["We cannot prove freedom *a posteriori*, because the lack of perception of determining grounds furnishes no proof that there are none. Nor can we know its possibility *a priori*, since the possibility of the original ground, which is not determined by another, cannot be comprehended at all. We can therefore prove it not theoretically at all, but only as a necessary practical *hypothesis*.",[]],"reflm:4725":["The practical concept of freedom is that which suffices for performing actions according to rules of reason, and which therefore gives the imperatives of reason their force; the speculative or sophistical [vernünftelnde] concept of freedom is that which suffices to [Handl] explain free actions according to reason. The latter is impossible, because it is the original in the *derivativum* (the derived).",[]],"reflm:4726":["Over and \nThat of which the ground (and its relation to the consequence) cannot be given at all through the senses, that is, the inconceivable. Now the ground of the higher faculty of desire is not sensible, and so cannot be comprehended [werden], since our reason has only the use *a posteriori* upon what has been given to the senses. |\nLikewise, the frequent repetitions of the one in the actually infinite (namely in the past in respect of time, [and the] one after another and with the present simultaneously) can [verb lacking in the text] to sense, but certainly the potentially infinite. The former are given as a whole, but the generation of them is never whole.",[]],"reflm:4727":["Whether God is free, although he can do nothing evil?",[]],"reflm:4728":["Spirit is a pure intelligence. (Pure is what is separated from everything heterogeneous.) Spirit is therefore an intelligence separated from all community with bodies. If in *psychologia rationalis* (rational psychology) I abstract from all *commercium* (community) with bodies, the concept of the soul becomes that of spirit, and *psychologia* becomes *pneumatologia*. If I leave out intelligence and take merely soul in community with body, there remain the *animae brutorum* (souls of brutes).",[]],"reflm:4729":["Negations in general mean: some realities not. But this posits the opposite, namely all realities with a limitation. Thus the concept of all reality is necessary to us as a *substratum* of reason; but for that reason we cannot regard a highest reality as necessary in itself. Further, according to the *principium exclusi medii* (principle of the excluded middle) every thing is considered in relation to everything possible, as in a division, and hence in a whole of reality. |\nThe existence of a thing can never be proved from mere concepts, because existence is not one of the predicates, and because from concepts nothing further than a relative affirming or denying, but not the absolute position of the object with its predicates, can be inferred. The concept that lies at the ground is a necessary presupposition, and for that reason seems to be a concept of a necessary being.",[]],"reflm:4730":["At the very bottom of the page:\nIn the original being the possibilities of things lie not as parts but as consequences, since otherwise the original being could be composed of *limitata* (limited things). Limiting concept of magnitude: *infinitum*. Of dynamics: *libertas*. Of existence: necessary.",[]],"reflm:4731":["The knowledge of God from the first mover grounds [gründet] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4732":["Around M § 822:\nIn *theologia naturalis* (natural theology) we know the original being only in relation to the world, and consequently not according to its absolute predicates (for we surely do not wish to think the *ens extramundanum* (being outside the world) through *praedicata mundana* (worldly predicates)), but according to predicates of relation with regard to the world; thus only the causality, and hence the exponent of the relation to the world. E.g. As a (a work of art) is to b (the intelligent artist), so c (the world) is to x (that which I |\ncall understanding in God). Whether I can infer so safely, and whether this concept is not sufficient for us, indeed even quite correct?",[]],"reflm:4733":["The objections with regard to the existence of God and his attributes [müssen] are all taken from the conditions of sensibility, which have been held to be intellectual, and from the subjective conditions of comprehensibility, which have been held to be objective. (Existence has a magnitude; that we know from time; this magnitude is entitled duration; afterwards, however, we abstract time from duration, and that is intellectual; from this arises eternity, in the concept of which there can be no contradiction except only with sensible conditions.) One must not go about this play of grounds and counter-grounds so zealously and anxiously. He who is in a fortress will not have a battery fired at every swaggering Pandour.\nInfinite and single space, the [Grund] condition of the possibility of all outer presence of things as they appear, is indeed not a proof of the existence of a ground-being and original being that comprehends everything and in which everything is sustained, and from which also all unity and relation derive, since [a thing] is possible, as it were, through its place in the All; but it is yet a proof that the human mind can think no connection without a common ground, and no determinations except in One which contains everything. Likewise with time, in which all existence lies. This serves to let the assumption of such a being be accepted as subjectively necessary, and hence also as sufficient for practice.",[]],"reflm:4734":["If we find in a being the connection with the very same effects, then the inner constitution of the determinations |\nwhich contain the causality may be what it will. We know the cause only from the causality and the effect. Thus if what is in God stands in the same relation to the misery of men as mercy does, we may call God merciful, wrathful, jealous, etc., but only *per analogiam* (by analogy), since we must not compare the absolute with ourselves.",[]],"reflm:4735":["God judges not according to kindness (indulgence) but according to justice; he gives laws according to holiness [helligkeit] and governs according to kindness. Thus, in the case of our faults, we must trust [hope] not in the kindness of the judge but in that of the ruler, both to help our impotence and, in keeping with this worthiness, to give us undeserved blessedness.\nHe cannot hope to come into heaven if he has begged off [gebete] and repented much, but only if he is a better man. Unless it be then etc. etc. The Old Testament was not so. Hence Christ prescribes actions and faith, not petitions and observances.\nThat the divine law is, in respect of us, a holy law we see from our conscience, which, notwithstanding our frailty, does not approve even the slightest transgressions but condemns them all.",[]],"reflm:4736":["The polytheism of subordination is properly monotheism;\nthe polytheism of coordination is Manichaeism.",[]],"reflm:4737":["On the natural knowledge of God according to the *analogia per viam reductionis et eminentiae* (analogy by way of reduction and eminence).\nThe absolute we do not know.",[]],"reflm:4738":["On M § 890:\nGod is the author through one will: *contra emanationem, Spinozam* (against emanation, against Spinoza).\nGod is the author through free will: *contra fatalistas* (against the fatalists).",[]],"reflm:4739":["(g The freedom in God is subjective necessity, but not natural necessity.)\nThe freedom of the divine will consists in the determination of all things according to his highest good pleasure, hence according to the necessary rules of the highest good pleasure, and so not [üb] in disposing over these rules. His will is holy, his end is kind, and his judgment is just. [Gott k] God is the lawgiver, not in that he gives the laws [freely] at pleasure, but in that he subjects creatures to his laws through his will.\nHe cannot dispense from laws. Yet he is kind, but only [in respect of] in such a way that he gives *complementa* (supplements) to *fragilitati humanae* (human frailty), not indulgently.",[]],"reflm:4740":["God's counsels are wise, his will is holy, his end (g government) is kind, his [judgment is] judging is just.",[]],"reflm:4741":["At the top of the page:\nPhysico-theology. Chief rule. One must appeal to God as an immediate cause in no single case, but only generally, in |\nrespect of the [first] last *substratum* in the world (substance), unless it is where a revelation is meant to be in question. For our reason is not a faculty whose progress we may cut short in its use at pleasure. It is a law to itself. It is contrary to reason to cut off further investigation, and wantonly to excuse oneself [at the last point] from all further effort, by presuming to judge what God has done immediately. For reason alone can decide what is fitting or not. There is, for us, an undetermined interval between an event or arrangement of nature and God, in which we must apply our powers to explain everything according to laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:4742":["At the bottom of the page:\nIn appearances nothing absolutely first is to be met with. But in the synthesis of the understanding there is. Thus there is indeed no first beginning, but there is a first cause, part [etc.], action, etc. etc. A first as *phaenomenon* would appear as a limit against nothing. The antinomy of reason is therefore nothing other than the difference of the principles of reason, so far as the data are given sensibly, that is, as dependent on objects, or intellectually, that is, from the mind itself; these principles agree in respect of (g single) possible experiences, but not in respect of the whole of them. Hence, considered *a posteriori*, all actions of men are empirically determined; *a priori*, undetermined and free.",[]],"reflm:4743":["Cause and beginning: the former is intellectual, the latter sensitive. The beginning is only in the world, but not of the world. Why did God not create the world earlier? God stands in no [abs] relation to absolute empty time.",[]],"reflm:4744":["The world is a proof of the divine omnipotence,\nof the divine wisdom,\nomniscience,\nof the divine goodness,\nof the divine justice.",[]],"reflm:4745":["Providence, which makes every end accord with the whole of all ends, is wisdom.",[]],"reflm:4746":["[Es] *Creatio et annihilatio in cursu mundi* (creation and annihilation in the course of the world) is contrary to the principles of the empirical use of reason; rather, the divine influence is *conservatio* (conservation), because in respect of him there is no temporal succession. *Conservatio non est influxus* (conservation is not influence). *Non concursus* (not concurrence) (g except in *actionibus liberis*, free actions). It is either *Providentia* (providence) (g in creation) or *gubernatio in conservatione Mundi* (government in the conservation of the world). The former, or *Providentia*, is only general or else also special; the latter [applies] to the state of the individual in general or to a singular case, and is then named *directio extraordinaria* (extraordinary direction).",[]],"reflm:4747":["One can ask where the order of nature begins, not of the whole world, but of a particular kind of things; e.g. the order of the constitution of the earth, or the production of each animal. Finally one can ask: How, in general, do we represent to ourselves the beginning of the order of nature?",[]],"reflm:4748":["*Concursus* (concurrence) is contradistinguished from *actioni solitariae* (solitary action). Co-operation or sole operation. The (g forces of the) world are divided into nature and freedom; in respect of nature God alone is cause, in respect of freedom he concurs. *Causarum sibi subordinatorum nexus non est concursus* (the connection of causes subordinated to one another is not concurrence).",[]],"reflm:4749":["How the *intima praesentia* (inmost presence) brought Spinoza to regard substances not as effects but as actions of the Godhead.",[]],"reflm:4750":["Inner sense contains a feeling of the divine omnipresence; for it is merely through our being immediately conscious of the cause of ourselves and of everything that we are conscious of our connections with other things.\n(s We cannot become conscious of ourselves as in God; rather, were we in him, God would be conscious of us as his determinations and we should not be conscious of ourselves at all.\nOf the divine omnipresence we cannot become conscious (g through sense).)",[]],"reflm:4751":["If God concurs in the action of the creature, then the creature also concurs in the actions of God; for the *concursus* is reciprocal, although not equal on both sides. It is a share on both sides in a third, as effect.",[]],"reflm:4752":["Predestination is that subordination of the things of the world to the divine decree in which all events are regarded as ends and all causes as means. In that case there is no order of nature.",[]],"reflm:4753":["Revelation as a dispensation of the extraordinary direction, according to which someone's insights improve religion; such writings are entitled imparted by God.\n(s To regard our own limitation of knowledge as divine wisdom.)",[]],"reflm:4754":["Revelation is, for every creature, nature according to its mode of knowledge.\nThe revelation of God through reason must precede every other; for it gives the first correct concept, by which I can test every other.\nRevelations of his existence or of his will.\n(s The pure concept of both God can reveal to us only inwardly, through our reason, and not outwardly, either through works or words.)",[]],"reflm:4755":["Use of not knowing that a God exists.",[]],"reflm:4756":["P. I:\nA. Space.\nDialectic of the [appearances] sensibility.\n1. Is space something actual (g real)? Substance, accident, relation. (g Ideality of space:) Hobbes: it is a *phantasma* of a thing existing as if external. Descartes holds space to be an abstraction from the extension of matter. Leibniz joins him in this. Clarke, however, defends the reality of space. Newton: it is the *sensorium* of the divine omnipresence. Epicurus asserted the subsistent reality of space, Wolff its inherent reality.\n2. Is there a *vacuum mundanum et extramundanum* [a void within the world and one beyond it]? There would be appearance without an object to determine the intuition and its positions. In the latter case there would be motion of the world-whole; in the former, outer intuition without objects. If *vacuum* means what is not filled with matter (the impenetrably extended), then a *vacuum* will be possible.\n3. All parts of space are again spaces. The point is not a part but a limit. Continuity.\n4. All given magnitudes of space are parts of a greater. Infinitude.\n5. Unity, hence pure intuition, and not a concept of the understanding.\nB. Time.\nAbsolute space and time, or adhering space and time.\n1. Time is nothing real. Clarke held it to be real as pure time; Leibniz held it to be an empirical concept of succession.\n2. Is there an empty time before the world and in the world? That is, are two different states separated by a time which is not filled by a continuous series of changes? The moment of time can be filled, but in such a way that no temporal series is designated.\n3. All parts of time are again times. The moment. Continuity.\n4. All given times are parts of a greater time. Infinity.\n5. Unity of time. Concept of the all [*Allbegrif*].\nDialectic of the understanding.\nTranscendental doctrine of magnitude.\n(g Continuity of space and time and of all magnitudes.)\nSpace and time do not consist of simple parts (g their parts are themselves magnitudes), that is, of absolute units: continuity.\nThe magnitude of a thing in space and time is continuous.\nThe magnitude of a ground is entitled degree: intensive, extensive, protensive.\nDetermination of magnitude through number and a given unit (and likewise the magnitude of the latter).\nThe infinite is greater than every number. (g Allness or totality (the all) cannot be understood in series, nor comprehended in the aggregate.)\nInfinite of continuation, or of taking together. (g Infinitely small of composition or decomposition.) Where the former is the condition, the latter does not obtain.\nInfinite space and an infinite time that has elapsed are incomprehensible.\nIn the world, extent, progress and division go on to infinity.\nWhence is mathematics demonstrative? Because it is [Vernunfterk] knowledge in intuition a priori.\nWhat are the limits (g of [demo] mathematical knowledge)? That which can be represented a priori in intuition: hence space and time and change in time.\nP. II:\nThe perfection of a thing (g *in sensu* [substantivo] *adiectivo* [in the adjectival sense]) is the totality of the manifold that is [zu e] requisite to constitute a thing. A perfection (*in sensu substantivo, transscendentali* [in the substantive, transcendental sense]) is reality.\nTranscendental doctrine of appearances.\nReality and negation.\n[Is there a vac]\n(g The sum-total of appearances. *In mundo non datur* [In the world there is no]\n*saltus* [leap], — — — —\n*hiatus* [gap], — — —\n*abyssus nihili* [abyss of nothing].)\n(g All parts of space and time are again times. Only in time does everything pass from nothing into something.)\n(g There is nothing simple in appearance, and consequently no immediate transition from a determinate state (not its limit) into another.)\n(g There is no empty time between two states. Change is only the continuation of the progress.)\n(g Every appearance again consists of appearances; no sensation is simple. [All] Appearance, however, does not consist of nothing and appearance, hence not of the empty and the full.)\nOf the ideality and reality of appearance in general.\nAll spaces and times are parts of a greater.\nAll parts of space and time are themselves spaces.\n––––––––––\nIn the propositions about the infinite of extension and of division, philosophy quibbles with mathematics. In those about the empty and the full, the quibbling breaks off.\nSpace and time belong only to appearances, and so to the world, and not outside the world.\nA *hiatus*, a chasm, is a lack of connection among appearances, where the transition between them is missing. Appearances are made possible through space; the emptiness of space is no chasm, and belongs also to the determination of sensible intuition.\nThe proposition that all change is continuous abolishes the ceasing to be of a substance.\nThe proposition that, if a change in the whole of appearance were to cease, it would never begin again; likewise that every part of the appearances is implicated with the rest and is changed along with them through their changes, so that every thing is constantly changed. (g Beginning belongs to appearance, origin to the idea of the understanding.)\n––––––––––\nTranscendental doctrine of experience.\n(g Antithesis: there is absolutely no [substance, but everything is appearances].)\n1. Something as substance, that is, matter, neither arises nor perishes; from nothing, nothing comes, that is, matter is eternal (g *ex nihilo nihil (in mundo) fit* [from nothing, nothing (in the world) comes to be]), though dependent.\n2. Every state of the world is a consequence, for given the continuity of change everything is in process of beginning and ceasing, and both have a cause.\nAntithesis. For there would then be no first beginning.\n3. All appearances together make up one world, and [nothing is isolated. Firstly, there is one world, that is, a whole of substance] belong to actual objects (against idealism). God as cause does not belong to the world. For only through the agreement of the representations with objects do they agree among themselves and obtain the unity which perceptions must have if they are to be appearances.\nOn n: 2. What happens has a ground; that is, it is determined by something according to a rule. Unity of [simultaneity] succession. For |\nwithout this rule unity of experience would not be possible. From this it follows that in the world the sequence of appearances has no beginning at all. Whether the world itself has an origin, however, belongs to intellectual concepts.\n===NOTES===\n- Brackets, (g ...) insertion marks and the sign | are carried over as in the German; the bracketed words are the Academy's deleted or doubtful readings, translated only so far as to show what they are. [Vernunfterk], [demo] and [zu e] are abbreviated or broken-off words and are left in the German.\n- Latin sentences (Hobbes, Descartes, Clarke, Newton) are rendered into English; the technical terms (*phantasma*, *sensorium*, *vacuum mundanum et extramundanum*, *hiatus*, *saltus*, *abyssus nihili*, *in sensu substantivo*) are kept in Latin.\n- \"Allbegrif\" (B.5) is a rare compound, rendered 'concept of the all' with the German added; \"Größe eines Grundes heißt Grad\" is translated as it stands, though 'Grund' may be a slip for 'Grad' or for some other term, and the reading is doubtful.\n- \"Alle Theile des Raumes und Zeit sind wiederum Zeiten\" (P. II) is rendered literally, although 'Räume' would be expected for space. 'Veränderung' is rendered 'change' (or 'alteration' in a single place), 'Anfang' 'beginning', and 'Ursprung' 'origin'.\n- \"chicanirt\" and \"chican bricht ab\" are rendered 'quibbles' and 'the quibbling breaks off'; the sense is that the philosophers' disputes are drawn into mathematics in the first case but not in the second.\n- The sentence beginning \"Der Satz, daß, wenn eine Veranderung ... aufhörete\" has no main verb. It is a note-fragment, and I have not completed it.\n- In the final item, 'ist wodurch bestimmt nach einer Regel' is smoothed to 'is determined by something according to a rule'. The text breaks off at the page division (|) and is left as it stands.\n- In 'Es ist eine Welt d.i. ein Ganzes aus substantz', 'substantz' is left in the singular as in the source. 'Zu n:' is rendered 'On n:' as a locus note.",[]],"reflm:4757":["Page I:\nThe principles of the possibility of experiences (of distributive unity) are at the same time principles of the possibility of the objects of ex|perience.\n(g appearance) (g of the experiences.)\n1. (g Unity) Of intuition, 2. of the giving or existence of appearances.\nImmanent (g space and time are conditions of appearance) or transcendent (g they are not) principles.\nThe former belong to the empirical use [of understanding], the latter to the pure use of reason. Agreement of reason with itself in the whole.\nThe former needs (g has) no first *a priori*, but only *a posteriori*, and from there *progressus* or *regressus in infinitum*.\nImmanent principles of the empirical use of understanding:\n1. There is no limit to the composition and decomposition of appearances.\n2. There is no first ground or first beginning.\n3. Everything is mutable and variable, hence empirically (*empirice*) contingent, because time is in itself necessary, but nothing necessary attaches to time.\nTranscendent principles of the pure use of understanding:\n1. There is a first part, namely the simple as *principium* of composition, and these are limits of all appearance taken together.\n2. There is an absolute spontaneity, transcendental freedom.\n3. There is something necessary in itself, namely the unity of the highest reality, in which the whole manifold of possibilities can be determined through limits |\nas the shapes are in space, and (g whereby) also all existence, as every state is in time.\nBecause space and time are only conditions of appearance, there must be a *principium* of the unity of pure reason, whereby knowledge is determined irrespective of appearance.\nGround of the antithetic, or of the apparent antinomy of pure reason.\nThe former [ge] are principles of the exposition of appearances, the latter of the spontaneity of pure reason. Agreements with itself in the whole, hence also of morality.\nPage II:\nWe must have principles of original unity, or of the systematic unity of our modes of knowledge, i.e. of the spontaneity [their] of the same, so far as we act independently and wish to [determine] the appearances themselves practically, or to determine ourselves originally (*originarie*) among appearances.\nThey are principles of the self-determination of reason [of the unity of actions which]\nor of the unity of the whole of our determinations of reason.\nFor reason all possible modes of knowledge make up a whole; hence the synthesis of absolute unity is the condition of reason.\n––––––––––\n(g The soul in the appearances is one substance with the body; it appears only as entelechy of the body. It is not in community with the body but in union with it, and in no relation of place to the body. For it is intelligible, whereas the body is merely *phaenomenon*. But there is no relation of *noumenon* to *phaenomenon*.\n––––––––––\nDialectic.\nRules.\n1. What does not belong to appearances at all is not to be judged by rules of appearance, e.g. God by space and time.\n2. What does not belong to outer appearance, e.g. spirit, is not to be subjected to the conditions of such appearance.\n3. What cannot be comprehended (g and what cannot be represented in intuition), the totality of the infinite or of infinite division, is not to be held impossible. The infinity of the series, the [infinity of] finitude [without] of the derived, without the *substratum originarium*.\nFurther, the principles of the absolute unity of reason are not to be confounded with those of empirical unity.\na. Simplicity of the thinking subject.\nb. Freedom as [principle] condition of [actions] rational actions.\nc. [Infinite] *Ens* [*infinitum*] *originarium* as *substratum* of all combination of its representations in a whole.\n[d Real unrestricted totality of the same, not with]\nd. Restriction of the world in respect of origin and content is not to be confounded with bounding.\n––––––––––\nPrinciples of reason are those which contain the conditions of the [determinations] unity of our knowledge, so far as it is determinable [under] *a priori*; consequently only those which contain the completeness of speculative knowledge *a priori*, which agrees with the ideas of the practical *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4758":["Page I:\nThe principles [of the synthetic use] of the synthesis of pure reason [*a priori*] in general are all metaphysical.\nThose of the synthetic use of reason in respect of intuitions which can be given in experience are princi|ples\nof the empirical or physical use. Those in respect of intuitions which cannot be given *a posteriori* are principles of the hyperphysical [use] or transcendent use. (The former are mathematical, the second dynamical.)\nThe principles of the hyperphysical use relate to the collective universality of synthesis, those of the physical use to the distributive.\n1. Principle. The principles of the possibility of experiences are also principles of the possibility of the objects of experience. Example.\n2. Principle: In everything that passes beyond the limit of experience we can assume only principles of the [synthesis] absolute unity of synthesis *a priori* (g i.e. of the unity of the use of reason *a priori*).\n––––––––––\n1. Mathematical principles *a priori* and their possibility. Evidence.\n2. Metaphysical principles of mathematical synthesis in general. Infinity of the synthesis of intuition, in respect both of composition (g progressive) and of decomposition.\nNo other synthesis of appearances takes place than with appearances, consequently one that is empirically possible, hence not with the empty.\nA. Dynamical principles.\nMathematical and dynamical principles of the possibility of experiences*: the former of intuitions, the latter of the relation to apperception, i.e. to existence.\n*(g All appearances stand under rules of a dynamical unity, in order that experiences may come about.)\nMathematics deals with nothing but what can be given *a priori* in intuition. Not with reality etc. Not with existence.\nObject and the existence of it.\nThe transcendent principles are principles of the subjective unity of knowledge through reason, i.e. of the agreement of reason with itself.\nObjective principles are principles of a possible empirical use.\nPage II:\nEverything actual must be capable of being known *a priori* (Possible) and must also be given or determined *a priori* (Necessary).\n––––––––––\nThere must be two kinds of principles of unity (g *a priori*): unity of the intellection of appearances *a priori*, so far as we are determined through them; and unity of the spontaneity of the understanding, so far as [it itself and] the appearances are determined through it.\nThe unity of relation to the whole of knowledge, and hence principles of the totality of synthesis and of the *terminus a priori*, the first, the outermost (for what is given in intuition is the *terminus a posteriori*), and hence the synthesis of composition and not merely of decomposition, are principles not of the empirical use of reason in respect of appearances, but of its architectonic and pure use. The simple (g of substance). The spontaneity of action, the [original] primal being, the universal cause, are the cardinal concepts upon which the unity of the [whole in] use of reason in the whole rests.\nThe understanding itself (a being that has understanding) is simple. It is substance. It is transcendentally free. It is affected through sensibility (space), and is in community with others. All its objects make up one (composite) whole, which is entitled world (unity of space). The totality of appearances is immeasurable, but limited, and limits. The whole is contingent or dependent. Everything is grounded on an original understanding, which is the all-sufficient ground [of] the world.\nThe (g necessary) unity of time and space is transformed into the necessary unity of a primal being, the immeasurability of the former into the all-sufficiency of the latter. The beginning (g of the world) in time into the origin of it. The divisibility of appearances into the simple. This breaks off.\nUnity of reason. Unity of the self-determination of reason in respect of the manifold of the unity of rules or principles. Not of exposition, i.e. of the analytic unity of appearances, but of determination (comprehension), i.e. of the synthetic unity, whereby |\nthe manifold, as given in general (not merely to the senses), necessarily has unity.",[]],"reflm:4759":["P. I:\nOf the principles of empirical [exposition] *intellection*, in distinction from the principles of *comprehension*.\nHow the dogmatic enemy of religion and the dogmatic religious speculator can both be deceived through an apparent antithetic, and so through a feigned sceptic.\nHow this scepticism itself can be refuted through the dogmatic principles [of the positive] in respect of the practical use of reason.\nHow, from the principles of the unity of reason in respect of totality, one can derive principles which conflict with the [conditions] principles of determination in appearances (which must always be partial and conditioned), but which must be regarded as belonging to knowledge as a whole.\nThere is a *synthesis prototypa* and an *ectypa*: the former is that of self-determination *a [priori] termino a priori, non empirice dato*, from nothing; the latter is *a termino a posteriori*; the former *simpliciter*, the latter *secundum qvid*. I am, I act, I together with everything manifold, I in so far as I exclude the manifold. The principles of the [s] absolute synthesis are rational and are conditions of practical knowledge a priori.\n––––––––––\n1. Principles of intuition. [Of] That they are conditions of intuition, not of things.\n2. Of the understanding, e.g. *principium rationis*.\n3. Of reason.\n––––––––––\nReason proceeds from the universal to the particular [or from the], the understanding from the particular to the universal. The latter universal is such only *secundum qvid*, and belongs to the empirical or physical use of reason. The former is absolute, and belongs to the free or metaphysical use; likewise to the moral.\nP. II:\n(g Exposition and [comprehension] rationality.)\n(g On appearances.) (g Unity of experience and unity of reason.)\nPrinciples of the exposition of appearances presuppose [them] these all together as conditioned, and hence nothing as posited absolutely.\n1.\nNo absolute totality (g *totalitas secundum qvid*) [of the synthesis] of composition, and hence the [*regressus* infinite] *progressus* is infinite.\n(g Unconditioned limitation *in mundo phaenomeno*. *Inter phaenomena non datur universitas absoluta*.)\n2.\nNo absolute [division of] totality of decomposition, and hence nothing unconditionally simple.\n(g *Non simplicitas absoluta*.)\n(g The infinite progress cannot be comprehended, and the unconditioned cannot be made intuitive.)\n3.\nNo absolute totality of the series of generation, no unconditioned spontaneity.\n(g *Non causalitas absoluta*.)\n4.\nNo unconditioned necessity. All things can be removed from time and space.\n(g *Non necessitas absoluta*.)\n(g World in the physical sense\nThe simple —\n—\n—\nSpontaneity\n—\n—\nNecessity\n—\n—)\nAll these propositions are certain (g objectively) as principles of the empirical use, but are contrary to reason.\n(g On things in general.)\nPrinciples of rationality or *comprehension* (g of the same). From the universal to the particular: absolute synthesis.\n1. Unconditioned All of the (g dependent) whole. Origin of the world.\n(g *In mundo noumeno datur universitas*.)\n2. The unconditionally simple.\n(g *Monas*.)\n3. Unconditioned spontaneity of action.\n(g *Libertas transscendentalis*.)\n4. Unconditionally necessary existence.\n(g *Necessitas absoluta originaria*.)\n(g World in the metaphysical sense\nSimple —\n—\n—\n—)\nThese propositions are subjectively necessary as principles of the use of reason in the whole of knowledge: unity of the whole of the manifold of the knowledge of the understanding. They are practically necessary in respect of [text breaks off].\nSubjective conflict between the partiality of sensibility and the totality of reason in the determination of modes of knowledge:\nof the conditions of the empirical use in the exposition of appearances,\nof the conditions of the rational use in the *comprehension* of appearances.\n[The empirical canons] The canon of the empirical use aims at *universalitas* (g of appearances), that of the rational use at the *universitas* of things.\nWhat cannot be determined in the appearance is not on that account impossible.\nThere must be principles of the self-determination of reason, which are distinguished from those in which reason is determined through appearances and their conditions. These are principles of the unity of modes of knowledge in the whole, and hence of a unity that is not partial but total.",[]],"reflm:4760":["P. I:\nThe ground of the antinomy of reason is the conflict: 1. All empirical synthesis is conditioned, the mathematical as well as the dynamical. A. Every appearance has parts and is itself a part. B. Everything that happens is a consequence (g that exists, is conditioned) and is itself a ground. There is therefore no first and no last: nothing simple, no limit of magnitude, no first ground, no necessary being. That is, we cannot arrive at these in the appearances (g and must not appeal to them). Over against this, 2. the transcendental synthesis (g through pure concepts of reason) is unconditioned, but also takes place through mere intellectual concepts; hence there is really no antinomy. The world is limited. It consists of the simple. There is freedom. There is a necessary being. [Ground of this synthesis] Ground of these principles: the unity of the entire use of reason, in that it has collective unity.\n1. Infinite (of addition (g composition) and of division (g decomposition)). (g Finite.) 2. Infinite of derivation. 3. Infinite of contingency.\n––––––––––\nThe principles of the possibility of experiences extend [text breaks off].\n––––––––––\nThe synthesis of the parts and of the whole [are] is in empirical knowledge always conditioned, and so too that of effects and causes (substantial) and of the contingent. For these concern the unity of appearances, where the manifold is given sooner than the unity. Determinable unity: where the unity rests on the rule of the *regressus*. On the other hand, the unity of the pure use of reason (simple, free, necessary) is determining, and is a *progressus* which begins from the condition a priori, e.g. from freedom in morals. These conditions do not belong to the field of appearances, and make synthesis a priori in general possible.\nWhat can never become an object of our senses is absolutely [impossible] impossible as appearance, and consequently there is also no empty space. That something may occur in the appearance which is regarded as a consequence of empty space is, however, quite possible.\nP. II:\n(g Only the conditions of the empirical synthesis are objective.)\n[Principle of the]\nSceptical principle of the *comprehension* of appearances.\nThere is nothing absolutely first in the synthesis of appearances (because all of it together is conditioned).\n(g No absolute limit)\n1. No first of the aggregate in space and time. (The most absolute therein is that whose limit is determined by nothing. (g The limit can be determined only among appearances.) Empirical synthesis is always conditioned.) That is, the whole of appearances is a priori unbounded. Totality can therefore not be determined through successive addition.\n2. No absolutely first of composition (no absolute limit of division) (nothing simple), no simple part [of what in the Re] of the extended or of alteration.\n3. No absolute limit of subordination [of consequences and grounds. No] of the sequence of actions and [consequences] effects. No first action. No transcendental freedom.\n4. No first cause (no original being).\nFor all appearance is possible only in space and time. Time (g and space), however, is determinable only through appearance; and it is without a first.\nThese propositions are contradicted only by (g which, however, does not contradict the exposition of appearances) the incomprehensibility of the propositions: there is a *regressus in infinitum* (from the empirical *terminus* onward) of dimension, of division, of [sub Entst] generation, and of dependence. With experiences we always remain within the chain of appearances.\nSo far, however, as the things lying at the ground of appearances are taken, which consequently are thought only through concepts of the understanding, the unity of their synthesis requires an absolutely first (g of the inner state of reason) (i.e. unconditioned) [of the synthesis of totality, of the] of origin, of composition, of action, of existence in general. These are conditions of the (subjective) unity in the use of reason in respect of appearances, just as those are principles of the manifold. In the former there is sameness of the condition; in the latter, unconditioned unity.",[]],"reflm:4761":["P. I:\nProof of a future life from the analogy of nature, with regard to the purposive determination of its predispositions.\n*evolution* [development].\nThe merely moral proof holds only for some human beings and only for a certain point of time of future existence, namely [for those who] have offended (g children, savages, the dull-witted) — and only until they have been punished |\n—. The (g physico-) teleological proof, on the other hand, holds for all, because the predispositions are in the whole of human nature, and because their development and practical use prepare similar consequences to infinity.\nEstimation of our knowledge. The proof has practical (g positive) sufficiency, and the metaphysical one negative sufficiency, with regard to the dogmatic presumption of the opponents.\nOn the *hypothesis metaphysica* [metaphysical hypothesis]*\nOn\npneumatic\nlife problematic.\n—\n—\n—\n*commercio* [commerce]\n—\n— —\n—\nAppearances (Erscheinungen) (g apparitions)\nThe latter [are] not to be denied dogmatically, but [there are] principles of the understanding's reluctance toward each individual account. Maxim of reason. Unity. *Miracula comparativa* [comparative miracles] are worse than *rigorosa* [strict ones]. *Ortus phaenomenorum univocus — aeqvivocus* [univocal — equivocal origin of appearances]. No laws.\n* We do not know whence the whole appearance of the corporeal world, and of our own [body], comes; but one cannot regard it as necessary.\nPractical sufficiency is that which suffices to connect, through reason, the incentive with moral laws, namely the *effectus a lege determinati* [effects determined by the law].*\n*(g From experience one cannot infer the existence of God in the strict sense.)\n(g Fatalism.\nCosmological proof from the relation of the contingent to a will.)\n––––––––––\n(g Sum)\nThe Wolffian proof infers from an existence in general, thus cosmologically, to the existence of an *ens realissimum* [most real being]; it is properly an ontological proof that something necessary must be *realissimum*.\nThe divine will is intellectual, self-sufficient and inde|pendent.\nWithout affect. Repentance, wrath (g *tamen iustitia punitiva* [yet punitive justice], not itself revenge). Compassion.\n*Voluntas antecedens* [antecedent will] toward the universal good, *consequens* [consequent] (decretum [decree]) toward [conseq] the individual, *possunt a se differre et absqve successione esse* [can differ from one another and be without succession].\n(g The *providentia generalis* [general providence] is natural, the *specialis* [special] supernatural; the latter extends to individuals, but the extraordinary direction to individual events.)\n*Beatitudo divina* [divine blessedness].\n*Caussalitas libera mundi qvoad substantiam est creatio* [The free causality of the world as regards substance is creation].\nIn the world there are beginnings, but not a beginning of the world. The world has always been. [God has created an eternal world, but not from E] Empty time before the world. The decree is not in time.\nThe cause\nof the existence of the world is\nCreation,\n—\n—\nof [its] duration — — —\nPreservation.\nNo world can [itself] create something [which to it s]. *Mundi partes omnes sunt res creatae* [All parts of the world are created things]. For otherwise they would depend externally on themselves.\nThe divine will directed to the particular in the world,\nso far as it belongs to creation, is\nProvidence,\nto preservation\n—\n—\nGovernance.\nP. II:\nNature (g simply) or the whole\nof appearances,\n— —\n[contingent] limited things,\n— —\ncontingent things.\n(g *Mundus* [world]. *Essentia compositi et mutui commercii semper est derivativa. ens derivativum* [The essence of the composite and of mutual commerce is always derivative; a derivative being].)\nIt refers necessarily to God as the original, non-derived being. (everything composite [is] is (g dependent) contingent, derived (g limited).)\n1. According to the mere concept.\n2. According to existence.\nAbsolute completeness [and], unity (g and necessity) of the original being. A being above nature and world.\nIt cannot properly be known through predicates of nature, but [only] by analogy.\n*Theologia vel rationalis vel revelata. Prior vel Transscendentalis vel naturalis. Posterior vel Metaphysica vel Physicotheologia vel Moralis theologia* [Theology is either rational or revealed. The former is either transcendental or natural. The latter is either metaphysical, or physicotheology, or moral theology].\n*Transscendentalis* — *Deista* [deist].\n*Naturalis* — *Theista* [theist]; one who denies revelation: *Naturalista* [naturalist]. [Deum] One who calls the world God *est Atheista* [is an atheist] (g *explicite* [explicitly]). *Deista est Atheus cryptice talis* [The deist is an atheist who is such in a concealed way].\nThe concept of a thing, so far as it is negatively determined, is always a derived concept, because it agrees only with a part of some other concept different from it. Of different things one must necessarily be negatively determined, and so its concept must be derived. There is therefore only a single being whose concept is a *conceptus originarius* [original concept]; the concepts of other things are only limitations of it. One always asks: whence then does this or that thing have just these limits?\nIn it they are all determined as to substance. From it originally springs every state. In it they are all in *commercio* [in commerce].\n––––––––––\n( g (g primus,) motor [first mover]. The highest being, the highest intelligence, the highest good. )\n(g Emanation or creation. Intell[ectual]: analogy (g not anthropomorphism).\nUnderstanding. Feeling. Will.\nForesight. Intimate presence.\nNot *mundi causa bruta* [brute cause of the world] (genitor [begetter]), but *libera* [free] (autor [author]).)\nThe transcendental concept of things, i.e. of things as beings in general, [hat ih] has, for knowledge of their difference, a fundamental concept of an *ens realissimum*, in which they are all determinable.",[]],"reflm:4762":["P. I:\n(g 1.) *Realitas* (reality) (s partly real, partly negative; negation presupposes reality; metaphysical good, *bonum metaphysicum*). *Negatio* (negation). *Realitas cryptica* (g *apparens*).\n2. *Unitas* (unity) [or] mathematical (of aggregating) or qualitative (of conceiving) (g of connecting) (s of subordinating). E.g. the unity of a system, of a speech, of a machine, of a commonwealth. *Multitudo* (multitude). [Def] *Indefinitum*. *Numerus* (number). *Infinitum*. *Quantum* (g *assignabile*). *Quantitas*. *Maius*. *Minus*. *Aequale*. *Mensura*. (*Continuum*, *Discretum*). *Limes*. (g *terminus*.) [*Constructio*] *Constructio* (g of intuition, or of magnitude in general). Arithmetic. Geometry. *Evidentia*. (*Mathesis*: a rational science, from constructions of concepts, so that it has quantity as its object.) [*Ens limitatum Illimitatum*]. *Perfectio formalis*. *Ens perfectissimum transsc.*: *Ens limitatum, illimitatum* (s the greatest, all-sufficient), *infinitum, reale*. (s Possibility, perfection, order, unity.) *Quantum* (s either *dabile* or) *assignabile*. (s The *omni assignabile* greater or smaller than every assignable.)\n*Maius, Minus.* (s Not given in space.) *Maximum, Minimum.* *Infinitum simultaneum, successivum addendi et comprehendendi* (the simultaneous and the successive infinite, of adding and of comprehending). (s The infinitely small (*infinitum divisionis*).)\nUnity as the manner of determining something: the unity;\n— — the determination itself: a unity.\nUnity, perfection, truth, possibility: either *praedicabilis* or *praedicamentalis*. *Unitas* either of determining or determined [or the unity of determination and the determining unity]. (s *Unitas determinans*, *unitas determinata*.)\nThere is no *quantum assignabile infinitum et infinite parvum* (no assignable quantum that is infinite or infinitely small).\nSomething can be greater and smaller if, besides the smaller, it contains something more, though of a different kind.\nIn space there is already a simultaneity; it is therefore an *infini|tum comprehensionis*, but we cannot think it, because our concept is an *infinitum successionis*.\nThe concepts of the maximum and of the infinite are not the same (s the greatest number is impossible, and the greatest multitude is infinite, but not so number).\nAn irrational is a [number] magnitude that is congruent with no number, but is either greater or smaller than any given one.\nThere is a natural use of our pure reason, but this use is dialectical. To set it within limits there is required a discipline of pure reason, and after this a canon. But beforehand, in transcendental logic, the analytic of the fundamental concepts and of the principles; the extent and limits of knowledge a priori.\n*Compositum et simplex* (the composite and the simple).\nUnity of taking together and of connection.\n*Phaenomena in spatio et tempore sunt quanta continua* (appearances in space and time are continuous quanta). The appearance in space is the extended; the appearance in time is change.\nP. II:\nWhat (g position which) is not a *quantum* is simple (g absolute unity).\nThat in which there is no composition is simple.\n*Causa ponens vel tollens* (cause positing or removing).\n–––––––––––\n(g Here we speak of nothing but real relations, hence of relations in existence.)\n(g If I leave out everything that is entitled inherence, there remains only that whose existence is subsistence, i.e. the substantial.)\nCategories of relation (s determination): 1. of the subject, 2. of the principle, 3. of the composite (of real things (g not of logical or ideal ones)). [a. subsistence and inherence. b. of cause and effect. c. of the parts and the whole] (g Inclusion, sequence and accompaniment.) (s Threefold unity:) a. relation of inherence (g subsistence), b. dependence (g causality), c. of community (*commercium*) (g *reciprocatio*).\n(g Those whose appearances include one another (g whose representation always [is] a part of the representation of another) (g e.g. of the rainbow and the rain), follow one another and accompany one another.)\n*Praedicabilia* are: (s A. The substantial and accidents (*substratum*).) A and B. *Agere*. *Pati*. *Vis, facultas, receptivitas*. A, B, and C. *Compositum substantiale*. *Compartes*. *Actio et reactio*. *Simplex substantia*.\n*Praedicabilia* of appearances. A substance, as appearance, is the abiding and constant, in whose [concept] representation that of the accidents is always included. An accident is the variable.\nA cause, as appearance, is that on which certain others follow according to a rule.\n*Compositum* ∗(g substantiale), [*quod*] in which (insofar as it is composite) the states of several are at every time simultaneous.\n∗(g *Commercium* (g reciprocal action). Insofar as [determ] it is determinable only simultaneously. Relation of the parts to one another, of the parts to the whole; whether they are simple, absolute unity.)\nTherefore through these categories appearances (g mode of composition) are subjected, as regards [existentiam constanti] existence, i.e. of experience, to perpetual laws, just as, as regards intuition, to [laws] rules, and as regards concepts, to founding norms.\nOn change.\n––––––––––\nIn all categories the relation is heteronomous, in that of the composite homonomous.\n(g The relation to perception (g given), to thinking and [sensing] determining a priori.\nAre not predicates.\nThing, non-thing. Essence, attributes. *Ens necessarium*. | Absolute, hypothetical.)\n( g analytic principles of possibility, synthetic — )\n––––––––––\nDescartes's false definition of substance.\n––––––––––\nOn causality. 1. Ground and consequence (real relation). 2. Ground of existence. Cause. 3. Substance as cause. *Causa afficientiae*.\nThe relation (*respectus*) of this substance to the accidents as principle is force. The causality of the force with regard to the accidents: action. The dependence of the accidents on the force of another: suffering. The possibility of acting: *facultas*; of suffering: *receptivitas*.\nThe principles of the exposition of appearances are principles of intellection, not of insight into them: to seek causes but not to determine them, and in general to determine nothing but the intuition.\n––––––––––\nThe ground precedes in position (in the concept of it), although it is simultaneous with the consequence. I first light the candle, and then it gives light, although, when it burns, it gives light at the same time. The adjacency of the ground to the consequence is simultaneous, just as the initial moment is simultaneous with the year, and yet precedes it.",[]],"reflm:4763":["P. II:\nEverything transient is a contingent thing, but not what is changeable.",[]],"reflm:4764":["P. II:\nThe multitude that is not a part (infinite). The unity that is not a multitude (simple). A ground that is not a consequence.",[]],"reflm:4765":["P. II:\nOn the principles [a pr] of the pure understanding.\nAll appearances have a determinate magnitude (whose relation to another is assignable).\nThe infinite does not appear as such, nor does the simple.\nFor appearances are enclosed between two limits (points) and are therefore themselves determinate magnitudes.\nSophistical principle (from subjective grounds of reason): [All possible magnitudes are] No object of intuition (g that is to say: no appearance) is infinitely great and infinitely divisible, i.e. everything has limits of extension and of division.\nThe totality in the case of the infinite and of the parts of the finite (infinitely divisible) is impossible for human understanding, but not in itself.\n––––––––––\nAll appearances are real and negation. Sophistical: All reality must be sensation.\n––––––––––\nEverything that is (g insofar as it is) is substance and accidents; insofar as it happens (g follows): a consequence from a ground; insofar as it is simultaneous: composed in a whole and [is] reciprocally determined.\nSophistical: [All substance is necessary] [Everything changeable is contingent] Everything abiding (g in the appearance) is substance. (determined in itself.) (*phaenomenon substantiatum*.)\nEverything that happens is determined in the series. There is no absolutely first beginning.",[]],"reflm:4766":["Unity (connection) (g agreement), truth (quality), completeness (quantity).",[]],"reflm:4767":["[A The marks] The manifold in a concept can be considered either in relation to the thing itself or to things in general.\nMetaphysical unity is real (substance, ground, whole).\nMetaphysical truth: *respectus ad data* (relation to what is given), if the conditions are given.\nMetaphysical completeness: *omnitudo realitatis* (allness of reality).",[]],"reflm:4768":["Necessity is best divided into the conditioned and the unconditioned; the former again into the internally and the externally conditioned. For the hypothetical is opposed not to the internal but to the categorical.",[]],"reflm:4769":["The necessary without a ground is incomprehensible, but the contingent determination with a ground is just as much so; the former is necessary in every respect, the latter just as contingent.",[]],"reflm:4770":["A concept cannot be altered in respect of what necessarily belongs to it.\nIt is noteworthy that the mark of all action is change, either *actu* or *potentia*: of inner action, inner [change]; of outer action, motion. One sees that position in time and its order nevertheless constitute the condition of all concepts of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:4771":["What is absolutely necessary is unchangeable for this reason, that it is [in ei] not subordinated in a series, and hence another state finds no ground.",[]],"reflm:4772":["At the top of the page:\nFirst, analytic of the categories, without synthetic propositions. Distinction of the pure concepts of the understanding from those applied to appearances (*phaenomena*).",[]],"reflm:4773":["An object is possible only insofar as it contains reality; otherwise the *intuitus* (intuition) is empty, i.e. without object. All objects, insofar as they are thought absolutely, i.e. through understanding, presuppose reality [and negations]. [What all] The sum total of all reality is only a single object; the manifoldness of objects is therefore possible only through the All of reality and its limitation. An *ens originarium* (original being) therefore lies at the ground of the possibility of the manifold.",[]],"reflm:4774":["At the bottom of the page:\nEvery reality borders on nothing on one side. For every sensation fills [the empty] time, and [out of] in the empty time, as having arisen from nothing through continuous position, it is thus to be regarded as a continuous magnitude. Reason begins from the highest reality.",[]],"reflm:4775":["At the bottom of the page:\nIt belongs also to the anticipation of appearance that the intensive magnitude of matter can decrease to infinity, down to empty space.",[]],"reflm:4776":["What does not [*potest*] exist except as the *rationatum* (that which is grounded) of another is dependent (*dependens*); what does not exist except as the *praedicatum* of another is an accident (*accidens*). What exists even though it is not the *rationatum* of another is independent (*independens*); what exists even though it is not the *praedicatum* of another is substance (*substantia*).",[]],"reflm:4777":["Descartes and Spinoza erred by confounding these concepts with one another.",[]],"reflm:4778":["Existence (*existentia*) signifies: 1. an accident [is] to be real; 2. not to belong to pos|sibility\nas a logical predicate, and hence not to be posited through identity nor understood through analysis.\nThat which exists only if something else is posited signifies:\n1. on the side of the predicate, inherence (in logic it is merely competence or agreement),\n2. on the side of the subject, power (*vis*).",[]],"reflm:4779":["At the foot of the page:\nContinuous quantity (*quantitas continua*) arises out of nothing in a time through continuous position.",[]],"reflm:4780":["At the very bottom of the page,\nInfinity is the absolute impossibility of a complete synthesis (not of the completeness of the object) of the composition or decomposition of a given object. The [synthesis of] appearance is infinite, and the division of it proceeds to infinity. This [synthesis concerns both] infinity concerns both the dynamical and the mathematical synthesis. In the intellectual, on the other hand, the synthesis is complete, [and the] but the condition for knowing this completeness in concreto is sensible (a first or outermost). Reason therefore demands independence of the sensible, but the determination of its concept can only be sensible (antinomy). The all-sufficiency of reason, regarded as determining, is, in respect of us, a source of practical laws of our reason, which necessarily presuppose completeness as a hypothesis.",[]],"reflm:4781":["Cosmology is necessary so that, when we take together everything that is composed one thing with another, everything contingent and everything that is a consequence, we may arrive at the absolute, which has no further *respectum dependentiae* (relation of dependence). Hence the consideration of limits also enters here internally (empty space, empty time). Ontology aims at the complete resolution, cosmology at the complete connection. Since now the world is the whole of actual things, which are related to another as their presupposition, we arrive at the concept of the original being, which contains everything completely and absolutely, is related to no thing (g *a parte*) *priori*, but is related to all *a parte posteriori*.",[]],"reflm:4782":["According to the laws of appearance a *regressus in infinitum* is not impossible, but it is in the intellectual subordination (*subordinatio intellectualis*); the former is subjectively impossible to think as a whole. Totality is impossible.",[]],"reflm:4783":["Free actions occur according to a rule* just as natural ones do. But they are not, according to it, to be determined *a priori* like these; both are therefore in accordance with reason, whereas blind fate and blind chance are *qualitates occultae* (occult qualities) and contrary to reason.\n*\n(g There are rules contrary to which actions cannot occur, although they are not to be determined according to these.)",[]],"reflm:4784":["At the top of the page and \nIf something happens, it must have a cause. But the causality of the cause is [as wied] also something that happens; it must therefore in turn have a cause, and so on to infinity. The same holds of the other synthesis.\nAn idea is a concept to which the empirical concept can indeed conform as to a *principium*, but only by approximating to infinity, never adequately (*adaeqvat*).",[]],"reflm:4785":["The egoist and the materialist are directly opposed to one another: the one denies the existence of anything in space, and so of things outside him, and consequently of bodies, and has merely a monadic existence; the materialist denies everything inward in substances. Egoism is more plausible.",[]],"reflm:4786":["All life is derived and conditioned. There is only one original and unconditioned life. All other life depends on the determining cause in time.\nThe first mover (*primus motor*) (s God as a living being), that is, he who gives the first beginning to the series of changeable states [first], is not a mere [blind] necessary nature, but an intelligence acting freely.\nThe life of creatures is the series of changes from an inner *principium*. This must have a beginning. God initiates all life.\n[Life in tr] Motion in the transcendental sense is life.",[]],"reflm:4787":["Opposite the heading »Voluntas Dei«:\nAgainst fatalism (*contra fatalismum*).\nHis freedom can be comprehended most easily, but not the freedom of his creatures.",[]],"reflm:4788":["At the bottom of the page:\nWe can understand and have insight into divine freedom very well, but not human freedom. If man were merely intellectual, we should be able to have insight into his power of choice (*Willkühr*) through reason; if he were a brute (*brutum*), likewise. But as a being both sensible and rational we cannot, because his action is afterwards a *phaenomenon*, but beforehand, under practical laws, a *noumenon*.\n(s The will is the property of a being whereby, through its understanding, it is cause of the objects — the faculty of desire only through its representations.)",[]],"reflm:4789":["When did God create the world? — Not in time, and hence not from eternity. — Yet we must distinguish the *actuatio mundi in initio* (actualization of the world in the beginning) |\nfrom the *actuatio durationis creatio* (actualization of duration). The general point is — it exists through God.",[]],"reflm:4790":["If matter existed originally, then (s matter would maintain itself) the preservation would be directed only to the form, and God could not be regarded completely as the author of all form, since matter cannot be merely passive, and consequently a part of the blame and of the merit must always be ascribed to it. Nor could all form have been originally laid in it; it would have to be produced in it successively; providence alone would therefore not suffice, but also government (*gubernation*), that is, an extraordinary *concursus* added to [Sch] providence.",[]],"reflm:4791":["The actions of freedom are (g externally) *phaenomena* (g internally *noumena*) and so far are not sufficiently determined among the *phaenomena*. On the whole all actions are good (in the intellectual world), although they are predetermined by God.",[]],"reflm:4792":["(g Preservation. Omnipresence.)\nCreation is the production of the world.\nProvidence is the arrangement [of the same of the grounds for the ends] (g in the creation), whereby the states (g following) one upon another become consonant with the divine ends.\nProvision (*Vorsorge*) is providence so far as it concerns the welfare of the creature; providence also extends to punishments.\nGovernment: the preservation (g according to universal laws) of nature, in conformity with the rule of providence.\nGuidance (*Lenkung*) (g direction), *direction*, is the [Ang] action of making the events of the world appropriate to his particular ends.\nPredestination (*praedestination*) (predetermination): the prior ordaining of the determining grounds for single ends, especially free actions.\nCooperation (*Mitwirkung*): the immediate supplementing of the natural powers for the fulfilment of single ends.\nAs creation is related to preservation in a causal connection (*nexu causali*), so is providence related to government in a final connection (*nexu finali*).",[]],"reflm:4793":["Providence is the [Besti] establishment of the order of nature.\nGovernment is the cause of the course of nature,\n*direction*: of single events,\n(g Freedom)\nNot the cause of evil.",[]],"reflm:4794":["The ground determines the consequent. The consequent bears witness to a ground, namely leads us to seek some ground or other.",[]],"reflm:4795":["Logical predicates are\naffirmations or negations,\nreal\n—\ndeterminations —\n—",[]],"reflm:4796":["Logical position: affirmation,\ntranscendental position: reality.\nThe opposition of realities to one another is real.\nThe opposition of realities to negations is logical.",[]],"reflm:4797":["*essentia est principium internum omnium determinationum conceptui entis adhaerentium.* (Essence is the internal principle of all determinations adhering to the concept of a being.)",[]],"reflm:4798":["Through experience we do indeed know empirically how the object is given to us; but we do not know through reason the *principium* of these determinations.",[]],"reflm:4799":["The first complete ground of the logical predicates or of the determinations.\nThe essence of things: substance, or of the determination of the same.",[]],"reflm:4800":["The complete fundamental concept of a thing is its essence.\nA fundamental concept (*Grundbegriff*) is the completion of the subordination of the inner determinations.\nComplete is the completion of the coordination of the inner determinations.\nWe therefore seek the essence because we like to complete everything, and because everything thereby acquires unity, when the manifold is known from one ground and in one whole, in one subject.",[]],"reflm:4801":["Above, beside and \nPossibility is agreement with the conditions of thinking in general; impossible is what conflicts with them. What agrees with the analytic conditions of thinking is logically possible; what agrees with the synthetic is really possible. Logical possibility without real possibility is the empty concept without content, that is, without relation to an object.",[]],"reflm:4802":["Concept,\njudgment (truth) and\ninference.\nPossibility,\nactuality,\nnecessity.\nUnderstanding,\nsense,\nreason.",[]],"reflm:4803":["Understanding,\nintuition,\nreason\nThought,\ngiven*,\ngiven by its being thought.\n*(g whose knowledge depends on the object. The object of empirical knowledge.)",[]],"reflm:4804":["Unity, truth and perfection can be taken formally (*formaliter*) or materially (*materialiter*). In the first case only a single one (*singularis*) is possible, since the manifold fits only one form; in the second case a plurality (*pluralis*) is possible. A constituent (*constitutivum*) of the multitude is taken materially, e.g. a part of something is so far perfect; but if it is the form, it is not a part.",[]],"reflm:4805":["Essential unity (transcendental) (g in concepts, not in the relation of concepts) is: 1. unity of the principle of the manifold (the essence is the *principium*); 2. unity of the agreement of one with another: of the essence and the attributes (*essentiae et attributorum*); 3. unity of the connection and composition into one being.",[]],"reflm:4806":["Thoroughgoing connection of that which belongs to the essence.\n*unitas*\n*Consensus*\n*unicitas*.\nUnity\nUnanimity\nOneness\nTranscendental unity: derivation (g of the manifold) from a concept. Essence.\nTranscendental truth: derivation of the manifold from one another. Attributes.\nTranscendental perfection: derivation of the concept from the manifold.\n(g Unity (g etc etc.) is here taken adjectively, i.e. *formaliter*, not as the concept of an object, but as agreement with the possibility of a concept of the understanding in general. This threefold kind of connection and of formal unity does not belong among the categories, but (s to) the unity of the understanding through the same.)",[]],"reflm:4807":["Unity is the form of the understanding.\nConnection of the manifold with One. Unity: understanding.\nConnection of the manifold with one another. Truth: power of judgment.\nConnection of the manifold into a whole. Perfection: reason.\nKant subsequently turned the thought of this Reflexion somewhat |\notherwise in the four following lines, which date from approximately the same time, and connected the addition with the words »mit Einem« (and thereby with the whole Reflexion) by a sign:\nthrough derivation (g determination) (g of the manifold through one) from One,\nthrough derivation from one another (in One),\nthrough derivation of the One from the manifold.",[]],"reflm:4808":["The rule is not the *propositio* itself, but is only expressed by it.",[]],"reflm:4809":["The rule must be distinguished from its formula. The former is only a universal ground of the use of a faculty, whereby the manifold of that faculty becomes harmonious. Through a rule the manifold in knowledge is brought under unity, is by that very fact comprehended [begriffen], and has something constant.",[]],"reflm:4810":["There are rules of the generation of representations through universal action, or of the comprehension of them together.",[]],"reflm:4811":["The formative power requires a form, the reflecting power a rule; the former for intuiting, the latter for thinking.\nThe rule is the validity of a concept for bringing the manifold to unity in a universal way. The understanding gives rules, reason gives laws.",[]],"reflm:4812":["Rules *a priori* are laws; those *a posteriori* are never without exception.",[]],"reflm:4813":["The unconditioned is either unconditioned necessity or contingency: fate and chance, with regard to events, and a series without a first among things.",[]],"reflm:4814":["Above and \nThe determinations of a changeable thing [are] can (g hypothetically) be necessary (g and are so). [They are not] (A state is not the contradictory opposite.) Another state is not the *oppositum* of the thing.\nBut the changeable thing itself cannot exist (g in itself) necessarily, since otherwise its existence would be determinable only from concepts, and hence only in a single way.",[]],"reflm:4815":["By itself alone nothing changes; pleasure does not transform itself into displeasure.",[]],"reflm:4816":["Changeability of concepts, or of things with regard to them;\nchangeability of things in existence.\nBesides contingency, something more belongs to changeability. The succession of states is [not] change. Persistence of the thing. Arising and perishing is not change.",[]],"reflm:4817":["*Realitas phaenomenon* or *noumenon*. The former is what corresponds in the appearance to perception (g sensation), or to the lack of perception. *Noumenon* is what in the object in itself is *position*.\nEmpty sensible intuition would be *phaenomenon absqve realitate*.",[]],"reflm:4818":["We go through the categories analytically.",[]],"reflm:4819":["Distinction of *quantitas* and *qualitas* in general.\nThe word determination (g predicate) is logical. Likewise the concept of a thing (object), of possible and impossible, is merely logical. Universal negation [or affirmation] *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4820":["Extensive magnitude in that which is simultaneous; protensive in that which is successive; intensive breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4821":["In the *continuum* every part is itself a magnitude. No simple part.",[]],"reflm:4822":["Above and \nAll magnitude is either an aggregated magnitude (g *aggregatum*) (*discretum*) or a continuous magnitude (*continuum*). The latter is the magnitude of every unit, and without it there can also be no aggregated magnitudes, because in all magnitudes the whole and the part must always be homogeneous. The whole must always bear the denomination of the parts, e.g. a *quantum* of money, but not a magnitude of ducats\\*.\n\\*(s A *quantum* which is thought before composition (g as such) is a *continuum*; that which is thought only through composition, where therefore the parts precede, is *discretum*.)",[]],"reflm:4823":["The subject of inherence and the ground of inherence.",[]],"reflm:4824":["We know the substantial only through what abides amid all sorts of determinations.\nEverything in substances that we know is force. *vis primitiva*.",[]],"reflm:4825":["The duty of the philosopher is to treat all force as derivative, yet not to deny that there are primitive forces.",[]],"reflm:4826":["Beside and \nConnection with the category of magnitude.\nSubstance is either absolute unity or in itself multiplicity: *compositum substantiale*.",[]],"reflm:4827":["The reciprocally (g determining) relation of simultaneity is composition; [the things] have a point of time in common, consequently [also a gr] in the understanding, insofar as they are composed, also a ground in common; hence simultaneity is the condition of community.",[]],"reflm:4828":["*Plura coordinata, h.e. se invicem determinantia in uno, faciunt compositum.* (Several coordinated things, that is, things mutually determining one another in one, make a composite.)",[]],"reflm:4829":["The relation of the manifold in a composite is *actio atque reactio sive actio reciproca et passio reciproca* (action and reaction, or reciprocal action and reciprocal passion).",[]],"reflm:4830":["At the lower margin.\nFrom the *phaenomena* one may well arrive at the *substantia comparativa* and *phaenomena substantiata*, but not at the substances themselves. That many monads underlie bodies cannot be inferred at all.",[]],"reflm:4831":["Beside and \nWhether there is a first subject for the senses, a first ground and first part for the senses. The *phaenomena* do not on that account have a first. The universe consists of simple parts, but not therefore bodies.",[]],"reflm:4832":["Physical division is the dislocation of the manifold: displacement. Space is not physically divisible.",[]],"reflm:4833":["*infinitudo progressiva est possibilis, collectiva non item. Totalitas.* (Progressive infinity is possible, collective infinity is not. Totality.)",[]],"reflm:4834":["In and \nWe find no difficulty in the infinity of the form of all magnitudes, namely space and time.",[]],"reflm:4835":["Under and \nThe infinite whole is distinguished from the infinite series.",[]],"reflm:4836":["At the very top of the page.\nA *quantum* is *infinitum* in which an infinite *progressus* is possible. — Thus [infinite] multitude can be regarded as infinite. *infinitum actuale* would be that in which the *progressus* would be total.",[]],"reflm:4837":["Among all sensations there are only two which are the same in all, and [therefore each also to all the rest] are intelligible to everyone: pleasure and displeasure in the inner, and motion or moving force and rest in the outer. Both also serve for metaphysics, the latter for that of nature, the former for that of morals, as the sole empirical element that can be laid at the foundation and to which the remainder of all [laws must reduc] *phaenomena* or principles must be capable of being referred.",[]],"reflm:4838":["Rest is natural to every body, motion is made. Everything, |\nso far as it is natural, is determined according to rules. All things have a nature, and things of the world belong to one all-embracing nature.",[]],"reflm:4839":["The first inner *principium* of everything (g according to a rule) that belongs to the possibility of something is the essence; of what belongs to its actuality, the nature.",[]],"reflm:4840":["157 E II 569.\nM 157':\nThe inner (s universal) *principium* of the *accidentia* (g determinations)\\* according to universal laws is nature. (s Nature and freedom.)\nM 157:\n\\*(s The first inner principle [of one] of everything that belongs to the existence of a thing. The principle of what belongs to existence is called law. Thus existence under laws.\nTo existence belong inner and outer determinations. The principles are substance, cause and *commercium*.\nNature taken substantively (g materially).)",[]],"reflm:4841":["Transcendental theology serves as the norm of the natural; for since in this [natural theology] the concepts are empirical, but the object is not an object of experience, we shall have to distinguish, |\nwhich empirical concepts, and with what restriction or extension, are suited to the original being, the highest being. First: these concepts cannot be abstracted from outer experiences, because these do not give what things are, but how they appear; hence only from inner ones. These predicates will be ascribed to God *per reductionem*, *per eminentiam*, and the rest *per analogiam*. Transcendental theology is thus the norm of the natural, indeed also its origin.",[]],"reflm:4842":["It is disputed whether the ancient peoples had concepts of the morality of the Deity and of a future life that were equally correct [as ours]. These concepts are natural and correct everywhere, though not refined. But their religions have two faults. First, they [the moralit–] laid down the moral law and the worthiness [required of us] so indulgently that [they] could reconcile religion with their own weaknesses. Second, they employed as a means to religion something other than morality, namely natural or prescribed acts of worship. In respect of the first fault the Greeks and Romans above all went astray; in respect of the second, Christians and others. Theology, then, is easy, but the concept of religion is difficult. The heathen worshipped subordinate deities to whom the government of the world was entrusted; the Christians worship the supreme Deity. That was a theological difference, but not a moral one at all, except accidentally or indirectly.\nSocrates and Christ are worlds apart in their morals (g by human or by divine demand). The former demanded morality in a heavenly manner (valid for heaven), the latter in an earthly manner (for men). Yet in Socrates the universal principle is good; only the determination of the particular actions that are contained under the moral |\nprinciple depended on each person's private judgment, and was not precise or pure.\nThe ancients had no concept of holiness, that is, of the moral purity of the heart; their [virtue] morality was virtue, that is, strength, and hence rough.\nChrist wished to improve, not theology, but morality in respect of it, whereby theology itself is also purified.",[]],"reflm:4843":["Wisdom is the relation of one's choice to the whole (g the union of one's aim with the whole) of all ends; consequently wisdom is a moral perfection, not mere natural perfection.\nWisdom is good in itself. Omniscience is not [good] immediately.\nJustice [rewarding] is not rewarding but punishing; thus goodness is limited by wisdom. Goodness is in proportion to morality. [It is] not the justice of revenge, although avenging. [*justitia*] *poenae vel vindicativae vel correctivae* (punishments either vindictive or corrective) (*hae vel animadversiones vel poenae exemplares*: these are either chastisements or exemplary punishments).",[]],"reflm:4844":["Moral theology. 1. (g Practical) proof of the existence of God. 2. Attributes (g so far as they belong to religion (g divine morality)).\nThe All is good (g *universitas*) — the best world, but not for that reason is everything good.\nEvil is also positive, arising from freedom, but together with its punishments it is in turn a part of the good.",[]],"reflm:4845":["The good is also from us; but because it rests on agreement with the order of nature, it is also from God. [This holds] especially because true moral good rests solely on faith in God.\nNot all utilities are at the same time ends, except those that follow according to the order of nature and not from freedom.\nUnconditioned decree in respect of morality.\nFate (*praedestinatio*, predestination) in respect of natural events.\nEvil is not from God, because it rests in freedom, and therefore not in the divine determination. (g It is contrary to the order of nature, and therefore to God.)\nOnly good conduct can entitle us to hope that God will make up [what is lacking] in an extraordinary way.",[]],"reflm:4846":["God is not at all the (g determining) author of our actions, but he is indeed the one who gives occasion for the good ones.",[]],"reflm:4847":["Tetens: *Philosophische Versuche über die menschliche Natur und ihre Entwickelung*, vol. I (Leipzig 1777), p. 19\nRight margin, addition to »Search«, line 2 from the top:\nTucker.",[]],"reflm:4848":["4847. P. 131.\nRight margin, beside the words »man diess letzte« to the end of the paragraph at »genennet werden soll«:\nLeaving out the determinations of an action can produce a general representation prior to all comparison.",[]],"reflm:4849":["P. I [also: XXIII 471,3–473,7]:\nThe purpose of metaphysics: [the restrin–] 1. To establish the origin of synthetic knowledge *a priori*. 2. To understand the restricting conditions of the empirical use of our reason. 3. To show the independence of our reason from these conditions, and hence the possibility of its absolute use. 4. Thereby |\nto extend our use of reason beyond the limits of the sensible world, though only negatively, that is, to remove the hindrance which reason itself (from principles of its empirical use [makes]) makes. 5. To show the condition of the absolute unity of the same [vornem–], so that it may be a [princ–] complete *principium* of practical unity, that is, of harmony with the sum of all ends.\n(g The same principles of extension are in turn negative in respect of the empirical use, where nothing but nature holds.)\nThe dogmatic use of our reason beyond the limit of (g possible) experiences cannot be objectively determining, and no new synthesis takes place. It is only a harmony of theoretical with practical unity, in that the practical use is carried beyond the limits of the pragmatic, and so also beyond the present world, according to the analogy of the empirical use, but in relation to conditions of a complete unity, and thereby our business of reason is completed *a parte priori et posteriori* (from the side of the ground and of the consequence).\n(g The liberation of the unity of reason from the restrictions of its empirical use makes the transcendental use possible.)\nSince the extension of reason is here merely negative, and yet the absolute unity of the knowledge of objects in general and of all their ends (free from all restriction of sensibility) is required for the absolute spontaneity of reason, the extension is practically necessary.\nReason is the faculty of the absolute unity of our modes of knowledge.\nP. II:\nThe principles of the completion of our knowledge, that is, of the (g absolute unity of the use of reason) absolute whole of it, are synthesis of reason.\nThey contain conditions of wisdom, that is, of harmony with the sum of all our ends.\nWe complete only through what is independent, hence not through sensibility.\nThe determination of all objects through mere reason is therefore the completion of our knowledge of the understanding (s in the progress of my existence).\n1. In respect of the self-knowledge of reason. Completion *in progressu*.\na. I belong in a universe.\nb. [I] am simple.\nc. Free. Intelligence.\nd. My existence is not externally dependent (g on the body), nor contingent.\n(s Among empirical principles belongs this: the kind of existence of all things of the world is contingent; only the *ens originarium* (original being) exists in every respect in a necessary way.)\nHere I consider myself not as soul but as intelligence. The synthesis is here merely negative, namely to separate the conditions of sensibility from myself as intelligence.\nAnd the ground of this synthesis is the freedom of reason from the restricting conditions of sensibility, which is a negative *principium* of morality, and so of wisdom.\n2. Completion *in regressu*, from the conditioned to the unconditioned.\nThere is an *ens originarium*,\na. which is all-sufficient and unique,\nb. simple,\nc. a free cause (intelligence),\nd. necessary according to its nature.\nThese are the conditions of the complete unity of all objects, and therefore of [all] modes of knowledge. This unity, however, is the condition of the harmony of everything practical.\nThese modes of knowledge are not dogmatic, but are only a liberation of the (g absolute) unity of the use of reason, in the theoretical and the practical, from the conditions of the empirical use, in order [according to their analogy] to establish principles of the pure practical use.\n––––––––––\nReason is free from the conditions of sensibility, and in the practical must be so. The continuation of the function of reason |\nto complete unity, beyond the restricting conditions of sensibility.\n––––––––––\n(s The concepts of the unity of reason, e.g. of the absolute whole or ground, cannot be represented *in concreto* according to the conditions of empirical knowledge. But neither do they relate to the sensible world, for that is no object of pure reason, but to the world of understanding, which lies at the basis of the former.)",[]],"reflm:4850":["P. I [also: XXIII 488,19–25]:\nThat which makes space the object of the outer senses is matter. That which makes time the object of inner sense is sensation and perception combined with consciousness. Just as the moving forces of matter strive toward the centre of a world-body, so they also act from it outwards, striving upward to the circumference, in that they do not cancel one another but penetrate one another reciprocally.",[]],"reflm:4851":["22. p. I, II:\np. I:\n( g Whether the concepts are merely *educta* or *producta*.* preformation and epigenesis )\n*(g *producta* either through physical (g empirical) influence or through [gel] consciousness of the formal constitution of our sensibility and understanding on the occasion of experience, hence still *producta* a priori, not a posteriori.)\nThe doctrine of *ideae connatae* [*ideis connatis*] leads to enthusiasm [Schwärmerei]. *Acqvisitae* are *acqvisitae* either a priori or a posteriori; the former are not always intellectual. Hence the division of knowledge into sensitive and intellectual is not the primary one; the primary division is into knowledge a priori and a posteriori. The former is either sensible or intellectual.\n(g The study of the object is dogmatic or sceptical; so the study of the subject is either physiological or critical. Critique separates 1. the |\npure from the empirical faculty of knowledge, 2. sensibility from understanding.)\nPlato took all knowledge a priori to be intellectual. So did Leibniz, and thus they did not recognise the sensible element in space and time. Leibniz also explains it as intellectual, but confused.\n––––––––––\nSynthetic knowledge a priori is possible only under the principle that all relation of representations to an object, and determination of the concept of it, is nothing other than the representation of their necessary connection in one consciousness. But representations cannot be connected in one consciousness unless they are [sich] regarded as belonging to a *datum* (as object).\np. II:\n(g Metaphysics of nature and of morals.)\n[See image below table for correct format]\nMetaphysica\n(g Generalis)\n(g Specialis)\ngeneral:\nparticular:\n[to] reason and its concepts\nto from reason\nthemselves constitute the object.\ndifferent\n(g Transcendental philosophy)\nobjects applied.\nCritique of\nOntology\n(g in the world.)\n(g the world itself and\npure\nOf experience\noutside it)\nReason\n(g *physiologia rationalis*)\nIdeas\nimmanent\n(g transcendent)\n*Physica rationalis*\n*Psychologia rationalis*\n*Cosmologia*\n*Theologia*\n(g Method: dogmatic or critical. (s Physiological: Locke.))\n(g with regard to the last, either: how we arrive at the principles and concepts, or: what they contain, and: how they are possible.)\n(g Knowledge is entitled transcendental in respect of its origin, transcendent in respect of the object, which cannot be met with in any experience.)\n(g From empirical principles one cannot arrive at transcendent ones, and yet these are the proper [vorn] end of metaphysics.)\nWhy not empirical psychology?\nThe *conceptus intellectuales* are *acqvisiti* (g but not from the senses). For [die] we acquire the forms of the bodies which we make from clay, hence the bricks, although we take the clay from the earth.\nParts: 1. Ontology, cosmology and *theologia rationalis*; these two: 1. *transscendentalis*, 2. *naturalis*. *Cosmologia naturalis* has for its object objects of the senses, outer or inner; and the principles are empirical or rational.",[]],"reflm:4852":["Philosophy is empirical or rational. The former is physics, the latter *metaphysica*. The latter is either *metaphysica generalis* (transcendental philosophy) or *specialis*.",[]],"reflm:4853":["Metaphysics deals not with objects but with modes of knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4854":["*primum principuum conceptuum est ens, iudiciorum est principium contradictionis.*",[]],"reflm:4855":["All philosophy is either *rationalis* (not logic) or *empirica*. The former: that whose *principia* are not from experience. The *rationalis* is either *pura* or *applicata*. The former is *transscendentalis*, the latter *metaphysica*; or: the former *metaphysica generalis*, the latter *specialis*. The *metaphysica specialis* has as its ground either sensation or feeling. The former is the ground (*materia*) of appearance, the latter of desires. The former is metaphysics of nature, the latter of morals. The former is either *physiologia rationalis* or *theologia naturalis*, *physica generalis* or *cosmologia specialis*, *psychologia rationalis* or *physica rationalis*.",[]],"reflm:4856":["In every relation in which we wish to make something intelligible, both members must be known [sind, sic]. Now the object is [known the] unknown, but sensation is known. Hence it cannot be made intelligible. On the other hand, sensation and the feeling of pleasure are both in ourselves; hence it is determined and intelligible through itself.",[]],"reflm:4857":["Pleasure and displeasure alone constitute the absolute, because they are life itself.",[]],"reflm:4858":["Transcendental philosophy requires first of all critique (g to distinguish it from the empirical). 2. Discipline. 3. Canon. 4. Architectonic.",[]],"reflm:4859":["Origin of transcendental concepts: 1. *per intuitionem mysticam*. 2. (g *influxum*) *sensitivum*. 3. *per praeformationem*. 4. *per epigenesin intellectualem*. (g *intellectualia* intuitive or discursive.)\nThe end of metaphysics is God and a future world.\nEpicurus: nothing a priori.",[]],"reflm:4860":["The imputations against Epicurus, which represent his doctrines as absurd, deserve no credence. The useful purpose of the history of philosophy consists in the presentation of good models and of instructive errors, and likewise in the knowledge of the natural progress of reason from ignorance (not from gross error) to knowledge. If someone tells me very absurd opinions of a person whom I have already recognised as acute by a single proof, I do not believe him. His expression was perhaps incautious or was not understood, just as I do not believe the slanders about a person in whom I have perceived an unsuspicious appearance of uprightness. What good does it do me to know it? It is ugly to play the accuser.",[]],"reflm:4861":["Logic considered as propaedeutic is good for beginners, but considered as architectonic only for masters.",[]],"reflm:4862":["Plato's doctrine of ideas was meant to serve to prevent our seeking from empirical principles what can have its sources and archetype in mere reason, namely true perfection. But to seek out, from things [welche], of that which has served merely as a means, the idea of its constitution (not of ends), is giddy, fanatical.",[]],"reflm:4863":["Organic or spiritual intuiting; the former through the body.\nThat our soul without the body, as spirit, should intuit other things, i.e. externally, is a transgression of the limits of the *datum*. For we know the soul only as the object of inner sense, and the body as the means of outer sense. Our intuiting is physical and not mystical; the physical is not pneumatic but organic.",[]],"reflm:4864":["It is necessary and certain that every true paradox should at the outset meet with resistance, in order to be sifted and tested, and thereafter |\nto be the more durable; just as certain foods must at the outset be distasteful, in order thereafter to please the longer.",[]],"reflm:4865":["Metaphysics is not an organon but a canon of reason: a ground not of doctrine but of discipline, not of dogmatic but of critical knowledge; its use is not to increase modes of knowledge but to keep off errors; it is concerned not with the object but with the rules of the subject; it is not the mother of religion but its bulwark; it is of not objective but subjective use. The important fundamental truths of morals and religion are grounded on the natural use of reason, which is a use according to the analogy of the empirical use and extends a priori and a posteriori to the boundary of the world, so far as it is the boundary, and so up to the bordering, but not beyond it. This natural use is not free from aberrations of speculation; it brings forth belief [Glauben] and not knowledge [Wissen].",[]],"reflm:4866":["M IX:\nLocke, a physiologist of reason, [concerned with] the origin of concepts. He committed the error of taking the occasion for arriving at these concepts, namely experience, to be their source. Nevertheless he made use of them beyond the limit of experience.\nWolff was an artist of reason [Vernunftkünstler]; he made use of it and did not inquire at all into its sources. Dogmatic, not critical.\nLambert analysed reason, but critique is still lacking. Crusius (everything that I can think is possible) assumed innate fundamental propositions (though not Platonic ideas); but since they could perhaps |\nM X: |\nbe only prin|ciples\nof the empirical use of the understanding, he was not sure whether he could also use them beyond the limit of experience.",[]],"reflm:4867":["The use of the understanding is either mystical or logical; the latter metaphysical or physiological. Aristotle and Epicurus.",[]],"reflm:4868":["Plato set forth mystical *intellectualia*, Aristotle logical *intellectualia*; the latter erred in saying that they were also contained in the senses. For the concept of cause never lay in sensible intuition.",[]],"reflm:4869":["Chief rule: that all principles whatever are only of empirical use, and hence that reason has altogether only rules of use in respect of experience.",[]],"reflm:4870":["Whether the use of reason in respect of the boundary-concepts of the world is transcendent? Whence, then, come the transcendent questions?",[]],"reflm:4871":["Despair is either faint-hearted or foolhardy; in the former, appeal is made to sound understanding, either to take metaphysics from it or to throw metaphysics away by means of it; in the second |\nappeal is made to mystical intuition and immediate endowment of the understanding. In scepticism there is no place of rest.",[]],"reflm:4872":["M XII:\nAll analytic judgments have as their ground the principle of identity and contradiction. The synthetic have as many *axiomata* as there are *conceptus synthetici* given through the nature of the understanding (*per intellectus naturam*). These *conceptus synthetici* are the grounds through which the understanding can obtain concepts of things drawn from experience [Wenn], and so the *principia synthetica* hold only in respect of the laws of experience, hence *a posteriori*, and are practically true. *In abstracto*, however, they are valid only subjectively.\nM XI:\n(g Judgments of experience)",[]],"reflm:4873":["In the inner margin, crosswise:\nTranscendental philosophy considers not the objects but the human mind, in respect of the sources from which knowledge *a priori* originates in it, and of its limits. Hence pure mathematics is no part of transcendental philosophy, though the sources from which it springs in the mind certainly are.",[]],"reflm:4874":["In the method of philosophy (empirical), two items as limits. |\nGeneral laws and rules, and the opposite of these, namely differences in the particular. [It] hovers between sense and reason, *a posteriori* and *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4875":["To one who is indifferent in respect of morality one cannot demonstrate the existence of God; and one need not demonstrate it to one who has decided in favour of the moral laws.",[]],"reflm:4876":["Misological maxims give a logical anarchy.",[]],"reflm:4877":["A science of the understanding which concerns objects in general can speak only of the rules of their use [i.e. the understanding's use], whether of use in general or of the use of the pure understanding. In the latter case it is transcendental logic. There are as many transcendental elements (categories) as there are logical moments.",[]],"reflm:4878":["If this work requires favour in order to get through, it may as well be rejected.",[]],"reflm:4879":["Maxim: that there be great manifoldness in the application, that is, in the closer determination, and yet unity of the rule.",[]],"reflm:4880":["The steps taken in metaphysics have hitherto been in vain. Nothing has been discovered in it. Yet one cannot give it up. Subject instead of object.\nOn the incompetence of the understanding in respect of objects that lie beyond the senses.\nOur pure reason is in itself, and by its nature, dialectical.\nPhilosophy of pure reason: 1. subjective, 2. objective part. The former is transcendental philosophy, the consideration of pure reason itself; the latter is knowledge of objects. The latter [is] metaphysics of nature and of morals.",[]],"reflm:4881":["Instinct drives the philosopher to systematic treatises, and this is of great use in the extension of knowledge. Talent confines him to single parts, and this would be of great use for correctness, if only there were a means of uniting and preserving them. The capacities of philosophers differ in this respect. One can consider everything in the large and in general, or extract rules; another better notices the parts and the differences. All want to make systems.",[]],"reflm:4882":["The appearance of conjugation is a subsumption of a given representation under the general faculty of ordering the sensations. The function of this faculty is the concept of the understanding, and the conditions of this function constitute the rules, namely of the transition from one representation to another. Hence nothing can be perceived save under the presupposition that it stands under a rule.\nThe concept of ground is no appearance but a function of the mind, under which everything must be capable of being subsumed, and consequently under rules. Observation is only [ein] that a case is given. Knowledge is the subsumption under the function and its rules.\nIf space were nothing subjective, how should I come to modes of knowledge of it *a priori*? How should this knowledge also fit objects?\nIn the same way, were ground nothing subjective, how should I arrive at it *a priori*?\nHuman reason advances with pleasure in extending its modes of knowledge over the world of sense and the moral world, but upwards it grows dizzy.",[]],"reflm:4883":["*theologia naturalis*. After the analogy of the empirical use of reason.",[]],"reflm:4884":["The correctness of moral propositions can be known independently of theology and of all the sciences.",[]],"reflm:4885":["Metaphysics leads to certainty not in the dogmas of reason but in its maxims.",[]],"reflm:4886":["*dilemma practicum*. The denier of God acts imprudently if he ventures it on the existence of God, or [acts] like a villain.",[]],"reflm:4887":["Bulwark of religion, critique and (g discipline.) canon of knowledge and organon of wisdom. Bulwark of religion.",[]],"reflm:4888":["If one asks, not whether a further proof of the existence of God could be discovered in metaphysics, but whether [the] of beings which are wholly distinct from the world, their nature cannot be discovered, so etc. etc. Otherwise, if one wants nothing but a proof for the already presupposed existence of God, that is easily found. Proofs for moral propositions.",[]],"reflm:4889":["Metaphysics is knowledge of nature *a priori*, whose object is at least given through the senses; transcendental philosophy is pure knowledge *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4890":["Knowledge *a priori* is opposed to the empirical; philosophy concerning it is transcendental philosophy. Everything that we know *a priori* in respect of the objects of experience lies therein.",[]],"reflm:4891":["The more recent philosophers have thought that, because they can make quite good use of their powers without knowing them and the laws from which they come (e.g. in mathematics), so it will also be in respect of the first principles of reason, in respect of quality. (One born blind who has seen a yellow piece of money can represent a hundred to himself, but not yet a white one.)",[]],"reflm:4892":["The chief point is that, before one ventures upon a doctrine of pure reason, a critique of it must first have been undertaken. But critiques require knowledge of the sources, and reason must know itself. One is driven to this investigation only after long errors.",[]],"reflm:4893":["Locke an influxionist, at the same time a physiologist of the understanding. Lambert an analyst and architectonic. Wolff a mere dogmatist and mathematical head. Crusius a preestablishist of reason. (g He denied the subordination of all principles to the *principium contradictionis* and yet wanted to give an origin; he can give no mark by which to tell which are innate principles and which are supposititious.) Empiricists of pure reason. Common sense. Misologists. Our aim of metaphysics. (*In sensibus nihil esse veri.*) Value.\n1. Difference between *phaenomena* and *noumena*.*\n2. Origin of the latter. Innate, mystical; or acquired, logical. Plato, Leibniz. Aristotle, Locke. Formulae abstracted from the senses, or not.\n*(g Difference of the objects, or of the knowledge of them.)",[]],"reflm:4894":["Plato the intellectual philosopher. Mystical.\nEpicurus the empirical philosopher.\nAristotle the [int] breaks off.\nLocke had the merit that, since he did not recognize the *intellectualia* as inborn (*connata*), he sought their origin.\nInnate intuitions or logical principles, Crusius.\nPlato's *intellectualia* were innate because they are *intuitus*; Aristotle's were acquired as *conceptus*. They are not, however, borrowed from the senses, otherwise Locke could not use them beyond the limits of appearances. Crusius has *conceptus connati* or *axiomata connata* through a preestablished knowledge (*per praestabilitam cognitionem*).",[]],"reflm:4895":["*Metaphysica est philosophia intellectus puri et transscendentalis est Critica intellectus puri. Metaphysica propria. organon.*\n[Metaphysics is the philosophy of the pure understanding, and the transcendental is the critique of the pure understanding. Metaphysics proper. Organon.]",[]],"reflm:4896":["Transcendental\nAnalytic is where the understanding weighs itself.\n—\n—\n—\nDialectic, where it speaks *a priori* of objects. Illusion.\n(g Logic of truth and of illusion.)",[]],"reflm:4897":["There is no transcendental doctrine; hence the organon of pure reason is a science which shows the use of pure reason in respect of the empirical in general. All philosophy of pure reason is therefore either the critique or the organon of it. The first is transcendental philosophy, the second metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:4898":["In the inner margin, crosswise:\nWe now no longer need to dissemble in metaphysics (g still conceal anything); we can put forward the objections of reason against theology |\nboldly and unabashed, indeed [ihr] strengthen them, since we make no partial distinction between them and the dogmatic speculations. For when we afterwards trace them to their sources, we discover the misunderstanding and unite reason once more with religion.",[]],"reflm:4899":["In the inner margin, crosswise:\nNothing, it is true, seems to be more repugnant to taste than metaphysics; but the ornaments which gleam on beauty lay at first in dark vaults, or at least pass through the gloomy workshop of the artist.",[]],"reflm:4900":["I do not occupy myself with the evolution of concepts as Tetens does (all the acts through which concepts are generated), nor with analysis as Lambert does, but solely with their objective validity. I stand in no rivalry with these men.",[]],"reflm:4901":["Tetens investigates the concepts of pure reason merely subjectively (human nature); I, objectively. That analysis is empirical; this is transcendental.",[]],"reflm:4902":["All modes of knowledge are philosophical, that is, belong to philosophy as materials, which are modes of knowledge from concepts. Philo|sophy itself, however, as the legislation of human reason, consists of metaphysics and morals. The rest belongs to erudition.",[]],"reflm:4903":["Pure mathematical theorems are methods of determining magnitudes. Here the method is [text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:4904":["Transcendental freedom is the necessary hypothesis of all rules,* and hence of all use of the understanding. One ought to think thus and thus, etc. etc. Consequently this action must be free, that is, must not be already determined (subjectively) of itself, but must have only an objective ground of determination.\n*(g It is the [condition of the] property of beings in whom the consciousness of a rule is the ground of their actions.)",[]],"reflm:4905":["Necessity, freedom and infinity are the three stumbling-blocks of reason. Against them there are three sophistical principles, on which the sceptical use of reason is grounded.\n[The question of] substance, action and composition;\nmodality, relation and quantity.",[]],"reflm:4906":["All categories concern objects of (s inner) intuition, (s of the activity. Repetition) of the understanding, of reason. The first is merely consciousness.",[]],"reflm:4907":["There is no science of pure intuition except mathematics. There is also no use of reason that is at once apodeictic and evident, except mathematics in respect of objects and morals in respect of actions. All other inquiry is investigation of nature.",[]],"reflm:4908":["Whether sensible representations are, according to Leibniz, merely confused representations of the understanding. The understanding does not intuit.",[]],"reflm:4909":["Whether through the representation of space we stand in relation with all creatures |\nin general. This is possible, even if space is something subjective, provided it is bound up with all our powers and all creatures are connected with one another through it. But in that case certain creatures, as spirits, will be represented in respect of space only mediately.",[]],"reflm:4910":["If the doctrines of the existence of God (g of freedom) and of another world rest on secure principles of the practical use of our reason (the major of the *dilemma practicum* [*dilemmatis practici Maior*]), then metaphysics may well be dropped as a dogmatic principle of them. It still serves critically to keep off pseudo-metaphysics, and indeed both to keep sensibility apart from pure concepts of the understanding and to keep dialectic apart from pure principles.",[]],"reflm:4911":["It is a great advantage if one can make a science technical, i.e. bring it under functions of the imagination and divide it accordingly; e.g. the table of the categories. The technical is either merely mechanical∗ or architectonic. The former is *a posteriori*, the latter *a priori*∗∗. Art and science.\n∗(s dividing by eye)\n∗∗(s according to a principle)",[]],"reflm:4912":["Metaphysics is not for children and youths, but for men. It is a kind of revision of reason. One must already know it in order to be able to estimate its validity. The theologian needs |\nno metaphysics; the simpler he is, and the more he keeps to empirical principles, the more useful he is. He can know the concept of the divine perfections without metaphysics. He needs it only at the close of his studies. Logic and morals make the beginning. The practical method of teaching morals also comes last. The logician, metaphysician and moralist in one first acquires true importance thereby. — — Probably no examination in it is possible.\n(g One will not take this judgment ill of a man who, being salaried to teach metaphysics publicly, wishes to convince himself that he is good [for something or other] for something.)",[]],"reflm:4913":["Academy of Sciences: a college of revision. It encourages by honours all writings which it improves. In it reports are read.",[]],"reflm:4914":[", across the inner margin.\nM XVII:\nThere are pragmatic laws of reason, namely that one ought to follow no other rule than that according to which it is possible to use reason. These are rules for the power of judgment in making use of speculative reason; |\nM XVIII: |\ne.g. the sight of miracles exceeds all reason in its probative force, but the narrative does not. Hence it is a rule for reason, i.e. for its use, not to ascribe credence to narratives of miracles. For even if something true has been introduced by miracles, it ought afterwards not to be believed on account of this narrative, but on its own account, since otherwise new narratives can overturn it again.",[]],"reflm:4915":["*a priori secundum quid* or\n— —\n*simpliciter*.",[]],"reflm:4916":["Through mere pure concepts of the understanding no object can be known as determinate. Yet these are nevertheless the ways of knowing the object, not as it appears, but as it is.",[]],"reflm:4917":["It is not enough to know what the representations contain in themselves, or to what occasions and conditions they owe their origin; [we must know] also in which capacities and faculties they have their seat.",[]],"reflm:4918":["Difference between Copernicus and Tycho.",[]],"reflm:4919":["Method decides everything: not how one has thought, but how one ought to think. To know philosophizing, or (g a) to know a philosophy. Philosophy must consist chiefly in the sources of knowledge *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4920":["Mathematics proceeds only with concepts which it can make intuitive *a priori*. Philosophy can only subsume under pure concepts and can never make them intuitive.",[]],"reflm:4921":["An idea is always a concept of reason according to which the manifold can be ordered. A *systema naturae* is therefore arranged either according to an idea (which, however, is only didactic) or according to a design [*dessin*].",[]],"reflm:4922":["That the radius can be carried round six times in the circle cannot be derived from the concept of the circle.",[]],"reflm:4923":["That the pure principles are of empirical use in respect of experience, and not of transcendent use in respect of ideal objects. They hold in relation to the way in which boundaries are to be determined by the speculative for our practical knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4924":["Dogmatic transcendent doctrine is sophistry [*Vernünfteley*], namely reason overstepping its |\nlimits. The disciplinarian of the sophists is the teacher of morals, or even the mocker.",[]],"reflm:4925":["Philosophy is the legislation (nomothetic) of human reason. The art of reason is the doctrine of the skill of reason according to rules (not laws).\nIn knowledge the philosopher is just such an ideal as the sage is in the use of his free will. He is the model of all use of reason.",[]],"reflm:4926":["We may appeal to common human understanding when we wish to determine the rule and the incentive of our conduct, and so that which really concerns us. But if we wish to ascend [so] further than our duty extends and to venture into theoretical assertions, we appeal to common human understanding in vain.",[]],"reflm:4927":["Philosophy is really nothing other than a practical knowledge of man; everything else is knowledge of nature and an art of reason. But the magisterial dignity over human reason and all powers, so far as they are subject to it, belongs to philosophy. Oh! it is to be regretted that we let this meaning vanish. Without such a distinguishing designation this knowledge is not set apart from others, and there is no real doctrine of philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4928":["Across the inner margin: M XIX:\nAll metaphysical axioms which are not apprehending (exemplar) |\nare entitled petitions, and are hypotheses of pure reason which have no ground in themselves. For all hypotheses are physiological: that is, the datum of their possibility is given, and only the assumption of it *in casu* is postulated. Nevertheless I can make transcendent hypotheses, in order to oppose something to equally giddy |\nM XX: |\n[ones], so that petitions may hold the balance against petitions. E.g. Perhaps [is] pure spiritual natures are the only true thing in the world. All this belongs to the negative means of securing practical propositions of reason against the usurpations of dogmatic speculation; they are retorsions or refutations by analogy.\nM XIX, between the lines of the Preface:\nSynthetic propositions without proof are either axioms or petitions or hypotheses. In the last the possibility is known beforehand, and only the positing is arbitrary; in the second the possibility is assumed.",[]],"reflm:4929":["We cannot at all comprehend a first beginning from freedom, but just as little a first beginning without freedom, i.e. through chance or necessity of nature (g blind accident and blind fate).\nDialectic is artificial or natural; the thetic of the latter [is] contains the true subjective principles and consequences.",[]],"reflm:4930":["The illusion [*Schein*] arising from lack of judgment is the semblance [*Anschein*] (the first *Schein*): *apparentia*. The *Schein* which conflicts with judgment and |\ndoes not yield to it is the natural *Schein*: *species*. Of such a kind is transcendental illusion. It will never be eradicated.",[]],"reflm:4931":["To transcendent assertions what stand opposed are not refutations but counter-assertions.\nPlausibility [*Scheinbarkeit*] is opposed to probability, and remains even with certainty of the contrary; it arises from the *vitium subreptionis*.",[]],"reflm:4932":["All synthetic principles, if they are objective, are either anticipations of appearance or of experience; if they are not the former, they are subjective.\nPresumptions of reason are those in which I assume that in which my reason can be fully exercised, e.g. unity of forces and causes.",[]],"reflm:4933":["The soul is unity as object of inner sense, but from this I cannot infer that it is abiding as object of outer intuition.\nConversely, [one cannot] infer from the synthetic yet apodeictic propositions of space that it is nothing objective, and consequently an object of observation.\nEmpirical anticipations are not apodeictic, and consequently not axioms. The expounding and adjective predicates.",[]],"reflm:4934":["The Analytic contains what is common to all transcendental knowledge, that is, the mere concepts prior to all judgment, and so the elements of judgment. Hence no falsity can as yet be in them; it is therefore the logic of truth.\nJust as logical analytic considers only knowledge in general and so gives the canon [Richtschnur] of truth.\nThe transcendental concepts have no hyperphysical use whatever, except *in subsidium* (in aid) of the moral.\nEpicurus erred in this, that he made the maxims of reason into axioms, and so made subjective rules objective. E.g. one must seek none but mechanical explanations, and cite no grounds save such as [sich] can be shown through the course of nature.\nThe maxims of reason are grounded on the best use of reason.\nEpicurus was a philosopher of method. He established the necessary maxims of reason rather than axioms and theories of reason. He wished to set reason within its own proper limits, but was not dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:4935":["The whole error hitherto seems to me to have been this, that in metaphysics men have wished to proceed from the parts to the whole, and indeed even by mixing in parts of a foreign kind. But here it is possible only to begin from the whole, in a wholly unmixed knowledge, and a single person must carry it completely out.",[]],"reflm:4936":["The contradictions and the strife of the systems are still the only thing which in recent times has kept the human understanding, in matters of metaphysics, from complete decay. Although they are all dogmatic in the highest degree, they nevertheless fully take the place of the sceptics, for one who regards this game as a whole. For this reason we may thank a Crusius no less than a Wolff that, by the new paths on which they set out, they at least |\nprevented the understanding from letting its rights lapse through prescription in a stupid repose, and the seed of a secure knowledge was still preserved. Analyst and architectonic philosopher. In this way the course of nature leads its beautiful, but for the most part enigmatic, order at last to perfection, even through destructions. Even a *système de la nature* is advantageous to philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4937":["Across the inner margins. M XXII:\nIt is of the greatest importance to make a science of reason technical. The logicians have tried in vain to do so with their syllogistic, as with a factory [verg]. Only in regard to magnitudes have the inventors of the algorithm succeeded. Might it not be so also in the Critique of Pure Reason, not for the extension, but for the purification, of modes of knowledge? |\nM XXI: |\nBy the technical method one can, in the notation, give each concept its function, or rather express the functions themselves, both in themselves and in relation to one another. (Algebra expresses them only in relation to one another; perhaps it would be so also in a transcendental algorithm. Oversights and omissions can be prevented by this alone.)",[]],"reflm:4938":["The use of reason in geometry is intuitive in regard to the object, discursive in regard to the form; the form of algebra is not discursive, but technical.\nThe thetic is the dogmatic part, not objective, but the dogmatic grounds of the method. Distinction of dogmatic semblance [Schein], that is, principle of truth.\nThe mathematicians have believed that, if they worked on the objects of pure reason, they would accomplish something superior. But it is to be regretted that, if they take this knowledge objectively, they are cultivating an unrewarding field. But in the critique of reason they can produce the greatest benefit. Holland.",[]],"reflm:4939":["Critical assertions are something quite different from dogmatic; the latter are objective, the former subjective.",[]],"reflm:4940":["The philosophy of ignorance is very useful, but also difficult, because it must go back to the sources of knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4941":["He who says that a God exists says more than he knows, and he who says the contrary does likewise. No one knows that one exists; we believe it.",[]],"reflm:4942":["Because metaphysics contains *quaestiones* (questions) which are of immediate and pressing concern and are connected with the highest ends, break off.",[]],"reflm:4943":["If someone speaks through synthetic propositions a priori, he speaks only of his own thoughts, e.g. that there is a necessary being, etc., namely that without this presupposition he does not have a complete concept of the existence of things. Or he speaks of conditions of knowledge from experience a priori, and then it is objective, e.g. every *compositum reale* (real composite) has as its ground a force of combination of all its parts. Yet these propositions are really analytic.",[]],"reflm:4944":["One cannot say that transcendent propositions are undecided. For without the condition for determining them they are not merely indeterminate but have no meaning at all, e.g. absolutely necessary. Just as, if the pole constitutes the zenith, it is not merely uncertain which region of the sky we have there, but all regions of the sky, through which alone we can determine our course at sea, cease to exist.",[]],"reflm:4945":["M XXII:\nThere can be no *quaestiones* (questions) of transcendental philosophy whose answer would be unknown to us. For if the predicate is not determined through the subject, this means that the question is nothing in itself, because in this case the predicate has no meaning at all, and is neither affirmative nor its *oppositum contrarium* (contrary opposite). So, when I ask [welche welche], where, if I am at the pole, I am to look for east. Our cosmological concepts have meaning only |\nM XXIII: |\nin the world, and therefore none in regard to the boundary or the whole of the world. The possibility of the sensible rests on the condition of what is given; now neither the boundary nor the whole can be given to the senses.\nWhether the world is 100 million sun-diameters in size, I do not know, because experience has not taught it. Whether it is infinite, I do not know, because I can know it through no experience whatever.",[]],"reflm:4946":["If I assume that a concept a priori has an object, I must also be able to know a priori of that object everything for which the concept contains the condition. There is then nothing uncertain and undecided; and yet reason contains nothing other than conditions of empirical use, and therefore all its attempts which are transcendent are impossible and in vain. The transcendent concepts are not concepts of objects. They are ideas, *ideae*.",[]],"reflm:4947":["Across the outer margin:\nIn metaphysics one must be subtle. For all this knowledge is a priori and, without derivation from its first sources, uncertain.",[]],"reflm:4948":["Across the inner margin:\nOn the influence of honesty upon insight, and of a good heart upon the extension of knowledge.",[]],"reflm:4949":["If we regard nature as the continent of our modes of knowledge, and our reason consists in determining the limits of these, we can know these limits only so far as we include with them what constitutes the limits, the ocean which bounds them, which however we know only by its shore: namely God and the other world, which are necessarily regarded as limits of nature, although distinguished from it and unknown to us.",[]],"reflm:4950":["One can comprise the whole of metaphysics, so far as it is expanding [erweiternd], in very few sheets. The analysis, or the elucidating [part], can still be continued onward.",[]],"reflm:4951":["M XXIII:\nIn transcendental modes of knowledge (g or discursive modes of knowledge) of pure reason no more than one proof is possible. For everything must flow from the concept laid before us, e.g. the existence of a necessary being from the concept of necessity.\nNot all concepts allow of construction. Only those of magnitude, because they constitute the synthesis of intuition. But the dynamical, whereby |\nM XXIV: |\nsomething which does not lie in the intuition is found from what is given, according to the law of transcendental association, requires empirical conditions and cannot be given a priori.",[]],"reflm:4952":["Sophistical dialectic is an art of semblance [Schein]. Philosophical dialectic is a science of the resolution of semblance, and has a paedeutic part, which contains the criterion of truth, and a sceptical part, which indicates the sources of semblance and secures truth against it.",[]],"reflm:4953":["The belief that there is no God, no other world, is impossible; but not so the opinion (s or doubt and uncertainty).\nOpposed to all philosophy of pure reason are misology and empiricism.\nThe empiricism of pure reason. Sound understanding.\nThe doctrinal concept [Lehrbegriff] is either the realism or the formalism of pure reason. The latter permits only principles of the form of the use of our reason a priori in respect of experiences. Hence it permits neither a dogmatic affirmation nor a dogmatic denial beyond the limit of experience. If religion and morality have sources of knowledge other than merely speculative ones, it shows that the practical principles of reason are necessarily bound up with theoretical postulates. But a *postulatum theoreticum* (theoretical postulate) is a necessary hypothesis of the agreement of theoretical and practical knowledge. Metaphysics then withstands all objections of pure reason and shows how they are dialectical, i.e. the dialectic of the same.\nIf [they]—the *dogmata cardinalia* (cardinal dogmas)—are secured *in respectu practico* (in the practical respect), though not *speculativo* (in the speculative), then metaphysics illustrates and defends them.",[]],"reflm:4954":["I have read excellent and acute thoughts, but they do not advance the science a single step, and they die out with their novelty. The reason is that there is really as yet no science, and the foundation has not yet been laid. As [einfalle] stand to insights, so do acute thoughts stand to the doctrinal edifice.",[]],"reflm:4955":["In human beings sensibility is not so greatly distinguished from reason that both should not extend merely to one and the same objects, or at least to such as are represented in one and the same way, notwithstanding that the one is valid for objects in respect of all possible positions of sensibility, the other not.",[]],"reflm:4956":["Two contradictory propositions whose hypothesis is false can both be false; e.g. that God moves in space for eternity, [and] also that he does not move in eternity.",[]],"reflm:4957":["The citing of books is not necessary in a system of transcendental philosophy at the stage of the outline, any more than in a geometry. The concurring judgments of others furnish a ground of proof only where the concern is not with the rule but with its application, i.e. with the power of judgment, and where it is not everything that has to be derived from an idea, but rather the concept that has to be derived from a multitude of compared observations.",[]],"reflm:4958":["We can make metaphysical hypotheses if we can test them on appearances; e.g. of a diversity of matters that is original; but not transcendental hypotheses.\nThe unity of the system is in all hypotheses a touchstone, but in the dogmatic [hypothesis] a *principium* (principle) of correctness.\nThe limit of appearances belongs with [the] to the appearance, but the thing that constitutes the limit is outside them. Consequently we have reason to infer a being as the cause of the world, and a future world, but no means of determining them.",[]],"reflm:4959":["M XXV:\nIt is now laughable to ask: what do you hold as opinion about the community of soul with body, the nature of a spirit, creation in time? Of this I hold nothing at all. But [I ask]: what origin these |\nthoughts have in the human understanding, in that it |\nM XXVI: |\ngoes beyond its limit. Why this question is necessary, and why, in respect of the object, it can be answered only subjectively. That I know, and there I am above all opinion.",[]],"reflm:4960":["The opponents of religion are provoked to attack truths which they would have allowed to pass as articles of belief, had they not been challenged by the brazen presumption of those who pretend to more than they know.",[]],"reflm:4961":["Freedom makes us regard as possible much that is not actual; e.g. that the river should take another course, because we can ourselves direct it.",[]],"reflm:4962":["In transcendental judgments it is not at all permissible to opine, any more than in geometry.",[]],"reflm:4963":["On sound understanding as a *qvalitas occulta* (occult quality). How far its authority extends; usurped authority. Lazy philosophy.",[]],"reflm:4964":["By this treatise of mine the value of my earlier metaphysical writings is completely annulled. I shall seek only to save the correctness of the idea.",[]],"reflm:4965":["To know something a priori is entitled: to have insight into it [einsehen] (the principles may well be empirical). To determine something a priori (i.e. to give and construct it) is entitled: to comprehend it [begreifen]. To comprehend absolutely is to do so without any empirical conditions.",[]],"reflm:4966":["All the difficulty of metaphysics concerns only the reconciling of empirical principles with ideas. The possibility of the latter is not to be denied, but they cannot become empirically intelligible; the idea is not at all a *conceptus dabilis* (a concept that can be given), not an empirically possible concept.",[]],"reflm:4967":["To the intellectual [aspect] of magnitude belong allness [Allheit], limits or finitude. Such a concept of magnitude is no mere concept of comparison and relation; it can be understood absolutely of a thing.\nThe whole, the part.\nLimiting concepts: the first, the last. All.",[]],"reflm:4968":["If the doctrine of space and time stopped short at this, that they are merely affections of the mind and not objective conditions, it would be a subtle but not very important consideration. But it is important that these concepts must not therefore be extended beyond the limits of sensibility. That we actually intuit everything in time proves that this is a condition of all our intuitions of the senses, but not that it has any meaning even apart from this condition.",[]],"reflm:4969":["All new theories which bring about a great change have had to be introduced by someone else. The inventor has never been able to set them going. It was confirmation by the reason of others that occasioned a favourable judgment in favour of the innovation.",[]],"reflm:4970":["M XXVII:\nThe philosopher deals with that which in all human modes of knowledge is the self-subsistent and lies at the ground of everything; hence the philosophical also pleases immediately, among all the doctrines.\nPhilosophy is the science of the conformity of all knowledge with the vocation [Bestimmung] of man. To this belongs, first, *philodoxie* (love of opinion), as the culture and instruction of all talents, i.e. the conformity of the modes of knowledge with all sorts of ends. Secondly, their conformity with the extended use of reason. Thirdly, the grounding of the supreme maxims of the speculative as well as of the practical use of reason; here great erudition, indeed even the rational artistry [Vernunftkunst] of famous men, drops out and is without philosophy. Metaphysics and morals, both architectonic, are the two hinges of the door of philosophy. The philosopher is no misologist, but one learned in the laws of human reason, and the chief laws are those which restrict the pretensions of reason to the end of humanity. First, extensive historical and rational knowledge, just as for a legislator there must be first a multitude of citizens and of arts. In order to prescribe laws to reason one must know it. But philosophy resides only in the maxims of reason; the other modes of knowledge are philosophical, i.e. belong to it.\nM XXVIII:\nHistory and description must first of all be treated in a manner conformable to reason (whereas before they were adequate merely to the desire for knowledge). Afterwards, their connection according to maxims of reason; and finally their system according to the legislative idea of reason.",[]],"reflm:4971":["It has happened to various persons that the maxims of reason have been held by others to be axioms of it, and the rules of method to be principles of the theory (g doctrin), and likewise the restriction of judgments to be a restriction of objects. [It is] just as Epicurus's ever cheerful heart, which can never be attained otherwise than through accord with the universal and essential order of things, and therefore with all that is called morality, was, through an unfortunate ambiguity of expression, taken for the pleasure of the senses.",[]],"reflm:4972":["In the world of sense we follow the principles of empirical knowledge, in the world of understanding the principles of pure knowledge of the understanding. The latter, however, are those which have no relation to the exposition of appearances, [but] by which we are affected, but solely to that which is given through the understanding (pure use of freedom), or have morality. Here, then, the necessary presupposition is that there is a God. A belief.",[]],"reflm:4973":["Metaphysics is something that cannot be learned at all.\nIn hypothetical and disjunctive inferences of reason the subsumption is a change of the problematic into the assertoric. How does this transition stand in ontology?",[]],"reflm:4974":["The understanding is the faculty of bringing everything that may be given through the senses under universal rules for all the powers of representation of the human mind.\n1. The conditions of sensibility under which alone the concept of the understanding is possible (*in concreto*, in the concrete).\n2. Of the acts a priori, to determine the peculiar function of each representation.",[]],"reflm:4975":["The faculty of rules is: 1. either to find rules for given representations (g to bring them under rules), or also for possible ones. 2. To bring representations under a given rule.",[]],"reflm:4976":["The transcendental antithesis occurs wherever I try to think something without the conditions through which alone it can be given; e.g., There is a first in the series of the contingent.\nAll analytic propositions of pure reason are correct *in thesi*, the synthetic ones only *in hypothesi*. The hypothesis is that they are to apply to experience, as concepts of it, or to the condition of sensibility, and yet without that condition, or to completeness and to limits. If the hypothesis fails, the propositions are arbitrary. If the new condition flatly contradicts it, they are false. The general hypothesis applies to everything that can be given to us, and is consequently an object of the senses. What exceeds the measure of the senses in respect of magnitude (g everything possible), or in respect of quality (as spirit), or in respect of universality (as substance, supreme being), passes beyond the limits of synthesis through the understanding. Such propositions are therefore not axioms but anticipations, if they are put forward without restriction.",[]],"reflm:4977":["The understanding knows objects as they are. This proposition can have two meanings:\n1. As they would appear if the senses were completely acute.\n2. What constitutes the origin of appearances: real functions. The being and not-being (g of something) of a thing in general.\n3. (Things according to their absolute quality, which correspond to these appearances.\nThis something in the concrete. The last is mystical and chimerical: spiritual intuition.)\nOur understanding knows *a priori* even the first sensitive grounds of appearances, or rather the elements of experiences and |\ntheir fundamental laws; this is also the reason why it can reduce the appearances [to rules].",[]],"reflm:4978":["Of the mathematical concept of magnitude, the only one that is capable of being extended to infinity *a priori* without any data of knowledge (likewise geometry), because it is nothing but intuition of our own arbitrary acts and no quality is mixed in with it, [but] also because no logical analysis is used in it, no synthetic principles of reason are met with in it. In the anticipations of empirical knowledge, on the other hand, a great difference is admitted between the subjective and the objective conditions, or rather between the relation to experience, by which we understand them, and the relation to reason, by which we know them *a priori*. In the case of quantity this is given to me arbitrarily in accordance with its conditions; in the case of the concepts of synthesis it is given through experience. Those of the thesis are accompanying concepts [of proper knowledge] of positing, either absolutely or *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4979":["M XXX:\nThe proposition, No magnitude is mathematically infinite, is a synthetic proposition; it therefore has only subjective correctness. On the other hand, No number is infinite, has objective correctness.\nOn the other hand, the proposition* No magnitude can be divided or diminished to infinity (the angle of contact actually divides every rectilinear angle to infinity, yet for that reason yields no infinitely small part, for such a part is not assignable [angeblich]) has not even subjective correctness. For where the part is possible only through the limits of the infinite, it is not to be assumed as consisting of elements. One must not appeal to the divisibility of the unit.\nM XXIX:\n*(g In the meantime the possibility of such a magnitude is not to be comprehended through reason, for it would have to arise through the generation of the magnitude from its parts. The proposition, The substantial is *stabile* and the accidents *fluunt* [flow], has objective correctness with respect to experience; for in this stability I know only subsistence. Likewise: Every *compositum substantiale* requires a power flowing in common. Also: Everything contingent has a ground; for I know contingency only by this, that the thing is not always, and the ground is what accompanies it, by which I can recognize it again.)",[]],"reflm:4980":["M XXX:\nWhether there is a single simple action?",[]],"reflm:4981":["Across the inner margin:\nWhat we intend by restricting the [sensitive] axioms of sensibility to mere use in this world |\nis not to make room for modes of knowledge through the pure understanding. Rather we show, with regard to these, that although the principles here may be different, the objects can nevertheless be only objects of the senses, though undetermined as regards their kind.",[]],"reflm:4982":["M XXIX. Across the inner margin:\nThe most eminent of all the sciences of reason are metaphysics and morals, the former however for the sake of the latter. Its utility is dialectical: in that reason sets something against the dogmatic objections of reason, the grounds of morals are left without hindrance.\nM XXXIII. On the lower margin:\nE.g., One turns the empirical propositions into dogmatic ones: that the soul does not think without the accord of the body becomes the insight that it cannot think. To this one replies in the same tone that perhaps the body is only the means of assistance against the hindrance of it. Against this new objections and replies are made; meanwhile reason betakes itself to its natural determination. The replies must be regarded only as *retorsiones* [retorts], not as dogmatic propositions.\nThe political constitution permits doctrines of compulsion, because it is all one with what disposition of heart they are accepted; morals does not, because sincerity is here essential.",[]],"reflm:4983":["In metaphysics the pure notions, in morals the pure idea.\nThe idea in the concrete is the archetype [Urbild] in the rational understanding. The rule in the concrete is the example. The analogue of the archetype is the symbol [Sinnbild] (*typus*); that of the example is the simile. The [acceptance for] imitation of the archetype is the copy [Nachbild]; the imitation of the rule is the attempt. The semblance of the [archetype] copy is* etc. etc. The [own] concordant consequence of the archetype is the counterpart [Gegenbild], e.g., man as the image of God, the subject obedient to the sovereign (his corresponding counterpart). Prefiguration [Vorbild], *prognosticon*.\n(\n* g The reproduction [Abbild] or depiction, the spurious image [Afterbild], the shadow-image [Schattenbild]. The idea of nature and of morality: the former is genetic, the latter exemplary. The principles for us are rules. Image ([knowledge] intuition of the object), archetype, copy, counterpart, symbol, prefiguration, shadow-image. The counterpart or companion-piece corresponds to a principal image. )",[]],"reflm:4984":["In metaphysics, to think and to think about it are different; the first all do. Some, instead of thinking about it, rave about it.\nMathematics, physics and metaphysics, although they are rational, contain no *dogmata*. Physics is not apodictic, because the first causes are given only contingently. Mathematics is not from concepts. Metaphysics has no objects that are given *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:4985":["The transcendental propositions of the pure understanding are thetic when they are restricted to the condition of appearance, i.e., to the [*data*] conditions of sensibility under which the object is given. |\nBut when they become transcendent, they are antithetic and furnish fine material for the sceptical method. The transcendental antithetic arises from the nature of reason itself and is neither accidental nor arbitrary.",[]],"reflm:4986":["The *organon* of pure reason is an instruction for determining its limits and rules. Instead of doctrine (objective) it contains discipline, and as criterion and norm the canon.",[]],"reflm:4987":["All philosophy has reason as its object: its maxims, its limits and its end. The rest is art of reason.",[]],"reflm:4988":["The logic of pure reason, which is merely critical, is followed by the *organon*, which is didactic, and by which not merely is judgment [set] right, but the procedure is also guided. It contains discipline and canon. Not doctrine, because it contains merely negative teachings.",[]],"reflm:4989":["[The] *organon* is practical logic.\nDiscipline is negative and a manly teaching. (Against error.)\nDoctrine is positive and here a children's teaching.\nThe method of my exposition has a disadvantageous form. It looks scholastic, hence subtle-spinning, dry, indeed narrow, and far removed from the tone of genius.",[]],"reflm:4990":["Reality is the *transscendentaliter addendum* [what is to be added transcendentally]; negation, the *subtrahendum* [what is to be subtracted]. It serves for transcendental magnitude, e.g., all matter that contains extension, all limits: extension (space), time intension.\nReality is the object of sensation in the abstract,\nnegation\nthe lack of it.",[]],"reflm:4991":["It matters little whether some propositions of pure philosophy about the object are true or false; it is more important whether they are thought in the proper method and have their proper place in the whole of knowledge, as on a general map.",[]],"reflm:4992":["M XXXI:\nIn judging the writings of others one must choose the method of participating in the common cause of human reason. When one tries to pick out what concerns the whole, one finds it worth examining, and worth while to lend a helping hand to the author, or rather |\nto the common good, and to treat the faults as matters of secondary importance. It is sad for reason as a whole to destroy everything.\nIt would have been vain to credit myself with such extraordinary good fortune or exceptional acuteness that, by the same method, I should avoid all pitfalls and alone hit upon the right path on so confused a terrain. I have therefore expected a happier outcome than all my predecessors had solely from the frame of mind [Gemüthsverfassung] into which I put myself and which I constantly maintained, and likewise from the length of time during which I kept my mind open to every new instruction. These are points which I doubt that anyone before me has observed. I have nowhere yet settled [auf dem Boden der] in the domain of the philosophy of pure reason. I have written no large books in it, and so have not placed my vanity under the necessity of defending them and of remaining by one and the same opinion.\nM XXXII:\nI have never even looked again at the small essays on it which I scattered abroad so as not to seem wholly idle, lest I should remain fixed to one and the same spot. And the critique of them [traf mich nicht mehr] from others, whose mildness yet did not take away my courage (breaks off?)",[]],"reflm:4993":["In mathematics the division into pure and applied mathematics may well be made, for the objects of experience give no *principia mathematica*; these are only applied to them. But philosophy must be divided into pure and empirical, because in the latter there lie even certain *principia philosophica* which in the former are undetermined. The universal element in the experiences of inner and outer sense, whereby they are all possible, is the boundary of pure reason *a posteriori*, and therefore lies in the |\napplication. For there are possibilities which are given *a posteriori* but serve *a priori* as grounds, to which the rest can be reduced.\nQualities [haben], in respect of their relations, must often be given anew through the senses. But quantity *qua talis* and its relations are at all times given *a priori*, even in applied mathematics.",[]],"reflm:4994":["Many acute and good thoughts have been set down, but to sheer loss; for they have no place in any system, since the outline of such a system has not yet been found.",[]],"reflm:4995":["The concept of God: is it a dogmatic concept or a concept of faith?",[]],"reflm:4996":["Knowledge of God is important only in respect of the practical. But this must be sufficiently certain in itself. Otherwise it would not be obligatory in itself, and would not become so through knowledge of God. Hence faith in God must spring from morality, which thereby supports itself. It is well that we do not know, but believe, that there is a God. Method of teaching this. For mere speculation it is an opinion which oversteps the limits.",[]],"reflm:4997":["To proceed by a guiding thread requires only diligence and attentiveness. But to find the guiding thread itself, and the detached pieces of it, |\nrequires the happy thought [Einfall], which in thinking is just what the stroke of luck is in events.",[]],"reflm:4998":["Across the inner margin. M XXXI:\nIn experience there is appearance [Erscheinung] and real concept. The experience of our alterations contains the mere form of appearance, namely time, and the concept of being. [Das Das letztere] Both together constitute something which, in the field of experiences, yields a correct ground. But the former, and hence |\nM XXXII: |\nthe concept of existence affected thereby, goes no further and has no inner reality. It has only the worth of a conditioned form of our representations; that is, it is not a thing, nor the affection of a thing in itself, but only the sensible representation thereof.",[]],"reflm:4999":["Across the outer margin.\nAppearance [Erscheinung] and illusion [Schein] are two different things. The former depends on how the object is given, the latter on how it is thought. The *apparentia* in an appearance rests on the judgment about the affection of sense; and even if this judgment is correct, it still remains *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5000":["In pure philosophy, the metaphysics of nature and of morals, only one proof can be given, because it must be drawn from a single concept.",[]],"reflm:5001":["A necessary hypothesis of the optional or indispensable use (practical hypothesis) of understanding and reason.\nA necessary theoretical hypothesis in respect of the whole of all experiences (existence of God).",[]],"reflm:5002":["In transcendental modes of knowledge only a single proof is possible, namely that from the concept of the subject.",[]],"reflm:5003":["In this respect the transcendental analytic is still the logic of truth, because it does not judge objectively; the dialectic, however, judges objectively. In the latter everything must be taken from the subject and yet judged objectively. Hence subjective laws here readily pass for objective. Those which rest on the conditions of comprehensibility pass for laws concerning the apprehension of the object, and these again pass for universal laws, although they are restricted by human sensibility.",[]],"reflm:5004":["Transcendental philosophy, in the part dealing with its propositions, is dialectical. For since without critique it carries with it no other touchstone of truth, its propositions must be considered problematically, and so with retention of their opposites, and can become dogmatic only through agreement with the canon.",[]],"reflm:5005":["Synthetic propositions constitute the content of our knowledge, what we know; analytic propositions only the materials for this knowledge. |\nIn regard to the former (g *in concreto*) I know no more than anyone else; but I know what the understanding can know of them. I know the rules of the understanding in regard to them.",[]],"reflm:5006":["Of the fundamental maxim of reason, to be difficult [schwierig] in respect of everything that diminishes its use according to universal laws. For reason consists in knowing something from the universal. Hence whatever restricts the use according to universal laws, and likewise the use *a priori*, is contrary to it. [Alle] Something as innate (breaks off)",[]],"reflm:5007":["Philosophy is the science of the laws [Gesetzkunde] of human reason. The artist of reason [Vernunftkünstler] needs rules, the teacher of reason [Vernunftlehrer] needs laws. *leguleius* [pettifogger].\n*Est nomothetica rationis humanae* [It is the lawgiving science of human reason].\n*Scientia regularum Rationis Humanae est Logica.* [The science of the rules of human reason is logic.]",[]],"reflm:5008":["[pra] The theoretical conditions of everything practical are freedom, origin and future; or, the inner and the outer principles of all our ends taken together. These are also the *crux philosophorum*.",[]],"reflm:5009":["Nothing more wretched can be found than to make rules of the expedients [Nothhülfen] of our over-curious reason, unless it is still more wretched to regard these presuppositions as divine revelations.",[]],"reflm:5010":["We do in fact make use of the sceptical method in many cases, in order to test whether a proposition also has universality; for if something false follows from it, it proves too much.",[]],"reflm:5011":["It is above all necessary to show why in mathematics so much, but in the philosophy of pure reason nothing at all, can be known as synthetic *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5012":["Transcendental knowledge is the only natural dialectic.",[]],"reflm:5013":["At the beginning of the transcendental science.\nMy aim is to investigate how much reason can know *a priori*, and how far its dependence on the instruction of the senses extends. [Es] That is, what the limits are beyond which it cannot go without the aid of the senses. This object is important and great, for it shows man his determination [Bestimmung] with reason. To attain this end I find it necessary to isolate reason, and sensibility also, and first to consider everything that can be known *a priori*, [to see] whether it also belongs to the domain of reason. This separate consideration, this pure philosophy, is of great utility.",[]],"reflm:5014":["The reason why, among the three parts of metaphysics, *Physica |\nrationalis*, *Psychologia rationalis*, *Theologia naturalis*, the last has the word nature last, is this: in the former the object is given through the senses and known through reason, whereas in the latter the object is given through reason and known according to the analogy of the senses.",[]],"reflm:5015":["It took a considerable time before my concepts had so arranged themselves that I saw them constitute a whole and could trace distinctly the limits of the science which I had in view. Before the disputation I already had the idea of the influence of the subjective conditions of modes of knowledge upon the objective, [but] afterwards that of the distinction between the sensitive and the intellectual. But the latter was with me merely negative.\nIt must not be believed that all that has hitherto been written and thought has been spent in sheer loss. [It changes] The dogmatic attempts may continue indefinitely, but a critique of them must follow upon them, and they can be used only to |\njudge of the illusion which befalls human reason when it confounds the subjective with the objective and sensibility with reason.\nTwo metaphysicians, of whom one proves the thesis and the other the antithesis, take, in the eyes of a third observer, the place of a sceptical examination. One must do both for oneself.\nI believe, indeed, that this doctrine will be the only one which will remain when minds have cooled from [heat] dogmatic heat, and that it must then endure for ever; but I very much doubt that I shall be the one to bring about this change. The human mind is of such a kind that, besides the grounds which are to enlighten it, time also is required to give them force and progress. And when prejudices are attacked, it is no wonder that these efforts are at first opposed by the very same prejudices. For it is necessary first to remove the impressions and the old habit. [One w] I could cite various cases in which it was not the originator of an improvement, but those who later, after long contradictions, found it anew, who brought it into vogue and set it going.\nI can still picture to myself the reproach that various things are not elucidated which ought still to have been said. [It] That is as much as if one reproached a man that, having wished to write only a small book, he did not write a large one. What is lacking in a writing does not constitute a fault (a missed aim); a deficiency does, however, when it is found in what is given out as complete. Moderation and power of judgment are required not to say everything good that one knows, and not to overload one's work with all one's ideas, lest the main purpose suffer. In the analysis I have said some things not unimportant, etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:5016":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nMetaphysical possibilities, e.g. that the spirit of man is prepared by birth, as by a school, for a greater development of ideas, count not as opinions but as answers to the objections that such cases could not be thought at all.",[]],"reflm:5017":["Along the inner margin, crosswise:\nI must confess that I have a certain superstition with regard to various expressions that have occurred to great minds. I do not seek the meaning behind them; but when a concept rises in my reflection and the word strikes me, [so] it seems that I feel the inspiration, or even the whole sensation, of the man who had the expression together with the same concept, and with him I sympathize.",[]],"reflm:5018":["Logic has no empirical principles. It is a science of the objective rules of reason, namely those of correct knowledge. Consequently the subjective principles are merely practical with regard to the human understanding, and belong to the organon. After *psychologia empirica* (empirical psychology).",[]],"reflm:5019":["I have cited no one from whose reading I have learned anything. I have thought it good to omit everything [di] foreign and to follow my own idea. I have not contended against systems, etc. etc. I have not cited myself either, but have overturned everything. I do not approve\n|\nthe rule that, when one has proved something beforehand in the use of pure reason, one should afterwards no longer call it in question, as if it were a firm principle.",[]],"reflm:5020":["In many passages my exposition would have become far clearer if it had not had to be so clear.",[]],"reflm:5021":["According to Priestley and Locke all modes of knowledge must be empirical, and no single synthetic proposition can have true necessity. This, however, conflicts with the use [of such propositions], which is universal without exception.",[]],"reflm:5022":["Transcendental philosophy is the grave of all superstition. Maxims of reason, conditions of intuition and of understanding.",[]],"reflm:5023":["M XXXIII:\nAll the remaining philosophical sciences must gain very much if this diversion [*Ableitung*] of our power of reason (which is without use in any case) is stopped up; |\nM XXXIV: |\nthe pre-eminence of mathematics is that it represents everything as it can be given in experience, which *philosophia transscendentalis* (transcendental philosophy) does not do.",[]],"reflm:5024":["(For the dedication.) You have honoured me with your letters. The effort to give you, at your desire, a concept of the method of pure philosophy [my answers of the still obscure in] occasioned a series of reflections for developing the concept still lying obscure in me, and, as the prospects widened with the progress, exposed my answers to unceasing postponement. This work can serve in place of [the er] an answer, so far as the speculative part is concerned. Since it is to be ascribed to your summons and hints, I could wish that it belonged wholly to you, through the effort of taking it up into your own treatment. Academies of the sciences ought to take into consideration the state of the sciences of their age in general rather than individual elaborations. Yours is the only — — breaks off.\nIt is hardly necessary to mention that this [work from the] dedication borrows a recommendation from your name, and is not there to the end that to the same the — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5025":["My method is not very well suited to hold the reader to it and to please him.\nOne must begin one's judgment from the whole, and direct it to the idea of the work together with its ground. The rest belongs to the execution, in which much may have been missed and may be improved.",[]],"reflm:5026":["Upon what does the plausibility of a metaphysical hypothesis (Swedenborg) rest? Upon a supposed *intuitus intellectualis* (intellectual intuition), formed on the analogy of the sensible.",[]],"reflm:5027":["Dogmatic metaphysics is a *magia iudiciaria*,* *visionaria* (a magic of judgment, visionary). It is not the organon but the *catarcticon* (purgative) of transcendent reason. Worms of the brain. Palliatives. Relapse. Thorough cure by purging.\n*(g Contemplativa; difference from *magia empirica*.)",[]],"reflm:5028":["Our method also greatly promotes sincerity. Formerly one |\nhad to conceal the difficulties against important articles of faith of philosophy and to adduce only what was advantageous. A paradoxical author could easily be refuted, for he was no longer allowed to reply. He who adhered to the common opinion kept the field alone. Now, by the sceptical method, a free gate is opened to every thought, and even the objection, with its *retorsio* (retort), serves to determine the limits of the understanding better.\nIt is also a *fallacia ignorationis elenchi* (fallacy of ignorance of the refutation) to begin with the harmfulness of a proposition when one is supposed to be investigating its correctness.\nWhen shall we at last lay aside this great remnant of a barbarous constitution, which imperiously prescribes its path to reason, which yet is the only thing that can guide us?\nIn such disputes he is always right who has the last word. But he always has the last word who maintains the orthodox proposition. For either the [bookseller] publisher is already under fiscal investigation, etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:5029":["The intellectual in the objects of the senses (or of experience) does not consist in their being given in some way other than through the senses, but in that whereby they are thought a priori, and in how one would think everything through concepts, however it might be given. The ancients seemed to wish to withdraw from reflecting knowledge, and believed that the understanding is capable of intuitions of its own.",[]],"reflm:5030":["That substance persists through all the alterations of nature, we infer from this: in this persistence we recognise only substance. |\nThe change of substance is therefore, for our [conditions] concepts of experience, a *contradictio in adiecto* (contradiction in the adjunct).",[]],"reflm:5031":["I have chosen the scholastic method and have preferred it to [the free flight] of the free movement of the spirit and of wit, although, since I wished every reflective mind to take part in this inquiry, I found that the dryness of this method would deter [the] readers of the kind who seek directly the connection with the practical. Even had I been in the fullest possession of wit and of the charms of [the] author[ship], I should have excluded them here; for it matters much to me to leave no suspicion that I wished to win over and persuade the reader, so that I might expect either no assent at all [from him] or only such as comes through the strength of insight.\nThe method too arose with me only through trials.",[]],"reflm:5032":["All synthetic propositions which express the condition without which it is quite impossible to know an object are objective. Those without which it is impossible to know it a priori [or] completely are subjective.\nAll our knowledge (g a priori) always has *correlata*; if one of these is lacking, we cannot know the other completely. All our knowledge of magnitude is possible only as determined through limits; hence we cannot know magnitude in general absolutely and completely, since here we are not to set any limits.",[]],"reflm:5033":["In arbitrary concepts one can very easily become aware of what one thinks in them. Hence the evidence of mathematics.",[]],"reflm:5034":["The antithesis of possibility (g priority) and actuality. No thing is possible without something actual. There is no actuality unless possibility is presupposed. The analysis of the transcendental concepts is the whole ontological doctrine, but the synthesis contains nothing but antithesis. There is no proposition without antithesis, and why? How do they conflict with one another?",[]],"reflm:5035":["Along the inner margin, crosswise. M XXXV:\nWolff did great things in philosophy; but he merely went straight ahead and extended knowledge, without sifting, altering and remodelling it by means of a special critique. His works are therefore very useful as a magazine of reason, but not as an architectonic |\nM XXXVI:\nof it. Perhaps this, although not indeed to be approved in Wolff himself, is nevertheless in the order of nature: that without correct method the items of information, or at least the attempts of the understanding, should first be multiplied, and only afterwards brought under rules. Children.",[]],"reflm:5036":["I could not have brought these reflections into this state without at the same time attending |\nto the other influences of pure philosophy, which I have completed along with them. For I do not share the opinion of an excellent man, who recommends [ein] that when one has once convinced oneself of something, one should afterwards doubt it no more. In pure philosophy that will not do. The understanding itself has a natural aversion to it. One must precisely weigh the propositions in all manner of applications, and even derive a special proof from these; one must try to assume the opposite, and defer judgment until the truth is evident from all sides.\nI have at all times had before my eyes that I had to deal only with transcendental philosophy, that the boundary of every science must be exactly observed, and that confusion of them serves only to produce illusions. But precisely thereby I have lost much that might have served to recommend the work.",[]],"reflm:5037":["If I achieve only [ver] so much as to convince [people] that the working out of this science must be suspended until this point has been settled, then this work has attained its end.\nAt first I saw this doctrinal system as in a twilight. I tried quite seriously to prove propositions and their opposites, not in order to set up a doctrine of doubt, but because I suspected an illusion of the understanding and wished to discover wherein it lay. The year '69 gave me great light.",[]],"reflm:5038":["In metaphysics fiction (*fictio*) counts not as probatory (*probatoria*) but as supple|\nmentary (*suppletoria*) or defensive (*defensoria*), replicatory (*replicatoria*). E.g. in regard to the possibility of a pure spirit, of the world of spirits, etc.: these are then opposed, as something that can be thought, to the objections drawn from what cannot be thought, and are assumed in themselves only problematically.",[]],"reflm:5039":["Division. The [Gesetz] nomothetic (legislation) of pure reason: 1. negative part, discipline; 2. positive part, canon. Lastly, architectonic.\n1. Transcendental philosophy. 2. Metaphysic. Organon.",[]],"reflm:5040":["Even if, like Hume, I had every embellishment at my command, I should still hesitate to make use of it. It is true that some readers are repelled by dryness. But is it not necessary to repel some, in whose hands the subject would fall into bad hands?",[]],"reflm:5041":["All further explanation of space, time and apperception by means of the understanding is impossible.",[]],"reflm:5042":["M XXXV:\nA concept of reason (world, freedom, necessity) never indicates an object, namely an object of intuition; consequently the question whether the world has a beginning |\nM XXXVI: |\ncan mean only: whether I can expound a pure concept of reason empirically. Here there is really no |\nobjective question. Hence it is also not problematic, but can always be answered with: No; that is, such an object cannot be represented empirically. In metaphysics there is no uncertainty.",[]],"reflm:5043":["The propositions which are seen immediately after the principle of contradiction are not seen from it. For this principle, in the whole extent of its validity, can after all be known convincingly only *in concreto*.",[]],"reflm:5044":["What was at first held to be a doctrine of pure reason is now its discipline, i.e. its training and animadversion. Discipline is a restriction of the powers or inclinations of the mind within their fitting limits. Discipline is negative. Not dogmatic. The spirit must not merely be instructed (*institutio*), but disciplined, i.e. broken of its bad habits.",[]],"reflm:5045":["That two opposites (*opposita*) can be false together but cannot be true together, because [they] in the one case take the subject sensibly and the predicate intellectually, in the second case the reverse. E.g. There is a first beginning of the world, and there is none. In the first, beginning means the supreme ground, which is abiding; in the second, the first member of the series. If now the first is taken sensitively, it is false; so also the second.",[]],"reflm:5046":["In future no synthetic objective modes of knowledge of pure philosophy will be expected. Yet it is good always to proceed in it problematically, in order [the subject] to discover the cause of the origin of the axioms, and the subreptions which turn subjective laws into objective ones.\nThe chief inquiry is this: how do we come to modes of knowledge in general, and especially to those a priori? What is the ground of their correctness and reliability?",[]],"reflm:5047":["Mathematics can hardly be of use in discovering objective philosophical propositions, since it cannot judge of the certainty of the data (*data*). But once these have been settled, a mathematical head can invent a transcendental analysis, just as he invented a universal arithmetic (*arithmeticam vniversalem*).",[]],"reflm:5048":["Mathematics is never empirical, but is either pure (*pura*) or applied (*applicata*). Philosophy, however, can be rational (*rationalis*) or empirical (*empirica*). The former is either pure or applied. Of magnitudes I can accept no principles save a priori. There are no opinions in this domain.",[]],"reflm:5049":["[Inner sense] Consciousness is the intuiting of oneself. It [would] would not be consciousness [so] if it were sensation. In it lies all knowledge, of whatever kind. If I abstract from all sensations, I presuppose consciousness. It is logical personality, [transcendental] not practical personality; the latter is the fac|ulty\nof freedom, by virtue of which one, without being externally determined, can of oneself be a cause. Moral personality is the capacity for incentives of mere reason, by virtue of which a being is capable of laws and therefore also of imputations.",[]],"reflm:5050":["There are errors that cannot be refuted. One must lead the perverse head into [sciences] modes of knowledge which enlighten it; then the error disappears of itself. It is in vain to take from anyone the blind opinion of sympathy. Let him be taught physics (*physiqve*)!",[]],"reflm:5051":["Along the inner margin, crosswise. M XXXVII:\nAll concepts in general, from whatever source they may take their matter, are reflected representations, i.e. representations brought into the logical relation of validity for many. But there are concepts whose |\nM XXXVIII: |\nwhole sense is nothing other than one or another reflection to which representations that present themselves can be subjected. These may be entitled concepts of reflection (*conceptus reflectentes*); and since every kind of reflection occurs in judgment, they will comprehend in themselves absolutely the mere act of the understanding which is applied to the relation in judgment, as grounds of the possibility of judging.",[]],"reflm:5052":["One can, if one wishes, derive everything from fundamental powers, only |\nnot the criterion of truth from a sense of the true and the false that is made specially for distinguishing between them. For then every touchstone of our judgments is lost.",[]],"reflm:5053":["Discipline. How negative modes of knowledge keep errors away.",[]],"reflm:5054":["Dialectic is that which contains the arguments *pro et contra*.",[]],"reflm:5055":["Four headings of the concepts of the understanding, with three categories under each. And to these various predicables (*praedicabilia*). The predicables that are mixed, e.g. in the verbs of action and passion, time and number.",[]],"reflm:5056":["Our faculty of invention has its limits; a kind of intuition other than our own cannot be thought, though neither can it be denied.",[]],"reflm:5057":["The rules for the formal use [use] of logical rules, without considering the particular kind of knowledge, are dialectical, namely rules concerning the play of representations according to logical laws, which is properly only a semblance of truth; for knowledge is not true merely because it has this form.\nHere only directions for the power of judgment occur, and there are properly in this no rules but knacks.",[]],"reflm:5058":["In all [allgemeinen] judgments illusion (*Schein*) rests on the confusion of the subjective with the objective, chiefly in principles of reason, where subjective grounds can also be objective grounds a priori.\nIn transcendental science everything must be derived from the subject, and only some of it is related through this to objects. Hence dialectic here is something that belongs to the nature of the understanding, and of it a science is possible.",[]],"reflm:5059":["The subreptions of the power of judgment. 1. That we know everything only through predicates, [so daß] and thus have predicates with an indeterminate subject; and that we take what is always the condition of the remaining predicates to be the first subject. 2. That we take the conditions of distinguishing to be necessary differences in things. 3. That of a subject which is thought inwardly (a thinking being) we predicate conditions of outer presence, and of the outer, e.g. a body, conditions of the inner. Representation.",[]],"reflm:5060":["Illusion and truth both belong to the understanding. We have a concept of things as they are, that is, as they are represented according to a judgment about |\nthem, which agrees with the appearances according to universal laws. What agrees only with the particular conditions of appearance is an illusion.",[]],"reflm:5061":["In regard to rules in general the following faults are committed. 1. Universal rules without particular determination, e.g. [Ub] to avoid indistinctness. 2. Many particular determinations without universal principles. The rules drawn from reason concerning a common object all have one principle, e.g. laws.\nAll reflection upon method is the most important part of a science. Method is either constitutive and scientific, or dialectical and a mere manner. Idea.",[]],"reflm:5062":["Other sciences can grow gradually through united effort and further additions. The philosophy of pure reason must be sketched out all at once, because here the point is first to determine the nature of knowledge itself and its universal laws and conditions, and not to try out one's power of judgment on the off chance.",[]],"reflm:5063":["Across the inner margin:\nTranscendental dialectic is the critique of illusion, just as the analytic is the doctrine of truth. Among the ancients it was for the most part |\na technique of illusion, or a sophistical art. The critique of illusion comes after philosophy. The critique of taste is no doctrine; taste [leid] gives rules and indeed admits of restrictions, but not precepts. One ought not to have given it the name of a science, least of all one borrowed from an old designation that has a quite wide sense.",[]],"reflm:5064":["The analysis of the concepts we already have falls far short of sufficing for a knowledge of things *in concreto*. By a synthesis, in which we consider the concept in many cases *in concreto*, we must gather up much that belongs essentially to the concept but does not lie in it. Otherwise such analyses are empty (Baumgarten: *perfectio phaenomenon*). It is just as if Philolaus said the earth moves, and Copernicus proved it.",[]],"reflm:5065":["I have a very high opinion of the genius of the ancients. We act as a corps, which not only makes genius dispensable (like bravery and skill with weapons) but even hinders it.",[]],"reflm:5066":["I am quite content if the best of these doctrines now set forth is found among the ancients. That a judgment of the understanding |\nwas already present in the sound concept of others is to be presumed, and it serves to unite the understanding of many. But I understood Locke's passage *de iudiciis coexistentiae* (on judgments of coexistence) only afterwards.",[]],"reflm:5067":["Those who, following the example of the Newtonians, assumed certain fundamental acts of the knowledge of truth did not consider that a force which is first in itself, with its own peculiar effects, such as gravity, may well have to be assumed. But a force originally to know the true, [weil dieses] that is, to know objects according to their true constitution, cannot be assumed without showing how either the objects [der E] of this fundamental force, or at least their representation, are given through it.",[]],"reflm:5068":["Belief in God and in another world is a consequence of the necessary maxims of theoretical as well as practical reason.",[]],"reflm:5069":["The sceptical method gives a proof *a posteriori* for the discipline of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:5070":["Transcendental philosophy is very necessary, for in an empirical philosophy we can be instructed about our errors by experience.\nIt is strange that here a critique, and so first attempts at modes of knowledge, must come before a canon can be established; for a doctrine it will never become.",[]],"reflm:5071":["If something intellectual is considered under a sensible condition, then with sensitive predicates (*praedicata sensitiva*) both opposites are false; e.g. *Qvod non est in loco, nec est mobile nec immobile* (What is not in a place is neither movable nor immovable). But these predicates are not really opposed contradictorily, but *contrarie realiter opposita* (really opposed as contraries). If God is not in places, then he is neither in one place nor in many places.",[]],"reflm:5072":["In all systems of reason something has always been left over, and reason has grown successively. Mathematics keeps what it has acquired, and its store increases as new things are added daily. In natural science, from Aristotle down to the present, something has always remained, even from false systems, after it had been sifted; but metaphysics annihilates itself entirely, in that it makes room for another. The ground has not been investigated. What was taken for the ground were the first stones that were laid, which slowly sank into a marshy foundation. This compels us to regard the methods with suspicion and to investigate the sources in the subject.",[]],"reflm:5073":["The critique of pure reason is a preservative against a sickness of reason which has its germ in our nature [nemlich]. It is the opposite of the inclination that binds us to our fatherland |\n(homesickness): a longing to lose ourselves beyond our own circle and to move into other worlds.",[]],"reflm:5074":["On discipline. One cannot put intellectual concepts in the place of appearances, e.g. in space, of which the latter would be only confused representations, e.g. *ens obscure repraesentativum* (an obscurely representative being).\nWhether nature is an automaton, or whether influences from outside the world also take place in it. Providence.\nPreface. To isolate sensibility and understanding.\nTranscendental ideas are concepts from principles of speculation, not of the intellection of appearances; hence they concern the absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances.",[]],"reflm:5075":["In transcendental science the concern is no longer to go forward but to go back.",[]],"reflm:5076":["Across the inner margin:\nThe boundary of sensibility. Reason cannot restrict it by objections. Reason [cannot do so] by conditions, nor by foisting anything upon it. Meanwhile it has a genuine territory with respect to its fundamental principles.",[]],"reflm:5077":["Speculative maxims of reason pertain to the appearances in the world and in nature, likewise to the limits of the world and the original being, in order to obtain extension and unity; they make use of the analogies of experience.",[]],"reflm:5078":["There is a remarkable difference between mathematical universal formulae and philosophical ones. The former are recognized as correct only when one is conscious of their truth *in concreto*. Hence they are never empty. In transcendental philosophy nothing is objectively uncertain, because everything rests merely on the mere relation to object [the text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5079":["Much that belongs to analysis and to critique can still be gleaned from ancients and moderns. But then what befell the Scholastics will happen: they will be laid aside for ever. The cause of metaphysics can no longer be maintained.",[]],"reflm:5080":["Among the maxims of reason belongs also this: that nature everywhere makes a system, although our knowledge is seldom one.",[]],"reflm:5081":["Baumgarten: The man was keen-sighted (in small things), but not |\nfar-sighted (in great things)*. Instead of his aesthetic, the phrase *critique of the beautiful* fits better.\n*(g A Cyclops of a metaphysician, who lacks the one eye, namely critique.)\n(g a good analyst, but not an architectonic philosopher; his outlines of sciences.)",[]],"reflm:5082":["The metaphysical obstacle to all morals is the denial of freedom, and to all theology the denial of necessity.",[]],"reflm:5083":["Across the inner margin:\nTranscendental philosophy proves that with our knowledge we can never go out beyond the world of sense. If they seem to be more universal, they are merely subjective. That even mathematics is the only science which, independently of experience, and so wholly *a priori*, can determine something; but that the qualities (*qvalitaeten*) must be given empirically.",[]],"reflm:5084":["Across the inner margin:\nWhen one leafs back and forth, nothing can seem more pedantic, although it is aimed quite precisely at the abolition of everything pedantic in matters that concern the nature of the soul, the future and the origin of all things. But human understanding errs here through subtlety, and must be refuted by it.",[]],"reflm:5085":["Across the inner margin: M XXXXIII:\nThat [crit] transcendental philosophy as critique should not itself be purged away along with the cathartic of the Pyrrhonian, but must necessarily be treated with diligence, just as weeds must always be rooted out, since human understanding contains the seed of them; nor can the vices be wholly destroyed |\nM XXXXIV: |\nat one stroke, any more than the pox.",[]],"reflm:5086":["In the world of understanding the *substratum* is intelligence; action and cause: freedom; community: happiness from freedom; the original being: an intelligence through idea; the form: morality; the *nexus*: a *nexus* of ends. This world of understanding already now lies at the ground of the world of sense, and is the truly self-subsistent.",[]],"reflm:5087":["M XXXXII:\n* There are only four sources of knowledge: 1. the senses, 2. the understanding, 3. divine communication, 4. mystical illumination and intuition. The last we are not entitled to assume, because it is presumption. Nor can [those who claim it] be convinced. The senses and the function of the understanding constitute all knowledge. This is not written for those who partake of the inner light.\nM XXXXI:\n*(g If only they could cite something in natural science that anyone had discovered through this intuition without going the way of abstraction; and if they cannot, how can they hope to go beyond it?)",[]],"reflm:5088":["The unity of the appearance for the understanding lies in the analogies of experience. The unity for reason of the principles of these different causes of the appearance: where does it lie? and how is it objective? Likewise, how does it lead to the existence of God and of another world? Likewise, to the unity of ends?",[]],"reflm:5089":["M XXXXI:\nDiscipline. We cannot judge synthetically through mere concepts of the understanding. They must always contain [data of the] conditions of appearances, and serve only for their exposition. Mere concepts of the understanding, no more than an intuition of the understanding, can relate to an object. This is always determined through sensibility, for our general concept is only a sign for the concrete. An intellectual cosmology is, just as much as a mystical one, an *ens rationis* (a being of reason). The usual scholastic and doctrinal method of metaphysics makes one stupid, in that it produces a mechanical thoroughness. It narrows the understanding and makes it incapable of accepting instruction; it is not philosophy. Critique, on the contrary, enlarges concepts and |\nM XXXXII: |\nmakes reason free. The school philosophers act like freebooters, who, as soon as they land on an unoccupied coast, at once entrench themselves.",[]],"reflm:5090":["We have something in our heads, and have grasped it, only when we can produce it out of ourselves, or at least in ourselves. One has words in the head only when one can pronounce them inwardly. (This requires a consciousness of one's organs.) We have grasped what a line is when we can draw it in thought; and the con|nection of things in the world we can know only when we can produce it through a general action, and so from a principle of inner potency. Substance, ground, composition.",[]],"reflm:5091":["The knowledge of pure (speculative) reason can never be applied further than to the field of experiences, and beyond their limit has no meaning whatever. Mathematics itself contains nothing that cannot be demonstrated in experiences.\nNecessity in the empirical sense, and freedom, signify [things] on the one hand what is at all times according to a rule, on the other what is not determined through sensibility but subjectively through the synthesis of the understanding.\nSulzer in his *Kleine Schriften* hopes in vain for demonstration.\nOne can here survey what, and how much, can be discovered.",[]],"reflm:5092":["To the dialectic of pure reason belongs also the sophistical reasoning against space and time as *realia* (real things) or *idealia* (ideal things), and likewise against infinite division.",[]],"reflm:5093":["Principles of pure reason are these: that an absolute completeness of the presuppositions of synthesis be assumed. Hence the *principium necessitatis, contingentiae, compositionis et decompositionis* (principle of necessity, of contingency, of composition and decomposition). They contain the condition of the absolute [unity] collective unity (systematic) [for] of knowledge in general. In reason there is a *nexus* of prosyllogisms and episyllogisms. The completeness of this synthesis rests on the completeness of the principles as well as of their application.",[]],"reflm:5094":["The concept of a substance already carries with it the concept of freedom; for if I could not act independently of external determination, my action would be only the action of another, and I should properly be the action of another, and so not a substance.",[]],"reflm:5095":["It is impossible that appearances should yield anything unbounded (g and absolute), whether in extension or division, or in causality, or in the condition of existence in general. These negative propositions are therefore objective and dogmatic; reason, on the other hand, demands the absolute and complete in synthesis (*principia a priori*, principles a priori). These are objectively practical, just as the former are objectively speculative.",[]],"reflm:5096":["The efforts of the metaphysicians have not all been in vain. Only, they have no permanence without a system of critique. They had this in thought.",[]],"reflm:5097":["One must make everything hard at the outset for presumptuous thinkers. They should not boldly appeal to their sound reason. They are not travelling in a cultivated country.",[]],"reflm:5098":["Am I travelling on a plain or on a spheroid? The curvature I must measure.",[]],"reflm:5099":["*Materia et forma* (matter and form).\n*Logica et realis* (logical and real).\n*Conceptus*\n*praedicati* (concept of the predicate).\n* *Consensus et oppositio* (agreement and opposition).\n[*Logica est*]\n*realitas* (reality)\n*negatio* (negation)\n*Absolutum et relativum* (the absolute and the relative).\n*Logica Identitas et diversitas realis* (logical identity and diversity; real).\n*(g *vel inter se*\n*vel respectu*\n*tertii; posterior est [vel]*\n*identitas et diversitas.*)\n[i.e. either among themselves or with respect to a third; the latter is identity and diversity.]",[]],"reflm:5100":["Across the inner margin:\n1. Morality: how I become worthy to be happy, without regard to the means of becoming a partaker of happiness. 2. Worldly prudence: how I become happy, without regard to my worthiness to be so. 3. Wisdom: how I become happy through becoming worthy to be so. Philosophy is the organon of wisdom, and must deal with the determination of my nature, and with the limits and ends of my faculties. The first has solely *principia a priori* (principles a priori), the second *a posteriori*, the third from both.",[]],"reflm:5101":["Locke thinks to get through with the claim that we have just as little a distinct concept of body as of spirit.",[]],"reflm:5102":["Those who confine themselves to what is generally accepted and is current, as it were by agreement, and therefore seems universally intelligible, have an easy game in displaying their shallowness to advantage; they spread easily, because they feel no difficulties and feel themselves bound nowhere; they seem to extend knowledge and gain esteem, because, as with bold persons, everything they do with confidence also appears to become them as reliable.",[]],"reflm:5103":["The intelligible world has laws according to which I fit into any world whatever, not merely into this one or the world of sense, into whatever arrangement of my own and outer nature or society I may come. It has its own *principium constitutivum* (constitutive principle): God, and *regulativum* (regulative principle): moral laws. It fits with the rules of prudence, if these are enlarged; it is not an object of intuition; no physiology of it is possible; it is an object of belief as to its substance, but of the reflecting understanding as to its general laws.\nWe also know God only through moral grounds, and through attributes bound up with morality. To establish the connection of the other world with this one, the transition is grounded upon analogical inferences. The principles, however, of inference by analogy, and the *argumenta practica κατ' ανθρωπον* (practical arguments, relative to man), to which the former also relate, constitute the *transitus* (transition) (*argumentum ad modulum humanitatis*, argument according to the measure of humanity) *secundum assumta humanae naturae, non hominis singularis* (according to the assumptions of human nature, not of the individual man).",[]],"reflm:5104":["The maxims of reason consist in assuming constitutive unity in the whole of appearances (if one begins a priori) and regulative unity in the parts (if one proceeds analytically from the parts to the whole). Hence that no causes are to be assumed save those whose law can be found through observation (though the ground of this law itself is not thereby ascertainable). Hence no spirits, no blind arbitrariness, etc. etc. But in actions, freedom a priori. That nothing be derived immediately from God, because his rules of action cannot be observed, although a priori God is regarded as the supreme principle of unity according to rules, even in the practical. In sum: that the understanding obtain its unity according to totality (constitutively), and the unity of rules. It does not, then, limit itself, but yet it demands a first as the principle of its synthesis.",[]],"reflm:5105":["Dogmatic principles are (g determining) modes of knowledge of reason through concepts. Hence the mathematical are not dogmatic; nor are the principles of the empirical use of reason from concepts, for these do not determine; still less the principles of pure reason, but only the moral.",[]],"reflm:5106":["Anyone who wishes to judge the products of pure reason literarily, that is, by wide reading, undertakes a fruitless labour. He can thereby provide himself with objects for judgment. But since he has not subjected his own understanding to a critique, his criticism is still always idiotic [idiotisch] and not philosophical. It makes use of principles whose examination is really the very aim. Philodox instead of philosopher.",[]],"reflm:5107":["The words »lediglich, blos, allein, nur« [merely, solely, alone, only], as against the words »überhaupt, schlechthin, schlechterdings« [in general, absolutely, utterly]. The former are words not of limits [Schranken] but of the *actus* of limitation [Einschränkung]. The words »an, durch, zu« [at/on, through, to] are the [relations (Verhaltnisse)] functions of the categories.",[]],"reflm:5108":["Among the learned living languages German is the only one that has a purity peculiar to itself. All foreign words remain forever recognizable in it, whereas English and French can be filled with such words without one's being able to notice that they had come to them from elsewhere. It is therefore worth the trouble to attend to this, and to prefer to use foreign words in parentheses. This attention gradually makes the language rich, and at the same time very significant and precise.\nLimits must be set to the new compounding of words.\nThe universal element of a language, and idiom [idiotismus].",[]],"reflm:5109":["M XXXXVII:\nThe original, as magnitude, is unlimited. As a thing in general, it is simple. Original action (g origin itself) is free. Original existence is absolutely necessary. Everything externally derived is limited; as something given in appearance (g *Mundus sensibilis*), it is composite; as having arisen, it is conditioned and dependent; as existing, it is conditionally necessary. The soul is not an appearance. In it lies, relatively, the whole [All] of the reality of all pos|sible\nappearances. It is, in respect of sensations, simple (I); in respect of actions [or] free; in respect of all the existence of appearances, a necessary *substratum*, which is subordinated to no appearance.\nWe have two principles (g a priori) of the empirical use of the understanding: principles of the exposition of appearances, that is, of the determination [Bestimmung] of concepts from them; and, second, of the architectonic use of reason in relation to the practical. The latter are merely intellectual.\nM XXXXVIII:\nIt is a necessary hypothesis of the theoretical and practical use of reason in the whole of our knowledge, and consequently in relation to all ends and to an intelligible world, to assume that an intelligible world underlies the sensible, of which the soul, as intelligence, is the subjective archetype, while an original intelligence is the cause. That is to say: as the *noumenon* in us stands to the appearances, so does the supreme intelligence stand in respect of the *mundus intelligibilis* [intelligible world]. For the soul really contains within itself the condition of all possible appearances, and in it all of them could be determined a priori, if only the data were given at the outset.\n(g Reason, *mundus intelligibilis*. Not a new synthesis, but one completed through limits a priori.)\nAmong appearances there is no end. The limit of them, and so the completion of the synthesis, lies in the *mundus intelligibilis*, according to the analogy of our soul and intelligence.\nThe principles: Everything in the world consists of the permanent and the changeable, which inheres in the former. Everything changeable, every state, has a cause. And: everything that acts upon [einfließt] anything is in community. This knowledge of the *mundus phaenomenus* [world of appearances] is never completed. But in the *mundus intelligibilis* everything must be completed; consequently it refers |\nto the *ens originarium* [original being], where all this takes place unconditionally. No decomposition is completed.\nM XXXXVII:\nThe [determinability (Bestimmbarkeit)] unity of the (g absolute) whole of our knowledge, determinable [bestimmbar] through reason, is the cause of the synthesis extended beyond the world of sense, that is, the synthesis of that world with the intellectual. From ideas. The idea of intelligence, independent of appearances; and of the *mundus intelligibilis*, which remains over; and of the transcendental principle [*principium*] [of] the unity of everything.",[]],"reflm:5110":["Reason, in respect of freedom and of ends in general, is determining, and freedom determines appearances. In respect of these there must be a first and sole ground of everything.",[]],"reflm:5111":["1. No absolute completion of the use of reason in sensibility.\n2. Liberation of reason from its restricting conditions. Antinomy of reason: there is a completion of composition [*compositio*], of decomposition [*decompositio*], of generation and of dependence, and there is also none. The completion is to be found only in the *mundus intelligibilis* and in its principle of unity, the *ens originarium*.",[]],"reflm:5112":["The mathematician, the man of fine wit, the natural philosopher: what do they accomplish when they indulge in arrogant mockery of metaphysics? Within them lies the call that summons them at all times to [make] an attempt in its field. If as human beings they do not seek their final ends in the satisfaction of the aims of this life, they cannot avoid asking: whence am I, whence is the whole? The astronomer is summoned to these questions all the more. He cannot refrain from seeking something that will satisfy him in this matter. With the first judgment that he passes on it, he is in the territory of metaphysics. Is he now to rely here, without any guidance, merely on the persuasions that may grow up [bey] in him, though he has no map of the field that he wishes to traverse? Into this darkness the critique of reason sets up its torch; yet it illuminates not the regions beyond the world of sense, which are unknown to us, but the dark space of our own understanding.\nMetaphysics is, as it were, the police of our reason in respect of the public security of morals and religion.",[]],"reflm:5113":["Across the inner margin:\nThe concept of completion [is] either through magnitude (the whole [All]), or through the limit, or through purity and separation from every attached condition (e.g. possibility, necessity), or again the absolute, which hangs on no [possibility] condition.",[]],"reflm:5114":["Dialectic:\nIt is not only that illusion [Schein] often deceives; it is also necessary to explain how it comes about that I am deceived, e.g. at the rising of the moon.",[]],"reflm:5115":["It is a strange fate of human understanding that, whether through a natural inclination or through [the importance and] the true interest that drives it, it sees itself entangled in, and as it were condemned to, a science which, after centuries of effort with the united strength of the most acute minds, still cannot be brought a single step further. If one wishes to give up the effort (g with resentment), [we would partly through a particular inclination] the natural movement of our mind draws us back to it; and besides, we come upon questions everywhere concerning our most important concern, in respect of which we can be satisfied only by some insight in this field. Of this kind I know, fortunately for mankind, only a single science, namely metaphysics, a theoretical philosophy of pure reason, that is, of reason free from all sources of experience. It is the stone of Sisyphus, which one rolls ceaselessly without ever bringing it to its lasting place. When I say that it has not been brought a single step further, I do not mean by this the analysis of the concepts of reason; for that is nothing other than a greater clarification of what we already know, and in this several have accomplished much in the exact determination [Bestimmung] of the meanings of words. But that is not what is sought; what is sought is modes of knowledge [Erkentnisse] of objects that cannot be taught us by any senses, that are therefore not inherent in us, but are to be sought out and created. It is in respect of these that all labour up to now has been in vain. One can easily convince oneself of this by considering merely that even a single item of knowledge [Erkenntnis] that is a determinate contribution to science must be ac|cepted\nas such by everyone, e.g. air in bodies, whereas these [the metaphysical claims] come into vogue in certain places only through persuasion.",[]],"reflm:5116":["M LI:\nI have not always judged of this science as I do now. At first I learnt from it whatever recommended itself to me most. In some matters I believed I could contribute something of my own to the common treasure, in others I found something to improve, but always with the aim of thereby acquiring dogmatic insights. For the doubt that was so boldly pronounced seemed to me so much ignorance in the tone of reason that I gave it no hearing. When one reflects in real earnest in order to find the truth, one in the end no longer spares one's own products, even though it may seem that they promise us some merit in the science. What one has learnt, or thought out for oneself, one subjects wholly to critique [Critick]. It was long before I found in this way the whole dogmatic theory dialectical. But I sought something certain, if not in respect of the object, yet in respect of the nature and the limits of this mode of knowledge [Erkentnisart]. I found gradually that many of the propositions which we regard as objective are in fact subjective, that is, contain the *conditiones* (conditions) under which alone we have insight into the object or comprehend it. By this, however, I was made cautious, but not instructed. For since there are indeed modes of knowledge a priori which are not merely analytic but extend our knowledge, I lacked a critique of pure reason brought under rules, | \nand above all a canon of it; for I still believed that I should find the method of extending dogmatic knowledge through pure reason. For this |\nM LII: |\nI now needed insight into how a knowledge a priori is possible at all.",[]],"reflm:5117":["All maxims of pure reason are principles of the unity of reason, in its empirical as well as in its transcendent use. What disturbs this unity of its use is to be rejected.",[]],"reflm:5118":["The world can have a beginning only for sensible intuition. For the beginning is a limit of the appearance *ab antecedenti* (in respect of what precedes). But such an intuition itself presupposes a sensible subject, which itself belongs to the world and so can never intuit a first beginning. The world therefore has no beginning. But this limitlessness belongs not to the magnitude of the things that appear, but to their appearance. For the pure understanding the world has an origin.",[]],"reflm:5119":["M IL:\nMetaphysics, so far as it would go further than the pure principles of the understanding in respect of experiences, has throughout no other than a negative use, alike in regard to the nature of the corporeal and of the thinking, and in relation to what lies beyond or\n|\nabove nature. The latter modes of knowledge must be known by analogy with the empirical principles extended beyond the limit [fortg] of the world, and hence as maxims of the universal unity of reason. In that case the metaphysical concepts serve [den] polemically against dogmatic doubt, in order to set limits to its objections.\nA physical utility of metaphysics is also to hold off the dogmatic synthesis a priori, which can hinder the continuity of knowledge according to laws of experience, and to that extent it serves to extend that knowledge: 1. that one should not come upon last parts; 2. that the differing density should not |\nM LII: |\npresuppose empty spaces; 3. that there be no immediate material influence without contact; 4. that the world have limits. The first and second assertions are principles of reason.\nUtility in (g rational) psychology: 1. that not all substances are material; 2. that experience is not the limit of all knowledge, and this world not the world in general.\nIn theology: 1. that the world does not comprehend all things; 2. that not everything is contingent, etc. etc.\nThe utility is therefore throughout negative: 1. to remove dogmatic negations which limit the empirical extension of knowledge; 2. to limit dogmatic assertions which would extend reason uselessly beyond its practical use.",[]],"reflm:5120":["Relation of appearances to the unity of the world. Unity of the system, that is, of the All.\nThe unity of the world is the unity of nature in the whole. The last [die letzte] is the unity of nature and has principles of the understanding. The other has merely principles of reason, according to which nature in the whole is referred to something outside nature as a ground of its possibility. For the connection of nature is contingent, because it rests on many beings. The ground of it must be unity of cause. Unity of nature is therefore |\nderived from the original unity of the original being [Urwesen]. Absolute unity is unity of reason. The absolute All is opposed to the hypothetical. The absolute unity of the synthesis rests on the conditions of absolute universality.",[]],"reflm:5121":["The only insoluble metaphysical difficulty is that of uniting the supreme condition of everything practical with the condition of speculative unity: that is, freedom with nature, or with the causality of the understanding in respect of appearances. For freedom is the possibility of actions from causes of the understanding. The spontaneity of the understanding in the series of appearances is the riddle. Next, absolute necessity is the second riddle, which nature does not set, but the pure understanding. The latter is the original condition of the possibility of nature. In the first case an appearance is not necessary but contingent, under the conditions of appearance. In the second case something is necessary without any condition: thus [we have] the first absolutely contingent and the first necessary.",[]],"reflm:5122":["Whether one can say through mere transcendental concepts: everything that exists is in substance; [hat] the contingent has a ground; and: whatever is found outside one another stands in a relation of possible community. The definitions of these concepts must then turn out differently.",[]],"reflm:5123":["If the actuality is to be proved of something whose concept cannot be other than transcendent, e.g. (g the *realissimum* [most real being]), the simple, freedom, absolute necessity, then the proof can |\nnever be conducted from experience, but only from transcendental grounds.",[]],"reflm:5124":["The proposition that synthetic propositions a priori find place only as principles of the possibility and exposition of experiences is independent of the explanation of the categories. For where else should they come from?\nAnalytic judgments, because they do not extend [knowledge], are not on that account to be despised.",[]],"reflm:5125":["My author Baumgarten is an excellent man in judgments of elucidation [Erläuterungsurtheilen], but when he passes to judgments of extension [erweiterungsurtheilen], which are chiefly what is demanded in metaphysics, he is without foundation.",[]],"reflm:5126":["An *organon* is either according to form or according to content. The *organon* according to mere form (of a mode of knowledge of reason) is the doctrine of method. Thus [we have] the transcendental *organon* is only the doctrine of method.",[]],"reflm:5127":["The first part of transcendental philosophy is that of immanent |\nknowledge a priori: analytic; the second that of the transcendent: dialectic. Both are parts of the critique.",[]],"reflm:5128":["Since we have modes of knowledge a priori which yet extend only to experiences, we thereby extend to things in general, because those modes of knowledge are not borrowed from experiences.",[]],"reflm:5129":["A science of things in general really abstracts from all difference and determinations of things as objects, and so treats merely of pure reason: transcendental philosophy.",[]],"reflm:5130":["At the lower margin:\nTranscendental philosophy has two parts: critique of pure reason and ontology.",[]],"reflm:5131":["At the very top of the page:\nOntology is the science of the first modes of knowledge of the pure understanding: 1. of concepts, analytic; 2. of judgments.",[]],"reflm:5132":["On the difference of concepts, intuitions and sensations. On empirical and pure intuitions and such concepts.",[]],"reflm:5133":["Transcendental philosophy (s which sets forth the elements of our knowledge a priori) is a science of the possibility of a synthetic knowledge a priori.",[]],"reflm:5134":["We may well hold impossible what contradicts itself; but not what contradicts the conditions under which alone we can know the object. What does not contradict itself is not on that account possible, but [the text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5135":["Something in the logical sense is an object; in the transcendental sense it is something or nothing, that is, possible or impossible.",[]],"reflm:5136":["In and\n*subiecti contradictorii nullum est praedicatum* (of a contradictory subject there is no predicate).",[]],"reflm:5137":["Above and\n*Principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction) is the negative universal principle, but not the only one.",[]],"reflm:5138":["*Principium contradictionis*: the restricting, not the constitutive, principle of all knowledge; *conditio sine qua non* (indispensable condition).",[]],"reflm:5139":["The *principium contradictionis* is the restricting, not the constitutive, principle.",[]],"reflm:5140":["Above and\nAn object in general is possible or impossible.",[]],"reflm:5141":["Impossible is: 1. that whose thought contradicts itself;\n—\n— : 2. that to which no object corresponds.",[]],"reflm:5142":["Being and not-being is the first; logically: copula, synthetic, analytic; really: possibility, actuality, necessity.",[]],"reflm:5143":["Object either possible or impossible.",[]],"reflm:5144":["Logical possibility, actuality, necessity, held to be real (of things).",[]],"reflm:5145":["On the first four words of M § 13:\nAlthough the thought does not contradi— [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:5146":["Beneath and\nA thing can be undetermined in relation to a concept; posited in itself it is thoroughly determined; 'posited in itself' signifies: relation to the whole possible concept.",[]],"reflm:5147":["Because the matter of possibility must be given to us, and we cannot think it beforehand, all thinking concerns the form. An object of thinking in general, for the human understanding, is *formaliter*: what does not contradict itself; and *realiter*: what is given.",[]],"reflm:5148":["Three principles. 1. Of contradiction, 2. of ground, 3. of determination.",[]],"reflm:5149":["The relation in a judgment is either analytic or synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5150":["The possibility of things according to mere categories cannot be discerned.",[]],"reflm:5151":["The supreme principles are such only in respect of form, because they apply to |\nsomething in general. [They prescribe] not that from which, but that according to which, judgment is to be made.",[]],"reflm:5152":["Through something's being actual, it can become an object of intuition. But if one abstracts from all actuality, it still remains an object of knowledge. This is possibility.",[]],"reflm:5153":["Beside and \n3 principles: 1. of possibility, 2. of truth, 3. of the necessity of judgments; the last always says *aut aut* (either–or), in order to prove apodictically and apagogically.",[]],"reflm:5154":["The *principium determinationis* (principle of determination) is distinguished from the *principium omnimodae determinationis* (principle of thoroughgoing determination).",[]],"reflm:5155":["The possibility of the concept (logical) is that the concept subsists in itself; that of the thing (real), that an object corresponds to the concept. [Real possibility is] therefore the relation to the universal conditions through which something in general is given.",[]],"reflm:5156":["Above and \nNothing [*Nihil*]. 1. *negativum* (negative), 2. *privativum* (privative), 3. *conceptus inanis* (empty concept), 4. *intuitus mere formalis* (merely formal intuition).",[]],"reflm:5157":["In the margin at left \nExplication and derivation, contradiction and opposition.",[]],"reflm:5158":["In the margin at right \nComparison, combination, connection.",[]],"reflm:5159":["Comparison, coherence, combination.",[]],"reflm:5160":["In the remarks on M § 14:\nIf I posit [the ground] something at will, and something else is also posited, then the former is the ground.",[]],"reflm:5161":["Which of the two, a or b, that stand in the relation of ground to consequent, is *hypothesis*? Relation of connection:\n*Categoria*\n*subiecti*,\n— —\n*principii*,\n— —\n*compositi*.\n(Category of the subject, of the principle, of the composite.)",[]],"reflm:5162":["The *absolutum* is that which is considered in every relation; because we cannot do this *in concreto* [kön] (i.e. *in casu vel qvaestione data*, in a given case or question), we consider it internally, and what holds positively holds also in every relation, but not so the negative.\nLater (φ) the following was written into the Reflexion with another ink:\nThe absolute the restrictions",[]],"reflm:5163":["What accords with the conditions of a concept in general is possible. What exists itself yields a concept. Mere possibility: what accords with the conditions of a concept *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5164":["5. E II 1369.\nM 5':\nBetween two logically opposed terms [*logice oppositis*] there is no third, but between really opposed terms [*realiter oppositis*] (gladly, unwillingly) there is the indifferent = 0∗. Motion and rest are not *contradictorie opposita* (contradictory opposites). For both fail to accord with God and with the world-whole. Opposites of this kind are both restricted by a condition, here that of existence in space; if the condition drops away, there is no contradiction. E.g. The actions of men are as appearances |\nM 5: |\nnot free, but as *intellectualia* free. The one proposition has here one condition, the other an opposite one; in this case the *oppositum praedicati* (opposite of the predicate) can be said also of the other, and thus two seeming *opposita* can be |\ntrue together. There is a necessary being *in serie ascendente* (in the ascending series), but not *descendente* (in the descending). In the one case it is a hypothesis of reason (g on presupposition of the members); in the other no such hypothesis is possible, for the former is conditioned, the latter categorical.\nM 5': |\n∗(s The first can be discerned, the second not.)",[]],"reflm:5165":["In and \nThe synthetic conditions (g principles) of possibility are objective only if they contain the conditions of the possibility of experience, not of comprehension.\nThere is a possibility where *data* for thinking are given; there is no possibility where there are no *data* for thinking.",[]],"reflm:5166":["In and \nThe logical concept of possibility: *principium contradictionis* (g analysis); or the real concept: *principia* of synthesis.",[]],"reflm:5167":["5. E II 505.\nSubjectively, representations A and B can follow one another in this order or also conversely. In order that the sequence may hold objectively, i.e. serve for experience, it must be determined, so that I cannot reverse it.\nWhy is the *principium rationis sufficientis* (principle of sufficient reason) put forward, next after the *principium contradictionis*, as the sole law?\nThe principle of |\nM 5: |\ndetermination, derivation and division.",[]],"reflm:5168":["In the case of *ratio* (ground) and *rationatum* (what is grounded), the consequence must necessarily be thought not objectively but subjectively, namely: that if I posit the ground, the *rationatum* is then posited.",[]],"reflm:5169":["The connection of the ground with the consequent is synthetic or analytic.",[]],"reflm:5170":["A mode of knowledge must 1. be possible as knowledge; 2. be true, i.e. grounded.",[]],"reflm:5171":["Every judgment has its ground, but not every thing is a consequent.",[]],"reflm:5172":["The expression *synthesis* is more general than *nexus* (connection).",[]],"reflm:5173":["The ground: *qvo posito* (that, being posited), if I posit it at will, something else is posited.",[]],"reflm:5174":["Possible, impossible∗ and empty.\n∗(g impossible is a = –a; for a – a = 0 is possible.)",[]],"reflm:5175":["We cannot pass off much as possible [for], nor yet as impossible, and yet no rational man ought to believe it. Ghosts.",[]],"reflm:5176":["Besides the *principium identitatis et contradictionis* (principle of identity and of contradiction) there must be other *principia* of *nexus* and of opposition. For through those only the *nexus* and *oppositio logica* (logical opposition), not the real, can be discerned. Which, then, are these *principia synthetica* (synthetic principles)?",[]],"reflm:5177":["5. E II 831.\nLogically impossible is that of which the thought itself is impossible; metaphysically impossible would be that of which the thought is indeed possible but the thing impossible. But this we can neither assume nor reject. Physically possible is what is possible according to the laws and conditions of nature. Morally.\nM 5:\nThe metaphysical possibility of a thing in itself, so far as it also cannot be an object of experience.",[]],"reflm:5178":["In and \nThe possibility of a concept [is determined] from the principle of contradiction; the possibility of the thing is not.",[]],"reflm:5179":["In and \nIn every relation (absolutely) it is possible to be upright, but not always to be happy: that is, to make oneself worthy of happiness, but not a partaker of it.",[]],"reflm:5180":["Under and \nThe possibility of things [is known] only through intuition, or as a principle of experience.",[]],"reflm:5181":["What is possible only under a certain condition (g presupposition), its possibility is restricted by the condition; what is possible in a certain case, its possibility is extended, until that which is possible in every respect is possible absolutely, i.e. without condition.\nConditional possibility belongs to the *causata* (things caused), absolute possibility to the first cause. Possibility *in abstracto* is merely the non-contradictory; this possibility signifies only the admissibility of the idea. Possibility *in concreto* alone is objective, i.e. that something be possible in its *omnimoda determinatio interna* (thoroughgoing internal determination). What is possible *omni respectu* (in every respect) is necessary, because it is then possible *absqve hypothesi* (without hypothesis) |\nand at the same time the ground of everything or the All itself, for it consents with everything.",[]],"reflm:5182":["See § 14. Combined [*verbunden*] are a and b whose position makes up one; [or] those whose combination is necessary according to a universal rule, i.e. of which one can be inferred from the other, are connected [*verknüpft*]; those whose combination is contingent, i.e. etc. etc., are composite [*zusammengesetzt*]. In all combination there is unity; only [in the case of] composition it is arbitrary. The necessary transition from a to b is either that from the inner possibility of b, which presupposes a (*accidens*), or that of actuality (*rationatum*), or that of relation, state (*totum et pars*, whole and part) (g what is reciprocally in combination in respect of determinations). The relation to something else constitutes the concept of an *accidens*, e.g. of thought, of motion. The former is a *principium essendi* (principle of being), the second a *principium fiendi* (principle of becoming). In the former it lets, from the given concept, on [Text] breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5183":["Above and to the right of the heading »Connexum«, but probably \nThe ground as ground consists in causality; as a thing, however, it can also be posited without a consequent.",[]],"reflm:5184":["The analytic criterion of possibility is that there be no contradiction, and this is not to be discerned. The synthetic criterion of possibility cannot be discerned from concepts. The synthetic conditions of the possibility of experience are at the same time the conditions |\nof the possibility of the objects of experience. This, however, is not the possibility of things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:5185":["*Possibilitas interna et externa* (internal and external possibility), whether for itself or in relation, is something other than conditioned and unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5186":["Conditioned possibility (s the possibility also *in nexu* is greater) is smaller than the absolute, for the latter is not restricted; material possibility *non solum in thesi, sed hypothesi* (not only in thesis but in hypothesis) is greater.",[]],"reflm:5187":["Inner or relative possibility; this is the absolute (in every relation) or the restrictive.",[]],"reflm:5188":["*Nexus* of subordination: through ground. All connection has a ground.\n*Nexus* of coordination: through a common ground.",[]],"reflm:5189":["7. E II 1072.\nThe *principium rationis* (principle of reason), as anticipation, and so in connection with the conditions of sensibility, is objective. (g For in time there is always something that precedes.*)\nThe *principium rationis*, as a postulate of *perspicientia* (insight), is subjective.\nThe *principium terminationis* (principle of limitation), as a demand of comprehension, is subjective.\nIf both are made objective, they conflict with each other; taken subjectively, they do not.\nM 7:\n*(g What is given as contingent (arising, *transitorium*) only [through] by means of sensation, and not a priori from [itself] ourselves, has a ground. (s But 'contingent' is entitled precisely that which exists not for itself but only as *rationatum* (that which is grounded).) Free actions are given a priori. Occurrences, however, or what takes place, are given only through experience and a posteriori. The former is the beginning (*terminus*) [and is] to be known according to a universal law; the latter cannot be known according to universal rules without an antecedent, and for this requires a ground.)",[]],"reflm:5190":["*Principium rationis* in the world (g is cosmological, not transcendental) is a *principium cognoscendi* (principle of knowing), not of acting (*agendi*).",[]],"reflm:5191":["This proposition means: nothing happens save according to a universal rule of the imagination. For that it is represented in the object, i.e. [of] the [represent-] determination by us in the object, comes precisely from this.",[]],"reflm:5192":["Beside and \nThe true proposition is: *Omne contingens est rationatum* (everything contingent is grounded). Only | that whose opposite is in itself possible has a ground; and where the impossibility of the opposite is concealed, the matter cannot be held true until this impossibility has become manifest.\nBut the existence of all things seems to be in itself contingent; at least we do not understand absolute necessity in existence, and our insight extends only to relative position. In this, however, what is relatively posited is always posited by something else, although the *nexus* between ground and consequence breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5193":["Everything in the world has a ground means just as much as: it can be known a priori (s as necessary, either absolutely or conditionally) and stands under a rule of order. *First synthetic proposition a priori. Everything has a ground of knowledge.\nEverything must be capable of being known a priori, i.e. from the act of the mind under which a connection is brought about.\n(g Conversely I cannot infer with certainty, as in no affirmative judgment.)\n*(s All conjugation presupposes the unity of the act in the mind, under which the appearance can be brought; thus, so far as the position of the one is connected with that of the other according to a rule.)",[]],"reflm:5194":["Everything that happens, i.e. upon which something follows, must always follow upon it, |\ni.e. stands, in respect of what precedes, under a rule. Appearances have a rule: 1. that something always precedes; 2. that thereby the consequent is determined (always follows upon it).",[]],"reflm:5195":["That through which something else is posited as determinate is ground; through the consequence a ground is posited as indeterminate.",[]],"reflm:5196":["In the last third of M § 20:\nEverything is necessary, absolutely or conditionally.\nEverything must flow from the universal as something particular; everything can be known a priori.",[]],"reflm:5197":["This principle is a maxim of the use of reason.",[]],"reflm:5198":["Everything whose inner possibility consists in a relation has a ground. This is an identical proposition. All composition. All consequence. What happens. Everything purposive.",[]],"reflm:5199":["Synthetic propositions. Everything is in a substance. 2. Everything contingent has a ground. 3. Everything united has a common |\nground. These same propositions applied to sensibility, e.g. What happens has a ground.",[]],"reflm:5200":["In and \nThe concept of ground gives a rule in general. The whole of the grounds, whether in an aggregate or in a series, is complete.",[]],"reflm:5201":["The non-determining ground is the ground of possibility, e.g. when someone has staked a bet in the lottery.",[]],"reflm:5202":["7'. E II 1085. 1084.\nThe *principium rationis* is the principle of the determination of things in the temporal sequence; for this cannot be determined through time, but time must be determined through the rule of the existence of appearances in the understanding. [It is the] principle of the possibility of experience.\nM 7':\nIt is therefore not possible to determine for things their place in time without presupposing this principle, by which the course of appearances is first made uniform. Succession in the concept of consequence concerns only the sequence [of that which] in positing.",[]],"reflm:5203":["In everything passive, or what is given, apprehension must not merely be met with but also be constrained, in order to represent it as given; |\ni.e. the single apprehension must be determined by the universal. The universal is the relation to the rest and to the whole of the state. By being distinguished from what is arbitrary it is regarded as given, and only by being subsumed under the categories, as something. It must therefore be represented according to a rule, in order that appearance may become experience, and that the mind may grasp it as one of its acts of self-consciousness, in which, as in space and time, all data are met with. The unity of the mind is the condition of thinking, and the subordination of everything particular under the universal is the condition of the possibility of associating, by one act, a given representation with others. Even though the rule does not fall within the senses, we must nevertheless represent the object to ourselves as in accordance with a rule, in order to conceive it as that which represents something, i.e. which has among the other determinations a certain place and function. The beginning cannot be determined according to a subjective rule, and hence is not a free action, which has its beginning at every point.\n(g Everything that happens, but not as a first beginning (freedom), has its determining ground.)",[]],"reflm:5204":["Contingency can be represented empirically or intellectually. In the latter case it is a concept of reason, i.e. a priori. That everything which is in itself undetermined a priori and yet is determined must exist through something else, is identical.",[]],"reflm:5205":["The proposition: everything contingent has its ground, is quite correct, only that contingency can be known solely through what happens; consequently the proposition does not hold beyond the limits of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5206":["A ground (*ratio*) is that, which being posited, something else is posited.",[]],"reflm:5207":["The question whether the *principium rationis* is subjective or objective, and in what sense it is objective, rests on the examination of the question: by what do I know empirically that something is a ground and something a consequence? Without doubt only by this, that A can never be without B, but B can well be without A. That everything is, as it were, in a series, and indeed where each is connected with an antecedent according to a universal rule of order, that is the ground — —",[]],"reflm:5208":["The things that are given to us a posteriori must have a relation to the understanding, i.e. a kind of appearance by which it is possible to obtain a concept of them, just as they must have a relation to sensibility, i.e. a kind of impression by which it is possible to obtain appearance. Hence everything that can become known to us a posteriori (through the senses) will stand under the universal conditions of a concept, i.e. will conform to the rules by which it is possible to obtain concepts of things and to connect everything with the concepts of things and subordinate it to them. Accordingly everything will appear in such a way that there must be a possibility of knowing it a priori. Free actions are already given a priori, namely our own.",[]],"reflm:5209":["Since we do not see the possibility of a real ground, how then are we to say a priori that there must absolutely be such grounds, except where something, according to the rule of identity, already refers to a presupposition? Does not this principle hold as anticipation, since without a rule we should also have no experiences, but this rule consists in the order of time and space according to universal laws?",[]],"reflm:5210":["The ground is that through which, if I posit it, something else is posited according to a rule, [not] not according to an empirical rule, which has no true universality, but according to a true rule.",[]],"reflm:5211":["Over and \nThat everything which appears must appear in relation to the whole can be seen from space and time. That it stands under a rule, however, can be seen from this, that otherwise it would not appear in this whole according to the unity of the relation to it.\nIn the world something is always, in respect of time and circumstances, a sequence in appearance. The question is whether it is also a real sequence, i.e. connected according to a universal rule.",[]],"reflm:5212":["The proposition: everything can be known through reason. Every assertion must be capable of being made a priori. For a posteriori only appearances find a place, whereas things are independent of their appearances. |\nAll knowledge a priori, however, proceeds from the universal to the particular, from what is necessary in itself to the contingent.",[]],"reflm:5213":["Beside and \nSomething can indeed appear, but can never appear completely, unless it stands under a rule a priori, that is, in a relation (*coniugation*) with the other which is determinable a priori. For since one appearance is connected with another because the unity of the action connects them, and they are therefore all connected with one another, [with] the whole state connected with a subjective ground, unity in the manifold must flow from this ground. Without such unity no real appearance can take place.\nFree actions as appearances are determined by grounds, but as (g *physice*, but not *practice**) automatic actions they are original, and the subject determines itself according to grounds. The subject is determined, but not passive, and the necessity of its action is, necessarily, unknown to it.\n*(g What is practically determinable must be physically undetermined.)",[]],"reflm:5214":["All determination in time can take place only according to a principle a priori, that is, in such a way that, according to a rule, each thing follows upon something that precedes.",[]],"reflm:5215":["That which is the condition under which we would posit something according to a rule is the cause. If I let a stork fly in winter, it does not become warm. Hence the stork is not the cause.",[]],"reflm:5216":["The empirical laws a priori contain the conditions of apprehension and conception (together, of intellection). In appearances we can connect nothing, and so give them no real form, except by connecting them with one another, through one another and together with one another, and by the appearance's determining the mind to this activity. Hence something is possible as an object of experience only in so far as it appears in conformity with the laws of apprehension; that is, if its appearance were complete, it would have to cohere according to the laws of apprehension. Just as nothing can appear except within the general sum-total [*Inbegriff*] of space and time, so nothing can become experience except in so far as it is connected with something else according to the general law of the activities of the mind. Nothing therefore happens contingently, that is, without being subject to a general rule in respect of that with which it is connected (whether this be with appearances or not). For we can find the ground of a particular connection only in an object, in so far as the object contains that which can be subsumed, for connection, under a general rule. Ground and consequence, namely, are not mere apprehensions, but inferences or general acts of transition.\nEverything that is given to us through experience must be possible to know a priori; that is, its possibility must be capable of being known from the laws of sensibility or of understanding, [which vo] in relation to which alone experiences take place. That it can be known a priori means that it has an object and is not merely a subjective modification.",[]],"reflm:5217":["Every object of the senses stands in a series in which it is determined a priori. The principle of empirical knowledge in respect of what happens, 'everything that is has a ground', is a *principium* of knowledge a priori, or of reason. Everything, |\nwhich has a ground, has a first ground: *principium* of comprehension.",[]],"reflm:5218":["A complete ground is determining.",[]],"reflm:5219":["Everything in itself contingent happens through a ground; for were it in itself necessary, it would happen of itself according to a rule and would be the *principium* of that rule; since it is not this, it is a *principiatum* of a rule.",[]],"reflm:5220":["The principle of sufficient reason is the *principium* of order in the course of nature; that of freedom is the *principium* of the beginning in the series of things. Since there must be a beginning in the series of consequences, there must be freedom, and even within the course [of nature] first beginnings are possible. If there is nothing but nature, the series of the [subordi] connected members is continuous. But if there is freedom, it is interrupted.",[]],"reflm:5221":["Everything that happens, happens according to a rule, [lies] is determined in general, can be known a priori. Thereby we distinguish the objective from the subjective play (fiction), truth from illusion. The appearance has an object if it is a predicate of a substance, that is, is one of the ways [of] knowing that which persists; thus appearances belong to the representation of something persistent only in so far as they are connected with one another and have unity through something universally valid. Something can indeed appear to us without the ground of it appearing to us; but we cannot know |\nit without the knowledge presupposing a ground, since otherwise it would not be knowledge, that is, objective representation.\nThis is therefore a condition of the knowledge of objects, and hence of the objects themselves, for mere appearance does not yet give an object. [Ob] It is indeed not a condition of apprehension, for that goes immediately to appearance without knowing its ground. But the appearance belongs to a whole of time, and in this the appearances can be connected only if they flow from what is general. Things are not connected through time, but in time through what is general in their determinations.\nWe [can] must, if we were to apprehend the appearance completely, be able to form a concept of its generation. This is possible, however, only if what is contingent, what happens, is necessary when taken in the whole or in its whole determination (g relation). If I did not represent the event as necessary in view of the whole, or as one side of what is abiding, I would not hold my representation to be knowledge, and so not to be something that pertains to an object.",[]],"reflm:5222":["The *qvo posito ponitur aliud* (that, the positing of which posits something else) signifies the position by the understanding, and thus one which I myself make, and so not *qvod datur ut phaenomenon* (that which is given as appearance).\nSecondly: that which, if I posit it at all, posits something else according to a general rule. It is therefore not the *nexus phaenomenorum* (connection of appearances). For these give no general rule. *Posito rationato |\nsecundum regulam communem non ponitur ratio* (once the grounded is posited, according to the common rule the ground is not posited), because the affirmative proposition cannot be converted.\n*Nexus* of insition, of subordination, of coordination.",[]],"reflm:5223":["Every concept is regarded as if it stood in a determinate relation to all possible predicates, and the object accordingly lies in a determinate position [*positus*] with respect to all possibility and is thoroughly determined.\n*Determinabile* (the determinable) is the transcendental matter.",[]],"reflm:5224":["All concepts a priori, arbitrary, transcendental and moral concepts admit of a logical essence, [but the] which is at the same time a real essence, because, unless the concept goes before, we should have none at all in concreto; but concepts a posteriori, which are of empirical origin, admit of no knowledge of the real essence.",[]],"reflm:5225":["Inner determinations are *affectiones*, outer ones *relationes*. The *relationes* of the first [ground] cause are not *affectiones*. The *relationes* of all created beings are *affectiones*; their relation cannot change (variation) [without] without an inner alteration (*mutation*) of them; this is the property of a thing which belongs, as a part, to a whole in general, and where this partiality is a condition of its existence. Can one not say of this being that all its *affectiones* are at the same time *relationes*, and that an inner alteration cannot occur without an outer one?",[]],"reflm:5226":["13. E II 993.\nA law concerns the intention, which is to be good. The rule concerns the execution and procedure with any arbitrary intention. Hence practical geometry has only rules, not laws, and likewise prudence. Physics, however, has laws, because it contains not what ought to be done but what happens, and therefore the agreement with that which accords with a ground not arbitrary but actually given. Laws have universal validity [*Allgemeingültigkeit*], rules can have merely common validity [*Gemeingültigkeit*]. The rules of arithmetic are of the first kind; the rules of thrift, of ha|bits,\nM 13: |\nof foods, of politeness are only *in subsidium* (as aids) of the practical understanding *in concreto*, and are necessary because we do not place much trust in its correct use or in our own inclination, to guide the former on the whole aright, to bring the latter under constraint. They are the leading-strings of weak heads, of the childish.",[]],"reflm:5227":["Plumb-line, guiding thread. Through the norm everything is determined down to the individual actions, e.g. spherical trigonometry. One need only walk by the thread. E.g. mechanical construction.",[]],"reflm:5228":["Possibility, actuality and necessity are indeed logical, but not [real] metaphysical predicates, that is, determinations. Through them we do not know (g something in the) things, but rather the relation of their concepts to the faculty of the mind to posit and to cancel. 1. The |\nrelation to the faculty (possibility), secondly to activity, thirdly to an activity the opposite of which is not within our power.\nThetic proposition: modal or pure.",[]],"reflm:5229":["If everything possible were *qvodammodo indeterminatum* (in a certain way undetermined), some determinations would not be possible.\nHence, since every determination (g also) must belong to mere possibility, but existence does not belong to it, existence is not a determination.",[]],"reflm:5230":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined, for it is posited objectively, that is, absolutely; but it is not on that account determined through its concept. Conversely: what is thoroughly determined through its concept is not on that account existent, precisely because it is posited only respectively, in relation to the concept.",[]],"reflm:5231":["Synthetic judgment. 1. Relative to the concept; 2. also beyond the concept, in itself; 3. thoroughly determined through the concept.\nAll existential propositions are synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5232":["On things [*Dinge*], things [*Sachen*] and beings [*Wesen*]. The thing [*Ding*] is [indeterminate with regard to the] without personality, but only in so far as it is thought; a thing [*Sache*], in so far as it is given.",[]],"reflm:5233":["The thing seems to be the object, as distinguished from its determinations.\nThat whose determinations contradict one another or are empty concepts is a non-thing (*Unding*).",[]],"reflm:5234":["Skill has rules, prudence maxims, morality laws. The lack of skill (g docile unskilfulness) is made good by a norm or guide-line (pattern); the [mere animal capacity] (g recalcitrant) needs discipline. [(Examples]",[]],"reflm:5235":["Prudence can be subjected [according to] to rules, which, however, can never be so determinate for all cases that a practical understanding would not still be especially required in order to recognize whether a case falls under this rule or not. For rules state only what, taken on average, it is good to do. Hence no rule is without exception. But laws are at all times without exception, though not without restriction, if they contain merely *motiva obligandi* (motives of obligation) and not *obligantia* (what obliges).",[]],"reflm:5236":["Morality has laws; practical instruction has rules or norms, a guide-line, merely so as not to err in practice. One sets oneself rules when one does not trust oneself to conduct oneself by prudence in individual cases.",[]],"reflm:5237":["The rule concerns only the manner in which what one wills |\nis to happen; the law contains the imperative of what is to happen (of the end); the maxim is the subjective law, that is, what one has set oneself to do universally.\nBecause the rule is the application of a law *in concreto*, for which sound understanding is required, whose place cannot be supplied by a general prescription, rules hold only approximately, since they are abstracted empirically from the majority of cases and for that reason have no true universality; and so there is no rule without exception.\nLaws are to be without exceptions, likewise axioms; but rules are never without exception, for they are the [breaks off]",[]],"reflm:5238":["Laws [are have merely] are objective prescriptions, rules prescriptions from a subjective ground. Rules are at all times practical, but serve for the proper application of a law *in concreto*. Of this no further laws can be given; rather, sound understanding is required, and, in default of it, rules, which never guide with certainty, but only fittingly in most cases; whoever therefore holds to his rule should know how to make the exceptions according to sound understanding. They are the go-cart (*Gängelwagen*) of the dull. When rules of politeness are given to someone, rules of life. What cannot be known a priori.",[]],"reflm:5239":["Rules that one makes for oneself in eating, in play, in social intercourse are often raised to laws when one does not trust oneself to do much good on account of the exceptions, chiefly because of affect.",[]],"reflm:5240":["The law (g the command, *praeceptum*, if it is the will of another), the ordinance (g *statutum*), the guide-line (norm), the clue (g formula, model, pattern, archetype. Idea).\nReligious laws contain the canon and the ordinances. The former are *principia normalia*, the latter observances (*formalia*). The canon is a *principium constitutivum*; the ordinances are *regulativa* and arbitrary, and so cannot burden the conscience.",[]],"reflm:5241":["Rules are prescriptions for optional use; thus all arithmetical, geometrical, etc. rules are problematic. Laws are rules of necessary use. Hence they enunciate a necessity. All transcendental rules are laws.",[]],"reflm:5242":["Nature has laws, skill has rules.",[]],"reflm:5243":["In relation to the data, e.g. that even for a thing possible in itself the conditions of existence are given, and consequently [the pos-] it is synthetically possible. E.g. a spirit.",[]],"reflm:5244":["Order is a condition of distinctness, and this a condition of the knowledge of truth, but order is not truth.",[]],"reflm:5245":["Perfection is a mathematical concept; it expresses allness, *completudinum*, and is only one (the maximum is the proper measure for objects of the understanding; for there it must not be arbitrary but given: perfectly evil, hell). The [form] quality in what is perfect is the *consensus* to one. But that is only the form of perfection. This pleases especially, whatever the material or end may be; just as talents without merit [please], because this *consensus* can nevertheless be directed to the good or imitated. The children of the world are wiser, etc., etc. Finally, a perfection presupposes the end, and hence the first grounds of satisfaction and dissatisfaction, and can be borrowed only from feeling.",[]],"reflm:5246":["The good is what reason knows as object of the will. Agreement with an end of reason.",[]],"reflm:5247":["Beside and \nThe idea is unique (*individuum*), self-subsistent and eternal. That is the divine in our soul, that it is capable of ideas. The senses give only after-images or indeed *apparentzen* (semblances).",[]],"reflm:5248":["Idea is the representation of the whole, in so far as it necessarily precedes the determination of the parts. It can never be represented empirically, because in experience one proceeds from the parts to the whole by suc|cessive\nsynthesis. It is the archetype of things, because certain objects are possible only through an idea. Transcendental ideas are those in which the (g absolute) whole [the] in general determines the parts in an aggregate or series.",[]],"reflm:5249":["The inner necessity of a thing cannot be thought at all; consequently all such necessity must be respective, either in every respect or only in some, that is, absolutely necessary. The inner [breaks off]\nNot every thing is absolutely possible.",[]],"reflm:5250":["Above and \nNothing can be thought as inwardly necessary, that is, without any relation, except merely a judgment in which a relation lies, but which itself stands in no relation to other judgments. But a thing can be absolutely necessary, that is, in every relation, if it contains the condition of all possibility: 1. as a *datum originarium*, the primitive and universally valid existence (matter of possibility); 2. as a |\nsingle thing, because the possibility of many rests on the difference of the negations. These, however, are limits. But limits ultimately presuppose the unlimited, and particularity presupposes the *omnitudo* (allness).",[]],"reflm:5251":["Inner contingency is not yet respective contingency, still less absolute contingency. Only freedom is an absolutely contingent action *respectu stimulorum* (with respect to stimuli). [It is free]\n*Omnimode* contingent (throughout): of this we cannot comprehend the existence.",[]],"reflm:5252":["The relation of every possible thing to the All, that is, thoroughgoing determination, whereby it as it were borders on everything possible and receives its determination merely through this bordering, proves that we represent it as contained in an All. Explanation of this appearance.",[]],"reflm:5253":["At the lower margin:\nAbsolute necessity we can indeed think, so that we understand it; but to have insight into it, to think it a priori, is not so easy.\nThe concept of the understanding of the absolute necessity of a proposition is problematic; the concept of reason is [dogmatic or] definitive. The definitive concept is either subjective and a concept of method, or objective and dogmatic.",[]],"reflm:5254":["On the inner margin, crosswise\nWhat is internally possible is not yet possible in every relation. |\nBut what is possible in every relation can be so only as a ground, and hence it is a universally valid and necessary being.",[]],"reflm:5255":["*Praedicatum est vel constitutivum vel modale, prius determinatio.* (The predicate is either constitutive or modal; the former is a determination.)\nExistence is no constitutive predicate (*determinatio*); hence it also cannot be found by analysis (*per analysin*) in the concept of a thing as belonging to its content. Therefore it cannot be proved objectively from concepts, but can well be regarded, in relation to everything else, as derived concepts, as a necessary substratum.",[]],"reflm:5256":["A thing cannot be inwardly necessary where it is not necessary in every respect; for the reason that, since its necessity is restricted by no condition, it is considered, regarded as inner.",[]],"reflm:5257":["The first hypothesis of everything is necessary in every respect, but the inner necessity cannot be comprehended.",[]],"reflm:5258":["The absolutely necessary can be represented only as the necessary condition of the knowledge of things in general, and indeed of all things. It is then thought as the condition of the possibility of things; and, because all possible relations are derived from |\nthe relation of each given thing to the All of possibility, it must consist in the all-sufficiency of the condition.",[]],"reflm:5259":["The absolutely necessary is the substratum of possibility in general, the *ens realissimum*, namely in so far as all possibility consists in the limitation of it.",[]],"reflm:5260":["The first hypothesis of possibility is necessary; that of all possibility is absolutely necessary. Necessary as hypothesis in every respect there is only a single one, because everything is only once.",[]],"reflm:5261":["Absolute necessity obtains when the opposite conflicts with the [condition] synthetic condition of possibility; *nihil vel logice tale vel transscendentaliter* (nothing is such either logically or transcendentally).\n(s Absolute necessity, since it cannot be known according to analytic principles of the understanding, will be [known] from synthetic ones.)",[]],"reflm:5262":["At the upper margin and at the outer margin\nSomething is indeed necessary in every respect (*absolute et sine conditione restrictiva*, absolutely and without restrictive condition) if it is necessary in itself (*interne*); but we have no concept of the inner necessity of a thing, |\nbecause positing and removing are equally possible in themselves; hence absolute necessity, as a necessity related to everything whatever, must be grounded on a presupposition.\nEither something is necessary under [every condi] a condition (restrictively), or in some application (*application*), extensively. Under every condition nothing is necessary; for if it already has a condition, it is not necessary under the opposite.\nAbsolute necessity is the necessity of a presupposition (*hypothesis originaria*) in regard to everything thinkable. Namely, the data for all determination, the sum-total of reality (*Allinnbegriff der Realität*). This is related to the whole of sensibility [both] and of the senses, both as to form and as to matter. If anyone were to have complete insight into himself in respect of all his sensitivity and form, then [would] we would be able to determine in advance everything that can be an object for us.\nThe *hypothesis originaria* is not the necessity under a hypothesis, but the necessity as hypothesis.",[]],"reflm:5263":["[What contradicts itself is not in accord with the conditions of understanding.]\nHow the conflict of subjective conditions, or their presupposition, imitates and substitutes for the truth of the objective ones. E.g. A mathematically infinite is possible, because it does not conflict with the rules of insight; it is impossible, because it conflicts with the conditions of comprehension. Everything has its ground, because without this the being of the thing cannot be seen into (*eingesehen*); something has no further ground above itself, because otherwise it would conflict with the conditions of conceiving. There is no freedom (transcendental). There is |\none. There is an absolutely necessary being, because without this there is no comprehension. There is no etc. etc. being, because the same conflicts with insight. But if these concepts are taken only according to the conditions of apprehension, they are restricted to the objects of the senses and have also only a restricted meaning. E.g. Everything that happens has, in a certain order, a first ground; or rather, the *axiomata* are not transcendent but hold only as anticipations, so that they hold only of the data of the senses, so far as these can be understood. E.g. What happens has a ground, because without a rule according to which it is given in accordance with laws of sensibility, one would by means of the representation think no object.\nThe *principium contradictionis* contains the conditions of thinking in general. The anticipations, which affirm the conditions of apprehension of the concepts of the understanding (e.g. In every substance there is *aliqvid perdurabile* [something enduring], or a substance always endures), contain the conditions (g *postulata*) of understanding and are therefore always true in respect of the sensible conditions.\n(To understand something under its determinations (representations) means: to think or posit an object that corresponds to them.)\nApprehension without the conditions under which something can alone be given is a function, but not yet a concept, or rather a potential. To understand (*verstehen*) something is: to know it when it has previously been given; to have insight into it (*einsehen*) is: to know it a priori; to understand logically is: to know something distinctly.",[]],"reflm:5264":["If I remove the light, I retain space with the negative predicate; if I remove the knowledge, I retain the subject; if I remove a part from the whole or a consequence from a cause, I retain the universe or the cause. But if I remove everything and retain nothing, this is an empty thought, because no object is left of which something could be predicated. Accordingly the |\n[emptiness] transcendental emptiness is nothing at all and also cannot be thought.",[]],"reflm:5265":["Another totality of things is absolutely impossible; another world is indeed possible in itself, but hypothetically impossible.",[]],"reflm:5266":["At the very top of the upper margin and across the inner margin:\nOnly the contradictory opposite of the determinations (namely *omni respectu*, in every respect) proves contingency; but the *opposita determinatio* at another time is not the *contradictorie oppositum* and therefore does not prove contingency. The change of determinations proves still less the change of things, and therefore also not the contingency of the subject. There is no transition from the principles of appearance to the concepts of reason, and therefore also none from alteration to contingency.",[]],"reflm:5267":["The successive opposite is the reverse (*Wiederspiel*).",[]],"reflm:5268":["The alterable is so far not determined through concepts. Hence it is logically contingent, not in respect of existence.",[]],"reflm:5269":["Above, beside and \nIf I think a thing as *partim reale, partim negativum* (partly real, partly negative), I think it first of all [as real] throughout as real and separate off some reality from it; for *negare est realitatem tollere* (to negate is to remove reality). Consequently, if I think it in its thoroughgoing determination, I must lay at the basis the concept of the *realissimum*.\nThat the *ens realissimum* is logically possible is not in doubt; but is it really possible, 1. so that all reality exists in it as subject, 2. or through it as ground, 3. or from it as part of a whole? Of this we can only know that the last cannot be the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5270":["42'. E II 1623.\nThe principle of thoroughgoing determination: *qvodlibet existens est omnimode determinatum, i.e. ens qvodlibet per se non nisi ut omnimode determinatum dari potest, sed per conceptum de ipso multimode potest esse indeterminatum* (whatever exists is determined in every respect; that is, any being in itself can be given only as determined in every respect, but through a concept of it can be indeterminate in many respects).\nThat proposition has in thought the sum-total of all possible predicates with their opposites and, since only reality belongs to existence, the sum-total of all realities with their opposites; and since every thing that does not contain all reality always presupposes another that contains it, it also has before its eyes the concept of a thing that contains all reality, as *ens logice originarium* (a logically original being), whose reality, or the consequences of it, give all things through limitation, and in relation to which [a t]hing alone can be determined, in distinction from others.\nIf I represent the understanding that thinks reality as light and, so far as it removes reality, as darkness, then one can think thoroughgoing determination either as a carrying of light here and there into the darkness, or the darkness as a mere limitation of the universal light; and so things are distinguished only by the shadows, reality lying at the basis, and indeed only a single universal reality. In the opposite case all things are distinguished only by their light, as if they had originally been raised out of darkness. But I can well think a negation if I have reality, though not if no reality is given. Reality is therefore *logice* the former, and from this it is inferred that it is also *metaphysice* and objectively the first. |\nM 42': |\nBecause objects of the senses are not given through the understanding (g and in general not a priori), negation is here the first, and the darkness out of which the light of experience works out shapes. Thus, in accordance with appearances, [is] are the manifold original, and unity arises when one abstracts from the manifold.",[]],"reflm:5271":["In and \nThe *principium omnimodae determinationis* (principle of determination in every respect) is: Every thing stands, in respect of its whole possibility, under the concept of the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5272":["One cannot say: every thing is either thoroughly determined or not; but rather: it is so at all times, only not always [through the] through the concept of it, except the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5273":["The metaphysical doctrine of the *genus summum*, *species* etc. etc. and of thoroughgoing determination is properly the doctrine of the *ens realissimum*, as the sum-total of all determination of things, the substratum from the all to some and to one.",[]],"reflm:5274":["The *principium determinationis* says that every thing is at all times determined in respect of one of the *praedicata contradictorie opposita* (contradictorily opposed predicates).\nThe *principium omnimodae determinationis* says that it is determined in respect of all of them together.\nThe fundamental principle is: All negations in things are limitations. Consequently the *ens a priori omnimode determinatum* is the *ens realissimum*, and lies at the basis of our concepts of the real possibility of things just as the principle of contradiction lies at the basis of logical possibility. The former is the material principle, and all negations are only the formal element of things, considered transcendentally.",[]],"reflm:5275":["Magnitude is a relation of comparison [aus], the three remaining ones of combination; the logical relation is also [of the] either of comparison: identity and diversity, or of combination: universal and particular.",[]],"reflm:5276":["*Quantum* and *mensura* (measure) (multitude, unity).\n*Compositum* and *simplex* (the composite and the simple).\n*Totum* and *pars* (whole and part).\n*Substantia* and *accidens* (substance and accident).",[]],"reflm:5277":["Mathematics is the science of the construction of concepts; hence it goes only to intuition as such and has only empirical use.\nPhilosophy goes only to concepts of being in general, hence of that which corresponds to sensation, and so cannot make its concepts intuitive. For just that reason it also goes to objects independently of the conditions of intuition.",[]],"reflm:5278":["On and \nEverything real exists either merely as the determination of another (the mode of being of another) or else merely as subject. That existence is inherence — In our state — this is subsistence.",[]],"reflm:5279":["On and \n*Nexus* of sustentation.\n*Nexus* of subordination: of series.\n*Nexus* of coordination: aggregates.",[]],"reflm:5280":["Dependence and adherence, [necessity] necessity and self-subsistence.\nThe *existentia rationati* (existence of what is grounded) is *dependentia* (dependence); that of the predicate is *inhaerentia* (inherence).\nThat which exists only as a part of another is limited (*ens mundanum*, a worldly being); that which exists even though it is not a part of another is the *ens realissimum*, the world (*omnitudo*, *vniversitas*: allness, totality).",[]],"reflm:5281":["On and \nThe first of things is the absolutely necessary.\nThe first of states is the absolutely contingent.\nNecessity and freedom are incomprehensible, because the former breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5282":["On and \n1. *Accidens inhaeret subiecto* (the accident inheres in the subject). 2. The subject is *causa* (cause). 3. *Accidentia faciunt totum* (accidents make up the whole).",[]],"reflm:5283":["Predicates are either determinations or *accidentia*. The |\nmagnitude is no *accidens*. Of intellectual relations, e.g. *substantia*, ground, whole.\nWhat is in space or time is its *accidens*. — *Antecedens*, *consequens*, *spatium*.",[]],"reflm:5284":["Categories of the real subject, ground and whole, in distinction from the logical ones. They are real relations.",[]],"reflm:5285":["Because our understanding cannot think otherwise than through judgments, we can also have no concepts of things except through predicates, [which] which are combined with something abiding as marks of the subject. Hence the concept of substance [otherwise] and accident has otherwise no meaning.",[]],"reflm:5286":["The existence of something in a *substratum*.\nThe existence of something through a *principium* in a series.\nThe existence of something in a *compositum*. There is no inherence there, but still a reciprocal combination.",[]],"reflm:5287":["Time combined with substance gives persistence or alteration in acting or suffering. When the substance is altered, this signifies its inner state.\nThat inheres which belongs to another as a predicate and has the ground of its existence in it.\nSubstances are things that do not inhere; accidents [are], however, in turn determinations in which nothing inheres. They all inhere in the same subject. Unity.",[]],"reflm:5288":["*Entelechia*, the first proper *substratum*, or the ground of all appearances, to be known through the pure understanding. The rest is *substantia phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5289":["At the bottom of the page,\n1. *Substratum* (g inherence). 2. *Principium causalitatis* (g consequence). a. *ratio realis*, or synthetic. The causality of substance with respect to accidents is *vis* (force): primitive, derivative. *Actio* (action), *passio* (passion), *influxus*. 3. *Commercium* (community). *Reciproca actio* (reciprocal action). Threefold unity, whose functions are a priori, but can be given (constructed) only a posteriori.\n*Substantia*, *ratio*, *compositum*. *Phaenomena* (appearances). Time is abiding, while [determinations] appearances change.",[]],"reflm:5290":["On and \nThe relation of a substance to the accident is merely *actio*. *Vis*. That of substances to one another can be *actio* and *passio*; if it is *mutua* (reciprocal), then it is *commercium*.\nSince we know a thing only through its predicates, we cannot know the subject by itself alone.",[]],"reflm:5291":["On and \nSubstance is only alterable and does not change. It is altered in that the state changes; that is, it persists.",[]],"reflm:5292":["2. Substance as cause, 3. as part of a whole in community.\nThe substantial is the thing in itself and unknown.",[]],"reflm:5293":["Real relations are opposed to ideal ones; the latter are only relations of the representations, not of the objects. The ideal are aesthetic or logical.",[]],"reflm:5294":["Bodies are *substantiae comparativae* (comparative substances), *substrata phaenomenorum* (substrata of appearances). It is ridiculous to want to think the soul as corporeal; for we have the concept of substance only from the soul, and we form that of body after it. The transcendental concepts must not overstep the limits of the *intellectualia* and make the sensitive into the intellectual in the same sense; e.g. substance as *noumenon* or *phaenomenon*; consequently not the proposition: bodies are divisible.\nNecessity cannot occur in experience, nor can substance; hence the intellectual concept does not hold of the sensible in its entire purity.",[]],"reflm:5295":["At the bottom of the page:\nThat which is absolutely (in every respect) (g in itself, not logically) subject, the last subject, which does not presuppose, as a predicate, a further subject, is substance. That predicate, [what] to which no |\nfurther other universal concept can be given as subject, is that through which substance is immediately represented as a something to which this predicate attaches.\nThe mind intuits substance. [The sustentation] Of outer things [we intuit] only the actions, from which substance is inferred, because they are predicates.",[]],"reflm:5296":["At the top of the page:\nSustentation seems to designate a relation of one thing to another, and inherence is nothing further than the representation of a real predicate, that is, of an action of substance, among which the fundamental action designates the substance.",[]],"reflm:5297":["Between substance and accident the logical relation is synthetic. The subject is itself a predicate (g for one can think everything only through predicates, except I), but it is entitled a subject only because it is no further predicate: 1, because no subject is thought for it; 2, because it is the presupposition and substratum of the others. This latter can be inferred only from persistence, while the other changes. Hence it belongs to the essence of a substance that [it] it be *perdurabel* (enduring). If one assumes that substance ceased, this ceasing proves that it is no substance; and since for this appearance no substratum is then thought, there are predicates without subjects, hence no judgments and no thoughts.",[]],"reflm:5298":["All concepts are predicates, and these are substance or accident |\nor relation. Space and time are neither of the two, hence not at all predicates of objects in themselves. Space and time are intuitions a priori. For from their concepts the propositions [not synthet] (synthetic) cannot be derived which we have of them a priori. How are intuitions a priori possible? Not otherwise than in that the form of intuiting something through the senses can be represented for itself without matter, that is, without a given object of the senses. Hence space and time are forms of sensible intuition. Hence we can know a great deal a priori of space and time and of the objects in them, that is, as objects of the senses, which for that very reason does not hold of these same objects as things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:5299":["A whole is either of derivation or of composition. The latter is that in which one thing in a certain state is not without another being in a similar state also. Thus a many makes up a real unity; e.g. when one part of a body cannot be moved without the other being moved with it. Everything here, in the case of substances, depends on the reciprocal influences of the alterations.\nComposition is the contingent unity of the many. Hence not every whole is composite; e.g. space, because the unity here precedes the plurality, or the plurality, in order to be thought in it, presupposes the unity. This unity consists in combination either with a third or with one another. This composition is either ideal or real; the first through taking together, the second through combination.\nA whole is therefore either of continuation or of composition; the former is *quantum continuum*, the second *discretum*. The former has contingent plurality in one, the second contingent unity in many.\nSomething through which many are posited is for that reason no whole, but that which consists of many (each of which subsists without the thing itself, and the thing itself only through each of them). God is therefore no whole.",[]],"reflm:5300":["On and \nThe simple of separation, that which is not composed of several of different kind, e.g. water.",[]],"reflm:5301":["*Coniunctio* (conjunction) is either of mere composition and is ideal, or of inherence and is real.",[]],"reflm:5302":["Something is simple either according to quantity or also according to quality: *compositum similare* (homogeneous composite); the first is that which does not consist of any parts; the second, that which does not consist of heterogeneous ones.",[]],"reflm:5303":["On the beginning of § 224 »Ens compositum est«:\n*Complexus* (g of several) of things mutually determining one another really. The one of several aggregated things is a *complexus*. [*Complexus* of aggregates] If the unity [aggrega] of the *complexus* consists in the identity of the *complexus*, it is [whole] a *quantum*. A *quantum* is an ideal *compositum*.",[]],"reflm:5304":["A *complexus* of several, insofar as it is thinkable only through composition alone, is called *compositum*, and this precedes the simple, if the latter is said to be possible in itself only through composition.",[]],"reflm:5305":["The ideal *compositum* is that whose parts, without belonging to the whole, are impossible by themselves.",[]],"reflm:5306":["The division of the inseparable is the classification [*Eintheilung*], and is a *compositum ideale* (ideal composite).",[]],"reflm:5307":["That composition which is to contain the ground of the possibility of the relation is ideal.",[]],"reflm:5308":["A whole is either quantitative or qualitative.* The latter is a *compositum* or *quantum discretum*. *Partes qualitativae* are entitled members [*Glieder*]. They differ from the whole in quality, e.g. the rainbow and an arc as such.\n*(g The relation of a whole made up of substances is *commercium*.)",[]],"reflm:5309":["Magnitude*, whole, composite: how do they differ?\n*(g What contains one and the same thing many times over.)\nThe *correlatum* of magnitude is unity.\nThe *oppositum* of magnitude is the simple, absolute unity.\nThe *correlatum* of ground is the consequence; the *oppositum* is *sterile*.",[]],"reflm:5310":["Every whole is divisible, or has parts that can be distinguished, but is not on that account composite; for the parts are at times possible only through division.",[]],"reflm:5311":["Any body can be regarded as composite only after the classification [*Eintheilung*]. If one wishes to remove all composition, all classification must first be thought as completed.",[]],"reflm:5312":["In und \nA *phaenomenon* that is a *substratum* of other *phaenomena* is not on that account substance, save only comparatively. In appearances we cannot know anything as substance (this is only a concept of apperception); something merely appears as the *substratum* of the appearance, to which everything in the appearance is ascribed.",[]],"reflm:5313":["That space and time are intuitions without things means that they must be not objective but subjective representations.",[]],"reflm:5314":["The possibility of a *compositum noumenon* cannot be assumed outright; for to that end we should have to think up a community.",[]],"reflm:5315":["Space is the form of all relations, and precedes all intuition of external things.",[]],"reflm:5316":["That of which the composition must be thought prior to the parts is ideal.",[]],"reflm:5317":["Time is the form of consciousness, i.e. the condition under which alone we become conscious of things.",[]],"reflm:5318":["Space is a mere possibility, but in actual things it is something actual.",[]],"reflm:5319":["Because inner sense alone is infallible and change is perceived through it, time seems to be something absolute. But it is none the less only the form of inner appearance, and we really have the representation of time, although what lies behind this appearance remains unknown.",[]],"reflm:5320":["Time is real as form of inner sensibility; thereby it is opposed to the *fictum*. For there really are alterations in us. Bodies are things even if they were not [present]. But space is at all times only form.",[]],"reflm:5321":["Of time we have the running (present), the elapsed, bygone (past) and the coming (future) time. Here time flows, the things stand still. But: a thing endures, it has lived through etc. etc.; this presupposes time as at rest. Eternity. Existence, either in general or of a thing. A thing covers much time and has much before it. It cannot make its existence in time fast; it passes through time in flight, or time flees.",[]],"reflm:5322":["Space applies only to objects of the outer senses, not to that which we posit only through properties borrowed from the inner senses.",[]],"reflm:5323":["Space and time are not things, nor their *praedicata*; hence they are nothing objective.",[]],"reflm:5324":["In und \nSpace does not apply to anything regardless of all determinations of sense.\nSpace does not apply to objects of inner sense; that is, it is no intellectual predicate.",[]],"reflm:5325":["Time is the form of inner sense, space the form of outer sense. Consequently, what we know only as an *obiect* of inner sense is not subject to the *condition* of outer appearance.",[]],"reflm:5326":["Whence the propositions of space, time and number can be seen a priori, before all experience.",[]],"reflm:5327":["If the concept of space were, as Leibniz holds, taken from things, the propositions about it, being empirical propositions, would have no apodictic certainty.",[]],"reflm:5328":["Physical division is separation of the substance; mathematical division is the distinguishing of the parts.",[]],"reflm:5329":["If space were something objective and necessary, whence should we know this? From experience we can derive no judgment that would at the same time be known as absolutely necessary; and a priori it is not known and not knowable. The representation would have to be innate [*anerschaffen*], but then it would not be necessary either. It must therefore be intuited in God.",[]],"reflm:5330":["The first subject. The first ground. The first part.\nThe subject that holds everything in itself. The ground that comprehends everything under itself. The whole that comprises everything.\nThe *totalitas absoluta* of reality. Of the series. Of coordination.",[]],"reflm:5331":["The magnitude of unity is degree. [It is] the difference in magnitude of one and the same object, e.g. the light of one and the same paper, the warmth of the same stove. There it seems not to be the magnitude of an aggregate. Objects have no degrees, but only determinations of the same reality. Relations have no degrees; but causality, unity, has degrees.\nExtension also has a degree, because, according to the meaning of the word, it arises from nothing and spreads out. Space has extensive magnitude.",[]],"reflm:5332":["The infinite, the greatest, the all-sufficient thing.",[]],"reflm:5333":["In und \nThe multiplicity that is given simultaneously, so far as it is unlimited, cannot be known determinately at all; for determination consists in limitation. That which is not given simultaneously cannot be known through limitation, but only through progression.",[]],"reflm:5334":["In und \nNo *quantum* can be given to us as *infinitum*, for it is not given in itself but only through the *progressus*, which is never given as *infinitus*. But a *progressus in infinitum* can be given; *indefinitum*: that whose boundary we leave undetermined.",[]],"reflm:5335":["*Infinitudo realis* (real infinity) is *omnitudo* (allness): that which is not limited. That which lacks bounds (*termini*) differs from that which lacks limits.",[]],"reflm:5336":["The concept of the *maximum* is relative; that of *omnitudo* is indeed absolute, but indeterminate. That of the *infinitum* is subjective.",[]],"reflm:5337":["The *limitatum* of sensibility: *non Infinitum*.\nThe *limitatum* of understanding: *non Omnitudo*.",[]],"reflm:5338":["Infinite means more than not finite or bounded; it means also, taken as a whole, great beyond all measure. Hence I can say: the world is not finite, nor yet infinite, for it is a *phaenomenon* and not a thing. As *phaenomenon* it can have no determinate boundary and can never be taken as a whole; one can only proceed onward in it.",[]],"reflm:5339":["At the top of the page:\nThe determination of boundaries [*Grenzen*] takes place in the infinite (space and time),\nthe determination of limits [*Schranken*] in the infinite thing.\nThe former in an infinite appearance, the latter in an infinite reality.",[]],"reflm:5340":["At the top of the page:\nThe infinity of the world in respect of magnitude (time and space) presents no difficulty. The difficulty lies rather in the prorogation of the *terminus* of the chain of grounds, postponed to infinity, [because] the interest of reason demands a complete comprehension.\nBut even if one concedes infinity as possible, the question still remains whether empty time and space cannot bound the world, i.e. whether an empty time and space could be the condition of the possibility of things.",[]],"reflm:5341":["The possibility of thinking to oneself an empty space and an empty time is nothing other than the diminution of reality in both (in the former, of impenetrable extension); and this, since it can be diminished to infinity, passes over into nothing. If I remove a part of it that is penetrated by another, a comparatively empty space remains. Space penetrates everything, i.e. something extended is the basis of the possibility of outer relations according to relations of space. A completely empty space is a mere idea of decomposition down to nothing, but not of complete removal. In space I must be able to distinguish where I am. There must therefore be correlates. The priority of space over the appearances. If an empty space and time (g outside the world) were possible, an empty space and time in place of the world would also be possible. I should then have time only in myself and space only in the representation of my body; consequently time and space would really not be empty. Wholly empty is without any intuiting being, and consequently nothing at all.",[]],"reflm:5342":["Inner difference is no condition of the concept of [the] distinct things, but rather difference of places. Nor is it a condition of appearance; hence the proposition is false, but for |\nreason true; i.e. through reason I can concede distinct things only where the inner determinations are different. I.e. a priori I can [keinen] not know the difference.",[]],"reflm:5343":["Opposite the heading »Simultanea«:\nEverything that is, is somewhere and at some time.",[]],"reflm:5344":["That everything in space is mere appearance, namely sheer relation —, that everything in time (also in inner sense) is appearance, mere representations of outer things and *relationes* [to us] of their representations to one another, to us and to our feeling.",[]],"reflm:5345":["The subject itself we know only through its relation to the change of place. Hence we do not know what is present in the place, nor the action which it exerts in different places. At the least, we do not know all this rationally; but the question is whether our thoughts do not still contain the inner [aspect] of the world.",[]],"reflm:5346":["Matter is the subject to which all outer appearances are referred. (The *phaenomenon* to which they all relate is impenetrability.) That to which everything is referred as subject must remain; for without this there is no unity of the accidents. All matter, as substratum, is therefore *constans*, *stabilis*, *perdurabilis*, *permanens* (constant, stable, enduring, permanent), without which |\nthe *fluxum* and *transitorium* of the states could not be perceived. This proposition is grounded on the possibility of experience as the unity of appearances.",[]],"reflm:5347":["Time is a series in which the order of the synthesis is determined: the first, the middle and the last. Three points as limits.",[]],"reflm:5348":["Why do we not say (g also) of things in different times (g as we do of those in different spaces) that they are for that reason different things? Because, without the identity of things in different times, these different times themselves could not be known as such. The persistence of things lies at the ground of the temporal sequence. But that is always possible only in space. Space persists; but I can perceive it itself only through things in it (which therefore also persist, and in which I know it (g as) persisting).",[]],"reflm:5349":["Opposite and \nAll arising and perishing (g of a state as appearance, i.e. all that happens) happens in time, because between 0 and reality there is an infinite series of transition.",[]],"reflm:5350":["A worth: that for which something else can be given as an *aequivalent* (equivalent). Virtue has no price. Dignity is inner worth, which therefore has no price.",[]],"reflm:5351":["Objective causes are only in the free will.\nHow these can at the same time be subjective, i.e. *causae efficientes* (efficient causes), is not to be explained. Hypothesis of freedom.",[]],"reflm:5352":["An action which has its inner worth has no *causa impulsiva* (impelling cause).",[]],"reflm:5353":["Of that which is possible only through a [mode of] knowledge, the original knowledge is idea. The thing in accordance with the idea is entitled archetype [Urbild]; if it cannot be produced: ideal.",[]],"reflm:5354":["The idea.* Archetype, copy, imitation, semblance [Apparenz].\n*(g A made representation. It is single, because it aims at completeness, which is only one, but not at the concept of something so far as it is given, but [of something that] can only be thought.)",[]],"reflm:5355":["Apart from ontology there is only cosmology and theology.",[]],"reflm:5356":["Metaphysics proper consists of *cosmologia rationalis* and *theologia naturalis* (rational cosmology and natural theology). The former has two parts: nature and freedom, and their laws *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5357":["At the very bottom of the page, \nThere is only one time and one space, hence only one world. This rests on the presupposition that everything (at least the finite) is in time and in space, and is an example of a *petitio sensitiva* (a sensible postulate).",[]],"reflm:5358":["We do not sense outer substances (only outer effects upon us); we merely think them in addition. But [we think them] only in relation to the affections of our mind; hence not as they are in themselves, but [we think] the *perdurable* in the appearance.",[]],"reflm:5359":["At the top of the page. \nIf anyone wished to maintain that the world should have begun earlier, he would have to show under what conditions this could take place |\nand by what mark it would be recognized *in concreto*. If some members [of the series] were human beings before Adam, one would say only that Adam is later, or that those are earlier in relation to him. Thus all [all] priority is merely relation. The world therefore cannot arise earlier; rather, one part [arises] earlier than another. If matter were to be added beyond the limits of the world, it would be added not to space but to the old whole.",[]],"reflm:5360":["Since empty space and empty time are no object of empirical knowledge, neither of experience immediately nor through any inference, and since the infinity of the world likewise lies beyond experience just as those [empty space and time] lie outside it, the (absolute) totality of the [world] appearances, i.e. the idea of the world, is a problematic concept. The intellectual possibility of one of these conditions of appearance does not apply here; for they are synthetic propositions, which hold only as conditions of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5361":["Here we prove only that an intellectual series has a first. (g The cause of the series is not in the series, hence is not a first. Hence the series can be without a first and yet have a cause which is not again in a series.)\nOf the series in appearance we do not speak.",[]],"reflm:5362":["Beside and \nThat no single supposed part of the *universum* lies infinitely far removed from another, but that in the universe a progress to infinity in every kind of magnitude is possible.\nWhere reason is cause, there a first is also found: freedom and supreme being. But where it only knows the connection, there the synthesis is infinite.",[]],"reflm:5363":["Below and \nFreedom (g in the world) and the absolute necessity of a free supreme cause outside the world are dynamical principles of reason.",[]],"reflm:5364":["Below and \nEvery series of the contingent rests on a necessary. But this does not mean that it is a first of the series.",[]],"reflm:5365":["The beginning belongs to the series, but not the cause of it. A series of contingent things can be without beginning, i.e. in that no empty time [gar] precedes, but [before every series] all appearances are determined in one series *a parte priori* and *a parte posteriori*; and yet it can have a cause.",[]],"reflm:5366":["There is no blind dependence (g blind chain) (a series of the dependent without an independent), no blind transition, no blind [generation] arising, no blind necessity.\nBlind is that which is [posited] assumed not in accordance with the laws of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:5367":["In the series of appearances there is no first cause; for that holds only of things in themselves, but there the series of the cause is not subordinated according to time.",[]],"reflm:5368":["On and \nThis means only that we can think no (g absolute) totality according to empirical synthesis, but must nevertheless think one according to pure intellectual [synthesis], because everything contingent must have a complete cause, not indeed in itself, but outside itself. We have no image, but yet a concept. For of the infinite no image is possible, and of the finite in infinite time and space there is no substratum for this image.\nIn empty space and empty time we cannot think the place of the beginning of the world as determinable. How something could begin absolutely in time, still less how time itself could begin.",[]],"reflm:5369":["In and \nBetween nature and chance there is a third, namely freedom.\nAll appearances are in nature, but the cause of the appearances is not contained in the appearance, and hence is not nature either. Our understanding is such a cause of the actions of choice [Willkür], which, as appearances, are indeed nature, but as a whole of appearances stand under freedom.",[]],"reflm:5370":["Chance [Zufall] is opposed to the purposive (g to that which human reason can take into account in the case of ends); the fortuitous [das ungefehr] to that which happens according to constant and known laws.\nWe do not see that nothing purposive can arise without a cause working according to ends, and that natural necessity could not just as well produce something purposive as could absolute necessity, a rational supreme being; rather, because experience teaches that there are laws of blind nature, we [untersch] see from this the distinction of choosing causes from physical ones.",[]],"reflm:5371":["That chance [wech] must make the event alternate with its opposite. That although the same event which happened before is possible each time, and so on all following occasions, yet the same event is not always possible in succession if it is to proceed from chance. Hence dice, if the same side always falls, are not held to be fair. We also find it probable that if something subject to chance has several times turned out successfully in a similar way, it is now more probable that it will fail than before. Whence does it come that, when we have had fine weather for a long time, we find rain more probable than after long rain? Yet one believes that some people are especially lucky, as are some days of play and certain cards. (g Whoever has often lost in the lottery, e.g. the first class, believes he has more hopes.)\nChance has laws, e.g. shipwrecks.\nSomething is called chance because its opposite also happens in just the same (generically identical) circumstances. Hence the opposite in chance must, *per hypothesin* (by hypothesis), be all the more necessary the more often something has happened.",[]],"reflm:5372":["In so far as an event does not happen under a particular rule of its causes, it is chance [Zufall]. But there is no rule in the causality of the causes when there are many of them which are not specially determined to this event. E.g. rain, frost, illnesses are chances in a military campaign. Nature, chance and design.\nIn human generation many variations occur through chance. So too in the happiness of man. A chance (fortune) that is attached to one man is an absurdity. Even under a divine providence the happiness of men is left very largely to chance, i.e. to the influence of many causes which are not attuned to the person and his merit, and which man cannot bring under a rule.",[]],"reflm:5373":["(s In the world there is no absolute necessity; this, however, can be outside it.)\nFate (destiné) and chance (good and ill fortune) are *qualitates occultae* (occult qualities). We need reason for explaining as well as for acting; blind haphazard and fate are opposed to both.",[]],"reflm:5374":["Principles of (g speculative) reason concerning everything that appears.",[]],"reflm:5375":["Everything stands [entweder] under a rule, either of necessity or of freedom.",[]],"reflm:5376":["Between two states there is a time. In this time, if the states are different, the thing is in neither the one nor the other. Hence there is between them either an empty time (*saltus*) or a time filled with smaller intermediate degrees of the states. Likewise there is no transition [aus] among appearances in space through the void, since this would be an appearance without perception.",[]],"reflm:5377":["*Mundus non est in abysso*∗: no beginning, end or boundary of the world.\n*in mundo non datur hiatus* (g *saltus. vacuum.* we fill all space by a kind of sensations.)\n*Nihil accidit per casum. Nihil per fatum.*\n∗(g There is no absolute time or space. Pure intuition here signifies not something that is intuited, but the pure formal condition which precedes the appearance. Absolute time is empty intuition.)",[]],"reflm:5378":["The world is not an *interruptum* but a *continuum*. The removal of matter from space is nothing but its infinite diminution.",[]],"reflm:5379":["All alteration or transition occurs in time through a continuous approximation.",[]],"reflm:5380":["Physical continuity obtains: if substances must ascend from the lowest classes to the generation of the greatest; |\nif a mineral must first be a plant, then an animal, for then it passes through all the intermediate degrees. If animals are properly plants which have passed through a certain degree. Through composition no true continuity arises, but only through generation from the smallest degree to the greatest.",[]],"reflm:5381":["Whether certain oppositions permit a continuity (a grain and a heap). Where a continuous transition is possible, the difference is only in magnitude. Between what is no vice and vice the difference is in quality. Whether understanding and learning differ only in magnitude. The transition from the one to the other is not a *continuum*.",[]],"reflm:5382":["The principle of continuity wants only to say: all *diversa* are *remota*, i.e. they are connected with one another only *per intermedia*, between which the difference is still smaller. That is, no difference [verk] is the smallest, because no transition is elementary and the smallest, and so always has a magnitude. A time belongs to transition, hence an approximation to a new state. The smallest difference would be called a *differentiale*; but since there is no smallest, it is called a fluxion.\nBecause the creatures between two genera are a determinate number, there is no continuity of forms. But there could be one *in potentia*, if matter organized itself from the mineral to man; for these forces lie in a unity, and the differences have a magnitude, between which infinitely smaller ones obtain.",[]],"reflm:5383":["If there were no continuity in alteration, the thing would for a time be unaltered, i.e. at rest.",[]],"reflm:5384":["The immediate connection of the limits of one and the same series.\nLaw of the *continuum*. Quality of magnitudes.",[]],"reflm:5385":["*Lex continui* in nature, the continuity of the *species* or affinity, mediate or immediate, is a mere play of wit. Complete manifoldness gives at the same time unity of progression. For then, if all manifoldness is to obtain, there must be met with, between two different things A and E, others, B and C, whose differences are smaller. For similarities exist among all appearances, since otherwise no *commercium* could obtain.",[]],"reflm:5386":["121. E II 1463. 1745.\n M 121':\nThere is no simple *phaenomenon*; for every *phaenomenon* will have a defined part, which would therefore occupy a part of space and time, which again is space and time. |\nM 121: |\ntherefore, in running through *phaenomena* from simple to simple, there is no transition except through infinitely many intermediates.\nTwo states, as series, do indeed follow one upon another; but not two simple determinations of space and time.",[]],"reflm:5387":["All motion in a triangle is interrupted, i.e. the body rests at its apex, and no two different states follow one another immediately, although two [verschi] alterations (series) can follow one another.",[]],"reflm:5388":["All differences of given representations have a magnitude, e.g. of velocity, of extension, of the series, and can be determined only thereby. But in the object the [groß] difference is not discrete — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5389":["Continuity. That every magnitude which begins from 0 arises in a time, and that every difference has a magnitude, hence none is the smallest. The first is clear from this, that between 0 and a given magnitude, likewise in the arising, there is a series, of which 0 and the magnitude are the extremes, likewise a degree of progression.",[]],"reflm:5390":["All appearances stand as representations in time and are determined in time. As a part of a whole appearance it cannot be determined in a single moment, but only in a part of time (be apprehended genetically). A part of time lies between |\ntwo limits and thus two moments, and is therefore itself [bestimbar in der] a time; hence every part of the appearance is exponible in time. Thus, like time itself, it does not consist of simple parts.",[]],"reflm:5391":["Two concepts opposed in themselves cannot differ (g merely) in magnitude. Vice and virtue.",[]],"reflm:5392":["In and \nEvery alteration in the world is only a continuation of a series already present, and just as much ceases as begins. Hence everything in the world does indeed alter; but the totality of the series does not alter, since things alter in time, but not time itself.",[]],"reflm:5393":["What appears externally can be united with a *principium repraesentativum* (representative principle), but not as external appearance.",[]],"reflm:5394":["Idealism is mystical; egoism is Spinozistic, if its defence is dogmatic. If it is sceptical, i.e. merely problematic, then egoism is a [pro] *docimastic* (a test or assay) of reason.",[]],"reflm:5395":["The idealist asserts: bodies [werden] are only a semblance [Schein]; the realist: they are an appearance [Erscheinung], to which nevertheless a particular kind of substances really corresponds. Semblance is that behind which there is nothing true.",[]],"reflm:5396":["*Compositum substantiale intellectualiter spectatum est quantum discretum, quod constat simplicibus; idem ut phaenomenon est quantum continuum.* (A substantial composite, regarded intellectually, is a discrete quantity, which consists of simples; the same, as phenomenon, is a continuous quantity.)\nThe *mundus sensibilis* (sensible world) can be considered by the understanding, but only according to principles of appearances. The *mundus intelligibilis* (intelligible world) is pneumatic. If I think the *mundus sensibilis* according to concepts of the *intelligibilis*, it is a *mundus mysticus*. The *mundus sensibilis* has substances, forces and effects, but not the absolute [und reale] of these, only their appearances, place and the alteration of it.",[]],"reflm:5397":["*Mundus intelligibilis est monadatum, non secundum formam intuitus externi, sed interni repraesentabile.* (The intelligible world is [a world] of monads, representable not according to the form of external intuition, but of internal.)",[]],"reflm:5398":["With regard to the existence of the things of the world — either idealist or dualist. The idealist either egoist or pluralist.\nWith regard to the grounds of explanation of the form [der] either materialist or pneumatist.",[]],"reflm:5399":["Imagination presupposes a sense, of which it can reproduce the form. If there were no outer sense, we should also be unable to imagine things outside us as such, hence in three dimensions of space.\nIf the cause of the intuition of space were in us, we should be able to become aware of it as a representation of inner sense, and in that case we should have to ascribe space and figure to our representations of things as to the things themselves.\nDreams can represent to us things as external which are just then not there; but [sie] we could not even dream anything as external, if these forms were not given to us through external things. That one must believe in the actuality of external things, if we cannot prove it, would not be necessary; for that has no relation to any interest of reason.",[]],"reflm:5400":["The question whether something is outside me is the same as if I asked whether I represent to myself an actual space. For this is something outside me. This does not mean, however, that something exists in itself, but that such *phaenomena* correspond to objects. For in the case of a *phaenomenon* there is never any question of absolute existence. Dreams [are to be understood] according to the analogy of waking. Apart from the representations of waking that agree with those of other men, I have no other mark of an object outside me; a *phaenomenon* in space outside me is therefore what can be known according to rules of the understanding. How can one then ask [whether] there are actually outer *phaenomena*? We are indeed not immediately conscious of their being outer, i.e. not mere imaginings and dreams; but we are conscious that they are the originals of all possible imaginings, and hence themselves not imaginings.",[]],"reflm:5401":["We find in bodies nothing but impenetrability and divisibility, i.e. the possibility of parts being without connection with the others. From this it does not follow that [something there] [the] some of these parts are the last, i.e. indivisible, except by means of the concept of substance. But this concept, in respect of [the] appearance, is merely what persists in respect of the alterations, i.e. the divisions, and does not signify the last subject of all relations. Hence the body is substance. But *in sensu intellectuali* [in the intellectual sense] a *corpus mysticum* is no substance but an aggregate of substances. The last subject does not appear outwardly. The *substratum* of outer appearance is merely what acts persistently; combination or separation is a matter of indifference in respect of it. It does not inhere further in any other, and is no *accidens* of another (g e.g. matter) which could vary. The *substantia noumenon* is not merely what persists amid the appearances, but is the last subject in every respect, and therefore cannot itself consist in relations. But this concept, that matter is the last subject in every respect, does not appear; rather, appearance cannot give substances at all, but only its *phaenomena*, which are all *continua*.",[]],"reflm:5402":["At the very top of the page:\nThe difference between substance as outer *phaenomenon* and the inner representation is this: the former is the subject of inherence without determining unity, whereas the latter determines unity through the I.",[]],"reflm:5403":["The *phaenomenon substantiatum* does not consist of simple *phaenomena*; rather, all *phaenomena* are *quanta continua*.",[]],"reflm:5404":["Beside, below and [the sentence is left incomplete]\nOne cannot argue about appearances further than the conditions of appearance extend. Hence one cannot argue from grounds of intellectual concepts [which] about space and time that they are not *substantiae*, *accidentia*, *relationes*. For from this it follows that it is only the form of appearance. Likewise, from impenetrability and extension one cannot argue further than to the divisibility which space permits, of which it is the appearance; nor from the concept of substance, if it has been abstracted from inner sense.",[]],"reflm:5405":["*Essentia* [essence] is the internal (g logical) principle of conceivability (*conceptibilitas*); *Natura* [nature] (g causal principle) is the internal principle of the causality of all determinations.",[]],"reflm:5406":["The first [pri] inner (g determining) principle of what belongs to the concept of a thing is essence [Wesen].\nThe first [pri] inner (g determining) principle, however, of what (g to) belongs to existence is entitled nature [Natur]*(s *materialiter*: the sum total of appearances; *formaliter*: the *nexus* of determinations).\n*(s Nature in contrast 1) with essence, 2) with art, 3) with freedom. In the last sense the essence is thought as something that gives itself laws.)",[]],"reflm:5407":["The nature (of a thing) taken adjectivally in relation to |\nnature (according to totality), substantively as against the hyperphysical. | Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:5408":["The inner principle of the concept of an object is essence. The inner principle of the actuality of everything that belongs to an object is nature.",[]],"reflm:5409":["All appearances lie in one nature.\nNature is the inner *principium causale* [causal principle] according to constant laws: *leges stabiles*. Substances have nature.\n*physice necessarium vel practice* [physically necessary or practically].",[]],"reflm:5410":["Nature takes its first rules from the understanding. *Commercium*. Every outer action of substances is reciprocal; indeed, all motion of bodies is reciprocal.",[]],"reflm:5411":["Nature in relation to chance or fate. Opposite: freedom.\nNature in relation to the supernatural. Opposite: absolute necessity, and the contingent nature of things in the world.\nNature of the world in general.",[]],"reflm:5412":["The mathematical antinomies concern the essence of appearances, the dynamical concern nature. The former concern appearances determined merely through space and time, the latter appearances determined through real grounds.",[]],"reflm:5413":["That which in the appearance of a rational being begins only relatively, and presupposes in time something else which determines its existence according to a rule, has in this [being], as intelligence, its ground in that which does not begin and is not subordinated in time to any preceding state. Therein consists the freedom of a rational being as cause through its reason. For reason is a power of determining itself *a priori*. For if the determining grounds were empirical and given subjectively *a posteriori*, the judgment of reason could not be *a priori*, and hence could not be regarded as absolutely necessary.\nIn order to judge objectively universally, and indeed apodictically, reason must be free from subjectively determining grounds; for if these determined it, the judgment would be contingent just as it is, namely according to its subjective causes. Reason is therefore conscious of its freedom in objectively necessary judgments *a priori*, namely that only the relation to the object is the ground of them.",[]],"reflm:5414":["Empirically one can indeed arrive at rules, but not at laws; as Kepler in comparison with Newton. For the latter involve necessity, and hence that they be known *a priori*. Yet one always assumes of the rules of nature that they are necessary (for that is why it is nature), and that they can be seen *a priori*; hence they are called laws *anticipando*. The understanding is the ground of empirical laws, and hence of an empirical necessity, where the ground of lawfulness can indeed be seen *a priori* (e.g. the law of causality), but not the ground of the determinate law. All metaphysical principles of nature are only grounds of lawfulness.",[]],"reflm:5415":["The harmony of the substances of the world is established either *generaliter* [generally] or *singulariter* [singularly] (*stabilita*); the first is necessary when many substances make up a whole in which [if a part] the state of a part always belongs also to the state of the whole.",[]],"reflm:5416":["The *harmonia generaliter stabilita* is never *praestabilita*; for the latter is always *singulariter stabilita*. Therefore: all *harmonia est vel interne vel externe stabilita* [harmony is established either internally or externally]; the latter *vel singulariter vel generaliter*, the former *vel occasionaliter vel praestabilita*.",[]],"reflm:5417":["The ground of the universal connection of substances is also the ground of space.",[]],"reflm:5418":["We cannot use the *sensitiva* to explain the *intellectualia*, and hence cannot use space to explain the *commercium*. [We can use] forces still less, tautologically.",[]],"reflm:5419":["*Principium commercii debet esse omnibus commune. Ergo causa communis.* [The principle of commerce must be common to all. Therefore a common cause.]",[]],"reflm:5420":["The harmony of substances is either[real or ideal. The first is influence, the other *harmonia externe stabilita*] founded on a *commercium* of the|same and is entitled influence: *harmonia naturalis*; or [ohn] on an external determination, and is then entitled [*externe stabil*] *artificialis*; this is either *per* — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5421":["Below and [the sentence is left incomplete]\n(s *influxus precarius, postulatus* [precarious, postulated influx])\n*Influxus hyperphysicus* [hyperphysical influx] is self-contradictory; that is, it is a *harmonia* [appar] that merely bears the appearance of influx.",[]],"reflm:5422":["*Influxus physicus* [physical influx] is either naturally original or derivative; the latter is rational.",[]],"reflm:5423":["*Harmonia* is either without *commercium* or from *commercium*.",[]],"reflm:5424":["[influxus] *Commercium* is either *originarium* or *derivativum* [original or derivative], and in the latter the *influxus* is either [*avtomaticus*] *physicus* or *hyperphysicus*. The latter is either *avtomaticus* or *occasionalisticus*.",[]],"reflm:5425":["The *nexus* of substances is either *physicus* or *hyperphysicus*.",[]],"reflm:5426":["If one cannot infer from the determinations of the one substance to the other, there is no *nexus realis* but an *idealis*.",[]],"reflm:5427":["*Influxus* is either real (*physicus*) or ideal. The latter is *harmonia* without *influxus*.",[]],"reflm:5428":["*Commercium* is either original or derivative; the former is the cruder *influxus physicus*. The derivative, from a common cause, is either *physicum* or *hyperphysicum*, and the latter of *praestabilita* or of *occasionalis harmonia*. The last is a *commercium* or harmony without nature.",[]],"reflm:5429":["Community (*commercium*) (the ground of which, *communitas*, consists in this, that what befalls one thing by way of a change has an effect upon all) is twofold: either [according to laws of the] real effects or ideal ones, which are merely the *phaenomenon* of the effects. The influences of bodies are merely the *phaenomena* of effects. For matter produces only relations of space; but these are not something actual, nor an *accidens*, and so are no true relation of one *substantia* to another such as would alter it inwardly. An outer relation without an inner one, however, is nothing true, but a mere *apparentia*. The community of bodies with one another can be understood according to *leges phaenomenorum*; the community of spirits only according to *leges intellectuales*, the application of which, however, requires experience. That of bodies and spirits cannot be comprehended at all, because the latter are known only through inner sense, and so their changes relate *realiter* [to what is real], whereas the effects of the former, and of their forces, relate only to *phaenomena* of relations in general, without their (g inner) determinations.",[]],"reflm:5430":["Nature and essence.\nNature and art.\nNature and freedom.\nThe nature of a thing (*formaliter*). Nature taken absolutely (*materialiter*): the world, the sum of the objects of the senses according to rules.",[]],"reflm:5431":["The inner *principium caussale* [causal principle] of all that constitutes the [state] actuality of a thing is the nature of the thing. [Likewise] of the world.\nThe inner *principium possibilitatum* [principle of possibilities] is the *essentia*.",[]],"reflm:5432":["The nature of a thing is the complete real ground of whatever follows necessarily, according to universal laws, from the constitution of a thing. Hence the natural is to be contradistinguished: 1. from the artificial or arbitrary, what is added, e.g. senses and learning; 2. from the essence, which contains what relates necessarily to the concept of the thing, and so is not always the whole nature [the]; 3. from that which arises externally: *accessorie*. [Something is] contrary to nature in so far as it resists the laws of the nature of a thing and the possibility of inferring, (g according to) universal rules, anything from it. (g 4. from freedom.)\nThe universal real ground of the determinations inhering in a thing is nature; it is therefore that through which, according to a universal law, whatever belongs to the predicates of its existence is determined.",[]],"reflm:5433":["The word nature is taken either substantively or adjectively. Taken substantively it is the aggregate of all objects of the senses; taken adjectively it is the (g inner) *principium* of the efficacy of a being.",[]],"reflm:5434":["Whether we have an experience that we are free?\n(s No! For otherwise we should have to be able to learn by experience, of all men, that they can resist the strongest *stimulus*. The moral law, on the other hand, says: they ought to resist, and consequently they must be able to do so.)",[]],"reflm:5435":["The will is a faculty of acting according to the representation of a rule as a law. A faculty of ends. *Stimuli* are pleasure which precedes the law. *Independentia a stimulis* [independence of stimuli] is where the law precedes the pleasure. (g *Arbitrium purum*.) (s Freedom is the causality of pure reason in the determination of choice [Willkühr].)",[]],"reflm:5436":["Freedom is the faculty of being determined only by reason, and not merely mediately but immediately, hence not through matter, but through the form of the law; therefore morally.",[]],"reflm:5437":["Either the object of the action or the action itself must interest. The good is either the useful or the good in itself.",[]],"reflm:5438":["The faculty of producing, absolutely and of itself, the motives of willing is freedom. This *actus* does not itself rest upon willing, but is the spontaneity of the causality of willing. For this we reproach or approve ourselves.",[]],"reflm:5439":["Beside and \nOuter freedom, also inner.\nThe faculty of choice [Willkühr] to act according to laws given by itself.",[]],"reflm:5440":["Freedom, in so far as it is a concept of reason, is inexplicable (g not even objectively); in so far as it is a concept of the activity and causality of reason itself, it indeed likewise cannot be explained as a first principle, but is a self-consciousness *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5441":["All our actions, and those of other beings, are necessitated; only the understanding (g and the will, in so far as it can be determined by understanding) is free, and a pure self-activity, determined by nothing other than itself. Without this original and |\nimmutable spontaneity we should know nothing *a priori*; for we should be determined to everything, and our thoughts themselves would stand under empirical laws. The faculty of thinking and acting *a priori* is the sole condition of the possibility of the origin of all other appearances. The ought would also have no meaning at all.\nFreedom and absolute necessity are the only pure concepts of reason which are objective, although inexplicable. For by reason we understand the self-activity of proceeding from the universal to the particular, and of doing this *a priori*, hence with a necessity absolute. Absolute necessity: in respect of what is determinable; and freedom: in respect of what determines.",[]],"reflm:5442":["Logical freedom in respect of all that are contingent predicates.\nAll contingency in the object is objective freedom (to think the opposite); if the thought suffices for the deed, also subjective freedom.\nTranscendental freedom is the complete contingency of actions. There is [transcendental] logical freedom in actions of reason, but not transcendental, that is, objective, freedom.",[]],"reflm:5443":["Talents give men importance, merits dignity, all morally good qualities a worth.",[]],"reflm:5444":["Nothing is good absolutely (unconditionally in every respect) except the existence of freely acting beings, and in these not understanding, happiness, etc., etc., but the disposition to make good use of everything that is mediately good, |\nin a fitting way. Thus the practical disposition of good intentions. This, then, is the goodness of nature [Gutartigkeit] of the will.",[]],"reflm:5445":["The moral laws do not arise from reason, but are that which contains the conditions through which alone it is possible that free actions can be determined and known according to the rules of reason. This happens, however, when we make the universally valid end the ground of actions, so that the particular ends may agree with those which can be regarded as if all things were possible through them.\nThe morally good requires complete unity of the ground of the action for reason, and consequently that it be derived from the *idea archetypa*, which is the end of the whole world.\nWhat accords with the conditions under which [something] everything happens according to rules *a priori* pleases necessarily. For it brings about agreement with the whole of nature, and thus a consciousness of the agreement of actions with themselves and with all others. Only from the whole and from the highest ground can that be derived which, according to universal laws [text breaks off].",[]],"reflm:5446":["Moral laws are those [of the necessity of actions from the] which contain the conditions through which free actions become consistent with the universally valid end, and so the private will with the original and highest will. [They accord] either with the universal end of nature or with that of freely acting beings. The will is thus considered [as] according to the unity of the ground, in so far, that is, as all will lies in one will: that which is the cause of nature and of every other.",[]],"reflm:5447":["The constitution of the morally good will is good conduct (of man); it is virtue if it proceeds from inclination, and fear of God if it proceeds from obedience to God.",[]],"reflm:5448":["The moral motives ought to have not merely *vim obiective necessitantem* [an objectively necessitating force] for the [practical] conviction of the understanding, but *vim subiective necessitantem*, i.e. they ought to be *elateres* [springs]. The subjective condition under which they can be so [is] is entitled feeling. Were it a real feeling (*proprie*), the necessitation would be pathological; the *causae impulsivae* would not be *motiva* but *stimuli*; it would not be the goodness [bonitas] but the agreeable (*iucundum*) that would move us. Therefore the *sensus moralis* is so called only *per analogiam*, and ought to be entitled not sense but disposition [Gesinnung], according to which the moral motives necessitate in the subject just as *stimuli* do. It is therefore in the proper sense (*in sensu proprio*) a nonentity, a mere *analogon sensus*, and serves only to express a faculty (not a receptivity) for which we have no name.",[]],"reflm:5449":["Above and beside the conclusion of M § 730 »servitutem — servile«:\n*Arbitrium servum est, in qvo imperium in semet ipsum cessat* [The will is servile in which command over itself ceases].",[]],"reflm:5450":["Nature and freedom are two principles of the determination of our concepts. For explanation we can make use of freedom only *secundum qvid* [in a certain respect]; for nature alone gives the ultimate determining grounds. For this alone contains principles of actual conduct. If I am to state the ground why someone conducts himself in this way or that, an appeal to his free choice is a circle.",[]],"reflm:5451":["The soul thought through intellectual determinations:\n1. in respect of the subject,\n2. in respect of the forces,\n3. in respect of the state.",[]],"reflm:5452":["The soul as *substantia*.\n*intelligentia* (not pure).\n*simplicitas*. *Vnitas*.\n*libertas*. *Commercium cum corpore* [community with the body].\n*Sedes* [seat]\n      as spirit.\n*immaterialitas* (s *personalitas*)\n       *ortus*, *mors* [origin, death] (s *conscientia*)",[]],"reflm:5453":["All inner experience [has] is a judgment in which the predicate is empirical and the subject is I. Independent, then, of this experience, there remains over for *psychologia rationalis* merely the I; for the I is the *substratum* of all empirical judgments.",[]],"reflm:5454":["Metaphysical predicates: 1. The soul is substance; 2. a substance distinct from matter, no object of outer senses; 3. simple, hence immaterial; 4. but it does not follow whether it persists.",[]],"reflm:5455":["Empirical psychology (*psychologia empirica*) contains no empirical conditions of the propositions about the soul, but only the empirical concept [by which] of the soul itself, with everything that belongs to it in order to distinguish it from everything else. It therefore does not belong to *philosophia pura* (pure philosophy), but neither does it belong to physiology; it belongs to metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:5456":["In and \nThinking and willing can in no way be an outer appearance. Now I know myself as soul only through thinking, feeling and willing. Hence we cannot represent the soul at all as an object of outer senses. Everything of which we are conscious in it [is] therefore indicates an immaterial being. We would have to perceive outer things and ourselves through one and the same sense.",[]],"reflm:5457":["In and \nOn the possibility of the *commercium* (community, reciprocal interaction) between what is only an object of inner sense and what is only an object of outer sense. In matter we know immediately only what is outer, in the soul only what is inner. The *commercium* (g among) the objects of outer senses we do not know originally and a priori, nor yet the *commercium* of the inner powers of the soul. But the first *data* of outer knowledge already contain concepts of *commercium*, and those of inner knowledge likewise. The *phaenomena* (appearances) are thereby compared with one another in respect of identity. We can therefore see a priori neither the natural possibility nor the impossibility; consequently all systems on the matter are in vain, and it is a proposition of experience that inner determinations are grounds of outer ones and conversely, and this is an original power.\nOn the seat of the soul: whether it is determined as a material substance, in respect of space, [externally] in the body, or only through the *commercium* with the body in respect of the rest of the world. It has its seat, that is, the first and immediate connection with the nervous system.\nEverything of which a universal rule can be known through experience is natural. Accordingly the *commercium* is natural. Immateriality is a correct problematic concept, which cannot be refuted at all.",[]],"reflm:5458":["The concept of the human soul is wholly independent of the concept of materiality. Nor can determinations of inner sense be understood as outer *phaenomena* (appearances), or the converse. The proof that the soul is immaterial [is] would be this: 1. it is not matter (*materialismus in sensu crassiori*, materialism in the cruder sense), hence simple; 2. it is no simple part of matter, for matter does not consist of simple parts. An animal is animated matter. All animated matter is at the same time ensouled. For the principle of life is the absolute inner (*absolute internum*), that is, thinking and willing, that is, determinations of itself according to choice.",[]],"reflm:5459":["*sedes animae* (seat of the soul). What is merely an object of inner sense can be no object of outer sense. Accordingly the substance of the soul cannot be subjected to the conditions of outer intuition, but only the subject of its influence; and the soul's efficacious power (outwardly) is a *phaenomenon*. There, however, everything that is an instrument of sensation is immediately the subject of its presence. It can act only by exciting the reciprocal action of the bodily parts, their attraction and repulsion.",[]],"reflm:5460":["In and \nThe soul does not move other things by moving itself.\nIt is no object of the *phaenomena*. It [moves] brings about motions in others which do not have their determining ground in matter [or], though the motion of these is not thereby altered in sum.",[]],"reflm:5461":["I, as the correlate of all outer intuition, am a human being. The outer intuition to which all the rest in me relate is my body. Hence, as a subject of outer intuitions, I must have a body. The conditions of outer intuition and of inner determine each other reciprocally (*commercium* of soul and body). The actuality [of a b] of bodies, in respect of myself, is the connection of outer appearances with one another; and in respect of others, it is the agreement, in the relations, of the outer appearances of that by which others designate their intuition, with the designations of my understanding, and consequently still the agreement of all outer appearances with one another. The actuality of bodies is not the actuality of things, but of appearances, which [are] referred to x, y, etc. etc. Something that is represented only as object of appearance and yet is supposed not to be one is nothing for us. Idealism denies more than one knows; realism assumes more than what is in question.",[]],"reflm:5462":["In the case of epigenesis we must assume that the soul belongs in general to the intelligible world; that it has no place in space; that, when an organised body has come into being through generation, it has |\nin itself the condition of being thereupon ensouled by the intelligible vivifying principle; and that in the body itself the soul is present not locally but virtually.",[]],"reflm:5463":["With empirical proofs we do not get out of the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:5464":["Empirical proofs hold neither for nor against, because we can make no experience other than in *commercium* with the body. We are harnessed to the body like a cart. Now its wheels must be in good condition, otherwise we cannot move it, and on that account not ourselves either. Here the argument is only κατ ἄνθρωπον (*ad hominem*). Semblance set against semblance.",[]],"reflm:5465":["1. A proof a priori from the concept* (I think) of a living being in general. [2. From the knowledge of another being a priori (of the necessary, God): *theologia moralis*] 2. Proofs a posteriori** neither for nor against the immortality of the soul are possible from empirical psychology, because we can make no experience other than in *commercium* with the body. A proof a posteriori can be conducted cosmologically.\n*(s of the (g immaterial) soul; 2, from the divine attributes)\n**(s either from the nature of the soul or from analogy)",[]],"reflm:5466":["M 314:\n* *argumentum est vel naturale vel morale***; the former either *rationale* or *empiricum* (s the latter either *stricte morale* or *theologicum*).\n*(s Immortality is not proved through the moral argument, nor strictly its universality and eternity.)\n(s The empirical is physical or teleological.)\nM 314':\n**(s The physiological is either a strict or an analogical argument; (g an argument) that stands in necessary connection compels; what consists in mere comparison touches. A strict argument is genetic, the analogical is elective.)",[]],"reflm:5467":["*Interitus corporis tollit vitam animalem, non spiritualem.* (The destruction of the body removes animal life, not spiritual life.)",[]],"reflm:5468":["1. That the continuance of the soul's life does not depend on the *commercium* with the body, because all matter is lifeless.",[]],"reflm:5469":["Whether animal life is all life, or the condition of the beginning of the spiritual?",[]],"reflm:5470":["The bodily means of thinking can fall away once the hindrances have fallen away.",[]],"reflm:5471":["Only the objections which the materialist makes against the immortality of the soul can be refuted metaphysically. The proof itself is only moral.",[]],"reflm:5472":["The proof of immortality is twofold. 1. From grounds of nature; 2. from moral grounds.\nI. a. From the nature of the soul known through experience; b. from the nature of a thinking being known a priori; c. from the analogy of nature in general. (g II.) The moral proof: a. theological, from the contingent will of God (g [as author of moral laws]) (g from moral grounds of the divine will); b. absolutely moral, from the necessary will of God, in that morality is regarded as necessary in itself and determines the belief in God and at the same time the concept of his will.",[]],"reflm:5473":["We have to prove: 1. the persistence (g of the substance) of souls: incorruptibility, for which simplicity is not enough;\n2. the continuance of [the person not merely] intelligence, not merely in capacity but also in act (*actu*). Sleep of the soul;\n3. the continuance of the person and of the consciousness of the identity of oneself. Not metempsychosis.",[]],"reflm:5474":["1. That the soul will exist after death,\n2. that it will live as an intelligence,\n3. that it will be conscious of the identity of its person.\n*immortalitas* (immortality). 1. From empirical psychology,\n2. from the concept of immateriality,\n3. from the analogy of nature.",[]],"reflm:5475":["The moral proof does not say that the soul will live in the future, but that the upright person cannot avoid assuming this and regarding it at least as possible.",[]],"reflm:5476":["If I had to assume the existence of God for the Pythagorean principle, it would not be a hypothesis but a postulate. Now, for good actions to take place, one must assume the existence of God as incentive, and these actions are nevertheless as necessary as geometrical propositions.",[]],"reflm:5477":["The moral *postulata* [postulates] are evident; their opposite can be brought *ad absurdum morale* [to a moral absurdity]. Their ground is that good conduct is the sole condition under which one is worthy to be happy. The ground of their *necessitation* lies not in their offering means to the desire for happiness, but in their prescribing to it the *conditio sine qua non* by which it is restricted. But our longing to be happy is equally necessary and at the same time reasonable [billig]. Now mere worthiness cannot move us without the hope that it will also make us partakers of the end; thus it is a necessary moral *hypothesis* to assume another world. Whoever does not assume it falls into an *absurdum practicum* [practical absurdity].\nThe *dilemma practicum* shows that prudence and uprightness can coexist in the hope of another world.\nIt is a principle of reason that we ought not to desire to be happy where we are not worthy of happiness. There is also another, that we should not be required to behave worthily unless we can hope to become so [happy: *es*].",[]],"reflm:5478":["The objections against the immortality of the soul are: 1. From experience during life: decline of the spirit; 2. From the contingency of generations and the wasting of germs. Pre-existence. Birth is appearance in the world of sense.\nSeparation of the soul and the body. Place.",[]],"reflm:5479":["Communion with the other world is either mystical or physical. The mystical cannot be admitted in this world, because laws of experience would thereby be interrupted.",[]],"reflm:5480":["Apparitions of spirits. Presentiment. Interpretation of dreams. Second sight. Sympathy of the soul.\nHappiness in this world consists in progress, and so can never be fully attained; every moment drives us out of the present state.\nMoral perfection likewise. Hence the future, [to determine] at what stage of progress it may be. Hence no improvement in dying. Not eternity |\nof rejection or of beatification, but the unforeseeable extent [Unabsehlichkeit] of progress.\nM 322':\nThat without which reason could have no known law is contrary to its maxim, e.g. appearances [apparitions], spirit.",[]],"reflm:5481":["In and\nWe can extend our concepts of beings a priori by omitting something that is empirical, but not by adding something that is not empirical. The concept of non-rational souls, or of beings not united with animality.",[]],"reflm:5482":["Possibility can be considered materially or formally. Likewise necessity. Formal necessity is always only the necessity of judgments. Something is materially necessary, and indeed absolutely unconditioned, without a restricting *respectus* [reference]. The proposition: there is a necessary thing, is inferred from this, that the *illimitatum* [the unlimited] must be presupposed prior to all negation.",[]],"reflm:5483":["If the proof [in s] for assuming necessity in the existence of a supreme being, according to laws of the understanding and maxims of the will, is also not apodeictic, it is nevertheless practically necessitating.",[]],"reflm:5484":["Proof a priori or a posteriori. The latter is physico-theological or moral. The former is transcendental or cosmological. The proof a posteriori cannot become demonstrative, because the ground for believing in a God amounts only to a necessary *hypothesis*.",[]],"reflm:5485":["He who attacks the proofs of the existence of God, or even sets up proofs of the opposite in order to show that the semblance is on the side of the opponent as well, contradicts only the knowing [Wissen] of the divine existence, but not belief [Glauben], which is the *principium* [arising] from principles.",[]],"reflm:5486":["*Ens summum* [the supreme being] is partly *originarium* [original], partly *ens entium* [being of beings].\nWhether an apodeictic or a practical proof of God is possible.\nThe proof is sufficient either logically or morally.\nA *propaedeutic* or introductory proof (Wolff), but no apodeictic proof.\nLimits of the world of sense, a priori and a posteriori.",[]],"reflm:5487":["Deism, atheism.\nWithout a determinate concept of necessity nothing follows but deism.",[]],"reflm:5488":["The proof of the existence of God is either transcendental or [met] natural (physiological); the latter is either metaphysical: from the existence of a world in general, or physico-theological: from the present world.",[]],"reflm:5489":["*Conceptus terminator* [limiting concept]. The object of all empirical knowledge is nature. The *principium* and the end of nature.",[]],"reflm:5490":["The being above nature we can know not through natural properties but only according to an analogy with them. Divine existence stands to all possible duration as time to eternity.",[]],"reflm:5491":["The natural concept.\nSome reality and some not: this is a division and presupposes the All of reality (s necessary presupposition in thinking in general). The proof is 1. from mere concepts, 2. from experience in general (something exists) or from determinate experience: physico-theological.",[]],"reflm:5492":["The necessary subjective condition of the (g inner) possibility of things is itself a (g in itself) necessary presupposition of the understanding. The being of all beings, the highest, the all-sufficient.\nThe necessary presupposition for the concept of the inner possibility of things is a subjectively necessary condition of the under|standing.\n(s That an absolutely necessary being must exist means: Our concept of possibility precedes actuality only in the case of appearances.)",[]],"reflm:5493":["The proposition of the ancients, from nothing comes nothing, or: possibility presupposes something actual, relates to the fact that our understanding can only order, but cannot produce *data*, and must find them ready to hand; hence all possibility is conditioned.",[]],"reflm:5494":["We arrive at the concept of God through pure reason in two ways: 1. in that we are led to the concept of an absolutely necessary [being]; 2. in that through concepts of possibilities we are led to the *summa realitas* [sum of reality]. From the latter, as a mere predicate, we cannot infer to the former. But both can be united.",[]],"reflm:5495":["M 332.\nMen would very much like to have a theoretical certainty of the existence of God, independent of all grounds of conduct, so that it would remain free to them to direct themselves by these modes of knowledge, and to act either in accordance with the moral laws or with the laws of inclination, according as they recognized that there is a God or that there is not. But the moral laws are of such a kind that with them there is no freedom of choice; and [they] there is no moral purity possible if, before the resolutions in regard to them, there precede modes of knowledge of the same, which make them unnecessary and reduce everything to prudence. Hence only so much light is given to us as [to] can subsist together with the originality of the moral incentives and their impelling force. If we were indif|ferent with regard to these, the (logical) grounds of proof alone would not convince us. Free belief in God is a merit; apodeictic certainty and compulsory belief from fear is none.\nM 332':\nHe who believes himself objectively free is a free-thinker [Freigeist] and a practical atheist. He who holds God merely to be the author of the general laws of nature, without particular government, is a naturalist and [also] without religion, as the former is without morality.",[]],"reflm:5496":["The author of nature (original being) is no *principium* of the government of the world according to the conditions of freedom.",[]],"reflm:5497":["The concept of God (g as supreme cause, necessary being) is a *conceptus terminator* [limiting concept]. It belongs to the world and yet is no part of the world; in accordance with the former [nach dem ersten] we can know in him everything that relates to the world, for which we need to know him as its cause; but we do not know what is in him. For in regard to that the concept also has no meaning.",[]],"reflm:5498":["Knowledge of the divine existence from practically sufficient principles is belief in it.",[]],"reflm:5499":["The necessity of a being. 1. As (g original being) independent, 2. As first cause, 3. As author, a. of the contingent in general or b. of order.",[]],"reflm:5500":["331'. E II 1646.\nM 331:\n* Absolute necessity is not inner (g whatever concept (g of the thing) I may choose to assume, its opposite is |\ninwardly possible). It is not outer, as hypothesis (g of everything) of existence. Consequently it is [necessity] as hypothesis of all possibility. Now the *substratum* of all possibility a priori is the highest reality. Therefore the opposite of it is not among the possibilities. For all negations are limitations; consequently in all possibility the unlimited is presupposed. This is laid at the foundation in every respect, hence is absolutely necessary: the positive concept of necessity. (g Descartes's proof. Wolff.)\nM 331':\n*(g The necessity of space and time cannot be proved, but only [shown] as a necessary condition of the possibility of things as to form, hence also of the *ens realissimum* [most real being] as a condition of their possibilities as to matter; meanwhile both grounds are only subjective.)",[]],"reflm:5501":["Proof from thinking in general, and not from the determinate concept of a thing.\nThe proof of a being that comprehends everything can be known neither a posteriori, nor a priori otherwise than from the conditions of our reason for determining the object of knowledge in the All.\nThe concept of absolute necessity also cannot be acquired otherwise. It rests on this, that everything that is to be thought must be given in sensation, and that *negationes* are limitations.",[]],"reflm:5502":["331'. E II 1628II.\nM 331:\nWe can think non-being determinately only in so far as we think the thing itself, and the non-being of the determinations when these |\ndeterminations are given to us. Thus [wen] I cannot think determinately that nothing at all exists, since I would have to say: there exists no body, no spirit, etc. etc. But this would not be thought if nothing at all were given.\nIn the concept of the *ens realissimum* alone are the concepts of all things generally (*omnimode*) determinable; hence it is the *substratum* of the thinkability [Denklichkeit] of all things. Absolute necessity differs from the conditioned in this, that the former is a necessary *hypothesis* for a determinate thinkable, the latter for everything.\nM 331':\nWe can indeed have a concept of a (form) shape without anything being there, but not of a reality; for this presupposes sensation, hence empirical intuition. Thus at the ground of all possible real concepts lies a *substratum originarium* (original substratum) as *realissimum*. Our thinking is only a reflecting. The material condition of all thinking (g of an object) is therefore that something be given as [object] in intuition. The first formal and real ground of thinkability is necessary. As I represent to myself different possibilities, I must assume a *substratum*. I can cancel any thing, but not the condition of its possibility.",[]],"reflm:5503":["That is unconditionally necessary which is necessary without restrictive condition, and consequently in every respect.",[]],"reflm:5504":["The possibility of a determinate, limited thing does indeed precede its actuality; but in regard to all things in general an actuality precedes all possibility. This is absolute reality.",[]],"reflm:5505":["The perfect precedes the imperfect a priori in the idea, and the latter is determinable only in the former. (g — thoroughgoing determination.)\n(g We would have no concept at all of the imperfect if we did not think the perfect.)\nThe cosmological proof is from the existence of the contingent, principally from the *primo motore* (first mover). The [Ursach] causality of the cause of the contingent is freedom. But the absolute necessity of such a being cannot be seen, i.e. necessity in every respect.\nPhysicotheology: author [Urheber] according to analogy.\nMoral necessity of assuming a God. Possibility is already enough — atheism.",[]],"reflm:5506":["The Cartesian proof is this: that a concept can be so constituted that actuality comes in among its predicates, although the concept of the thing in itself makes no difference with regard to existence or non-existence. That hence the non-being of this thing contradicts itself. But the cancelling of a thing with all its predicates is always possible; consequently this shows the falsity of the presupposition that it is feasible to make such a concept as could not be cancelled at all. [Note: the German has \"ob [sie] gleich\"; rendered here as \"although\"; the bracketed pronoun is the edition's.]",[]],"reflm:5507":["Near M § 815:\nThis all comes down to the faulty concept: to take existence from a thing; it should read: to take away a thing itself. Existence constitutes no *constitutivum* (constitutive element) of a concept; hence no concept contains it internally, and thus absolute necessity |\nis no internal necessity, but a respective one, yet in every relation, namely of the universal *substratum* to everything possible.",[]],"reflm:5508":["Even if the existence of God does not flow from the conditions on which we ground the concept of possibility, enough nevertheless follows from them for it to be conceded by anyone who is able to judge about this a priori. The subjective conditions of thinking therefore serve very much to convince *cat anthropon* (humanly speaking), not apodictically.",[]],"reflm:5509":["The supreme *principium* (principle): 1. of the possibilities of things; 2. of nature; 3. of [Mo] morality.",[]],"reflm:5510":["At the very top of the page:\nThe *summum bonum reale* (real highest good) is the universally valid cause.\nThe *summum bonum formale* (formal highest good) (g *finale*) (*ideale*) is free choice [Willkühr], which is universally valid for every choice; hence this is also the form of the causality of the former.",[]],"reflm:5511":["It belongs to moral theology that in the most perfect world moral evil must be possible, because freedom and incitement to evil must be in it. Whether it is possible that God does not prevent it can be seen only by one who knows the relation of the parts to the whole.",[]],"reflm:5512":["M 335:\nOf the *ens originarium* (original being) (s *omnisufficiens*) (s necessity, all-sufficiency, unity. Noumenon) 1. *Necessitas*. 2. *substantialitas*.* 3. im|*mutabilitas*.\n4. *impassibilitas*. (g b. *omnisufficientia*. *infinitudo*.) 5. *Vnicitas*. 6. *simplicitas*. 7. *immaterialitas* (s *supramundanum* (against the Spinozists)). 8. *ens extramundanum*: outside space and time (s Not in *commercio*, but certainly in *nexus* with the world.) 9. *aeternitas*. 10. *omnipotentia*. **11. *omnipraesentia*. (s 12. *immensitas*. *omnitudo*. 13. Analogy as *ens extramundanum* against the anthropomorphists.) (s Is not an *ens homogeneum* with the world. Is not a member in the series of degrees of things.)\nM 335':\n*(g Immutability rests not on necessity, but on this, that the *realissimum* is not determinable *ad oppositum realitatis*.)\nM 335:\n**(s If something exists as conditioned; the possibility of creation in time or from eternity.)",[]],"reflm:5513":["M 335':\nThree kinds of perfection: 1. *transscendentalis* (transcendental), of causality; 2. *naturalis*, as author; 3. of ends (s *summum bonum*). *Ens optimum* (the best being). As supreme |\nM 335: |\nmoral *principium* (s holy being).",[]],"reflm:5514":["*Theologia rationalis* (rational theology) against (g *pharisaeismum*) *clericismum* (*plebeii* (g *laici*)).\n*Theologiae communitas* (the common character of theology) against sophists (*idiota*).\n(g *Theologia arrogans* (s or *popularis*).)",[]],"reflm:5515":["Speculative theology is only negative, serving to keep errors away. Practical theology is positive.",[]],"reflm:5516":["Moral theology alone requires a determinate concept of a highest being; natural theology does not, but makes the existence intuitive.",[]],"reflm:5517":["Beside and \nCosmological proof: An *ens limitatum* (limited being) can be determined in a twofold manner, NB. *respectu* (with respect to) its concept, but not as to existence or non-existence. An *ens realissimum* only *vnico modo* (in one way), but not with regard to existence.",[]],"reflm:5518":["Near and probably also \nAll possibilities presuppose the concept of an *ens realissimum*. And this concept presupposes [voraus ein] existence, because realities, unless they have been given, cannot be thought in sensation either; but what has been given in sensation exists.\nAbsolute necessity rests on the presupposed condition of all possibility, not on the identity of a concept with itself, from which no existence follows.",[]],"reflm:5519":["*Necessarium absolutum* (the absolutely necessary). Since difference can be thought only through limitations, [so liegt] so the ground of |\npossibilities cannot lie in many beings, because these themselves always presuppose something else; rather one must contain everything, and plurality [der] or manifoldness rests on the limitations of this unity.\nFor in our subject there is unity of all concepts, because possibility is only [mit] agreement with the whole of the inner *data*.",[]],"reflm:5520":["M 336'.\nA well-constituted soul is easily* convinced to belief (g in God and a future), but an evil one cannot be helped. The hardness of his heart makes him eager merely for speculation, and he at most fears a God, but does not believe in him, i.e. does not accept him.\nM 336:\n*(g From the Italian proverb: Deal with your friend as if he might one day be your enemy. God, protect me from my friend; from my enemy I shall guard myself well enough.)",[]],"reflm:5521":["The specific mark of God is not greatness but the necessity of his nature; but this is at the same time inseparably connected with the concept of the all [All] as condition of knowledge through reason.\nOne can proceed from a smaller to a greater being by degrees, but not from the contingent to the necessary.",[]],"reflm:5522":["The ground of the transcendental proof lies in this. We can think to ourselves [keine erste] possibilities only as derivative, not as original; |\nconsequently the All given to us is the *substratum* of possibility, on which, through limitation and through altered relations, all our thinking a priori rests. The subjective ground of all possibility is therefore this (subjective) sum total [Inbegriff] of reality, which constitutes unity in us; for only thereby can the thoroughgoing relation [was] and agreement, which is the form of possibility, be understood.",[]],"reflm:5523":["At the very top of the page and \n[Nicht Ma] In itself nothing at all is necessary, i.e. according to the laws of our understanding; for the non-being of a thing has in itself nothing contradictory. But from the fact that something is not known as necessary in itself I cannot conclude that it is contingent, i.e. that it could happen that it be or also not be, i.e. that its non-existence be possible as something that follows upon existence, or existence which follows upon non-existence. Although the existence of all things in itself can be cancelled, namely the thought, the thing is not on that account cancelled. The proof, therefore, of the necessary from the fact that if something exists it must be necessary or contingent, is not valid. It is neither of the two. Its existence has no connection at all with the thought of the thing.",[]],"reflm:5524":["On M § 826?\nTranscendental theology contains merely *praedicata logica* (logical predicates) of the *ens realissimum*; the *determinationes* as *praedicata realia synthetica* (synthetic real predicates) belong to theism.",[]],"reflm:5525":["1. Inner logical necessity proves no necessary existence: modalities of determinations are not themselves determinations. |\n(g Absolute necessity from respective predicates to possibility.)\n2. The conditioned is the only thing possible under given conditions. The totality of the conditions of what exists is the only thing possible absolutely, that is, in every respect; that which exists at all is, in thoroughgoing respect, the only thing possible. The supreme condition must therefore be the *principium* (principle) of all possibility, and consequently the *ens realissimum*.\nThe transcendental proof is conducted from the concept of the *ens realissimum*, not as a *conceptus determinati* (concept of a determinate thing), but as a *principium determinans summum* (supreme determining principle; *quoad existentiam omnem*, in respect of all existence). Necessary existence is not inner but respective, as the necessary hypothesis of all possibility.",[]],"reflm:5526":["Around and \nThe first subjective condition of thinking, that is, of the representation of objects in respect of their possibility, is that all matter of representation be given in sensation, and, objectively, in perception; the second is the form of the combination of the manifold. Hence that which contains the matter and data of everything possible is presupposed as an object of perception; that is, the matter of everything possible exists as a necessary presupposition. The manifoldness of objects in respect of this totality of reality rests on the form of the limitation of this totality. Hence there lies at the ground the absolute unity of the whole, within which everything is possible through limitation. Something must therefore exist as a substratum of possibility.",[]],"reflm:5527":["Over, beside and \n*Ens necessarium* (necessary being) is a subjectively necessary hypothesis for the thinking of all possibility. All possibility of a limited thing is derived in the idea, and presupposes a greater [reality] which is limited. All possibility thus has the *ens realissimum* as its condition.",[]],"reflm:5528":["Beside and also perhaps \nTranscendental theology has this distinct advantage, that in it alone the necessity of a highest being can be known. Hence it alone can also determine that being thoroughly, as unity, etc., etc.\nIt alone purifies *theologia naturalis* (natural theology) of the influence of empirical predicates, that is, of anthropomorphism. The disadvantage is that the ground of proof is only subjective; for existence cannot be known wholly a priori. But as a presupposition it can nevertheless be necessary in the practical use of the understanding.",[]],"reflm:5529":["*Anthropopathôs*.\nThe deist wishes to prevent anthropomorphism.\nWe can represent God to ourselves only according to analogy with human beings, but not according to similarity with them. Anthropomorphism is often more harmful than atheism.\nSpeculative anthropomorphism.\n[The] practical [anthropomorphism].",[]],"reflm:5530":["(g Presupposed existence of a world.)\nThe Wolffian cosmological proof of the existence of God is properly transcendental. (g Something exists. I am.) There must be a necessary being, one or several. [It exists.] I am not the necessary being. The necessary being must be unlimited. For the concept of the limited admits of many, and hence the possibility of the opposite; but the concept of the unlimited does not admit the opposite of a single determination.",[]],"reflm:5531":["*Theologia naturalis*. The primordial being [Urwesen] as a cause of nature, and so in relation to nature, since causality is expressed through determinations of nature. The chief concept of causality is freedom, as causality in respect of the contingent, and consequently intelligence.",[]],"reflm:5532":["Anthropomorphism, *crassior* (cruder) and *subtilior* (subtler). Its prevention *via reductionis* (by way of reduction); the purity of the concept of God.",[]],"reflm:5533":["The archetype [Urbild] is nowhere in the world, but solely in the Godhead. (g It is an idea, that is, a knowledge which contains the ground of the possibility of objects and of all judging of them, and cannot be abstracted from them.) There is no creature which is equal to the idea of morality; at least, moral perfection can be judged only according to the antecedent idea of it. We have an empirical idea of the beauty of a human being and of a horse; otherwise we could not say what each of them lacks. This idea is single (ideal in the head); everything in it is determined, if it is to have the greatest absolute perfection. That which exists in accordance with the idea is the copy [Nachbild] (imitation), and the sensible representation of it is semblance (*apparentz*). We must study the archetype of things that are possible only through the understanding from ideas, and not from experience. The idea of morals and of religion.",[]],"reflm:5534":["353. E II 1713.\n1. To determine God through psychological predicates, but from ontological grounds of the concept of a *realissimum*;\n2. from physico-teleological grounds.\nIn all theology, even if we determine God through psychological or moral predicates, we must yet, in order to infer them in God, derive them from the concept of the |\nM 353: |\n*realissimum*. This [raises the question]: whether as *complexus* (aggregate) or *ratio* (ground).",[]],"reflm:5535":["Whether God through his understanding (g not reason) also contains the ground of essences and of their possibility.\nThe difficulty is not how God knows the future, but how he knows at all what is actual, when it is present to us. [He knows it] only through the consciousness of his own will that it is there; but through this free actions seem to drop out.\nFreedom is absolute spontaneity of actions. The question is whether that of which God is the cause can have absolute spontaneity. If the power which he imparted is capable of determining itself to act from objective grounds, and consequently reason is an efficient cause, then he has imparted absolute spontaneity; the possibility of this is not to be seen. It comes down to how God can make a substance.",[]],"reflm:5536":["Whether God knows things and their actions by producing them?",[]],"reflm:5537":["*cognitio quiescens et operativa* (quiescent and operative cognition).\nThe former: where God knows himself and everything possible.\nThe second: where he [sei] knows the products of his will.",[]],"reflm:5538":["The possibility of foreseeing free actions has its difficulties only from the possibility of freedom itself.",[]],"reflm:5539":["The difficulty of the divine foreknowledge of our free actions lies not in their being future, for before God nothing is present, past or future, but in this, that God would in any case also know present free actions, and so in how even the *scientia visionis* (knowledge of vision) of them is possible.",[]],"reflm:5540":["The effect of God upon the human being as a [free] free being is the effect of self-subsistent reason upon our limited nature. For God is only an idea of reason.",[]],"reflm:5541":["(g In nature there is nothing evil, but [only] ill in the parts. In freedom alone is there evil. The whole of nature is nevertheless good, and the evil concerns only the part, just as each part can have collision.)\nImperfection is to be distinguished from ill (which is positive), and this from evil, which is an absolute ill and not merely respective, |\n. All ills come from the lack of unity in the world-whole. Hence conflicts arise. With perfect unity, not only in substance but also in forces, everything must agree with the nature of the being and so be good. In the human being evil rests on the animal [element], what [on the rational] on impressions and inclination, and on the conflict of these against the rational, which is directed to universally valid ends. If we considered the human being, before the development of his reason, as a species of animals, we should find the origin of evil.",[]],"reflm:5542":["The new discoveries in astronomy not only extend but also somewhat alter physico-theology. For if the human race is the whole genus of rational beings and the only one, one cannot well comprehend how this agrees with the wisdom and goodness of God, since something more perfect can certainly be conceived. But if there are millions of other worlds, this is one grade of rational creatures, which, together with its defects, could not be lacking.",[]],"reflm:5543":["Every series has its beginning; but that the whole series has a beginning presupposes that it can be considered as a whole.",[]],"reflm:5544":["A first beginning cannot be thought according to the laws of sensibility. A sequence without beginning cannot be thought according to the laws of reason.\n*conceptus hybridus* (hybrid concept), which constitutes the limiting concept [Grenzbegriff].",[]],"reflm:5545":["We must philosophize about nature as if the world had no beginning, and about God as if he had no succession.\nTo regard God as the uppermost point of the series is a confusion of kinds.",[]],"reflm:5546":["Beside and to the last three sentences of M § 926, especially the word »creare« (1909-12):\n*architectus et creator, moderator* (architect and creator, governor) (s 1. *Auctor mundi* [author of the world], not *causa physica* [physical cause]). *actuator substantiae mundi est creator, formae est architectus*: the actuator of the substance of the world is creator, of its form architect.",[]],"reflm:5547":["*Theologia applicata* (applied theology):\nCreation,\nGovernment,\nDirection to ends (determination, completion).",[]],"reflm:5548":["Over, in and \nThe bordering of the world upon an empty time and an empty space is impossible. Outside the world there is no possible appearance. Now the world, with all its predicates, is only the object of appearance.",[]],"reflm:5549":["In and \nWhy is the empty time before the world, or the empty space outside the world, not filled; or are both full; or is there no time and space at all outside the world? But if I nevertheless remain within the world, is the time of measuring it bounded or unbounded?",[]],"reflm:5550":["Whether God, in his creation of the world, comprehends [*Erschaffen*] everything that is in itself possible? and so also every possible free action? and whether it would then be free. How it is possible to create a being that acts freely. What chooses from its own liking according to a universal law is free. The motives do not determine it; it determines itself. But the grounds of the opposite also become, according to universal laws, merely motives for dissuading from the actions. The connection of the *causae moventes* in freely acting beings therefore consists only in this, that their actions are to be determined according to universal (g objective) laws of the power of choice (*Willkür*).\nChoice according to a universal law of the power of choice is the ground of the appropriation (*Zueignung*) of what has been chosen [*beliebt*]. But a desire that has been determined not from an inner principle but singly, as an impression, could not be appropriated.\nChoice according to universal laws of nature would be physically determined. But choice according to logically universal grounds, according to a [reflected] universal principle, in that I have the faculty of comparing something with all the grounds of my liking, is choice at pleasure [*beliebige Wahl*], and I am then free from single determination. All motives can be compared with these universal rules of liking and have their force, whether or not I am subjectively determined to these actions.",[]],"reflm:5551":["The honour of God does not properly name an end. It says only that the world is so constituted that it does the greatest honour to a divine author, that is, that one can recognise his perfection from it and can also have the sources for acquiring it therein, so far as is possible for a created being. But wherein this perfection consists is not thereby indicated, and it [is] the highest moral and physical finite good: to be happy and worthy of happiness.",[]],"reflm:5552":["P. I:\nConcepts of reflection (their amphiboly)\n[which lead to paralogisms].\nA paralogism is an inference of reason that is false in form, although it is correct in matter (in the premisses). It arises when [a] the middle concept is taken in a different meaning in the two premisses, namely when the logical relation (g in thought) in one of the premisses is taken in the other for a real relation (of objects of intuition):\n1. Identity and difference. 2. Agreement and opposition. 3. The inner and the outer. 4. The determinable (matter) and determination (form).\nDifferent relation to the faculty of knowledge: to sensibility or to the understanding, for difference of things and identity [of] before the former also such before the latter are [breaks off].\n1. Things identical in quality and quantity are not different (many) things, but one and the same.\nBefore the understanding, indeed, two drops of water, one egg equal to another, but not in intuition in space, as *phaenomena*.\n2. What is not logically opposed is also not opposed in space and time (really): a –a.\n3. [Sub] Outer things (g substances) must have inner determinations, but the determinations of matter consist in nothing but outer relations. Hence I cannot infer monads that have representations, because these are the only inner [thing].\n4. Matter (the constituents of a thing) precedes form. But in intuition the form, which is given by itself alone, precedes the matter.\nSomething and Nothing\n1.\nConcept without object;\nthis is nothing. *ens rationis*.\nThought-entity.\n2.\nEmpty object of a concept.\n*nihil privativum*. Shadow.\n3.\nEmpty intuition\nwithout object.\n*ens imaginarium*.\nSpace.\n4.\nEmpty object without concept.\n*nihil negativum*.\nNos. 1 and 2:\nthought-entity is distinguished\nfrom non-entity.\nAxiom, anticipation, analogy, postulate.\nThe synthetic propositions *a priori* are\nprinciples of possible experience, and so\nextend only to objects of the senses.\nConclusion of ontology.\nThe understanding prescribes the law to nature, but no law reaching further than that of the form of appearances, which grounds the possibility |\nof experience in general. For nature, as object of empirical knowledge, must conform to this, since otherwise it would not be nature for us, it being impossible to find in it a connection that would accord with our capacity for bringing the manifold of appearances into one connected consciousness; nature would accordingly not be knowable.\n(g Empirical intuition and concepts constitute experience.)\nWe can have synthetic knowledge *a priori* of objects of experience, namely when it [on] contains principles of the possibility of experience in general.\nP. II:\n( g 1. That the possibility of synthetic judgment *a priori* depends solely on the ideality of space and time. (g That if we were to know things in themselves, we should perceive them, and so should not know them *a priori* as so necessary.) (g Because it is only from the fact that our faculty of intuition has this form that we can know *a priori* how objects will be intuited by us. These forms are the merely subjective element of the faculty of representation, and this is objective with regard to things as appearances.) )\n3.\nThat we must supply a schema for all our pure concepts of the understanding, a [relation] way of composing the manifold in space and time. That this schema lies merely in the sensible representation of the subject, so that we [thereby] 1. know only objects of the senses, and consequently do not reach out to the supersensible (geometry). 2. The concepts, however, can be extended to all objects of thought in general. But they yield no enlargement of theoretical knowledge. In practical respect, however, where freedom |\nis the condition of their use, [thus] practical-dogmatic modes of knowledge [propositions] can nevertheless have place: God, freedom, and immortality (spiritual nature).\nIn nature, however, that is, in space and time, nothing unconditioned can be met with, and yet reason demands it as the totality of conditions, because it wants to make the object itself. Hence in cosmology, where nature is considered as the whole of all objects of the senses, antinomy is encountered. In theology, where we have to consider an object only with a practical-dogmatic purpose, the relation of the supersensible object, lying beyond nature, [only] to the things of the world can be known only by analogy with an intelligence of nature, and even so only in so far as it is thought in moral respect with regard to human beings.\nThree kinds of intellectual (g intelligible) (noumenon) contain the unconditioned, and of freedom and its laws one can have knowledge, and thereby prove the objective reality of humanity as noumenon in the midst of its mechanism as phenomenon. God as unconditionally necessary substance. Freedom as unconditioned causality. And immortality as personality (spirit) independent of the *commercium* with the body (as condition).\nThe categories applied to the intelligible can nevertheless ground practical-dogmatic modes of knowledge, namely if they are directed to freedom and determine its subject only in relation thereto. For then we know God only by analogy with the subsistence [of it] of a thing through all change of the accidents in time (g duration), freedom by analogy with causality in the [sequence] connection of the force with its effects in the succession of time, and immortality by analogy with the connection of many at all times, and hence [with the] the simultaneity of [breaks off].",[]],"reflm:5553":["P. I:\nAs the senses are related to the understanding, so is the understanding to reason. The appearances of the former obtain in the second |\nunity of understanding through concepts, and concepts obtain in the third faculty unity of reason through ideas. (Through *prosyllogisms* a higher subject is always found, until at last no other can be found of which the preceding one would be the predicate. The same holds of conditioned inferences, except that there the *prosyllogism* proves the *minor*.)\nThe modes of knowledge *a priori* which relate to the objects themselves are exhausted in the transcendental analytic, and are the categories of the understanding. Reason, therefore, cannot relate to the objects (appearances) themselves, but only to the concepts of the understanding concerning them. How, indeed, could a concept of reason (if there are any such) be objective, since it expresses [the condition] not the unity of (g possible) experience (through which all objects are given to us), but that of the modes of knowledge of the understanding? Yet modes of knowledge of reason that contain a wholly *a priori* unity, and not an empirical one, would have to be borrowed from the logical form of reason. The one qualification is that they would have to concern a synthetic unity to which the concepts of the understanding would be subject, and would thereby relate mediately to a particular [unity] determination of the unity of appearances. Reason seeks to bring modes of knowledge of the understanding (judgments) under higher (universal) conditions, [so can] so [far as] long as such a knowledge can still be regarded as conditioned. Hence it can be said that reason is a *principium* of the use of the understanding, [in the series of] namely to find the unconditioned for the conditioned. [This unconditioned now (g which contains the rule) in all relational concepts of the understanding. For] Now the relational concepts are nothing other than the [connection] unity of the conditioned and its condition, and reason carries this relation up only to the condition which is itself unconditioned. — Thus all modes of knowledge of reason will be parallel to the three kinds of inferences of reason, and more than these will not be possible. The principle of pure reason is this: all conditioned knowledge stands not merely under conditions, but ultimately under such as are themselves unconditioned. Whether this be a mere |\npetition or a postulate (which we do not yet wish to decide), it is at least the ground of all application of reason, which we at least approach. Every inference of reason is nothing other than [the knowledge] a judgment by means of the subsumption of its condition under a universal rule, which is therefore the condition of the condition of the conclusion. The conditioned inference of reason is so entitled improperly, for there are more conditions of judgments than merely the ground in respect of the consequence (*conditio consequentiae*). There is also a condition of inherence, etc. etc.\nThe proposition, If the conditioned is given, then the whole series of all the conditions by which the conditioned is determined is also given, is correct, [in da] if I abstract from the objects or take them merely intellectually.\n––––––––––\nThe unconditioned can never be given, but must at all times be present in thought. Hence ideas. The absolute totality of the conditions is the only unconditioned. Unity of the principle for reason. Approximation. Rules of the synthesis of the subordination of empirical concepts.\nP. II:\nSystem of the transcendental ideas. 1. The headings are three, according to the three kinds of inferences of reason; the dialectical inferences are four, according to the four categories. In the ideal of pure reason, since there [a Ha] all the categories are together in one idea, we have no need to distinguish them; for it is the *principium* of all possibility, whereby the categories themselves are determined.\nOn the three kinds of transcendental illusion. There are two ideas and one ideal.* The paralogism of pure reason is properly a transcendental *subreption*, in that our judgment about objects [vor] and the unity of consciousness in it is taken for a perception of the unity of the subject.\n*(g The first illusion is that in which the unity of apperception, which is subjective, is taken for the unity of the subject as a thing. The second: that in which the subjective determination of sensibility and its condition is taken for an object. The third: that in which the universality of thinking through reason is taken for a thought of an All of the possibilities of things.)\nThere is no deduction of the transcendental ideas except a negative one.\nOn the conclusion of the dialectic: that all dialectical questions be completely answered.\nAll concepts of synthesis require a third: either possible experience or the idea. In respect of the transcendental idea they cannot have objective validity, but can nevertheless be deduced as necessary problems or questions.\n––––––––––\nIn the understanding the concepts conform to possible experience; before reason, however, possible experience conforms to the concepts, just as all virtue in its practice must conform to concepts and is possible only through them, although it never attains to the concept. Possible experience, in the regress to the conditions, conforms to the concepts of reason or transcendental ideas. Therein consists precisely the business of reason, namely to procure for the use of the understanding, in the greatest manifoldness, unconditioned unity. That concept of reason which connects the greatest particular unity with this universal unity agrees with possible experience, and is to that extent a correct rule. But the concept cannot be objectively valid which does not stand (g in) relation to possible experience.\nThe ideal must be thoroughly determined, for the idea is the |\nstandard. It is the unmutilated being of a thing, and in it, as members of the disjunction, all possibilities lie.\n––––––––––\nAll three transcendental ideas are connected through one inference of reason. Namely: All objects of the senses are ultimately grounded on a (g existing) *noumenon*.\n––––––––––\nReason serves to give the understanding [extension in the] necessity, and to give the sphere of its use extent and unity.\nThe way of regarding the particular as a determination of the universal (of the true universal, which is not drawn by induction from the particular) is a unity *a priori*, wholly distinct from the unity of experience.\nP. III:\n(g Lucian's Writings. First part. Zürich, at Gessner's. 1769.)\nThe system of the dialectical inferences of reason rests on their unity with one another in a single inference of reason. In this there is, first, subjective unity of all representations, i.e. unity of the subject; second, [collective] synthetic unity of the object or of the appearance; third, [dritt] this unity either of appearances, collectively, or of thoughts of objects in general, distributively (disjunctively).\nReason [seeks] first cognizes the universal *in abstracto*; then it seeks to find the universal for the particular, in order from the former to infer again to the particular.\nFirst section: on the [transc.] ideas in general.\n––––––––––\n1. Remark. In inferences of reason, if the major is a proposition of experience [concept] (All men are mortal), it is nevertheless a universal concept. But since it is such only through induction, it is still no knowledge from concepts. Such [concepts] are therefore ultimately demanded by reason, in order that true universality may arise; for from |\nthis alone does reason wish to regard anything as determined in itself and necessary.\n2. The analytic inferences are indeed from concepts; but the major is a universal identical judgment, which expresses not unity of the diverse, but of the harmonious and identical.\n––––––––––\nThe unconditioned subjective conditions of thinking.\nThe unconditioned (g objective) condition of appearances.\nThe unconditioned objective condition of all objects in general.\n––––––––––\nBy the idea I understand a concept which is sufficiently grounded in reason, but to which no [corres.] object can be given in any possible experience.\nTo what, then, does the idea relate, and why is it grounded in reason, so as to relate to objects? The idea of the soul is grounded on this: that the understanding must relate all thoughts and inner perceptions to the I, and assume this as the one abiding subject, in order that the most complete unity of [Er] self-knowledge may result.\nThe idea of the unconditioned for all conditions of appearance is grounded in reason as a precept to seek the completeness of all knowledge of the understanding in subordination.\nThe idea of the unconditioned unity of all objects of thought in one *ens entium* is necessary, in order to seek the affinity among everything possible, and thereby both thoroughgoing connection and unity of the principle.\nIn law mere concepts of reason are needed, in morals, however, ideas. To the former a congruent object can be given in experience, to the latter not, because they contain the highest unity of reason, namely of freedom as agreeing with itself and with all the ends of its determination.\nLegislation has need of ideas, [ob sie] and these can never be completely carried out. They are not on that account void and superfluous.\nTranscendental philosophy requires ideas just as necessarily as morals.\nPlato's hyperbolical exaltation of the ideas as archetypes in the highest intelligence, when they are personified, is not to be censured; they are the standard of things, which limit one another and singly do not fulfil their end, so that no experience is congruent with them.\nP. IV:\nWhether the inference may not run from the human soul, through the concepts of the world, to God, and from him back again to the intelligible life of the soul?\nWhence, however, comes the dialectical illusion in the case of the transcendental ideas? That which produces all illusion, namely the confusion of the subjective conditions of our thinking with the objective. This we cannot avoid, because we must think an object as unconditioned, and have no other way of thinking it than that which the particular constitution of our subject brings with it. We can, however, unravel the sophistical inferences, whether in form, or in the minor, or in the major, because the synthesis goes further than the data and the mode of inference permit. The *suppositio* of pure reason occurs when one presupposes what is to be proved, and proves it through the consequences. For what is thoroughly determined *a priori* is presupposed, in order thereby to represent to oneself the thoroughgoing determination of everything possible.\nIf I assume: I am no appearance of inner sense, but a thing in itself and *noumenon*, then my inhering *accidentia* must also be *noumena*. *Atqui*: I represent to myself the series of conditions in the appearance; therefore they must be given in me in themselves. This conclusion, made the minor in the *episyllogism*, runs: If all appearances are given in One in themselves, then the idea [of the thoroughgoing], which determines things in themselves, is given in some intelligence.\n(g One says that something is a mere idea when one cannot even approximate to it.)\n1. The unconditioned of inherence (g or of aggregate). 2. That of [consequence] dependence or of the series. 3. That of the concurrence of all possibilities in one, and of one in all.\n(g As inferences of reason determine a judgment in general by subsumption, so reason determines the objects in the totality of the conditions.)\nIn transcendental psychology the identity, simplicity and modality of passive existence flow from the one concept of substance.\nThe absolute is as much as the unconditioned, and this as the completed, which is thought negatively without restricting condition.\nWe can think the universal only through the separation of all restricting conditions. The abstraction from the determinations of the self makes the I appear unconditioned.\n(g How can morals rest on ideas, when it is nevertheless required that actions shall conform to them? They can only be called concepts of reason. [The sage is an idea.] In their whole purity they are ideas. Justice.)\n––––––––––\nWe can entitle reason the faculty of ideas*. There are ideas of sensibility, and also those of pure reason. These are either practical or speculative; the latter are transcendental ideas. These are necessary concepts of reason to which no object can be given in the senses. As (g pure) concepts, however, they must nevertheless be taken from the categories; as concepts of reason they must, however, be merely inferred; as necessary concepts of reason they must contain the necessary conditions of the whole use of the understanding, i.e. of its use in its totality; and as transcendent concepts this totality must go so far as to surpass all sensible intuition, for otherwise they would be immanent. For this the absolute is required, etc. etc. How does the transcendental inference of reason run?\n*(g Reason is a faculty of determining [something] in the whole extent of its condition. When I say: Caius is mortal, I consider first mortality in the whole extent of the concept under which Caius is contained, i.e. men, and subsume the former [further] in this extent, in order to determine it therein.\n1. The unconditioned unity (g of the synthesis) of the subject. 2. The unconditioned unity of the synthesis of the conditions in the appearance. 3. The unconditioned unity of the synthesis of thinking in general. All through the four categories, so far as they indicate unconditioned unity.\nThe transcendental idea can have as its object nothing but the powers of knowledge, or representations in general in relation to them. Thus: 1. apperception, second, the apprehension of the appearance, 3. the concept of the understanding in general. The first is the concept of reason of the subject, the second of the object, so far as it can be given, the third of the object of thinking in general.\nThat pure reason has no objective content in its dialectical inferences.)",[]],"reflm:5554":["P. I:\nThe reflective concepts can be taken logically, and so as merely [intellectual] analytic, or transcendentally, and so as synthetic. Sameness and difference, taken intellectually, is analytic; but when determined sensibly it is synthetic, and difference of place, irrespective of the identity of the things in themselves, constitutes numerical difference. Agreement and conflict, logically, rest on the principle of contradiction; in empirical use they are two realities which together yield one reality or a negation. The inner and the outer, logically, is what is a predicate of the thing itself or of another; empirically, it is what is in another place of space. The determinable and the determined: the substantial and the substance.\nThe most celebrated proposition of subreption springs from this: the *principium indiscernibilium* (principle of indiscernibles).\nOur three higher faculties are directed to unity, truth and perfection, through understanding, power of judgment and reason. The last, in the transcendental use of the understanding, produces all sorts of confusion.\nThe whole monadology can be regarded as the *systema* [system]\n... of Leibnitz derived from the concepts of reflection. The inner and the outer, taken intellectually, give the *monas* [monad], because a *compositum* [composite] is a *totum relationum* [whole of relations], and give *repraesentativa* [representative], because the representations are inner, whereas place and extension are outer. Space is nothing but the *phaenomenon* of the outer. But what then do the monads represent, if they merely represent one another, each therefore the representations of the others? (Nor can one, with Maupertuis, derive the outer of motion from the inner.) Leibnitz: *harmonia praestabilita* (pre-established harmony), because the monads do not stand in *commercio* [community] by virtue of coexistence in space.\nP. II:\n*Noumenon* means (g properly) everywhere one and the same thing, namely the transcendental object of sensible intuition (g though this is not a real object or given thing, but a concept in relation to which appearances have unity); for something must correspond to this, even though we know nothing but the appearance of it. But we cannot say that the (g pure) categories have objects; they merely determine the transcendental object in relation to our sensibility, through the synthesis of the manifold of intuition. Hence no *noumenon* corresponds to them.\nThe transcendental object which corresponds to appearances (g or indeed any object) can be entitled *noumenon* only in so far as it can be represented through a concept of the understanding. Now this is impossible through the |\ncategory, since the conditions of intuition are lacking; hence we have no concepts of *noumena*.\nWe can [think and] use these concepts by analogy with the sensible ones; but because they have objective validity only in relation to the synthetic unity of apprehension in time, they relate in themselves to no object whatever, and under sensible determination only to *phaenomena*.",[]],"reflm:5555":["χ−θ? (ψ?)\nRemark by Kant on the back of the undated letter from Glave (X 243 [2 X 260]) in vol. II of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant, p. 273:\nJust as a pure concept of the understanding arises only through the form of judgments, in that I make them synthetic (g and thereby think an object), so a pure concept of reason arises through the form of a syllogism [*Vernunftschluss*]. This form, however, is the subsumption under the universality of [a] the condition of a judgment. The concept is therefore a representation of the totality of the conditions for knowing an object in accordance with one or other relation of a judgment. The logical condition of the judgment is the relation to the subject, etc.; the concept of a thing through this logical function is the category. The [totality] universality of the [condition] relation is the logical form of the syllogism; the concept of a thing through the representation of the totality of the condition [of the judgment] of the (g application of the) categories is the concept of reason.\nThe concept of the totality of synthesis according to the categories of relation is the pure concept of reason.\nWithout the concept of reason we should indeed have experiences, but the collective unity of experience would be lacking, whereas all empirical knowledge must be determinable in it.\nThe totality of synthesis: in a subject; second, in a series; third, in a system. A *suppositum* is the presupposition of what is to be found, together with its conditions, in order to find the synthesis |\nof the modes of knowledge by which we are to approach it. An idea is such a *suppositum*, which cannot indeed be given in itself, but can nevertheless bring formal unity of reason into our knowledge.",[]],"reflm:5556":["Analytic possibility rests on the principle of contradiction and is the possibility of the concept. Synthetic possibility, which must be added to it, that is, that an object can correspond to the concept, rests on this: that something is in accordance with the conditions of an object of experience (g in general). (g Only in experience can an object be given.)\nThe actual is opposed to the impossible and at the same time to the merely possible.",[]],"reflm:5557":["Possibility, actuality and necessity are not determinations, but modality of the positing of the thing with its predicates.",[]],"reflm:5558":["The propositions of modality, e.g. A is possible (really) (g actual, etc., etc.), are synthetic propositions; but something is added not to the concept of the thing but to thinking in general.",[]],"reflm:5559":["That possibility (g of a thing) from which all possibility must be regarded as derived is presupposed absolutely, and consequently is regarded as *positio absoluta* [absolute position] and as an existing thing; and here the |\ninference from the *esse* [being] of a thing to the *posse* [possibility] of all others holds, whence also the *posse* in general presupposes an *esse*. From the possibility of things in general we can infer to a determinate thing as ground.",[]],"reflm:5560":["The unity of the hypothesis consists (g 1.) in this: that, if I assume something as ground, the manifold can be derived from it. Unity.\n2. That I can assume this [unity] ground or concept, and that it therefore has reality. Truth.\n3. That the given consequences all together agree with the one ground, so that no *hypothesis subsidiaria* [auxiliary hypothesis] is needed. Perfection.\nHypothesis of a spiritual nature of the soul:\n1. That the phenomena of thinking, and of independence of the body, can be explained from it.\n2. That such a thing also has reality, and consequently synthetic possibility; for the analytic is clear. But since this would have to be known from the analogy of experience, which is lacking to it, truth falls away; *ens rationis ratiocinantis* [a being of reason that reasons].\n3. That all phenomena, so far as we know them, can be derived from it together, and so agree with this ground as the sole one. But this fails; for the dependence of the soul on mechanism requires yet another hypothesis, namely the influence of matter upon a spirit. Likewise the concept of God.\nIn distinguishing an idea (g *ratiocinata*) from a chimera, truth comes before [perfection] order; in distinguishing experience from a dream, order comes before truth, and order of nature is a criterion of it.\nFormal or material unity, truth and perfection. The unity of a thing, or unities.",[]],"reflm:5561":["These concepts are not the categories [but] of things; they are logical criteria of agreement with the laws of the understanding.\n1. Unity of the concept, that it be not [yet an]other than this one; or unity of the meaning of the word — Clearness.\n2. Truth of the elementary propositions (their plurality) — Distinctness.\n3. Completeness of the marks in a definition — Fullness and precision.",[]],"reflm:5562":["The transcendental concepts concern the relation of a multitude of representations to an object. 1. Unity of consciousness of something; 2. connection of the representations with one another in one consciousness; 3. the representation of the object that arises from this.\nThe three concepts of the possibility of a thing [as] in general: unity, truth and perfection, relate to the three formal principles of all judgments: that of contradiction, of sufficient reason, and of the determinability of the same in respect of all possible predicates (*judicia categorica, hypothetica et disjunctiva* [categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive judgments]).",[]],"reflm:5563":["[Logical] criterion of empirical truth. Order of nature, or order in itself, that is, connection according to rules, proves the relation to an object and not mere arbitrary choice.",[]],"reflm:5564":["Is it possible that nothing at all should exist?",[]],"reflm:5565":["Analytic possibility does not yet prove synthetic possibility. The non-being of a thing is without contradiction. From this I cannot yet conclude that it is really possible that it should not be.\nPossibility and necessity of propositions or of things.",[]],"reflm:5566":["The necessary is (g so far) determinable in only one way (*oppositum est impossibile* [its opposite is impossible]); the absolutely necessary is only a single one; otherwise, if there were more, one could cease without all existence being thereby abolished.",[]],"reflm:5567":["That which is [not] necessary without a real condition can nevertheless be necessary as a condition, which is yet not absolute but restrictive necessity.\nAbsolute dominion can indeed be conditioned, but not restricted.",[]],"reflm:5568":["Necessary is that whose existence can be known a priori.",[]],"reflm:5569":["Necessity is either logical or real: analytic and synthetic. Synthetic necessity [from] is either from concepts or from intuitions, or from the relation of concepts in general |\nto intuitions. Necessary existence is either derived or original. *Necessitas originaria vel derivativa* [original or derivative necessity]. Necessity as condition is properly hypothetical, yet not conditioned; it is necessity as presupposition.\nThe necessity of a thing in itself is that of existence, not of the relation of a predicate to the subject or of the conditioned to the condition. Existence does not belong necessarily to a thing necessary in itself. For then it would lie in the concept; rather, it is the condition for positing an existence at all.",[]],"reflm:5570":["Hypothetical necessity is to be distinguished from the necessity of a hypothesis, which is opposed to postulated necessity, that which is absolute. The former is the necessity of a premise, the latter that of a principle of knowledge. (g The absolute necessity of a thing is a necessary hypothesis of reason.)\nLogical necessity: that whose opposite is logically impossible from concepts, i.e. contradicts itself. Real necessity: that whose opposite is a nonentity [*Unding*], i.e. whose non-being cannot synthetically subsist together with a concept. The former is the necessity of judgments, the latter that of things.",[]],"reflm:5571":["A thing in general, as limited, is so far indeterminate in itself with regard to many predicates; but as unlimited it is thoroughly determined.\nBut as an individual (this thing) it is also thoroughly determined, only not from the concept of a thing in general.",[]],"reflm:5572":["Lower margin\nReal necessity is the necessity of the existence of things; logical necessity is the necessity of the connection of modes of knowledge, e.g. in judging, and likewise the necessity of a hypothesis for the contingent.",[]],"reflm:5573":["At the top of the page:\nOur concept of possibility is properly derived; for it presupposes something given, which (g with) the conditions of the understanding [ge] agrees in respect of form. Only a single thing is originally possible, namely the *realissimum* [the most real being], and this is presupposed.",[]],"reflm:5574":["Above and \nWhether something can be determined otherwise or not, we see either from the principle of contradiction; and then, if it [not in anoth] can be determined, i.e. does not contradict itself, real contingency does not follow;\nor from alterations: but there too not.",[]],"reflm:5575":["*Nulla mutatio est absolute necessaria* [no change is absolutely necessary]; it always has another cause. Hence no *absolute necessarium* [absolutely necessary thing] can be *mutabile* [changeable].",[]],"reflm:5576":["How is alteration in general possible? This cannot be explained, because it is determination in time and presupposes forces, and these only a posteriori.\n[All] alteration is not an arising or passing away of things but of their determinations, while the thing remains. E.g. motion is alteration of the relation of the body, not of the body itself. Inner motion is alteration of the body.",[]],"reflm:5577":["Mere form without reality (g thought as thing in itself) is *ens imaginarium* [imaginary entity]. Space.\nMere negation without reality is *non ens* [non-entity].",[]],"reflm:5578":["Realities in the appearance (experience) can well conflict with one another, but not in *noumena*, because in these the opposite of reality must be thought a priori, and consequently can be only logical, i.e. negation.",[]],"reflm:5579":["Opposite and \nBecause an *ens partim reale, partim negativum* [entity partly real, partly negative] is always thought as a defect (*qvoad entitatem* in general [as regards entity]), and this presupposes the concept of the *completum* [the complete], the concept of the *realissimum* lies at the ground of all things. The *realissimum* is also represented through its concept as a *singulare* [individual], and all its determinations indeed only generically and not specifically — breaks off?",[]],"reflm:5580":["To every reality a there corresponds its opposite =0 and its counter-play (*contrarium*) –a, i.e. a ground which, combined with a, becomes =0. Pleasure, pain, indifference; truth, error, ignorance. But light, darkness, and privation of light by dark bodies, i.e. shadow. Virtue, vice, *adiaphoron* [the morally indifferent]. In general: cause, inefficacy (inertia) and hindrance. Utility, harm, being indifferent.",[]],"reflm:5581":["The quantitative [disposition] decomposition, the division of the homogeneous, is to be distinguished from the qualitative (g resolution [*Zersetzung*]) of the heterogeneous, or from mixture (of matters differing in kind). Both go on to infinity. Water, for example, consists of [infinitely] matters, which again consist of others to infinity. No chemical elements.",[]],"reflm:5582":["(g There is no *quantum discretum*, but only *quantitas discreta*. Number.)\nQuanta are different in respect of quantity, the same in respect of quality, namely that of decomposition without absolutely first parts. Yet the quantum of absolute position or reality (g sensation) is distinguished herein from other quanta, that the former vanishes into nothing, whereas the latter still leave a positive boundary, though no part remaining. Boundaries of space (threefold) and of time (one). The generation of reality has a moment; that of the *quantum extensivum* has a (g *quasi*) element: differential. (The former is to be regarded as the line which in one time [with accelera] describes a surface.) As a different moment |\nproduces different degrees of velocity, so difference of impression produces different degree of sensation. Intellectually, however, a reality is not [ans] to be regarded as a multitude of different ones, but is* in continuous connection with nothing; division of parts.\n*(g We have 3 quanta: space, time and sensation (motion, reality). The first has a positive of the boundary, which is a quantum; the second, which is no quantum; the third nothing positive at all and no boundary, but limits [*Schranken*]. Reality taken transcendentally is not of different kinds.)",[]],"reflm:5583":["Mathematics, as synthetic knowledge a priori, grounds its possibility on this, that it can construct its concepts; for it has to do only with space and time, [which] in which objects of intuition can be given a priori. These, however, are quanta, so it is a science of quanta. But it also considers quantity by means of number, by means of multitude, which can be constructed in time by counting. (g This science cannot nevertheless go further than the world of sense, because only of this can intuition a priori be given.)",[]],"reflm:5584":["Possibility of the construction of concepts, since intuition is thus given through synthesis a priori.",[]],"reflm:5585":["Principle of the possibility of mathematics as synthetic knowledge a priori. It is the synthesis in intuition a priori, i.e. space and time. Pure mathematics.\nPrinciple of the mathematical knowledge of appearances: Every appearance has, as intuition, its extensive magnitude and, as sensation, its degree. For (as regards the latter) every sensation arises from non-being, because it is a modification. Hence through alteration. But all alteration passes from 0 to a through infinitely [small] many small steps.",[]],"reflm:5586":["The magnitude of that which is represented as multitude or as unity.",[]],"reflm:5587":["Some concepts cannot be constructed, such as the concept of reality, of cause, etc. etc. The magnitude of something, so far as it is represented as multitude, is extensive; as unity, intensive. Space and time are extensive. Velocity is intensive. Magnitude of the ground.",[]],"reflm:5588":["48'. E II 615.\nM 48:\nThe magnitude of a thing, so far as it is not intuited as composite*, but its arising and passing away can be regarded as composite (in time), is a degree.\nM 48':\n*(g because its parts are not outside one another in space.\nIntellectual magnitude is that of the ground.)",[]],"reflm:5589":["1. Possibility of (g pure) mathematics.\n2. [Application] Possibility of the applied. For all things (g as appearances) have a magnitude: extensive and intensive. (g Thereby mathematics obtains objective reality. It does not concern *entia rationis* [beings of reason].)\n3. All things as objects of the pure understanding also have a magnitude, namely a metaphysical one, but no transcendental magnitude, because in the one case they are compared, as things in general, with all others, in the other case, however, as concept (g of the thing) with the essence of the thing itself.\nThe possibility of natural science rests on the dynamical principles, namely those that concern the existence of things in connection. The former concerned only the (g empirical) intuition and the possibility of [according to] empirical concepts of the objects, but not so far as they belong to a nature.",[]],"reflm:5590":["All properties of things have a degree, but the thing itself (substance) does not.\n(g All intensive magnitude must yet in the end be reduced to extensive.)\n(g Inner) possibility [in itself] has no degree, but possibility in connection does. Possibility in all connection (absolute) has the highest degree; [ab] conditioned possibility, on the other hand, which obtains only in some connection, has a degree smaller by as much as there are many conditions belonging to it. E.g. many throws are needed to hit a *terne* [a triple, in the lottery].\nConnection is not always condition. Contentment is possible in solitude, in business, in want, in sickness. But these are not its restrictive conditions.\nConnection obtains either with its condition, which, if it is complete, is called complete possibility; or with other grounds, i.e. circumstances.",[]],"reflm:5591":["If a magnitude is given as a thing in itself, the whole precedes the composition. I cannot then infer, from the fact that this |\ncomposition can never be completed [k] and that its *quantitas* can therefore never be wholly known, that such an [qua] infinite *quantum* is impossible. It is only impossible for us, by our way of measuring magnitudes, to know it wholly, because it is immeasurable. It does not follow that another understanding could not know the *quantum* as such a whole without measuring.\nSo too with division.",[]],"reflm:5592":["Only in appearances can a first in time be sought, and in them there is no first at all. But a first of ground in general must be sought in the intellectual.",[]],"reflm:5593":["Mathematics has this peculiarity, that it (g only) deals with objects which can be represented *coram intuitu* [in the presence of intuition]. Hence it always has empirical confirmation.\nIt is possible as a synthetic knowledge *a priori* because there are two intuitions *a priori*, space and time, in which a synthesis of composition is possible *a priori*. These two objects are *quanta*, and indeed *originaria*, and the mere synthesis of them is quantity (*quantitas*). All concepts of *quanta* can be constructed in them, that is, given *a priori* in intuition; likewise all concepts of quantity, that is, of number, which requires time as well as space. The universal is here given [considered] in the singular, in intuition, and in the singular the universal of the synthesis is contemplated. In the case of qualities (*qualitates*) this is not possible. No mathematics can arise through discursive modes of knowledge. Mathematical modes of knowledge, as rational knowledge *a priori*, are apodictic, and as intuitive are demonstrative; both together make them evident.\nMathematics is distinguished from philosophy not by the |\nobject (both consider magnitude) but by the *modus cognoscendi*. This mode, however, determines the difference of the objects. (A dynamical principle.)\n(s Philosophy treats of magnitudes so far as one can get on by mere concepts, and mathematics treats of qualities so far as mere intuition extends. For example, the former deals with the question of simple parts, the latter with the cause of the moon's gravitation.)",[]],"reflm:5594":["93. E II 892.\nWe have no other concept of a contingent being (*ens contingens*) than that it is one whose non-being can be thought. But then everything is logically contingent. Really (*realiter*) contingent would be that whose non-being is in itself impossible, although it can be thought; but of this we have no concept, and it is problematic. We represent substances to ourselves as beings from themselves (*entia a se*), together with their states. Ends are beings from another (*entia ab alio*).\nWe have to do only with appearances and not with beings through themselves (*entia per se*). Hence the question concerns only contingency in the appearance, |\nM 93: |\nthat is, the contingency of what happens.",[]],"reflm:5595":["(s The principle of the final nexus (*nexus finalis*) is: everything is good.\nThe principle of the effective nexus (*nexus effectivus*) is: everything has its natural ground.\nThe supreme cause of final causes (*causae finales*) is God;\nthe supreme cause of efficient causes (*causae efficientes*) is nature.)\nAn effect whose concept can, through reason, be the cause (*causa efficiens*) [of] that same effect is an end; and the nexus of such effects, or [of] efficient causes, is a final nexus (*nexus finalis*).\nThe end is objectively the effect (g of an action), subjectively the cause of it.",[]],"reflm:5596":["Principles of the understanding are principles of the exposition of experience. Principles of reason are those according to which experience itself is given *a priori* (through the understanding) (freedom and necessary being). Sophistical [*vernünftelnde*] principles arise when the subjective conditions of reason are taken for objective. Principles of reason are transcendental maxims (g of speculation). The sophistical are transcendental paralogisms.\nTranscendental ideas of reason determining itself *a priori*.\nThe sophistical principle of reason is: what does not (g wholly) stand under the conditions of empirical determination is false. Hence every synthesis of magnitudes which is endless is impossible, and every dynamical synthesis which is not mediately determined (freedom and necessity) is impossible. On the other hand, the possibility of the idea of reason is likewise not to be seen.",[]],"reflm:5597":["The concepts of the world, of the simple, of freedom and of the supreme cause are pure concepts of reason, because they cannot be shown *in concreto* in experience. Their principles are therefore not principles of the empirical* but of the transcendent use of rea|son;\nnevertheless they lie in reason's advance towards the completion of the synthesis.\nDistributive or collective unity of experiences in general. All experience is not the totality of experience, and the whole of possible experience is no object of experience. But here there does occur [whereby] what is the condition of the possibility of a whole.\n*(g not to expound experiences, but [a pri] to determine them in an idea by limitation of the synthetic whole. 1. Advance to the synthetic unity of the parts. Beginning from the synthetic whole.)",[]],"reflm:5598":["Principle of commerce (*principium commercii*).\nAll connection of things, so far as they are simultaneous, is a *commercium*. For so far as they are simultaneous, that is, so far as I can pass from A to B just as well as from B to A, and each can be the consequent (*conseqvens*) in the synthesis of the other, they determine one another reciprocally; that is, they are in *commercio*. If, on the other hand, I could [only] determine B through A and not A through B, then B follows A.\nEvery thing has, in respect of what coexists with it, a relation of community. In temporal succession nothing can be reciprocally determined in the same sense.",[]],"reflm:5599":["At the top of the page. For the conclusion of the Ontology?\nThe principles of the possibility of experience are independent of experience. The connection of the principles *a priori* (necessary) with experience is either this, that they depend on experiences, or this, that experiences depend on them.",[]],"reflm:5600":["Beside and \nWe call nature the object of possible experience. Hence all our knowledge *a priori* extends only to nature.\nPrinciples of the understanding are rules *a priori* which contain the conditions of the synthetic unity of possible experience.",[]],"reflm:5601":["Up to this point we have spoken solely of concepts whose objects can be given to us in the senses, except in the concepts of the most real being (*ens realissimum*) and of the simple (*monas*).\nWe now come to concepts of the unconditioned totality of the synthesis (concept of the world).\nThe [whole] composite of substances as an absolute whole (is either a *mundus sensibilis* or a *mundus intelligibilis*).",[]],"reflm:5602":["The use of the concepts of the understanding was immanent; that of ideas, as concepts of objects, is transcendent. But as regulative principles of the completion, and at the same time of the delimitation, of our knowledge, they are critically immanent.",[]],"reflm:5603":["In the Ontology we have spoken of concepts of the understanding whose use in experience is possible, because they themselves make experience possible.",[]],"reflm:5604":["Cosmological ideas rest on the thought of the absolute totality of conditions in a whole, which is for that very reason unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5605":["Opposite and \nThe doctrine of monads must not pass over into physics.\nAre bodies, then, substances, or [not] accidents in themselves? They are neither the one nor the other; for they are not things at all, nor objective reality, but are appearances, of which the doctrine of monads does not hold. Something substantial may lie at their ground.",[]],"reflm:5606":["Atomistics, although it is very arbitrary, is nevertheless more suited to philosophy than monadistics. The former gives mechanical explanations from figure and laws of motion; the latter gives metaphysical explanations from merely intellectual concepts, which cannot be principles of phenomena (*phaenomena*).\nPhysical principles (*principia physica*) are either mechanical or dynamical.",[]],"reflm:5607":["All possible objects of experience have their nature, partly their particular nature, partly that which they share with other things. Nature taken substantively [i.e. materially] means the sum of all objects of experience. [The] Nature rests on forces (fundamental forces) and is, in general, the conformity to law of appearances.\nNature is opposed to chance — —\nto freedom — —\nto fate }\n}\n}\n.\nThings are not appearances in themselves, but only because there are beings that have senses; likewise they belong to a nature |\nbecause we have understanding. For the word nature signifies nothing in things in themselves either, but only the [unity] order of their appearances through the unity of the concepts of the understanding, or the unity of consciousness in which they can be combined.\nWe do not have understanding because there is a nature; for we could never know its rules (g laws) from experience; [sie best] their necessity consists precisely in this, that they are known *a priori*.\nFor this very reason we can have knowledge *a priori* of appearances as well as of the nature in which they are connected, because the form of our sensibility underlies the former, and the form of our understanding the latter, as the principle of possibility.\nTo say \"we can determine *a priori* the constitution of things\" and yet at the same time \"these things have such constitution independently of our faculty of determining it\" is a contradiction; for where, in that case, do we get our knowledge from?",[]],"reflm:5608":["Things are represented as appearances because there are beings that have senses. These same beings, however, also have understanding, under whose laws the appearances stand, in so far as their possible consciousness must necessarily accord with a universally valid consciousness; that is, they have a nature.\nAppearances stand to one another in the relation of the manifold [the] in a pure sensible intuition, and their consciousness stands in relation to a common apperception, both *a priori* and necessarily.\n1. Nature taken generally and *formaliter* (formally): nature of a thing; [the] corporeal and thinking nature.\n2. *Materialiter* (materially): as the sum-total of appearances, in contrast with the intelligible world.\nNature is opposed to blind chance (*Zufall*) and to blind necessity (fate, *Schiksal*).\nTo the latter belong miracles, if I do not assume a cause outside the world.\n(*non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, hiatus*: there is no abyss, no leap, no chance, no gap.)\nNatural necessity is opposed to the supernatural.\nNature is not opposed to freedom, but is distinguished from it.\nFreedom is the independence of causality from the conditions of space and time, and therefore [of] the causality of the thing as a thing in itself. Natural mechanism and freedom do not conflict with each other, because causality is not taken in one and the same sense.\n*Casus* is absolute contingency; *fatum* is unconditioned necessity in the world.\n(g All laws known through experience belong to heteronomy; those, however, through which experience in general is possible belong to autonomy.)\nM 143':\nThe infinity or the finitude of composition or division must in both cases (thesis and antithesis alike) be assumed in the sensible world. Yet these ideas of absolute totality belong to the concept of things in themselves; therefore both are false. With the concept of natural necessity and that of freedom, on the other hand, the first belongs to the sensible world and the second to the intelligible. The idea of totality belongs to the things themselves. With [natural necessity] the idea of freedom might conflict, if it were reckoned as belonging to the sensible world; but as belonging to the intelligible world both can be true.\nThe causality of a being, in respect of appearances, can without contradiction be thought as independent of the determining grounds of the sensible world, if [one] the being is given only under the concept of a thing in itself. Now a rational being is given as such, as intelligence; hence freedom can be thought in it. Of the intelligible [element] in bodies, on the other hand, no causality can be thought, for their appearances betray no intelligence; |\ntherefore no freedom can be thought of their *substratum intelligibile* (intelligible substrate) either, and we know it through no single predicate.\nWe need freedom only for the objectively practical, on grounds *a priori*.\nIt is the capacity to begin an event absolutely (without the causality itself beginning).\nIn bodies we do indeed have a representation of the intelligible, but we do not know it as cause. In intelligences the intelligible is presupposed in respect of causality; the question here is how something can be a cause.\nAntinomy.\n===NOTES===\n- *Beyderseits* rendered 'in both cases (thesis and antithesis alike)': my reading of the argument.\n- *welcher* ('welcher die Idee der Freyheit ... wiedersprechen mochte') has an uncertain antecedent; I take it to be *Naturnothwendigkeit* and supply '[natural necessity]'. Doubtful.\n- 'if [one] the being': the Academy's bracketed *man* is carried over as it stands; the construction is awkward in the German too.\n- The bracketed [the], [of] and [one] are the Academy's apparatus (*der*, *von*, *man*), translated in place.\n- Sentence in M 143' beginning 'Die Unendlichkeit' smoothed slightly; *Sinnlichkeit*/*Sinnenwelt* = sensible world; *Gegenstand* not at issue here.",[]],"reflm:5609":["*Maxima realitas phaenomenon* (the greatest reality of a phenomenon) is the most perfect world (*mundus perfectissimus*): the most real composite (*compositum realissimum*).\nThe principles of the possibility of experience hold only in the world, and do not extend to the world-whole. Hence the latter can be thought neither as finite nor as infinite.\n===NOTES===\n- *Phaenomenon* is taken as a genitive singular ('of a phenomenon'); the form *phaenomenon* is Kant's, and the reading could also be plural. Doubtful.\n- *Weltganze* rendered 'world-whole'.",[]],"reflm:5610":["Opposite and\nIn the world there is no abyss (*In mundo non datur abyssus*), i.e. there is nothing unconditioned in the sensible world, for this belongs to the intelligible; the senses, however, [verlie], when they take space and time as determinations of things [*Sachbestimungen*], go here into the infinitely empty or full.\nIn the world there is no leap (*In mundo non datur saltus*), i.e. all appearances are generated through all the degrees of things from 0 to something.\nWere time and space conditions of the existence of things in themselves, one could not say in the mathematical antinomy that both opposed propositions are false, for one would have to be true; nor [could both] |\nin the dynamical, that both can be true, for one would have to be false. Now the contradiction is encountered only because space and time, as things in themselves, are nevertheless supposed to contain something unconditioned.\n===NOTES===\n- 'Gegenüber und' is a broken-off fragment at the head of the note; translated literally as 'Opposite and'.\n- '[verlie]' is a truncated word in the Academy text (perhaps *verlieren* or *verlieffen*); left untranslated, as I do not fill the gap.\n- 'nor [could both]' / 'in the dynamical': the bracketed *beyde können* is carried over; the sentence is elliptical, and I have supplied 'one could not say' from the preceding clause only by parallel.\n- *Sachbestimungen* rendered 'determinations of things'.",[]],"reflm:5611":["Reason is not in the chain of appearances, and is free in respect of all of them, in respect of its own causality (the actions of reason itself are likewise not appearances; only its effects are). Were everything determined by reason, everything would be necessary, but also good. Were it determined by sensibility, nothing would be evil or good, and there would be nothing practical at all. Now actions are for the most part occasioned by sensibility, but not wholly determined by it; for reason must supply a complement of sufficiency. Reason gradually draws sensibility into *habitus* (habit), arouses incentives, and so forms a character. This character, however, is itself to be ascribed to freedom, and is not sufficiently grounded even in the appearances. Hence all actions cohere well with the laws of sensibility, but not with omissions of the understanding, the opposite of which is always possible according to all grounds of sensibility. If we now ask whether the understanding itself, in itself as well as in respect of what it does or omits, is not determined, we must say: no single possible experience can give this, since it would always still be only appearance. The understanding is itself no object of sensible intuition. Beyond experience, however, determinability by other causes does not hold. For beyond this boundary the understanding can be represented only as cause (supreme understanding), and the concept of cause serves only as principle of the synthesis of appearances, not of the actions of the understanding.\n===NOTES===\n- First sentence: the German repeats *in Ansehung ... in ansehung*; I have smoothed it into one construction.\n- Kant passes between *Vernunft* (reason) and *Verstand* (understanding) without marking it; both are rendered as written. 'Omissions of the understanding' may be meant of reason; flagged, not altered.\n- The last sentence is divided at 'For'; *Bestimbarkeit* = determinability.\n- *Complement der Zulänglichkeit* rendered 'complement of sufficiency'.",[]],"reflm:5612":["277. E II 1552.\nM 277':\nWe explain free actions already performed according to laws of man's nature, but we do not thereby know them as determined; otherwise we should not regard them as contingent, and demand that they ought and must have happened otherwise. In free actions reason enters in not merely as a comprehending principle, but as an effective and impelling one. How it does not merely reason [*vernünftle*] and judge, but takes the place of a natural cause, we do not understand; still less do we understand how it is itself determined by impulses to act or to omit. (How the representation of the good in general, which abstracts from my state, is yet effective on my state, and how this deliberation, which itself contains no affection, can be contained in the series of natural appearance. For the good is a relation of pure reason to an object. We must therefore regard future actions as undetermined by everything that belongs to *phaenomena* (appearances). Reason makes use of the constitution of nature, according to its laws, as incentives (honour, peace of mind), but is not thereby determined.\nThe solution of this is as follows. The connection of reason with the *phaenomena*, with which it is supposed [not at all] to stand in *commercio* (reciprocal relation), can not at all |\nM 277: |\nbe understood (g they are *heterogenea*). The true activity of reason and its effect belong to the *mundus intelligibilis* (intelligible world). Hence we also do not know in what measure we are to impute. Nevertheless we know this much of the influencing power of reason, that it is determined and necessitated by no *phaenomena*, but is free; and in imputation we judge the action merely according to rational laws. The actions here in the world are mere schemata of the intelligible; meanwhile these appearances (this word already signifies a schema) cohere none the less according to empirical laws, if one regards reason itself, according to its manifestations, as a *phaenomenon* (of the character). What the cause of this may be, however, we do not find in the *phaenomena*. In so far as one knows one's own character only from the *phaenomena*, one imputes these to oneself, although they are in themselves determined by |\nexternal causes. If one knew it in itself, then all good and evil, together with the good and harmful consequences, would be ascribable to no external causes but to the subject alone. In the intelligible world nothing happens or changes, and there the rule of causal connection falls away.\n===NOTES===\n- '277. E II 1552.' and 'M 277:' are Academy locus/reference lines, carried over as they stand.\n- The parenthesis opened at 'How the representation of the good' is never closed in the German; I have left it open rather than invent a closing.\n- 'The solution of this is as follows' restates *Die Auflösung hievon ist.* The sentence 'Gleichwohl wissen wir so viel ...' is smoothed; 'in imputation' (*bey der imputation*) moved to the end of the clause.\n- The bracketed [not at all] (*gar nicht*) is the Academy's insertion; the |, M 277: and | markers are kept where they stand, inside 'can not at all ... be understood'.\n- 'g' before 'they are *heterogenea*' is the Academy's siglum for a later addition, kept unchanged.\n- *Vernünftle* is Kant's pejorative verb ('to quibble by reasoning'); I give it in the German with the plain sense 'reason'. 'Dies wort bedeutet schon Schema' is translated as written; its sense is obscure.",[]],"reflm:5613":["Every action, as appearance, has its determining ground in another action of mine, positive or negative, this again in another, and so on to infinity. There is therefore no complete ground at all among the appearances, and so always only a necessity conditioned by my own pleasure (in applying or turning away this or that attention). But this condition, because it always remains [so], and because it is the condition of my own self-activity, is never an externally conditioned necessity. The same action, however, as noumenon [determined by my good or evil will], does not stand under the rule of that which (g in me) happens and must be represented as connected with another determining ground [den Zurei]. The exception is a good will; there the objective necessity (which is a freedom from physical causes) is at the same time a subjective one. In the case of an evil will, on the other hand, since it is nevertheless a will and not nature, all its actions are objectively impossible and subjectively contingent. For this contingency is the condition under which an objective law can be thought, in respect of which an action can be represented as evil. An [evil] action evil in itself, which one ought to omit, is evil precisely because we do it without an objectively sufficient ground; and the will is evil because it is subjectively not determined by that very same rule.\nFreedom is the determinability of the power by mere reason. Reason, however, is not a mode of knowledge that contains the manner in which we are affected by objects; consequently the use of reason is itself freedom.",[]],"reflm:5614":["As appearance, not only an action but also its |\nopposite could have its sufficient ground in the regress ([innerhalb] between two states), to infinity (by decomposition). But this series of grounds can never be complete, and cannot be composed a priori starting from the smallest elements of the ground.",[]],"reflm:5615":["Pleasure in the action either precedes the law or follows from it. In the latter case it is respect (*Achtung*).",[]],"reflm:5616":["279'. E II 1551. 1513.\nM 278':\nEverything that happens is sufficiently determined, but not from appearances; it is determined according to laws of appearance. For in beings that act freely there is a constant influence of intellectual grounds, while the opposite is possible as appearance. But the action or its opposite will be grounded among the appearances in such a way that only the moment of determination is intellectual. This, however, cannot be used in empirical explanation, because it is not perceived. For from the intellectual to the determinate action there lies an infinite intermediate series of incentives, whose connection with the given state can be known only according to general laws of possibility. For example: |\nM 279': |\nSomeone entices me to drink; this enticement seduces me [But before this], and can therefore be explained according to laws of the senses. The seduction would also be necessary if I were merely animal. Meanwhile it is possible that intellectual choice (*Willkühr*) intervenes, which is exempt from the law of dependence on the senses; this determines only a different course of sensibility. This course can also be [explained and] connected with the first given state according to laws of nature, but only through an infinite intermediate series of appearances. Thus vice as well as virtue happens according to laws of nature and must be explained accordingly. (Honour, health, reward.) Even the morally good actions [arise] from the above incentives, education and temperament. The explanation also has its ground; only the first direction |\nof these causes, the moment of determining them, [is unknown and] is not met with among the appearances, yet cannot be missed there either, because we cannot observe the appearances back to the moment of their beginning.\nThe law of cause and effect (*causalitatis*) rests on the condition of the possibility of a unity of experience. In free beings this unity cannot fully obtain, except where they are wholly intellectual.\nThe higher choice is the faculty of making use of incentives or sensible stimuli according to their laws, yet always in conformity with the representation of the understanding (in relation to the final and universal ends of sensibility). A posteriori, therefore, we shall have reason to find the ground of the action, namely its ground of explanation, but not of determination, in sensibility. A priori, however, when the action is represented as future (*antecedenter*), we shall feel ourselves undetermined with regard to it and capable of making a first beginning of the series of appearances. If there is free choice, the appearances of rational beings make no *continuum*, except where there are fixed principles of the understanding. Only actions governed partly by understanding, partly by the senses, can therefore be explained by no rule of the one or of the other. Before the action we place ourselves solely at the standpoint of the understanding. Since the understanding is not properly affected, but can affect sensibility, its action is not predetermined but determining spontaneously (*spontaneo*); and the opposite of what happens without understanding could always have happened. The action is therefore contingent only conditionally (under conditions of the understanding), in so far as it is unreasonable.",[]],"reflm:5617":["Among the appearances everything must be determined, but either according to [laws] pathological or moral laws. If the former, the opposite is nevertheless possible according to laws of reason, and man is therefore free; if the latter, the subject is also free. Agreement with laws of reason does not belong among appearances, and the transition from |\nsensibility to reason *in regressu* cannot be observed either. Rather, so far as anything is observed, everything happens by means of sensible incentives, through which the action is determined.",[]],"reflm:5618":["Pure freedom acts according to laws of inwardly determining grounds, but these do not fall within the senses. Animal choice proceeds according to laws determinable through the senses. The mixed human choice (*libertas hybrida*) also acts according to laws, but their grounds do not [vorkom] wholly occur in the appearance; hence, given the same appearances, the same man can act differently. Here one must first await a character, and then one has a law for explaining the appearances, but never for determining them.",[]],"reflm:5619":["The difficulty regarding freedom is: how a (g absolutely) first action is possible that is not determined by a preceding one. For the latter [determination] is required for the unity of appearances, in so far as they are to yield a rule of experience. But if we do not count the actions of reason among the appearances (principle of reason), and [reckon] the determination by it to action, by means of incentives, under laws of sensibility (association, habit), then everything is necessary *quoad sensum* and can be explained according to laws of appearance. It cannot, however, be predetermined, because reason is a *principium* that does not appear, and so is not given among the appearances. Hence the causes and their relation to action according to laws of sensibility can well be known a posteriori, but not their determination to the act. This connection of actions according to laws of appearance, without determinateness through it, is a necessary presupposition of practical rules of reason, which are in themselves the cause of a regularity of the appearances, because |\nthey pass over to actions only by means of sensibility. In the appearances there is no *hiatus* for the understanding, but neither can these be determined a priori, that is, from the absolutely first.\nAltogether the difficulty here concerns not the lack of a sufficient ground, but only the lack of one among the appearances. If the action is determined in the higher powers, in their omissions or perfections, then (g then) it is [no longer] a [matter of discussion] question of the ground of what happens, but [it shows] what is always there, namely the rational will, from which the opposite of the evil was always possible.\nReason determines itself with regard to its concepts; sensibility is determined by the object. Hence the former is not grounded on conditions of apprehension and apperception either, but determines the synthesis a priori.\nOne cannot say that the opposite of all our actions must be subjectively possible in order that one be free (good actions), but only that of those arising from sensibility. But even in this case they are determined under sensibility, though, taken generally, still undetermined. Sensibility and reason do not determine one another, but each works according to its own laws; yet they direct one another (harmony).\nThe causality of reason is freedom. The determining causality of sensibility: animality.",[]],"reflm:5620":["Just as reason judges not through the senses, yet in reference to them, in accordance with the universal conditions of knowledge in general, so likewise it judges not through feeling, yet in reference to it, in accordance with the conditions of the [possibility] universal validity of the judgment upon liking and disliking. It is appetition made universal, as the former is apprehension made universal. No feeling distinguishes right from wrong; rather, reason sketches the conditions under which alone a rule for judging about this obtains. The liking for regularity is proper|ly\na liking for the ground of constancy and security in respect of everything agreeable that is intended through the use of freedom. It is also a satisfaction in the use of reason and in the happiness wrested from blind caprice.",[]],"reflm:5621":["We must indeed (by virtue of time) transpose the soul into the world of sense, but we cannot transpose it into the corporeal world; we can only transpose the latter into its representation. Hence the soul as such has no place in the human body, and its place is the place of the body in the world. Hence there is no *sedes animae in communi sensorio* (seat of the soul in the common sensorium). No departure from the body. Virtual.",[]],"reflm:5622":["On existence after death. 1. *Spes vitae futurae* (hope of a future life). 2. *Immortalitas* (immortality): a. not universal, not necessarily eternal; b. /either from empirical or from rational grounds. The latter: 1. from the principle of the immateriality of that which lives, and of the body as a hindrance of life. For [then] it has lived before the human being. 2. From simplicity — 3. From the analogy of nature.\n*Status post mortem* (state after death).\n*Personalitas* (personality). Sleep of the soul. Existence as pure intelligence or as vehicle.",[]],"reflm:5623":["Anticritique is either sceptical or dogmatic. The first re|jects\nall [philosophical] extension of knowledge through pure concepts, and at the same time the investigation of this reason itself. The second maintains the former and rejects only the latter. The first is *misologie* (hatred of reason), the second *ignava ratio* (lazy reason).",[]],"reflm:5624":["On theology: every presupposition that can be proved neither a priori nor a posteriori, but is made only for the sake of our insight of reason, is a hypothesis if the propositions for whose sake the presupposition is made are contingent, e.g. contingent perfection in the world; but it is a postulate if they are necessary a priori, e.g. moral propositions and their moving force. Thus the presupposition of the existence of God for the sake of morals is a postulate. A hypothesis does not exclude that there may be other grounds of explanation as well, e.g. that a being other than the most perfect might be the cause of the perfection perceived by us. A postulate determines the ground as the only possible one. Likewise, if I found order and perfection everywhere in the essence of things, the existence of God would be a postulate, and the original being would have to be presupposed as the all-sufficient ground of the possibility of things in all teleological considerations. [The deleted \"Fol der\" stands in the text before \"Satze\".]",[]],"reflm:5625":["(s No empirical theology.) *Theologia est vel rationalis vel revelata* (theology is either rational or revealed). *Prior vel transscendentalis vel naturalis vel moralis* (the former is either transcendental or natural or moral). *Naturalis vel cosmotheologia vel Physicotheologia* (the natural is either cosmotheology or physicotheology).",[]],"reflm:5626":["*Entis originarii possibilitas originaria* (the original possibility of the original being). *Ens realissimum* (the most real being).",[]],"reflm:5627":["Of [different] things that fall under a certain universal concept we can think different ones without contradicting ourselves; but of God only a single one can be thought that does not contradict itself (*conceptus singularis*, not *communis*). All transcendental theology rests on determining this concept; but from the fact that all concepts except a single one (g [of]) cannot without contradiction belong to a Godhead, it cannot be concluded that the object of this concept is itself necessary.",[]],"reflm:5628":["The cosmological proof (g If anything exists, something must also be necessary) errs in this: according to the (g universal) concept of an *ens limitatum* (limited being) many various things can be thought, but according to that of a *realissimum* only one thing, so that the opposite of a given thing cannot be thought; and from this it is inferred that the former can be only contingent, the latter alone necessary. But a thing that is determined otherwise than a given one is indeed determined by opposed predicates, yet it is not the opposite of the former, (g precisely) because it is another thing. The opposite is the *determinatio opposita* (opposite determination) of the same thing.\n(g The consequence is false: limited things cannot be necessary, because the concept of a thing can be determined in them *ad oppositum* (to the opposite).)\nIt would read better if I said: all *limitata* have a *possibilitas logice derivativa* (logically derivative possibility), but the *ens necessarium* (necessary being) is to be *originarium* (original), and so it cannot be *limitatum*.\n(s On analogy, the use of empirical predicates *per reductionem et eminentiam* (by reduction and eminence). Anthropopathos Theosophia.)",[]],"reflm:5629":["Above, beside and \nUnbelieving is he who admits no acceptance of [truths] propositions of reason other than those that have logical certainty.\nPractically unbelieving is he who will not be upright except in so far as he knows for certain that it also has its reward (not from self-interest), and so not upon or in faith; or also he who ascribes to the moral disposition so much power as to abstract from all consequences and to content himself with merely acting rightly.\nThe former is the unbelief of distrust.\nThe latter is the unbelief of self-conceit.",[]],"reflm:5630":["Were we to have complete insight into the nature of things, nature and freedom, determination by nature and determination by purpose, would be wholly one and the same. So it is with God; hence all ends in the world at once follow from the essence of things and are, in an original being, one and the same with its nature.",[]],"reflm:5631":["On divine blessedness. Self-sufficiency.\nDifference from happiness, which rests on external things. Analogue of self-contentment: needing nothing external.\nWe have considered the attributes of God [as] [according to] physicotheologically. Now the existence of such a being is still to be proved according to physical principles.\nConclusion of physicotheology.\nThis consists (after the concept of God has been determined physiologically) in proving the existence of God from experience of the constitution of this world. Thus not the cosmological proof from the existence of some world, but from the constitution of this world. Here there are the following difficulties.\n1. [We] Only so much as we know of this world's perfection can we prove of God. Hence the concept of God is [likewise] wholly unstable and changeable. When the earth was regarded merely as the theatre of the Godhead, many gods had to arise, working [very much] against one another, and even the supreme Godhead had to |\nhave human failings. Now that we regard it only as an infinitely small part of the whole, we can at least ascribe to God more greatness [than] and goodness than shines forth in his work. — One must have infinite knowledge in order to recognise something as a work that proves an Infinite.\n2. [Even if we also the Un-] We can indeed, if we otherwise already know the existence of the *ens realissimum* (most real being), explain the imperfections in the world as mere semblance, but we cannot infer the highest perfection from this imperfect [world].\n3. Thus physicotheology is a ladder without its highest rungs; it presupposes a pure theology of reason.",[]],"reflm:5632":["M 394e:\nProvidence (*Vorsehung*, foresight [*Vorsorge*]): the establishing of laws; it concerns the arrangement of the world. (s [im Weltan] wisdom laid down in the beginning for the future.)\nGovernment (*gubernatio*): the preservation of the whole (g [of the] course of the world) in accordance with laws.\nGuidance (s dispensation) (*directio*): the determination of each part to agreement (g of individual events) with the laws. (s It is government in respect of the individual event.)\n(s Destining (*praedestinatio*, fate): is a predetermination of an event in which the free will has no share.)\nProvidence is either the general, according to general laws of nature, or the particular (specific), which is related to individual beings.\n*(s Particular providence is distinguished from particular direction. The former [nach d] works by means of the order of nature. The possibility (g of a particular care) according to an order of nature |\nis incomprehensible to us, because we know nature only as general conformity to law and not as a whole.)\nAll things (g and events) stand under a general and a particular providence; but not everything that happens happens through a particular providence, for which quite particular arrangements, directed to individual cases, would have to be sought**. For not everything that agrees with God's end is [itself] his end, and it does not always contain the motive of his arrangement. *Providentia legislatoria* or *generalis* is to be distinguished from *providentia decretoria* or *specialis*. The *actus* of a *providentia specialis* [are] belong not to ordinary but to extraordinary providence, and are distinguished from the *directio specialis* only in that the former are thought as already included in the creation. The divine decree is a single *actus* and extends to the whole, and all particular arrangements must be derived from the idea of it: it is not composed out of parts, for then an aggregate of ends, and no system, would result.\nM 394:\nThe *providentia specialis* is at all times a miracle (*miraculum praestabilitum*) and can be admitted only as such. What is peculiar to God's *providentia generalis* is that here the welfare of the individual is not sacrificed to the art [Kunst] in the whole. No individual event can occur that does not also belong to the best of the whole; all events happen as if they had been pre-established (*praestabilirt*) by a particular divine providence. The cause of this is that the very possibility of things rests on the dependence of their essences upon the highest wisdom, and that the parts in the whole are always determined immediately by wisdom; but according to our way of explaining we cannot express this in that manner, because the derivation from general laws of nature would thereby be hindered and the *ignava ratio* introduced.\nM 394e:\n**(s The (g wisdom in) supplementing of the lack of purpo|siveness\nin the freedom of the beings of the world through divine [wisdom] influence is the *concursus*.)",[]],"reflm:5633":["On the use of the knowledge of God permitted to human reason. 1. Not to pursue and investigate the concept of him with a speculative aim, but only in order [so wohl] to regard it as a necessary limiting concept of all natural philosophy and as the *non plus ultra* of human reason. Thus we are not to seek to investigate him in himself. Nor are we to build explanations of nature upon him as cause, and so set aside the maxims of the use of reason that alone is proper to us. 2. To regard him as a concept belonging to morality, though not in order to make morality possible, but in order to give it [pra] the force of an incentive. 3. By connecting him with morality, to use him for a religion, in which, however, it is morality and not theology that prescribes the rule, lest in thinking anthropomorphically we pervert even the moral principles themselves [unsern]. In respect of religion, to follow the [maxims] concepts of reason according to (g general) laws of nature, in that we regard these laws themselves as a divine institution. To derive divine punishments and rewards from the general and at the same time moral connection of nature and freedom. To regard the fortunes of this life as the progress of nature toward better ends. To regard the future life as an effect according to laws of nature, and future weal or woe as something that we naturally expect from ourselves and from our own conduct; in a word, to seek God in the order of nature and so to revere him. For what he is in himself we cannot grasp; and if we depart from the order of nature, we pervert our vocation [Bestimmung].",[]],"reflm:5634":["Knowledge of God is not to serve to alter our use of reason in accordance with the order of nature and of morals, but |\nto give it completeness, so that we may connect the former [the order of morals] with ends and the latter [the order of nature] with physical laws of the course of nature.",[]],"reflm:5635":["*Revelatio* is either symbolic or [intellectual] intuitive.\n[The] Revelation is to be considered either in respect of him to whom it has happened, or in respect of those who are to believe the report of it.\nRevelation can also have as its sole aim to set going a doctrine which needs no revelation to maintain itself once it is there, because it has its proof in universal human reason. In that case revelation is of merely local and temporal necessity.\nRevelation of such things as man does not recognise through reason as a duty, and hence not as a duty necessary in itself, is in itself contingent.\nAll (g merely) revealed theology (g which cannot be known through reason) and religion [wir] in time yields a merely learned religion. A merely natural religion also can have been set going through revelation; in this case it is indeed not necessary, but yet useful, that the learned [religion] should go hand in hand with it.",[]],"reflm:5636":["P. I:\nThe *quaestio facti* is in what way one first came into possession of a concept;\nthe *quaestio iuris*, by what right one possesses it and uses it.\nThe universality and necessity in the use of the pure concepts of the understanding betray their origin, and that it must be either wholly inadmissible and false or non-empirical.\nIn pure sensibility, pure imagination and pure apperception lies the ground of the possibility of all empirical knowledge *a priori* and of synthesis according to concepts, which has objective reality. For it extends only to appearances (which are in themselves contingent and without [Verb] unity), so that strictly one knows only oneself as the thinking subject, but everything else as in this one. Self-knowledge [*Heavtognosie*].\n––––––––––\nAll representations, whencesoever they may come, are in the end modifications of inner sense, and it is from this point of view that their unity must be regarded. To the |\nreceptivity of them corresponds a spontaneity of synthesis: either of apprehension, as sensations, or of reproduction, as imaginings, or of recognition, as concepts.\n(g Transcendental principles of mathematics (not mathematical principles), namely, that all intuitions and sensations are magnitudes, and that the mathematical propositions concerning magnitudes have reality, though only as holding of appearances.)\nP. II:\nNo appearance can ever prove an empty space or an empty time. Since appearances are in themselves nothing, that is, not objects subsisting for themselves, empty space is a perception of an extension without the matter of appearance.\nEvery magnitude has a quality, that is, continuity. Every quality has a magnitude, that is, intensity (degree). The boundaries of extensive magnitude are not at the same time limits of the intensive; the latter can, notwithstanding, grow to infinity. The [boundaries] limits of intensive magnitude, e.g. weight, are therefore not yet the boundaries of the extensive (or if the latter are equal, the former are also equal); rather the former can grow to infinity. Against atoms and the void.\n––––––––––\nSince the objects of our senses are not things in themselves but only appearances, that is, representations whose objective reality consists only in the constancy and unity of the connection of their manifold, it is not the objects that give the concepts; rather the concepts bring it about that we have in them objects of knowledge. Since, as representations, they are also modifications of inner sense, their possibility rests upon the synthesis of appearances in time.\n––––––––––\nIt is a very important question whether the categories are of merely empirical use or also of transcendental. On schematism.",[]],"reflm:5637":["P. I:\n(g Key — through the nature of synthetic judgments a priori.)\nWere no space given a priori in our subject as the form of its (g sensible) intuition, and were objects outside us given solely in this form, then no synthetic propositions that hold at the same time of actual outer objects would be possible a priori. For if we were to derive the representations of the objects from them as they are given in themselves, everything would rest merely on experience, and no synthesis a priori, together with necessity of judgments, would hold, at least not objectively. Were time not given subjectively, and so a priori, as the form of inner sense (g and no understanding to compare them), apperception would not know a priori the relation in the existence of the manifold; for in itself time is no object of perception. Apperception would also determine, indeed, the succession and coexistence of representations, but not the place of the objects in time, and so could not make any experience, unless it had rules of the [determined time] in the time determinable in the object; but these rules it cannot take from the object.\nI ask anyone whence he proposes to take the mathematical and necessary (g synthetic) propositions about things in space, if space were not already in us a priori the condition of the possibility of the em|pirical\nrepresentation of the objects, whereby these can be given to us. We determine a priori the manifold of appearance, as regards existence, through the categories. I ask: whence is this synthesis to be taken, if time, the condition of the possibility of all perception, did not lie a priori at the foundation, and if the rule of the determination of existence in this time, and through it of existences in relation to one another, did not flow, prior to all perception, from the subjective constitution of our [intuit] sensibility, upon which all that is objective rests?\n[Nevertheless limits] From this it follows, however, that our synthetic propositions a priori can hold only of appearances, not of things in themselves. [It follows] that the former are given only through the synthesis, and so far as it extends; that the concepts therefore do not extend, as concepts of them [taken] as things in themselves, beyond all possible synthesis. Hence arise the misunderstandings of advancing from the relative totality, which alone is found in all empirical synthesis, to the absolute.\nReason, which will not submit to this limitation, supposes that our experiences, and also our knowledge a priori, relate immediately to objects, and not primarily to the subjective conditions of sensibility and of apperception, and by means of these to unknown objects which are represented through those conditions alone. It therefore takes various paths. 1. The empirical path, and universality through induction. 2. The fanatical path, of intuition through the understanding. 3. That of predetermination through innate concepts. 4. The *qvalitas occulta* (occult quality) of healthy understanding, which gives no account whatever. |\nP. III: |\nIf these are admitted, they abolish all critique of pure reason and open a wide field to all fictions. Hence it belongs to the discipline of pure reason to examine them and, as the case may be, to block up such paths.\nP. IV:\nOn 66. [Against this cheerful One can] To be healthy, to remain equally active into late old age and useful to the world through insights, is a good fortune which no one should begrudge. If only the same worthy |\nmathematical man would abstain from all judgment about this (g his) sphere, or [could abstain. Perhaps one sees oneself once but one must in this and] (g with that *de gente hircosa centurionum*) say (g could say): *qvod sapio, satis est mihi*. But since these problems of human reason are essential and can never be dismissed, at least one person will have to fast over them to the point of illness, so that afterwards everyone may be healthy and yet [his the inquiring] reason be satisfied.\n––––––––––\nReason not only overlooks the ideality of the objects of the senses, but also bristles against it, as against everything that limits its sphere of action. Hence it is necessary to examine the paths it takes. The first is empiricism. But mathematical knowledge a priori refutes the falsity of this alleged origin of our modes of knowledge; and, besides, the concepts which occur in experience contain a necessity (cause) which experience could not teach. So [empiricism] falls away, after Locke, who earned almost too much honour by it, after Leibniz had already refuted it. There remain, then, epigenesis, mystical intuition, involution. Finally there comes the *qvalitas occulta* of common reason.\nThat our sensibility is nothing other than a confused intellectual representation is false. [This is an] incorrect concept of the sensible.\n––––––––––\nAll our knowledge is grounded on the subjective, in so far as it represents an object through the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition.\n––––––––––\nNo dogmata.\n––––––––––\n(g On the Canon: the aim of the whole of metaphysics is God and the future, and the aim of these is our conduct—not whether we should arrange it in accordance with morals, but whether it [sie] is without consequences.)\n[The Be-] Transcendental propositions are either those in which there is indeed an object but no concept, only the problem of it (g paralogism); or those in which there is indeed a concept but no object (reality of it). Both come from this, that reason extends further than understanding.\n(g We have no permission to appeal to God. Contingency of mathematics, and possibility of explaining all order from laws of nature. God. We also cannot prove synthetically the necessary generation through understanding.)\nDiscipline of the opponent. He too speaks more synthetically than he can assert.\nWhether there are pure hypotheses of reason. Permission for them.\nFinally, on the speculative interest of reason. Even if one has already realized those ideas of reason, one must nevertheless explain nature as if there were no beginning, nothing simple, no freedom, no absolute contingency, and as if there were no cause situated outside the world. For nature is our task, the text of our interpretations. Who knows what Epicurus thought of this, and what his gibberish explanations of nature are supposed to have been.\nP. II:\nIn all our thoughts there is not the slightest trace of (g intuition of) objects other than those of the senses, and no thoughts that went to anything other than the exposition of appearances. An intellectual intuition of objects outside us, which do not exist through us, also seems to be impossible.\n(g If one assumes intellectual intuitions, this gives no knowledge of the understanding through concepts, and so no thinking, and also no knowledge that can be communicated.)\n––––––––––\nSuppose we had everything a posteriori, through experience and immediate perception of the objects, and so space and time also; then we should know of them nothing but contingent truths. A priori we can know nothing of them synthetically, unless these intuitions are given to us a priori, consequently not through objects but through the subject; but [we know] the relation to the objects, because these are given as appearances through those subjective conditions.\nBlank space of 1 cm.\nReason now abhors principles which are not its own work. It is its maxim to assume everything as explicable. Consequently [there is] no sensible primitive intuition.\nQuite at the bottom of p. II:\nThe logical system of the modes of knowledge of the understanding is now either the empirical or the transcendental. The former [is that of] Aristotle and Locke; the second is either that of epigenesis or that of involution, acquired or innate. The so-called healthy understanding is *asylum ignorantiae*.\nP. III. At the very top, under Obiecte als (27421):\nThe modes of knowledge need not be originally inspired; we see this in the synthetic modes of knowledge a priori of mathematics.\n––––––––––\nThat one must assume no *mundus intelligibilis*.\nAbout 6 cm lower:\nA (g sinnen [sic]) world in general is the object of an intelligence. It would not be so, however, were it not the work of an intelligence. For without rules of order there is no nature. These [rules] are, however, contingent, and consequently an arrangement [Anordnung].\nAt the bottom of the page, under 27228:\nEvery explanation of something purposive according to universal laws of nature would otherwise have to distress [betrüben]. E.g. the flattening of the earth. It for|bids\nitself, no doubt, to explain the *organisatio* mechanically; but if it could be done, one could always aim at it. Who, then, makes the existence of a most perfect being comprehensible? Contingency, after all, grows with perfection.",[]],"reflm:5638":["P. I:\nOur concept of the world is, for the empirical synthesis, neither too large nor too small, provided that by it is understood only the totality of appearances [not restricted by any] restricted by no limiting condition, and hence a problematic concept, which serves merely as a rule.\nIf it is said that the conditions are [to a given] given along with the conditioned by the very fact that the conditioned is given, this means that the rule is given according to which [they] each of them can be found. The totality of the series, the collective All, is not thereby given. The synthesis (g of the *regressus*) is unrestricted (*indefinita*). This seems to amount to saying that the series is infinite (*infinita*). The *synthesis inversa* [of] *in consequentia* has a beginning. This seems to say that the series has a beginning. We have an outermost limit of the senses (relative), and also a first point in our use of reason; from these we start.",[]],"reflm:5639":["P. I:\nThe proposition that the concept of an absolute totality of the series of conditions must be either too large or too small means that no such concept is possible at all. For absolute time would have to be determined either by the synthesis's coinciding with a part [of] of it or with the whole of time. But we have a concept of the magnitude of time only by means of appearances. Our concepts of the world are transcendent, and by such a principle it is asserted that they can all be adequate to the ob|ject only if they are immanent, and only through that. But that there can be no totality in the (g empirical) synthesis of appearances means that [through] in respect of the empirical the synthesis is *indefinita*, but is not given as infinite, since it is given only through the synthesis, which is at every time finite.\nIt is a noteworthy rule or maxim of reason, belonging to its discipline, that no [proposition de] transcendental proposition of reason may be proved apagogically from concepts, since by such proof it is often shown only that our concept is faulty on both sides. For example: that there can be no absolute freedom of religion, and on the other side, that there must be an absolutely complete one. One has a faulty concept of religion or of freedom. [and through the] But [through] an antinomy of this kind [becomes a] nevertheless serves as a sceptical method for testing the correctness of our concepts and presuppositions. One shows the hindrances and contradictions on both sides, and is thereby restrained from judging dogmatically on one side or the other, and so is impelled merely to criticise one's judgment.\n[The unrestricted synthesis is not] The infinity of the synthesis in a series is not the infinity of the manifold of the members considered as given, for this [member] manifold is given only through the synthesis. It is, as in the *progressus*, merely potential.\n––––––––––\nSince the series of conditions cannot be given, whereas the concept can, one should rather say that the series is too large for the concept than that the concept is too small for the series; for the series is adapted to the concept, and not conversely.\nP. II:\nWhether, when I say that the world [must] is too large for our thoughts, this means just the same as that our thoughts are too small for the world. What is given is the world, and not the thoughts. Where, then, does the fault lie: in the world or in the thinking? The fault lies in the thinking, because we think further than what is empirically given; for a world is not empirically given, but everything given, and whatever we can think, belongs — in the world.\nIt should be said that the thought of the world must be neither too large nor too small for it, and hence must be exactly adequate to the world as the sum-total of all [given] appearances. The world, however, is a mere synthesis of appearances, in which the ground of the synthesis can always be determined only internally and not outside the appearances. The synthesis according to empirical laws, and as *indefinita*, is adequate to the world.\n––––––––––\nBecause the concept of appearances is given not prior to the synthesis but only through it, the synthesis is in itself indeterminate in respect of appearances, and consequently [infinite although] it proceeds to infinity, although the appearance is not on that account given as infinite. The synthesis is thus at every time finite, and the whole given world is finite, reckoned from the point *a priori*. On the other hand it is *potentialiter* [of the appearance] infinite in semblance, namely if one considers the synthesis as given through the object. In this way the thought or concept in accordance with which we are to think the world is for it neither too large nor too small, but is entirely adequate to this problematic concept, or to the problem contained in the concept, that is, to the possibility of all empirical knowledge in the field of appearances.\n(g In the senses there is no completed synthesis, and nothing complete and unconditioned.)\nThat the world must be neither too large nor too small for our thoughts means this: we must think it in such a way that its concept agrees with the conditions of the thoroughgoing empirical synthesis and with its rule. Or conversely: the concept of the world must itself arise from this. Now this [concept] is a synthesis proceeding without end from the conditioned to conditions, and a *progressio indefinita*, [through] in which time itself is determined through the synthesis of appearances, and so [neither] time neither determines the appearance nor limits itself in respect of appearances. For the world is then an idea, whose object is given only through this synthesis and its rule, but never as an absolute whole in itself, nor as all possible synthesis in a collective unity.",[]],"reflm:5640":["[Also: XXIII 483,12-485,6]\nA reason that was determined by sensibility would not be reason.\nOn innate and yet imputed wickedness.\nIn short, the key is this: freedom signifies the relation of an action as appearance [partly] (g on the one side) to causes in the appearance [partly] and on the other side to an intelligible faculty of the same, whereby they themselves are the cause of the appearances, and in respect of which the empirical conditions are not determining.",[]],"reflm:5641":["On the back of C. Spener's letter to Kant of 28 Apr. 1781 (X 248 [2 X 265]):\nQvant:\nQval. Relat:\nModal",[]],"reflm:5642":["P. I. At the upper margin:\nMy seeming idealism is the restriction of sensible intuition to mere experience, and the prevention of our straying with them [the intuitions] beyond the boundary of experience to things in themselves.* It is merely the prevention of the transcendental *vitium subreptionis* (fault of subreption), whereby one makes one's representations into things. I once entitled this doctrine transcendental idealism, because one has no name for it.\n*(g It would not have been worth the trouble, for the sake of the objects of experience, to lay down one's principles so high. Wherever one may get the know|ledge *a priori* from, the principles are confirmed through experience and, so far, reliable in use. Nevertheless it would be agreeable here also, for the sake of what is scientific. But where the use goes further than experience, where there is danger of fiction, where a powerful and plausible conflict is found, there it is necessary, etc. etc.)\nP. II:\nFor judging, concepts are required, and for the concepts [judgments] intuitions. The concepts, so far as they relate to intuitions *a priori*, cannot arise through the single empirical consciousness of the manifold; otherwise they would not be concepts of the connection of intuition. They are possible only through [the relation of the] the combination of the intuitions in one apperception, by means of the unity of their synthesis. And in this the concepts *a priori* consist.\n––––––––––\nDialectic. Up to this point we have had to do only with appearances, in whose exposition, principles and use there is nothing but truth, and there was no idealism. For truth consists merely in the thoroughgoing connection of representations in accordance with laws of the understanding. In this consists all the difference from a dream; not in the images' existing thus by themselves, apart from the mind. But now there arises, to begin with, an illusion, and indeed a natural and unavoidable illusion, in that our judgments say something of objects which is not contained in our concepts of them, i.e. in possible experience; and there our theory is the refutation of idealism.\nThe illusion consists, in the first place, in this: that in the field of experience, according to mere laws of experience, we represent to ourselves a *progressus* (advance) which is not an empirical *progressus* but a mere idea, which can be no experience. We remain in the world of sense [but] and are guided by nothing save the principles of the [law] understanding which we employ in ex|perience; but we make our possible *progressus* into an object in itself, in that we regard the possibility of experience as something actual in the object of experience.\nHere antinomy shows itself. All the ideas which constitute the ground of this dialectic are contained in it, the psychological as well as the theological, but only so far as they belong to the series of possible experience, which is to limit itself. Here the ideas are only to complete the *progressus*, and are cosmological. But there shows itself yet another illusion, where they do not belong to the series and are to add by themselves [such a one] something to experience; and there the ideas are partly psychological, partly theological. The hypothetical syllogisms of reason are the guide [Leitung]: to the first [the cosmological ideas], the hypothetical; to the last, the categorical and the disjunctive. The former [the categorical] lay at the foundation the subjective connection of all representations in a subject; the latter [the disjunctive] the objective connection in an idea. Of the first subject there is then no concept; of the second object there is only an ideal.\nIf we demand for truth something more than the thoroughgoing connection of intuitions according to laws of the understanding, wherein should we then place it, if this were not at the same time the representation of a determinate object? If it is besides to be in agreement with something else which does not lie in our representations, [s] how are we to compare it with that? All objects (g are determined only through the representations in me; what they may otherwise be in themselves is unknown to me) are at the same time in us; an object outside us is transcendent, i.e. wholly unknown to us and unusable as a criterion of truth. Dialectic.\nIdealism admits no beings other than thinking ones. That we never do; but only [this,] that we do not regard our representations as their properties. |\nContinuation at the upper margin: |\nIn the senses there is indeed neither truth nor error, since they do not judge at all, [sonde] hence all appearances are so far without possible error, and are no illusion.\nThe idealist concedes that extension and bodies outside us might indeed exist, but do not actually exist; hence they are merely a dream in us. |\nWe maintain that these are mere representations and can be only in us, but that their objects may nevertheless be outside us, though we know nothing of what they are in themselves.\nBetween 2807–14:\nSynthetic unity of apperception *a priori* is the synthesis of the manifold according to a rule *a priori*. The logical function is the act of uniting one and the same consciousness with many representations, i.e. of thinking a rule in general. The unity of intuition *a priori* is possible only through the combination of the manifold in one apperception, which must accordingly also take place *a priori*, and consequently also the unity of the synthesis of all empirical intuitions, because they are to be met with in space and time.\nWhat, then, would one demand that I should assert, in order that I might not be an idealist?\nIdealism is a metaphysical whim [merely in order], which goes further than is needed to rouse [us] to thinking. It fares with the philosophers as with the theologians. Syncretists. Semi-Pelagians.",[]],"reflm:5643":["P. I:\nIn all synthetic knowledge a priori there must be thought, first, intuition a priori, and secondly, a concept of the synthesis of the manifold a priori. On this are grounded the principles of synthetic knowledge in general. For these contain nothing but the conditions under which alone certain intuitions are brought under concepts of the synthesis of them. The latter are entitled categories.\n(g We can sense something without thinking; [wir] the question is whether we cannot also think without sensing. (a priori.) But we can also intuit without sensing. If, then, we are to think without sensing, this must relate to that intuition.)\nIntuition is the immediate relation of the faculty of representation to a single object. A concept is the representation of it through a mark |\nwhich it has in common with others. Intuition belongs to the senses, concept to the understanding.\nConcepts of the synthesis of the manifold of possible intuitions are nothing other than the combination which the representations have (g can have, so far as they are necessary in regard to an intuition) in one consciousness, but only thought synthetically, i.e. in such a way that to one [each] (g to the concept of the object) something other is added, which brings forth the consciousness of the representation of an object.\nIn the representation of an object, which is to contain the manifold of its intuition, the synthetic unity of the latter is necessary. The representation of this necessary unity, under which all the manifold of intuition must stand, is, if it is to become knowledge of an object of intuition, the principle of synthetic knowledge in general; and this principle must itself obtain a priori.\nP. II:\nSomething that is determined in regard to the functions of [magnitude] judgments is the object, and this determination is determination of the object, and so in the rest. The categories are therefore concepts for the determination of the objects of our knowledge in general, so far as intuition has been given for this. They are therefore principles for making experience out of appearance, [experience] being purely objective, i.e. universally valid empirical knowledge; the synthesis must therefore be determined a priori, since otherwise it would be neither necessary nor universal. For we know an object only as a something in general, of which the given intuitions are only predicates. How these can be predicates of a third cannot be known through comparing them, but through the way in which, in one consciousness, the consciousness of the manifold in general can be regarded as necessarily combined. [The concept] In the representation of an object the manifold is united. All intuitions are only representations; the object to which they are referred lies in the understanding.\nA synthesis can never be recog|nized\nas necessary, and so a priori, from the representations which are to be synthetically combined, but only from their relation to a third concept, in which [the connec] and in relation to which this combination is necessary. This third concept is that of an object in general, which is thought precisely through this synthetic necessary unity, and which is determined in regard to the logical functions of such unity. For only thereby does the manifold of representations first become objective, i.e. knowledge, and appearance empirical knowledge.\nThat something is objectively determined I cannot know a posteriori, without determining it objectively according to a rule a priori; for everything that is objectively determined must allow itself to be determined a priori from the concept of the object, not indeed as to matter, yet as to the form of connection. Through the very same representations through which the concept of an object is determined, a concept comes about which conversely determines those representations objectively.",[]],"reflm:5644":["19. P. I, II.\nP. I:\n(s Nature does not satisfy us, either in regard to efficient causes or to ends.)\n(s It is something different: science of pure reason, and: science of the principles of pure reason in general. The possibility of such a science is transcendental philosophy.)\n(s Metaphysics. Either [pura or] *universalis* — ontology, or *specialis* and *applicata*: 1. cosmology, which contains objects of experience —, 2. theology.\n*Metaphysica pura* (pure metaphysics). Ontology is the system of the pure principles a priori. Logic is a science of the principles of all reason, itself indeed pure, but it does not treat of the principles.)\nA science is not sufficiently determined by the fact that it contains the first principles of human knowledge, but [by this]: that this knowledge is of a particular kind, a priori. [Logic also] Knowledge from principles a priori is knowledge of pure reason, and is either pure mathematics or pure philosophy. Metaphysics: the system of pure |\nphilosophy (g of speculative or practical philosophy (of nature or of morals). All rational knowledge from concepts has its metaphysics.) Mathematics is rational knowledge through construction of concepts in intuition, just because it goes only to objects of the senses. Philosophy, rational knowledge from concepts, therefore also goes to things which are not objects of the senses. (g The very possibility of mathematics.)\nThings can be given only through the senses, and yet we can know much of them a priori. Things that are not given to the senses, if they are to be known a priori, (s that we become aware of them only on occasion of experience proves nothing) constitute *metaphysica pura*.\n(s Metaphysics proper has 2 parts: the first goes to things as objects of experience: psychology and general natural science; the second to objects as ideas: world and God, which nevertheless necessarily belong to reason. Thus 1. all elements and fundamental principles of pure thinking, with abstraction from all objects (g ontology) — 2. application to objects: a. of the senses, b. of pure reason.)\nThus metaphysics is either *pura* or *applicata* (applied).\n(g Philosophical is a rational knowledge from concepts,\nmetaphysical: pure rational knowledge from concepts.)\nBefore metaphysics there precedes transcendental philosophy, which, like logic, does not treat of objects, but of the possibility, the [extent] sum total and the limits of all knowledge of pure reason (also of pure mathematics). It is the logic of pure rational knowledge.\nBefore transcendental philosophy, critique of reason in general.\nP. II:\n(g So long as I merely analyse concepts which have use in experience, or set forth principles of them, it still goes; but if I depart from this and go further, there the difficulty begins. I shall first present everything critically, then [denn] propositions of my own.)\nThus, first, metaphysics itself is the object, and what investigates its possibility is critique.\n(s Ontology is the sum total of all concepts and principles of pure thinking.)\nSecondly, in (g proper) metaphysics, the system of all pure concepts a priori which we relate to objects*: transcendental philosophy** (s pure reason as a whole), ontology (s *conceptus dabiles*, concepts that can be given), cosmology and theology (s ideal). (s To explain hyperphysically. Hyperphysical. Metaphysical. Physical.)\n*(g If concepts are a priori, and hence not given through objects, they can only be taken from the understanding itself in relation to objects in general. 1. To things in general: ontology; 2. connection in a whole; 3. dependence [of this] on a ground.)\n**(g What logic is in regard to all knowledge, that is transcendental philosophy in regard to pure knowledge a priori.)\nThirdly, these principles applied to objects of outer and inner experience in general. *Physica rationalis*, *psychologia rationalis*.\nNot *psychologia empirica*.\n(g The 1st part occupies itself with things;\nthe 2nd part with ideas (in themselves).)\n(g All philosophical knowledge has its metaphysics, which determines the principles a priori.)\nWe have many modes of knowledge a priori which we need in regard to experience, e.g. everything has a cause. But in regard to these we need no metaphysics.\nThe objects of experience do not fully satisfy reason itself according to these principles. Hence reason goes beyond experience, and in regard to this metaphysics is necessary: God and another world. In metaphysics there is a part which merely analyses the use of reason in regard to experience. This is\n|\nvery useful for culture, but dispensable for science. Hence there is yet another part: synthetic, through which concepts are made transcendent.",[]],"reflm:5645":["36th sheet 1. 2.\nSheet 1, p. I:\nIn order to find out the need of our age with respect to the danger of getting happily through between the two rocks of dogmatism and scepticism, and [thereby] at the same time to determine both concepts in a manner suited to this need, we must first of all establish its character with respect to the mode of thought which makes that caution necessary.\nExtensive information and the possession of a great multitude of sciences do not yet constitute the character of the mode of thought [and because]; for this depends on the quality and specific constitution of the power of judgment (g and on the principles that determine it), that is, on what use one intends to make of that knowledge. Whether our age has advanced far in knowing [sey da] and whether its knowledge is to be called great, can be judged only comparatively; our descendants will perhaps find it still small. But a [skill] faculty may already now have reached its maturity, so that the later world has nothing further to add (since here what matters is not the quantity but the quality [of the manifold] in the use of our powers of knowledge), and this is the faculty of judgment (*iudicium discretiuum*).\nOur age is the age of critique, that is, of a (g sharp) appraisal of the foundation of all assertions. To this the |\nexperience of long ages has brought us, and perhaps also the cautious investigation of nature through observation and experiment, set going by the celebrated Bacon of Verulam. It has brought us to it not only in the assertions of natural science but, by analogy, in all the others as well. Of this the ancients knew nothing, and so they were accustomed to wavering opinions. In this respect a future age will hardly be able to surpass us, even though through negligence we often do not make [all] (g as we ought) use of these principles of critique. Certainly no past (g age) surpasses us in this, and this can therefore be called the (g scientific) character of our own.\nIn all science, if we abstract from the multitude of items of knowledge, [is] the essential aim is that it distinguish itself from mere opinion, and hence certainty. The method which is employed in it is merely the means of attaining this. Certainty is the unalterableness of the holding-for-true [Vorwarhalten]. But the holding-for-true is unalterable either objectively: when we recognise that no overbalancing ground to the contrary is in itself possible; or subjectively: when we are convinced that neither we nor any human being can ever get possession of greater grounds to the contrary.\nThe (g conscious) unalterable holding-for-true is knowing [Wissen]; the subjectively unalterable holding-for-true is believing [Glauben]. The [indeed] holding-for-true with the consciousness of its alterableness is opining [Meynen].\nExample from history. The word believing can signify either [in respect of] the source of our knowledge or [the kind and] the degree of the holding-for-true of it. In the former signification no knowledge of history can arise otherwise than through a trust in the testimony of others, that is, through our believing others. In the second signification a knowledge of history can quite well be a knowing, and |\np. II: |\nit need not, as regards the degree of the holding-for-true, be distinguished by the designation of believing from one's own experience, to which it does not dispute the name of knowing, [to be distinguished]. Thus one knows that a Louis XIV lived, just as surely as if one had seen him oneself, and so a good part of history is true science; the holding-for-true is therein objectively unalterable. It is impossible [that ever] to admit sufficient grounds to the contrary of the|same.\nOn the other hand, I hold the history of the first 7 kings of Rome to be true, though only on insufficient grounds, yet on such as are of the kind on which all history rests; and besides I am convinced that no one will ever prove the contrary, since all documents for it are lacking. That is, I believe it. But that perhaps even before the founding of Rome there was a cultivated people and a flourishing city on the same site, from which [the] perhaps the Cloaca Maxima still dates, is an opinion that can be listened to; |\nfor it is not impossible that a proof to the contrary might yet be found, if perhaps this underground work were cleared out and inscriptions by its builder (perhaps Servius Tullius) were found. I therefore do not recognise unalterableness in my holding-for-true.\nAll knowing is either empirical [or], that is, derived from experience, or rational: arising from reason, and hence possible a priori and self-subsistent. To the former belong one's own experience and history (that is, trustworthy report, hence a knowing, of the experience of others). The second certainty is independent of all experience.\nAll empirical certainty is bound up with the consciousness of the contingency of the truth; for experience teaches indeed that something is [be] constituted thus or otherwise, or has happened so, but never that it could not possibly be otherwise constituted or have happened otherwise.\nCertainty of reason is inseparably bound up with the consciousness of the necessity of what is held for true. All knowledge which, [with the] according to its essential constitution, is bound up with the consciousness [of its] of its necessity, is apodictic. Therefore [is] must [all] every proposition of reason be apodictically certain, and in assertions of reason (*assertionibus*) neither opining nor believing finds a place.\nAll apodictic propositions, however, are of two kinds: they are known either from mere concepts or (g only) through the construction of concepts. The former are entitled *dogmata*, the second *mathemata*. If it [could be proved], as Leibniz held, could be proved from the mere concept of a body, as a composite substance, that it consists of simple parts, this would be a *dogma*; but if the divisibility of a space *in infinitum* is proved through the geometrical exhibition of the space which a body occupies and the equally geometrical division of this space, then this proposition is a *mathema*. Philosophy alone |\np. III: |\ncan therefore con|tain *dogmata*,\nsince it is precisely in this that it differs from mathematics, which, just (g as) like philosophy, is [a] knowledge of reason: philosophy judges merely from concepts, whereas mathematics judges only through the construction of concepts.\nPhilosophy is thus capable of apodictic certainty, but not of the intuitive certainty [but] by means of intuition a priori, such as mathematics can procure, but only of the discursive certainty from concepts. Hence the former contains indeed principles, the latter alone axioms; the former proofs (*probationes*), the latter alone demonstrations; the former [furnishes] procures conviction, the latter at the same time self-evidence (*evidentiam*).\nMarginal addition: |\n(g Critique. The art of distinguishing opining from knowing.)\n[All knowledge, even if it were not knowledge of reason at all but only historical, still requires certain principles which contain the criteria of truth. Thus there are certain principles by which one judges the probability of a history. These are not merely such as [flow] from the nature of the things narrated and are therefore taken from reason, but also those which determine the way in which the experience of ancient times can be conveyed to us (g with trustworthiness).]\nHistorical certainty can be [set against] set beside certainty of reason, by which one understands the empirical certainty that rests not on our own experience (and hence on reports of the experience of others). In the same way there is also a belief of reason, which is analogous to historical belief, although it rests on other grounds. In certainty (g of) both kinds objective unalterableness of the judgment is thought; in belief of both kinds only subjective unalterableness. That [it in] the planets contain rational inhabitants can be believed according to reason; for the grounds of proof for it, as many as one can reasonably expect given our distance from them, [make] furnish (g so) great an analogy between them and the earth as is necessary for this inference; and one is moreover certain that no one will ever know anything more about them with which to prove the contrary.\nBelief of reason is entitled moral certainty when it [according to] is sufficient for the |\nobligation to lay it at the basis of one's actions. For since the moral laws, which contain the grounds of obligation, (g also rest on reason and so) are objectively unalterable, a certainty [reason-belief reason] can to that extent take place. This cannot, however, be entitled certainty of reason (*certitudo logica*), because it does not follow [merely] from theoretical [principles of reason but as] principles, but must be assumed as necessary merely for the sake of practical maxims, and [may], because these maxims rest on moral laws, which have objective unalterableness, one may not entitle such a belief of reason moral belief, but one can entitle it moral certainty. Thus the propositions: There is a God, there is a future life, would yield for speculative knowledge only a belief of reason, and yet would [as] count as morally certain. For only through them are all [well-founded hindrances] (g objections to) the obligation under moral laws removed (namely those taken from the consideration that observance or transgression of the latter, by the mere course of nature, could have no effect proportioned to them) [these however objective certainty]. So their acceptance [just s], on account of this their necessity [and objective unalterableness], is likewise [an unalterable] practically necessary, and it is impossible that there could be grounds to the contrary, since otherwise grounds to the contrary of morality could also find a place, and morality itself would then be doubtful and uncertain.\np. IV:\nBy *dogmata* one understands apodictic propositions from concepts. Hence mathematics contains indeed apodictic propositions, but no *dogmata*, since they are possible not from concepts but only through the construction of concepts. The propositions that between two points only one straight line is possible, or that in every triangle the sum of all the angles is equal to two right angles, of which the first is an axiom, the second a demonstrated theorem, are *mathemata*, that is, modes of knowledge [which] whose (g insight and) certainty is intuitive (g yet at the same time apodictic). But that [everything\n|\n(g which) exists contingently is a proposition must have a cause is indeed also an apodictic but only discursive proposition from concepts hence] every statement must be truthful, and that lying is reprehensible, is an apodictic but discursive proposition from concepts and can be entitled a *dogma*.\nAll apodictic certainty is [therefore] contained only in two [sciences] sciences which comprise knowledge of reason a priori: in mathematics or philosophy. *Dogmata* can be met with only in the latter. Philosophy, however, is either theoretical, where reason has to do merely with itself, or practical, where it has to do with the laws of the will. The former part, so far as it contains apodictic, that is, pure, modes of knowledge of reason, is entitled metaphysics; the second, which contains practical laws of the same kind, pure morals.\n––––––––––\nSummary concept.\n[Dogmatism is the mode of thought]\nBetween dogmatism and scepticism the middle and sole lawful mode of thought is criticism.\nThis is the maxim never to accept anything as true otherwise than after complete examination of the principles.\nAll principles are (g on the one hand) a posteriori, that is, taken empirically, and these (g again either) one's own experience or testimonies (g of) the experience of others, hence experience (*in sensu stricto*) or history.\nAll principles are on the other hand a priori and taken from reason, and these (g either) from reason so far as it judges merely according to concepts, hence philosophical principles, or so far as it judges merely in accordance with the construction of concepts, that is, their exhibition a priori in intuition.\nOne's own experience and mathematics [not] do not need this at all, and the former only seldom needs a critique, lest semblance be taken for perception. But all the more does history of remote ages, among the empirical, and speculative philosophy, among the modes of knowledge of reason, need critique, that is, examination of principles.\np. V:\nDogmatism is the mode of thought of adhering to assertions without [examin] critique (that is, examination |\nof the principles). The most natural propensity of man in respect of modes of knowledge is towards dogmatism: 1. because of laziness, since going back to principles is harder than proceeding to the application of principles once accepted and put into circulation; 2. because through critique knowledge is not extended but only secured; 3. from fear of uncovering the poverty of our knowing to our own eyes and those of others.\nScepticism is a chosen principle for doing damage to dogmatism, yet not [by means of crit] with the intention of introducing in its place [truth] true conviction, but only of overthrowing the persuasion of others. The inclination to it is not natural but artificial, and can arise only from displeasure at the usurpation of dogmatism. The occasion for it is given to it [in historical] (g firstly in) empirical knowledge by the polymathy of all, chiefly of ancient history and the system-building of it; secondly in knowledge of reason by [meta] speculative philosophy, namely metaphysics and general morals with their [questions] assertions concerning the highest good. It is therefore indeed a malice, in that it seeks to do nothing but harm, namely to rob human reason of all hope in the most important questions of reason; but it is not unprovoked malice, for dogmatism, as (g an assent) usurping self-conceit, cannot at least complain of the resistance of the misologist as of a wrong. Yet it is (g to say the least) folly; for it transforms all holding-for-true into semblance, which it opposes to truth, must therefore nevertheless concede criteria of the difference between them, and yet it denies these altogether.\nDanger is the possibility of a greater evil. There is therefore danger in dogmatism as well as in scepticism: in the former, of the gathering up of a heap of errors among a small number of truths, and of contempt for the latter themselves on account of their kinship with the former; in the latter, of the denial of our duty to make use of our reason everywhere, and of a laziness of the former glossed over by a specious pretext.\nThis danger can be removed only by the greatest critical diligence: on the empirical side, by tracing the sources (g of history) and the derivation |\nof these to us; and on the side of reason, by investigating the nature and capacity of human reason, as well in its speculative use in metaphysics as in its practical use in morals, and by establishing convincingly its limits, likewise its extent and the necessary principles of the latter.\n===NOTES===\n- Apparatus (square brackets, (g ...), |, line breaks) is carried over in place. Bracketed words are Kant's deleted or replaced readings, translated; many are fragments (e.g. [se], [son], [ode]) and are rendered as 'be', 'but', 'or'.\n- Vorwarhalten (= Fürwahrhalten) is rendered 'holding-for-true' throughout. Wissen = 'knowing', Glauben = 'believing/belief', Meynen = 'opining', with the German given at first use. Erkenntnis(se) = 'knowledge' / 'modes of knowledge'. Gewißheit = 'certainty'. Augenscheinlichkeit is rendered 'self-evidence' alongside *evidentia*.\n- Doubtful reading: 'nicht ungewitzte Bosheit' (p. V). 'ungewitzte' is unclear and may be a slip for 'ungewitzigte'. I rendered it 'not unprovoked' from the sense of the following clause.\n- The antecedent of 'der ersteren' in 'Faulheit der ersteren' (last paragraph but one) is ambiguous. I kept 'of the former', which may mean reason or dogmatism. It could also be a defective construction.\n- Lines 'Es ist unmoglich, [daß jemals] ... des|selben' and the unfinished sentence on moral certainty ('und [darf], weil ... so darf man ...') are irregular in the German. I smoothed the English only slightly and kept the deletion brackets. The bracketed passage 'alles ... mithin' on p. IV is a garbled deleted sentence, translated literally.\n- 'Sätze ... in einem jeden Triangel ... zweyen rechten gleich seyn' has an infinitive in the German; I rendered it as 'is equal'. 'Philosophie ... enthalten' is split by | across 'ent|halten' and I kept the break as 'con|tain'. 'iudicium discretiuum' is kept with Kant's spelling.\n- 'Zuverläßig' is rendered 'trustworthy'. 'Vielwisserey' is rendered 'polymathy'. 'Denkungsart' is rendered 'mode of thought'. 'Urtheilskraft' is rendered 'power of judgment'. 'Criticism' stands for Kant's Criticism as a mode of thought, and 'critique' for Critik as examination.\n- Locus headings ('36. Bogen', 'S. I' etc.) are rendered 'sheet' and 'p.'. Long sentences were mostly left whole, with minor splits where Kant's syntax stacks clauses (first paragraph on history; the Bacon sentence).",[]],"reflm:5646":["p. I:\nThe I.\nJudging by the appearances (*Erscheinungen*) of inner (g as well as outer) sense, we can never regard ourselves as the identical self, not even in respect of the sensible character. Only in regard to morality, which [Int] is the pure consciousness of ourselves independent of determination in space and time, is the subject of free actions always the same under the same laws [as long as] in everything of which we are conscious. There the whole of our actions is regarded as a unity. And we cannot believe, merely because we have once amended ourselves, that we therefore already have a different personality and could not be punished on account of the former, as [almost] almost all men believe. Of course this cannot be done by men (s as judges). In the same way one reproaches oneself for the evil of one's childhood years (Rousseau: the ribbon story), or also for what we have done in drink. Only, the amendment is after all an experience that the character in us is not so wholly evil.",[]],"reflm:5647":["p. IV:\nReason has no other than an analytic use. The synthetic use, by which it makes principles, is dialectical. Hence the |\nprinciples of the understanding are either immanent or transcendent. The objective principles of reason are dialectical, the subjective dogmatic.\nThe principles are either anticipating or ...., the latter are dialectical. The anticipating principles are either of transcendental apprehension or of association, both of recognition; this breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5648":["Remark of Kant on the empty back of the letter from G. Hufeland of 11 October 1785 (X 388f. [2 X 412]) in vol. I of the Dorpat collection of letters to Kant, p. 206:\nOne must have thoughts to give up.\nOne cannot get away from modes of knowledge that concern the first beginning.\nThe decrying of hypotheses.",[]],"reflm:5649":["p. I:\nTiedemann.\nMetaphysics can take three paths: 1. That universal modes of knowledge (g of nature) are possible solely through experience (their concepts also). 2. That universal modes of knowledge are possible solely from concepts a priori and through reason. 3. That [Erfa] universal modes of knowledge do indeed take place a priori and prior to all experience, but only in so far as they are grounds of the possibility of experience. The first path is empirical, |\nthe second dogmatic, the third critical, because the critique (g and resolution) of experiences alone can point it out. For the last path it is required that the (g empirical) intuition be grounded in [in de] pure sensible intuition a priori, and the empirical concept in pure concepts a priori, which relate solely to the unity (g of consciousness) of these intuitions.\nThat, however, besides sensibility and understanding (both a priori) the faculty of reason also contains principles of the use of both, namely to restrict the concepts of the understanding to experience, and those of sensibility likewise to it, so that their conditions may not be extended to beings in themselves, God and spirit. At the same time, therefore, to give reason freedom to think something beyond experience, which is indeed necessary for the completion of our use of the understanding, but can never be thought through theoretical concepts otherwise than negatively, and through moral concepts alone positively, and which contains the totality of conditions for everything.\np. II:\nHow greatly one wishes, when one would raise oneself to supersensible objects, to be rid of the limiting concepts of space and time; and likewise, when one would arrive at an absolute whole of all conditions of the understanding, to be [rid] of the conditions ever persisting in the understanding, which make totality impossible although they belong to every experience; in a word, to be rid of that which properly belongs only to the possibility of experience and goes no further.\nThe pure concepts of reason, God (g freedom) and another world, are properly of moral origin.\n––––––––––\nWith space and time one can take only two ways: 1. that they are [mere] concepts; 2. that they are mere intuitions. In the first case they are a. empirical concepts or b. concepts a priori. In the second case they are [ans] 1. intuitions of things in themselves through observation, and yet neces|sary; 2. [anschau] formal intuition a priori, i.e. consciousness of the way in which [we] objects are represented to us to the senses.",[]],"reflm:5650":["P. I:\n(g If the concept of space and time were not borrowed from the form of our sensibility, the synthetic propositions about them would have to have been taken from the things themselves. Space and time would then have objective reality in themselves, either as substances or as accidents or as relations. [These] two, however, precede the things, and so they would have to be mere concepts; but synthetic propositions cannot be derived from these.)\nMetaphysics.\nSubstance is the ultimate subject of reality. Its relation to the [dieser: this] existence of the latter is entitled force, and this alone is what designates the existence of the substance and in what its existence itself consists. Now since every force has a degree, there can be, for much smaller degrees of the same kind, many subjects, and a whole can be composed of them if they stand in external community. Or they can also [in] be so combined that they constitute only one force, in which no external community of different subjects is to be found. That is to say, unity of the subject [bey: with] a great degree of force [viel [Graden]: many [degrees]] is just as much as plurality of subjects of smaller degrees; indeed |\none can be transformed into the other. For if a drop of water became a drop of quicksilver (as regards weight), the number of parts, and so of subjects, would not be increased, and yet the same result could arise if 14 drops of water were swallowed up into one. The simple subject is therefore not indivisible on that account; for it is divisible, not as composed of many subjects, but as [a single] (g unity of the) subject which, having a great degree of force, is changeable into many subjects of lesser degree. 'The soul is simple' means: it does not consist of many subjects in space. Nor can it so consist, for we know it through no forces that can appear in space. But from this it does not follow that the absolute unity of the subject could not be transformed into a plurality of subjects without change in the degree of reality in the whole.\nThe constitution of something as an absolute subject, which does not inhere in others, signifies a force [deren Größe: whose magnitude] which does not consist of a plurality of forces mutually determining one another, but [Einheit und: unity and] consists in degree.\nThat a being can exist as a unity of substance and yet be resolved into a plurality of substances involves no contradiction. For it is not necessary [necessity] that a plurality of subjects must already have been there beforehand, and consequently that the substance must be thought, even before the resolution, as composite, that is, as containing, as parts, that plurality of subjects which becomes possible in it through the resolution. For if this resolution is merely the effect of setting apart, one outside another, what previously inhered in one, then inherence is transformed into subsistence, and the accident into an absolute subject. This always happens in the resolution of a degree. Here we never have, of the subject, the concept of plurality, since it is considered inwardly and not in its [in] external relation (extensively) according to magnitude. Nevertheless intensive magnitude can always be regarded as a potential plurality of subjects, which can be separated. For instead of all the forces ceasing as regards degree (whereby the |\nwhole subject would also cease), these degrees [all] (g of all forces), and with them also the concept of a subject in which they inhere (for this is not at all distinguished in the one from the other), can exist separated from one another. The resisting force of a body could diminish, in that degrees of it were entirely lost, or also in that what was taken from it (g without any part, outside another, being removed, but each remaining, though with diminished degree) existed as a particular subject.\nP. II:\nThat we could infer from immediate perception, and indeed with validity for all thinking beings, that they must be simple [nature] beings would be impossible; for perception, unless a contradiction were met with in the opposite [könnte: could be], can furnish no necessity. This would hold unless the thinking subject were here considered merely as object of its own inner sense. Only the subject of all one's own thoughts can be its object; and since it must be distinguished from all objects and their manifold, it can be represented through nothing but the unity of apperception. For that very reason, however, it represents nothing at all but a mere something, without further predicates (as subject). [Wenn] Thinking, therefore, can take place only in the unity of apperception. Outside myself I can perceive no thought, and consequently no thinking subject as such. A thinking being can therefore [aus: out of] represent its thoughts as having arisen through the united representation of different thinking subjects (g external to one another).\nOn existence in subjects in general.\nWe can think only through judgments, because we have concepts only through the (g necessary) unity of consciousness of the manifold of representations. In judging, everything that we think of the object is predicate, and the object that we think is, in a further respect, |\nitself a predicate. In what exists we must nevertheless think subjects in themselves, which are not predicates; but our concept of their reality is none other than that of reality and its inherence, hence of force, and of force of certain degrees. The subject, however, remains merely a something. Every degree, moreover, can be represented as composed of smaller ones, and each as belonging to a subject, in which case it is a *compositum*; or all as belonging to one subject, in which case it is *simplex*. Whether, then, what has existed as unity of the subject, that is, a great degree of force, can exist as plurality of subjects, and what is required for something that existed (g as) unity of the subject to exist as plurality, can be neither explained nor refuted.\nOn metaphysics.\nI have proved that human reason in its speculative use [nicht weiter kommen: cannot get further] can extend to no objects other than objects of a possible experience, and of these also to nothing more than can be given in some possible experience. Consequently, since metaphysics places its importance not in those modes of knowledge [Erkentnissen] which are found [durch: by] [an: on] the path of experience, or can at least be confirmed by it, but rather in what goes beyond the limit of all possible experience, all its dogmatic utility [völlig: entirely] lapses; indeed its very existence (g as useless) would have to lapse, were it not that the modes of knowledge which we actually have a priori, and without the aid of experience, led us to believe that their use, since they are independent of experience, might well reach beyond experience; and that from this important modes of knowledge might again arise through false but deceptive judgments, [Einwü] attacks and difficulties. The question now was how modes of knowledge a priori, and so without being derived from experience [gebor], could nevertheless relate everywhere to objects of experience, and indeed to nothing else. I accomplished this by showing intuitions a priori and also concepts a priori, of which the former presented nothing but the form of appearances, the latter the form of the concepts of things in general which appear; and the use of these, although they are representations a priori, |\n(g extends itself) solely to experience. [Daß] Here everything that has to be accomplished was comprised in one problem: How are synthetic modes of knowledge a priori possible?",[]],"reflm:5651":["S. II:\nA root is a quotient that is at the same time the divisor of a given magnitude. In every division two magnitudes must be given, the *dividendus* (dividend) and the *divisor*. The quotient, i.e. the number, is to be found which comes to be from unity just as the dividend comes to be from the divisor. Thus the relation of the dividend to the divisor is given, and (g now) the relation of the divisor to unity is to be found, which (g is equal to the former and which) the quotient expresses. But in the extraction of a root only the dividend is given* as (g the) second power of an unknown magnitude x, which is to be found, namely a:x = x:1, so here –9:x = x:1, so that x² = –9. Now x may be +x or –x, yet x² is always =9, [and] never =–9.\n1:3 = –3:–9 | √–9 = (√9) × (–√9) [not (√9) × √–9] = 3 × -3.\n*(g The divisor, which is at the same time the quotient, is to be found, thus the mean geometrical proportional number between the given one and unity. Unity must here always be positive, otherwise –9 cannot [with it only] the product breaks off.)\n[The mag] The [un]conditioned connection (g in an unconditioned totality) is expressed by the sign –, the breaks off.\nIf for a *substantia phaenomenon* (substance as appearance) the substantial, for a *causa phaenomenon* (cause as appearance) the (g sensibly) unconditioned cause, and for a *commercio* (community) the unconditioned unity of different substances in their reciprocal|action is to be found, or the ground of the same, then this unconditioned is -a. But this multiplied by a gives [du] in the whole –, i.e. the whole lies outside sensibility, and for a magnitude of this kind no root can be found. [but] Yet the root can be found as category, designated only with + and – at the same time. In the former case it is a *phaenomenon* (g but not whole), in the second a [phaen] *noumenon*, which would indeed be positive but cannot be given. Hence it signifies only the root with the sign –, in so far as it, multiplied by itself without sign (as category), [ab]: signifies the concept [ein abso] of a whole with the sign –, of an absolute whole, which is yet impossible. The regulative principle lies here in the removal of all limits from the sensible, i.e. in the progress to infinity and the assumption of a supersensible √–a, which however cannot be given.\nThe categories of quantity come close to addition, those of quality to subtraction, those of relation to multiplication, those of modality to division; and if all these are to be regarded as sensibly unconditioned compositions, the last are impossible roots.",[]],"reflm:5652":["S. I:\nThe similarity of the categories to the *species Arithmetices* (the species of arithmetic).\n1. Those of magnitude are known through addition; 2. those of quality through subtraction of the parts from the whole (which is given); 3. those of causality through multiplication, for here a state of the world arises from a preceding one, just as this arises from the one before it. They are really generated in this way, and in the series of causes there is true multiplication of appearances. 4. Those of modality through division, where the cause is to be found |\nwhich arises from the first in the same way as the given effects each arise from their causes.\nIn all this it is to be noted that the categories are here applied only to appearances. Hence, because quality in the appearance is the object of sensation (the empirical in space), it is given as unity, and only divisibility can be thought in it. — On empty space and time in connection with quality, and the proof that it cannot be experienced but also is never proved; and since its opposite also cannot be proved, one is compelled always to assume degrees of filling as small as is needed for the explanation of appearances.\nThe ideal of the universal original being is to be compared with the irrational magnitude. For to seek the square root means to seek, for a given number, that number from which the given one comes to be just as that unknown one comes to be from unity. Thus it means to seek that cause which is necessary through itself. It is an unnameable root, for [it] it cannot come to be from any alleged unity [in itself] as the world comes to be from it. That is, the necessity of the effect from a cause can be stated completely, but the necessity of the cause that is not an effect requires a generating concept, which is not to be met with in our whole reason, but which is nevertheless not self-contradictory.\n––––––––––\nIn  intuition is magnitude, A the reality, –A the *remotio* (removal) of it. A–A is the objective negation =0. [A] d × 0 (*h.e. determinatio, qvae semet ipsam tollit*, i.e. a determination which cancels itself) *est impossibile* (is impossible). *Substantia determinat temporis quantitatem* (substance determines the quantity of time) [*Mutatio et*], *Causalitas successionem et positum in tempore subordinatorum* (causality determines succession and the position in time of things subordinated), *commercium simultaneitatem* (community, simultaneity). *Substantia est* =ant, *accidens* at. *Causa* adt, *causatum* dta.\n––––––––––\nThe general refutation of the empirical origin of time and space is that both yield synthetic propositions a priori, and from this there follows also their ideality as intuitions.",[]],"reflm:5653":["P. I:\nAgainst (g material) idealism.\nIt is grounded on this: that we are immediately conscious of our own existence, but of outer things only through an inference. The inference runs from the immediate consciousness of mere representations of [Etwas: something] things outside us to the existence of those things. But this inference is not evident in its conclusion, as the property of our imagination known [uns: to us] proves; for imagination is a faculty of representing objects to ourselves intuitively even without their presence.\nAgainst this argument it is sufficient to point out only the following. The transcendental consciousness of ourselves, which accompanies the spontaneity of all our acts of understanding, but which consists in the mere 'I' without determination of my existence in time, is indeed immediate. The empirical consciousness of myself, however, [d.i. d: that is, th] which constitutes inner sense (as the former constitutes the form of the intellectuality of my subject), is by no means immediate. Further, the consciousness of other things outside me must be simultaneous with the determination of my existence in time, and so must the determination of their existence in space. (This consciousness must likewise be presupposed as intellectual, and so far is not a representation of them in space, but can be entitled intellectual intuition, through which we have no knowledge of things.) Hence I am not more conscious of my own empirically determined existence than of that of the things outside me (which I do not know as they are in themselves).\nFor it is in space alone that we posit the permanent; in time there is unceasing change. Now the determination of the existence of a thing in time, that is, in such a change, is impossible unless the permanent is also combined with its intuition. This permanent must therefore be intuited as [given] outside us, as object of outer sense. But since [in so far as I] am at the same time determining my existence, and so far am not empirically conscious of myself, I can also so far not be empirically conscious of the permanent outside me, that is, as given in space. I can be conscious only of my determination of the representation of it, in so far as I am merely affected by them in accordance with the form of space, [welcher: which] in that I draw this [space], and thereby at the same time become conscious of my own existence in time.\nThe intuition of a thing as outside me presupposes the consciousness of a determinability of my subject in which I am not myself determining. This determinability therefore does not belong to spontaneity, since the determining factor is not in me. And in fact I cannot think any space as in me*. Hence the possibility of representing [einen: a] things in space [als: as] in intuition is founded [blos: merely] upon the consciousness of a determination by other things. This signifies nothing further than an original passivity of myself, in which I am not at all active. That the dream produces [eben dergleichen: just such] an illusion of existences outside me proves nothing against this; for outer perceptions must in every case have preceded. To obtain originally a representation of something as outside me, without being in fact passive, is impossible. —\n*(s and through space [wird: is] the representation of an object as outside me (in intuition) first obtains reality. Conversely, I should through space also obtain the concept of the existence of something outside me, were not the concept of a relation belonging to *commercium* (community), and indeed as given in perception, at the ground. This concept, however, is that of mere passivity in a state | of representations. That this [concept] is not inferred, because we do not perceive the cause of the existence of a representation in ourselves, but [in de: in th] that it is an immediate perception, must be proved. — If we were affected merely by ourselves, without yet noticing this spontaneity, only the form of time would be met with in our intuition, and we should not be able to represent to ourselves any space (an existence outside us). The empirical consciousness, as determination of my existence in time, would therefore go round in a circle and presuppose itself — but above all would be impossible, since the very representation of the permanent would be lacking, in which there is no continuous synthesis such as there is in time.)\nP. II:\nIn the margin:\n(g That this is the only possible ground of proof.)\nAbove and below 308.30–309.8:\n(g That we must always carry out the determination of space and of time together, yet may just as little determine our existence in space as the existence of the things of space in time thereby.\nPermanence inheres intrinsically in the representation of space, as Newton says. The permanence of the form in our mind is not the same thing (for the form of time is just as permanent). It is permanence as representation of something [Beharrlichem: permanent] outside us, which we lay at the basis of all determination of time and for that reason (g as) represent as permanent; consequently we also cannot regard it as spontaneity of self-determination. — The proposition is: [unser: our] the empirical consciousness of our existence in time is necessarily combined with the empirical consciousness of a relation to something outside us; and the one is just as little an illusion arising from a fallacy, and also (g indeed) just as little an inference, as the other.)\nThe representation of space lies at the basis of the determination of time, on account of permanence. (Likewise, only in space can one obtain a representation of time as magnitude, through a line which I draw while I am conscious of my synthesis merely in the subject.) | Now the permanent cannot be thought merely [eine Bestimmung: a determination] in the determination of time and belong to the spontaneity of self-determination; for then it would not lie at the basis of the determination of time. Consequently it must be represented in relation to the mere receptivity of the mind, that is, in relation to something affecting, which is distinct from me; and this representation cannot be inferred, but must be original.\n––––––––––\nNot everything that is in time is at the same time in space, for example my representations; but everything that is in space is in time. For in time I represent only myself, both by myself alone and in community, and that not through inferences but immediately. That is, I represent a *correlatum* to my state, without yet knowing it; and space is the sensible but real representation of this outer relation. This representation itself, however, and so also everything that is represented in space, is in time.\nThat, when I make myself object, space is not in me but (g yet) in the formal subjective condition of the empirical consciousness of myself, that is, in time, proves that something outside me, that is, something which I must represent in a different manner from myself, is combined with the empirical consciousness of myself. It proves also that this [consciousness] is (g is) at the same time a consciousness of an outer relation, without which I could not determine my own existence empirically.\nThe point is that I should be able to become conscious of myself in an outer relation through a special sense, which, however, is required for the (g time-)determination of inner sense. Space proves a representation which is not related to the subject (g as object); for otherwise it would be the representation of time. That it is now not related to the subject, but immediately to something distinct from the subject as existing, is [derselben: of the same] the consciousness of the object as a thing outside me. Thus that we have an outer sense, and that even imagination can impress images upon us only in relation to it, is the proof of *dualismus* (dualism).\nAll objects of the senses are in time; but not everything that is in time (that is, all objects) is in space. But if all representations of things outside us were only objects of inner sense and representations of ourselves, then [müßte ich alle Dinge: I should have to ... all things] the [Vorstellungen: representations] objects of inner sense would at the same time be all objects, [auch: also] and space would itself be time.\nThe proof of dualism is grounded on this: that the determination of our existence in time [unver: un-] by means of the representation of space contradicts itself, unless one regards this representation as the consciousness of a wholly different relation to the subject from that of the representations in us, namely as the perception of the relation of our subject to other things, and space as the mere form of this intuition. For if the perception of space were founded merely on ourselves, without an object outside us, it would at least be possible to become conscious of [des Grundes: the ground of] these representations as [inne: within] containing merely a relation to the subject. But since in the latter way only the intuition of time ever results, the object which we represent to ourselves as spatial must rest on a representation of something other than our subject. That we can be conscious of an outer relation without ever being able to know the object itself, but only the form of this relation of ourselves to its presence [zu: to], presents no difficulty. Nor does the fact that in dreams and vivid fancies it is possible, without actuality of the object ... Yet the subjective character of this intuition constitutes no objection. For without an outer sense, whose representations we merely repeat and combine in another way (as also happens with inner sense in fancying), we could not have dreams at all.\nAcross the left half of the page:\nOn idealism.\nIn the margin (phase θ3-4):\nPreface.\nIf, in order to explain the purposiveness of the things of the world, we cite a cause which is cause after the analogy of an understanding, this is a tautological explanation. For purpose means that which has such a form as if the representation of the thing were at the same time the cause of it. But if we apply this very causality (g to) the world in so far as it is a moral whole, and to the reality of its laws through their result, namely the highest good, then it sounds otherwise. For there it is not explained tautologically, *idem per idem*; rather an effect is brought out which would not have followed from the world alone.\nNow the question still remains whether one can say that God is the cause of substance without having at the same time determined all its actions. We have, to be sure, not the slightest concept of the possibility of such a causality, nor an example of its reality. But if this is assumed, it would concern only the intelligible, in respect of which the concept of freedom is already of itself necessarily combined with the concept of a substance; for substance must be the ultimate subject of its actions and cannot itself be the mode of action of another. How the substance of the world may be constituted in itself we do not know. But (g it in) its empirically knowable character, which concerns appearances only, is neither a thing nor an object of creation; yet in it everything is always internally determined in the sensible world, which does no injury to the freedom of the intelligible. If one now said: in the intelligible character everything is good (because time vanishes there), only the causality through | freedom is not to be comprehended at all. But the possibility of being the cause of a substance is still less to be comprehended. The difficulties drawn from this against freedom are therefore void, since no concept can be combined with the *hypothesi* (hypothesis).\n===NOTES===\n- Editorial signs (g, [ ], |, *, ––––) are carried over; text inside Kant's own deletions or insertions is translated within the brackets, with the German added only where the reading is doubtful.\n- Restated the long second paragraph as several sentences, and supplied 'Hence' for the loose 'ich mir also ... nicht mehr als dessen'; the grammar there is irregular and I smoothed it.\n- 'indem ich denselben Ziehe' (capital Z) I read as 'in that I draw this [space]'; the antecedent of 'welcher' ([space]) is uncertain, and the passage is partly cancelled.\n- 'Über und unter 30830-3098' is rendered as Academy page/line numbers 308.30–309.8; the notation is the editors', and the reading is uncertain.\n- 'Anschauung' = intuition, 'Gemüth' = mind, 'Receptivitaet' = receptivity, 'Gemeinschaft' = community (cf. *commercium*), 'das Intelligibele' = the intelligible; 'Zustande' is split by the page-break sign.\n- Broken or unfinished sentences ('ohne Wirklichkeit des objects Doch ...'; 'nur die Caussalität durch | Freyheit ...') are left as fragments; nothing is supplied.\n- 'Zeit' in 'Zelt' (308-309 passage) is read as a slip for 'Zeit'; 'Wieder' read as 'Wider' (against).",[]],"reflm:5654":["1788). L Bl. D. 7. S. I, II. R I 200-202.\nS. I:\nAgainst Idealism.\nIf there were no outer objects of our senses, and hence no sense at all but only imagination, it would still at least become possible to be conscious of this its action as a spontaneity. But this representation would then belong only to inner sense, and would contain nothing persistent that could underlie the determination of our existence in empirical consciousness. The mind must therefore (g sich) be immediately conscious of a representation of outer sense as such. That is, it must not be conscious of it through an inference from the representation, as effect, to something outer as cause. Such an inference, because it is valid only [Hyp] as hypothesis, affords no certainty.\nBut how [ist] can the consciousness of a representation of the senses [doch nicht] (g als) as merely [als] passive determination take place, and how can we become conscious of the object of this representation as outer, and at the same time of it, or of its appearance, as persistent?\nIt is to be noted here that every object signifies something distinct from the representation, which distinct something is, however, only in the understanding. Consequently inner sense itself, which makes ourselves the object of our representations, must (g sich auf) relate to something distinct from our self (as [Gegen] transcendental object of apperception). If, then, we did not relate the representations to something distinct from ourselves, they would never yield knowledge of objects. For, so far as inner sense is concerned, it consists only in the relation of the representations to the subject, whether they signify something or nothing.\n––––––––––\nThe above proof means this: if there were not an outer |\nsense, that is, a faculty of becoming immediately conscious (without inference of reason) of something [außer] as outside us, and of ourselves in relation to it, then not even the representation of outer things as such would be possible in us as belonging to intuition; that is, not even space. For inner sense can contain nothing but [die Folge] the time-relation of our representations.\n––––––––––\nOne can indeed posit time in oneself, but one cannot posit oneself in time and determine oneself therein; and yet empirical self-consciousness consists in just this. In order, then, to determine one's existence in time, one must intuit oneself in an outer relation to something else, [in] which for that very reason must be regarded as persistent.\nTime cannot be perceived outwardly in things, since it is only a determination of inner sense [so]. We can therefore determine ourselves in time [vorstellen] only in so far as we stand in relation to things outside us and consider ourselves therein. And being outside us brings with it an existence [welche kei] in so far as it is subject to no change; that is, being persistent.\nThe existence of a thing in time cannot be determined through the relation of its representation [zu] in the imagination to other representations of the imagination. It can be determined only through the relation of it, as a representation of sense, to what is persistent [in A] in the objects of that sense.\nS. II:\nI [arbeite noch bis] climb, even through hard subtleties, to the summit of the principles. I do so not so much because common sound understanding [Gesunder Verstand] could not arrive at it without this detour, as in order to deprive completely of their force all the sophistical subtleties that rise up against it.",[]],"reflm:5655":["P. I:\nTrichotomy. Every relation of representations through concepts has a threefold dimension: 1. the relation of one representation to consciousness, 2. that of one (g other) representation to consciousness, 3. the connection of both together in one consciousness. Only thereby does the connection of representations with one another first become possible (*connexa uni tertio sunt connexa inter se*, things connected with a third are connected with one another).\n––––––––––\nI cannot say (g through inner sense): [that I am in space, but] that space (g or the relation of space) is in me, but time or the relation of time is in me. On the other hand, time is in me, and I am in time. That [I as] the thinking being [through] in the representation of inner sense am to myself mere appearance means nothing more than if I say: I, in whom alone the relation of time is to be met with, am in time. The *continens* (container) is at the same time *contentum* (contained).\nThe proposition: I am (g conscious) to myself (g of myself) [as an] (g as) object [of the] of a sense, means just as much as: I know myself in appearance, *vor* [sic] namely, how my existence [vor] is given to me for thinking. It also means just as much as: I am in time, but the relations of time are merely in me (they cannot be represented (g as) outside me, as those of space can, although the representation of the latter too is in me).\nIt is not possible that I intuit the relation of time [merely] as merely in me [on the other hand myself] and yet also represent myself (g myself) in this time as object of intuition [think], [without myself] if this consciousness concerned me as a thing in itself.\nI intuit myself in time, but not in space; but time is (g a relation) in me, whereas space is [something] a relation outside me.\nThat I am in time, which yet is a mere relation in me, and consequently that the *continens* is a *contentum* and I am in myself, already indicates that [that when I place myself in time I myself as] I think myself in a twofold meaning [place].\nP. II:\n† Likewise, when I attend to the representations of inner sense, | I resolve everything into mere relations of time, and the absolute for the understanding is lacking. — [For if we except the feeling of pleasure, which however is no knowledge, then is] Everything (g is) in us representation, [in (the] posited in relations of time; and if we ask what it then represents, it is either [outer things] the outer [in space], of which we have just seen that it relates to mere relations of space, in respect of which the thing in itself is unknowable for us, or the inner relation of these representations to one another in time, where the pure synthesis which the concepts of the understanding express is again nothing other than the connection of these representations [is only] with regard to the unity of time. Here the feeling of pleasure and the faculty of desire (g connected with it) furnish only the mere relation of those representations to the subject, without knowledge, or to the object through the determination of the causality of the subject; consequently they too yield no knowledge of the thing in itself. Of this nothing remains over but the idea of something which my (g my) self-consciousness, independent of all these conditions of time, [my] indicates as an object [best of its nature determined and kn] but gives nothing in hand [as a G] by which it could be known what it is in itself and without relation to the causality of myself in the world of sense.\nThat which speaks decisively [in the empirical conscious] for the (g empirical) consciousness of myself as an appearance, not as a thing itself, is the mere mode of determination of my existence in this consciousness. Time is as an aggregate of relations in me [and yet am I] (not of the relations outside me) [and yet I say I am in time], i.e. I must presuppose my existence (g time is determination of my existence) in order to be able to think time (g as determination of [the same] this my existence and of the existence of all things outside me). Nevertheless I also say: I am in time, i.e. (g I am determination of time) I must presuppose time in order to be able to determine it (g through) my existence (empirically) [know]. Were my existence here to be understood (g in) the same meaning, there would be in this |\na contradiction. Hence my existence, which I presuppose, must be taken in another meaning than that same existence when I regard it only as determination of time. Now before all determination it is merely the existence of a thing, and yet, although not determined in time, it is still thoroughly determined as existence (in itself), though unknown to me as such. Therefore, so far as time must be presupposed in order to determine me and my existence, it is mere appearance. Nothing is thereby taken from the empirical knowledge of myself; only this knowledge is not extended to the whole [Erk], although to the whole only possible knowledge, and so the supersensible is left over, but at the same time every attempt to determine it theoretically is declared transcendent (*überschwenglich*).\nThe first means: all things outside me are appearances, for the [subjective] condition [them] of determining their existence is in me. — The second: I myself am appearance, and time, which is merely in me, can serve as condition only to myself, so far as I distinguish my pure I from it.",[]],"reflm:5656":["1788).\nRemark by Kant on the address side of the letter of Ch. Fr. Heilsberg of 23 Nov. 1788 (2 X 554), in the third Dorpat volume of letters, leaf 11':\nThat an object corresponds to the categories (g of magnitude) can be shown only in a sensible intuition. If one departs from this even in a single case, the whole beautiful precision of the system, in which one also sees the ground whence all knowledge a priori comes, is lost.\nConcepts of number are those which first determine the concept of time as a *quantum*. They do not presuppose the concept of time, but only its sensible form, and they first determine the concept of time as a *quantum*. Thereby, however, neither an object in time (such as space, all of whose parts are at the same time) nor my own existence in time is determined; rather there is given merely the syn|thetic\nunity of the manifold, whereby a magnitude is possible, and so reality is secured for the concept of magnitude, without touching in the process any quality of it (because all qualities can be given only in time).\nImputation is the judging of an action (the free one, according to laws) with regard to its origin from freedom. This origin, however, can only be [through the] thought so far as the action is under moral laws, for that is causality from freedom.",[]],"reflm:5657":["P. I:\nNB. On transcendent ideas. I cannot subordinate even the categories to the concepts of the original being and of the supersensible generally. But if I call it cause, I understand thereby only a being from whose concept I could derive [the Or] my knowledge of the order of the world (the physical and the moral), just as I derive this from things in the world which I know through their effects. Thereby I properly ascribe nothing to this being, but only think to myself a principle unknown to me, for my theoretical and above all practical use of reason, as a being in the world in which I am to contemplate and to act, and indeed in accordance with a determination not limited merely to the sensible. The holding-true of my way of thinking in accordance with this principle, namely that it also harmonizes with the world in a wider [connection] extent and duration than I may be able to see, is belief in such a being. The theoretical determination of it consists of mere words, which, apart from the relation of such a concept as principle of the practical (where it has an absolutely necessary interest), have no meaning.",[]],"reflm:5658":["Meiners: *Historia doctrinae de vero deo omnium rerum auctove atque rectore* (Lemgo, 1780), Part I, p. 63.\nMargin at the top, above the beginning of Section II, which deals with the Phoenicians:\nIf the concept of God precedes morals, it is conceived in a very immoral manner.\n*Trinitas probat conceptum antiquum moralem.* (The Trinity proves an ancient moral concept.)",[]],"reflm:5659":["In the same work, at I 131, in the sentence: Indi »tam bonos quam malos genios vel Deos vel Debos vel etiam Dewtas romano verbo nominare solent ... loca porro taetra et formidolosa, in quibus scelesti quondam et nefarii homines ob impia facta omni tormentorum genere cruciantur, Gehennae nomine indigitant«, Kant has underlined in pencil the words spaced out by me, and has put two exclamation marks in the margin beside the last word, beside the words »romano verbo«:\n*pecus!* (brute!)",[]],"reflm:5660":["In the same place, II 263, in the right margin beside lines 7–8 from the bottom, which deal with the Ionian natural philosophers:\nThales *Physicus*, Pherecydes *Theologus*.",[]],"reflm:5661":["-Sch. XI 1 pp. 261–3. Hb. IV p. 499f. Ki. L p. 193f.\nAnswer to the question: Is it an experience that we think?\nAn empirical representation of which I am conscious is perception. What I think, in addition to the representation of the imagination, by means of the apprehension and comprehension (*comprehensio aesthetica*) of the manifold of perception, is the empirical knowledge of the object. The judgment that expresses an empirical knowledge is experience.\nIf I think a square *a priori*, I cannot say that |\nthis thought is experience. I can say it, however, if I apprehend in perception a figure already drawn, and think the comprehension of its manifold, by means of the imagination, under the concept of a square. In experience, and through it, I am instructed by means of the senses. But if I think an object of the senses merely at will, I am not instructed by it. In my representation I depend in no way upon the object, but am wholly the author of that representation.\nBut the consciousness that I have such a thought is likewise not an experience, precisely because the thought is not an experience, and consciousness is in itself nothing empirical. Yet this thought does bring forth an object of experience, that is, a determination of the mind which can be observed, in so far as the mind is affected by the faculty of thought. I can therefore say: I have experienced what is required to grasp in thought a figure of four equal sides and right angles in such a way that I can demonstrate its properties. This is the empirical consciousness of the determination of my state in time through thinking. Thinking itself, although it also occurs in time, takes no account whatever of time when the properties of a figure are to be thought. Experience, however, is impossible without connecting a determination of time with it, because in experience I am passive and feel myself affected in accordance with the formal condition of inner sense.\nThe consciousness I have when I institute an experience is a representation of my existence, in so far as it is empirically determined, that is, in time. Now if this consciousness were itself in turn empirical, the same determination of time would again have to be represented as contained under the conditions of the determination of time of my state. Another time would therefore have to be thought, under which (not in which) the time that constitutes the formal condition of my inner experience would be contained. There would thus be a time in which, and together with which, a given time elapsed. This is absurd. The consciousness of instituting an experience, or indeed of thinking at all, is a transcendental consciousness, not experience.\nRemarks on this essay.\nThe act of the imagination whereby an intuition is given to a concept is *exhibitio*. The act of the imagination whereby a concept is made out of an empirical intuition is *comprehensio*.\nApprehension by the imagination: *apprehensio aesthetica*. Comprehension of it: *comprehensio aesthetica* (aesthetic comprehending). I put the manifold together into one whole representation, and so it receives a determinate form.",[]],"reflm:5662":["-Sch. XI 1 pp. 263–5. Hb. IV pp. 500–2. Ki. L pp. 194–6.\nOn miracles.\nNeither through a miracle nor through a spiritual being can a motion be produced in the world without effecting an equal amount of motion in the opposite direction, and consequently in accordance with the laws of action and reaction of matter. Otherwise a motion of the universe [*Universi*] in empty space would arise.\nNor, again, can any change in the world (and so no beginning of that motion) arise without being determined by causes in the world according to laws of nature in general. It cannot, therefore, arise through freedom or through miracles in the strict sense. For time does not determine the order of events; on the contrary, events, that is, appearances, determine time in accordance with the law of nature (of causality). An event which occurred or was determined in time independently of this law would therefore presuppose a change in empty time, and the world itself would consequently have its state determined in absolute time.\nRemarks.\n1. Miracles may be divided into outer and inner, that is, into alterations of appearance for outer sense and for inner sense. The former take place in space, the latter in time. If miracles in space were possible, it would be possible for appearances to occur in which action and reaction are not equal. For all changes in space are motion. But a motion that is to be produced by a miracle is one whose cause is not to be sought among appearances. The law of action and |\nreaction, however, rests on the fact that cause and effect belong to the world of sense (to appearances), that is, are represented in relative space. Since this does not hold of the cause in the case of miracles in space, they will not stand under the law of action and reaction. If, then, a motion is effected by a miracle, then, as it does not stand under that law, the *centrum gravitatis* of the world will be altered by it. In other words, the world would move in empty space. But motion in empty space is a contradiction, since it would be the relation of a thing to a nothing; for empty space is a mere idea.\nIn a similar manner it is proved that there can be no miracles in respect of appearances in time. An appearance in time is a miracle if its cause cannot be given in time, and does not stand under the conditions of time. But the effect can be determined in relative time only through the fact that both cause and effect belong to appearances. This cannot happen, therefore, with an effect produced by a miracle, since its cause does not belong to appearances. A supernatural event would accordingly be determined not in relative but in absolute (empty) time. But a determination in empty time is a contradiction, because for every relation two correlata must be given.\n2. A miracle is an event whose ground is not to be found in nature. It is either a *miraculum rigorosum* (miracle in the strict sense), which has its ground in a thing outside the world (and so not in nature); or a *miraculum comparativum* (comparative miracle), which does indeed have its ground in a nature, but in one whose laws we do not know. Of the latter kind are the things ascribed to spirits. The *miraculum rigorosum* is either *materiale*, where even the force that produced the miracle lies outside the world; or *formale*, where the force is indeed in the world, but its determination lies outside the world. For example, if the drying up of the Red Sea at the passage of the children of Israel was held to be a miracle, it is a *miraculum materiale* if it is given out as an immediate effect of the Deity; whereas |\nit is a *miraculum formale* if it is made to dry up through a wind which, however, was sent by the Deity.\nFurther, the *miraculum* is either *occasionale* or *praestabilitum*. In the first case it is assumed that the Deity intervened directly; in the second the event is made to be brought about through a series of causes and effects, all of which exist for the sake of this one event. —",[]],"reflm:5663":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 271f. Hb. IV S. 507. Ki. L S. 202.\n    On the formal and material significance of certain words.\nThere are several words which, when used in the singular [*in Singulari*], have a different sense from that which they have when used in the plural [*in Plurali*]. In the singular they are to be taken in a formal, in the plural in a material, signification. These words are unity, perfection, truth, possibility*. Unity, used in the singular, is qualitative; used in the plural, it is quantitative. Qualitative unity is to be regarded as the ground of the whole, quantitative unity as a part of the whole. Thus, for example, we cannot say that warmth consists of tepidities. Its magnitude is therefore determined not by the parts which it contains, but by the effects which it produces, for instance, that it expands bodies. Hence we cannot ascribe to it a magnitude in the proper sense, but only a degree; the unity found in it is accordingly qualitative unity. — The units of which discrete magnitudes (numbers) consist are quantitative unities.\nThe perfection of a thing (used formally) is the agreement of its realities with an idea; perfections (used materially) are these realities themselves**.\nTruth in the singular (used formally and qualitatively) is the agreement of our knowledge of an object with that object; truths in the plural (used materially and quantitatively) are true propositions.\nPossibility of an object (used formally and qualitatively); possibilities (used materially and quantitatively) are objects, in so far as they are possible.\n*(g It will be seen that this rests upon the headings of the categories: quantity, quality, relation and modality.)\n**(g Thus we speak of the perfection of a clock, in so far as there is found in it what can be expected of a good clock. Perfections of a clock are properties of it which agree with the concept of a good clock. — But we must also distinguish quantitative and qualitative perfection from perfection.)",[]],"reflm:5664":["Science is a whole, (1) as an aggregate, (2) as a series. Science of first principles.\n( s subordinated: bounded at both ends  coordinated: *in infinitum* )",[]],"reflm:5665":["Logic deals with thinking without an object. Physics deals with knowledge of [Geg] things from experience. Metaphysics deals with knowledge of them prior to all experience. The origin is twofold: (1) how we have come by it .. psychology; (2) how modes of knowledge *a priori* are possible: transcendental philosophy.",[]],"reflm:5666":["What precedes is prior either in kind, namely principles *a priori*, or in origin, that is, historically.",[]],"reflm:5667":["Metaphysics is the system [of the principles] of all knowledge *a priori* (g from concepts) in general.\nThe science of the possibility, extent, etc. etc. of knowledge *a priori* is transcendental philosophy. The sum total of metaphysics. To extract transcendental philosophy from the pure reason that lies before us, and to determine its limits: this is the critique of pure reason.",[]],"reflm:5668":["M I:\nSome modes of knowledge are *a priori* *secundum quid* (in a certain respect), others *simpliciter* (without qualification), in which there is nothing empirical.\nM II:\nThe mark of knowledge *a priori* is necessity.",[]],"reflm:5669":["In knowledge we may attend to the connection with grounds and consequences, and to the generation [of knowledge].",[]],"reflm:5670":["Every mode of knowledge which is *a priori* relatively [respective] is entitled a principle.",[]],"reflm:5671":["All our modes of knowledge are either (1) connected with others *a priori* or *a posteriori*; or (2) absolutely *a priori*, which cannot be drawn from experience (strictly speaking, not from the senses), or absolutely *a posteriori*.",[]],"reflm:5672":["All knowledge has its origin either *a posteriori*, from experience, or *a priori*. Metaphysics is a science of modes of knowledge *a priori*.\nThe *principia* of philosophy are either *a posteriori*: [physiol:] physical, or *a priori*: metaphysical.",[]],"reflm:5673":["For the understanding, our knowledge consists in a connection of grounds and consequences, of the derived and the original.\nWe distinguish knowledge:\n1. according to connection,\n2. according to its kind and origin.\nThe connection is twofold: subordination and coordination; in the latter there are no limits.",[]],"reflm:5674":["Metaphysics is the science of the principles of all knowledge *a priori*, and of all knowledge that follows from these principles. Mathematics contains such *principia*, but is not the science of the possibility of these principles.",[]],"reflm:5675":["Metaphysics cannot be the foundation of religion, but it can be its bulwark, and as such it is indispensable. For the opponent possesses a dialectical metaphysics, to which we must oppose the critical; and this opponent lies in every natural human reason.",[]],"reflm:5676":["From faintheartedness one man becomes an empiricist, another a mystic; the former maintains that crude understanding is better than cultivated judgments.",[]],"reflm:5677":["In metaphysics one can either attain to science, or not: the sceptical position. Reason can be studied through completely.",[]],"reflm:5678":["The two cardinal propositions: God and a future world.",[]],"reflm:5679":["Metaphysics deals either with objects of pure reason, or with objects of experience through pure reason, not according to empirical, but according to rational principles.",[]],"reflm:5680":["1. Of principles of pure reason: transcendental philosophy. 2. *Metaphysica applicata* to objects: nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:5681":["In all philosophy, what is properly philosophical is the metaphysics of the science. All sciences in which reason is employed have their metaphysics.",[]],"reflm:5682":["Instead of the distinction between synthesis and analysis: *synthesis regressiva* and *progressiva*.",[]],"reflm:5683":["The mere possibility of the proposition that there is a God is sufficient for religion, and this possibility is apodeictically certain.",[]],"reflm:5684":["Why is the supreme analytic principle expressed negatively?\nThe *principium summum* is expressed negatively in order to indicate necessity. Principle of negative judgments.",[]],"reflm:5685":["In and \nThe principle of the determination of the undetermined: Every thing is in itself thoroughly determined, but not always through its concept; the latter is the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5686":["The *principium contradictionis* is the principle of all logical possibility, that is, of concepts, in so far as these can be known *a priori*, but not of things.",[]],"reflm:5687":["The possibility of analytic combination can be seen *a priori*, but not that of synthetic combination.",[]],"reflm:5688":["The possibility of a concept rests on its not contradicting itself; the possibility of a thing rests on the concept's having objective reality, on its being possible to give an example of it, that is, on an object's corresponding to it, e.g. *ens absolute necessarium*.",[]],"reflm:5689":["Dogmatic metaphysics is that which proceeds without critical investigation of the main question: how is synthetic knowledge *a priori* possible? So it is also with history without criticism.",[]],"reflm:5690":["*Omne per se impossibile est etiam [hypothetice] absolute impossibile; non omne in se possibile est absolute possibile, sed tantum sub hypothesi aliqua.* (Everything impossible in itself is also [hypothetically] absolutely impossible; not everything possible in itself is absolutely possible, but only under some hypothesis.)",[]],"reflm:5691":["Possibility is either internal or external. The latter is either restrictive external possibility, under certain conditions, or absolute, in every respect.",[]],"reflm:5692":["»Only under a condition« or »also possible under it« is opposed to the internal: either under a restrictive condition |\n(g as consequence) *hypothetice*, or [in nexu] under an extensive condition as ground. Finally, absolutely possible, without any restriction, and the greatest extensive possibility.",[]],"reflm:5693":["Hypothetical possibility can be considered restrictively and also extensively. The latter, if it is complete, is absolute possibility. For the more respects and external relations in which something is possible, the nearer it approaches to absolute possibility.",[]],"reflm:5694":["What contradicts itself is impossible in every respect, that is, is nothing in itself, because it is internally impossible. But this does not hold conversely: what, considered in every respect, is not possible, is impossible in itself.\nAbsolutely impossible is something other than impossible in every respect. The former relates to restrictive, the latter to extensive conditions.",[]],"reflm:5695":["* Possible *in thesi* (s according to the concept: logically), but not *in hypothesi*. (s no real possibility.)\n*(s »in hypothesi« is extensive, »sub hypothesi« restrictive.)",[]],"reflm:5696":["Generally possible *in thesi*, or generally *in hypothesi*; the latter is wider.",[]],"reflm:5697":["At the top of the page:\nIt is remarkable that the categories of quantity and quality have no opposites and correlates, as those of relation and modality have. (Although those of quality do contain an opposite as a gradation, namely negation.) The reason for this seems to be that the first merely contain the apprehension of intuition and produce it synthetically, whereas the second are concepts of relation, either of the objects to one another or to the faculty of knowledge.",[]],"reflm:5698":["Over and \nSomething is hypothetically possible either *sub conditione restrictiva* or *constitutiva*. The first is the condition of agreement, which must not be contradicted; the second, the condition of derivation. What is impossible under no *conditio restrictiva* is absolutely possible, that is, possible under every hypothesis.",[]],"reflm:5699":["The *principium rationis* holds only of experience; for we can have no concept of a real ground except through experience, namely when, if something is posited, something else follows according to a rule (and is thereby determined). Hence from mere concepts we also cannot say anything about the relation of things to real grounds.",[]],"reflm:5700":["Every thing (s existing) is in itself thoroughly determined, but through its concept is undetermined in respect of many possible predicates. Only a single thing is thoroughly determined through its concept: the *ens realissimum*.",[]],"reflm:5701":["The consequence does not determine the ground, but the ground does determine the consequence.\nAll predicates are determinations of an object in general.\nA *praedicatum logicum* can be analytic;\n*determinatio est praedicatum syntheticum* (a determination is a synthetic predicate).",[]],"reflm:5702":["My concept determines a thing, or is determined in a certain way. Synthetically, no thing is determined through its concept; it is so determined, therefore, only in relation to another mode of knowledge [Erkentnis], e.g. intuition or experience.",[]],"reflm:5703":["All determination [Bestimmung] is synthesis.",[]],"reflm:5704":["*Qvo posito ponitur aliud, illo hoc non ideo determinatur. Si illo determinatur, illud est ratio.* (That upon whose positing something else is posited does not on that account determine the latter. If it determines it, it is the ground [*ratio*].)\nTo determine logically is to affirm or to deny a predicate of a thing (the copula in a judgment), without regard to content. To determine metaphysically is to attribute to a concept a predicate which in itself contains a being [Seyn] or excludes one. |\nWhat in itself excludes a being is negation, and the real can be met with only in what is opposed to it.",[]],"reflm:5705":["The first inner ground of what belongs to the possibility of a thing is essence [Wesen]; of what belongs to its actuality, nature [Natur].",[]],"reflm:5706":["The first logical ground of all [determinations belonging to the] predicate of a thing is the logical essence;\nthe first (g inner) real ground of everything that belongs to the existence of the thing, the nature.\nDeletions were made afterwards (in phase ψ3) and words added, so that the first sentence now takes the following form: |\nThe first inner ground of everything that necessarily belongs to the concept of a thing is the logical essence. |\nIn addition, the following was added above the first two words of the Reflexion: |\nlogical, identity, |\nand at the end: |\nThe analytically first ground is the logical [essence], the synthetically first ground the real essence. Nature. On the complaint that the essence of things remains hidden from us.",[]],"reflm:5707":["Subordination is analytic or synthetic, the latter carried back to the first inner real ground: essence.",[]],"reflm:5708":["On the rule in general.\nIt is the objective unity of the consciousness of the manifold of representations [Vorstellungen] (and consequently the unity that holds universally). The rule is empirical if the condition of the unity lies in mere perceptions. It cannot, therefore, be objective otherwise than in relation to possible experience, as knowledge of the objects of perception. The possibility of experience is thus the ground of the [necessity] objective |\nvalidity of the rules of perception; and this possibility of experience is grounded on the necessary unity of the consciousness of representations, so far as knowledge (of objects) is to arise from them. All representations must be represented in relation to one consciousness, and so as universally subject to the [Bed] unity of consciousness. (We are not always conscious of this relation to consciousness, and the representation is then obscure; yet it is always compared with this consciousness.)",[]],"reflm:5709":["On the existence\nof outer things.\nIdealism is the opinion that we experience immediately only our own existence, whereas that of outer things we merely infer (an inference from effect to cause which is in fact uncertain). But we can experience our own existence only in so far as we determine it in time, and this requires something persistent, a representation which has no object in us. [If it] This representation cannot be grounded either on the mere imagination of a persistent outside us [Gr], for an imagination is impossible to which no corresponding object can be given. It [the persistent] is what gives the object in intuition, and our representation, so far as it belongs merely to the consciousness of ourselves, has no such object.",[]],"reflm:5710":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined; but this thoroughgoing determination does not constitute the concept of existence. That consists in this, that a thing is posited absolutely and not merely in relation to its concept.\nI know existence through experience, but not thoroughgoing determination; this comes about through reason.",[]],"reflm:5711":["The proposition 'a thing A is actual' is synthetic.",[]],"reflm:5712":["*Possibile* etc. etc. are not synthetic predicates.\nSomething is either merely thought (a priori), or given (to be represented also a posteriori), or given through this, that something is thought at all (or that it itself is only thought): necessary (hypothetically or absolutely).",[]],"reflm:5713":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:5714":["Being is then taken only problematically when it is to consider judgments solely in relation; but always assertorically when it is considered in itself. If, however, the problematic position is at the same time analytic, it is not distinguished from the absolute and is apodictic.",[]],"reflm:5715":["God contains attributes from each of which all the rest can be inferred. Such a thing cannot belong under a species, but is possible only as a single individual: *conceptus singularis* (singular concept).",[]],"reflm:5716":["Beside and \nThere is more in existence than in possibility, but not in the existing thing.\nExistence is not a determination of the thing, but of the understanding through the object.",[]],"reflm:5717":["That there is a difference between possibility and actuality in things in themselves, in that something is possible (g in every respect [*omni respectu*] and in |\nits thoroughgoing determination) although it is not actual, we cannot see, because we should have to know the thoroughgoing determination of actual things.\nThat whose opposite would be impossible in its thoroughgoing determination would be absolutely necessary, i.e. necessary absolutely, not respectively to a concept. In this way perhaps everything would be absolutely necessary, although considered merely respectively it is contingent (in a certain respect [*respectu*]).",[]],"reflm:5718":["The cause of our distinguishing the possible from the actual is this: that we do not know things through intuitions of the understanding, and so consider them first respectively to our faculty of thought, and only afterwards in relation to intuition.",[]],"reflm:5719":["Something is actual because it is possible, since, if it is possible only as *rationatum* (that which is grounded), then, if the ground is actual, it must also be actual. But nothing is actual merely because it is merely possible, although everything that is possible may be actual. For possibility is a mere relation to our understanding; actuality is either a connection with our fundamental experience, space and time, which contain an original actuality, or a connection with an infinite understanding; here, however, an existence is always presupposed. That we can remove every being (of the real) proves not the possibility of non-being, but the dependence of our concept of being on the connection with experience.",[]],"reflm:5720":["The possibility of the thing is distinguished from the possibility of existence, and the possibility of the [Nicht] opposite of the thing from the possibility of non-being; e.g. *possibilitas oppositi* (possibility of the opposite) in the finite, which does not on that account prove contingency.",[]],"reflm:5721":["How can the possibility of a thing (not merely of the concept) be known a priori? A triangle is possible from three sides, two of which together are greater than the third. A circle is possible. I can give them in pure intuition. What can be given in experience in general, and so accords with the categories, is objectively possible; but its opposite is not on that account impossible.",[]],"reflm:5722":["We can assume no object as possible except one which we can exhibit in intuition, and so can establish its actuality; otherwise, if the representation does not contradict itself, the thought is indeed possible, but not the thing.\nLogical (g inner) possibility (g according to the principle of contradiction) without real [possibility] (g to which no intuition corresponds): empty concept.\nImpossibility we can know only according to the principle of contradiction, when the discussion concerns objects to which no intuition can be given.",[]],"reflm:5723":["We place the difference between possibility and actuality in connection with space and time, which we regard as necessary in themselves and hence as the foundations of all actuality. If now we consider things merely according to the form of space and time, but not connected with them, they are merely possible. This difference must therefore fall away when the discussion is of the thing in itself. The second distinction between the two concepts is merely logical: namely, the undetermined is merely possible; in thoroughgoing determination only what is actual is possible. For the former contains a mere relation of the object to the understanding, the latter a relation to my existence. What is possible in thoroughgoing determination is necessary if it is possible as ground in every respect (and consequently as *independens*, independent). If it is possible only as consequence, then, if the possibility is thoroughgoing, there must be a ground for it. The concept of thoroughgoing determination is a relation to omniscience.",[]],"reflm:5724":["*non ens* [is *ent*]: nonentity [Unding];\nimpossible, empty\nconcept; or *ens rationis*: thing of thought [Gedankending]*.\n[",[]],"reflm:5725":["an impossible, an empty, a negative concept; a mere form of representing things, which is not itself a thing: *ens imaginarium*; that whose concept itself is impossible is logically impossible, A non A = 0: no concept. [If] The object which is impossible, although its concept is possible, is A × 0: *nihil privativum*, *non ens*.",[]],"reflm:5726":["17. E II 639. 597.\nSpace and Time.\nOn the beginning, according to the distinction between analytic and synthetic knowledge and the principle of contradiction. Every object of thought is either something or nothing. Either [it is nothing] in so far as no thought corresponds to the object, that is, the thinking itself is nothing, that is, contradicts itself; or in so far as no object ((g none) in intuition) corresponds to the thought (which does not contradict itself). Thinking, regarded subjectively as representation, before it is analysed, always has an object; but if the thinking contradicts itself, then [it] the thought, and therefore the object, is nothing, and both are struck out. Where the thought remains, there the object, after the analysis, is problematic.\nSomething can now be [posited] merely posited, or posited repeatedly (*iterative*), in order to bring about the representation of the object. In the latter case it is plurality; in the [second] first [unity] it is One. All plurality is therefore homogeneous, and repeated positing is addition. [This] The object whose representation arises through the plurality of given [units] is a *quantum*; and the representation of it as an object that contains many things in itself is that of magnitude. In all magnitude there is composition. That out of which something is composed is entitled unity, and it is comparatively (that is, out of like unity) not composed, hence simple. Thus units are relatively simple, but in themselves they can again be composed, that is, be magnitudes.\nAn object of a certain kind is uniform in the same kind, e.g. space. As plurality, however, the unity can nevertheless be heterogeneous with the *quantum*, and the unity can be a *quantum* that is in turn composed of units of an entirely different kind. Accordingly not every plurality is a magnitude, |\nM 17: |\nbut only that which is homogeneous with the object in which the magnitude is considered. What multitude is, what counting, what number, what infinite magnitude: given (this is contradictory), or only infinite progress, whereby the magnitude is determined only negatively. Space and time are *quanta a priori*. *Quantum continuum et discretum*. Measure. The measure of magnitude in itself is allness; of comparative [magnitudes] it is the given unit (in intuition). We know only comparative magnitudes. Boundaries (*Grenzen*): space and time alone have them; for magnitudes of pure thought there are limitations (*Schranken*). [Das was] In thought there are limitations, in intuition boundaries.\nWhen the following category is finished, it must be considered in connection with the preceding one. Immediately after the categories, the concepts of comparison: identity and diversity; similarity, equality, con|gruence. *Oppositum et contrarium*. And thereafter the categories themselves compared. On division in general.",[]],"reflm:5727":["(g The) magnitude [is] of a thing is [that] the unity which can be generated by the mere repetition of one and the same. The repeated positing of precisely the same is multitude (*Menge*); what therefore has a magnitude (*quantum*) contains a multitude [deren E]. Magnitude is the united multitude, or unity, that is identical with a given multitude. The determinate concept [der einer] of the magnitude of a thing rests on the unit's being given. All continuous magnitudes can be known as to their magnitude only relatively. The divided magnitude can be known absolutely, namely through the absolute unit and the number.\nWe can construct the concept of magnitude, that is, exhibit it a priori in intuition. But we can form a concept of a magnitude *in concreto* only if we bring about the [Begr] empirical concept according to the rule of construction, e.g. by measuring and counting, and thus becoming aware that we could also have thought and determined such a magnitude a priori.\nDynamical magnitude (ground), mathematical (magnitude).",[]],"reflm:5728":["All magnitude is composed because it is divisible, and not conversely; the [compositions] parts are not prior to the continuous magnitude.",[]],"reflm:5729":["How great something is can be known only relatively. There is no absolute unity except the *ens realissimum*; therefore unity is not always that which, repeated several times, makes up the magnitude; for the *ens realissimum* is unity, but cannot be posited repeatedly.",[]],"reflm:5730":["The inner ground of distinction of things, in so far as they are [not] considered as |\n*quanta*, is the quantity; in so far as they are not considered according to their quantity (intuited or judged), it is the quality. Figure is quality. Through it even equal spaces can still be distinguished.",[]],"reflm:5731":["That something is a *quantum* can be known from the thing itself; but its quantity only through something else that is assumed. E.g. one cannot know the magnitude of the earth through German miles, if of these one has nothing but the concept of the fifteenth part of a degree, and has not measured the degree by rods (thus *alio assumto*).",[]],"reflm:5732":["The measure of the *quantum* must always itself be a *quantum* of the same kind. For, first, it must be homogeneous, because, *aliquoties sumtum* [taken several times], it is to bring forth the former. Second, it must itself be a *quantum*, because as *simplex* it would be heterogeneous from the *compositum*. A *quantum* that as such consisted of simple parts would be a *continuum* ...",[]],"reflm:5733":["The unity of a thing (qualitative) and of a unit (quantitative, mathematical).",[]],"reflm:5734":["Over, beside and \nUnity, truth and completeness (transcendental perfec|tion)\nare the requisites of every mode of knowledge, [die] respectively with regard to understanding, power of judgment and reason (for the last, apodictic certainty is required, that is, complete truth).\nAll derived from One.\nAll combined in One.\nThe one derived from all.\nUnity of the subject, of the ground and of the whole.\nPossibility\nActuality\nNecessity\nThere are three transcendental criteria of the possibility of things in general. Threefold formal unity.",[]],"reflm:5735":["In and \nUnity of the rule (universality); truth in the subsumption under rules, for these alone make something objectively valid, that is, true; finally perfection, the connection of all predicates in one principle. The essence is the unity, the essential constituents the truth, all attributes together the perfection.",[]],"reflm:5736":["Unity of the object and unity of the manifold in the object. The former is quantitative, the latter qualitative. 1. Unity out of many; 2. unity of the many among one another; 3. many out of One.",[]],"reflm:5737":["Formal unity is philosophical, material unity mathematical.\nUnity of a thing\nA unit\nMany agree with One, many agree among one another, One with many.",[]],"reflm:5738":["Determination of a thing with regard to its essence (g as thing) is transcendental.",[]],"reflm:5739":["*consensus unius ad varia, variorum inter se, variorum ad unum* [agreement of one with many, of many among themselves, of many with one].\nThe three criteria of the inner possibility of a thing*.\n*(g of a thought-thing [*Gedankending*], e.g. hypothesis: *unum*, possibility; *verum*, actuality; *bonum*, necessity.)",[]],"reflm:5740":["In the relation of a representation to an object in general, unity of the object must be thought through a concept; plurality of the predicates that flow from the concept; allness of the determinations (g as ground) for the whole concept: totality.",[]],"reflm:5741":["Transcendental properties of things are those that are essentially bound up with the concept of a thing in general.",[]],"reflm:5742":["In hypotheses: unity of agreement is possibility, of connection actuality, of derivation necessity.\nThe three requisites of a definition besides the formal one, namely distinctness, because it is logical.",[]],"reflm:5743":["For the knowledge of a thing there is required: 1. Unity of the concept (of the object), that the concept represent not [several] still others than the one object. *Conceptus vagus*. Distinction from all the rest. 2. [Unity] Plurality as determination of this unity, combined in one consciousness, or truth. 3. Allness as plurality [bestim] determined through the unity, or perfection.",[]],"reflm:5744":["1. The object as determinable. 2. The determination of it with re|gard\nto its difference from others. 3. The thoroughgoing determination through a concept.",[]],"reflm:5745":["1. Unity of the object, [dazu] to which the manifold is related, that is, concept. Unity of consciousness.\n2. Agreement of the manifold with the object according to rules, that is, truth.\n3. Connection of all rules out of one concept, that is, out of principles, that is, perfection.\nThis is nothing but the distinction of an *ens rationis* from an *ens reale*.",[]],"reflm:5746":["1. The determinable something is One with regard to all manner of *praedicata opposita*.\n2. The determination of it and the truth thereof.\n3. The possible thoroughgoing determination with regard to what belongs to its essence, that is, perfection.",[]],"reflm:5747":["I assume, e.g. by hypothesis, [die] an evil principle: 1. It lacks unity, for there can be no completely evil [one]. etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:5748":["On concepts that can be used either merely as predicate (g adjective), or also as subject, formally or materially. In the latter case they admit a plural, e.g. unity, [perfection] truth, perfection, possibility, reality.",[]],"reflm:5749":["1. Unity of the determinable object.\n2. Plurality of the determinations and their agreement among themselves according to laws of the understanding: formal truth, logic.\n3. Allness of the determinations in the [Be] complete concept of an object.\nThereby we distinguish the thing from the non-thing.\nThese propositions are [subjective] merely logical and not metaphysical, that is, not objective, as determination of things.",[]],"reflm:5750":["[The unity] The relation of the many to one another, so far as they are contained [together] in one, is combination (*Verbindung*). Combination according to a rule is order.\nIn the mind all order is [either] in time, and indeed successive. What can be intuited reciprocally one after another is simultaneous. Harmony and melody.\nA rule is the universality of the condition in the determination of the manifold.\nOr it is the unity of the condition under which something is determined with universal validity; or the determination of a concept, so far as it is at the same time universally valid.",[]],"reflm:5751":["The unity of the condition under which something is posited universally is a rule.",[]],"reflm:5752":["The concept of perfection belongs to the concept of allness or *totalitas*: what has been accomplished (g completed) (*perfectum*), in contra|distinction to what has merely been begun (*affectum opus*). The perfection of a thing does not as yet constitute a perfection that arises from my positing the thing, and so [it does] not [hold] with regard to things in general. All completion contains a reference to satisfaction (*Wohlgefallen*): 1. with respect to a problematic end, and then the satisfaction rests on the unity of the manifold and on the subjective ground of comprehensibility; or 2. with respect to a categorical end, and then it is entitled a perfection. Perfections in the metaphysical sense are realities [there]. The good is the object of choice; hence a thing is not good for the reason that it is perfect of its kind; but the requisites belonging to its perfection are, relatively, good for it. Perfections in man are means to all sorts of possible ends of his existence in general, hence talents, not well-being and health.",[]],"reflm:5753":["Perfection can mean either (g in the theoretical sense) mere completeness; and then, as the perfection proper to the essence of each thing in relation to the thing itself, it is transcendental; as completeness of the entity or reality of all things in general, it is metaphysical perfection. The first is the perfection of a thing, the second a perfection of which there are many.\nOr it means in the practical sense the good; and then either what is good in itself (good will), or what is good as a means to all ends, the fitness for all ends, e.g. understanding.",[]],"reflm:5754":["On Chapter II. In the categories of modality being is considered, and indeed the possibility not so much of the thing as of its existence, whereby it is considered not merely in itself but also in relation to all [things] requisite conditions. If it is connected with these, it is actual. If these conditions [not merely contingently] are already given in its concept, it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:5755":["[Necessity] Existence which can be known completely *a priori* is [that of the] absolutely necessary; existence which [is] only under a condition, and so *secundum qvid a priori*, is hypothetically necessary. The concept of an *absolute necessarium* is problematic, that is, the possibility [of] such an object cannot be comprehended. The human understanding cannot know any existence *a priori* from the mere concept of a thing. It completes hypothetical necessity.",[]],"reflm:5756":["The opposite is either logically impossible or really impossible. The latter is either absolute, without condition, neither logical nor real, and is a mere idea; or under the condition of a possible experience, e.g. freedom [it its opposite] is really impossible in experience.",[]],"reflm:5757":["Agreement with the conditions of an experience in general: possibility.\nConnection of a thing with experience in general: actuality.\nThis connection, so far as it can be known *a priori*, that is, independently of experience, is necessity.",[]],"reflm:5758":["From mere concepts the existence of a thing cannot be known |\nand so not *simpliciter a priori*. But neither [through] on the presupposition of pure intuitions. Since beyond concepts and intuitions *a priori* there is nothing further but experience, experience must precede the necessary, for [necessity]: necessity, that is, it must precede from the condition of actuality.",[]],"reflm:5759":["Everything that exists is thoroughly determined. The absolutely necessary thing is to be regarded through its concept as existing, and consequently also as thoroughly determined. *Deus est conceptus singularis* (God is a singular concept); that each of his predicates belongs to him necessarily, and that he is determinable in only one way (*vnico modo*), does not prove the necessity of his existence.",[]],"reflm:5760":["I can say: we can think a *ens necessarium* (necessary being) only as *realissimum*, because this concept alone is thoroughly determining. But not conversely: I must think an *ens realissimum* as *ens necessarium*, for in that case I should be going beyond the concept, in positing the object outside it. *Omnimoda determinatio* (complete determination) is not a *definitio existentiae* (definition of existence).",[]],"reflm:5761":["E II 332:\n*Necessarium ens est, cuius nonexistentia est impossibilis.* (A necessary being is one whose non-existence is impossible.) *Absolute tale non involvit contradictionem, sed transscendit conceptum humanum.* (Such a being absolutely does not involve a contradiction, but transcends the human concept.) For non-being involves no contradiction in itself because being, as a synthetic predicate, is not identical with the concept of the thing. In a contradiction, namely, precisely what was affirmed in a concept, and so lay in it, must also be denied. If space and time were objects outside thought, or their properties, they would be *entia necessaria* or properties of such. For their concept is already a being, which contains the ground of the possibility of things. |",[]],"reflm:5762":["On the relation of the real ground to the consequence, and that it cannot be known and distinguished otherwise than *in concreto* which is the ground and which the consequence, except that when [A] I posit A, B constantly follows upon it, and not conversely.",[]],"reflm:5763":["Possible is what is determined with regard to time in general, hence with regard to the concept of time, in which therefore the conditions of determination in time lie; e.g. the real possibility of attractive force. (g It is grounded in general.) This possibility we know only through experience. Secondly, [also possible] actual is what is determined in relative time, and hence is at some time, that is, is determined through other things given in time and with regard to them. Finally, a necessary being is what is determined with regard to absolute time, on the presupposition of which therefore all relative determination of time rests.",[]],"reflm:5764":["29'. E II 1092.\n M 29:\nThe difference between something's being thought and its being given is this, that it is an object of indeterminate or of thoroughly determinate thinking. In themselves there is here no difference between things, but only in relation to our knowledge *a priori*. What can be thought *a priori* only as thoroughly determined is necessary; what is thought only as indeterminate is to that extent contingent. Although our concept is never thoroughly determined, since it does not contain everything, the understanding nevertheless |\nrepresents the object as thoroughly determined in itself, |\nM 29': |\nand thinking and apperceiving are one and the same, and consequently possibility and existence. Nor can it be distinguished in things themselves, but only in their relation to the understanding, at the basis of which apperception lies. Consequently I myself am determined in only one way in relation to everything.",[]],"reflm:5765":["What is conditionally necessary, as hypothesis, is for that reason not [necessary] in itself.",[]],"reflm:5766":["In and \nWe have no example of hypothetically necessary things except with regard to their form; e.g. [water] the formation of ice, of salts from mixture.",[]],"reflm:5767":["A judgment about the existence of an object is always synthetic. Necessary judgments do not for that reason represent the object as necessary.",[]],"reflm:5768":["There is certainly a difference between absolute, that is, inner, necessity and hypothetical necessity, when the latter is thought only under a possible hypothesis. But if it is carried back to its highest actual ground, the difference vanishes, so far as the degree of necessity is concerned. For the necessary consequences of a necessary cause are inseparable from its absolute necessity. But an absolutely necessary being can have only a single complete effect of its existence; and if all possible existence is grounded upon |\nthe same, there can also be only a single complete possibility, namely the whole of the actual.",[]],"reflm:5769":["Possibility seems to be distinguished from actuality only with regard to the appearance (*Erscheinung*) of things. The opposite of the predicates of a thing may well be possible, but not the opposite of its existence in the thoroughgoing determination.",[]],"reflm:5770":["Contingency and necessity are not differences in the existence of things, nor in the determination of things in themselves, but merely in their concepts, and so always relative to this concept, and hence never in themselves. The possibility of change can be declared to belong to the thing itself.",[]],"reflm:5771":["Everything necessary must be known a priori as existing, that is, from mere possibility and from the concept. To know it hypothetically, however, is to regard this concept of something merely possible as attaching, in respect of its condition, to something actual. If, then, the thing which contains the condition is posited, the conditioned follows from the mere possibility of that thing. E.g. »Glass must break through a fall«: a priori, from brittleness in general. This brittleness attaches to the glass, and the breaking therefore also exists as necessary. [This is] to infer actuality from mere possibility.",[]],"reflm:5772":["The possibility of things is distinguished from the possibility, actuality or necessity of their existence. The former consists merely in this, that their concept contains nothing self-contradictory; it is, so to speak, the agreement of their archetype with the understanding that thinks it. The |\npossibility of existence, on the other hand, signifies the agreement of the *position* (positing) of such an object outside the understanding. Now if the thinking itself is not the cause of this, then the matter and the connecting ground of it must be outside the understanding. Existence does not belong at all to the idea of a thing, and the possibility of it, if it is complete, cannot be distinguished from actuality and necessity.",[]],"reflm:5773":["On and \nContingency, that is, possibility of the opposite, cannot be known through alteration, but only from the fact that all alteration is a *caussatum* (something caused), and is therefore contingent in itself and apart from there being a cause for it. A priori it cannot be known in anything.",[]],"reflm:5774":["The necessary can, in respect of the predicate in regard to which it is determined, be determined in only one way. Existence, however, does not belong among these [predicates].",[]],"reflm:5775":["Either a thing or a concept of a thing is absolutely necessary. The concept of the *realissimum* (the most real) is necessary in every respect, in relation to concepts of things in their thoroughgoing determination. Its removal leaves a complete void for the understanding.",[]],"reflm:5776":["*vnico modo determinabile* (determinable in only one way) is, for the author, a thing which is thoroughly determined through its concept; of this kind is only the *ens realissimum* (most real being); for the *mere negativum* (the merely negative) is no thing, and the *partim reale partim negativum* (partly real, partly negative) is thoroughly determined through no concept. Now Leibniz, following the lead of Descartes, had attempted [aus] to prove that a thing which is thoroughly determined through its concept also exists necessarily; but the inference is found to be invalid. Thereupon he, or Wolff, attempts to convert the proposition and to say: Every necessary thing is thoroughly determined through its concept (consequently, what is not thoroughly determined through its concept is no necessary being. Since, then, some necessary being must be, there must also exist a being thoroughly determined through its concept, that is, a (g such as) *ens realissimum*), and consequently an *ens realissimum*. But then the [opposite] (g *per accidens*) converted proposition would also have to be true, [which it can be] only because just one single thing can be *ens realissimum*; and indeed, for the very reason that the concept of it is a |\n*conceptus non communis* (not a common concept) but a *singularis* (singular one) [weil zw] and the connection of convertibility must therefore [thereby] constitute a *conversio simpliciter talis* (conversion simply as such), and that from mere concepts, necessity would in the same way have to follow from the concept of the *realissimum*, as that concept would from necessity; which is nevertheless false.",[]],"reflm:5777":["Through no single concept of a limited thing is this thing thoroughly determined; for a priori no thing can be thoroughly determined except through the idea of the *maximum* (the greatest).",[]],"reflm:5778":["The necessary is determined in only one way. The concept of a thing which, according to this concept, is determinable in only one way is the *conceptus entis realissimi* (concept of the most real being), and hence it is inferred: only the *ens realissimum* can be *absolute necessarium* (absolutely necessary); but that is, only *necessitas logica* (logical necessity). The thing itself to which this concept belongs can be determined otherwise than according to this concept.",[]],"reflm:5779":["Only the *omnitudo* (allness) is absolute unity and *individuum rationale* (rational individual).\nThat the necessary must be determinable in only one way is a *conditio sine qua non* (indispensable condition), but for that reason not yet the sufficient ground of its necessity, any more than that it contains no contradiction.\nAnd then all this is only logical, because the possibility is grounded merely on |\nthe absence of contradiction and not on the possible unity of all these realities in one thing.",[]],"reflm:5780":["Even if the concept of a thing can be determined in only one way, these predicates belong to it necessarily, but not existence; and an existence might be necessary, though not from the concept which we have, or from any other; for from the concept of no thing can an existence be inferred.",[]],"reflm:5781":["That a necessary being cannot be altered follows, indeed, if time is a mere form of sensibility and determines the existence of no things in themselves [seyn], but never if it belongs to the determinations of the thing in itself. For then, if it is actually altered, this is not the contradictory opposite of its determinations in existence.",[]],"reflm:5782":["In and \nThe necessary must be determined in only one way, because it is to be known a priori as existing. Now the *ens realissimum* (most real being) is thoroughly determined through its concept; therefore only this can be an *ens necessarium* (necessary being). If, then, we wish to assume one, we must think it in this way; but from the concept of the highest reality existence cannot be known a priori, and yet even then everything is only relative to the human understanding.",[]],"reflm:5783":["The *ens necessarium* (necessary being) is that whose opposite is absolutely impossible. The human understanding, however, cannot see this impossibility, |\nexcept through the fact that non-being contradicts its concept. Now the non-being of a thing never contradicts the concept of the thing in itself; therefore the concept of the *ens necessarium* is unattainable for human reason, yet must necessarily be assumed, because otherwise the series of the conditionally necessary is never brought to an end.\nThat it is determinable in only one way follows from this, that its existence is supposed to follow from the mere concept. Now everything that exists is thoroughly determined, and existence can therefore be [da geschlossen werden] inferred only from a concept which is thoroughly determined [oder]; otherwise it does not follow at all from the concept, but from some other assumed existence.\nThere is a difference between assuming the necessary being for the sake of something else, and knowing a being as necessary through its concept.\nWe cannot say that a being exists contingently because it is altered, but [only] that we could not then know its existence from its mere concept; for then it would have to be determinable in only one way. But we can know the existence of no thing from its mere concept, although, if we could know it, the thoroughgoing determination would at the same time be contained therein.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* (most real) alone is suited to the concept of the *ens necessarium*.",[]],"reflm:5784":["* I posit something either as object of a partial or of a thoroughgoing determination. In the first case I posit, through the concept of a thing, the universal, and therefore the manifold. In the second case, the individual [und dessen], which cannot be manifold. The substratum of thoroughgoing determination is that which contains the matter for all determinations of a thing in general; consequently I cannot say, »if I assume such a thing«; rather, reason presupposes something of the kind in all possibility. In regard to thoroughgoing determination, therefore, possibility and actuality are one and the same, and its thoroughgoing determination is not thought as universal, but at the same time as individual.\n*(s We cannot prove that, if a concept is of such a kind that it includes in itself the thoroughgoing determination of the object, this object exists necessarily; we can prove only that, if we could derive existence from mere concepts, these would at the same time have to contain the thoroughgoing determination. That there cannot be more than one *necessarium* (necessary being) cannot be seen at all from concepts.)",[]],"reflm:5785":["Our concept of absolute necessity signifies not the inner necessity from the concept of a thing, but the externally necessary presupposition and condition of their possibility, in relation to everything possible (the *ens realissimum*), just as time is regarded as absolutely necessary because everything must be in it.",[]],"reflm:5786":["The proposition: the necessary being is determinable in only one way, means: all determinations belong to it necessarily. This does not, however, prevent there being still other, and differently determined, necessary beings. But if the proposition is to mean: all determinations must be given through a single concept, this indicates only that we could otherwise have no concept a priori of a necessary being according to which thoroughgoing determination would at the same time be given. So then only the concept of the *realissimum* is suited to this, if we already know that a being is necessary. But from this we cannot infer even the possibility, still less the necessity, of such a being, but only that in this way alone [which] we could determine it thoroughly from a mere concept, as *ens singulare*.",[]],"reflm:5787":["If something [as ei] can [is to] be known through concepts as a necessary being, then this concept can be none other than that through which the thing is at the same time thoroughly determined, and is therefore determinable in one way only. For otherwise it would be thought not as (necessarily) |\nexisting, but only as a merely possible being. But the former is impossible [therefore also].",[]],"reflm:5788":["The existence of the *contradictorie oppositorum* which is not contradictory. When a predicate conflicts not with the thing but with another predicate.\nChange concerns existence alone.",[]],"reflm:5789":["That the concept of time is presupposed in the concept of the combination of the *contradictorie oppositorum*; *contradictorie opposita* at different times do not contradict one another.",[]],"reflm:5790":["Something is entitled unchangeable either in so far as it does not exist in time at all (God), or in so far as it is not considered in respect of its existence at all (the essences of things). That is nominal unchangeableness, and it must not be used at all. Real unchangeableness is to be found only in appearances. In them, however, it is only relative, not absolute.",[]],"reflm:5791":["Change is the combination of determinations with their opposite in the existence of a thing *post se invicem* (the one after the other). The thing remains, but the determinations arise and pass away.",[]],"reflm:5792":["The possibility of change, that is, [how] a thing (g as) can think itself as determined in its existence in a twofold, opposite manner, is not possible a priori, from mere concepts, but only in time; and even in time the cause of it can be found only in experience.",[]],"reflm:5793":["What is changeable does not for that reason exist contingently, and the |\nnecessary being is not for that reason unchangeable. But if we take the necessity which we can comprehend and grasp, namely existence from concepts, then, since time drops out of the concept of a thing in itself, everything [what the] opposite of the predicates must also be contradictory in respect of the thing (its concept). For in the existence of a changeable thing predicates are opposed only to predicates, and can be one after another without contradiction, because none of these predicates is determined through the concept of the thing.\nIf I want to think the concept of an unchangeable being, I must be able to specify a concept which would at the same time contain the existence of the thing, and hence the thoroughgoing determination through one concept, and not this alone but also the objective validity of that concept.",[]],"reflm:5794":["All changeability proves the contingency of the thing according to mere concepts of the understanding (g as thing in itself), because through the concept it is not determined as regards existence, and so is not thoroughly determined; but it does not prove contingency as appearance.",[]],"reflm:5795":["The logical possibility of the opposite does not prove the real possibility in the combination.",[]],"reflm:5796":["In and \nEverything changeable is undetermined through its concept in respect of the *praedicata opposita*, and is therefore logically contingent.",[]],"reflm:5797":["In and \nContingency is inferred from change only because the thing is then in time. But time too can be empty. Everything in time is contingent.",[]],"reflm:5798":["What is changeable is hypothetically necessary, but the change, with its whole ground, is necessary. Everything changeable is contingent, that is, not absolutely necessary. For it is in time, hence appearance; it is therefore nothing absolutely necessary. That is absolutely necessary [can] whose opposite cannot occur at all, not even one after another.",[]],"reflm:5799":["Change itself can be absolutely necessary, and what is changeable is contingent.",[]],"reflm:5800":["Bottom margin, \nThe possibility of change, that a state should contain the ground of its opposite in what follows, cannot be comprehended; but for the comprehension of temporal sequence in experience it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:5801":["The determination that follows is in itself contingent, but not the sequence and the series.",[]],"reflm:5802":["The possibility of change cannot be known a priori.",[]],"reflm:5803":["In and \nContingent is that whose opposite is possible in its place.",[]],"reflm:5804":["In and \nChange of relation is not always change of the thing; e.g. when fire makes [something] hard or soft.",[]],"reflm:5805":["Over and \nChange is the combination of contradictorily opposed determinations in the existence of a thing (which yet do not contradict the con|cept\nof the thing, but only *praedicatum praedicato*, not *subiecto oppositum*).\nWhat makes possible that which, according to the mere concept of a thing, is impossible? Time. (g *determinationes oppositae* can only succeed one another.) Thus time does not belong to the concepts of things in themselves, but to the way in which we intuit them.",[]],"reflm:5806":["The change of what belongs to the essence of a thing is merely a change in the determination of concepts, [not] therefore logical, and not a change of things and of their existence.",[]],"reflm:5807":["Inside margin, \n*Contrarie* opposed determinations [have] are *realitas ut ratio opposita realitati* (reality as a ground opposed to reality).",[]],"reflm:5808":["From change we can indeed infer that a state is [necessarily] contingent, but not that a thing is contingent. The first means that the state has a cause; but this does not hold of the thing, which contains the cause of it.",[]],"reflm:5809":["Change first makes possible the concept of contingency in existence; hence everything contingent is indeed changeable, and everything that is not changeable is necessary. Change makes necessary only the inference to a cause.",[]],"reflm:5810":["Logical unchangeableness in respect of the concept is commonly taken for real unchangeableness in respect of existence; and many infer from the latter the permanence of things in respect of certain properties.",[]],"reflm:5811":["In themselves, things can determine themselves *ad oppositum* (to the opposite) neither by themselves (g alone) nor in community; for *nulli subiecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*, and *ratio* [cont] <8A *est etiam ratio* >8 *Non A*. Time, therefore, in which alone change can be thought, is no [representation of the] determination of things in themselves. Consequently all changes are mere determinations of appearances, and time itself, in which existence is determined as changeable, is not something that attaches to the existence of things in themselves, but only to our mode of sensible representation (not of the understanding). Unchangeable, therefore, is not only the (g in itself) necessary being, but also everything intellectual. In the objects of the senses, however, [In] every [change necessary] determination which constitutes the change is necessary, but only ever hypothetically, to infinity. The principle of contradiction, in so far as it is restricted to what is in time, does not apply to concepts of things, but [to] those of mere appearances; and it involves no contradiction that both opposite predicates (s only not at the same time) should belong to the latter as successive in appearance, and that neither should belong to them as things in themselves.\nI, as object of sensible intuition, am changeable, and cannot bring the concept of myself under transcendental predicates of things in themselves, e.g. the simple or the composite, etc. etc., but can speak only of my intuition. But I, as subject of thinking and merely as object of reason, can in turn know no predicates through which such an object would be given to me *in concreto*: neither extension, nor permanence in time, nor yet their opposite. Hence all psychology as doctrine concerns only man as a thinking being, not the mere I in general; but psychology as critique prevents what holds of me in so far as I am man from being taken for the only thing that holds of me as a thinking being in general.",[]],"reflm:5812":["Beside and \nThe concept of the *ens realissimum* is the ontological locus for all |\npossible things, in so far as they distinguish themselves from one another as things in general. Their difference then consists merely in the limitation of the concept of the *omnitudo realitatis*. Instead of thinking each thing, in accordance with the *principium exclusi medii*, as determinable in respect of the real of all predicates, or rather for the sake of this determinability, in order to represent them analytically, one takes as the ground of all of them something thoroughly determined.",[]],"reflm:5813":["A merely possible thing cannot, so far, be changed, because change is the existence of the *oppositorum* in time.\nThe only contingent (of nature) is that which is regarded merely as actual through freedom.",[]],"reflm:5814":["*Realitas phaenomenon* (reality of appearance) is what corresponds to sensation. Full and empty space.\nWe do not always need true reality, in which no negation (*non esse*) is thought at all; at the outset we have to do also with *realitatibus phaenomenis* (phenomenal realities).",[]],"reflm:5815":["On M § 135:\nNegation is not a lack of things, but of the determination of things. — *Realitas, negatio, limitatio*; every *negatio* is either merely *limitatio*, i.e. the *oppositum* of quantity, or *negatio repugnantiae* and an *oppositum* of quality. What holds of a *quantum* holds also of the *limes quanti*; for the quality remains.",[]],"reflm:5816":["Beside and \nThe negation [as] is either according to quantity or according to quality. In the first case it is to be regarded as a vanishing *quantum* and |\nas nothing but mere limitation, and is not contradictorily opposed to reality [for] so that one of the two would obtain, but is a *disparatum* or *defectum*. In the second case it is *negatio oppositionis*, as when I say: a spirit is not composite, consequently simple. On the other hand I cannot infer: a spirit [is not in] does not occupy a space, consequently it is present in a point. For here the same quality of presence is assumed, and the point is a vanishing space. God does not move, consequently he is at rest.",[]],"reflm:5817":["39'. E II 1387.\nM 39:\nIn the appearances (*phaenomena*) negation is nothing other than restriction; in the pure understanding it is *remotio*, and so the qualitative *oppositum*. Limitation is either of the determination merely or of the counter-action. The former is logical cancellation, the latter real. Motion is transformed into rest either merely as *defectus*, through a lack of determining grounds, or through reaction.\nM 39':\nIn the mathematical antinomy the one proposition is the quantitative contrary of the other, but they have the same condition and are therefore both false. In the dynamical propositions the one is the qualitative contrary of the other, but the condition is different; hence both can be true.",[]],"reflm:5818":["At the very top of the page.\nThe counter-play (*Wiederspiel*) is what can be the positive ground of the contrary of a reality.",[]],"reflm:5819":["The *contrarium realitatis* (contrary of a reality; when relatively so) is the ground for removing the reality, e.g. descent with respect to ascent. A reality absolutely so [does not have contr] is not contrarily opposed to a reality that is likewise absolute.",[]],"reflm:5820":["Negation is either that of a deficient ground or that of a removing ground (really oppo|sed).\nWe do not know whether the negations arise from lack or from real conflict.",[]],"reflm:5821":["Limitation proceeds directly to 0, and so likewise does reality.",[]],"reflm:5822":["In and \nThe real is also opposed to the merely formal in intuition. Hence empty space, in which no being of anything is represented.",[]],"reflm:5823":["In and \nOnly reality in the appearance can be opposed to a reality, and negation can agree with a reality.",[]],"reflm:5824":["Beside and \nNegation is not specifically distinct from reality. It is the mean between two opposed realities [and the Ub], which is common to both. Between contradictory opposites (*contradictorie opposita*) there is no mean, but there is between those that are really opposed (*realiter opposita*).",[]],"reflm:5825":["Beneath and \nThe possibility of alteration rests upon the contrariety of certain *realitatum phaenomenorum* (realities of appearance).",[]],"reflm:5826":["41. E II 676. 677. 1640.\nM 41':\nOne can regard negation either as opposed to reality or as opposed to affirmation; in the latter case it is the logical *oppositum*, in the former [the *evanescens*] limitation. [Extension in] \"A spirit is not extended\" does not mean as much as \"its extension is vanishing\", but \"it cannot have extension at all\". The point, on the other hand, is not extended as a vanishing space. Spirits therefore cannot be regarded as points.\nIf I assert the non-being of a predicate, then by this |\nthe mere vanishing of it must not be thought at once, and I must not reckon the subject in the same kind of things, but often must count it in another kind.\nAbsolute reality is what belongs to being in respect of a thing in general. Comparative reality is what belongs to the being of a certain kind of [E] perceptions; thus pain is comparative reality, but not on that account absolute. *Negatio absoluta* is the mere limitation of the existence of an object as a thing in general. *Realitas evanescens est defectus in sensu absoluto* (a vanishing reality is a defect in the absolute sense). [Ein D] Existence in which all [existence] possible existence is contained is without limits. But it cannot be composed of much limited existence. Therefore existence without limits is that of an *ens realissimum* (most real being), regarded at the same time as ground. From this the possible existence of every other thing is regarded as derived, because as a thing in general, in respect of its negations, it can be represented merely as (g ein) a limited *ens realissimum* or as its limited product [am]. Possibility we can no longer distinguish from actuality as soon as we have arrived at the first substratum of the determination of all concepts. |\nM 41: |\nIf non-being is regarded merely as limit, all realities are assumed to be of one kind.",[]],"reflm:5827":["To a thing there belongs neither a predicate nor [the] its non-being, so far as the latter can be regarded merely as a diminution of the predicate. For that is not the contradictory opposite.",[]],"reflm:5828":["Above and \nNon-being (g of B), as a contradiction of the predicate B with the concept A, cannot subsist together with the concept A at all, and is its contradictory opposite. But the non-being which [dem Begriffe nicht] contains no contradiction with the concept is the privative opposite or *defectus*. E.g. \"The soul is not extended, because it is not determined at all with regard to space, or because [ihr] ex|tension [wieder] contradicts its concept\" would also contain the proposition: \"the soul is not present in space as a point\". For the point is nothing but vanishing extension, and the concept of it does not contradict the [Ausdehnung] concept of extension, but is only a lack of the extended.",[]],"reflm:5829":["Above and \n*Opposita logice* (logical opposites) under an inadmissible condition, e.g. extension, are both false. E.g. the soul is either extended in space or unextended in it, i.e. a point. *Opposita* under two different conditions are not contradictorily opposed; the opposition is inadmissible, and both propositions can be true. E.g. the will as appearance is under natural necessity, and as intellectual it is free. Both conditions are to be thought in all beings, but only in the will do we notice the latter.",[]],"reflm:5830":["If I say: God is not alterable, because nothing breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5831":["The *oppositum* can be a qualitative or a quantitative *oppositum*. The first is contradiction; the second is *quantum* = 0, or limitation. Hence in a formula rest can be a motion = 0, simplicity an extension = 0, unaltered duration an alteration = 0.",[]],"reflm:5832":["A cask of apples is indeed a *compositum*, but not a *quantum*. [In the latter the] The latter is [its Ei] a *compositum* whose unity, assumed at will, produces the *compositum* by frequent repetition.",[]],"reflm:5833":["Space is a *quantum*, matter a *compositum*, body a *totum*. The world, however, is a *totum absolute tale* (a whole absolutely such).",[]],"reflm:5834":["A *compositum* is (g one) the same thing together with several things conjoined. If the conjunction of several has been made a number of times, the positing of the same is a *quantum*.\nA *compositum*, so far as it is not a part, is a *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5835":["A *quantum* is one and the same with many (s homogeneous) (taken together). This, as *totum*, contains *omnitudo* (allness).",[]],"reflm:5836":["A *compositum* is that which is represented through the conjunction of several. If these several are homogeneous, it is a *quantum*; if it is the *omnitudo* of the several, it is a *totum*; thus *pars* (part) belongs to the *compositum*, to the *quantum* and to the *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5837":["Cognate concepts, which represent several things posited in one: *compositum*, *quantum*, *totum*; each has parts. In the first I begin from it as unity; in the second I determine another unity through its repetition.",[]],"reflm:5838":["Allness (*totalitas*) is the greatest, not infinity. Unity (as homogeneous part of a magnitude) is so far the smallest, and absolute unity the absolutely smallest. The simple has no magnitude at all, and is also not a homogeneous part of a *quantum*. [abs]",[]],"reflm:5839":["Not every *quantum* can be regarded as a *totum*.",[]],"reflm:5840":["*Unitas multitudinis est omnitudo* (the unity of a multitude is allness). *Totalitas est omnitudo synthetica* (totality is synthetic allness) (collective).\nIt is the category of allness in the synthesis of magnitude, or of aggregation.\nOf the absolute All.",[]],"reflm:5841":["If I do not look to the homogeneity of the many, then *simplex*, *compositum*, *totum* stand in contrast with the *quantum*. *Unum, pluralitas et omnitudo* (one, plurality and allness).",[]],"reflm:5842":["In and  M 45:\nIf the several are homogeneous, it is a *quantum*∗; a real *totum* (in which there is no ideal but a real connection) is a *compositum*. The one that has arisen through repetition of the same is a *quantum*; that which has arisen through addition or conjunction of several is a *compositum*.\nM 45':\n∗(g Every *quantum* is thought as *compositum*, not conversely; for the parts can be heterogeneous.)",[]],"reflm:5843":["A thing can be understood as *compositum* (in a series), but without totality (of the aggregate). Thus the concept of the *compositum* is not yet that of a *totum*; in the *compositum* homogeneity is not required, but it is in a *quantum*. The *totum* is always regarded as a *quantum* according to a certain concept.\nTotality belongs to the concept of a *compositum* as homogeneous, i.e. as *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:5844":["A *quantum*, through whose concept of quantity the multitude of the |\nparts is determined, is a *discretum*; one through whose concept of quantity the multitude of the parts is in itself undetermined is a *continuum*.",[]],"reflm:5845":["[Spatium] A *quantum* all of whose possible parts are *quanta* is a *continuum*. Therefore it is what does not consist of simples. Therefore it is that in which the number of the parts is in itself undetermined. Therefore every *phaenomenon* [is a *continuum*].",[]],"reflm:5846":["Opposite and \nThe *quantum* in which alone all quantity can be determined is undetermined with regard to the multitude of the parts, and is a *continuum*. Space and time.",[]],"reflm:5847":["Every *quantum* is a *compositum*, [which] whose parts are all homogeneous with it; consequently it is a *continuum* and does not consist of simple parts. Consequently, not every *compositum* is a *continuum*, but every *quantum* is.",[]],"reflm:5848":["Every *quantum* has a quality (*continuum*), and every *quale* has a quantity (degree).",[]],"reflm:5849":["Every *quantum* (g *continuum*) as such is that through which a multitude [of] homogeneous parts is posited; consequently it necessarily precedes composition. The composition that precedes the multitude gives the *quantum discretum*.",[]],"reflm:5850":["Since the measure of magnitude is itself magnitude, all magnitude can be determined only relatively; and in the case of *phaenomena* it is also only a [representation] concept of the way in which representations can be composed through a common unity. There is no concept of abso|lute\nmagnitude, except where the whole is already determined through the concept [itself] of the thing; only metaphysical perfection is absolute magnitude.",[]],"reflm:5851":["Extensive magnitude is the multitude of the homogeneous in an object, or in what is posited through the object; it is therefore the magnitude of a whole and that of a ground. That something is a ground is quality.\nUnity, possibility, truth have extensive magnitude in the plural, intensive in the singular.",[]],"reflm:5852":["The magnitude of a *quantum* as multitude (given) is extensive. The magnitude of unity is intensive, i.e. degree. (s magnitude of the aggregate, magnitude of the ground.)",[]],"reflm:5853":["All objects as objects of intuition have extensive magnitude; as grounds of sensation, intensive magnitude.",[]],"reflm:5854":["Threefold way in which things determine existence. Thus (g real) relation: first, of the subject; second, of the cause; third, of the union of substances in a whole.\nThe category of relation (of the unity of consciousness) is the foremost among all. For unity properly concerns only relation; hence relation constitutes the content of judgments in general, and alone can be thought as determined a priori.\nOn the section on *substantia* (g the relation of the realities). A category is the concept through which an object in general is regarded as determined with re|gard\nto a logical function of judgments in general (i.e. of the objective unity [of] in the consciousness of the manifold); that is, I must think the manifold of its intuition through one of these moments of the understanding.\nThere are [only] three logical functions [of] under a certain heading, and hence also three categories, because [the] two of them show the unity [of consciousness [of] two *oppositorum*] of consciousness in two *opposita*, while the third again connects the consciousness on both sides. More kinds of unity of consciousness cannot be thought. For let a be a consciousness which connects a manifold, b another which [dasse] connects it in the opposite way: then c is the connection of a and b.\n*Judicia infinita*. »*Anima est non mortalis*« means not merely that A belongs under the sphere non-B, but under the sphere C outside B, which restricts and bounds B; it therefore means a limitation of the proposition »*a est b*«.",[]],"reflm:5855":["The *per se esse* of a substance is not an *a se esse*.",[]],"reflm:5856":["In and \nThat whose existence is never a determination of another thing is substance. That whose concept is a determination of other things is, logically, subject or predicate.",[]],"reflm:5857":["A *realitas* whose *esse* is *inesse* is [subst] an accident; one whose *esse* is *subsistere* is a *substantia*.",[]],"reflm:5858":["In and \nEverything can be thought as subject or predicate, but not everything can exist as subject.",[]],"reflm:5859":["Relation and modality belong to the consideration of the nature of beings, quantity and quality to the doctrine of essence.",[]],"reflm:5860":["In substance there is: 1. the relation of inherence (accident); 2. that of causality (force); 3. that of *commercium* (influence).",[]],"reflm:5861":["The sensible concept of sustentation (the bearer) is a misunderstanding. Accidents are only the way in which a substance exists, in respect of the positive.",[]],"reflm:5862":["We cannot think up any fundamental force, or know its possibility a priori, e.g. impenetrability.",[]],"reflm:5863":["*De commercio*.\nIt lies in the concept of space that one substance of the world cannot flow into another without suffering from it.",[]],"reflm:5864":["Logic, which sets out objective rules for the use of the faculty of knowledge, and ethics, which does the same with regard to the faculty of desire (the ought), presuppose for both only capacities |\nof the mind. Psychology, which explains what happens and does not prescribe what ought to happen, is concerned with forces of the mind.",[]],"reflm:5865":["*Unum plurium coniunctorum* (one of several conjoined things) is, by reason of the combination, a *compositum* and, so far as it is no part of a larger *compositum*, a *totum*. Thus we can think something as a *compositum* without on that account regarding it as a *totum*, e.g. when we assume no limits or determinate magnitude for it at all.",[]],"reflm:5866":["*Complexus*, comprehension [Zusammenfassung], is not a *compositum obiective tale* (objectively such).",[]],"reflm:5867":["To subdivide [*Eintheilen*] is not to divide [*theilen*].",[]],"reflm:5868":["Among all *coniuncta* only that is to be called a *compositum* which is thought through the combination of a manifold of which one member is possible without the other.\nA one made of many that mutually determine one another (*unum plurium se mutuo determinantium*) is a *compositum*.\nA one made of many that mutually influence one another (*unum plurium in se mutuo influentium*) is a *compositum substantiale*.",[]],"reflm:5869":["An ideal *compositum* is any *quantum* whose parts do not precede the possibility of the *compositum* (s but whose parts already presuppose the whole as given), e.g. space.\nA real [*compositum*] is one whose parts are in *commercium*. Therefore the relation of cause to caused is not composition.\nThe *compositum* made of parts which can also exist outside their combination with others is real.\nWe cannot call the *accidentia* parts. For their *nexus* is inherence in a third, not an *aggregatum* with one another.",[]],"reflm:5870":["On the possibility of a change.\nA thing arises and passes away; a state begins and ceases. A thing whose state begins and ceases is changed; and what is changed remains.",[]],"reflm:5871":["We can observe alternation only in what persists. If everything flows, even the flowing itself cannot be perceived. Experience of arising and passing away is therefore possible only through what persists. Therefore [Subst] there is something in nature that remains (neither arises nor passes away), and this is substance. Only the *accidentia* alternate. *Principium* of the possibility of experiences. Place designates the substance. In different places there are different substances; [die accide] what adheres to the persistent in one place and is distinguished from the persistent is an *accidens*.",[]],"reflm:5872":["*Nihil in mundo oritur ex nihilo* (nothing in the world arises from nothing).\n*Nihil in mundo annihilatur* (nothing in the world is annihilated).\nNo experience of this is possible.\n(s propositions of the persistence of substance.)",[]],"reflm:5873":["We can know things only through predicates; hence arising and passing away [can be known] only as predicates. Now one can have no concept of something as predicate without a subject; so the subject must remain through everything that passes away. Otherwise we cannot know the passing away itself through experience.",[]],"reflm:5874":["*Partes compositi realis sunt in commercio* (the parts of a real *compositum* are in *commercium*).\nThreefold relation: of the *accidens* to the substance, of the effect to the cause, and of the parts in a composite to one another.",[]],"reflm:5875":["Above, beside and \nThe only thing that is known a posteriori in appearances is their matter, or the real in them, namely what corresponds to sensation. The formal element of appearances, space and time, is known a priori and is pure intuition. Experience requires, besides the [appearance] intuition, also the real in general and the unity of the manifold of intuition.",[]],"reflm:5876":["69. E II 414. 355.\n M 69':\nOf space and time.\nIt is so far from being the case that the sensible intuitions of space and time are confused representations, that they rather furnish the most distinct modes of knowledge of all, namely the mathematical. And that they are the forms of sensible intuition makes it comprehensible how mathematical modes of knowledge of [appearances a priori] things a priori are possible. This would 1. not hold if the objects of the senses were things in themselves, and 2. not hold either if appearances were nothing other than indistinct intuitions of things; for then our knowledge of appearances would always be derived only a posteriori, since the form of them would lie not in our senses but in the things.\nM 69:\nThe mathematical properties of matter, e.g. infinite divisibility, prove that space and time belong not to the properties of things but to those of the representations of things in sensible intuition; for since composition is what is essential to these representations, if I remove it nothing (and so nothing simple either) remains.\nSpace is not a concept a priori but an intuition, which precedes the concept. For whence should synthetic judgments a priori come? And what sort of object would be represented thereby, since space as yet contains no object?\nSpace is itself a synthesis a priori.",[]],"reflm:5877":["A composite (*compositum*) is either formal (*formale*), which contains nothing but the form of composition, e.g. of sensitive intuition (*intuitus sensitivi*) [this]; or real (*reale*), and this is either a real *phaenomenon* or a real *noumenon*.",[]],"reflm:5878":["Between M § 230 and 231:\n*Phaenomena*, so far as they are outside one another at the same time, are in *commercio* (community).",[]],"reflm:5879":["The (concept of) space is itself only a form of composition; so if this is removed, everything is removed and nothing remains.",[]],"reflm:5880":["In and \nThat from which, when the thought (*compositio*) is removed, the object itself is removed, is mere appearance.",[]],"reflm:5881":["In and \n*Continuum* — *discretum per se* (discrete in itself).",[]],"reflm:5882":["A composite (*compositum*) is such either originally (*originarie*) or derivatively (*derivative*). The former is that from which, when all composition is removed, nothing remains, and whose division precedes composition. That, on the other hand, whose composition precedes all division is derivative. That whose division is wholly quantitative, i.e. into homogeneous parts, |\nis a *continuum*; that whose division is heterogeneous, into simple parts (not *quanta*), is qualitative.",[]],"reflm:5883":["Space, time and sensation.",[]],"reflm:5884":["*Respectus* (relation) of coordination in what is simultaneous, of subordination: what is successive, both in time. In the first, the *respectus* of extraposition.",[]],"reflm:5885":["Space and time are *composita idealia* (ideal composites), neither of substances nor of accidents, but of relations, which precede things.",[]],"reflm:5886":["In and \nSpace contains the form of all coordination in intuition, time that of all subordination.",[]],"reflm:5887":["The magnitude of a multitude is extensive.",[]],"reflm:5888":["Everything that occupies a part of time or of space has extensive magnitude, extension and alteration. Every reality has its degree between a and 0, infinitely small breaks off.",[]],"reflm:5889":["All division is either quantitative or qualitative. The latter is separation (Scheidung); both are either *divisio immediata* (immediate division): breaking up (Zerfällung), or *mediata* (mediate): dissolution (Zersetzung), *decompositio* into mediate parts. All dividing is either [logical] ideal or real. The ideal is logical or mathematical. The former is either of the sphere or of the content. The real is that of a composite substance.",[]],"reflm:5890":["A given *quantum* (g in space and time) which is nevertheless *illimitatum* (unlimited) is impossible. Space is infinite progressively, but not collectively.",[]],"reflm:5891":["The *quantitas* (quantity) of an aggregate is extensive, that of *ratio* (*rationis*) intensive: *gradus* (degree).",[]],"reflm:5892":["Allness (totality) as a collective concept cannot be thought of space and time. For there multiplicity is only successive addition, and the completeness (*completudo*) of it is never possible. On the other hand it can well be thought of a thing that is thought through pure concepts of reason: *illimitatum*.",[]],"reflm:5893":["Beside and \nThe greatest and unrestricted is unique (s not (it does not comprehend All in itself)). Absolute totality is what is in a certain respect unrestricted.\nThe progress in the construction of a magnitude is either finite or infinite. Both concern not the magnitude of the thing but that of the measurement, and hold only of appearances. The infinite is never given, but only the condition of the possibility of the *progressus in infinitum* (progress to infinity) or *indefinitum* (to the indefinite).\n(s *infinitudo* is not the idea of *omnitudo* (allness), nor of the *maximum*, nor of totality.)\nThe infinity of the possible or actual addition.",[]],"reflm:5894":["Negation of lack =0 can be regarded as an infinitely small degree, but not negation of the contradiction of concepts (opposition of quality).",[]],"reflm:5895":["In and \nThe infinite in a thing in itself is not impossible, but the infinite of a given appearance is, because if the representation is not given here as a whole, the thing too is not given. For the object here is only representation.",[]],"reflm:5896":["The totality of that which can be thought only through a *progressus* (progress) into the infinite is impossible. What is thought merely through a concept of the understanding as a *quantum* can also be represented as given infinite; for it is given prior to the *progressus*.",[]],"reflm:5897":["What is given only through composition is [therefore] always finite, although the composition goes on to infinity.",[]],"reflm:5898":["Space and time are both nothing but composites of sensible impressions. This composition goes on to infinity, but is never infinite. The magnitude of space presupposes the magnitude of time.\nInfinity of division. In infinite time, the infinitely small.",[]],"reflm:5899":["*Indefinitum* is a general concept which is indeterminate in respect of magnitude. A *quantum singulare* (single quantum) is not *indefinitum*.",[]],"reflm:5900":["A *quantum infinitum* (infinite quantum) in itself may be possible, but not as *phaenomenon*, since here the *progressus in infinitum* is thought as completely given. Hence there is no infinite whole, and no infinite multitude of parts of a [whole] given whole.",[]],"reflm:5901":["If, because the line ab can be divided to infinity, it also actually consists of infinitely many parts alongside one another, then the line af too, because infinitely many parts can be laid off along it, must actually consist of infinitely many parts; and if the first does not follow, the second cannot follow. Further, the line ab can be traversed in a finite time, and to each little part of time there can correspond a velocity with which the space ae or ef is traversed. Hence, if that infinite multitude of parts were actual, the traversing of an infinite space in a finite time would also have to be possible.\nIf all parts of the possible division (g at once) were actual, there would be an absolute magnitude of space, determinable from the multitude of all its parts. But since an infinite multitude requires an infinite time to know it as a whole, an infinite time would have to be capable of being given as a whole.",[]],"reflm:5902":["A thing in itself does not depend on our representations, and can therefore be much greater than our representations reach. But appearances are themselves only representations, and the magnitude of them, i.e. the idea of their generation through the *progressus*, cannot be greater than this *progressus*. And since this is never given as infinite, but is possible only to infinity, the magnitude of the world as appearance is also not infinite, but the *progressus* in it goes to infinity.",[]],"reflm:5903":["If space and time were properties of things in themselves, it would not follow from their being mathematically infinite (i.e. from the progress in them, so far as they are given as infinite wholes, being greater than every number) that they are impossible, but only that they are incomprehensible to us. But space and time are not things in themselves, and their magnitude is not given in itself but only through the *progressus*. Now a *progressus in infinitum* that has been given as a whole is a contradiction, |\nand so an *infinitum mathematicum datum* (a given mathematical infinite) is impossible, while a *quantum in infinitum dabile* (a quantum that can be given to infinity) is possible. From this it does not follow, however, that space and time have limits in themselves, for that too is impossible, but only that they are not things in themselves at all, and always have limits only where our thoughts and representations come to a stop.",[]],"reflm:5904":["In the same way nature is not the order of things in themselves, but the order which the understanding sets to appearances. Of things in themselves I can say that they are not subject to the order of things; and of our understanding, that it makes appearances possible in accordance with the order of nature, but not as determined by it.",[]],"reflm:5905":["In the *noumenon* I can think quantity only through the relation to the *illimitatum* (the unlimited), that is, to that which contains *omnitudinem* (allness), as the standard of measure, and indeed only through mere negations. The *ens realissimum* (most real being) is a determinate *quantum*, but not the *infinitum*, since a *quantum* is not determined in itself, as to how great it is, by the impossibility of measuring it completely. Conversely, the *maximum* is for that reason not also *infinitum*. But it is nevertheless a *totum absolute tale* (a whole that is absolutely of that kind). In *phaenomenis* there is no absolute totality.\nThe *infinitum reale* (of things (g of existence) in general), in which everything is, is Spinoza's concept. Limitation is there where, in space, the boundary is. There being comes first, and the determination of it afterwards.",[]],"reflm:5906":["*Argumentum* on the objective reality of time.\nIt was objected that the unknown something x, which at one time produces in me the appearance of the egg, produces at another time the appearance of the chick; hence something in the object must have changed, since it cannot contain the ground of two opposed determinations at the same time. I answer: It is the same object which produces the ground of the appearance of two opposed states as existing successively, and therefore the appearance of a change. This is no harder to explain than how [appearance] change is possible, that is, how a thing, or a multitude of things, can contain the ground of two opposites. |",[]],"reflm:5907":["Leibniz's proposition is that numerical diversity (*numerica diversitas*) must also be specific diversity (*specifica diversitas*), and conversely that total specific identity (*specifica identitas totalis*) must also be numerical. This latter proposition holds of an object of pure reason; e.g. an *ens realissimum* is *specifice totaliter idem* with another *realissimum*. Were it in space and time, numerical identity, namely that it is a single being, would not follow from this.\nAppearances are different things merely through difference of places; for difference of places precedes a priori and, irrespective of the quality of the things, [lets] makes numerical difference.\nOn the other hand, the (g specific) difference of things in things in themselves, or the difference of qualities, always makes different things. But with *phaenomenis* it can be, and indeed must be, one and the same thing which is differently determined at different times; so here the rule is: *numero idem potest esse specifice diversum*, if by this one understands every determination through different quality.",[]],"reflm:5908":["Difference of places makes difference of things, because places in space, with all inner identity, are nevertheless different, as being situated outside one another.",[]],"reflm:5909":["The parts of time do not determine one another reciprocally, but |\nonly the preceding is determining. Hence time has only one dimension.\nEverything that determines one another mathematically in space and time does not for that reason determine one another dynamically.",[]],"reflm:5910":["The proposition of contingency means to say that everything is necessary, either in itself or as *causatum alterius* (effect of another) (for the *causatum* too is necessary). But this proposition holds only of this: that I can know a priori the existence of a thing, [never otherwise,] if I can think its non-being, only by means of a cause; e.g. a motion that is not known through any experience. — But what is properly contingent is recognised not so much [in de] in the alteration as in the form of purposiveness. This, in matter (since it has no life), must have a cause that is distinct from it.",[]],"reflm:5911":["We know the things in the world only through the relation that they have to a possible event; that is, as causes and according to their forces.",[]],"reflm:5912":["Our concept of a contingent thing is the concept of a thing that is possible only as *causatum alterius*, not of one whose non-being is impossible, that is, unthinkable. Hence the proposition *quodlibet contingens est causatum alterius* is tautological; e.g. we call the existence of organised beings contingent. For we can know real possibility only through the object's being given in some manner. If we wish to know it a priori, the object must be known not through (empirical) intuition but, as existence, through something else that determines a priori, that is, a cause.",[]],"reflm:5913":["If something is contingent in itself, it is so far represented merely through understanding, and must have a cause. But if something is given merely in a contingent way (in appearance, as something that alters), the thing itself is not on that account contingent.\n(s To infer from one's concept of a thing to the necessity or contingency of its existence is to leap out of oneself.)",[]],"reflm:5914":["93. E II 893.\n*Principium contingentiae* signifies that, properly, nothing exists as absolutely contingent; that is, that its existence must be known as objectively determined if it is subjectively determined in perception. — (g Everything is necessary, either absolutely or hypothetically; |\nM 93: |\nlikewise nothing is absolutely necessary, but only in relation to the possibility of the objects of experience.)\nM 93':\n(g *Contingens* is either, in the logical sense, that whose non-being can be thought, or that whose non-being is possible; the latter we cannot know. It requires |\nM 93:|\nknowledge of the necessary.)",[]],"reflm:5915":["That something exists cannot be known through reason, if it is contingent. [If it] Through experience it also cannot be known as existing (objectively), except in so far as the perception of it is necessary according to empirical laws. In a word: the objectively valid and the necessarily valid are one and the same. What I am to say of the object must be necessary. For if it is contingent, it holds only in the subject, but not of the object.",[]],"reflm:5916":["*Leges pertinent ad existentia, item mutabilitas.* (Laws pertain to existents, likewise mutability.)",[]],"reflm:5917":["Every thing, considered according to its existence in itself (not as appearance), is an object of the understanding and not of mere experience; it must therefore be determined, according to principles of the understanding, as absolutely or hypothetically necessary. That whose existence is known only through the understanding is known a priori, and therefore as necessary.",[]],"reflm:5918":["In and \nThe contingent as such cannot be known a priori. But every object must be capable of being known a priori as such.\nWhat is given in experience is thereby thought under laws, and consequently as necessary.",[]],"reflm:5919":["The existence of a thing can be known as contingent or necessary only relatively to a concept which I have of it, never absolutely.",[]],"reflm:5920":["Everything that is, is necessary, either in itself or through a cause.",[]],"reflm:5921":["In and \nThat something is contingent with regard to a certain concept I can indeed know a priori, but not that it is contingent *in singulari* and *in individuo* (in the single case, in the individual).",[]],"reflm:5922":["We can know the contingency neither of things nor of the state, neither a priori nor a posteriori. For the non-being that follows upon existence does not prove that it could have taken the place of existence. But the principle of contingency holds only of the empirically contingent. What is persistent is empirically necessary;\n|\nwhat arises or perishes is empirically contingent. Hence every state has its cause, but not the substance; and this proposition holds only in the series of appearances, but not of these as things in themselves, in relation to something outside the series.",[]],"reflm:5923":["95'. E II 983.\nDeduction of the pure modes of knowledge a priori.\nIt is in experience alone that our concepts can be given altogether *in concreto*, and so their objective reality be fully exhibited. Concepts whose nature is opposed to being [so] exhibited in experience are mere ideas. Hence it is in relation to possible experience that the objective reality of all concepts [is to be sought], i.e. their meaning is to be sought. Others, namely those that are mere ideas, may indeed be assumed as hypotheses, but cannot count as demonstrable.\nNow when the issue is the possibility of pure knowledge *a priori*, we can transform the question into this: whether experience also contains (g sheer) knowledge that is given only *a posteriori*, or whether something is met with in it which is not empirical and yet contains the ground of the possibility of experience.\nFirst, there belongs to all experience [immediately] representation of the senses. Second, consciousness. This, when it is immediately combined with the former, is entitled empirical consciousness, and the representation [which] (g of the senses), combined with empirical consciousness, is entitled perception (*Warnehmung*). Were experience nothing more than an aggregation of perceptions, then [outside] in it nothing would be met with that was not of empirical origin.\nBut the consciousness of perceptions refers all representation only to ourselves, as modifications of our state. They are then separated from one another, and above all they are not modes of knowledge of any thing and relate to no object. They are therefore not yet experience, which must indeed contain empirical representation, but must contain it at the same time as knowledge of the objects of the senses.\nM 95':\nIf we ask logic what may be entitled knowledge in general, then a concept is [that which] a representation (g or sum of representations) that has been referred to an object and designates it. And in combining (separating) one concept with another in a judgment, we think something of the object that has been designated by a given concept, i.e. we know it, in that we judge about it. All knowledge, therefore also that of experience, consists of judgments; and concepts themselves are representations prepared for possible judgments, in that they represent something in general, which has been given, as knowable through a predicate.\nThus experience is [are] possible only through judgments. In these, perceptions indeed constitute the empirical materials, but the [form of the] relation of these to an object, and knowledge of the object through perceptions, cannot depend on empirical consciousness alone.\nThe form of every judgment, however, consists in the objective unity of the consciousness of the given concepts, i.e. in the consciousness that these must belong to one another, and thereby designate an object in whose (g complete) representation they are always met with together.\nBut this necessity of combination is not a repre|sentation of empirical origin [and yet], but presupposes a rule which must be given [is] *a priori*, i.e. a unity of consciousness which has place *a priori*. This unity of consciousness is contained in the moments of the understanding in judging, and only that is object in relation to which unity of consciousness of the manifold representations is thought [a prio] *a priori*.",[]],"reflm:5924":["The general proposition is: Synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* from mere concepts are impossible; they are possible, however, 1. from the construction of concepts, 2. from rules which contain the possibility of experience and through which perceptions become objective modes of knowledge. For it is in experience alone that pure modes of knowledge *a priori* get their meaning and use.",[]],"reflm:5925":["NB. Synthetic (g pure) modes of knowledge from concepts which cannot serve for the determination of the objects of experience in general, such as the transcendental ideas are, have the meaning that they determine the limit of all knowledge of experience, i.e. show that this is never self-sufficient and complete. Consequently, in every respect something must be assumed that lies at the ground of experience; but we can know this only so far as it lies at the ground of experience, and are entitled to think it hypothetically only so far as practical propositions that go beyond the grounds of experience require it.\nThe ground why we can know something *a priori* is that the objects of [experience] perception are appearances. The cause why we cannot know them completely is that we would then not take them as objects of experience, since this is never | complete, and if we do so take them, we always contradict ourselves.",[]],"reflm:5926":["Space and time are themselves nothing other than forms of the composition of the objects of sensation; hence too, if one removes all composition there, nothing remains. Now the unity of consciousness in this composition, so far as that unity is considered (g as) universal, is the concept of the understanding; and that unity belongs to experience as objective knowledge. Concepts of the understanding, therefore, are likewise required *a priori* for the possibility of experience. Something, then, must precede experience itself*, whereby it itself becomes possible; but it is in experience alone that all knowledge *a priori* must have its reality.\n*(g For the logical form of the understanding in judging must indeed precede, and appearances [must, as determined] (as mere representations) must be regarded as determined in respect of each of these forms; otherwise no experience can arise from them. We may also put the word experience in place of the words objects of the senses. For things in themselves we do not know; we can know nothing of them except all the experience of them that is possible to us, and this so far as it is determined *a priori* by the form of sensibility and that of the understanding.)",[]],"reflm:5927":["It is absolutely impossible to know anything synthetically *a priori* of things in themselves; such knowledge is possible only of appearances, because synthetic judgments require intuitions, either pure or empirical, and synthetic judgments *a priori* require pure intuitions. This [are] is possible, however, only as a form of our sensibility [because we], and holds only of appearances, not of things in themselves.\nCategory is the representation of the relation of the manifold of intuition to a universal consciousness (to the universality of consciousness, which is properly objective). The relation of representations to the universality of consciousness, and consequently the (g transformation of the empirical and particular) unity of the [particular Bewus empirical] consciousness, which is merely subjective, into a consciousness that |\nis universal and objective, belongs to logic. This unity of consciousness, so far as it is universal and can be represented *a priori*, is the pure concept of the understanding. This can therefore be nothing other than the universal of the unity of consciousness, which constitutes the objective validity of a judgment.\nThe manifold, so far as it is represented as necessarily belonging to one consciousness (or also to the unity of consciousness in general), is thought [as belonging] through the concept of an object; the object is always a something in general. The determination of the object rests merely on the unity of the manifold of its intuition, and indeed on the universally valid unity of the consciousness of it.\nTwo elements of knowledge have place *a priori*: 1. intuitions; 2. unity of consciousness of the manifold of intuitions (even of the empirical). This unity of consciousness constitutes the form of experience as objective empirical knowledge.",[]],"reflm:5928":["Synthetic propositions through concepts are at all times *a priori* and impossible. But through the construction of concepts (in sensible formal intuition in general), or through the combination of universality with empirical synthesis in general, they are not only possible but also necessary. For experience is nothing other than the synthetic [knowledge] combination of perceptions in one consciousness (as containing necessity, and hence universality).",[]],"reflm:5929":["If I think something through the understanding as contingent (g in itself), I cannot think that same thing through the understanding as existing without a cause. (But why must I think it as existing through the understanding, why not rather through experience?) (g What does contingency designate?)\nIf I wish to think a thing through the understanding, something must thereby be thought as ultimate subject, to which everything else belongs as predicate |\n(but what designates for me the ultimate subject?). If I represent a thing as [multiplicity] an aggregate of many homogeneous items, I must think it as a magnitude; but what is given to me as magnitude, prior to the multiplicity that is thought in it? Every thing has reality; but how is its difference from 0 possible to be thought?\nAll this indicates that our understanding does indeed have laws of its own for thinking something, but that we can give these thoughts application and meaning only through sensible intuition, which we bring under the condition of the unity of consciousness of the manifold. It indicates also that, in the end, the reality of the concepts of experience lies only in experience, and indeed in experience in general as such. Experience, without those concepts, would be a mere aggregate of perceptions; those concepts, on the other hand, without experience [diese], would be a mere manifold *modus* (mode) of the thoroughgoing consciousness of oneself in the manifold consciousness of the representations.",[]],"reflm:5930":["The objective unity of consciousness of the manifold of representations is the connection of that manifold either with one and the same concept, e.g. all men [or] (in a word: a universally valid combination (g of the concepts) in one consciousness); and then the unity is entitled logical. Or else this logical unity of consciousness is regarded as determined in the concept of a thing and constitutes its concept; that is the synthetic or transcendental unity of consciousness. In the former case the unity is represented [distributively], and concerns merely the relation of the [representations] concepts; in the latter it is the unity which itself constitutes a concept of the thing, through the union of its manifold in one consciousness. E.g. [All] Many [men] things are outside one another; and, on the other hand, space is one, and comprehends many things outside one another. The former is the quantity [of one] of the concept of a judgment; the latter is the concept [of] a thing as a *quantum*.",[]],"reflm:5931":["A category is the (g necessary) unity of consciousness (g in the composition) of the manifold of representations (g intuition), in so far as it makes possible the concept of an object in general (in contrast |\nto the [sub] merely subjective unity of consciousness of perceptions). This unity in the categories must be necessary. E.g. Logically, a concept can be subject or predicate. An object, however, considered transcendentally, presupposes something that is necessarily merely subject, and something else that is merely predicate. — Ground — Logically, something can be reciprocally (*reciproce*) ground and consequence; but really (*realiter*), everything that stands in community of time must stand in community. In the case of a magnitude there is necessarily unity of the many.\nTo experience belong: 1. intuition (the manifold); 2. connection of the manifold in one (g empirical) consciousness; 3. unity of the connection of the manifold, which is universally valid. Intuition is given a priori (the progress in it is also finite, as is the unity of the connection).",[]],"reflm:5932":["Through the category I represent to myself an object (g in general) as determined in respect of the logical functions of judgments: of the subject (not predicate), of the consequence as ground, of the multiplicity in its representation. But why must I represent every object as determined in respect, not of one only, but of all logical functions in judgments? Because only thereby is objective unity of consciousness, i.e. universally valid connection of perceptions, possible, and hence experience as the sole reality of modes of knowledge.\nThis unity of consciousness (g of the connection of our representations) is given a priori in us as the ground of all concepts, just as the form of appearance is given as the ground of intuitions. But both hold only of human knowledge, and have objective meaning only in respect of it; indeed the |\ncategory could have no meaning a priori if there were no intuitions a priori.\nThe category, then, is the concept of an object in general, in so far as it is determined in itself a priori in respect of a logical function of judgments (so that one must think the combination of the manifold in its representation [by no other] through this function).\nAll objects which we are to think must be determined in respect of all the logical functions of the understanding; for only thereby can we think. And it is in that something [is represented as determined in respect of these functions], that thinking in general is determined (how it is to be thought), that it is an object, i.e. something to which a particular thought, distinct from others, corresponds.\nAll principles [of the from] of the pure understanding relate to sensibility, and indicate the conditions under which the representation of sensibility belongs under a category. They therefore determine the rule of judgments in general in respect of appearances, and are principles of possible experience; for without the categories our representations could not be related to objects, since the categories alone determine thinking in general in relation to something in general.",[]],"reflm:5933":["The unity of consciousness [of the representations in] of the manifold in the representation of an object in general is judgment. The representation of an object in general, in so far as it is determined in respect of this objective unity of consciousness [determined] (the logical unity), is category.\nThe unity of consciousness is either empirical: in the perception of the manifold, connected by imagination. Or it is logical: the unity in the representation of the object. The former is contingent and merely subjective; the latter is necessary and objective. The former is required for concepts, the latter for judgments and for their possibility in general. The schematism indicates the conditions under which |\nan appearance is determined in respect of the logical function, and so stands under a category; the transcendental principles indicate the categories under which the schemata of sensibility stand.",[]],"reflm:5934":["100. E II 984. 408.\n On M § 100':\nExperience is knowledge (g a posteriori, i.e.) of that which is [in] the object of sensation (a posteriori). Sensations yield no knowledge at all; so something (g a priori) must be added to them [so f] if experience is to become possible. To the representation a posteriori only that a priori from concepts can be added, and this can only be the connection (synthesis) [se a priori], in so far as it is determined a priori (for the mere comparison of sensations yields nothing but sensation, and no object).\nThe universal (g formal) principle of possible experience is therefore: *all perceptions [stand] are determined a priori in respect of their connection in one consciousness (for consciousness is unity, in which alone the connection of all perceptions is possible, and, if it is to be knowledge of the object, must be determined a priori). The objective unity in the consciousness of different representations is the form of judgment. Therefore all perceptions, in so far as they are to constitute experience, stand under the formal conditions of judgments in general, and the [de] determination of them through this function is the concept of the understanding. [Experience therefore] All experiences, as possible perceptions, stand a priori under concepts of the understanding, through which alone they can become empirical knowledge, i.e. representation of objects (a posteriori).\nOn M § 100:\n*(g All appearances are determinable a priori in respect of their connection [through that in respect of the] in accordance with the unity of consciousness in all judgments in general, i.e. they stand under categories.\nSpace and time are the forms of combination in intuition, and serve to apply the categories *in concreto*.)",[]],"reflm:5935":["All synthetic modes of knowledge from mere concepts relate only to things as appearances, never to things in themselves, and to the former only in so far as they are intuitions (mathematics), or as belonging to a possible experience.\nWe can represent to ourselves nothing a priori except that of which we ourselves contain the grounds in our faculty of representation, either in sensibility or in the understanding: in the latter, either where the understanding merely determines a priori the unity of consciousness (theoretical), or where reason, conformably merely with the understanding, directs actions to objects (practical).",[]],"reflm:5936":["Ontology is the science of things in general, i.e. of the possibility of our knowledge of things a priori, i.e. independently of experience. It can therefore teach us nothing about things in themselves, but only about the conditions a priori under which we can know things [which to us] in experience in general; i.e. principles of the possibility of experience.",[]],"reflm:5937":["Apart from ontology, the cosmological antinomy (among pure ideas of reason), the psychological paralogisms and the theological ideal also belong to metaphysics. The last two are transcendent, but without contradiction. The cosmological ideas, however, conflict with one another. The psychological ideas assume the intellectual as empirically given; the theological assume the intellectual as given a priori. Existence from mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:5938":["The transcendental ideas serve to limit the principles of experience, so that these may not be extended to things in themselves, and to show that what is not at all an object of possible experience is for that reason no nonentity (*Unding*), and that [it outside of] experience is not sufficient to itself and to reason, but always goes further and so refers us away from itself.",[]],"reflm:5939":["Ideas. Through the cosmological ideas we want to know the absolute whole of the series of conditions in appearances. Through the psychological ideas we want to know the absolute constitution of an object of experience (g not in relation to the senses) as a thing in itself. Through the theological idea we want to know the existence of things from mere concepts (g without any experience); that is, we [want a] make for ourselves an idea which at the same time carries with it, inseparably, existence a priori.\nTo want to know the soul, as to what it is in itself, from inner sense is vain.",[]],"reflm:5940":["The world is a *totum finitorum* [whole of finite things], because one part is the complement (*complementum*) of another to the whole, and hence none of them is infinite; and secondly it is a whole *contingentium* [of contingent things], because one part depends on another.",[]],"reflm:5941":["*Mundus intelligibilis* [the intelligible world] is the absolute whole of substances *per se*; *mundus* [intelli] *sensibilis* [the sensible world] is the whole of phenomena (*phaenomena*).",[]],"reflm:5942":["In and \n*totum absolutum* (*qvod non est pars*) *substantiarum* [an absolute whole (which is not a part) of substances] is the world in the transcendental sense. World of sense and world of understanding.",[]],"reflm:5943":["*Mundus noumenon* (*intelligibilis*) is the idea of a whole of substances which is no further a part [of anything]. *Mundus phaenomenon* is the application of this idea to appearances.\nOf the *mundus noumenon* one can think several worlds outside one another; in the *phaenomenon* there is only a single one, through space and time. The *commercium* [community] of the substances of the first is possible only through *harmoniam praestabilitam* [pre-established harmony], 1. because many substances can be in community only through a common cause; 2. because this harmony would be no nature, were it not already laid into the creation of the things of the world, and thus pre-established.\nIn the *mundus sensibilis* physical influence (*influxus physicus*) holds. In the *mundus noumenon* all substances are intellectual; at least we cannot think them through any other predicates than those of representations. In the sensible world they are extended. Motion. The influence of the first and of the second is really nothing, for the changes of the second are only *phaenomena*. Bodies are not substances, and motion is not an accident of them, but both are only *phaenomena* of the intelligible.\n(g intelligible or intellectual.)",[]],"reflm:5944":["Over, beside and \nOne cannot say *corpus* [ph] *noumenon*, but *substantia composita noumenon* [composite substance as noumenon] or *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5945":["Over and \n*Harmonia absqve commercio* [harmony without community] is that which does not occur according to a universal rule of nature, and thus without the cause lying in the substances themselves.",[]],"reflm:5946":["Beside and \nWhether the state of the world does not always remain the same, while that of the things changes.",[]],"reflm:5947":["The question of the oneness (*unicitas*) of the world [is] concerns either copulative plurality or disjunctive plurality.",[]],"reflm:5948":["*Contingentia mundi* [the contingency of the world]. Everything contingent once was not (for this is the mark by which contingency is recognised); hence it is a consequence of something preceding, and hence has a cause. Only that is held to be contingent which is in a series that presupposes another.",[]],"reflm:5949":["The fourth antinomy. We need an absolute necessity, but we cannot arrive at any in the regress through the world. For causality must always begin, and such a cause is contingent. This antinomy is unavoidable if we regard time as a determination of things in themselves and appearances as things. If this is not so, there is nothing necessary in the time-series; but that which contains the ground of appearances is not in the time-series, and can therefore have causality without beginning and be necessary.",[]],"reflm:5950":["Whoever asserts the *regressus in infinitum* [regress to infinity] does not thereby yet assert an *infinitam seriem rationatorum datam* [an infinite series of consequences given], if they are phenomena; for these are given only in the regress.",[]],"reflm:5951":["Since the *regressus* goes *in infinitum*, the absolute totality of the world of sense is not to be thought.",[]],"reflm:5952":["In and \nThe magnitude of the world-whole as an aggregate or series (the latter in time, or the series of causes). The magnitude of the series of appearances is not different from the magnitude of the *progressus in infinitum*.",[]],"reflm:5953":["Opposite and \n*Regressus in infinitum absqve caussa prima* [regress to infinity without a first cause] is impossible among noumena, but among phenomena, which are not things but themselves subsist only in the regress, it is necessary.",[]],"reflm:5954":["In mundo [non] (*vniverso*) *non datur* (s *infinitum*) *abyssus* [In the world (the universe) there is no abyss], either of things, or also of empty time and space. One of the two is necessary if phenomena are to be represented in their absolute totality, that is, as given in themselves.\nThere is here no completion, neither in the infinite nor in the limit; but all sensibility has a cause outside itself, for it is not a thing in itself.",[]],"reflm:5955":["The proposition: In the world everything is nature, that is, *non datur hiatus* [there is no gap], in which something happens contrary to nature and is torn from its chain. We can explain events only according to determinate laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:5956":["In and \n*Casus* [chance] in the theoretical or teleological sense. If space and time attach to things in themselves, then their causality | \nand that of their cause must also be determined in time, and hence be naturally necessary, and so nothing unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:5957":["*non datur hiatus* [there is no gap], for otherwise we should have to perceive time and space in themselves. *Vacuum metaphysicum non datur* [there is no metaphysical void].",[]],"reflm:5958":["No absolutely empty space (for there would be no outer relations). No empty time; for nothing can begin absolutely. No leap, no chance, no fate.",[]],"reflm:5959":["123. E II 1393. 1438.\nM 123':\n*Abyssus*, *Saltus*, *Casus*, *Fatum* (s *Hiatus*: *vacuum intermedium*; *circumfusum*) [abyss, leap, chance, fate (gap: the intervening void; the surrounding void)] are all of them concepts of the unconditioned, which can be thought in a *mundus* [phaenomeno] *noumeno*, or at least in connection with it, but which do not fit the *mundus phaenomenon*.\nThe absolute totality of composition must be thought in the *mundus noumenon*; in the *phaenomenon* the world-whole (g as) given (g) according to space and time would be an *abyssus*, and the like cannot be given. In the *noumenon* [there is] the absolute totality of division; in the case of the *phaenomenon* this would yield a *saltus*. In the first, where the condition of time does not attach to existence and there is no change that requires a cause, freedom can be thought; in the second it would be *casus*. |\nIn the *noumenon* a being necessary for itself can be thought as cause of the world. In the [world] I can, according to its laws, think no other relation of the cause of the world to the world [ders] than one in time, and there causality is always contingent, and hence the cause itself.\n(g In the world of sense all (g synthesis) unity is only that of the regress. Without limits nothing is given in it. Thus also [not] |\nM 123: |\nin space and time, which are unbounded, is anything given with an absolute totality of synthesis, anything unconditioned.)",[]],"reflm:5960":["The totality of composition in the (g world of sense) world, and that of division, are both false and must be so, because it is taken in space and time.\nBut the totality of cause and effect*, likewise of necessity, can both be true, because the one can be considered in the intelligible world and the other wholly outside every world, in the intelligible, and hence outside all dependence, that is, contingency.\n*(g for these concepts do not necessarily belong to objects of the senses, if one namely wants to know nothing through them, but only to have the possibility of a thought of them.)",[]],"reflm:5961":["123'. E II 1401.\nM 123:\nThe whole dialectic comes to this. One insists on holding the (g world of) sense to be a thing in itself, although it can be thought only in space and time. Now as a thing in itself there must cer|tainly\nbe in it an absolute totality of conditions. But this is possible in space and time neither in composition, nor in decomposition, nor in respect of origination. — But if everything is merely phenomena, that is, representations, which can posit something only under the conditions of time, then |\nM 123': |\nneither the world nor anything in the world is given in itself, but everything is given only so far as the *progressus* reaches; and one contradicts oneself if one thinks a *totum* [abso] which is to be given in itself, and yet places it in space and time.",[]],"reflm:5962":["132'. 133'. 133. E II 376. 1386.\nM 131':\nIf space and time were regarded as conditions of the existence of the world in itself, and the *mundus noumenon* (noumenal world) were not distinguished from the *phaenomenon*, then God's causality in respect of the world would also be determined in time. God would accordingly belong to the world [the W], and [its] his causality would, through time, belong to a series of causes and effects. God would therefore be contingent, like everything that belongs with the world to one whole.\nThere is the possibility of reckoning one and the same subject, which belongs to the world of sense, among intelligible beings, in respect of that world [connection through] the categories (of cause and of existence in general). It could thus belong either to the intelligible world or to what is outside all worlds. This is properly the possibility of using the categories not merely in respect of the objects of the senses but for things in general. They would be used, however, only for something which we do not otherwise know, save that it is not appearance. This is quite possible, because it signifies only the possibility of thinking, not the possibility [to] of determining what is thought through predicates of the sensible world.\n1. The same being which, as a member of the world of sense, has causality [under the], and this causality is always conditioned [according to] under rules of time-determinations, can yet be unconditioned in respect of time. In that case it can be the free cause of the very same caused thing (*causatum*) in the world of sense; that is, it |\nstands not under the condition of time-determination, and yet belongs to a world together with the intelligible substratum of the *mundus noumenon*.\n2. Everything that [with the] belongs in time belongs to one (g and the same) world, even if it is assumed to be the cause of the things in it, and it exists contingently. For time does not necessarily require the existence of things, and substances that were necessary would in general belong to no whole and to no world. It is possible, however, to think its existence outside time, and so also without reckoning it to a world, and hence without robbing it of necessity. The category of existence is at least a concept that still always remains over, although it cannot be known determinately, e.g. as duration without time. — In a word: if space and time are regarded as properties of things, it is not possible to get out of the contradictions.\nM 132':\nThe reason why the first two antinomies are both false is that I had to lay at the basis of both of them a self-contradictory concept, namely that of a whole in space and time, which yet was supposed to be an absolute whole, and consequently a thing in itself. For the conflict concerns the composition and division of an intuition, which [always] must necessarily take place in space and time, because [this] these themselves make possible the concept of it as a magnitude. By contrast, causality and modality of existence — or better — Those antinomies concern the [possibility] inner possibility of appearances themselves (as *quanta*); the other antinomies concern their possibility through something else, and so the dependence or independence of their state or of their existence itself (that is, the conditioned or unconditioned possibility [of their] of existence). In order to answer the first questions I had to seek the conditions |\namong appearances alone, and these are here never complete. In order to answer the second I could seek the unconditioned outside the appearance, in the non-sensible (since it can be something else as correlate), and there both propositions could be true. — Thereby the ideality of space and time is proved indirectly, because contradictions with itself follow from the opposite. But I have also proved it directly, namely from this: that synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* exist; that these, however, are impossible without intuition *a priori* (pure); and that, finally, pure intuition is impossible where its form is not given in the subject prior to the object. Consequently we can anticipate only appearances, and hence all objects of the senses are mere appearances.\nFor the rest, the knowledge of God remains entrusted to the same grounds of sound understanding as before. Only the fanatical rashness of deciding about it by speculation is removed, whether this springs from ignorance of the capacity of pure reason or even from a presumption [of] to infer so high by analogy with the principles of experience; and the determination is wholly entrusted to morals.\nM 133:\nThe opposite of the proposition »the world is infinite in respect of space and time« is »it is not infinite (given)«, and of these two propositions the latter is true. But if one asks »what then is it, if it is not infinite in space or in time?«, one can answer in two ways. Either »it is finite in space and time«, and then one runs into nothing but absurdities; or »as a given whole it is not at all in space and in time«. For in neither can an absolute whole be given, because the world is a whole of phenomena, that is, of representations of the senses. These are given only in [experience] perception, not in themselves (although the ground of possible perceptions, the thing in itself, is given without perception). And since the progress of perceptions never has a limit possible [through] in perception, they permit a progress into |\ninfinity, which therefore never constitutes an absolute whole. The very same holds of division. These two propositions can both be false, because the one contains more than is required for contradiction. [They are a] That is the logical resolution of the antinomy. But they are also both false because they contain, along with the rest, an impossible condition, namely that the world be given whole in space and time (just (g as) a *compositum* given whole (g that is, as world)), and yet be given in space and time. For the first proposition is grounded on the presupposition that a whole of appearances is given in itself and outside the representations, which is contradictory. And that is the transcendental resolution of the antinomy.\nSecondly. The opposite of the proposition »all events (g things) in the (g sense-)world stand under the mechanism of natural causality« is »they do not stand under it«. Here the two propositions cannot both be false, but they could both be true, because the second contains less in itself than is required for contradictory opposition. For to that end it would have to be added that, as things of the world of sense, they do not stand under the mechanism in question. But it may still be the case that the things of the world of sense [also as], which are the causes of the events in it, could also be causes when considered as [things of an] intelligible beings. (For this can be thought without contradiction, and without the concept of cause thereby being knowledge.) So far as this is so, they could be exempted from the mechanism of natural necessity, without contradicting the fact that, as things of the world of sense, they belong to it.\nThe other proposition, that all things are contingent, because [it] they are determined in space and time as dependent on one another and alterable in respect of their existence, can be granted. And the opposing assertion does not conflict |\nwith it, namely that there could be some necessary being. For this being is then outside space and time, and hence outside all dependence on the world; rather, everything is to be posited as dependent on it.\nM 133:\nLeibniz's pre-established harmony is perhaps only the idea of an intelligible world without space and time. In this world the divine universal presence is the principle of the real *nexus*, as of an intelligent cause. Through it the relations in which finite beings intuit themselves (as the form of appearance) are already pre-established in creation [to agreement] in agreement with the rules, for thoroughgoing harmony with the intelligible world, in which alone immediate truth is.",[]],"reflm:5963":["God is in space either at rest, or moved, or both at once (like bodies on the earth), or neither of the two. Now the last is true; hence all the others, and consequently the disjunctive proposition made up of the first two, are false. The truth of the last is really the explanation of the falsity of the first two, for God is not at all in space.\nMatter (g in itself) consists either of infinitely many parts or of a determinate multitude of parts. Neither of the two is true, for each presupposes the totality of the division of space, and this presupposes the absolute reality of space.",[]],"reflm:5964":["125. E II 1382.\nM 125':\nWe can first consider a disjunctive proposition problematically, namely as if its disjunction were not yet settled; and then it runs: every thing is either A or B, or: both A and B, or: neither A nor B. [Everything] Something in the world is either nature or chance, or both nature and chance, or neither |\nnature nor chance. If both can be true, the opposition is not correct; if both can be false, the division is not complete.* The world either has a beginning (in time) or is from eternity. [Both cannot (g together) be true, so the opposition is correct; but the division is incomplete, because the condition of a] Since here a condition attaches to the world, namely time, which contradicts completeness, the division is not complete, and the world is neither eternal nor begun in time, because it is not a totality; for as appearance no totality is to be found. But if I say: the human soul is either, in its actions, under the mechanism of nature (g\n|\nM 125: |\nif I add 'merely', this is an exponible proposition) or it is free, or both together, or neither of the two, then the soul is taken in different respects, partly as *phaenomenon* (appearance), partly as *noumenon* (thing of thought), since I am conscious of its absolute self not merely as appearance, a priori. (I am: a proposition that is not empirical.) And there both propositions can be true, the opposition is not correct, and totality is not to be found in it as *phaenomenon*, but certainly as *noumenon* with regard to the *phaenomenon*. The flower is either red or blue or neither of the two: yellowish, or both of them: violet.\nM 125:\n*(g The opposition is correct, but the division is not complete. The actions of the human being are not merely nature, but by no means on that account chance; rather they are free, i.e. are also to be regarded as if they did not stand at all in the series of the determining grounds of appearance, but were determined a priori.\nFreedom is a faculty of determining oneself a priori to act, not through empirical causes. Nature and chance both place the action under conditions a posteriori, namely the [determining] members that in time determine each event according to its *positus*, or alterations of the world.)",[]],"reflm:5965":["In the division into nature and freedom the ground of the |\naction is taken either in the series of appearances [or], and then it is everywhere nature; or outside the series, and indeed [from the series itself] either from each member of the series separately, and then there is no nature but sheer chance; or from each member together with the series, and then nature and freedom are possible together, and indeed in the same subject, taken partly as *phaenomenon*, partly as *noumenon*. For the soul can posit for itself a priori objects of which reason itself can also be a priori the cause.",[]],"reflm:5966":["The correctness of the disjunctive proposition is established by this, that both copulative propositions of the disjunction, the affirmative and the negative, are annulled.",[]],"reflm:5967":["The vocation [Bestimmung] of our reason with regard to the transcendental (g cosmological) ideas is 1. [to] regard everything in the world of sense as empirically conditioned, and therefore always to proceed onward in the series of conditions, and indeed within the world (not to leap to something unconditioned outside it), and thereby to procure no ease for its reason. Rule of immanent use.\n2. To procure the conviction that all objects of experience are nothing but appearances, and that things in themselves must lie at their ground, and hence to prevent the transcendent use of the empirical. Through the latter all antinomy is removed, which rests merely on this, that appearances are considered as things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:5968":["That in the world of sense, in every kind of connection, nowhere an |\nabsolutely first, and likewise no infinity, can be represented as given as a whole, and consequently no absolute totality, proves that the absolute must be thought outside it, and that the world of sense itself subsists only in relation to our senses.\nIf the first can be taken according to mere conditions of sensibility, which happens in the composition and division of appearances in space and time, then completeness of the regress is impossible, and there is always a conflict between the assertion of completeness through an infinite series and the assertion of it through a bounded series.\nBut if the first is not necessarily a determination in space and time (dynamical idea), [so is] but can also belong to the intelligible world, which lies at the ground of the sensible as its substratum, then a first, and hence totality, is possible in the same world as *noumenon*, and at the same time infinity without totality is possible as *phaenomenon*.",[]],"reflm:5969":["The infinity of the regress and the totality [hence the absolute] cannot be united. The limitation in the regress of appearances cannot be united with space and time, i.e. with sensibility.",[]],"reflm:5970":["115. E II 1397.\nM 115':\n*In mundo non datur abyssus, saltus, casus, fatum* (in the world there is given no abyss, leap, chance, fate). For the totality of the connection of the conditioned with the condition, i.e. the absolute whole of the series of connection, is either that of the mathematical connection: the composition of appearance, or that of the dynamical connection: the derivation of existence. Both [turn on] 1. the manner in which the series is (g totally) given; 2. how a wholly given series is resolved; mathematically: the *compositio* or *decompositio* (composition or decomposition); dynamically: the first arising, or unconditioned existence in general.\n*non datur abyssus*: namely, the totality given through* an infinite, filled space and time, or through a finite series that has been given as absolutely whole in an infinite empty space and time.\n*(g The world is given neither as infinite nor as finite in an infinite space and time.)\n*non datur saltus*. The leap in the metaphysical sense is the transition from a *quantum* in appearance to the simple =0, as a part, both in the concept of appearance and its possibility, and in the arising or perishing of it, i.e. alteration. Everything in appearance is a *quantum*, both so far as it contains time or space (*extensive tale*, such extensively), and so far as it fills them, i.e. is contained in time or space (*intensive tale*, such intensively: reality in sensation).\n*non datur casus*. Event without a determining cause (in the world). Totality through determining causes in the series of appearances, and in time generally, is impossible. Arising of itself (*casus*) is also impossible; but the action of just the same acting subjects as things in themselves, so far as they determine the appearances but (in that they themselves do not belong to the series of *phaenomena*) are not determined by them, is freedom. Hence |\nM 115: |\nconnection of events according to laws of nature is possible through freedom, e.g. actions from intellectual principles, not from sensible impulses.\n*Non datur fatum*, i.e. absolute necessity in appearance and in its arising, but certainly in the intellectual cause, which is no part of the world of sense, nor even a substrate [of it].\n(g *Saltus** is the immediate connection of the *oppositorum* (opposites) in the same subject one after another. All appearances are *quanta*, and so also are all parts of them; for were something existing simple, it could only be generated or perish *per saltum* (by a leap). But the possibility of generating in time makes the possibility of appearances.)\n*(g *est progressus immediatus a determinatione aliqva ad eius oppositum, ergo a mera negatione ad qvantum vel viceversa* — it is an immediate progress from some determination to its opposite, hence from mere negation to a quantum, or vice versa.)",[]],"reflm:5971":["The immediate connection of the opposed in the same |\nsubject is not possible, except so far as I think the 0 as the infinitely small of the quality of A. E.g. the arising of pain out of indifference is possible only if the latter is regarded as an infinitely small pain and is so intensified.",[]],"reflm:5972":["Freedom is causality without external condition. In the *mundus phaenomenon* (world of appearances) it does not occur. But it is possible that there is natural necessity in this world, and freedom in the *noumenon*. Likewise absolute necessity in existence is possible, but not in *phaenomena*, which exist in space and time at a certain place, [and this place] is always contingent.",[]],"reflm:5973":["116. E II 1394.\nM 116':\n*Non datur saltus* [there is no leap]. Every difference (in appearance) is a *quantum*. But the *quantum* must be possible through *repetitam positionem eiusdem* [repeated positing of the same]. Hence 0 must be regarded as homogeneous with A, but only as vanishing or infinitely small. Hence there is no progress in the determination of a thing to another state except through an increase of the same quality, starting from the infinitely small.\nAll appearance is a *quantum*, and indeed a *continuum*. Hence the appearance of opposed determinations of the same thing, i.e. alteration, is also a *continuum*.\n*Non datur casus* [there is no chance]. No event happens of itself; it is always determined by natural causes. In this way, however, no absolute totality is to be expected. But if I say: a being is *a priori*, and therefore of itself, the cause of an event in the order of nature, i.e. the manner in which [it] it connects such events with other events takes place according to an order of nature, then there is freedom in one relation, namely in relation to the subject as thing in itself, and natural necessity in another relation, namely in relation to a subject as a part in the series of appearances. — *Non datur hiatus* [there is no gap].\n*Non datur fatum* [there is no fate]. All necessity is natural necessity of events, i.e. is always determined through other grounds in the same series. In this way, however, there is no totality in the series. But if |\nI relate the whole series to something outside it*, [whose] that is no *phaenomenon*, then absolute necessity of the whole series can exist, though not in it, [but] by my going from the parts to the whole, but by everything in the series being thought as dependent on what [is given] outside it.\n(s *Lex continui* [law of continuity].)\n*(s The connection of the conditioned with the condition is always a series; in the dynamical field, however, totality is possible through something outside the series, i.e. through what is not *phaenomenon*.\nM 116: |\n*Non datur hiatus*. There is no empty space or empty time in the world; for neither is an object of possible experience. To infer the possibility of an enclosed empty space from the difference of specific gravity, or even from the figure of a cavity, nevertheless presupposes that such a space is of itself an object of perception. But if it cut the world into two parts, a *saltus* from one to the other would still be necessary in order to connect them, and this could not be inferred from nothing.)",[]],"reflm:5974":["It seems that no absolute totality is to be expected, and neither is it [to be met with] except in appearance.",[]],"reflm:5975":["*Non datur casus*. In the world everything happens according to the mechanism of nature, namely as a consequence of that which itself happens, so far as the world is a *phaenomenon*; except so far as the cause of this mechanism itself lies in the subject, i.e. so far as the subject can be regarded as *noumenon*, which [not] determines itself of itself, independently of *phaenomena*, i.e. a reason as principle [of a] of spontaneity. There everything indeed also happens according to the mechanism of nature in the world of sense; but this connection itself is grounded on the [subject] ground of appearances in general.\nThe necessity of events in nature is not the necessity of the things themselves, [these as] i.e. of the existence of nature. |\nThis [necessity], if it is ascribed to appearances, is *fatum*. Consequently there is in the existence of nature itself no inner necessity, [therefore] because it is no absolute whole, and therefore [lies] entirely outside it.\nThe faculty of representing things in themselves, and through this representation of being cause of appearances, is a faculty of [acting] according to the mechanism of nature and yet of acting of itself, i.e. of bringing about events without itself being dependent upon or determined by an event, i.e. freedom.\nThe faculty of originally giving laws to oneself is freedom. (g The faculty of determining this mechanism itself *independenter* [independently] of the mechanism of nature is freedom.)\nThe things of the world of sense can be considered in two ways: 1. as appearances, and there everything happens according to mechanism in general, while they are the subjects in which something happens; 2. as subjects, [whose repre-] which represent appearances to themselves; and there they indeed represent to [themselves] themselves what happens, but nothing happens in them; rather there is in them the ground of the representations, so that something may happen.\nIf among the things of the world we also meet with some that act according to reason, then these themselves [not ap-] are so far not appearances; for reason as cause is no object of appearance, and is not determined by it either, and consequently is so far free from the mechanism of nature; yet, as regards the appearance of its effects, it is operative according to the mechanism of nature.",[]],"reflm:5976":["The proposition: everything happens either according to the mechanism of nature or through blind chance, admits a third alternative, namely: through freedom, that is, a [su-] sufficient ground in the world, but not as *phaenomenon*, but as *noumenon*.",[]],"reflm:5977":["I cannot explain freedom; this it has in common with other funda|mental forces.\nBut neither can I prove it empirically; for it is a mere idea of something that does not belong to experience at all.",[]],"reflm:5978":["The sensible is grounded in the intellectual, but not in the same way as it is grounded in other conditions of sensibility, and so not according to the mechanism of nature. That is, freedom from mechanism can subsist together with [Ub]conformity with it in one subject, in different meanings.\nI call it mechanism of nature where the causality (g of the cause) of an event is itself again an event; and this is how it stands with everything that happens, so far as the cause is appearance. So far, however, as the cause is thing in itself, the causality is not itself an event, for it does not arise in time. The objective grounds of reason and of these faculties, [according to] determining through them, remain, and herein nothing happens and nothing changes.\n*In mundo non datur nec casus nec fatum* [in the world there is neither chance nor fate]. For there is a connection of the sensible with the intelligible: either as substratum of the world of sense, or as cause of the appearances [but] which is distinguished from the substratum.\nIn the first [understanding] case it is reason itself as a being in the world, so far as it can act from objective grounds; in the second, a being that can be known only through reason, which contains the ground of the substrata of the world of sense. In neither case does the cause make up a part of the series, and it is therefore not subject to the mechanism of nature, but itself determines the mechanism.",[]],"reflm:5979":["The world is 1. in respect of space and time (g no absolute totality) neither infinite nor finite (but a *progressus*, nothing more, because we can speak only of possible experience). *Non datur* |\n*abyssus* [there is no abyss]. (g *Mundus intelligibilis* [intelligible world] is totality without relation to time and number and *progressus*.)\n2. In respect of division, consisting neither of infinitely many nor of a finite number of parts (because in decomposition too we speak only of possible experience). Hence no monads. But the *mundus intelligibilis* consists [of] not of *substantiis phaenomenis* [phenomenal substances], and so of monads. These have in their outer relation nothing of space, and so only inner forces, i.e. *vires repraesentativas* [representative forces].\n*Non datur saltus*. There is nothing simple in time and space, no simple alterations.\n3. In the world as world of sense everything is nature, and therefore there is an infinite regress in the series of causes without a *causa simpliciter tali* [cause simply of such a kind]; for nothing can begin absolutely, and an empty time that determines nothing cannot precede.\nBut at the same time, as intelligible world (because here I think not the connection of space and time but that of causes and effects, which are mere categories), freedom can be thought, namely as determining *phaenomena* without being determined by them. But freedom [is possible] only where there is *causalitas intellectualis* [intellectual causality], i.e. in intelligences that are causes through reason.\n4. In the world of sense everything is contingent, because everything is *rationatum* [that which is grounded].",[]],"reflm:5980":["The egoist or pluralist with regard to the unity or plurality of substances. The pluralist is either idealist or materialist or dualist.",[]],"reflm:5981":["Strictly one ought not to say: God has created the appearances, but rather: [he has created] things which we do not know, but in accordance with which [gemäß] a corresponding sensibility has been arranged in us. We can represent things in themselves to ourselves only as thinking beings; for otherwise we have no determinations that would be distinguished from appearance. Therefore |\nhe has made the actions of thinking of these beings harmonious, and connected with one another outside themselves, through an arrangement of sensibility. *Harmonia praestabilita* (pre-established harmony). It is universal, and an ordinance of nature. Bodies do not flow into spirits, nor conversely; rather, the former, as appearances of beings that are also spiritual, are merely proportional to the inner determinations of the latter. *Composita* (composite things) consist of *monades*, but bodies do not; they are *phaenomena substantiata*. *Causae occasionales* would indicate two kinds of substances, of which one would be only a *phaenomenon substantiatum*, or else a continual harmony of appearances with things in themselves would have been established. When I speak of appearances as substances (although I yet judge that they are only appearances), [so I must] think a pre-established harmony [set] among them all, and yet can think therein only the *nexus realis* among the *substantiae noumena*.",[]],"reflm:5982":["In the *mundus phaenomenon* nothing but relations are given to us in outer objects.",[]],"reflm:5983":["The best is always the [greatest] object that is the greatest objective ground of the world; the most perfect is the [greatest] object of the greatest unity of knowledge.",[]],"reflm:5984":["146'. E II 1080. 1197.\nHow is alteration possible (and hence causality)? How is *commercium* (community) possible? Among *phaenomena*, certainly.\nM 146':\n(g The *commercium* of the soul with matter as *phaenomenon* cannot be thought at all; for that would have to be in space. But the soul is no object of outer intuition; hence it must think itself, as well as matter, as *noumenon*. Consequently its existence in time also [cannot be so thought]. But if it is to [think] determine this, it must have something outside itself and in community with it, not given in space, but whose intelligible representation it must (g as) determine in space, in order to determine its own existence in time.\nWe must determine something as in space outside us, in order to determine our own existence in time. That outside us is therefore, prior to this determination, represented as *noumenon*.)",[]],"reflm:5985":["The *commercium* of substances as phenomena in space makes no difficulty — the other is transcendent.",[]],"reflm:5986":["*Nexus substantiarum* (the connection of substances) is either real or ideal; the former is *commercium*, the latter *harmonia absque commercio* (harmony without community).",[]],"reflm:5987":["That occasionalism and *praestabilismus* are an idealism.",[]],"reflm:5988":["These are not three systems for explaining *commercium*; rather, they are the harmony of the *substantiae* either *per commercium* or *absque commercio*. The former is the *influxus physicus* (physical influence). In the world of sense, by virtue of space, a condition of *commercium* is already given, and outer causality (of influence) is no harder to comprehend than the inner causality of *actiones immanentes* (immanent actions). Causality cannot be comprehended at all. If, however, we assume substances as *noumena* (without |\nspace and time), then they are all isolated; consequently, in place of space a third substance must be thought, in which they can all stand to one another in *commercium per influxum physicum*.",[]],"reflm:5989":["The existence of things in appearance is determined according to universal laws. The sum total of all appearances is entitled nature in general.",[]],"reflm:5990":["World already signifies as much as nature taken substantively. Without an inner principle of its alterations [would] in accordance with laws it would not be a world, as a being distinct from God.",[]],"reflm:5991":["Over, beside and \nThe all of nature, logically [collective], is to be distinguished from the whole of natural things, i.e. from the totality of composition; the former is thought in contrast to the intellectual, the latter in contrast to a (g mere) part of the sensible.\nThe question is whether God wished to make an arrangement of nature or an arrangement of art.",[]],"reflm:5992":["Over and \nThe all of nature is not the whole. The first concept is negative, namely: each without exception; the second is positive, and is the concept of a *quantum* as *completum*.",[]],"reflm:5993":["All (g philosophical) derivation of what is given, or can be given, in our knowledge is either physical or metaphysical or hyperphysical. The first is from empirical principles of nature as known through experience; the second is from the [faculty] prin|ciples\nof the possibility of our knowledge *a priori* in general, independently of the (g empirically known) nature of things; the third is from the representation of objects beyond nature. The last kind carries our knowledge wholly outside the conditions of the use of our reason *in concreto*. The metaphysical mode of explanation is objective if it rests on the universal conditions under which alone we know objects as such as can be given to us. It does not exclude the supernatural, but merely restricts our reason to the natural.",[]],"reflm:5994":["The order of nature, i.e. its form according to rules, not merely in respect of events but in respect of original predispositions, is, as regards its origin, either automatic or organic. The former is grounded on the inner constitution of the individual, from which the connection in general arises; the second is grounded on an idea, which makes use of the individual as an instrument for an arrangement that would not have arisen from the individual natural things according to universal laws. The first order comes about through inner forces by means of physical influence; the second through a force distinct from nature, according to a pre-established harmony. The order of natural things according to pre-established harmony is no order of original nature, but of supernatural art; e.g. the figure of the heavenly bodies, the proportion of their magnitudes and distances, plants. Even if we ascribe an origin to nature, and indeed from a being in which the idea contains the ground of causality, but at the same time also of the essence of things, the order is nevertheless physical and not arbitrary.",[]],"reflm:5995":["If I think in nature, besides the material (not merely formal) mechanism, also life, i.e. an activity of natural things according to laws of the faculty of desire, [so] the concept\n|\nof needs and of an organism arises, whether it be automatic or grounded on pre-established harmony. Since the things outside every living being do not act for themselves, but must necessarily be originally arranged for the need of the preservation of living natures, the order of nature here [can hardly properly] scarcely be grounded on anything other than the idea of an author, and hence [s] be organism.\nThe causality of these living beings, i.e. the determination of their faculty of desire, is either autonomy or heteronomy; in the latter case it is grounded through instinct, as *organon* of ends, in the idea of another, in the former through freedom, in the beings' own idea. In the former case there is always only a formal mechanism of nature according to physical laws, in the latter a spontaneity according to practical laws, and their nature is determinable not (g merely) organically [but] and physically, but also morally. In so far these beings do not conform merely to their natural needs, i.e. to an alien and impressed idea, but to their own, which can arise *a priori* from themselves, and [it] their causality is freedom.\nThus all causality is either [mere] material mechanism, or instinct, or freedom.",[]],"reflm:5996":["Order of nature is requisite for experience; it is also requisite for the recognition of the world as a particular substance.",[]],"reflm:5997":["Neither through a miracle nor through a spiritual being in the world can a motion be produced without effecting just as much motion in the opposite direction, and consequently according to the laws of action and reaction of matter. For otherwise a motion of the *universum* in empty space would arise.\nBut also no alteration in the world (hence no beginning of that motion) can arise without being determined by causes in the world according to laws of nature in general, hence not through freedom or |\noccasional miracle. For since time does not determine the order of events, but conversely the events, i.e. the appearances, determine time according to the law of nature (of causality), an event that occurred or was determined in time independently of this would presuppose a change of empty time, and consequently the world itself would be determined in its state in absolute time.\nMotions therefore cannot begin of themselves, nor through anything that was not itself [moved] previously moved; and freedom is not to be met with in the phenomena, nor are miracles in them, such as would be occasional, but at most *miracula praestabilita* (pre-established miracles) in a world that itself had no beginning.",[]],"reflm:5998":["The being that represents to itself solely time, and itself in time, cannot know itself as persistent; only that being can do so which is outside it.",[]],"reflm:5999":["Immortality rests on this, that the soul is in itself a substance, an intelligence, and a person.\nIf immortality is to be proved, then the spiritual, and not the animal, must be proved, from transcendental and not psychological grounds.",[]],"reflm:6000":["Practical freedom is the consciousness of absolute self-activity,\nfreedom the faculty [Vermögen] of the causality of pure reason.\nWhether the soul is a particular substance?",[]],"reflm:6001":["In the transcendental apperception the soul is *substantia Nou|menon*;\nhence there is no permanence of it in time, and this can be found only in objects in space.",[]],"reflm:6002":["The first substances, which underlie matter, must also be simple, but they yield only composite appearances. So perhaps also the soul.",[]],"reflm:6003":["The difficulty regarding the *commercium* rests upon the concept of immateriality, not upon the influence of thought on motions.",[]],"reflm:6004":["If the question is whether the intelligible *substratum* of thinking is not one and the same as that of bodies, when these are regarded as *monadatum*, then the immateriality of the soul is not proved.",[]],"reflm:6005":["We cannot say that soul and body are not *heterogenea* because I cannot compare them through one sense, namely the soul not through outer sense, the body not through inner sense.",[]],"reflm:6006":["Leibniz presumably aimed his *harmonia praestabilita* at this: 1. All alterations in the corporeal world must take place according to laws of mechanism. For otherwise, if a spirit moved bodies, the *centrum gravitatis universi* would be moved. And if this spirit nevertheless produced the motions according to the law of action and reaction, an alteration would occur in the world, yet not according to the law of causality in the corporeal world. Moreover, in the spirit itself something would begin, as it were, in empty time, since time can be determined only through the relation to things in space.",[]],"reflm:6007":["We have no need to prove the actuality of freedom; for that, as psychological, lies in the moral law.\nNor need we prove the possibility, [for] but only show that there is no contradiction in it.",[]],"reflm:6008":["*Qvaestio*, a question: 1. concerning the hope of a future life; 2. concerning immortality; and, for the latter, concerning the perdurability (*perdurabilitas*) both of the substance and of the personality.",[]],"reflm:6009":["The hope of a future life can be grounded upon resurrection, without the existence of the soul.",[]],"reflm:6010":["Of the *communi sensorio* [the common sensorium].",[]],"reflm:6011":["An argument, either empirical or rational, either according to laws or according to analogy with the laws of nature; 2. either metaphysical or moral. The proposition is transcendent.",[]],"reflm:6012":["Immortality is the necessary continuance of personality.",[]],"reflm:6013":["It is necessary to prove that the future life is universal and also everlasting, with the identity of the person.\nImmortality of the soul (g impossibility of the end of life, of the animal life indeed, but not of the spiritual) is the continuance of the person.",[]],"reflm:6014":["(s *perduratio* of the substance, of the personality. Separation of the soul from the body.)\nFuture life (*vita futura*), either animal or spiritual. The former either with the body of this life* (or with a little body) or with another body** (*metempsychosis*). If it be spiritual: whether in this life we can already become conscious of this *commercium*.\n*(s resurrection)\n**(g *Palingenesis* (s either of resurrection or [in reno substitutionis] of metamorphosis; metamorphosis either of evolution or of migration.))\nState, either of the dreaming soul or of the awakening soul. The Lethean cup.\nPlace in heaven or in hell.\nProgress, either (g continuous) to infinity, or [per] natural or [in inf] by a leap.\n(s Great objection against immortality, from the *epigenesis* of the soul. Moral teleology.)\n(s The departure of the soul from the body.)\nFreedom of any intelligence whatever: causality through reason, or the practical faculty [p] of pure reason.\nIt cannot be proved *a posteriori* nor comprehended *a priori*.\nIndependence of the causes of the phenomenal world that determine the will.\n(s Freedom is the independence of causality from all determining causes in nature (the world of sense).)",[]],"reflm:6015":["Above and \nThe departure of the soul from the body is the cessation of sensibility |\nand the beginning of the spiritual and intellectual life. The other world is the present one, intuited either through other senses or spiritually.",[]],"reflm:6016":["Theology is either rational or historical,\ndoctrine of reason or doctrine of faith. The latter presupposes the former; the former breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6017":["Natural Theology.\n1. The concept of the *ens [necessarii] originarium* (original [necessary] being) as the being of all beings (quantity); that there are not two kinds of *entia originaria*, God and matter.\n2. Of the highest of reality: that He is not merely the ground of all reality but its content.\n3. Of the highest of causality: cosmological proof; not Spinozism, inherence.\n4. Absolute necessity.\nThe last is not to be proved merely *per hypothesin* (by hypothesis) but to be understood synthetically. This transcendental theology must do from the concept of an *ens originarium* [either] as the content of all possibility (but then Spinozism results from it).\na. The existence of no thing can be derived from concepts [proved]; for it is a synthetic proposition. (Ontological proof.)\nb. Absolute necessity cannot be derived from any effect, as for instance from the contingent existence of something. And even if it must be assumed in some thing, it gives us to know no properties [because from no] from which absolute necessity would follow; therefore it yields only a deistic proof.\nc. Nor from the constitution of the actual world. For that would require that we survey all possible worlds.\nd. All of this is not physico-theoretical, not teleological, hence [to be taken] merely as a practical presupposition, and also as determination of the concept of it for the latter purpose. (s *Immutabilitas*, *libertas*, *praescientia* [immutability, freedom, foreknowledge], determinism, *voluntas antecedens et consequens* [antecedent and consequent will].)",[]],"reflm:6018":["*Subsistentia* (subsistence) diverse from [causality]: the independence of the *ens a se*. Spinoza.\nOmnipresence. Not as world-soul, not local.\nEternity.",[]],"reflm:6019":["(g Logical possibility lies in concepts, the real in God.)\nPhysicotheology. 1. The *ens originarium* as cause of the world is a free cause (g because otherwise things caused would be in it): an intelligent being. By analogy: understanding, blessedness, will.\nThe principle of possibility, that is, of essences, in so far as the concept of the *ens realissimum* is such for us.\n*Scientia simplicis intelligentiae, libera, media* (knowledge of simple intelligence, free knowledge, middle knowledge).\nCreation of the world, not emanation, nor as an architect. Subtle atheism. In time, conservation. Moral concurrence with human freedom. (s End of creation.)\nProvidence through nature, immediate direction.\nDecrees. Predestination. Absolutism with respect to freedom.\n(s The system of cosmological apotheosis, in which God is the world according to Spinozism, or the world is God: pantheism; or God is the soul of the world: zootheism.)",[]],"reflm:6020":["The ground why we cannot think the absolute necessity of a |\nthing is that we can represent nothing to ourselves without thinking, and can know nothing through mere intuition. Hence we make a distinction between possibility and actuality, since the latter is supposed to be something posited over and above the thought in itself. If we could know through intuition, we should find no distinction between possibility and actuality (if the former is not thought through concepts). Only what is actual is possible.",[]],"reflm:6021":["The immutability of God must be inferred solely from this, that his concept is a *conceptus Noumeni* (concept of a noumenon), and that his existence cannot be represented as in time; consequently alteration is not a predicate of it. For otherwise one could not at all infer from alteration to the contingency of the thing and of its alteration.",[]],"reflm:6022":["God 1. as primordial being, 2. as author, 3. as supreme head of the *mundus intelligibilis* (intelligible world).\n*causa* (cause) —\n*conditor* (founder) —\n*summus imperans* (supreme ruler), monarch, legislator.\nWorld in general, world of sense — intelligible world.",[]],"reflm:6023":["*Ens originarium* in the logical sense is that whose concept is derived from the concept of no thing, and which therefore must be thoroughly determined *a priori* (from concepts). It is *omnitudo realitatis* (the allness of reality). [Log] Its existence is original if it is necessary in itself, not as a presupposition.",[]],"reflm:6024":["We have a concept of impossibility only through contradiction.\nOnly possibility in appearances is thought prior to actuality, because sensibility precedes the object as its condition; logically, the concept precedes the predicate of the thing. The concept of existence is subaltern.",[]],"reflm:6025":["In the logical use of the understanding the concept precedes the thing; in the transcendental use the thing precedes the concept.",[]],"reflm:6026":["The world is either *ens a se* (a being from itself) or *causatum* (something caused). *Causa mundi est Deus*: the cause of the world is God.",[]],"reflm:6027":["332'. E II 1590.\nM 332:\nThe ontological proof would have to run thus: A highest perfection must exist in relation to possibility in general; not, however: a being that we think as most perfect must therefore exist. For this latter does not follow.\nM 332':\nThe proposition that a being (under whatever [I its] concept I may think it) exists is a synthetic proposition and cannot be proved *per analysin* (by analysis).",[]],"reflm:6028":["Originally we can think no possibility except merely the form of it.\nAs regards content, the real thing must be given to us.",[]],"reflm:6029":["Whether the highest perfection alone constitutes the concept of a primordial being.",[]],"reflm:6030":["Transcendental theology has merely negative utility.",[]],"reflm:6031":["The concept of God is an idea. *Vnicitas* (uniqueness) — ideal. Yet only regulative.",[]],"reflm:6032":["Whether the ground of possibilities, of the *essentiae* (essences)?",[]],"reflm:6033":["Something exists, etc., etc., derivatively; therefore something must exist originally, and this must be necessary.",[]],"reflm:6034":["If the *ens originarium* (original being) is assumed to be dynamically *realissimum* (all-sufficient [*omnisufficiens*] as ground), that is, if it is assumed not to contain all reality in itself as determination, though it is the sufficient ground of [that reality] (e.g. not understanding as such, although it is the original ground of all beings endowed with understanding: Hume, or those who assume a world-creator who has proceeded from the original being. Tibet.), then this is wholly different from the concept in which it |\nis assumed to be mathematically *realissimum*, with all reality as homogeneous in a single [being].\n(g What matters is whether certain realities derive from others of a different kind, or can only be *sui generis*, that is, can rest only on homogeneous causes. Understanding on the highest understanding.)\nThe derivation of the possibilities of things through limitation of the *realissimum* seems to be self-contradictory; for reality cannot be limited in a necessary being. But the limitation of the consequence or effect to manifoldness is possible.\nThat all reality should be found in a *compositum* is impossible, because the combination in one substance is precisely the form of the highest being. Absolute necessity, too, is found not in the *compositum* but in the *simplex*.",[]],"reflm:6035":["The *infinitudo* must not be regarded as mathematical, and so as *commensurabilis* (commensurable) with the reality of creatures. It is an *ens illimitatum* (unlimited being).",[]],"reflm:6036":["God is unchangeable because he is not in time at all. He therefore neither begins an action nor ceases from one. If something begins in man which is ascribed immediately to God, then man must be the cause of the altered relation. He becomes a better man, and God becomes gracious. He is more diligent, and God assists him. In God there is always the same *principium* and *actus*.\nThat God grants extraordinary assistance means only that, beyond the divine order which we know, there is to be met with a far more extensive order which we do not know, although both accord with one order of nature, springing from a single decree and principle. If God were diverted, through the events of the world, even in his decree made prior to the world, he would not possess the unchangeableness of an original being. From this one should conclude that no miracle is possible through a single original being. |\nHe who denies the possibility of miracles could say: there is indeed power for it, but no ground that would divert its exercise from a principle from which the order of nature springs; for this ground would have to lie in nature, and yet make an alteration of nature necessary.",[]],"reflm:6037":["The beginning of the world can be thought only as an existence preceded by a time in which the thing is not. An absolute beginning of the world can be thought neither in the world alone nor in dependence on God; for in him there would otherwise have had to be a state in which the causality of the world was not. In the course of the world God can be the cause of a change without himself changing. For the altered state of things produces altered relations to the divine activity (which is always the same). But before the existence of the world nothing could change that had worked towards its coming into being, except God himself.",[]],"reflm:6038":["It is a necessary hypothesis of reason [Einheit], as a principle of the unity of all our modes of knowledge, to assume a single universal original being as the *principium* of everything; to assume this being to be possessed of understanding, because only in so far as it is, through understanding, the cause of everything is the world ordered according to rules, whereby it becomes an object for our understanding; and finally to assume it as a cause through rational choice, so that it may be for us a *principium* of a rational will and of the universal unity of all our free actions. Theism is therefore not a dogmatic assertion, but a necessary hypothesis for the thoroughly consistent use of reason, especially for its self-sufficiency.",[]],"reflm:6039":["Only one Godhead can know the world as one that is worthy of a Godhead; for this [Godhead] would have to contain [alle mo] the highest possible perfection of a world in general, and to this belongs the knowledge of all possible worlds.\nPhysico-theology therefore has this advantage, that it thinks the concept of God through *realitates in concreto* (understanding, will); but with regard to the degree and the number of the [Urs] authors it can give no determinate concept.",[]],"reflm:6040":["From the existence of the world one cannot prove that there is a God, but only that he is an intelligence.\nThe highest understanding, the highest self-sufficiency, the highest will.",[]],"reflm:6041":["The divine understanding is entitled the highest and pure [unbe] understanding, which knows things absolutely as they are in themselves. It is not sensibly conditioned. It is no receptivity, but absolute spontaneity. It is an *intellectus originarius*, not *derivativus*. Its modes of knowledge are intuitions, not concepts; yet not sensible intuitions, but ideas, which do not presuppose things but make them possible. *Intellectus archetypus*. Not thinking. Not reason by a roundabout way. One thinks it in regard to the world *secundum analogiam* (according to analogy), but only in so far as its concept is to be a regulative principle; but *per eminentiam* (by way of eminence), without any analogy, when it is spoken of absolutely.\nM 352':\nGod knows all things *a priori*, and consequently his understanding is a pure understanding. In this there must be subjective conditions of the possibility of things (but not of their appearances, for his knowledge is not sensible), and consequently subjective conditions of the possibility of things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:6042":["On the heading »Intellectus Dei«:\nAccording to psychological concepts *in concreto*.\nThe living God.",[]],"reflm:6043":["Understanding and will, because these are the only attributes that have absolute spontaneity.\nNot divine reason.",[]],"reflm:6044":["On the heading »Intellectus Dei« and M § 863:\nThe highest intelligence. Author of the world. *Causa libera* (free cause). (g *theologia naturalis qvoad conceptum* breaks off?)\nPhysical teleology does lead to an *ens intelligens* (intelligent being), but does not give the determinate concept of an *ens summum* (highest being).",[]],"reflm:6045":["Physico-theology requires the transcendental [theology]: 1. to supplement it, 2. to cleanse or purify it; analogy.\nThe necessity of an author distinct from the world is inferred from the contingency of the order, not of the existence. Hume: doubt whether the divine perfection is not just as contingent.",[]],"reflm:6046":["[moralische Theologie] A theological morality is not fitting, because there morality makes the existence and the will of God the *principium* (g of morality); but a moral theology is good, because morality is made the *principium* of belief in a God.\nIn the first case morality is derived from the existence and will of God; in the second, (g belief in) the existence of God and his will is derived from morality.",[]],"reflm:6047":["Physico-theol. Faculty of knowledge. Feeling of pleasure and displeasure. Faculty of desire.\nthe only wise — blessed\n— holy\nin theoretical relation; as the highest good the order is reversed, and wisdom is derived from blessedness (goodness) and holiness.",[]],"reflm:6048":["It is hardly conceivable how any intuitive understanding other than the divine should take place. For the divine understanding knows in itself, as the original ground (and *archetypus*), the possibility of all things; but finite beings cannot know other things out of themselves, because they are not their authors, unless it be mere appearances, which they can know *a priori*.* Hence we can know things in themselves only in God.\n*(s It is supposed, however, that all knowledge *a priori* is knowledge of things in themselves; but it is just the opposite, being always only of things as appearances, that is, as objects of experience.)",[]],"reflm:6049":["The divine knowledge is not empirical, nor [is it] pure sensible [knowledge] *a priori*. Nevertheless he knows our sensibility.",[]],"reflm:6050":["355. E II 236. 306.\nOn philosophical enthusiasm [Schwärmerey].*\nPlato rightly observed that through experience we do not know things as they are in themselves, but only learn to connect their appearances according to law. (He further saw that to know things as they are in themselves [an intuition a mode of knowledge which does not require] would also require an intuition of things in themselves, that is, pure intellectual intuition, of which we are not capable.) He observed that, if our representation is to agree with the object, it must be thought either as produced by the object or (g as) producing the object. The latter would be the original representation (*idea archetypa*), of which we human beings are not capable, if it is to be original in every respect. Therefore the ideas can be found only in the original being. [Now he believed all] But the ideas of this original understanding cannot be concepts; they can only be intuitions, and intellectual ones. Now he believed that all modes of knowledge *a priori* are modes of knowledge of things in themselves**. Since we partake of the former, we also partake of the latter, and under these he reckoned mathematics. But from ourselves we cannot come to partake of them, and consequently only through a communication of the divine ideas. [Now] But since we are conscious of them not as merely historically imparted and handed down, but as immediately seen into: they must be not innate |\nconcepts which are believed, but immediate intuitions which we have of the archetypes in the divine understanding. [So they are intuit] But we can develop these only with effort. Therefore they are mere recollections of the old ideas from our communion with God. Now this would not yet be enthusiasm [Schwärmerey], but a mere mode of explaining the possibility of modes of knowledge *a priori*. But then there comes a conjecture that we may even now come to partake of this communion with God and of the immediate intuition of these ideas (mystical intuition), and perhaps also to find in it the [obj] immediate object of all our inclinations, which have hitherto turned to appearances, as the types of that object, only through a misunderstanding (mystical love of God). But since it is [genii] probable that between us and God there is a great [ser] ladder of creatures extending from us up to him (g *genii*), astral spirits, aeons, one could first arrive at communion with these and at a foretaste of intellectual, original intuitions. And since the original ideas are the cause of the reality of their objects, one could hope thereby to exercise a superior power over nature. And so the Neoplatonic school, which called itself eclectic, because it professed to find its wisdom in all the ancients, since it read [these] its own dreams into them, was ready for all the raving enthusiasm with which it has afflicted the world. (g finally Spinozism. (Theosophy through intuiting.)) The Aristotelian philosophy displaced this delusion. One began from concepts which we reach on the occasion of experience (*nihil est in intellectu* —). But now |\nM 355: |\none arrived at modes of knowledge *a priori*, without inquiring how this is possible according to the above principle. These extended themselves, and, because everything that remains within the sensible world is always conditioned, reason drove the principles valid there higher up and beyond the sensible world, in the confidence that they would [always] yield an acquisition just as secure as the explanation of what is given which they had furnished up to that point. Now the subjective conditions |\nof reason, in respect of comprehensibility, began to be held to be objective conditions of things in themselves, and, since reason is not content until it has comprehended the whole, conquests began to be made in the supersensible world. Since no limits can be given where one could stop here, one had at last, when all things had been deprived of their individual and separate possibility of existing, to deprive them also of separate existence and to leave them only inherence in a subject. Spinozism is the true conclusion of dogmatising metaphysics. Critique of the propositions accomplishes nothing here; for the difference of the subjective from the objective in respect of their validity [can] cannot be noted there, because those subjective [conditions] which are at the same time objective are not previously distinguished. The necessity of assuming them is once for all there, and one does not notice that it is merely subjective. That experience is possible only through principles *a priori* occurs to no one. Only critique of reason itself can accomplish anything here. Meanwhile men of clear understanding, whose reason surveys the great extent of the use and misuse of opinions, still hold back this decline into enthusiasm for a time. — If one will not take the path of critique, one must let enthusiasm run its course and laugh at it with Shaftesbury [Schaftsbury].\n*(g The great difference of intellectual from empirical knowledge misled the ancients into this.)\n(g Sect in China: I am the highest being.\nSect in Tibet: God is the assembly of all the saints.)\n**(g And the very opposite is the case. For only of things as appearances can there be synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori*. For the form of sensibility, in respect of intuition, can be known before all objects, since it is given in the subject: space and time. In this intuition, however, many propositions can be given synthetically *a priori*, which then hold of all objects of possible experience, but also of none beyond; although the concepts of these, as objects in general, are merely intellectual, yet they are only monograms, which give nothing to be known *in concreto*, but only knowledge *in abstracto*.)\n(g Analytic modes of knowledge one has, to be sure, *a priori*, when the concept of the object is given, be it empirical or rational. But synthetic judgments *a priori* would not be possible without pure intellectual intuitions, which are found only in God. What human beings are to know synthetically, and indeed *a priori*, must have an object of sensible intuition as its object.)",[]],"reflm:6051":["354. E II 237. 238.\n M 354':\n* The origin of all philosophical enthusiasm [Schwärmerey] lies in Plato's original divine intuitions of all possible objects, that is, the ideas, since we intuit them only through their appearances, hence only passively. On this there is based, first, [the conjecture] Plato's opinion that all our knowledge *a priori* [chiefly] (mathematics), chiefly that of perfections, derives from the recollection of these former intuitions, and that we must now (g only) seek ever more to develop them; but from this springs the second step of mysticism, to intuit everything even now in God, which then makes all inquiry into synthetic knowledge *a priori* unnecessary, since we read it in God; third, since other beings may be nearer to God, we perhaps must, so to speak, first learn to know those |\nideas through reflection, and consequently must have dealings with spiritual natures, and so on.\n*(s Even before Plato, men distinguished intellectual modes of knowledge from [the] empirical ones [which one] and understood the latter when one called them sensible, and made even a distinction between intelligible and sensible things. One held all knowledge *a priori* to be intellectual, hence mathematics also; and since various |\nM 354: |\nsensitive things, and properly only these, can be known *a priori*, one had examples of a supposed intellectual knowledge. But to find this distinction important, a need of reason was required to go beyond the empirical, because the latter is always conditioned and therefore cannot be a thing in itself, which must at all times have its complete conditions.\nThe necessity of the hypothesis of such a something was taken for insight into the necessity of these things.)\nM 354':\nThe highest degree of enthusiasm is that we ourselves are in God and [in ourselves] feel or intuit our existence in him. The second: that we intuit all things according to their true nature only in God as their cause and in his ideas as archetypes. The third: that we do not intuit them at all, yet derive them only from the concept of him, and so infer from our existence and our rational concepts of things straight to the existence of God, in whom alone they can have objective reality. Now back from the lowest degree to the highest: Spinoza.",[]],"reflm:6052":["The cause of enthusiasm [Schwärmerey] is the want of a critique of reason itself; for if I arrive by my own powers at the derivation of all things from One, and also at the qualities of this One, how do I come to it? 1. By analysis of my concepts? Then the concept would have to be originally inspired in me. 2. By the synthesis of the laws of experience? But then I remain in the world. 3. By |\nprinciples which I take from neither of the two? These would then have to be a secret intuition of the supersensible.",[]],"reflm:6053":["Superstitious religion is grounded on a principle of the subjection of reason to the delusion of perceptions.\nIn fanaticism [Schwärmerey] man raises himself above humanity.",[]],"reflm:6054":["Delusion [Wahn] is the persuasion that it is unnecessary to test one's judgments by universal laws of nature.",[]],"reflm:6055":["On Plato's Ideas as self-subsistent archetypes. The Idea of humanity as the ground of the existence of the world. We must make our judgment conformable to these Ideas.\n*Theosophia* and *Theurgia*. *Theologia Mystica*.",[]],"reflm:6056":["Anthropomorphism, thought regulatively ((g is the relation of an unknown cause [to d] according to laws of sensibility), is nothing but [the sche] the conditions of sensibility applied to divine actions, as a schema for applying them in the use of experience. Anthropomorphism taken constitutively is the absolute representation of the divine attributes according to laws of sensibility. The latter exposes us to our opponents; without the former we are deists.",[]],"reflm:6057":["God has given man not independence of himself (God), but independence of the incentives of sensibility, that is, [moral] practical freedom. The actions of man are appearances, and so far stand under merely inner conditions of humanity. To these be|long\nalso punishments and rewards. What God did is all good, but does not lie in the world of sense, which is a mere schema of the intelligible. Thus space is nothing in itself and no thing as a divine work, but lies in us and can also have place only in us. So likewise the agreeable and its distinction from the good. Appearances are strictly not creatures, hence neither is man; he is rather merely the appearance of a divine creature. His state of acting and suffering is appearance and rests upon him as bodies rest upon space. Man is the *principium originarium* (original principle) of appearances.",[]],"reflm:6058":["Because the understanding is originally the faculty of regarding itself in relation to objects, the causality of an *ens originarium* (original being), or what determines that causality, can consist in the understanding alone.",[]],"reflm:6059":["The question is: what determines a self-sufficient being to causality outside itself? It is content in itself.",[]],"reflm:6060":["The highest understanding of God, his highest self-sufficiency, this as all-sufficiency combined with the knowledge of all other possible things outside him, the causality with regard to the greatest whole of these things, consequently the (g *summum*) *bonum originarium*, the origin of the *summum bonum derivativum*.",[]],"reflm:6061":["The will is nothing other than causality through the understanding. This is, by means of self-contentment in his all-sufficiency, |\ndiscerned. — Whether a physico-theology is possible without a cause, without understanding and will?",[]],"reflm:6062":["*Intellectus* — *actiones immanentes*\n*Voluntas* —\n*transeuntes*.",[]],"reflm:6063":["The highest being is conscious of his all-sufficiency through understanding, and takes in this (in himself) the highest satisfaction. In this his causality now consists.",[]],"reflm:6064":["Under and \nThe consciousness of his existence as a (g in itself) sufficient ground of everything else is self-sufficiency.\nThe causality of representations with regard to the existence of objects is desire; the relation of the [objects] representations to the subject [and its], the power of the same, or causality, to determine, is pleasure.",[]],"reflm:6065":["A will without incentives from the object. Satisfaction, not stimulus or need.",[]],"reflm:6066":["Divine feeling. Self-sufficiency with regard to the feeling of pleasure in his existence is blessedness.",[]],"reflm:6067":["In him the understanding determines the will immediately, not by the mediation of a pleasure or displeasure; the will of God is no faculty dependent on the feeling of the object.",[]],"reflm:6068":["Feeling of pleasure and displeasure in God is self-contentment; how is a willing of objects outside him possible in this?",[]],"reflm:6069":["We cannot here make out any interest that God would take in the existence of things.",[]],"reflm:6070":["How does the actuality of things outside him interest God? What motive [Bewegursache] has he to create them? He has indeed an aim [Absicht], but no motive. Omnisufficiency.",[]],"reflm:6071":["The divine understanding is a pure understanding, not affected by sensibility; hence the will also is a pure will.\nThe divine will desires nothing.\nPleasure in the existence of the object does not precede. Pleasure in his all-sufficiency.\nA will which, without the mediation of a feeling of pleasure in the object, is determined immediately by the understanding is free.",[]],"reflm:6072":["A manifold cannot agree with ends (which are concepts) except in one of two ways: either this manifold must be the cause of the understanding, or of the thinking and the representation of the ends, that is, it must be able to think, or [d] a thinking being must be the cause of the manifold and of its purposive combination.",[]],"reflm:6073":["The cause of the world through freedom, that is, author. Not, however, on account of stimuli through intellectual appetition (*appetitio intellectualis*), but determined by the intellect through [merely] purely objective reasons, subjectively through mere acquiescence in himself (*acquiescentia in se ipso*).",[]],"reflm:6074":["Opposite and also perhaps \nThe causality of the divine understanding with regard to the objects, which stand in the greatest connection with it and with one another, [is] is the will itself. And the consciousness of this all-sufficient will is the highest pleasure, which is therefore not immediately in the object but in |\nhimself: just as in a moral action the pleasure is not in the object, but his own good will brings forth the deed.\nNot the cause of possibility, nor limited by external impossibility.",[]],"reflm:6075":["External effects of God, as things contingent in themselves, we can ascribe only to freedom; inner actions (such as the knowledge of God) only to natural necessity. In the former the Idea of the best must lie at the ground; in the latter evil as well as good is the object.",[]],"reflm:6076":["Opposite and \nThe negative concept of freedom is independence;\nthe positive concept: autonomy through reason.",[]],"reflm:6077":["Opposite and \nTranscendental freedom (of substance in general) is absolute spontaneity of acting (in distinction from *spontaneitas secundum quid* (spontaneity in a certain respect), where the subject is yet determined from elsewhere (*aliunde*) through *causae physice influentes* (physically influencing causes)). Practical freedom is the faculty of acting from mere reason.\nThe causality in freedom is *originaria*, although the cause is an *ens derivativum* (derivative being).",[]],"reflm:6078":["The [divine] freedom of the divine will does not consist in his having been able to choose something other than the best; for not even human freedom consists in that. It consists rather in this, that [he] the Idea of the best necessarily determined him, which man lacks, and which therefore also limits his freedom.",[]],"reflm:6079":["M § 898, end:\nThis *fatalismus theologicus* (theological fatalism) would have place if God had created the world in |\ntime; for one would have to posit for it a fate of the determination of his power.",[]],"reflm:6080":["Divine blessedness [text reads: Seelichkeit].\nDifficulty concerning the freedom of man under the divine decree.\nThe physico-theological proof from the ends of nature does not suffice by itself, but is sufficient for sound understanding in a practical respect.",[]],"reflm:6081":["The divine will is, with regard to the end, either manifest or hidden. Presumption, to ascribe our connection in willing to the Godhead. Unsearchable are his ways. Even in the universal of nature.",[]],"reflm:6082":["The will in the use of means, with regard to a presupposed end, is indeed often manifest, but with regard to the end itself it is hidden; e.g. why man is formed thus or thus is manifest, but to what end the human race exists at all is hidden.\nIf a human being is once to exist, it is manifest that he must be upright, wise, etc. etc. But that a human being (g and why he) must exist is hidden. What always remains hidden is unsearchable.",[]],"reflm:6083":["In the proper psychological sense we cannot think any end of God, because an end indicates a dependence of contentment, and hence is only an analogue of the ends in the world. The divine end is a satisfaction without interest, in which existence would therefore be a matter of indifference, which is self-contradictory.",[]],"reflm:6084":["The perfection, order and purposiveness of the world make the reference to an original will, and hence physico-theology, necessary; and physico-theology for its part extends the knowledge of the world as a system of ends.\nBut the incomprehensibility of the divine will, when all anthropomorphism is set aside [and], has the result that one can derive this perfection also from the essence of God as the origin of all beings and [itself] therefore from the essence of the things themselves, because the will is only God's self-contentment with himself as a being of all beings.\nBut the unsearchability of this will limits our physico-theology, in the determination of the divine ends, to nature. It is soothing.",[]],"reflm:6085":["We have brought the psychological attributes of the divine will to an end. It is incomprehensible. The ontological attributes are tacitly understood along with it — Physico-theology is possible.",[]],"reflm:6086":["At the very top of the page:\nOn moral theology.\nWhether the concept of God is a merely speculative or a moral concept? How it arises from morals, as a rational knowledge *a priori* (not for the sake of experiences), and how it makes the concept of God determinate also ontologically, which physico-theology does not do.",[]],"reflm:6087":["The reader feels a certain timid [fear] apprehension in entering upon Hume's reflections and objections, and sees in this the expression of presumption. Yet something noble, upright and unfeigned also shines forth from them: that he ventures, without slavish anxiety, like Job, to pass judgement, not in order to condemn God's ways |\nbut to confess his scruples openly to himself [and], without being led by the worry that one will thereby become irreverent into suppressing them and into flattering praises, as Job's friends were. God's government is not despotic but paternal. It does not say: Do not reason, but obey; it says rather: Reason diligently, so that from your own conviction you may freely and undaunted show the reverence to God which would be [nothing] of no worth at all if it were extorted. With one who believes slavishly, and for that very reason also tyrannically moves others to this belief, nothing can be done. Whoever loves peace does not begin with them on grounds of reason.",[]],"reflm:6088":["The lawgiver must be benevolent; otherwise his command creates no obligation to obedience (he must also be omnipotent); but his law, if he is wise, must be holy. As lawgiver, then, God is holy; as ruler, benevolent; and since this is possible only under the condition of agreement with the former, and for the sake of wisdom, he is, as judge, just.",[]],"reflm:6089":["Benevolence under the limiting condition of the worthiness of the *subditus* (subject) is justice. It therefore presupposes an *imperium* (rule). God as ruler is benevolent, but under the limiting conditions of his holiness. Legislative holiness is the perfection of the will, not merely in the legality of actions but also in their morality. The divine will is holy; it demands morality.",[]],"reflm:6090":["Benevolence, so far as it is limited to the conditions of conformity to law, is justice.\n(s Is the wisdom of God in the limitation of his benevolence |\nto the condition of the agreement of rational beings with his holy law.)",[]],"reflm:6091":["370. E I 680.\nWe are not in a position to know the wisdom and benevolence of God in this world *in concreto* and at the same time in the whole of its connection, because we know rational beings as [objects] subjects of all ends only on the earth. The new astronomy has been of great service to theology in this respect. For if we had to regard our earth as the whole stage of divine wisdom, great scruples would arise.\nOn our earth the arrangement has been made that we should bring forth all that is good, both in ourselves and in our outward condition, even the knowledge of the good and the pleasure and satisfaction in it, out of ourselves. In that case it was impossible that we should be guided by agreeableness; for then we should have had to be able to foresee the good and also to have a liking for it (we were, however, to bring ourselves to our vocation [*Bestimmung*] by culture). Activity, therefore, had to be the course of our vocation, and pain the spur of activity. But in this way it also could not come about that each individual [its] attained the vocation of its existence [fulfilled]; defects therefore always remained. Only the species was to attain it. Elsewhere it may be otherwise; so too in the future; belief.\nThe disproportion between our natural predisposition and its development in each individual gives ground for belief in a future life. We need not first assume God as benevolent, but need only infer by the analogy of nature, even without God; then a guidance toward the highest wisdom will always be found here, and a theology which flows from the firm resolve of progress toward perfection.\nM 370:\nMen were even to bring forth out of themselves a system of the public good, with a universal inclination to refer everything to private|inclination,\nfrom which nothing but strife and violence arises. They were to need, from their own species, a master or at least a supreme authority, and many apart from one another. They were to invent universal peace.\nThe role of a human being is perhaps, of all in the system of this planet, the most artificial and the most burdensome, but also, in the outcome, the most glorious. We have happiness here too, but, according to our concept of happiness, not *in abstracto* but *in concreto*; for we can conceive none without the effort to work one's way through hindrances, without dangers in labour, in a word: without the reward of our strength in bringing it out of evil.",[]],"reflm:6092":["We speak of happiness as contentment, but [we] as of an idea which we have *in abstracto*, whose reality, however, we cannot exhibit by any determination *in concreto*, not even in fiction. If it is to be a complete satisfaction of all desires: why then desire, that is, lack, in order to acquire it? For this means, after all, that one was not happy before. (Moreover, the satisfaction of desire increases the longing for more and thereby makes contentment wholly impossible.) If, on the other hand, we are to be in possession of well-being without desiring anything, this state seems to make the being quite useless, since there is no activity in it; moreover, persistence in the same state is impossible for a being that drags its existence through time, [only] and yet with complete contentment, because a part of its existence always lies before it, in respect of which it expects something different from the previous state.\nReligion can be moral only so far as we revere God in a threefold person. [So] As lawgiver he cannot be benevolent, that is, his law cannot be subordinated to the aim at the welfare of the creature, or mixed with it. It is directed to freedom and not to happiness: that creatures become worthy of happiness, not that they |\nbecome happy; otherwise we represent his law as indulgent, suited and yielding to our weaknesses, but not as holy. As benevolent [he has the that] and from no other ground he wills the existence of the creature; but as holy he wills, if they exist, that they must conduct themselves so as to become partakers of benevolence. As just he does indeed presuppose benevolence, but with limitation by the holiness of the law; justice, however, is not benevolent, nor is it (g merely) holy, but benevolence in conformity with the holiness of the law is distributive justice. As benevolent he would not punish; as holy he will not reward (for everything is a duty), since he demands the deed irrespective of the happiness of the condition. Justice, therefore, is the third personality.\nIn men the three personalities are distributed among three individuals; in God there is a threefold personality. This, however, belongs only to the concept of God in a practical, not a speculative, respect: an idea of the relation to human morality and freedom.",[]],"reflm:6093":["That is the holy symbol of moral theology, the monogram of his mysterious being, but [given] in order to prevent theosophy and theurgy.\nThe threefold function in relation must presuppose in God a threefold original principle of his activity; but this we cannot see.\nNumerical identity is the unity of the individual: of that which has been considered as many in different relations.\nThe [specula] consideration of God in this threefold personality is not theoretical but moral. Theology as theosophy is presumption and fanaticism.",[]],"reflm:6094":["Theology is not theosophy, which would know God according to his nature in itself; it is to know him only in relation to us and the morality of our will. Likewise religion is not theurgy, which would exercise an [immediate] influence upon God and his will by formulas, by exertions of the spirit, purifications, penances; its knowledge of him is to be directed to the improvement of ourselves. The question is |\nonly: what makes us better men? The moral concept of God and of the threefold personality is relative to our practical maxims, not in respect of his own nature. By a good course of life one does not try to act upon God, but only to make oneself receptive to the divine benevolence.",[]],"reflm:6095":["The judge is in himself indifferent; he does not pronounce his sentence out of love; he is impartial; he cannot be entreated; he can only pronounce [be]fore laws. This becomes very confused if we assume benevolence and justice in one person. But also if we assume holiness and justice in one. For then he does not speak from the standpoint of a man. Who can stand against him? Job. Besides the laws he has benevolence in advance and is ready to bestow happiness.",[]],"reflm:6096":["Threefold personality, which I think in the highest good. 1. I desire to be happy from inclination, and relate [mein] this wish to an author of happiness. 2. I recognise myself as subject to the moral law through reason, and relate this obligation to the will of a highest being, which can bind me because it has my fate wholly in its power. 3. Reason in me decrees happiness only in so far as I act in accordance with my duty. And this I relate to a third person, whose place conscience takes. [Al] A benevolent person, then, represents my inclination, the second my reason, the third my conscience.",[]],"reflm:6097":["Considered subjectively, my own person is the first. I should like it very much if my happiness were restricted by no conditions at all. But objectively I must represent my happiness through another person, and reason is the first person, which prescribes the con|ditions\nof happiness. Subjectively (g from inclination) I should like the application of the moral law to me to take place according to benevolence [angewa]; but reason teaches that it takes place not merely according to the holy law (g nor yet according to benevolence alone), but [aus] benevolence combined with holiness, that is, according to justice.",[]],"reflm:6098":["The question is whether the concept of God is a concept necessary in a physiological or in a moral respect. Because (g then) nature must be regarded as contingent, inner necessity cannot be inferred from it; it is only a necessary hypothesis (namely among the properties that are to constitute the concept of a deity), in order to indicate, [die] for the phenomena, the highest [zureichenden] ground sufficient for them (not for all other questions of reason). For the perfection and greatness of the world do not require the presupposition of an [voll hochsten] all-sufficient being. Hence as a physiological fundamental concept it is not determined, and its existence is only hypothesis.\nWere morality grounded on physiological conditions, the very same would follow. We should have to presuppose the existence of God only with regard to our need and our aim at happiness. But all morals is necessary from the essence of things, and that without which it would have no objective reality is necessary a priori, though only in practical relation. Also, in regard to morals, God must be represented first as the highest good, and then, in order that he may be it, also as the highest being.",[]],"reflm:6099":["M 372':\n(g If it were possible to know that a God is, would morality then still remain in man?)\nIn moral theology\nGod is represented as the head of a necessary system of ends, not of a contingent system, which we assume hypothetically as universal and believe, but of one which we know, |\nnot through a choice among contingent things and a will that arbitrarily fixes the ends, but whose will is one and the same with what in the order of ends among rational beings is absolutely necessary; consequently not as despot, but as the highest good itself through the nature of things in general. God as *summum bonum originarium* (original highest good). The moral properties are not his [unterordnung] supreme dominion over beings according to laws of morality, but the self-subsistence of these laws themselves. Here we lay at the foundation what we know with certainty, namely the necessary system of all ends, to which all others must be subordinated as to their *conditio sine qua non*. Hence the errors of moral theology are really pernicious, above all anthropomorphism, which however can be more easily prevented if we regard God not as the author of the laws but of the obligation in accordance with these laws.\nM 373':\nThereby moral theology becomes at the same time ontotheology. It is nonetheless a merely practical and subjective certainty, which is grounded on an interest, but on an interest that is objectively necessary and inseparable from the essence of reason, and subordinate in no respect. Were morals only a pragmatic system of prudence, belief in God would be mere hypothesis; as it is, it is a postulate.\nThe highest wisdom in physicotheology is merely relative, and concerns the sufficiency with regard to the system of all ends; but in moral theology it is self-subsistent, the source of the possibility of the things themselves. Thereby we are led to derive everything from the order of things as necessary in itself, and yet at the same time from God as the self-subsistent principle of all order. We are not to turn to God as a being who gives laws that are in themselves contingent, and from which he can dispense; rather, what in the nature of things is necessary according to laws of morality we are also to expect from nature according to physiological laws.\nAll moral properties of God are here to be determined only objectively, according to our concepts, not subjectively — for in the latter case we have two correlata, whose connection we could represent to ourselves anthropomor|phically\n: the moral order, and God, who wills it. But his will is this moral order itself. He is benevolent, and indeed infinitely so, because from him in himself all possible good flows; but also holy, because it flows only according to the moral order; and just, because this benevolence does not establish the moral order but is determined in accordance with it.",[]],"reflm:6100":["Justice is the restriction of benevolence through holiness. Properly speaking, therefore, it is not rewarding (s for were reward merely in accordance with justice, we could expect little), but only benevolence rewards. For where [Heiligkeit] justice does not restrict benevolence according to principles of holiness, there benevolence is at work.\nProperly it is we ourselves who restrict divine benevolence through our guilt, and punishments must not be regarded as positive.",[]],"reflm:6101":["How evil is possible under a good God rests on the question how freedom is possible in beings who are nevertheless wholly and entirely dependent in their existence, and hence how morality in general is possible in them; for were everything nature, everything would also be good, though only physically, and there would be no morality at all. The possibility of freedom we cannot comprehend; but we must nevertheless presuppose it, for rational beings can act only under the idea of it. Thus it is practically certain. The question how freedom [moglich sey] is possible in created beings is the same as the question how substance, which exists only derivatively, is possible.\nWith regard to God, for whom the progress to infinity, which for the creature is never completed, is only an *intuitus* (intuition), there is no evil. Evil is found only in the parts, because they are not the whole. Evil seems to belong merely to appearances; in itself it is only a manifoldness of the good according to degrees. Yet for us appearances are the things themselves. We also in fact do not depend on ap|pearances\n(g in acting) in ourselves, and are therefore free. Nevertheless we necessarily represent ourselves as depending on them with regard to happiness [han] — in the sensible world we depend on it; hence we are nonetheless dependent on it with respect to the object of the will.\nWhat holiness, benevolence and justice are.",[]],"reflm:6102":["(g Without astronomy these objections would be important.)\nObjection against the holiness of God: that evil (vice) is in the world.\nObjection against the benevolence of God: that ill is in the world.\nObjection against the justice of God: that [beydes] happiness and ill are not distributed in proportion to the worthiness of rational beings. *Vide* p. 283.\n* We are to expect everything from nature, our own as well as that which surrounds us in this sensible world, so that we may bring forth the good and happiness out of ourselves and not exist as foster-children. The conflict of animality with the principle of reason in us must be overcome through gradual culture; one generation forms the next. Falsity, laziness, cowardice are vices; but in the whole they are, as it were, corruptions that serve for the generation of the good. The whole lies in the intelligible world; this is only appearance.\nIll is a cause of dissatisfaction only according to our idea of happiness, but it is a spur to activity. We are not to want to become happy here; we must die.\nLack of justice. Were it as we wish, all morality would be transformed into self-interest.\n*(g Evil arises from our looking only to one part of the world and not to the whole course of the world, and it is also only the imperfection that attaches to the part. It rests on this, that [Menschliche] man in each generation makes up only a part of the whole race.)",[]],"reflm:6103":["The good in the sensible world is progress toward perfection, not possession of it; so happiness is progress, not possession of contentment.",[]],"reflm:6104":["Beside the first half of M § 915:\nHow God is the cause of evil is just the question as — breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6105":["The removal, i.e. the abolition, of actual evil in the world through progress toward perfection is the *perfectio phaenomenon* [perfection of appearance], although evil is not a means to the good.",[]],"reflm:6106":["In all (g moral) attributes of God which carry an obligation with them, i.e. a necessity of being subject [himself] to the moral laws, the attributes are considered only [after as] relative to us; e.g. *iustitia non commutativa, sed distributiva* [justice not commutative but distributive].",[]],"reflm:6107":["M 376:\n1. Morality subsists by itself (according to its principle) without the presupposition of a Deity.\n2. The highest good is not the determining ground but the object of a will determined through the moral law, and the possibility of this good, and consequently also the existence of God, is practically necessary.\n3. The assumption of the existence of God is the consequence of the moral law and of the disposition in accordance with it; for only through this does the highest good become object of the will.**\n*(g [The] Nature cannot of itself agree with morality without a supreme author with a moral will; for that would have to be a nature in which the good will of the creature were also the sufficient efficient cause of the happiness in agreement with it. But this is not wholly within his power, whereas the disposition certainly is.)\nM 376':\n**(g The subjective ground of holding-to-be-true is here grounded on an objective practical law, and so is objective, though sufficient not in a logical but in a practical respect.)",[]],"reflm:6108":["(g Of theoretical belief [and its chan] as well as of practical rational belief and its unchangeableness.)\nSufficient holding-to-be-true from subjective grounds [which] [must at the same time rest on objective grounds] must be so constituted that the subjective ground is at the same time objective, or is necessary from an objective ground; that is, that the moral law, which commands us to promote the highest good, is at the same time a maxim. Whoever has this maxim is sufficiently convinced, in a practical respect, of God and of a future world; but he can convince no others of it, because he cannot presuppose the maxim in others. It is thus a conviction in a practical respect, a rational belief, not a historical one, which would be merely an opining and would be bound up with changeability.\nHow knowing is here harmful. Minimum. *Qvantum est, qvod nescimus. qvantum est, cujus scientia non indigemus.* [How great is what we do not know; how great is that of which we have no need of the science.]",[]],"reflm:6109":["M 377':\nIf we were to say: it is highly probable that a God exists, this would be a judgment quite unsuited to the constitution of the knowledge in question, and, even if one admitted it, would say far too little. For probability can be thought only in a kind of connections |\nwhose possibility is, for the rest, certain, e.g. that planets too are inhabited. But the possibility of a causal connection can hold only through experience, and hence only of causes in the world; but of a cause outside the world, and not [the] one in nature but of nature itself, we have, by inference according to the analogy of nature, no ground. Probability is only a judgment pending closer knowledge.\nM 377:\nBut it can be necessary to assume something, and indeed from subjective causes of the use of the understanding in general, although we do not know the object; e.g. that the weather and other events, e.g. of whole states, occur according to natural laws and from natural causes, because otherwise we could not make use of our understanding. That is a postulate of universal human reason. Here lies a practical postulate: we ought to think events in such a way that we can make use of our understanding on them. But this practical postulate always has only conditioned necessity, namely if we will that our understanding agree with itself according to principles. But there are absolute practical postulates of willing, and these are the moral ones. We ought to be faithful and honest without the condition of any arbitrary end. Whatever must always be assumed, in respect of certain objects, in conformity with this postulate, is also a practical postulate. I must think it so, and it is not merely hypothesis.\nHere the ground is objective, but not theoretical, rather practical, wholly a priori; but the conviction too is only relative to the practical; it is not probability but firm belief.",[]],"reflm:6110":["M 377':\nWe cannot say: we know with certainty, indeed not even: with probability, from objective grounds, that a God exists. For probability (g is approximation to that of which certainty is possible) requires laws which are subject to those of nature as we know it, and according to which we should here have to assume that the origin of nature itself also must be explained; we thus pass from the law of nature to a wholly different order (*μεταβασισ εἰσ αλλο γενοσ* [transition into another kind]) |\nand want the same rule to hold for things outside a certain kind which may hold for things of this kind; not to mention that no probability can entitle us to an inference from a limited effect to an unlimited cause, and that, if one cannot prove a highest being, it is without use to prove the existence of any other.\nBut we have subjective grounds, in both the speculative and the practical use of our reason, for presupposing such an existence, because without it we find no satisfaction for our reason, and also no |\nM 377: |\nthoroughgoing unity of it possible. Above all, the practical laws, which are after all objectively necessary, have no subjective force without that presupposition. Thus we have enough for rational belief, indeed even for it as a necessary presupposition; for in speculative philosophy it is not necessary that I attempt to explain the origin of nature (g There is no objective necessity to speculate, but certainly to obey the moral law); but moral laws I must acknowledge, and hence must also presuppose, as an unavoidable hypothesis, that without which moral laws would have no binding force for rational beings.",[]],"reflm:6111":["Pragmatic interest, from the principle of happiness as a subjectively universal end, makes belief in God only a hypothesis; moral interest, from the principle of the system of all ends as the objectively necessary final end of rational beings, makes of it a postulate, i.e. an absolutely necessary presupposition of pure reason. God, as the highest [Go] self-subsistent good, in whom morality, as the highest formal good, alone can have reality, i.e. can become a ground of the existence of an intelligible world, i.e. of a kingdom of ends.\nHappiness as a consequence of morality belongs to it [as a corollarium de] and to its transcendental truth; but as a ground of it, it [he] abolishes it.\nIf the knowledge of God were a knowing and not merely a believing, would morality then well remain over? To believe in God is already moral and has a worth. Whether one can say: devils also believe? They fear that there is one; they have no need of him; they do not want there to be one.",[]],"reflm:6112":["If I am entitled only in a moral respect (g and indeed one established a priori) to assume something distinct from nature, such as God, freedom and immortality of the soul, and can establish none of these through insight, nor may lay it down as ground in any other respect, then the field of rational hypotheses is hereby closed and all fanaticism is deprived of its refuge.",[]],"reflm:6113":["(g Of the world as the object of the most perfect will, as of the *summi boni originarii* [original highest good].)\nThe divine end is not the mere happiness of the creature outside him, nor the satisfaction of an inclination to honour in him, but that which is the necessary consequence of the highest original good, namely the highest derived good. The best world, i.e. happiness under the conditions under which each is worthy of happiness. The former, as the benevolent will, is in itself unrestricted and therefore infinite; the latter, as the holy will, binds it to conditions. Thus the product of the most benevolent and holiest will is the highest good. Justice is the consequence of this. In this, however, consists the practical perfection of the highest being. The world, as the *ectypon* [copy], i.e. the effect which bears witness to the cause, has therefore its greatest perfection in this, that it bears witness to an (g practically) most perfect, not merely benevolent, author; and its perfection is therefore the honour of God, not that the creature honours him [but that] through high praise, but through action and deed. And so be happy in the highest degree.",[]],"reflm:6114":["[In] Under a good principle evil must always be in process of vanishing, and this diminution and progress in the good is the perfection of the world of sense. Evil is not substantial; it abolishes itself.",[]],"reflm:6115":["*Prosperitas summa* [highest prosperity], the highest well-being [Wohlfarth], is happiness; the highest self-contentment is blessedness.",[]],"reflm:6116":["Pleasure in one's state is well-being [Wohlfart].\nPleasure in oneself is self-contentment.",[]],"reflm:6117":["Contentment with our whole existence is happiness; in man this requires also physical causes, i.e. well-being [Wohlfarth]. Happiness independent of physical causes is blessedness. Hence only from self-contentment. God is therefore the sole blessed one.",[]],"reflm:6118":["Of the author of the world we have three heads to consider:\n1. Creation, 2. Preservation, 3. Government.\na. Causality is either *per emanationem** [through emanation] or *per libertatem* [through freedom]; *caussa libera est autor*. In the latter case God is either *creator* or *architect*. 1. *Creatio*. Whether from eternity or in time? b. *conservatio*: 1. *omnipraesentia*, 2. (g The preservation of the form) government. Wisdom [as] in respect of all particular events in the course of the |\nworld is either laid in the beginning of the world** and is entitled *providentia*, or is exercised in the duration and progress of the world and is entitled (g *concursus*) *gubernatio*. As *concursus* it is entitled *directio*, and indeed chiefly in respect of freedom. The *directio* is either *ordinaria* or *extraordinaria*.\nThe *decretum* of providence is either *absolutum* (*praedestinatio*) or *hypotheticum*.***\n*(s The absolute necessity of existence as From its nature; it can never be a ground of the existence of other things, for this natural necessity is isolated and can have only *rationata immanentia* [immanent consequences].)\n**(s or causality in respect of particular ends: *concursus*)\n***(s Difficulty, how God can be author of freely acting beings and yet not of their free actions. The latter involve a difficulty only if these actions were not appearances.)",[]],"reflm:6119":["System 1. (g of natural causality, or of the mere appearance; to intuit all things in God) of inherence or [of emanation], of causality (Spinoza, the ancients); 2. of free causality (s or of emanation, this however either of the architect or of the creator) either of the architect or of the creator; 3. *influxus* [influence], either through *commercium* [community] (world-soul) or through providence.",[]],"reflm:6120":["*Creatio* and *conservatio* [creation and preservation] concern the substance. (s *Providentia* and) *Gubernatio* and *concursus* [government and concurrence] (s relate to the freedom of the beings of the world; but as such they are not free) concern the state. *Providentia* is the divine government, laid in the creation.",[]],"reflm:6121":["*Creatio*; *conservatio*; *gubernatio*; *concursus*, not to nature but to freedom. *Providentia* either [through] and *directio ordinaria* and *extraordinaria*; absolute decree.",[]],"reflm:6122":["Categories of quantity: unity, plurality, το παν [the all] (pantheism). 2. Reality: substratum of all reality, or limited (g infinity). Categories of modality: possibility, actuality, necessity.",[]],"reflm:6123":["1. God as cause of the world: *nexus effectivus* [efficient connection],\n2. as author of the world: *nexus finalis* [final connection],\n3. as ruler of the world: *nexus moralis* [moral connection].\nPantheism does not mean: everything is God, but: the All is God.",[]],"reflm:6124":["System either of inherence or of causality; the former Spinoza's, the latter that of Deism.",[]],"reflm:6125":["The absolute necessity of nature is only the ground of immanent, but not of transeunt, actions; for the latter there belong representation of the object and freedom.",[]],"reflm:6126":["*Caussalitas Dei respectu mundi* [causality of God in respect of the world] 1: in respect of — [the note breaks off here].",[]],"reflm:6127":["Divine causality *per libertatem* [through freedom], i.e. *auctor* [author].",[]],"reflm:6128":["How God could bring forth something outside himself cannot be proved; Spinoza.",[]],"reflm:6129":["Either *per emanationem* [through emanation]: necessity of nature; or *per libertatem*: through freedom (s *autor*). The latter either as *creator* or as *architect*. The architect is not a complete author (s *demiurgus*); matter also bears the blame and the merit.",[]],"reflm:6130":["In and \nWere matter not a creature, it would be an aggregate of necessary beings. These could not, then, stand in *commercium* [community]. Moreover, God would be limited by the nature of the self-subsisting subjects.\nOne cannot say that the essential imperfection of the creature limits God.",[]],"reflm:6131":["Of God we can represent to ourselves no *actus successivos* [successive acts] in respect of the world, but we can represent successive effects of one and the same *actus* of creation and conservation; e.g. that, when we amend ourselves, God from an angry becomes a gracious God. But if the different effects of God upon the world are to be capable of being developed out of a single *actus*, that means that they must be explicable from the *actus* of creation, and so from its effect, that is, from nature. For nature is nothing other than the single *actus* of the divine power, from which everything else in the world can be explained according to universal laws.\nHence no successive creation can be thought, since this would not be [a series of] events developing successively out of the single *actus* of creation in accordance with the order of nature.\nA miracle would be something which, though [occurring] in accordance with an order of nature, can be known in respect of its laws only through knowledge of the nature of a world-whole as such. Thus only in so far as the intelligible ground of appearances were known to us, which ground is always moral.\n(g Creation does not belong among events in time. The world, with its existence, must be thought through it alone as possible.)",[]],"reflm:6132":["Moral theology has as its object God as the highest self-subsistent good, as author of the world, which for that reason is the highest created good.\nThe principle of moral theology is this: that the (g positive) idea of freedom, as the ground of all morality, is derived from the idea of the highest good, which alone constitutes the system of all ends, in which we think ourselves as members and from which point of view we ought to act, since it is to be possible through us and our freedom. Now this system of all ends from freedom is nothing other than a world in which (g highest) happiness stands in proportion to worthiness to be happy. Morality therefore includes this idea in itself as a principle. But such a world is possible not merely through our freedom; nature must [this] contain the agreement with this law. Yet nature can contain it only if there is a God.",[]],"reflm:6133":["God, in his holiness, willed the moral worth of rational |\nbeings as the condition of happiness. This worth, however, consists in the fact that a being can impute all good to itself. The less, therefore, God gave in the way of predisposition to it, so that man could become from freedom the author of it, the greater the moral worth. Great predispositions to the good would have diminished the imputation of the good. Now evil arose precisely from the conditions under which alone the greatest moral worth could arise: namely a moral character acquired by one's own effort. Ills serve to put the strength of morality to the test by resistance, and to exercise it.\nThe lack of prompt execution of divine justice in this world is also the best means of exhibiting morality in its purity. All apparent objections are therefore rather consequences of the necessary spontaneity, energy and purity of the moral character, which could be preserved only in this way, and upon which God has directed his highest purpose in this life, as the highest condition of the good.",[]],"reflm:6134":["In organized beings we assume that everything is purposive, although we do not see it. Why not the same in all things of the world?\nWithout a best world there is no morality.\nThe world of sense, as world of sense,* is in the whole the best.\n*(g perfect, namely as a passage to the intelligible, where morality will govern nature and take its place. This holds both in that ills befall the virtuous also, and in that there is progress to the good through vice.)",[]],"reflm:6135":["In a living world the corporeal nature must accord with the thinking nature as means, and conversely the thinking nature as end; otherwise it is not one nature, because it would not maintain itself. Here ends constitute the principle of existence.",[]],"reflm:6136":["Teleology. The principle that everything has its end and is good either in itself or for something, is the supreme principle of nature in natural science. It has not been derived from the hypothesis of a rational author, but [rests on this]: that otherwise we should have no standard for our idea of the good, namely of what it is as means, if it were not nature. Anatomy: all things that hang together in a system. From this need arises that of the hypothesis of a rational cause — for things in nature outside one another cannot be represented as acting on one another purposively of themselves. Hume.",[]],"reflm:6137":["By experience it cannot be proved that this world is the best, and so one cannot infer from it the highest perfection of the author. One must lay the latter at the foundation in order to infer the former; but then this proposition can well be justified through experience, chiefly teleology. For the ancients, who held the earth to be the sole end, it was harder. Hume's objection, taken from the ills of men: they are deserved. Guilt is itself an effect of our freedom and limits, and the limits a particular ordering of the progress to perfection.",[]],"reflm:6138":["End of creation. By this is understood either the whole of what is created or the *actus* of creation, thus either the effect or the determination of the cause to the effect. The end of the former is the whole of all possible ends in the *universum* (of its members), |\nconsequently the free will in a system and, through it, happiness in a system. That is the objective ground of the divine will. The subjective ground, or the incentive, cannot be distinguished in God from the objective. We can state it only negatively: in the self-sufficiency of a being as the supreme source of all good and in the possession of its infinite causality.",[]],"reflm:6139":["The free will as harmonising with a whole of all possible ends, or from the principle of the possibility of a universal system of ends.",[]],"reflm:6140":["The objective end of God is (s the absolutely (unconditionally) good of the world together with what attaches to it as consequence) that the world be the greatest (g created) good in itself, not that it be good for something.\nBut it is the greatest good in itself in so far as rational beings [gl] are happy therein and worthy of this happiness.\nAs a system of ends through freedom, and then also through nature. (s Honour: no subjective end.)",[]],"reflm:6141":["God has no subjective motive [Bewegursache], i.e. no interest in creation.",[]],"reflm:6142":["The concept of God is not a speculative but a moral concept; not in relation to nature, but to the interest, and indeed the moral interest, of human beings, |\nM 386': |\nhereby he is also determined as a perfect being.",[]],"reflm:6143":["(g *mundus optimus* [the best world], because otherwise a better will would be possible; the highest happiness is not the supreme end, for it is only conditionally good; nor is morality, for it is not the whole end.)\n*Summum bonum (finitum) derivativum* [highest good (finite), derivative]. That rational beings in the world be happy, in so far as they are not unworthy of this happiness.\nFaith in God through morality. This gives the interest. The moral disposition is impossible without this, although the appraisal [Beurtheilung].\nHow is it possible that God should have an end outside himself? Not as incentive [Triebfeder], but this is in him and in the consciousness (of his highest fruitfulness) as *summi boni* [of the highest good].",[]],"reflm:6144":["Desire requires the existence of a thing; will goes only to action, which pleases in itself because it brings forth something good.",[]],"reflm:6145":["However good the world may be, we still ask: what interest has God in creating it? Its existence could after all be a matter of indifference to him. Was he weary of solitude? Did he seek honour? Moral interest.",[]],"reflm:6146":["Above, beside and \nNot that God should be an object of our love, but of our veneration [Verehrung], i.e. as holy lawgiver to the morally good, and only thereby to the physically good, has he created the world.",[]],"reflm:6147":["In and \nThe concept of the honour of God is nevertheless better than the concept of the happiness of creatures; for the former goes to the perfection of the world, but it is nevertheless ambiguous.",[]],"reflm:6148":["Every will has an object, but not always a motive. The highest good and its existence has for a most perfect will no motive, and hence also no end distinct from the object.",[]],"reflm:6149":["1. God as world-cause. 2. As world-creator and preserver, author. 3. As world-ruler, and the world not merely as a realm of nature, but also as a realm of ends. Moral theology. Existence. Faith.",[]],"reflm:6150":["End, considered according to its objective constitution, is the highest good; according to its subjective ground there is none at all, but an *analogon* [analogue]. The former is an immediate end.",[]],"reflm:6151":["The will of God has merely objective motives, no subjective, and hence no incentives [Triebfedern].",[]],"reflm:6152":["*Attributa 1mo moralia* [attributes: first, moral]; *in subsidium theologia transscendentalis et naturalis* [in aid, transcendental and natural theology]. Evidence: |\nM 386': |\nthe greatest practical conviction. The world as *summum bonum derivativum* [derivative highest good].",[]],"reflm:6153":["God created the world to his honour, not for the sake of his honour. That is: the objective, not the subjective end; honour is objectively that which in itself determines the rational judgment on [the worth] all worth of the person. God has therefore created the world for the sake of rational beings, and indeed in so far as they were to be capable of estimating themselves and their true worth.",[]],"reflm:6154":["The question still remains: 1. whether God has any subjective motive for the creation of the world at all, i.e. has an interest in its existence, as the ground of desiring that it should be; [or] 2ndly what motive it |\nmay be. If the first is answered in the negative, the second drops out. The will is twofold: a, that which arises from the [Bed] proper need of the subject, since this requires, for its complete contentment, the existence of other things; or b, that which arises from all-sufficiency, where the subject takes satisfaction in itself [as a] and in its sufficiency for all good outside it. Self-contentment with oneself as a ground, as a source of the possibility of all other things, is at the same time the causality with respect to the existence of the greatest [and with] effect, which in form agrees with the form of his self-contentment. Hence a distinction between an *appetitio originaria* [original desire], which rests on no subjective motive or incentive, and a *derivativa* [derivative one].\nThe (g objective) honour of God refers to rational beings, and not to the motive which these get in the world to love him, but to honour him. (g\n|\nM 387: |\nGod is the highest good in so far as [he] his will is a morally perfect and holy will, and under this condition alone a benevolent one.) |\nM 387': |\nThis rests on his benevolence, [ver] limited and determined by his holiness, i.e. [on] on this, that the world should be a stage of moral good conduct for them, in order that they may make themselves worthy of the benevolence of God. Thereby alone is he honoured; and since the good which is brought about thereby alone does not flow [se] to him, but is immanent in the world, God is thereby indeed served [gedient], i.e. we act as in the service of God for the best of the world, but God is not waited upon [bedient], i.e. through any cult some end [outside] or effect outside the world and in God is intended. Hence there is no immediate service of God, but only [through] thereby, that we carry out his commands in the world and thereby ourselves become 1. good, 2. worthy of the [ge] goodness of God.",[]],"reflm:6155":["Above, beside and \nWhether God created the world from eternity or in time. That is: whether creation is a beginning of the world, or a [world] causality with respect to the substance of the world without any relation to time. Only the existence of the world appears to us in this relation. This appearance is only a mode of representation [Vorstellungsart] for us, not a constitution |\nof the things in themselves which appear to us thus. So far the world is neither from eternity in itself, nor has it a beginning, but only the duration in time which our regress in it contains, which goes to infinity without a first therein; yet no series, as e.g. of generations, must be regarded as actually infinite, but as in itself not determinable.",[]],"reflm:6156":["Creation refers either to a beginning of the world, or to the existence of the world without reference to time as the form of sensibility.",[]],"reflm:6157":["It belongs to the end of God that the rational creature should be fully adequate (through holiness) to the will of the same [derselben]; but no creature attains this. Hence it is the progress thereto to infinity, where, if the disposition is such that it lets us firmly hope for this, this is in accordance with the divine end.",[]],"reflm:6158":["Respect for an unconditioned (personal) good is veneration [Verehrung], for the highest, *adoratio* [adoration].",[]],"reflm:6159":["The world is a whole of ends (g kingdom of God); God: creator, as architect;\n[for] as rational beings (g containing) also a realm of ends; God: ruler (g progress to the highest good).\nIn so far as they themselves are to co-operate toward the highest end. *Civitas Dei* [city of God]. Judge.\n(g 1. as realm of nature,\n2. as realm of grace,\n3. as kingdom of heaven, where [everything] nature wholly under the condition of agreement with the moral in the highest breaks off.)",[]],"reflm:6160":["*actuatio initii — durationis* [the actualising of the beginning — of the duration].\n*Creatio et conservatio* [creation and preservation] (g the opinion about the preservation of the form, because it cannot be maintained through the nature of things) go to the substance. *Providentia* and *gubernatio* [providence and governance] go to the state of the world. (The former is the arrangement of the agreement of the world with the divine ends, in so far as it is already laid in the creation; the second, in so far as it belongs to preservation, and hence also in the sequence of time.) *gubernatio est vel directio vel concursus* [governance is either direction or concurrence]. The first [concerns] the supplementing of the imperfection of things with respect to form, the second with respect to force.\n(g The determinations either of the forces of natural things, or the supplementing of them as means to particular ends.)",[]],"reflm:6161":["The principle of the appraisal of all *actuum* [acts] of governance [Gubernation] is: that they all belong to one *actus* of creation, and hence also of preservation.",[]],"reflm:6162":["*directio* is the determination of the forces of nature [through d] as means to the divine purpose; *concursus* is the supplementing of them by divine force.",[]],"reflm:6163":["The action of God whereby he has laid the *nexus finalis* [final connection] in creation is providence; whereby he brings it forth in preservation is *gubernatio*.\nOnly one *actus*.\n*Concursus. cooperatio.*",[]],"reflm:6164":["(g *Conservatio non est operatio peculiaris, sed rerum respectus differt tantum* [Preservation is not a peculiar operation; only the respect of things differs].)\n*Omnipraesentia virtualis — perpetua, intima* [virtual omnipresence — perpetual, intimate];\n*non commercium ut anima mundi* [not commerce, as of a world-soul],\n*non ut unica substantia* [not as sole substance],\n*sed ut ens extramundanum* [but as an extramundane being].",[]],"reflm:6165":["That the ills in the world (on our earth) do not refute the divine wisdom and goodness, but leave it possible to regard [sie in] their permission as not unworthy of a Godhead: this manner of judging is greatly justified by the astronomical discovery of so many worlds. There are so many infinite grades (though in such a way that every rational creature also attains to the higher ones).\nYet this very same discovery in turn strikes down all man's claims to providential care and eternity, and so annihilates him in his own eyes, that he does not credit himself with importance enough to be an end of creation.",[]],"reflm:6166":["*Concursus* (concurrence) in nature or in freedom.\nGod is the author of all determinations of nature, whether they occur in accordance with the order of nature or not. (g Of moral determinations he cannot be author, whether of the good or of the evil.)\n*Concursus* properly means a joining in, in distinction from the *causa principalis* (principal cause).\nIn the moral sphere, if it is to be said of God that he concurs, he must not be author, i.e. *causa solitaria* (sole cause), of the good. He concurs in freedom.",[]],"reflm:6167":["The *nexus* (connection) of a *causa remota* (remote cause) with the *causa proxima* (proximate cause) in respect of a |\n*causatum* (thing caused) is not the *concursus*, but a *nexus causarum immediatarum* (of immediate causes), where one constitutes the *complementum* (supplement) to the *insufficientia* (insufficiency) of the other. Now one cannot think that God concurs in natural effects as such (g for he is not *concausa* (co-cause) of nature, but *causa solitaria*), that is, in the insufficiency of his own causality in creation. Hence God cannot concur otherwise than in the causality of freely acting beings, toward his moral ends in the world; for of their free actions he must not be regarded as the cause. That which gives to free actions the *complementum ad sufficientiam* (supplement to sufficiency) toward divine moral ends (holiness) is entitled the Spirit of God. These, however, if the actions are [themselves the] still to remain imputable, must not be *causata* (things caused) of the holy Spirit either, but only a removal of the hindrances to freedom. The Spirit of God is that which gives moving force to the moral laws, and so is an inner moral life, wholly impossible according to laws of nature, working in us. Everything morally good in us is an effect of the Spirit of God, and it is imputed to us that we give it room.",[]],"reflm:6168":["We can say: everything morally good and evil comes from ourselves and is to be imputed to us; and at the same time: all [comes] good comes from God. This is to guard against *concursus* and mysticism. The good comes from God according to the order of nature, the evil from freedom.",[]],"reflm:6169":["1. Conservation is no *concursus*, for the latter is the *nexus* of *concausae* (co-causes) with a *causa*; [so f] hence *causa solitaria non concurrit* (a sole cause does not concur). 2. The *concursus* in the state of substances is either in nature in itself or in freedom in the world. In the first [is] the divine working is also not *concursus*, e.g. God and the physician. 3. Hence only in freedom: in the worthiness to be happy and in happiness. For here God is not, in respect of the world of sense, *causa solitaria*. 4. Also in respect of this, either *per providentiam* (through providence) or *occasionalismum* (through occasionalism); occasionalism in a *nexus finalis* (connection of ends) is hyperphysical: hence *concursus per naturam* (concurrence through nature).",[]],"reflm:6170":["Of *concausae subordinatae* (subordinate co-causes) the superior (nor the inferior) does not concur, but is *causa solitaria*. If the actions are regarded as natural effects, God is to be regarded as *causa solitaria*: *non concurrit*.\nBut of actions from freedom God is not cause, and there he can concur toward the highest good: 1. so far as it contains happiness; 2. so far as the freedom of man is under hindrances and God can remove these, [daher] not so far as he gives us hyperphysical powers, for then he would be *causa solitaria*. Lead us not into temptation.",[]],"reflm:6171":["The *concursus* in nature presupposes that nature has a causality independent in itself; for if God is its cause, then the rule is: *causa solitaria non est iuvans* (a sole cause does not assist). God therefore concurs [zur] only in freedom; for nature is his own work, and in it he cannot concur. But he concurs in freedom only so far as it has the highest good as its end: either as [Würdigke] subjective condition thereof, or as its objective consequences.\n(g The actuation of the highest good in successive execution is entitled providence.)\nThis *concursus* occurs either in creation; there it is entitled providence, and, because it aims at freedom and its use as condition, can only be general. All providence is either in a physical or in a moral respect; the latter is concurrence. Or it occurs in conservation, and then it is entitled *gubernatio* (government), guidance. Direction [ist], dispensation (*Fügung*), is an *actus extraordinarius* (extraordinary act) of *gubernatio*, [nicht] which is directed to an individual aim.\nIt is not necessary to assume any *gubernatio*, but only providence and extraordinary direction. Ordinary direction is providence, considered in so far as it agrees with all ends in particular cases, although it has been derived from the end in the whole.",[]],"reflm:6172":["General providence already includes particular providence within itself, and the latter therefore cannot be disputed if the former is conceded. But whether providence proceeds from the general to the particular, (g that is,) from the whole to the parts, or conversely, is the question. We can think no conformity to law and harmony of the particular ends according to determinate laws, except in such a way that it begins from what [allgemein] is common to all, and from the whole.\nMany ends cannot harmonise unless they have first been compared with the whole of all ends. This idea of the whole contains, according to our concept, *generalia* (general things), with regard to which special ends were excluded. In order to preserve the species, and more species of animals, the rest had to be sacrificed to nature.\nProvidence is not merely *generalis* (general) but also *specialissima* (most special), but it is not to be thought after the manner of our foresight. For there a system of ends cannot arise from many individual ends, and the whole we can determine only through the individual; or, if the idea of the whole precedes, we must set aside intentions regarding the parts, as in a state various estates must do without freedom and comfortable living, so that in the whole [aber nicht] there may be happiness, but not an absolute whole of happiness suited to every individual and his merit. After our manner of thinking, then, we must ascribe to God merely care for species, but not for *individua* (individuals). Not everything is an end, but a by-consequence.",[]],"reflm:6173":["b.\nThe actuation of the beginning (g of the world itself in existence) of substance is creation; the actuation of its continuance is *conservatio* (conservation). In regard to the state [is the conser] of things in the world, the actuation of their purposiveness in the continuance of the world is: 1. laid into the beginning: providence; 2. in the course of events, either as *gubernatio* (government), so far as the forces themselves are not found in the [nature] world, or as *directio* (direction), so far as they are indeed in the world, but are not the determining ground of the purposiveness.\nBoth, *gubernatio* and *directio* together, are *concursus*.\n*Concursus* is distinguished from providence.\n(s Creatio, conservatio, gubernatio.\nEvery extraordinary providence is a miracle. Direction is a formal miracle, *concursus* a material miracle.\nGod is the author of the perfection of the world either through the order of nature or contrary to it. The first is the ordinary providence; the second, as *miraculum praestabilitum*, the extraordinary providence.\nThrough the order of nature, according to our concepts of it, the final end is not attained.)\nM 394b:\n(s Matter as an aggregate of substances: [not] no being necessary in itself can exist. For necessary beings cannot be in *commercio* (community), and therefore cannot constitute anything composite.)\nM 394aI:\n(s Moral proof. We find ends in the world; these give us, for our insight, an indication of a being which, by analogy with an intelligent being, is the cause of the world. Through this, however, its concept is determined neither for the theoretical nor for the practical principles of our use of reason: since in regard to the former it explains nothing, and in regard to the second determines nothing at all.\nBut reason, through the moral law, sets us a |\nfinal end. This cannot be attained by our powers, and yet we ought to have it as our aim. It can be brought about only in the world, and consequently only so far as nature agrees with it. But a nature that agreed with the moral final end would be a morally working cause. Hence we must assume a being outside nature as its author, which, as a moral being, endowed (g with) understanding and will, is the cause of the world. This is now theism.\nWhat we must merely assume on subjective grounds of reason (with respect to the determination of our will), but cannot prove, is a matter of faith. Thus the existence of God is a matter of faith of reason, and through this proof alone is a determinate concept of the original being given. — With this the natural concepts, omniscience etc. etc., are then combined. Creation, and its difference from preservation, does not concern God, but merely the determination of the world. To assume creations in the continuance of the world is contrary to the principle of the possibility of experience.)",[]],"reflm:6174":["In God the universal signifies the all, which contains everything else within itself.\nIn men the universal signifies the little that is contained in all the rest.\nIf divine providence is general, it must also be special; this is not the case with human foresight. For the general in God is the knowledge of the whole, in man the knowledge of the genus. Now what is contained in the particular is not in the concept of the genus, but is indeed contained in the knowledge of the whole, which, however, it is impossible for us men to grasp. God's knowledge determines every part in the whole; that of men, the whole through the parts.\nThus in God the particular providence is de|rived from the general.\nThereby it is also possible that, since genera and species can subsist only through the preservation of individuals, in God this end as well as all other ends can be preserved according to an order of nature which finds the ground of its possibility in him himself.\nParticular [foresight] dispensation (*directio*) is that in which an (g individual) event is regarded as end and the causes as means. It is therefore not regarded as possible according to the order of nature, because this goes from the general to the particular, whereas that lets the general follow (g from) the particular.\nThe reason for assuming such a dispensation is that one cannot represent to oneself how else all individual events in the world could turn out purposively, since we can form no concept of how God proceeds, in his ends, from the whole [to the pa] and its end to the parts.\nThe *concursus* of God to freedom (g so far as it is morally determined), in order to make nature accord with it and with its moral law, can also be regarded as lying in the order of nature, and must be so regarded.\nPresumption of judgments in regard to the determination of events as ends (g and of causes as means) and to the particular direction of the divine will upon the plans of human wisdom.",[]],"reflm:6175":["The agreement of divine providence with the perfection of the world according to laws of nature is entitled a *concursus* to freedom and to morality. It imparts capacities, opportunities and incentives to the good. The extraordinary providence in regard to single good or evil actions is possible; but we can never determine it. God's *concursus* to the *progressus* [in] of the good in the world is the essential point here. God is not *auctor mali* (author of evil), because man is free and God gives no incentives to evil; but he can be regarded as *auctor* of the moral [law] good, because he is *auctor* of the obligation under this law.",[]],"reflm:6176":["Whether divine providence is merely general providence, or also particular providence?\nThe highest good is to be regarded not [an] (g as) aggregate of single aims, but as a system of ends derived from one idea. For unless the former is derived from the latter, no unity of aim is possible, e.g. a clock. In a system of ends, agreement toward the end is possible only in that whatever contributes to the aim is derived from the idea of the final end, and whatever does not contribute to the aim is limited only to this, that it does not hinder the aim. Hence not every part can be an aim.\nOne can indeed say: *quicquid valet in genere* (g *omnibus*), *valet etiam in specie*, but not: *quicquid valet de genere* (g *toto*) etc. etc. That is, what belongs to all that falls under a genus etc. etc., but not: what belongs to the genus as a whole etc. etc. All men die, but the genus does not die. Particular providence would be that in which every event had already been predetermined in the creation, as means or end, with intent. In that case one would have to assume either that the [general] order of nature, according to general laws, necessarily led to such results as would, [even] if one considered them apart from this *nexus*, have been necessary in themselves in the *nexus* of ends. But then there would also be no need to assume, as supreme cause, an intelligent being, but only a *causa bruta* (blind cause). Therefore, if the order of nature cannot present every event as end or means, special providence is possible only through supernatural effects and, since these already lie in the creation, is a *miraculum praestabilitum*.\nEverything stands also under particular providence, but not everything happens through it.",[]],"reflm:6177":["If providence is regarded as the most special in its aim, i.e. so that all events occur through divine particular providence, it cannot be regarded as *generalis* (though indeed as *universalis*); i.e. [the events] it cannot be directed first and preeminently at the whole and be determined only in relation to it. For this is possible only through an order of nature (according to our concepts). |\nThe events would all be supernatural, and the world would be no *totum reale*, but merely ideal.",[]],"reflm:6178":["Intuitive universality is to be distinguished from discursive. In the latter the particular is contained not in the universal but under it; in the former it is indeed contained in it. General providence contains the particular within itself according to general laws of nature. Particular *directio* is distinguished from the providence that is contained in the creation. It asserts that something is not contained in the end according to general laws of nature. But this is possible, because God is a moral author.",[]],"reflm:6179":["How is it possible, through the universal dependence of beings upon God, that they are nevertheless in accordance with his ends according to general laws? E.g. a tree with the spread of its branches. Twilight. Snow for plants. Snow-light in long nights. The moon, in its waxing part, is visible to the polar regions for half a month.\nIf rational beings are to become subordinate authors of the good in the world, they must have before them nothing but the order of nature, so that the good could be imputed to their freedom.",[]],"reflm:6180":["*Directio* (g guidance) is properly *concursus formalis*; the *concursus materialis* is a particular act of creation, a material miracle.\nOrdinary or extraordinary *directio*. Everything stands under it, but does not happen through it.",[]],"reflm:6181":["*Concursus* (concurrence) either to nature or to freedom; the latter does not increase the moral good in the world, since it is not to be imputed to us.",[]],"reflm:6182":["*Gubernatio* (government): Since the world continues to exist, we also distinguish in God a continuance of actions.\nDirection is *concursus formalis*;\nopposed to it is *concursus materialis*: cooperation.",[]],"reflm:6183":["Particular providence is that in which a single event is placed in nature as an end, and the cause as a means.",[]],"reflm:6184":["With regard to [what belongs to] the perfection of the world as a moral whole, it seems that God must concur. But what is really meant is this: the concept which rational beings have of God, that is, religion, must concur to this end, in order that good may be done in faith; and this faith we can have only through the presupposition of God and of his providence.",[]],"reflm:6185":["Extraordinary divine direction is not necessary in respect of the ends of nature, nor in respect of God's ends in regard to our freedom (for through it no moral worth would accrue to these ends). It seems to be necessary merely for the sake of the harmony of freedom with nature, in rewards and punishments, because, as it seems, freedom is a kind of lawlessness which disturbs the divine purpose.\nIf everything in the world rewarded virtue and punished vice, moral worth would fall away and God's end would not be attained. It can come about only in eternity, that is, in the infinite.\nIt is contrary to moral warrant [*Befugnis*] to determine extraordinary divine direction here or there.\nIt is also contrary to the logical determination of our reason. For to assume a miracle means to repudiate entirely the maxim of our reason, which suffers completely through a single exception. Reason, where it is to judge of itself, and therefore according to its own nature, can know nothing as supernatural; for that would be self-contradictory. That a miracle is possible in itself may be conceded; but if it occurs, the conviction of it must be brought about by this very miracle.",[]],"reflm:6186":["Whether God, as lord or (g lawgiver), and also as proprietor of human beings, can sacrifice one [to himself] to another as a means to an end.\nWhether, if the latter is not the case, God is restricted by laws, and so is not *dominus plenus* (full lord)?\nHe has the right to obligate morally; but this presupposes freedom.",[]],"reflm:6187":["Obligation is the constraint of a rational being through the mere knowledge of the law. But the law can constrain a rational being, and this being can recognise itself as obligated, only if it recognises the law's universal validity for every will, and therefore also the necessary agreement of its own will with the law. Now a rational being can agree to no end in which it would be used not at the same time as an end itself, but merely as a means. Therefore it cannot recognise itself as obligated to such an end either.\nThe warrant to dispose of [et] an object as one wills, without its consent to the law (g *disponiren*), is property. God is therefore lord, but not proprietor; sovereign (*autocrator*), but not despot, who would dispose of subjects as his property, and hence as of things, according to a will that does not have [their] happiness as its end.",[]],"reflm:6188":["A despot is one who regards his subjects as his property. He may indeed be benevolent, but without asking the will of the people, in what manner they can will it to be so. He therefore does not regard them (a tyrant is a cruel despot) as ends in themselves, from whose will alone, at the same time, he may give laws, but merely as instruments of his ends. (E.g. to sacrifice one as an example to others.)",[]],"reflm:6189":["It is very agreeable to observe divine providence according to universal laws of nature; for this gives the understanding unity according to rules. But it is something absurd to allege a particular providence for individuals, because such providence consists wholly of interruptions of the laws and, without a relation to freedom for making [them] upon individual laws of providence, subjects everything to our pleasure. But hope in particular providence, yet without the presumption of wanting to determine it, is in turn a law, as much of nature as of good conduct.\nIn regard to the history of human freedom we do not refer to a divine predetermination [wede], that is, providence, whether according to universal laws through ordinary arrangements or through particular arrangements by extraordinary provision, but to divine direction; and even this it is beneficial for reason and morals to observe in history. It is the gradual development of the natural predispositions toward the vocation of humanity.",[]],"reflm:6190":["The doctrine of predestination is grounded either on the concept of [natural] necessity of actions as opposed to freedom, or on the concept of moral incapacity for the good, so that the good can never spring from ourselves, but can only be wrought by God.\nHere the rational being is a mere machine for divine purposes. Damnation is no punishment, and blessedness no reward for good conduct.\nEnthusiastic concept of the elect. To be merely passive in regard to the divine decrees, both externally, in respect of death and life, and internally, in the improvement of oneself.\nShrinking from ascribing the good to oneself. False humility.",[]],"reflm:6191":["In predestination the concept of freedom is violated. Since this concept is incomprehensible, those who assert predestination are to be excused.",[]],"reflm:6192":["The making known of what was hidden is revelation.\nGod has hidden himself from us. Through morality he first gives the impulse to seek him in nature. Extension. Yet he wills merely faith (practical), not that we investigate speculatively.",[]],"reflm:6193":["God wills to remain hidden from our speculative curiosity in regard to [her] his decrees, and still more from our intuition. — Nature is a mirror that has its own colour, anamorphotic.",[]],"reflm:6194":["All revelation is directed to practical knowledge.",[]],"reflm:6195":["All religion, even natural religion, is a matter of faith. For it is doctrine objectively and disposition subjectively.\nTo regard all duties as divine commands, and the whole object of these duties, the highest good, as possible only under a divine legislation and government, and consequently to expect this outcome merely in faith. Whether there is a duty to believe in God, or whether rather (g historical faith without rational faith does not improve) the knowledge of our duty brings forth faith in God?\nThe divine will must first be known through reason, for otherwise one can test no revelation. But thereby I know God's will only from the moral side. In regard to [our] the pragmatic laws, as sanctions, it must be revealed. But only the moral can be a universal duty of human beings. Revelation, as a disclosure of what is necessary merely for knowing, and through which no one becomes a better human being, cannot be for all human beings. But in respect of the moral it is not on that account unnecessary, because human beings can also see it through reason.\nWhat belongs to the introduction of a religion is not itself a prescription of religion for all times.",[]],"reflm:6196":["Revelation contains *credenda* (things to be believed) and *agenda* (things to be done); but the former belong to religion only so far as they are directed to *agenda* as moral ideas. Sanctions bring forth observances; only laws bring forth universal duties of human beings.\nThere can be no obligation to faith; for faith is a consolation, not a constraint: one holds to it gladly, constraint one hates.",[]],"reflm:6197":["(g An unbeliever is only one who does not) believe morally [un-], that is, one who does not want to accept what alone can strengthen morals.",[]],"reflm:6198":["An unbeliever is one who, in respect of what is not made certain to him by his own experience, will not hold it true except through certainty a priori. Thus he will not hold it true as a hypothesis for the comprehensibility (g or the practical use) of other propositions certain a priori, nor on account of the experience of others. *Ungläubisch* (incredulous).",[]],"reflm:6199":["Faith in revelation is moral if it is a duty; but only historical if it is not a duty, but merely a means to duties.",[]],"reflm:6200":["Whether God reveals himself to us (g announces himself) through pure reason (the practical, not the speculative), or through nature (he opens himself, but does not disclose himself to us), or in a supernatural way through outer or inner experience?",[]],"reflm:6201":["Whether God can reveal himself through appearance?\nWhether through [immediate] speech, so that we know that it is God who speaks?\nWhether, if this is so, he communicates his will, which cannot be known by us through reason, or only the confirmation of the moral (g natural) will in our souls. Effects of grace.\nWhether he reveals himself only to the one to whom he speaks, through the [generation] conviction wrought at the same time in him, or also to others, equally certainly, through [this] that person's narrative, which, as historical, requires examination according to the criteria of history.",[]],"reflm:6202":["*athei*. (g Diagoras.) Strato. Epicur. Anaximander.\nDiagoras. *Practici*.\nNatural theology is necessary:\n1. because by its concepts we judge the genuineness of the revealed [theology],\n2. because it is needed to preserve the latter in its purity.\nIts origin may be through revelation, but when it is there it must at all times be capable of being understood by everyone through reason.\nIn exposition, to look chiefly to the moral attributes of God.\nTo seek to guard against superstition, fanaticism and unbelief.\nIn a word: empty ratiocination about God.",[]],"reflm:6203":["The believing mode of thought is either receptivity or spontaneity of belief. The latter is practical and consists in the principle of doing good in believing. In respect of truths, in respect of the knowledge of which we must be merely passive, he does not count this passive attitude as an obligation but only as a warrant to accept them. He neither denies what he does not know sufficiently, nor presumes to vouch for its truth.",[]],"reflm:6204":["Healthy understanding [gesunder Verstand] is, like beef and mutton, a dish for burghers and peasants, etc. etc.\n(g The human being who adopts nature as a principle but allows himself as many exceptions as he pleases, or thinks yet another nature which is not experience, is unenlightened.)\nTo be enlightened is entitled: to think for oneself, to seek the (g highest) touchstone of truth* in oneself, i.e. in principles.\n*(g of my judgment. The ground of holding-true; for I must answer for it.)\nFanaticism [Schwärmen]: [lies] in (g spirit-) sensations*, which pretend to be the feeling of truth. Panegyrists of common human understanding.\n*(g I do not have these from myself. Only reason sets criteria for itself.)\nSuperstition: [lies] in facts [alone]; for these still have a touchstone above them.\nUnbelief (g the blameworthy lack of belief, or the resolve not to believe): [lies] in theoretical reason alone. (g For practical reason needs, as incentive, hypotheses also.)\nNaturalist: how many meanings [it has]. Whether there are atheists. A free spirit [Freygeist] in the historical meaning is a freethinker [Freydenker], who subjects himself to no statutes.\nThe morally unbelieving man is a free spirit.",[]],"reflm:6205":["Delusion [Wahn]: 1. of lazy trust in passive reason: mysticism; 2. of distrust of all reason; 3. of the self-conceit of the speculative [reason].",[]],"reflm:6206":["Th II:\nHuman reason has the peculiar characteristic that, [not only the mo] beyond what is required for forming a concept of a thing for some purpose, it seeks to complete not merely this concept itself by means of everything that constitutes it, but also the object of the concept within the kind of things to which it belongs. We are not content with what would suffice for the ordinary use of words, namely, to know distinctly the concept of a body, of a man, of a plant. We seek [it] to become conscious of it in all its marks, and from this, when the law of parsimony is added, the definition arises. But besides this, when we have counted the object as belonging to a certain kind of things, |\nwe seek to think it completely in respect of this kind. Body belongs to matter, and what in its extension is not matter is empty space; hence we form the concept of a perfectly dense body. It has coherence; we think of one that is perfectly hard, without troubling ourselves whether such a body is actual or even (g only) possible. Thus the completeness of a thing of a certain kind serves us only as the standard for all the other concepts [things] that we could form of it, in so far as they differ from one another merely in magnitude. These magnitudes are variable; they must be compared with one that is invariable, that is, with that of a thing which contains everything that can be contained in its concept in relation to its kind.\nSome of these concepts of completion we can determine, in that we have a determinate concept [all], one given in experience [at least one to be specified negatively as concept], of what belongs to their completion (e.g. the diameter, among all the chords in a circle). Others are of such a kind that we can indeed think the completion, but cannot ourselves complete the concept. The concept of well-being is empirical, but there may still be much [to be lacking] lacking in contentment with one's condition. Here, then, a concept is needed to whose content nothing is lacking, that is, the greatest and [best] lasting well-being, that is, happiness (g\n|\nTh I: |\nwhich, however, we can never think determinately). If this does not depend on contingent external causes but springs from ourselves: blessedness (*Seeligkeit*). Now we can extend this concept of the satisfaction of a rational being still further, by adding [thun] to what it enjoys what it does, that is, contentment with its person and the morally good. The complete moral good is the highest virtue. If this itself is free from all inclination to evil: holiness. Holiness of the will and blessedness of the condition together constitute the idea of heaven. On the other hand, since that [what not] whose concept consists in the relation of cause and effect has a twofold opposite, a negative = 0 and a privative = –, one can think a condition that leaves no contentment at all: misfortune; and, in so far as the being contains the cause in itself: misery. Likewise one can think a will that acts with |\nintention against all moral laws, and so the idea of hell arises. Hence\nheavenly joys and infernal torments, heavenly virtue and infernal vice\n}\nIdeas.\nTh III:\nThese ideas are quite necessary, even if no [der] object corresponded to them. It is not a question whether any such thing exists, but only how we can better compare our concepts of what is with |\none another, by holding them together with a third, though this be a mere idea, as a common measure. (The Julian period precedes the beginning of the world by 532 years.)\nIf we now think an individual according to these ideas, it is the good or evil primordial being (*Urwesen*); for both are considered not according to what they contain in themselves, but according to what they are the cause of. (Whether in the world there is as much evil on the one side as there is good on the other, and whether, as motion cannot be imparted in one direction without producing or taking away just as much on the opposite side, *lex isonomiae* [law of equality], the principle would indeed be good, but its work would necessarily contain as much evil as good.) Both would contain everything required for activity, but the one would always contain the ground of the reaction against the [activity] effect of the other. If the evil being is derived in its existence, but is nevertheless, though limited, the cause of all evil, then it is the Evil, or the Devil: an idea that is not precise, since one does not know how far its influence and effect extend.\nIf we think a perfected being not according to determinate concepts of good and evil but as a thing in general, then this is the transcendental idea of a highest being. This idea is also necessary if we are to think the differences of all possible things, in thoroughgoing determination, in respect of something and nothing in general. First everything is thought in One, in order afterwards to think more in one thing and less in another, through mere limitation of the archetype (*Urbild*). I keep always the same subject, a thing in general; and through different limitation of the essence which contains everything positive, I obtain all things, in so far as they are something. The differences rest, as it were, on the combination of something with nothing in infinite space. The evil primordial being is also thought as infinite reality, but in its effect as the counterpart of all the causality of the former; so that the world, while containing no contradictions, contains nothing but conflict of aims.\nApart from the necessity of the idea of a most perfect being for |\nthe concepts of all others [counts], reason [also] judges it to be necessary for the existence of things. These, considered merely as things, can be distinguished only through [reali] negations, and so only through limits of a highest reality. Now the existence of limited things seems to be just as derivative as their concept, whereas the existence of a being that has all reality seems alone capable of being original. All shadows are only remnants of the infinite nothing, namely of the night, which without the all-illuminating sun would fill space. It seems natural, therefore, that light mixed with shadow should not exist first, but should arise only through greater or smaller limitation of the sunlight, which must precede it. The highest reality can limit its own effect, and thereby reveal its existence outwardly; but limited reality has in itself no sufficient principle of its possibility, and consequently its actuality also is to be regarded as dependent. Further, from the principle of thoroughgoing determination there arises a thoroughgoing community of descent, and so a kinship of everything possible. This is because it is possible only in one concept, since all negations are possible only through the limitation of the highest reality.\n===NOTES===\n- Broken first sentence smoothed (\"beyond what is required...\"); the bracketed fragment [not only the mo] left as in the apparatus. \"(g\" = deletion marks, carried over as they stand.\n- Several sentences restated as shorter ones. Brackets such as [it], [things], [lacking], [best], [thun], [what not] are the edition's insertions, translated in place.\n- Sehnen = chords (of a circle). Wohlbefinden = well-being; Wohlgefallen = satisfaction; Seeligkeit = blessedness; Urwesen = primordial being.\n- The parenthesis on good and evil in the world (lex isonomiae) is syntactically incomplete in the German; rendered closely, not repaired. \"Das Übel oder der Teufel\" taken as Evil, or the Devil.\n- Doubtful: [auch], [zählt], [reali] and [Thätigk] are bracketed alterations or deletions; whether each is deleted or inserted is not clear from this text.",[]],"reflm:6207":["The *principium exclusi medii* [principle of the excluded middle] [Der du], that is, of determination, contains this: that if the concept is compared with one of two *oppositorum* [opposites], one of the two must belong to it. The principle of thoroughgoing determination says that the concept of a thing in general, in order to constitute the representation of an individual thing, must be compared with all possible *praedicatis oppositis* [opposed predicates]. Thus, if the thing has been determined in respect of one of them, it must in this determination be compared with other opposed predicates; and it can therefore be thought as determinate, as a thing in general, only through its relation to the *ens realissimum* [most real being] alone. Hence it comes about that a universal concept determines itself thoroughly and becomes the concept of a single being.\n===NOTES===\n- [Der du] is an unreadable or abandoned start in the text; left bracketed and untranslated, not guessed at.\n- The sentence \"so daß, wenn es...\" is tangled; I divided it and took \"es\" as the thing (the concept of a thing in general). The last clause is rendered as the German gives it.\n- Latin terms kept in italics with English beside them at first use.",[]],"reflm:6208":["The principle of thoroughgoing determination is a synthetic proposition; for it requires that the concept of every thing should stand in community with the con|\ncepts of everything possible, and hence that to every determination there belong many more which do not belong analytically to the concept of the thing. Consequently the universal concept is to be regarded only as a part of the concept that contains the predicates for all that is possible.\n===NOTES===\n- Restated as two sentences; the German is a single sentence with a final clause.\n- The word break \"Be|griffen\" is kept as \"con|cepts\" with the Academy's page-break sign.",[]],"reflm:6209":["The *principium exclusi medii* [principle of excluded middle] says only that, of two *opposita* [opposites], both cannot belong to a thing or both be removed from it. It does not say that the thing must necessarily be compared with all possible predicates [or also], i.e. exist only in relation to them and to the determination according to the *principium exclusi medii*. This latter is the principle of thoroughgoing determination. The first is logical, and signifies only the nature of disjunctive judgments, which contain the *oppositio contradictionis*. The second is metaphysical, and signifies the derivation of the concept of each thing from the sum-total of all possibility, namely from the highest reality.\nThe human understanding requires, for a determinate concept of a thing (namely not of what is common to many things but of an individual thing), that the thing be capable of being distinguished thereby from everything possible, since the understanding judges only through general concepts. It must therefore compare the concept in idea with all possible predicates, and so thinks thoroughgoing determination, which yet no understanding can possibly carry out. It thus presupposes that, in order to know a thing completely, one must know not only what it contains but, besides, everything that it lacks, so that one may know it also in relation.\nThe principle of determination is that of excluded middle. The principle of thoroughgoing determination is entitled: every individual thing (i.e. so far as it is distinguished from all others; for a thing, considered universally as genus and species, is distinguished only from those that do not stand under this concept) is, in respect of everything possible, to be distinguished through its relation to the sum-total of all possible predicates.",[]],"reflm:6210":["(g Theology. We can exhaust this object of knowledge; for it is not given to us in itself.)\nThe reason of man requires a threefold completeness: 1. The completeness of the determination of a subject in respect of all possible predicates. 2. The completeness of the derivation of its existence from the existence of others. 3. The completeness of the derivation of the existence of all from one existence, i.e. of the community of all in one single cause. Three ideas.\n1. The completeness of the determination of a thing relative to a certain concept, e.g. the concept of figure, as rectilinear when the figures are regular (as the equilateral triangle, the square), or as curvilinear: the circle. The determination from the principle of ends in respect of all ends: morality, or also of an end that conflicts with all [others]. Completeness of friendship.\n2. The completeness of derivation. Independent existence, the first beginning, the cause that is not itself a *caussatum* [something caused]. The idea of freedom as a kind of causality that is not externally conditioned. The first man. The beginning of the world, or of chaos (the beginning of order), and finally the first existence in general.\n3. Completeness of community, i.e. thoroughgoing determination of the many among one another through the dependence of their existence upon One. E.g. a commonwealth under one lawgiver; the unity of the church under one head. The causality of many causes, so far as it is thoroughly determined through their dependence upon one. The world and God.\nWe have 1. only one concept of a thing in general through which it is thoroughly determined: *ens realissimum* [most real being]; 2. [only one concept which] so far as this concept is the sole one that requires no other for its determination, also an existence which is no consequence of another existence, hence that of an original being [Urwesen]; 3. so far as all other existence |\nis derived, and none but that is original, a community of origin of all things, and consequently a complete concept of their connection with one another. The best world. The all-sufficient being: 1. as the highest being, as subject; 2. the original being, as cause; 3. the being of all beings, as cause of everything. Theology.",[]],"reflm:6211":["How can the *maximum* be a standard of our judgment, when the idea of it surpasses all our given concepts? Because the limitations are positive, and these I leave out entirely in the *maximum*. Thus it is because it is the pure idea, just as the concept of allness arises from multiplicity without limitation.\n[Likewise]\nThus the divine understanding is a pure understanding, the divine will a pure will, the divine presence pure, eternity, etc., etc.\nThe idea of the *maximum* is that which determines most, and is therefore also determining.\nThe idea of the *maximum* alone is *praecisa* [precise, cut off from all else], e.g. perfect justice and morality.",[]],"reflm:6212":["The three tasks (g of metaphysics): God, freedom and immortality, correspond to the three latter antinomies, where (if one takes this series in reverse) simplicity, (g absolute) causality, and necessity can all be applied to the intelligible. The antinomy of the magnitude of the world in space and time does not come into account here, because it is wholly restricted to sensible conditions. Yet through its inadequacy to the unconditioned in general it can lead to the intelligible (the first beginner of the series).",[]],"reflm:6213":["From the above grounds, from the need natural to reason, in |\nrespect of the concepts that spring from itself, to ascend to the [completeness] idea that shall determine those concepts completely, there now arises the concept of a most perfect being: 1. as a thing in general; 2. as a perfect nature (s according to psychological concepts); 3. as the most perfect principle of the system of all ends. [These are] the transcendental, the natural, and the moral perfection. The highest being, now considered [as] at once as the most perfect nature and as the highest good, is God.\n(g Greatest transcendental perfection, greatest natural perfection, greatest practical perfection.)\nThe system of our knowledge of God is now a science, and is entitled theology.\nQuestions. 1. How does theology, as a possible science, stand to the capacity of our reason? Can it be brought to completeness, or does it, like the others, always remain capable of extension? Answer. The object far surpasses the human understanding, and what we might know of it, if it pleased it to reveal itself to us, extends to infinity. But what we can know of it by the light of reason can be measured [completely] exactly, and the natural theology possible to man can be completely set forth, without any extension of this insight ever being hoped for.\n2. Of what kind will be the modes of knowledge (g extending or rectifying) which such a science can supply to us? As knowledge of the common understanding (s as hypothesis, in order to complete the [Nat] chain of natural causes) it will contain various positive [propositions], which however lie before everyone's eyes even without science. As science, however, its business is merely negative: a. to ward off errors, and hence to prevent our contradicting ourselves in the concept of the highest perfection; b. by a sound critique clearly showing the limits of our reason, to moderate the presumption of conceited sophists, who, through groundless arrogance, pretend to be able to penetrate so deeply into the nature of things that they suppose they can refute the rational belief [as ei] in such a being.\n3. What interest has reason in this science? No speculative interest, but merely a practical one, and of this the moral interest alone. This knowledge is meant to make us not more learned but better men, or to give emphasis and constancy to our dispositions already good beforehand. It is to spring from morality and in turn to react upon it. (s It belongs not to physics but to morals.)\n4. With this aim, will common understanding suffice for the knowledge of God, which interests everyone, or will learning and deep speculation be required? Common understanding; for this knowledge must be capable of being demanded of everyone.\n5. What is the minimum of theology? That it be at least possible that a God is, and that no one can know so much as to refute us if we believe in him.\n6. How far does knowing [Wissen] go on this point? Merely so far that our concept agrees with itself and does not conflict with its own aim. The object is a mere idea, and so cannot be given according to concepts of experience; and our positive knowledge [Erkenntnis] here cannot be knowing [Wissen].",[]],"reflm:6214":["P. I:\n(s Theology, either rational or revealed.)\n(s Physicotheology, from the first mover.)\nThe concept must be thoroughly determined *a priori*. This is only the concept of the *ens realissimum* (the most real being). (g Hence this theology is the foundation of the rest.)\n1. *Ens originarium* (original being) (s whose concept and existence are alike not derivative) as *ens summum* (highest being): *Theologia transscendentalis* (transcendental theology). (s This com|prehends ontotheology and cosmotheology.) (s Utility of transcendental theology.)\n2. *Ens originarium* as *summa intelligentia* (supreme intelligence) (s living): *Theologia naturalis* (natural theology).\n3. *Ens originarium* as *summum bonum* (highest good): *Theologia moralis* (moral theology).\n(s The concept of God: 1. as* [isolated concept] *sibi sufficiens conceptus* (a concept sufficient to itself), 2. as limiting concept, 3. as architectonic concept.)\n*(s Possibility from concepts, 2. necessity as ground of the series, 3. of the system.)\nHe who accepts only the first theology asserts merely a world-cause.\n(s Anselm:\nontotheology.\nWolff:\ncosmotheology.)\nHe who accepts the second theology also asserts God as world-author.\n(s Physicotheology.)\nHe who accepts the third theology also asserts God as world-ruler (s lawgiver).\n(s Concept — nature — will of God)\n(s Speculation or moral theology. Moral theology is not theological morals. In the latter, morals is derived from the existence of God; in the former, the existence and the concept of God are determined through morals.)\nDeism, theism. Relig: *Theismus moralis*.\nThe world as a realm, 1. of nature, 2. of ends, 3. of grace.\nThe principle for proving the existence:\n1. From mere concepts, or from experience in general;\n2. From the object of a particular experience, knowledge of the world;\n3. From our ends.\n(s *Ens realissimum* as ground is the principle of all beings and of their [material] real possibility.)\nCertainty is apodictic, or physical (s hypothetical), or moral.\nTo know, to surmise, and to believe.\n(s Utility of transcendental theology: negative.)\nAttributes. 1. Transcendental theology: purely ontological predicates.\n1. *Possibilitas* (possibility) (g *omnimoda*, in every respect). (g *Essentia* (g *originaria*)) (g *Ens summum* (*entium*) as aggregate or as ground. I only restrict the consequences.) (s *contra Atheistas dogmaticos*, against dogmatic atheists) 2. *Existentia* (g *necessaria*) *contra Atheistas*. 3. *Necessitas* *contra scepticos*. 4. *Substantia* (g *non unica*) *contra Spinozam* (g *mundus non aliud*). 5. *Immaterialitas* *contra Anthropomorphismum*. 6. *Vnicitas* *contra Polytheismum* (g Manichaeism). 7. *Extra Mundum* *contra Stoicorum Animam Mundi*. (s *Omnisufficientia* *contra independentiam materiae*.) 8. *Infinitum* (s *incommensurabile*) *contra Anthropomorphismum subtiliorem*. (s We |\nrepresent to ourselves the possibility of all *finita* as derivative.) 9. *Summa perfectio* (g formal, as *consensus* with all reality outside it, hence not as destroyer, but as *conditor realitatis*) *contra Manichaeismum*. 10. *Aeternitas*, *Omnipraesentia*, *Omnipotentia* (*Omnisufficientia*) (g not only formal, but material). (s *Attributa operativa*.) (s *Causa, qvae non habet concausam*, e.g. *Materiam causam mali*.) *Immutabilitas*. *Impassibilis*.\n(s Whether the concept of God is a physically or a morally necessary concept, necessary in order to reason better or to act better? First, the determination of his idea as the supreme principle of all existence. Anthropomorphism. Either from the concept of the highest being to the concept of the original being, or conversely. From the order of nature contingency is inferred. The understanding of the author does not prove his necessity.)\n(s Transcendental theology for the sake of the purity of the concept of God.)\nP. II:\n(g Moral proof of the existence of God, as a necessary hypothesis for the speculative and practical interest.)\nSceptical atheism is godlessness; dogmatic atheism is the denial of God. The first disputes only the possibility of forming a concept of him; the other disputes the possibility of the being itself.\n(g All concepts except that of the *ens realissimum* are undetermined with regard to the remaining predicates.)\nUnity follows from the *omnitudo realitatis* (allness of reality), because in the concept of the *ens realissimum* as *noumenon* space and time do not occur; otherwise two beings with the same properties could be in different places.\n(s The subjective necessity of reason to assume God holds objectively for us.)\n(g from concepts or from *facta*)\nThe ontological proof is one and the same with the pure cosmological proof, except that in the former the necessity is inferred from the concept of the *ens realissimum*, whereas in the latter the concept of the *realissimum* is inferred from the necessity of some being (as independent existence or supreme ground of the actual). The first errs in regarding the proposition: the *ens realissimum* exists, as analytic, whereas it is synthetic and as such cannot be proved. The second errs in taking the concept of the relative necessity of the determination (of a thing (g in general) with regard to the (g opposed) predicates that can belong to a thing in general) for a concept of the absolute necessity of the thing itself*; that is, it holds that the *ens realissimum* is thoroughly determined with regard to all its predicates and that these belong to it necessarily, to be just as much as to say: it exists necessarily. Analytic necessity is again confused with synthetic.\n*(g The error is really this, that one believes one can infer [the real nec] from the logical necessity of the predicates of an *ens realissimum* (since only this concept is throughout necessarily determined) to real necessity. We ought only to say: through this we have a necessary concept of a being, not: a concept of a necessary being.)\n(g Contingent is that which exists only conditionally. Hence only the unconditioned is necessary. We can know this only by its being the condition of everything else. Time and space are contingent forms of our own intuition.)\n(g Anthropomorphism is most opposed to the moral proof.)\nThe theistic concept is a merely relative or regulative, not an absolute and constitutive anthropomorphism. The concept of God is a limiting concept of knowledge of nature according to the speculative laws of reason as a whole.",[]],"reflm:6215":["On Th 2':\nWe live in an age which has not had its like in the history of human understanding. The human mind [indeed] has perhaps already exhausted every possible folly and delusion of a straying reason, and for every new piece of nonsense one will probably always be able to find an example in ancient times. But that all kinds of absurdities and delusions show themselves at once, and indeed publicly, while [the true inquiry of its] reason carries on its business openly and calmly: this is [the] to be regarded as an unavoidable abuse of the freedom to think, which is only now beginning to sprout. Just as in states that have thrown off despotism, this freedom must first produce anarchy and civil disorder, but in the end a lawful civil condition. The [civil] authority of the magistrate does not meddle in any disputes that concern merely (g public (written)) thinking, save only so far as this touches religion, because religion is really a mighty support of morality, and by means of morality of civil [peace] security and union. |\nStates which have an inner stability do indeed allow these disputes free course so far as they remain among the learned. But as soon as separations and factions begin to be generated from them, which in matters of religion commonly produce either partiality towards some or hatred against others, the states become concerned about the outcome, for the balance is hard to maintain. [Were it allowed] It is best, after all, that they let these things go their way and favour reason and science; for in this way alone can a lawful freedom of thought (instead of anarchy) and the supremacy of reason (instead of the despotism of orthodoxy) arise.\n(g Nothing is more harmful than impudent, stupid ignorance.)\nNonsense rests for the most part on the chatter about religion without a prior determination of theology, and indeed first of that theology which has its origin in reason alone, and which must also be laid as the ground of every other theology, whether it be founded on history or on immediate inspiration, |\nas the criterion of its correctness. — It is therefore especially in our times of the greatest importance to set forth a well-considered [theol] theology of mere reason, carried out completely in its whole compass [to be accomplished]; and this can indeed be done, since what is demanded is not to know everything that belongs to the object, but what human reason can know of God. When theology and religion are mixed together, a confusion of concepts arises, in which one regards theology as a necessary consequence and duty of religion, and so already proceeds with partiality. With regard to the former, [all] speculative reason must first be left alone and free.\nIn order that we may first settle the mode of thinking, and thereby the subjective causes of the disagreement of human opinions [here], and, when we sift and test these, may at the same time discover the source from which the absurdities spring, and so be able to divide the history of the aberrations determinately, we must first set the genuine use of reason against the manifold delusion with regard to the use of reason in general, and consider this delusion in particular.\nDelusion is the [maxim] opinion of a freedom in thinking without [the Ber-] regard for the laws of reason. It therefore has as its ground imaginative| power\nor the semblance of the senses. Imitation. Presumptuous ignorance.\nOpposed to delusion is the experiential use of reason. This does not mean that reason must take its grounds from experience, but that it must not think further than so far as it can be applied [to] to experience in accordance with the laws by which experience itself is possible.\nOn Th 3':\nWhat is the experiential use of our reason?\nWe can give meaning to all our concepts, and reality to our judgments, only so far as [that we] so far as (g they) agree with the principles of a possible experience according to them (otherwise they are paraphysical). All principles for explaining the things of experience which are not in conformity with the rules of a possible experience, but [unab-] transcendent. Breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6216":["Someone adopts (g [to]) [principles of experience that ... ] experiences, in connection with which no use of reason is possible at all, and is superstitious; or adopts as a principle of reason something in connection with which no connection with experience is possible, and is fanatical.\nOr someone rejects everything that is not demonstrable either through reason or through experience, and he is unbelieving. He, on the other hand, who also concedes something as a necessary hypothesis, so far as it is indispensable for the completion of the theoretical or practical use of reason, is a believer.\nThe believer indeed adopts something that can be no object of experience at all. Breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6217":["Whoever, then, adopts an idea (g of reason) with which no laws of experience can agree is fanatical; whoever [appear-] adopts laws of experience with which no use of reason can subsist together is superstitious. Whoever indeed recognises the reciprocal agreement of reason and experience in [an ob-] the judging of [an] an object as necessary, but at the same time will concede no |\nother object than one whose existence is sufficiently demonstrable through reason or experience, is unbelieving. (Baar.)\nRational belief is [the necessity] the principle (g maxim) of reason, to assume that which [in the] for the completion of the series of grounds of a reason entirely in agreement with the experiential use is unavoidably necessary. Now the first member of this series can be [proved] proved neither through reason nor by the way of inferences from experience as a determinate object. Rational belief is therefore indispensably necessary for the complete experiential use (by which I understand the derivation of the maxims of reason exercised on objects of experience). Fanaticism is madness; superstition, stupidity; and unbelief, folly, that is, a frivolous abandonment of the main end (namely the completeness of the grounds for whatever can in general be the end of speculation or of practice). The investigative use of reason, then, which [se-] concludes with a rational belief that completes it, is wisdom.",[]],"reflm:6218":["The mode of thinking according to ideas of which no experiential use can be made (g is fanaticism). God, freedom, immortality are ideas of which an experiential use can be made. But secret powers to pervert nature, and spiritual intuitions, are ideas of which, just because they make experience impossible, no experiential use can be made.\nThe mode of thinking by principles drawn from facts of which no experiential use can be made is superstition; for example, presentiments, significant dreams, apparitions of the dead.\nUnbelief is the principle of denying everything that cannot be an object of experience. For there are always still subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true when objectively sufficient ones are lacking. But the subjective ground with regard to the supersensible is reason, which is never sufficiently satisfied through the sensible, in its theoretical and moral employment.",[]],"reflm:6219":["It is (g it) first of all most necessary to |\ncorrect the speculative concept of God; but its value cannot be rated so high that the moral concept would not have to give it its chief determination. Even the unity of this being, if one does not at the same time ascribe to it a holy will, can furnish no secure religion.\nThe mode of thinking [der] of a reason that separates itself entirely from the use in experience is fantasticality (Phantasterey). (Whim-chasing.) (g Chimera. Dreaming.)\nThis sentence has subsequently been altered by words or letters written above, as follows, without the words that no longer fit the new context having been struck through:\nThe mode of thinking of a reason that conflicts with the principles of the use in experience is delusion (Wahn).\n* Fantasticality is: 1. Fanaticism (Schwärmerey), i.e. a [through] fantasticality that seeks to raise itself, by a higher reason (g by means of pure intuition), above the reason that adheres solely to (is limited to) the use in experience. 2. Superstition: a fantasticality that, by means of (g empirical intuition) experience (one's own or that of others), would raise itself above (the use in experience of) reason. (g To this experience are reckoned narratives of wondrous effects (which conflict with all laws of experience), or of wondrous events united by fantasy as cause and effect.)\nThe former is delusional madness (g madness), the latter is stupidity. The first is of two kinds: either of the delusion of immediate illumination or of transcendent speculation, e.g. of astral nature-spirits. The supposed derangement.\n*(g The transcendent use of reason is that which ventures to judge without any [possibility] relation of it to possible experience. Diametrically opposed to this is the denial of reason in respect of what deviates wholly from experience. Consequently a denial of the use of reason in experience as well as of its transcendent use.)\nUnbelief is the maxim of admitting no other use of reason in experience (g hence no use of reason at all) than in regard to an object of experience. Hence it must hold everything that |\nis not an object of the senses either to be impossible (this it can do only if it regards the senses as the mode of knowledge through which alone objects are immediately represented as they are) or, as contrary to the use of reason in experience and so to the maxims of its use in general, at least as dispensable and wholly groundless, to assume such a thing, e.g. God [as], who is no object of experience, and on that account to regard him as nothing, or at least (his presupposition) as wholly dispensable and unnecessary for reason. The former unbelief is the empiricist, the second the sophistical or rationalist, which [believes] thinks to explain [to be able to] everything, or also to be able to furnish all practical laws with sufficient moving force [thus an influence of the if] and asserts a use of reason in experience, namely its power to produce good actions, although it assumes merely objects of experience and no other real things, but makes use merely of reason.\nThe believer* is he who admits (g to assume) for his reason what is indispensably necessary to its completeness, whether in theoretical or in practical use in experience, although it cannot prove it.\n*(g The small number of believers who, from moral interest, are capable of a rational belief. They want to have it in cash: either in modes of knowledge or in the advantages of life.)\nThe belief without which it is impossible to make even the use of reason in experience, whether theoretical or practical, [completely] self-sufficient, is a pure rational belief.\nWithout a pure rational belief (g the use of reason becomes) either omniscience (pansophy) or misology, [self-] suicide of reason.\nTo become superstitious: to this men have a natural* propensity; they personify natural causes, and do not yet know the lawfulness of nature, nor the importance of the use of reason solely under [the] presupposition of that lawfulness. They are therefore easily brought, when something is unusual or they desire something vehemently (or when they dream), to imagine something they do not see, which knows their wishes and can be moved or appeased by them. Superstition without determinate representations of its objects.\n*(g Fanaticism is boastful (presumptuous) ignorance and a convulsive condition that is contagious through sympathy.)\nWe can make no other applied use of reason, i.e. one in which the object is given (g immanent use), than by being able to produce in thought, in accordance with laws of experience, what we think.",[]],"reflm:6220":["The delusion of honour on account of fame after death.\nThe delusion of honour on account of the high esteem of what has no inner worth.\nMoral perfection, although it cannot be wholly attained, is yet no delusion.\nFanatical and superstitious delusion. God is yet no delusion.\nThe use of reason in experience is that use of reason which can prove [according to the rules] (g its reality) its truth by exhibiting its (concepts and) principles in experience, even if one assumes something that is not an object of experience, e.g. the soul as an incorporeal being, or pure morality. If one merely assumes something of this kind without being able to prove it, the very assumption of it is nevertheless in accord with the use of reason in experience, in respect of the form [of the] of its application and of the principles, just as we proceed in general with regard to hypotheses.\nEvery use of reason that does not agree with the principles of the use in experience is delusion; e.g. to sense heavenly influences, to have influence on the realm of spirits.\nAll use of [pure] reason is either a use in experience, whose application to experience is possible, or a [over] chimerical use of it. The maxim of the latter is delusion.\nTo the former there is laid at the foundation [is] either merely an object of experience or also an object of mere reason, but with application to objects of experience according to the rules of the latter. The first is the physical, the second the [pure] use in experience of pure reason.\nHe who denies the possibility of the last is an unbeliever.",[]],"reflm:6221":["Th 5':\n(g The need of reason to think, beyond the things of experience, something further which cannot be experienced and [und wegen der Zweke Vernunft hat: the text appears defective] on account of the ends has reason, is the first cause of assuming gods.)\nHow did men first come to the opinion that invisible powers exist, which the ordinary course of experience could not have taught them?\nEither 1. by the path of reason, or 2. by that of imagination; (g for objects which neither experience nor reason has given are possible only through imagination;) on the first: a. through proof of reason, b. through rational belief [Vernunftglauben]; on the second: a. through enthusiasm [Schwärmerey] (g intuition of reason), or b. superstition.\nThe first path is not the one which men took at the beginning.\nOf the second path, guidance by enthusiasm is likewise not the first, for it presupposes trials and even some beginnings of rational knowledge, which however do not satisfy (g reason) imagination. Hence it is imagination without the guiding thread of reason, and so supported by apparent experience, without knowing (g demanding) its (g constant) conformity to law (in which the empirical use of reason consists); that is, it is superstition, which first brought invisible powers, or even [personal] might, on the scene.\n(g Rational belief is earlier than proof of reason. Both are grounded on the striving of reason after completeness.)\nThese [powers] could at first be only powers of visible things (g objects of experience) which, however, did not stand under the rule of experience: fetish, manitou, talisman, or the shades of the dead (g objects of past experiences), or finally invisibly ruling beings. The last found a place only in the condition of civil constitution under chiefs, and so arose the proper concept of gods. Whether men at first represented these as fearful, because misfortune and dangers make men superstitious and yearning hopes also play a part, or whether the provision of nature, as being their work, led men to represent them as benevolent, cannot be the question.\nThat these gods should even be the authors of nature could not occur to men; they held them to be themselves natural things (g or men who had formerly lived), which merely had more power, but held all natural things to be necessary in themselves.\nThese gods had to be persons, otherwise men could not direct their superstitious wishes to them, but powerful without morality. Alone, however, they could not think of any god, just for the reason that being alone seems a misfortune; hence polytheism. Finally it comes to moral concepts: a law and a lawgiver. Unity of God, and now all perfection. — Up to this point sheer superstition, and now reason. — Men were not content with the influence on morals. — Now arose enthusiasm. Neoplatonic sects last as long as proofs of reason alone are to count. — Only rational belief, with consciousness of its ignorance, can keep enthusiasm away.\nTh 5:\nRational knowledge [Vernunfterkentnis] is either subjective, that is, knowledge of one's own reason, or objective: knowledge of an object (g distinct from ourselves) through reason. The necessary pre-|supposition of a first ground in the series of things subordinated to one another is not a knowledge of the necessity of this first [ground] in itself. That would be knowledge of its absolute necessity, of which we are wholly incapable. Nor is it the known hypothetical necessity of the thing, but the knowledge of the subjective necessity of the hypothesis according to principles of reason, or rather of the assumption of an absolutely necessary thing. This necessity of a thing determined through concepts which do not lie merely in pure reason, for the explanation of appearances or of practical rules, is not knowing, not opining (hypothetically), but believing.",[]],"reflm:6222":["God signifies to crude men a being which has reason and has more power than all the voluntarily acting powers of nature known in experience. Yet at times lifeless things, such as fetish and manitou, have been taken for it. Departed men.",[]],"reflm:6223":["In and beside, perhaps also on, Th § 4:\nReason requires a great deal in order to satisfy itself in the concept of a supreme ground of things, above all not merely in pure use, but as applied to all experience as well as to morals. Nature and freedom.",[]],"reflm:6224":["On Th § 2:\n[The] religion is the consideration of moral laws as divine commands.\nMorals teaches how we become good men, [theology] religion how we become men well-pleasing to God.\nMorals teaches how we may become worthy of the highest good, religion teaches how we may become partakers of it.",[]],"reflm:6225":["On Th § 2:\nTheology, so far as it has influence on morals, is (moral) religion; so far as it [itself] contains a special object of morals, it is *cultus*. This would presuppose religion.\nFor religion belief is enough; for *cultus* one must know, otherwise it is hypocrisy.",[]],"reflm:6226":["On Th § 2:\nMoral religion is that which makes men better.\n*Cultus* is the religion which, if it is to be genuine, already presupposes good men, since they are to take duty toward God itself to heart.\nThe mere possibility of the divine existence is already sufficient for moral religion, though not so much as belief.\nBelief is absolutely necessary for *cultus*, indeed hardly sufficient.\nFor actual divine worship knowledge is required; otherwise one worships only to keep oneself safe in every eventuality, not from conviction.",[]],"reflm:6227":["On Th § 2, conclusion:\nReligion is the moral disposition so far as it has the knowledge of God as its ground (not the pragmatic). There is indeed no religion without knowledge of God; but this need not be knowing at all; it can be merely a pure idea of God, which is morally correct (although as speculation full of errors), and secondly can contain only the conviction that it is yet possible that there is a God, or besides this a firm belief. For the former, morality is not required; but if it is there, it can together with that problematic judgment [zus] in combination yield religion. For the last, a morally good disposition is already required.\nWhat is the minimum of theology that is requisite and sufficient for religion? (1. (g With regard to existence:) the possibility of assuming one. 2. With regard to the concept: the moral and the metaphysical concept connected with it. (The ancients had a *cultus* without religion (g moral), hence also without theology from reason, but [only] tradition.) Philosophical theology cannot extend that minimum with regard to the positive about God, but can bring it to science by means of the negative, through which the errors unavoidable in common theology are kept off. — Philosophical (g rational) theology is not divinity [Gottesgelahrtheit]; the *revelata*, as grounded on ancient documents and ancient languages, alone is divinity.)",[]],"reflm:6228":["On Th § 3:\nHas our knowledge of God dignity, and can we occupy ourselves with nothing else more worthily? The object has the highest dignity, but our acquaintance with it is very meagre: it consists for the most part only of limitations of what we know, namely so far as it is merely creature. Hence our vocation is not to search into the divine nature, and this knowledge is of no value; but rather: 1. To conclude and crown with it our knowledge of nature and of morals, by regarding everything as belonging to the infinite cause, hence in the highest connection, and likewise by representing our will as contained under the universal legislation in the realm of ends. 2. Not to inquire what God is, but what we ought to be in relation to him, that is, what use the idea of him can be to us.",[]],"reflm:6229":["On Th § 3, heading:\nSpeculative (logical) truth here consists merely in the possibility of this concept; practical truth in the necessity of presupposing this being in regard to all moral laws.",[]],"reflm:6230":["On Th § 3 »der eigentliche Gottesgelehrte«:\nAre there also divines of natural religion?\nAs regards knowing, the philosopher here knows merely his ignorance, that is, he knows the limits of his reason; but this too requires science. The delimitation of nature in every respect makes the concept of God necessary to us, but, since it goes beyond the concepts of nature, also unattainable as speculation.",[]],"reflm:6231":["In Th § 3 no. 1 Kant inserts after »haben«:\nthat I think something in connection with them and can give them in examples.",[]],"reflm:6232":["Under Th § 3:\nAmong the Greeks: Thales physicist, Anaxagoras theologian, Anaximander (physicist), Xenophanes (theologian).",[]],"reflm:6233":["On »Irrthümer« in the heading of Th § 4:\nThose which are not practical are to that extent also pardonable.",[]],"reflm:6234":["On ZuTh § 5 beginning:\nThe real difficulty consists in this, that the concept is transcendent, and that no property of it can be given in any possible experience, so that no example can be added to the concepts.\nWe [müss] ought to leave out everything sensible from it; but then nothing remains but a concept without corresponding intuition, thus without examples and without application *in concreto*, except only by analogy and *symbola*.\nZuTh § 5 No. 1:\nThe limits do not hinder us so much as the inclination to overstep them, and the self-conceit and presumption of reason, which introduce false judgments and pass off incomprehensibility as impossibility.",[]],"reflm:6235":["Moral theology has as its principle: If the laws of duty stand firm *a priori*, and consequently flow necessarily from practical reason, but yet have no force to move the will without the presupposition of a rational, morally perfect being as author of the whole of nature, then this presupposition is inseparable from practical reason, and the idea of God must be framed not according to the observation of nature but according to the need of morality. In this I am relieved of all the circuitousness of speculation. I can declare it wholly unfit for this purpose, and demand only that it at least cannot prove that there is no God, and I have enough in faith. The proposition has no difficulty; the difficulty lies in the speculative attainment of it.",[]],"reflm:6236":["In moral theology it is [kan] enough to presuppose that it is at least possible that a God exists, and that no one can ever prove his non-being; hence we are entitled, in a practical [respect], and indeed for the sake of necessary laws, [um] to lay this existence at the basis by way of hypothesis. For these laws are absolutely necessary, but cannot become subjectively practical without that presupposition.\nThe practical indifferentism of theology makes speculative theology the only possible one, and, if the critique of reason is not favourable to it, leads to sceptical atheism. Moral theology, however, permits a theism which can at the same time be critical with regard to speculative theology.\nTheology as principle of virtue serves only to remove from morality the hindrances that could be drawn from the objection of an empty ideality. As principle of religion it is itself, through duty towards a higher being, a motive to virtue. As principle of a divine service [*Gottesdienst*] it is the ground of actions whose effect is directed immediately at God. — A subjectively necessary hypothesis is one where I see no other ground of explanation; an objectively necessary one, where I see that no other is possible for human reason.",[]],"reflm:6237":["The atheism of doubt: sceptical (g which denies all knowledge of and conviction about God).\nThe dogmatic atheism of assertion (g which denies the existence of God).\nThe critical deism (with regard to the capacity of pure and speculative reason) is moral.\nThe physico-theological deism is in itself indeterminate and a source |\nof all theological errors contrary to religion, of polytheism and anthropomorphism.\nIn physico-theology I infer from the contingent connection of ends to the existence of God, and this concept can therefore not be determined.",[]],"reflm:6238":["Even if neither reason nor nature proved to me the existence of the most perfect being, reason would nevertheless need a concept of it, in order merely [zu] to estimate what is derived in accordance with it.",[]],"reflm:6239":["The supreme concept (g which) lies at the basis of all things.\n1. As complete concept of a thing in general,\n2. as complete concept for nature, as ground of explanation,\n3. as complete concept for the ends of rational beings.\n1. God as the highest being.\n2. God as the supreme universal cause.\n3. God as the highest good.\na) For pure speculative reason,\nb) for the empirical use of reason with regard to nature,\nc) for the practical.\nAlways the unconditioned and at the same time the complete.",[]],"reflm:6240":["Belief of a thing and [seines] of a being is distinguished from belief in this being. The latter is the presupposition of its existence as supreme practical ground, thus of one in which I take a moral interest.",[]],"reflm:6241":["First, the need of (g pure) reason with regard to (g the concept of) a complete thing in general: to serve all others as foundation and measure. Second, the need of reason with regard to an existence: to serve as ground for all other existence. Third, the need of reason with regard to an |\nexistence which is possible only according to concepts, namely of the ends actually present (of nature), or of the merely possible ends, yet at the same time necessary as such (of freedom).\nFor all this reason requires unity: 1. of [Besti] thoroughgoing determination (g of each thing for itself), 2. of (g thoroughgoing) connection in one world, 3. of purposiveness.\nThis Refl. has afterwards been greatly altered, from the 1st *dienen* on, by additions and deletions, so that it now reads as follows:\nand with regard to an existence: to serve as ground for all other existence. Second, the need of reason with regard to an existence which is possible only according to concepts through nature, namely of the ends actually present (of nature). Third: to serve as principle of that which is possible only according to concepts through [Fr] our freedom* (necessary ends (of freedom)).\n(* g A.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to those (g of the first of the things) which are possible only as *causata*. *Primus Motor*. B.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to (g the first of) those which are possible only (g as *caussata*) [durch] according to concepts (purposiveness). (g of the first of causality according to concepts) C.\nNeed of a (g first) thing with regard to that which outside nature, thus of freedom, is possible only [durch] according to concepts, but in harmony with nature. )",[]],"reflm:6242":["God is a concept (idea) of the first of all beings, which is the supreme cause of everything else.",[]],"reflm:6243":["The last end is moral theology. Therefore the concept of God must be sufficient (g determined) for it.",[]],"reflm:6244":["Minimum of rational theology for the sake of morals, since it makes better human beings.\nThat it is possible that a God exists is sufficient for religion, but not for cult [*Cultus*]; for this presupposes not merely belief but knowledge. The proposition 'one ought to believe' presupposes that he who says it knows what (g he) wants to have believed.\n(g The minimum of rational theology is a concept of God that agrees well with itself and with the need of reason in principles, above all the practical ones, together with the possibility of his existence, and consequently the warrant to assume it (g to opine). The maximum is knowledge [*Wissen*], i.e. (g complete) holding-true [*Vorwarhalten*], so far as it rests on proofs. The middle one, [angemessene] suited to the least capacity and to the best will, is belief [*Glauben*], which is the acknowledgment of the necessity of such a hypothesis for one's own either theoretical or practical use (theoretical and practical belief).)\n(g For virtue [gehört] the minimum of theology is (g sufficient for virtue), namely mere opinion; for fear of God (g religion) the *majus*, namely belief; for cult (divine service) the maximum of knowledge [*Erkenntnis*], namely knowing [*Wissen*].)",[]],"reflm:6245":["Since the concept of the original being is wholly negative, namely that in its possibility and existence it derives from no other ([und] is not derivative), whereby, however, one does not know what it is, the concept of the original is here to lead to a concept thoroughly determined *a priori*.\nBut the concept of an original being is wholly arbitrary if one does not presuppose that something exists; in the latter case, however, it is necessary to assume such a being. But since some existence is then presupposed, the proof is not ontological but cosmological, though yet transcendental.\nIf the existence of the original being is to be established by ontological grounds of proof alone, the existence must be inferred from mere concepts and not precede *a posteriori*. In the ontological way, then, there must be found, from mere concepts of possible beings, that one which includes existence in itself, and this [ist der] seems to be the concept of the *ens realissimum*.\nThis has the peculiarity that through a universal concept the object is at the same time thoroughly determined, and so far is similar to the concept of an existing thing. But since, besides the concept in its thoroughgoing determination, something else must be added, namely, not a new determination, but the position [*Position*] of the thing in itself outside the concept, i.e. existence, the thoroughgoing determination cannot yet include existence, and the proposition: *ens realissimum* exists, is not an analytic but a synthetic proposition; hence it cannot be found by mere analysis.",[]],"reflm:6246":["Why, however, if all reality is to be laid at the foundation of the concepts of all things, is all reality in one being and not in many? Because the multiplicity of things, as things in general, is possible only through the diverse limitation of the unity, and so of the whole [all] of reality. Consequently each of these beings is possible only through a greater one [only], and the greatest alone can therefore contain original (not derivative) possibility. So many beings that cast shadows presuppose a foreign light by which they are illuminated; only that which is [no] light without any possible shadow, i.e. the original light, presupposes no foreign one.",[]],"reflm:6247":["It is a question whether the abso|lute necessity of its existence is also required for the concept of the *ens originarium* (original being). May one not perhaps say that it is neither necessary in itself nor contingent in itself? For both relate only to relative position and to the existence of the inhering, i.e. of an incomplete being, which is itself no thing. The subsisting may perhaps indeed be thought as non-existent; but non-being can never be something that can befall it once it is considered as existing, nor can its coming-to-be either.\nAll proofs of the existence of God and of the determination of his concept rest in the end on the transcendental proposition: that the most perfect being is an *ens originarium* (s *possibilitas originaria* and *existentia originaria*), or, conversely, that the *ens originarium* is a most perfect being. Consequently there cannot be more than one such being, and everything else must be derived from it. The first of these two propositions is the ground of ontotheology, and there existence is inferred from the concept of its properties. The second is the content of cosmotheology (s *theologia naturalis*), and there, conversely, its concept [auf das] is inferred from the existence of something in general. [Die Physi] Thus all theology is properly built upon the transcendental concept of the *ens realissimum*. Physicotheology makes use of the contingency of the |\nthings of this world (chiefly on the ground of their purposiveness) in order to infer a being distinct from the world. Afterwards, however, it follows the transcendental path in order to infer from this the concept and existence of the most perfect being; so far it has no advantage over transcendental theology. But now it infers from the particular properties of this world to the (g psychological) properties of the highest being as a highest intelligence, and so transforms deism into theism. Moral theology adds to the realm of ends, as a realm of nature, the concept of a sovereign in the realm of morals, and makes of the highest being the highest good.\nIf ontotheology does not succeed, neither does cosmotheology; if both, i.e. transcendental theology, do not succeed, then neither does physicotheology, and consequently there is no knowledge [Wissen] at all. What then remains is belief. Cosmotheology gives God as first cause of the contingent, and in this has an advantage over ontotheology, because it gives indication of a free will (*summa intelligentia*, highest intelligence). Physicotheology gives indication of a will governing the world: *summus imperans* (supreme ruler).",[]],"reflm:6248":["The transcendental concept of God is no knowledge, because no object can be posited as corresponding to it, e.g. 'A most real being exists.' It is a thought through mere logical concepts, but not through categories, because these require intuitions to which they can be related.\nThe *omnitudo* (allness) in the concept of the *realissimum* is not the same as logical universality (one in all). For the latter is distributive, the former collective (many that make up one), namely synthetic unity. Whether objective reality belongs to this concept I cannot divine from my thoughts; I must adduce an intuition, e.g. a circle. But this is always sensible and is not to be applied to the *ens realissimum*, and another intuition I do not have. Hence I cannot procure objective reality for it, i.e. my thoughts cannot be entitled modes of knowledge of the object. Reality is that whose representation in itself contains a being [ein seyn]; [ob dergle] what this is supposed to mean I do not even rightly understand, although I do understand logical being in the relation of concepts. I want an example, and that is sensation (of the senses); but this cannot serve as an example for God, and consequently I cannot give him objective reality.",[]],"reflm:6249":["To assume the existence of the unconditioned, reason has an objective ground of knowledge. This knowledge is objectively certain. But |\nhow is it to be determined? All concepts of possibility apart from the *realissimum* are conditioned; for everything is either *realissimum* or *limitatum* (limited), and with us the concept of the *limitatum* is at all times derivative in respect of the *realissimum*. The latter alone is *conceptus originarius* (original concept), namely a *principium cognoscendi non derivativum* (a non-derivative principle of knowing). And, what is noteworthy, reason, in order to represent a possibility according to thoroughgoing determination, and so as complete, must conceive the principle of all possibility in the *realissimum*. But on that account the *ens originarium* is not straightway the *principium essendi* (principle of being) of all things.\nOf the *ens originarium* [soll b] it is thought that it is not merely no *derivativum* but itself a *principium* (*non principiatum*, not itself derived from a principle). That is its [negative] general concept as the unconditioned principle. In order to determine it I must think it as *realissimum*, i.e. as a subjective ground, i.e. a need of human reason and a necessary hypothesis, merely in order to make the possibility of things thinkable. But if the practical need, by which I am constrained (g to it) to think it, is added, then a belief results, since theoretical and practical reason agree and yet there is no insight.\nThus: 1. The subjective condition of thinking possibilities is taken for the objective condition of the possibility of the things themselves, as dependent on an *ens realissimum*.\n2. The concept of the *realissimum* as an all-sufficient ground, or as a being containing all reality [be] in itself.\n3. The determination of this concept through predicates of intuition (because this is sensible and not adequate to it) is impossible. Thus I can think an *ens realissimum* through logical functions, but cannot bring it under categories by theoretical reason, because these relate merely to intuition.\nThe proposition that the unconditioned must be the condition not merely of one |\nkind of knowledge but at the same time of all, follows from this: that otherwise it would itself be limited, and so its possibility would again be conditioned.",[]],"reflm:6250":["On Th § 9:\nWhether it is an invention to ascend to the concept of a most perfect being.\nWhether a given determination belongs to the concept of the most real being one can never know with certainty. Is it extended or not? Here we can only say that extension does not belong to it, because it comprises limitations, being and non-being, and consequently non-being belongs to its [Re] determination; but in non-being we know nothing real. Where we believe we know something merely real, e.g. knowledge, there |\nit is uncertain whether it does not stand in real conflict with other reality, which is still more fruitful, i.e. whether the grounds of the one do not cancel one another in the same subject, or the consequences reciprocally annihilate each other.",[]],"reflm:6251":["On Th § 9, heading:\n(g independent:) The primal being [Urwesen],\n(g perfect:) highest being,*\n(g all-sufficient:) being of all beings.\nThe word infinite can come only afterwards.\n*(g This concept is of such a kind that it thoroughly determines its object, and indeed in respect of what underlies all opposed predicates, namely the real.\n*Ens summum est igitur ens entium* (the highest being is therefore the being of beings), for all manifoldness is that of limits.\n*ideoque ens summum non est derivativum, sed originarium* (and so the highest being is not derivative but original). An idea as *prototypon* (archetype).)",[]],"reflm:6252":["On Th § 9:\nEither we begin from the *realissimum* or from the *necessarium*, and infer from the former concept to the latter, or conversely.",[]],"reflm:6253":["The concept of a negation is a derived concept, for it presupposes that of reality. But neither is it by itself the concept of a thing (because every *ens* must be something real, and negation is therefore only the determination of a real). It is thus only limitation. And since the limitation of a thing already in part negative presupposes just such a limitation, the original concept on which all thoroughgoing determination |\n(g of a thing as a thing in general) rests is that of an *ens realissimum*. Thoroughgoing determination is an idea which cannot be carried out *in concreto* (g as *limitatum*); but for a thing as a thing in general it would be that of a thing as *realissimum*. For the complete is the ground of the possibility of determining everything incomplete [as a thing].",[]],"reflm:6254":["The concept of an *ens perfectissimum* (most perfect being) does not contain as much (g and is also not so determinate) as the concept of an all-sufficient being (*omnisufficiens*); for even if no being were sufficient for everything possible, one could still be a sufficient ground for most of what is possible. Some moral concepts already contain the relation to this *omnitudo* (allness) without containing a *superlativus*. These are the solely wise, the holy, and the blessed, because all three [the highe] contain relation to the highest good, in respect of knowledge, of the [feel] will as legislating, and of the same as good.",[]],"reflm:6255":["A most perfect being, of whatever kind it may be, is thoroughly determined* through this concept, and if it is thought only according to metaphysical perfection, it is likewise thoroughly determined through this concept (although the concepts of place and time cannot be found in it). Now we call something, so far as through its concept it is still undetermined in respect of many predicates, to that extent merely possible; for the actual is thoroughly determined, although [what is thoroughly determined is] not on that account actual. For although I can say: everything actual is thoroughly determined, I cannot say: everything thoroughly determined is actual.\n*(g Hence Plato's Ideas are represented as individual beings which, unchangeable and eternal, are the self-subsistent archetypes of things.\nAs a thing in general (*metaphysice*) only one being is thoroughly |\n[deter] determined through its concept. All other things can be considered as if, through the omission of some determinations, they gave all possibilities.)",[]],"reflm:6256":["It is impossible to determine a concept thoroughly; but it is subjectively necessary to represent to oneself the thoroughgoing determination of it as the idea of the completeness of the concept, i.e. to represent the object without this subjective limitation; and so we think each object, so far as it is posited in itself, namely in the completeness of the determination of the concept of an object in general. If this concept is at the same time that of a real object, i.e. of a thing (g consequently the concept of an object so far as already more closely determined), then the complete determination of it is the concept of the *ens realissimum*. No other concept puts into our hands a rule for determining the object in respect of each given predicate. It is therefore the condition of the application of the principle of thoroughgoing determination to an object thought a priori.",[]],"reflm:6257":["On Th § 10:\nWhether one can really think, objectively, many realities, or one without limits?\nThe greatest reality as ground is at the same time bound up with all the others. Whoever posits one attribute of God posits all.",[]],"reflm:6258":["On Th § 10:\nWe seek to recognize true reality (g not phenomenal) only by trying whether it can belong to God.",[]],"reflm:6259":["On Th § 10:\n*realitas phaenomenon* (phenomenal reality) is (g not) pure reality and does not belong to the *ens realissimum*.\nThe ideal *prototypon* [all] as *logicum*. Everything positive requires the determinations of things by the understanding. For we take from the real of our (g possible) experience only what belongs to the concept of the understanding.\nDependence alone constitutes the sure mark of a *realitas phaenomenon*. The question now is whether we can ascribe to him even a single one besides the transcendental [realities], which are properly only titles for concepts, or whether all that we can give *in concreto* can be ascribed to him only *per analogiam*.",[]],"reflm:6260":["On Th § 10, Remark, sentence 3: »Ein endlicher — Grössen«:\nwere that so, we could not use the concept of our understanding, by raising it to completeness, in order to represent the divine understanding to ourselves.",[]],"reflm:6261":["On Th § 11, sentence 2: »widerspreche«:\nbut indeed [it contradicts it] in this respect: that it cannot subsist with it in one subject, [without] namely, can follow from a common ground.",[]],"reflm:6262":["On Th § 11:\nAll predicates of God are derived either from his concept as *ens originarium* (g of his independence) or as *realissimum*. The first, which are at the same time those of absolute necessity, are the most certain.* (g e.g. simple — immaterial.) Unity from the concept of the *realissimum*.\n*(g for that the *ens originarium* is a *realissimum* is itself only a *hypothesis*, inferring from subjective conditions of reason to objective ones.)\nOneness (*Einigkeit*) says that the highest reality cannot be distributed among many things, nor belong in its entirety to many things. The first [is] follows from the simplicity of the *originarium*.\nWe cannot indeed see the possibility [God of the concept of] of God as the sum total of all reality [but] (for the possibility of the concept, since realities do not contradict one another, is not enough); [for] but yet we can be sure that no one can even make the opposite comprehensible; for realities that conflict with one another in one being must throughout be *realitates phaenomena*, and these are not in God.",[]],"reflm:6263":["On Th § 11? On Th § 12?\nThere is a great difference whether one regards the *ens realissimum*, in respect of all reality, as its subject or as its ground. According to the latter idea, beings possessed of understanding could come from it without its itself having understanding. For the cause need not have the quality of the effect. The expression »God is the highest reality« is the genus under which those two concepts stand as species.",[]],"reflm:6264":["On Th § 12, beginning:\nThe highest reality: 1. as content of the thing, 2. as ground.\nAll reality and the All of reality [In ei] as a single being.\nThe All is the standard of mere reason, which recognizes no limited unity otherwise.",[]],"reflm:6265":["On Th § 12, beginning:\nWhether I should not rather say: a reality in its highest degree?",[]],"reflm:6266":["On Th § 12, Remark 1, sentence 3:\n[The] Infinity is a mathematical concept.\nThe magnitude of the given mathematically infinite is also determined through this concept, but only relatively to human understanding. It is that which, in relation to the unit as measure, is greater than every number.\nThe metaphysically infinite is that which contains everything, and hence the whole possible magnitude of a certain reality.",[]],"reflm:6267":["On Th § 12, Remark 3, concluding sentence: »bestimmbar — finitam«:\n*definitam*",[]],"reflm:6268":["On Th § 13:\nInner infinity: extensive, intensive infinity.\nInfinity in real relation: just as before.",[]],"reflm:6269":["On Th § 13:\nGod as *ens summum*. Deism.\nThe concept of the *ens realissimum* as *ens summum* determines itself thoroughly (g in respect of the (g ontological) predicates), but not that of the *infinitum*.\nBecause all negations are here regarded as limits, the *ens realissimum* is an *ens illimitatum*.\nWhether each reality, so far as it is represented (in relation to all possible reality) as absolutely complete, could not furnish a central concept of the *ens realissimum*, and whether the highest understanding, the highest power, the Eternal, the All-blessed, each taken separately [the Go] do not constitute the complete concept of God.\nIf his existence can be seen a priori, then the *ens summum* is a necessary being.\nIf a determinate concept of the absolute necessity of a being is possible, then its existence is thereby also proved.",[]],"reflm:6270":["Probably with reference to Th § 13 Note 2 no. 4:\nPossibility and actuality are not distinguished in things in themselves; rather, [through] in relation to the concept the thing is merely possible, and in relation to a completely determinate intuition it is actual. — When I say: a thing is possible, this means: it can be (but not: because it can be, therefore it is). (g Our concept of possibility goes |\nmerely to relations; the absolute admits of no concept of possibility that would be distinguished from actuality.)\nOf every thing we can ask why it is, because we cannot derive its existence from any concept; but on that account it is not contingent.\n[Possibility] Necessity and contingency are therefore only relations of things to the concept. In themselves things are neither necessary nor contingent; they [are] exist, and their non-being can very well be reconciled with their concept alone; but under the condition of connection with another existence the non-being [of it] of the same is impossible, i.e. they can be regarded as necessary in a conditioned manner and yet as inwardly contingent according to concepts. In themselves, however, no such separation from the conditions takes place, and so there is neither conditioned nor unconditioned necessity. For possibility is distinguished from actuality only in relation to concepts.\nOnly with regard to the idea of freedom is possibility distinguished from actuality. Actions as appearances are determined in space and time (the determining ground of actuality), and there actuality is one and the same with possibility, i.e. with necessity. On the other hand, these very same actions are free in respect of the intellectual subject, which determines itself merely according to concepts, since in themselves they are nothing and are possible only in respect of the activity according to concepts, as ways of representing this activity sensibly; thus the actions, as [appearance] determination of the intellectual subject, are neither sensibly neces|sary\nnor contingent, but not sensible at all, and so far free from natural necessity.\nThe absolute possibility of a thing we cannot comprehend, i.e. cognize and have insight into a priori, for that would already be the insight into possibility without anything being given; yet the possibility is to be known without actuality and prior to it, and it is to be synthetic. Synthetic impossibility can never be comprehended, e.g. that of a figure, unless a synthesis in our sensible intuition [i.e. a] is previously laid at the foundation, through which the object is given a priori in relation to our sensibility. Hypothetical possibility can be comprehended when something has already been given as actual, together with the law of effects and causes. The absolute necessity of a thing, however, lies wholly beyond our concept; yet we see that possibility precedes actuality only in relation to our concept, and that in themselves the one is not separated from the other.",[]],"reflm:6271":["On Th § 14:\nEverything that belongs to its determinations belongs to its essence, for it is thoroughly determined through its concept.\nIts possibility is not distinguished from its actuality, and so neither is its essence from its nature.\nYet here there is as yet no talk of existence, but only of the concept of the *ens realissimum* (most real being).",[]],"reflm:6272":["On Th § 14 sentences 3–5:\nThe transcendental concepts are pure only when one leaves out the conditions of their empirical use and in general all conditions of sensibility, e.g. presence, or cause, upon which something follows.",[]],"reflm:6273":["On Th § 14 sentence 3: »ohne Bedenken beilegen«:\nFor thereby we determine a thing only in general, without specially limiting it and distinguishing it from the most real of all.",[]],"reflm:6274":["On Th § 14:\nWhat first matters is to show what lies in the concept of the *ens realissimum* with regard to the ontological predicates, [as] so far as it is considered according to its content or as ground. Afterwards, existence.\n1. Analytic predicates. Ontological, except existence.\n2. Synthetic: first* the absolute, secondly the relative, as cause of a given world.\n*(g of its absolute or relative existence [of the] in relation to a world. In Spinozism God has no relative existence at all.)",[]],"reflm:6275":["On Th § 14:\n(g The atheist posits no existence: the dogmatic atheist (Gottesleugner) denies the possibility, the sceptical (Ohngötter) denies every proof of actuality. The former [denies] the concept, the latter the ground of proof.)\nThe atheist must nevertheless concede the analytic predicates of God (g for the possibility of such a being cannot be contested).\nThe deist also concedes the synthetic ones, but only transcendental ones, not the physiological, from fear of anthropomorphism. He therefore also has no moral use of the knowledge of God.\nSpinoza was no atheist in the transcendental sense, nor a deist either, for he denied only the cosmotheology.\n[He] His error, however, arose from a false ontology, in that he so framed the concept of a substance that there could be only a single one.\n(g One could say with much plausibility in favour of Spinozism: if all forces and faculties of a substance created and preserved by God are merely divine actions, and if apart from these nothing in them can be thought by us, then one cannot see at all how the subject of them is to be posited outside God. On the other hand, if we perceive in ourselves action, and in regard to other things counteraction, then again it cannot be seen how we should be accidents, which can never be subjects of acting and suffering.)\nThe [is] deist is so often from modesty, the theist is so (g often) from arrogance.\nThe [reality] relative existence of God as creator of the world, not as world-soul.\nThe synthetic predicate of absolute existence,\nof relative existence.\nUnity. *conceptus singularis* (singular concept).\nMany gods, as necessary beings, would not stand in *commercio* (reciprocal community). Manichaeans.\nCosmological argument. From alteration we cannot |\ninfer contingency, but, if the infinite series is not admitted, only the necessity of a first; yet the first action of this being we cannot make comprehensible either.\nOntological argument. From the concept of the *realissimum* the necessity of existence.\nCosmological argument. From the concept of a necessary being to infer its quality as highest reality.\nIn the cosmological proof I infer either from the concept of the dependence of everything that is alteration to a first which is without alteration (*primus motor*, first mover), or from that of contingency to something necessary, and then I ask: what properties has a necessary being? The necessity of existence, however, can be derived from no properties at all and is absolutely incomprehensible.",[]],"reflm:6276":["Through the predicate of existence I add nothing to the thing; rather I add the thing itself to the concept. In an existential proposition, then, I go beyond the concept, not to another predicate than was thought in the concept, but to the thing itself, with precisely the same predicates, neither more nor fewer, only that [it as added] absolute position is thought in addition to relative position (*complementum possibilitatis*, complement of possibility). The ground of the illusion lies in this, that the concept of the *ens realissimum* contains the *omnimodam determinationem* (complete determination in every respect), whereas all others leave the object in many ways undetermined.",[]],"reflm:6277":["At the lower margin under Th § 15, but surely still belonging to Th § 14:\nThe whole difficulty of transcendental theology rests on this, that it is not possible to determine the concept of the absolute necessity of a thing, i.e. to say on what its thinkability rests. |\nThe necessity of propositions is absolute only if the proposition is analytic; but the absolute necessity of things is a synthetic proposition.*\nThe deduction of this concept yields that it is a necessary hypothesis.\n*(g One can adduce no case of it; everything is logical necessity. The opposite of no thing contradicts itself.)",[]],"reflm:6278":["According to Mendelssohn, God [after all] knows the contingency of all things outside himself, even in relation to his own nature, and so the thoroughgoing contingency of nature, that is, theoretical [necessity] contingency. But he knows at the same time their practical necessity through his will, as the best; and so the contingency of existing things is a proof of the existence of an intelligent cause, without which they could not exist. His own necessity he knows absolutely (though we cannot comprehend it). But since we have no other concept of the way in which possible things can become actual otherwise than through nature, save through a will, we make this concept, which is taken from experience and has only subjective validity, the ground of things in themselves. If we substitute for the concept of contingency the concept that we can think the opposite, not of the conditioned but of the unconditioned, then the argument concludes thus: what we cannot think otherwise than so, not on account of contradiction but because otherwise no rule of thinking is given to us, that is necessary; and so we see that all of this consists merely of subjective presuppositions.\nIn the cosmological argument (*a contingentia mundi*, from the contingency of the world*) one would have had to infer the contingency of the things of the world, not from their alterations, but from their limitations; but then one would have had to presuppose [prove] what was to be proved, namely that the *ens realissimum* (most real being) alone exists necessarily.\nBut if anyone assumes that time and all alteration are not a determination of things but only a particular form of their sensible intuition, then the world could nevertheless be necessary.\n*(g This argument, if it is drawn from the alterations of the world, can serve only [only] to show that the world cannot be the *ens necessarium* (necessary being). Otherwise it can be drawn thus: If anything exists, a necessary being exists. *Atqui* (but) [something] exists. Therefore [The ne] ... But no limited being can be necessary (because it is not thoroughly determined through its universal concept, and consequently it is contingent whether it is limited to this extent and no less or more); therefore the necessary being is unlimited.\nThe alterations in the world lead in the end only to contingency and to a first, as necessary, being. Hence all that matters is this: what properties belong to it, so that from the concept of such a being its necessary existence may at the same time be known? Or is the possibility of an absolutely necessary being to be derived from some other concept? (It is a synthetic proposition, and so cannot be derived from mere concepts at all.) Absolute necessity is a limiting concept, at which, as everywhere, we must necessarily arrive in the first place [without] and which can [can] be assumed only for the sake of the consequences, but is not in itself seen into or comprehended.\nTo infer from effects to the existence of the *ens realissimum* as cause proves it only as *realissimum tanquam causa* (most real as cause); but to infer from the possibilities of things, which are regarded only as determinations of one single universal possibility, namely that of the highest being, proves the existence of the *realissimum* as sum-total [Inbegriff], and consequently also, if understanding [is] reality, that it is intelligent.\nAll error consists in this, that we take our way of determining, deriving or dividing concepts for conditions of things in themselves. — One can use Spinozism to overthrow dogmatism. The critical and practical philosopher fears nothing from such fanaticisms.)",[]],"reflm:6279":["If we wish to represent to ourselves a thing, whatever it may be, according to a certain concept, and to represent this concept completely, with everything that belongs to the thing even outside the understanding, we take thoroughgoing determination as the schema of every complete concept, and lay it at the foundation. The concept of a thing in general, as determinable in accordance with this, or as the mere subject of the determination, is merely possible; as congruent with this schema outside the understanding, it is actual; and if it could be regarded through the mere concept as also posited [through] outside the understanding, it is necessary. In a thing, everything is entitled contingent with regard to which the thing is undetermined through its concept. Hence [those determinations are called, we call] that seems to us necessary which is thoroughly determined through its concept.",[]],"reflm:6280":["On Th § 15? Th 21':\nThe inference *per metabasin eis allo genos* (by transition to another kind) is impermissible in logic, where I use what holds of one kind of things or of knowledge as a |\nprinciple for another kind of things or modes of knowledge. Yet the passage to another kind in inference can nevertheless be permissible, indeed necessary, namely from objective principles of reason to subjective ones: 1. for the sake of the completion of the grounds of reason in their derivation; 2. for the sake of separating the grounds of reason from all determining grounds of sensibility, and because of their independence, for a procedure which is to determine concepts wholly *a priori*, as in the moral. In such a *metabasis* no probability holds, but no knowledge either; instead of the former there is necessity of the idea, instead of the second, hypothesis, which indeed has an analogy with theoretical presuppositions, in that I must presuppose something, [which I do not] which I neither know nor anything similar to it, nor can represent determinately to myself, yet as necessary in order to obtain, for my use of reason, completeness, or at least security of the empirical use. Strictly I do not pass over to another thing, but to another kind of use of reason, and the necessity of this, so far as it is to be practical, justifies the theoretical use. Without morality the hypothesis would always be groundless, and purposiveness in the universe would at the very most lead to a Spinozism or emanation. But morality, without such a presupposition, has no prospect of the connection of the objective principle of the (good) will with the subjective one (happiness). No system of nature corresponds to the system of reason (g and freedom), and so the moral concept would concern a mere *ens rationis* (being of reason), which would dissolve into nothing.\nThe existence of a merely happy being without morality has indeed some worth for this being, but not for a mere spectator. The existence of a merely moral being without happiness has indeed the greatest worth for a spectator, but not for the subject itself. The worth of existence, however, must be determined and certain objectively as well as subjectively. |\nFor I can indeed say: I must be truthful, even if fortune should deny me all favour; but this holds only so far as I am here and live, I exist as a good being. But I do not know why I should be there merely in order to act. The very same will (in the idea) which commands me this, why has it commanded my existence? I cannot agree to this. Morality is therefore necessary conditionally, but the condition (my existence) is then not merely con|tingent,\nTh 22': |\nbut impossible for my wish. The will therefore agrees in respect of matter with that which, in respect of form, does not agree. — A necessary hypothesis of reason, which however in transcendental theology is itself only hypothetically necessary, namely under the subjective condition that I wish to explain. Morals gives an objective condition.",[]],"reflm:6281":["Morality is clear in itself, if the rational being exists; but its existence itself has no worth for it, if good conduct is not rewarded.",[]],"reflm:6282":["The concept of the unconditionally necessary being is the most unavoidable and yet the most unattainable concept of human speculative reason.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* (most real being) was only subjectively necessary; that of unconditioned existence is objective. To know this through reason means: to [represent] think it to oneself as necessary. — The necessity of the hypothesis is distinct from the necessity of insight.\nThe necessity of the thing can be distinguished thus: 1. in that the opposite of being* would, or 2. in that the possibility of all other things would, thereby be abolished.\n*(g The possibility of the same thing; to see into the possibility of an *ens necessarium* (necessary being) means: to prove its actuality.)",[]],"reflm:6283":["[Die Exi] The concept of an absolutely necessary being is a necessary hypothesis of our reason: first, in the series [leading] from the dependent to the independent (negative concept); secondly, in the series of parts [leading] to the complete whole (*ens realissimum*), in order to derive everything limited from the unlimited. The latter is only a greater ease, not a subjective necessity. In morals, on the other hand, this hypothesis is necessary for the harmony of nature with freedom, because otherwise freedom is a transcendent concept, and its laws would therefore also be transcendent and so could not be subjectively practical.\nThe difficulty of comprehending the absolutely necessary, and yet the (g subjective) necessity of assuming it, is common to the theologian |\nand the atheist. Hence the concept of the *ens realissimum*, as the concept that is least opposed to him [the atheist], is nevertheless a permissible hypothesis. — From this follows immutability, not so much in the sense that it remains the same throughout the whole of time, as in the sense that it is not in time: eternity (duration) without time. *Impassibilitas*. *Solitudo*. All these are concepts derived from absolute necessity. Those derived from the highest reality now follow. In transcendental theology there is no anthropomorphism, and it is therefore not necessary to ascribe anything to God [*secund*] *per analogiam*.\n(g The warrant, indeed even the subjective necessity of reason, for assuming an *ens realissimum* rests on the presupposition of an *ens necessarium* as the independent ground of whatever exists, a presupposition which even the atheist cannot avoid. Now such a being must have certain properties, namely those that are connected with its complete independence and at the same time with its sufficiency as the supreme ground of the contingent. We cannot, to be sure, specify this concept with objective validity. But there is in our reason a single concept which alone is, for reason, original in respect of the thoroughgoing determination of a thing, and which cannot be derived. This is the concept of the *realissimum*. It is, moreover, a *conceptus singularis*, and is suited to complete derivation and at the same time, thereby, to the affinity of all possibilities. We have, then, [subi] a subjectively sufficient ground for assuming such an existence. This is a rational belief [*Vernunftglaube*], which objectively is neither certainty nor probability. Rational unbelief has nothing to say against this assumption as a hypothesis. Even the advantage of the unity of reason condemns its [unbelief's] maxim.)",[]],"reflm:6284":["The relative predicates of God (g with regard to the existence of other |\nthings) are not to be found at all in Spinozism, if by relation an external relationship is understood. But if an inner *respectus* is also meant by it, namely that of the accidents to a substance and conversely, then relative predicates are ascribed to God there also. The relative predicates of God in relation to the world are therefore: either 1. Those of inherence: Spinozism. 2. Those of dependence or causality, according to the system of creation. 3. Those of *commercium*, according to the theory of the world-soul. The system of causality (g as regards substance) is either that of emanation or that of creation. The system of causality as regards form is either [des Ursp] that of the origination of the form or that of its mere modification (hence a mixed cause: matter and God. *Fatum*).\nThe relative predicates of God in respect of the state of other things: 1. Conservation. 2. Omnipresence. 3. Concurrence.",[]],"reflm:6285":["On Th § 17, conclusion:\nThings in space are all bound to the condition of space *a priori*. If space were something in itself, it would also be necessary, and God would also be bound to existence in it. God must |\nbe present to things in themselves, and so also to thinking beings, and must thereby make the idea of space in them the necessary condition of their outer intuition.",[]],"reflm:6286":["On Th § 19:\nThe deist ascribes to the *ens summum* all reality only *in abstracto*, but none *in concreto*. How, then, is the theist to proceed in order to ascribe realities to God *in concreto*?\nIn the choice of the realities we proceed by the way (g *tam*) *negationis quam eminentiae* [as much of negation as of eminence], but in the manner in which we ascribe the realities *in concreto* to the highest being, *secundum analogiam*.\n*Per analogiam*.\nRealities cannot be thought *in concreto* through mere understanding, but are always affected with conditions of sensibility. First, therefore, I shall *via reductionis* free the reality, as far as possible, from what belongs to it as phenomenon (*adhaerentibus sensitivis* [sensible adherents]), for otherwise anthropomorphisms result. Thereupon, however, [das] I shall raise it infinitely *per eminentiam* as *realitas noumenon* (even if all particular determinations *in* |\n*concreto* should fall away). (Before the reduction the way of eminence must not be taken; for human perfection also could grow to infinity without differing in species.) But since the removal of everything sensitive also removes the concept *in concreto*, which would turn all theism into mere deism, there remains the way of application according to analogy. In accordance with it I confess that I do not know how the divine properties are constituted in themselves, but know only that they are thought in the same relation to the world as human properties to their products.",[]],"reflm:6287":["On Th § 20:\n1. Possibility (g against dogmatic atheism) of the *ens rea|lissimum*:\nThat all reality can be contained in a common ground, i.e. the real possibility of this we do not see, but only the logical possibility.*\nThis is not directed against sceptical atheism, for it doubts only the proofs of actuality.\n*(g Whether all perfections can be grafted upon one stem and can spring from an inner principle of one and the same thing, we do not see (though neither do we see the contrary); even in men different perfections are scarcely to be united, e.g. great activity and zeal with cautious examination and investigation, etc. etc.\nAgainst dogmatic atheism it suffices to show that the impossibility of a highest being cannot be proved, since that would have to arise through the contradiction of this concept with itself; but we do not on that account see its possibility.\nAgainst sceptical atheism it suffices to show that not every way of attaining conviction is thereby cut off, |\nbecause speculation does not provide it (for so the sceptical atheist concludes) (*Ohngötter* [godless ones]). For even if speculative conviction does not take place, moral conviction is nevertheless possible.\nThe speculative doubter is he who maintains that a thing must not be assumed because its existence cannot be proved. This certainly holds in all speculative knowledge. But the same man, if he is a doubter in every respect with regard to the existence of God, is a sceptical atheist. To this, however, he is by no means entitled, unless he is obliged to hold all moral laws to be empty imagination; for then he has no need, either, to assume a God for practical purposes. He who maintains that virtue rewards itself has no need to assume a God.)",[]],"reflm:6288":["On Th § 21:\n*Ens realissimum est ens originarium* [the most real being is the original being], if it can be proved that all possibility finds place only in and through it.\nExistence from mere concepts.",[]],"reflm:6289":["On Th § 21:\nOne could conclude in the same way that the most perfect of every being exists in its kind, because [it necessarily] existence is also perfection.",[]],"reflm:6290":["On Th § 21: Th 30':\nThat something is [possible] actual because it is possible according to a universal concept does not follow. But that something is actual because through its concept it is thoroughly determined among everything possible, and is distinguished from everything possible as one, means as much as this: it is no [longer] merely a universal concept, but [the concept] the representation of a single thing thoroughly determined through concepts in relation to everything possible. This relation to everything possible, according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination, is, in terms of concepts of reason, the very same as the 'somewhere' or 'sometime' is in terms of the conditions of sensible intuition.* For space and time determine not merely the intuition of a thing but at the same time its individuality, through the relation of place and point of time, because |\n(g in the case of) space and time possibility cannot be distinguished from actuality, for the reason that both together contain in themselves all possibility in appearance [consequently all] as substrata that must first be given.\nFrom this it follows only that the *ens realissimum* (the most real being) must be given beforehand for the real concept of all possibility, and hence, just as space cannot be thought beforehand as possible but only as given, so too the *ens realissimum*; yet not as [something] an object actual in itself, but as a mere sensible form in which alone objects can be intuited. Consequently the *ens realissimum* is to be taken not as object but as the mere form of reason, in its thoroughgoing determination, for thinking the distinction of everything possible, and so as an idea which is actual (subjectively) before anything is yet thought as possible; from which, however, it by no means follows that the object of this idea is actual in itself.\nNevertheless one sees that in relation to the nature of the human understanding (g and of its concepts) a highest being is just as necessary as space and time are in relation to the nature of our sensibility and its intuition.\nTh 30:\n*(g Something whose relation (g to everything possible) is determined in absolute space and time is actual. Likewise that whose relation to everything possible is determined in the absolute representation of a thing in general is actual. Both belong to the thoroughgoing outer determination with regard to possibility in general, and [therefore] thereby also constitute the thoroughgoing inner determination of an individual.)",[]],"reflm:6291":["Formal ground of possibility — *principium contradictionis* (principle of contradiction).\nMaterial ground of possibility — *principium omnimodae determinationis* (principle of thoroughgoing determination). As sum-total.\nThe first concept of the absolute necessity of a being is (g logical, i.e.) this: that [being] its non-being contradicts itself.\nThe second concept of the absolute necessity of a being is metaphysical: that its non-being would remove the possibility of all things.\nProof of a common ground even of the possibilities of things, from essential purposiveness.",[]],"reflm:6292":["Th 30':\nThe contingent agreement with ends can perhaps, as the atheist would have it, be explained also through mere chance. But the necessary agreement with them cannot be thought at all if I assume the essences of things to be given independently, each for itself; for then it is impossible that they should necessarily agree together in the unity of an end (e.g. to draw geometrical proportions). For this one must necessarily lay at the basis the unity of the being from which all possibility is derived, and in which possibility and actuality are not distinguished; for only then do all agree necessarily with one another in unity, because they stem from such a being.*\nTh 30:\n*(g One must therefore infer, not from the concept of the *ens realissimum* to its necessity (inner impossibility of non-being), but from that of absolute necessity (as of a being whose removal removes everything possible, and hence the possibility of an *ens summum* as highest ground, *ens entium*) to complete reality.)",[]],"reflm:6293":["On Th § 22:\nIf the objective necessity of the highest being is not indeed seen through this proof, yet the subjective necessity of a hypothesis of it, as substratum of all possibility (of the |\nthoroughgoing determination of every thing in general), is seen in our reason itself in its speculative use, although this use is not in itself necessary.",[]],"reflm:6294":["On Th § 23:\nIf the highest |\nreality of a necessary being could be derived from its concept, then among all possible beings one would be determined as absolutely necessary, and I should have no need here to lay experience at the basis.",[]],"reflm:6295":["On Th § 23, heading:\nCosmological",[]],"reflm:6296":["If it is impossible to prove the *necessarium* from the concept of the *realissimum*, it is still far less possible to prove its properties (realities) from the concept of the *necessarium*.",[]],"reflm:6297":["On Th § 23. Th 34':\nProof from the indication of existing things to an *ens originarium* (original being), and from this the inference that an *ens originarium* is also an *ens realissimum*.\nThis proof assumes that an *ens originarium* is an (g absolutely) necessary being, and infers that the absolutely necessary can be none other than the *realissimum*. All this comes about through our first applying the concepts of contingency and necessity to the existence of all things (not merely of the conditioned), and so thinking an *ens originarium*, whose concept is merely negative, through a positive predicate, namely the absolute necessity of its existence.\nIf we separate possibility from actuality, this is a sign that we have not yet thoroughly determined our concept of the thing; for then the object is thought only as something that can be determined in one way or another among all [possible] predicates, in regard to [which] these it must nevertheless be thoroughly determined if it is to exist. The difference between possibility [is al] and actuality is therefore no difference of things but of concepts, in so far as [that concept] which is still regarded as undetermined in every respect constitutes the concept of a merely possible thing, whereas [that] which is regarded as objectively determined constitutes the concept of an actual thing. [The *ens origin*] Existence is conditioned when it cannot be known a priori otherwise than under the presupposition of a cause; it is unconditioned when it either can be known a priori without yet (g being) a *rationatum* (something grounded), or, even if it cannot be known a priori at all, and consequently not as *caussatum alterius* (caused by another) either, must nevertheless itself be presupposed as cause.\nThe cosmological proof presupposes the ontological. For if from something's being an *ens necessarium* it follows that it is also *realissimum* (because the latter can be only a single being, and the concept of the absolutely necessary must be thoroughly determining, so that many kinds of beings are not to be understood under it), then conversely it must also be possible to infer that the [absol: necessarium] *ens realissimum* is an *ens necessarium*, for they are reciprocal concepts. I cannot convert the proposition 'all triangles are figures', because the predicate fits more things than triangles. But if I find that the predicate is suitable only and solely to this subject |\nTh 34: |\n, e.g. 'is enclosed in three sides', then I must be able to convert it. [Now the inference goes] And since the proposition is certain a priori, its converse too must be certain a priori of itself; i.e. in our case necessity must flow from the concept of the |\n[abs: nec:] realissimum. Chiefly, however, the inference from the *necessarium* to the *realissimum* proceeds through this: that the *necessarium* is determinable in only one way, consequently is only a single being, and this single being must already by its concept be distinguished from all contingent beings.",[]],"reflm:6298":["On Th § 23:\nWe can ask why anything at all is actual; for possibility we here presuppose as independent of existence. But is the concept of possibility then really independent of all actuality and experience? The logical element of possibility, the principle of contradiction, is so, namely the possibility of the concept. But the possibility of the things themselves requires more, namely synthesis. At the basis of this lies the actual nature of our sensibility and of our understanding, and it extends no further than to objects of possible experience. But even there the possibility, e.g., of a building where none is present is nothing but the determination of a concept through various predicates, which however is not yet extended to thoroughgoing determination, and hence not to the relations either. Complete possibility we can never see, [but] because we cannot determine a concept thoroughly and fully in concreto. For if we could do this, there would have to belong to it predicates which can be taken only from experience, and whose meaning too consists only in the relation of the faculties of knowledge to possible experience.",[]],"reflm:6299":["On Th § 23:\nThe cosmological proof: If anything exists, then something exists absolutely necessarily, i.e. it must be impossible that it should not exist. If this means: it must be self-contradictory [that it should not exist], then no being is absolutely necessary. If it means: I must presuppose its existence as necessary in accordance with laws of reason (not as necessary in itself), then it is necessity of a hypothesis. Synthetic propositions a priori which relate to no object [de] of possible experience can never have objective necessity. But they may well have subjective [necessity], arising from the presupposition by reason of a *realissimum* as the substratum of all possibility [bey] in the principle of thoroughgoing determination, and that suffices for us.\nReason always connects one existence with another and can posit nothing for itself. It is therefore only the faculty of connections a priori, not a faculty of knowing absolute necessity in existence. Nevertheless, absolute necessity is an unavoidable presupposition of reason for the sake of its totality. Hence it is subjectively, not dogmatically, necessary.",[]],"reflm:6300":["On Th § 24:\n*Theologia naturalis* (natural theology): God thought as cause of nature, by analogy with natural causes, i.e. by psychological predicates.\nPhysicotheology. Spinoza assumes two attributes of the Godhead, extension and thought; the modification of the former is motion. But he will not have motion come from thought, but immediately from the nature of the being; and then God is no being distinct from the world.\nTo infer from cosmotheology to the intellectuality of the original being would proceed thus. The world is a series of successive states. A being can make the first beginning only through freedom; but freedom presupposes understanding; consequently the *ens originarium* (original being) is *summa intelligentia* (supreme intelligence). *Primus motor* (first mover). This proof is better than the one which holds that intellect belongs to the highest reality. — The prevention of anthropomorphisms in theism — *secundum analogiam* (according to analogy) and *via reductionis* (by the way of reduction). Incomprehensible. — Of him there is no example, nor even anything similar.\nLiving God. *Summa intelligentia*. *Summa sibisufficientia* (supreme self-sufficiency) (g blessedness). *Summum numen* (supreme divine power) (g will). First only from the physicotheological concept, then from existence.",[]],"reflm:6301":["On Th § 24:\nThe expedient, in the case of the highest being, necessary in itself, which can be known through nothing given in experience (as knowledge through appearances), of nevertheless forming for ourselves some concepts drawn from nature that can have their examples in concreto, rests not on his unfathomableness [Unergründlichkeit] but on his incomprehensibility [Unbegreiflichkeit]. The former he has in common with all things; the latter is peculiar to all his properties. We consider his relational properties only according to analogy, but do not transfer the absolute to him. These relations are thought through |\npure categories; but one cannot say: the analogue of these properties of the world belongs to him, for that would prove a similarity in the constitution of the thing. What we mean to say is only that, according to our manner of representing to ourselves the possibility of things, we must think his properties in accordance with the same relations as those in which we represent to ourselves the properties in the world.",[]],"reflm:6302":["On Th § 35:\nThe preservation of the species can be regarded either as entirely natural or as requiring a supernatural influence. In the first case the origin of the species too would probably have to be regarded as natural. For every generation is to be regarded as a new origin, in so far as there are so many extraneous causes which can modify and alter the formative power that, unless there were a counteracting principle according to universal laws, the regularity in propagation could not be explained from a predisposition once created. This is especially so if one assumes epigenesis.",[]],"reflm:6303":["On moral theology. Here morals is laid at the foundation of all theology.\nConscience is 1. the faculty of becoming conscious of the rightfulness or wrongfulness of one's own actions; 2. the inner authority of this judging faculty, as of a judge, to call us to account for the warrant of our actions.\nThe supreme principle of conscience is: that nothing is permitted to be done of which the agent is not entirely certain that it is (generally) permitted to be done. We can undertake nothing at the risk of acting wrongly.\nA hypothesis, the contesting of which carries with it the danger of doing wrong, (g through) whose acceptance (g we) however can never do wrong, is morally certain, and the presupposition of it, with a view to the confirmation of morality, is moral belief. Moral belief is therefore derived not from the agreement of our judgment with the object, but from its agreement with our conscience.\nMatters of belief are those in which the morality of holding-for-true is the essential thing. The existence of God and of a future world are matters of belief of mere reason. In respect of speculation they are of no great importance, and also cannot be proved apodictically. But when morality is laid at the foundation, they are indispensable hypotheses for putting it into effect.\nThe rule of conscience here, given a morally good intention, is not to contrive more conviction than we are capable of, so that we may be sure of not doing wrong in this knowledge.",[]],"reflm:6304":["On Th § 49 Note 2 »Spinoza«:\nεν και παν. [one and all]",[]],"reflm:6305":["On Th § 71:\nOn the anthropomorphic element in the expressions, which yet must be understood Θεοπρεπωσ [in a manner befitting God].\nOn the God-like element in the idea of a perfect man.",[]],"reflm:6306":["On Th § 71 Note 1, sentence 2 »als Mittel« [as means]:\nnever merely",[]],"reflm:6307":["On Th § 71:\nOf God as the cause of the world. The ideal of humanity in its whole perfection is his first-born son, the reflection of his glory; in him and through him are all things made [he himself is from |\neternity]. Hence he is also entitled the self-subsistent or original Word* (the ground of becoming). In him alone (g only) does God love the world, and in relation to him the Creator of the world is also entitled Father of men in his Son, i.e. as in his *ectypus* (ectype, copy). To bring men to this agreement with the ideal of the Son, the sanctifying Spirit is in him from eternity; it unites the defective creature with the most holy will, through the striving to become like the ideal of the Son and through the supplementing of what is lacking in righteousness.** He is the judge within us, who holds the holy law before us and judges us by it, but also supplements what we lack in righteousness through the ideal of humanity, if we are on the way to approaching it ever more closely, and brings us, in infinite, uninterrupted progress, nearer to it and at the same time to blessedness.\nIn the [image] consciousness of the first Adam, in whom we have all sinned, and in the humble consciousness of the frailty of every (g human) virtue, which, however great it may be, can be overthrown by chance circumstances of temptation, and in faith in a second Adam, i.e. a man who withstands all such temptations, even the hardest, through disgrace and suffering, and thereby proves the practicability of what the holy law demands, and hence that it is not an empty but a practical ideal: in the ever-advancing [Besse] approach to this example of perfection, in this spirit of humility and at the same time of hope, we can become objects of God's love in his Son, by being made like him and justified with him. Here there are no feelings and super|natural\ninfluences, nor historical sources of knowledge, but mere human reason.\n*(g He is the uncreated, yet the archetype of the most glorious that can be met with in creation; the man who is like him, so far as the limitations to which as a creature he is unavoidably subject permit, is united with him in one person.)\n**(g He proceeds from the Father and is sent by the Son. For since no creature is equal to the ideal of humanity, in that it can be holy not by being created so but through freedom alone, the holy law has as its aim the influence of this law (g in itself) on the will of creatures, whereas the goodness of the Father in regard to the Son has as its aim the will to receive the creature through him into his sonship. Hence a morally [erh] quickening and sustaining power of God is necessary, to bring man nearer to the ideal of humanity, to direct him inwardly in accordance with it, and to ground and confirm faith in it for the striving after an ever-continuing approach to it. This Spirit is therefore the means of bringing to actuality the object of creation, humanity [in] well-pleasing to God, namely the founding of [the community of] a kingdom of God under laws, a benevolent administration and a just judgment, and indeed under the only possible paternal one, through the sonship of the subjects.)",[]],"reflm:6308":["On Th § 73:\nIn religion the requirement is, objectively, right belief (g orthodoxy), but subjectively conscientiousness, that is, [pure] tested sincerity in the profession of what is taught as orthodox.\nIf someone, to the best of his ability, [that as] chooses [to] what now seems to him right belief, he can have religion with complete conscientiousness; and in fact there is religion only where there is pure conscientiousness. Where, then, there is no freedom of [choice is] public investigation, where either the forestalling of inquiry by implanted prejudices or compulsion hinders investigation, there is unconscientious religion, that is, priestcraft [or the submissiveness power de], slavish [God's] or hypocritical submission under the pressure of pious observances. One ought not to call that religion: it is priestcraft. For re|ligion must be conscientious, and freedom belongs to conscience. One cannot say that he who accepts even a true religion under compulsion proceeds conscientiously; for he must know, and see for himself, that something is his duty, and cannot rely on the assurance of others. What he does herein in accordance with the moral laws of reason, the effort to establish himself firmly in uprightness, etc. etc., that alone can be counted to his credit; the rest is labour lost, and moreover a hazard, hence impure and mere canvassing for favour. Priestcraft is everywhere one and the same; Catholicism and Protestantism are essentially distinct. So there are arch-Catholic Protestants and probably also Protestant Catholics. Once one admits that among the things we can do to please God there is something more than a good course of life, there are no limits.\nIf the character of a people is corrupted in one respect, this extends to several others. Certain observances still seem necessary for the [practice no] formation of religion, and in this there must be a mechanism not easily altered; [but that it with that which] they must be so constituted that, even if they were unnecessary, or founded on false histories or alleged revelation, they would at least be morally indifferent. But professions of faith as to their truth are a burden upon conscience. What I merely believe, [need] I cannot [to] (g as true) swear to; and to swear to it as believed by me is indeed a guarantee for the present, but I cannot swear that I shall always believe it, [because] and consequently cannot pledge myself to it. And a sovereign who |\ntakes office from his servants because they will not act against conscience (unless it be the office of a teacher) always does wrong.",[]],"reflm:6309":["On Th § 73:\nOf sincerity, probity and uprightness.\n(g The opposite of sincerity is impurity [Unlauterkeit], in a higher degree falsity, in the highest unconscientiousness, which is distinct from villainy.)\n(g Outer sincerity (g in utterances to others) is truthfulness; the inner is probity; both together, so far as they are [on] tested [are] secure, are uprightness.)\n*Integer vitae scelerisque purus* [upright of life and free from crime]. This seems to be the easiest thing, and what is in the [force power] capacity of every man. For it means nothing else than: never to do anything with the consciousness that it is wrong, or (g with the consciousness) of an surmountable ignorance whether it is right. Yet this is precisely the impurity that is rooted in our nature, which is laid aside latest of all, and everyone seeks to deceive himself and others. Truthfulness is what is first required. What is true is often hard to determine; but one can be truthful in all cases, if only one earnestly wills it. The propensity to semblance, which the servants of forms nourish, is the effect, and in turn the cause, of this corruption of character. Formality is very necessary in serious actions, but the end (g and disposition) must be truthful. At most it can (g then) become pedantry. But to set formalities as a surrogate for dispositions corrupts minds in civil transactions, in religion, even |\nin social intercourse. Someone can be honest, but he is not on that account also upright in the sense of probity, that is, honesty from principles (toward others); uprightness is still more, namely sincerity with regard to oneself under the most searching self-examination. Job. The harm that arises from this for the sciences if one is not honest, and for religion if one is not upright. What is truth? is a logical question in religion, and there orthodoxy differs. What are truthfulness and uprightness? is a practical (moral) question, and there one can readily see that everyone must follow his reason and conscience. The material of religion is: to be a better man; the formal: to adopt the manner and fashion of a disposition devoted to God through actions in which there is in themselves no moral content. Thus formalities as final end, or as the essence of religion.\nOaths are formalities for awakening conscience; otherwise, as asseverations, they are, as Christ indicates, absurd. But they do great harm, like the subscriptions of the English clergy to the 39 Articles. They make lying permissible, provided only that one has not sworn. The first thing in education.\nThe *argumenta a tuto* [arguments from safety] (nothing is safe except what is conscientious) and *ab vtili* [from utility], used as proofs of truth, corrupt the character. How many a man conceals his own uncertainty and speaks aloud as if it were decided, because he thinks it useful. To read into Scripture a sense of which one is not certain that it is in it, if only this sense is moral, means: to ascribe to it a good intention, which we are always bound to do so long as the contrary is not proved, and which is also the principal thing. Those who always ask what God does to make them blessed, not what they must do to become partakers of blessedness, believe, by confessing the former and praising it highly, that they are doing all on their own part to become partakers of it, without being worthy of it; thus they seek to win heavenly favour by flattery, and corrupt the character.\n(g Solemnities in matters of religion are very precarious means. They are formalities as business. Formalities as method are good in the |\nadministration of justice and in religion. But they must not displace the realities, and must be honestly meant.)\nThe look of a man who is not sincere (he squints, a fixed stare).\nFreedom in thinking alone can make sincere men; compulsion in religion makes either hypocrites, or at least such as, since they investigate nothing, because (g it) is of no help to them anyhow, as they have no choice, are unconscientious. The very same freedom, it is true, also makes windbags and the frivolous.",[]],"reflm:6310":["On the Son of God. Every creature has duties. For all of these it must have a model.\nThis it cannot find in any creature (g for no creature is wholly adequate to any duty, because before it there is still a temptation to be found which overthrows its good will), nor yet in the Creator, for he has no duty, |\nthus only in that which proceeds from God and serves humanity as archetype, so far, then, as it is man, yet can exist only in God, and so is bound up with his essence as the Son of God, that is, in the idea of humanity in the divine understanding. (None is good but the one God.) This, then, is the God-man; and whoever is in progress toward this archetype is [is] taken up by him.\nBut if the creature also has this model, whence does it take the capacity to be adequate to it? It must take it from itself, for otherwise it could not be imputed to it; but since no creature at any point of time can be fully adequate to it, there must be something that can serve, in respect of this progress to infinity, as guidance and can give courage [i.e. the God-man]. This confidence the creature again cannot derive from itself. Thus only by consoling itself with the holy, but at the same time also kindly, providence: that, in the measure in which it makes use of its powers, it will also be equipped in its progress with new (g greater) capacity for the future, or else, in the contrary case, be forsaken by it. That is the sanctifying Spirit, the source of the moral life, which at the same time judges.",[]],"reflm:6311":["-Sch. XI 1 S. 265-7. Hb. IV S. 502f. Ki. L S. 197f.\nRefutation of problematic idealism.\nIdealism is divided into the problematic (that of Descartes) and the dogmatic (that of Berkeley). The latter denies the existence of all things other than that of the person making the assertion. The former, on the other hand, says merely that this existence cannot be proved. Here we shall confine ourselves solely to problematic idealism.\nThe problematic idealist grants that we perceive alterations through our inner sense. But he denies that we can on that account infer the existence of outer objects in space, since the inference from an effect to a determinate cause is not valid. — Alteration of inner sense, or inner experience, is thus conceded by the idealist. If, then, one wishes to refute him, this can be done only by showing him that this inner experience, or, what is the same thing, the empirical consciousness of my existence, presupposes outer perception.\nHere we must distinguish carefully between transcendental and empirical consciousness. The former is the consciousness 'I think', and precedes all experience, in that it first makes experience possible. This transcendental consciousness, however, furnishes us with no knowledge of ourselves. For knowledge of ourselves is the determination of our existence in |\ntime, and if this is to take place I must affect my inner sense. I think, for example, about the Deity, and connect with this thought the transcendental consciousness (for otherwise I could not think), without however representing myself to myself in time. That would have to happen if I were conscious of this representation through my inner sense. If impressions are made on my inner sense, this presupposes that I affect myself (though how this comes about is inexplicable to us). Thus the empirical consciousness presupposes the transcendental.\nIn our inner sense our existence is determined in time, and this therefore presupposes the representation of time itself. But in time the representation of change is contained, and change presupposes something abiding in relation to which it changes, and which makes it possible for the change to be perceived. Time itself is indeed abiding, but it cannot be perceived by itself alone. Consequently there must be something abiding in relation to which change in time can be perceived. We ourselves cannot be this abiding, for as object of inner sense we are precisely determined through time. The abiding can therefore be posited only in what is given through outer sense. Thus the possibility of inner experience presupposes the reality of outer sense. For suppose one were to say that the representation of the abiding given through outer sense is also merely a perception given through inner sense, which is represented by the imagination only as if given through outer sense. Then it would still have to be possible, at least in general (even if not for us), to become conscious of it as belonging to inner sense. But then the representation of space would be transformed into a representation of time; that is, it would be possible to represent space to oneself as a time (of one dimension), |\nwhich is self-contradictory. Outer sense therefore has reality, since without it inner sense is not possible. — From this it seems to follow that we know our existence in time only always in *commercium* [reciprocal community].",[]],"reflm:6312":["S. II:\nBy what do we know the simultaneity of things, when yet our representations follow one another in apprehension? By the fact that we can apprehend the manifold there both forwards and backwards. Now since in inner sense everything is successive, and hence nothing can be taken backwards, the ground of the possibility of the latter must lie, in the relation of the representations to [einen S] something outside us. And this must be something which is not itself in turn mere inner representation, i.e. form of appearance, and hence is a thing in itself. The possibility of this cannot be explained. — The representation of the abiding, too, must be directed to that which contains the ground of time-determination, but not in respect of succession, for in that there is no abidingness. Consequently the abiding must lie only in the [simultaneous], in what is simultaneous, or in the intelligible, which contains the ground of appearances.\nThat even the empirical determination of one's own existence in time is not possible without [his] consciousness of his relation to things outside us constitutes the ground why this is the only possible refutation of idealism.\n––––––––––\nWhether objects (g outside us) or their representations affect us (of which the first [would be] the reality of the [Materi] outer sense, the second the mere —), [unter] can be distinguished as follows. We need space in order to construct time, and thus determine the latter by means of the former. Space, which represents the outer, therefore precedes the possibility of time-determination. Since now in respect of time we are affected only by representations, not by outer things, |\nnothing remains but that in the representation of space we must be conscious of ourselves as affected by outer things. This we do not know through an inference. It lies in the manner in which we affect ourselves in order to construct time as mere form of the representation of our inner state. Something else, not belonging to this inner state, must still always be given to us therein (i.e. something outer, [in] of which the construction [jene Zeit s] at the same time contains the intuition of time and lies at its ground).\n––––––––––\nIn order that something may seem to be outside us, something must actually be outside us, though not constituted in the way in which we have the representation of it, since other kinds of sense could furnish other kinds of representation of the same thing. For the representation of something outside us could never otherwise come into our thoughts. We can be conscious only of our representations as inner determinations, and have as the object of these the inner sense, which however we carefully distinguish from the outer.",[]],"reflm:6313":["P. I:\nAgainst Idealism.\nIt can be refuted, first, by showing that the representation of outer things must lie not in the imagination but in an outer sense. The form of representation in time, without the form in space taken along with it, would make no empirical consciousness of one's own existence in time possible, and hence no inner experience.\nSecond, by showing that the matter of the representations in space could not possibly occur in the mind without an outer sense. For the imagination can procure a representation of the outer only by affecting the outer sense (in the interior of its organ), and there would be no material for outer representations in the imagination if there were no outer sense. Now it is not demanded that we be able to give a certain universal mark of each object of outer sense and of its actu|ality;\nit is sufficient to have shown that there is an outer sense.\nThird, since the imagination (g and its product) is itself only an object of inner sense, the empirical consciousness (g apprehensio) of this state can contain only succession [the time-cond.]. But this succession itself cannot be represented otherwise than through the permanent, with which that successive is simultaneous, [determined]. This permanent, with which the successive is simultaneous, i.e. space, cannot in turn be a representation of the mere imagination, but must be a representation of sense, since otherwise that abiding element would not be in sensibility at all.\nNB. I. The simultaneity [of the representation] of A and B cannot be represented at all without something permanent. For strictly speaking all apprehension is successive. But in so far as the succession can proceed not merely forwards from A to B but also (g as often as I like) backwards from B to A, it is necessary that A persist. The representations of the senses A and B must therefore have a ground other than that in inner sense, [hence] yet in some sense, and hence in outer sense; consequently there must be objects of the outer senses. (And as regards dreaming, the object which produces the illusion of the presence of several outer objects is the body itself.)\nNB. II. We could not perceive even the succession in ourselves, and hence could carry out no inner experience, unless we could [at the same time] also become empirically conscious of simultaneity [but this is possible only through the permanent in the object of the representations]. This latter, however, is possible only through an apprehension carried out forwards and backwards, which does not take place in respect of the objects of inner sense. Therefore even inner experience can be thought only by means of the relation of our sense to objects outside us. (g Inner sense would otherwise have to represent us to ourselves as outside us, etc. etc.)\nIf our knowledge of outer objects had to be a knowledge of them (g and of space) as things in themselves, we could never prove their actuality from our sensible representation of them (g as outside us). For only representations are given to us; the cause of these can be (g either) in us or also outside us, and on this sense decides nothing. But if the representations of inner sense, as well as those of outer sense, are mere representations of things in appearance, and if even the [inner sense] determination of our consciousness for inner sense is possible only through representations outside us in space — breaks off.\nP. II:\nTurn over. But if the soul is only appearance to itself, and hence its empirical intuition is only the sensible form of the manner in which its own subject is affected through the apprehension of the manifold of a given intuition, then this latter must be an intuition other than inner, i.e. an outer one, so that this alone is immediate.\nIn the distinction between idealism and dualism one must distinguish the transcendental consciousness of my existence in general; 2. of my existence in time, and consequently only in relation to my own representations, in so far as I determine myself through them. This is the empirical consciousness of myself. 3. The knowledge of myself as a being determined in time. This is empirical knowledge. — That the latter can only be [as] the [Dase] of myself as a being existing in a world, and indeed on account of empirical consciousness and its possibility, in so far as [it] I am to know myself as object, is proved in the following way. — I cannot know time as beforehand determined, in order to determine my existence therein (thus only in so far as I, according to the law of causality, [the] connect my alterations at the same time). Now in order to determine that [an de] empirically, something permanent must be given, in whose apprehension I can know the succession of my representations, and through which alone the simultaneity of a series, in which each part passes away as the other arises, can become a whole, wherein I posit my existence.",[]],"reflm:6314":["P. I:\nOn idealism.\nWe cannot represent any number to ourselves [without] except through successive enumeration in time and then the taking together of this multiplicity into the unity of a number. This latter, however, cannot happen otherwise than by my placing the items side by side in space; for they must be thought as given simultaneously, that is, as taken together in one representation, since otherwise this manifold constitutes no magnitude (number). But simultaneity cannot be known to be possible by anything other than this: that, apart from my act of composition, I can apprehend (not merely think) the multiplicity as given, forwards and backwards. There must therefore be given an intuition in which the manifold is represented outside one another and [simultaneously represented] side by side, that is, the intuition [of space], which makes the representation of space possible, (g in perception), in order to determine [the existence] my own existence in time. That is, an existence outside me lies at the ground of the determination of my own existence, i.e. of the empirical consciousness of myself. Therefore [it is just so] I must, just as I am conscious of (g my) existence in time, also become conscious of the existence of outer things, though only as appearances, yet as actual things. No one can have inner sense alone, and that moreover for the purpose of knowledge of his inner state; and yet idealism asserts this.\nComposition in the representation of the composite is always merely our own work. How, then, can we say that the object agrees with it? This agreement cannot consist (g in this, that) the quality of the composition [not] be similar to the composite, but in this, that one of the two is the ground or consequence of the other (the latter is the case if the object is mere appearance). That (g in the representation) which is [belongs] related to the object of the senses in itself is sensation; but there the representation is related merely to the subject (as regards its quality), and the object is a mere something in general. If I leave out this something (g sensation) and at the same time the composition, there then remains the form of intuition and the object |\nas appearance. (Sensations, related to the object, constitute illusion [*Schein*].)\nP. II:\nThe critical philosophy brings it about that we need not ask what attributes God has, in order to know what we have to do in the world. We need only consult within ourselves the voice of reason, which teaches us immediately what we have to do, and [the will] which assumes in the supreme cause of our existence such a will, of which those laws of reason are the commands. With this, then, there are also all the promises for our wishes which the same reason in us represents as in agreement with such conduct.\nWe shall not learn his will first from [the] his revelation, whether in his works or in Scripture; for these can be interpreted in many ways, and only that meaning which we read into them by virtue of our moral vocation [*Bestimmung*] is indubitably the morally correct one, the revelations then serving to strengthen this vocation in us.\nWe shall not need to engage in combat with the [doubts] theoretical doubts against those theoretical dogmas of the divine nature and of his purposes, or of the doctrine of immortality. For since we can convict the adversary that, in pronouncing a denial on these matters, he understands exactly as little of these objects as (g others) do in affirming, we shall stand firm in our place without wavering. For [our] the law of our conduct, the hope of being able to fulfil it because we ought, and the disinterested conjecture of a state of things which accords on the whole with morality (which for reason is the [chief] highest condition of the possibility of a world according to rules of wisdom), will [a] raise us to the idea of a deity [in] wisely governing the world, and will at the same time furnish this deity with the attributes that strengthen and uphold our morality.",[]],"reflm:6315":["P. I:\nOn idealism.\nExperience is knowledge of objects that are present to the senses. Imagination is intuition even without the presence of the object, and [this intuit] the object is then entitled a *phantasma*, which may be a production (fiction) or a reproduction (recollection) of an intuition previously had. — The assertion that we can never be certain whether all our supposed outer experience is not mere imagination is idealism. It is therefore not precisely an assertion that this is so, but only that we can produce no proof of the contrary, and consequently [always] (g can still always doubt the reality [of the] of an outer experience taken to be such).\nThe idealist accordingly assumes that it is possible that we have [actual] no outer sense, and in respect of outer intuitions have only imagination. — But the Critique proves that this is impossible. For the form of intuition of inner sense is time, which contains only one dimension of sensible intuition. [All outer objects would therefore have to thereby] Hence, in order that my intuition may have three dimensions (g as space contains them within itself), we should have to think this representation of ours (g inner) as situated outside us, which is self-contradictory. — To take imagination of outer objects for perception (to dream) is indeed possible, but only on the presupposition of an outer sense, that is, that [it actually] our outer intuition is related to objects actually situated outside us. For otherwise all these intuitions, being at bottom merely inner, would have the form (g and dimension) of time and not (g that) of space; and this form is not thought but intuited, that is, related immediately to an object, even if we do not know what this is in itself, but only how it |\nappears to us. Were this not so, we should also have no imaginings; for these are only sense-intuitions of outer objects, reproduced as regards their form, which may indeed be fictions, but not in the respect that they have no outer objects at all. We are ourselves previously an object of outer sense, for otherwise we could not perceive our place in the world and intuit ourselves as standing in relation to other things. — Hence the soul, as object of inner sense, cannot perceive its place in the body, but is in the place in which the human being is. — Leibniz's *harmonia praestabilita* (pre-established harmony) necessarily carries idealism with it: for since each of the two subjects is, without the influence of the other, by itself in the play of changes, one of them is wholly unnecessary for the determination of the existence and state of the other. — But even inner changes cannot, as regards their possibility, be comprehended without something outer which contains the ground [of that possibility].\nP. II:\n# see p. 2, C, below.\nNevertheless this doubt, which when put forward dogmatically is called idealism, may, besides being a stumbling-block for metaphysics (from which, since it promises so many prospects as yet unopened, one expects that it should not have an objection raised against it concerning a matter lying so near to us), also form a hindrance to advancing to what constitutes the final end of metaphysics, the supersensible, if everything sensible is posited merely in us.\nWe have two kinds of intuition: sense-intuition [and which the], for which the object must be represented as present, and imagination, as intuition without the presence of the object. Imagination, when one is conscious of it as such, can also be regarded as (g inner) sense-intuition.\nThe problem now is whether sense-intuition can be distinguished from imagination (g of outer objects); the idealist denies |\n[the former, and consequently also, and so there falls] this, without doubt on the ground that we perceive our representations (g as inner determinations of the mind) immediately only through inner sense, but not their cause, which we merely infer; and the inference from an effect to a determinate cause is not certain, since more than one cause may produce the very same effect, as here (g either) the outer object or the subject itself may be the cause, in which latter case the intuition would be imagination. An example of the latter is the dream, or also madness, from which therefore the [sense] outer sense-representation as such cannot be distinguished.\nNow I say: outer sense-intuition is distinguished with complete certainty from inner by the mere form of the former, space, in which we posit outer objects, in contrast to the other, whose object we only [posit] in time [the text breaks off].\nIf we cannot distinguish them immediately, neither will it be done mediately by inferences to their cause, for [if we]. I am myself an object of my outer intuition in space, and without this could not know my place in the world. Hence the soul cannot know its place in the body, because it would have to perceive itself through outer [place] sense, and so as outside itself.\nMy representations cannot be outside me, and an outer object of representations cannot be in me, for that would be a contradiction. But although the representation is in me, its object may without contradiction be outside me, or else [the object] the representation together with the object may be in me. According to idealism it is asserted that it is not possible to distinguish whether [not] together with the representation [an] its object is also in me, even if this is represented (g in the intuition) as existing outside me. — Against this the realist of outer intuition asserts that this is possible, and rightly, on the following ground. [That my representation cannot exist outside me (g the subject) What I What] I represent to myself as spa|tial\ncannot be counted among the representations of inner sense, for the form of this is time, which has only one dimension. Likewise what is mere representation I cannot make an object of outer sense, for the form of this is space. — Now the question arises whether the intuition which has the form of outer sense, such as imagination is (in dreams or in fever), is not so much one with that which also has an object of outer sense, that the two cannot be distinguished from each other. The answer is: that in this state of imagination they certainly cannot be distinguished, for this is a deception of the power of judgment; but the question is properly whether they cannot be distinguished at all, that is, whether one cannot become conscious that the one is a sense-intuition, the other indeed sensible intuition, but only in imagination, for which the object outside the representation is not present. The answer is: consciousness can accompany all representations, and so also those of imagination, which, and whose play, is itself an object of inner sense, and of which it must be possible to become conscious as such, since we actually distinguish such representations from sense-intuition as inner representations, and therefore as existing in time.",[]],"reflm:6316":["P. I:\nAgainst Idealism.\n1.) The ideality of space and time, which is merely formal, does not involve real idealism, which pretends that no object outside the representation is given to the perception of things in space. What it means is only this: this object [because] or these outer objects (which remains undecided) do not themselves possess in themselves that form of space under which we intuit it or them, since this form belongs merely to |\nthe subjective mode of our faculty of representation in perception. This may be inferred from the fact that space contains nothing in itself that could be the representation of a thing, or of the relation (g of different things) of such things to one another, as they are in themselves. If space is regarded as such a determination, [to the *entibus*] it is, as an *ens imaginarium* (imaginary entity), a *non ens* (non-entity).\n2.) The representation (g) of the object outside us in space is not illusion [Schein], that is, something does not merely seem to be outside us; it is outer perception, and not merely inner. But (g it) does seem to us to contain this form of space in itself, because we can know only through inferences that it is merely appearance.\n3.) This form of things in the appearance is sufficiently distinguished from every other form, which contains what attaches not to the objects outside us but merely to our mode of representation, by the fact that through it we can determine appearances a priori. We cannot say this of a tone, as distinguished from sound, nor of warmth, as distinguished from the perception of a fluid that penetrates all other matter and expands it. Hence the form of appearance concerns (g outer) sense in general, and not certain particular ways of sensing and (g immediately) perceiving.\n4.) Therefore, with regard to this universal element, sense can (g and also must) be distinguished from imagination by a correct inference. The one exception would be if the objection drawn from the possibility of this confusion in particular representations of the imagination, in madness or dream, were to count as a proof that we might perhaps actually exchange these faculties themselves (sense and imagination), and indeed without any means of escaping from this ambiguity. [because] But in the realism of outer sense nothing else is asserted than this: that no imagination could make things present to us at all [etwa] as objects of the senses outside us, as such, were there not actually such a sense. Hence we distinguish this sense as a faculty different from imagination, not indeed by sensation alone, but by a sure inference |\nand [we distinguish] that something outside ourselves underlies the imagination (even though it is not [the] contained in the perception now given (g supposed))).\nIn the margin beside No. 4:\nIf we know a law a priori, we prescribe this law to the object: to nature, if it is a law of nature; to freedom, that is, to ourselves, if it is a moral law; but not arbitrarily, rather as necessary.",[]],"reflm:6317":["S. I:\nN. I. |\nOn the Critique in respect of Theology.\nIn order to prove that it is unavoidable for reason to assume the existence of God, and indeed in accordance with a concept that is sufficient for the theoretical as well as the practical employment of our reason, so far as it aims at the ultimate principles *a priori*, I had to prove that speculative reason can neither render its concept consistent with itself, nor prove [beweisen] such an existence, nor even establish the reality of this concept. — For had I conceded the latter, I should (g either) have had to proceed to the employment of reason in respect of objects of experience; and since I should have had to regard these as things in themselves, I should first have run into antinomies, in which all speculative reason founders, and finally I should have turned the divine being into something sensible [sensificirt] and anthropomorphic; or else I should have had to regard everything as appearances and to seek the Deity alone among things in themselves, through pure ontological concepts, where no knowledge at all would then have been left to me. I had therefore to establish the incapacity of the merely theoretical employment of reason in this matter. Thereby it still remained [the case] that it [der] did not contradict the concept of God and of his existence, whereas otherwise wholly false concepts of God, and in the end the impossibility of thinking such a being, would have resulted.\nOn the Possibility of Knowledge *a priori*.\nThe question is whether, if the causal connection were not given to me in experience, I should have to have insight into it *a priori*. If a manifold existence follows one member upon another, the time in which it follows must also be perceived. But this is possible only if something is there with which all this manifold, which succeeds among its members, is nevertheless simultaneous.\nOn the Mode of Proof in Relation to Possible Experience.\nIf the forms of things, according to their universal properties, are given [gegeben] *a priori* as condition of the possibility of these things, then I can know immediately what these things themselves are in respect of the categories, e.g. of magnitude. But if I wish to know things in general, and not merely their form, through categories, I can know this *a priori* only in relation to possible experience [beweis]. E.g. that all things have a magnitude can be proved only by showing that they can become objects of experience solely through being given in space and time; but under this condition their apprehension is always a magnitude, and therefore the object also.\nLaw of continuity (*continuitas*).\nBetween a and –a (attraction and repulsion, e.g. in a magnetised rod) there is a point at which the predicate of the thing vanishes, becomes = 0. Thus an acute angle as well as an obtuse can be taken for a right angle which is only infinitely little smaller or greater; but an acute angle cannot count as an obtuse. Usefulness can be regarded as an indifferent matter when it is very |\nsmall, but not as harmfulness. Hence it is impossible that virtue should be the mean between two vices (opposed to each other in degree). For the vanishing vice is innocence.\nOn the Occasion of the Critique.\nWe have modes of knowledge *a priori* which we extend without experience; the question is whether we can also extend them beyond experience (g and its objects). If we attempt the latter, we cannot discover our errors through experience, [weil] and possibilities of thought remain always without reality. Further: even in the case of propositions that seem necessary, we cannot, without a critique of reason, distinguish the objective necessity of the propositions from the subjective necessity of thinking the objects thus and not otherwise, in accordance with the particular nature of our faculties of knowledge, in respect of given consequences. — But nothing depends on all this; for if we have erred, this is merely the missing of an aim at speculative knowledge, by which we wished to extend ourselves beyond the limit of possible experience. We may at most err if we say: the objects are really so, e.g. the supreme original ground of things is an intelligent being, because, given the constitution of our faculty of knowledge, we cannot otherwise make the *phaenomena* comprehensible to ourselves; but this has no detrimental consequences. — But there is in us a property (or reason ascribes it to us as practical beings) which is wholly distinct from the natural properties, and whose laws (g are) wholly distinct from the laws of nature, indeed are even contradicted by these: freedom, and under this concept the law of morality, given [the] our independence of nature. Here, then, we have two laws certain *a priori* which contradict each other in the same subject, if I represent the subject to myself [in] according to the theoretical principles in the same meaning. Hereby I am compelled to think my own subject, as object of the senses and (g at the same time) of reason, as existing in two different ways: 1. as object of the senses; 2. as a being [which], so far as it is not at all an object of the senses. This compels me to a critique of sensibility. But even that would signify nothing, were not empiricism and predeterminism contrary to all morality. |\nMorality, therefore, runs danger through speculative reason without critique. But even here the power of the moral disposition could outweigh speculation. The same practical reason, however, compels us to accept those laws as divine commands, because otherwise they would be without lawful effect, and the course of nature, as principle of practical laws so far as these aimed at one's own happiness, would not correspond at all to the course of nature in relation to the moral [laws]. I must therefore think a God and assume him, but I cannot prove his existence and cannot comprehend him.\nS. II:\nNow it becomes of interest not to make the conditions of the knowledge of things that is possible to us into conditions of the possibility of the things themselves; for if we do this, freedom is abolished (g and) immortality, and we can obtain from God no other than contradictory concepts. This compels us to determine exactly the possibility, extent and limits of our speculative faculty of knowledge, so that Epicurean philosophy may not take possession of the whole field of reason and ruin morals and religion, or at least may not make men inconsistent.\nBesides, space and time are such necessary determinations *a priori* of the existence of things that, were they determinations of things in themselves, they would have to be made, together with all the consequences attaching to them [der Eingeschranktheit], not merely conditions of the existence [Gott] of the Deity, but, on account of their infinity, absolute necessity and necessity, even divine attributes. For once one has made them so, there is no ground why one should restrict them merely to finite beings. Theology, in order not to contradict itself, finds itself compelled to make both only the form of our sensibility, and to place beneath all things that can be known by us, as *phaenomena*, *noumena* which we do not know, in respect of which, however, the unconditioned alone holds. Now since the conflict between the principles of the unconditioned in synthesis and the principles of what is conditioned in space and time [schle], and hence the antinomy of reason, absolutely cannot be settled without making this distinction between the objects and their representations, theology leads to the aesthetic critique.\nIn respect of the theoretical knowledge of objects of possible experience, the critique has the advantage that it resolves the antinomy between the principle of the unconditioned according to mere concepts and that of the always conditioned according to conditions of intuition, by showing that this [intuition], pure as it may be, is always only sensible, and represents the object not as thing in itself but merely in appearance [übrigens]; e.g. the antinomy of the beginning of the world, of the world-whole in respect of space, of absolute and unconditioned causality, and of the unconditioned necessity [oder Zuf] of things. Meanwhile it is still necessary [to argue] against scepticism, which aims precisely at robbing reason of confidence in itself through contradictions between its clearest convictions — idealism is indeed not so dangerous, but it greatly restricts the field of experience and gives a belief a claim even upon our empirical knowledge. — But if it is shown that the determination of our own existence in time presupposes the representation of a space, in order that we may also be able to represent the relation of the determinations of inner intuition to the abiding object; and that space, which is merely a form of intuition, yet cannot be the form of inner intuition, precisely because the inner is not space but time: then reality (as things in themselves) can be secured to outer objects (g the) precisely by not assuming their intuition to be that of a thing in itself. For were it that, and the form of space the form of a thing (g which) would adhere to it in itself even without the particular constitution of our subject, it would be possible that we should have the representation of such a thing even without its existing. But if it is a particular kind of intuition in us, which cannot represent what is in us, and hence what exists in the change of time, because it, as mere representation, could then be thought only in time-relations, then such an intuition must subsist in actual relation to an object outside us, and space signifies actually something which, represented in this form of intuition, is possible only through relation to an actual thing outside us. — Hence a refutation of scepticism, idealism, Spinozism; likewise also of materialism, predeterminism.\n|––––––––––\n[Da] Some judge that it is difficult to make the content of the critique, and by means of it of metaphysics also (g together with its final aim), comprehensible from a single point of view. Moreover, even among those who (g are fair enough) not to misjudge its good intention, some hold it to be a wholly useless chicanery with the [bisherigen] merely speculative proofs of reason [Von Gott] for the most sublime [Glaubens] ideas, upon which the tranquillity of men rests, since in the end the critique comes out upon just the same, though only as articles of belief of pure reason; whereby there is also that which one Apostle says to another, that in it things are found which are too high for many and confuse the simple. [Zu dem Ende] As to the first, the very ease of surveying the whole plan is a strong recommendation of this system and a confirmation of the unity of its principle; and as to the confusion, such a critique is not drawn up to be presented to the simple, but to the subtlest reasoners, who thought [dacht] no matter too high for them. This critique is then to prove precisely that these [matters] are far too high for them, and for everyone, as regards speculative insight, and to confuse them in the delusion of the latter (since it is their own fault to persist in it) [ist die Vorbereitung] in order to condescend to that lowly stage of which all men are capable, for which |\nthose articles of belief must be as accessible as to the subtlest investigation.\nThere are three supersensible objects with which human reason has been incessantly and at all times occupied, and will remain so: God, immortality and freedom. Of the last alone have we an immediate conviction of its actuality, without being able nevertheless to have insight [geh] into it. It is natural to start from this, in order to judge accordingly our possible knowledge of the rest.",[]],"reflm:6318":["On psychology. We cannot know the nature of the soul as a spirit, and consequently cannot know its immortality either, since we can never free it from corporeal influence. — We cannot know God as *realissimum* (the most real being), since we can ascribe to him neither understanding nor will without transferring our own limitation to him.\nThe concept of the *realissimum* is a *conceptus originarius* (original concept), because negations presuppose reality and are removals (*remotiones*) of it, and are therefore derivative. These negations, moreover, can be represented only as limitations of the *realissimum*. Hence the representation of the highest reality as the |\nmatter of all things, so that all evils are supposed to spring only from the form, which consists in negations. — But from the necessity of presupposing that concept it does not follow that a *realissimum* is possible.",[]],"reflm:6319":["R II 254f\nS. I:\nIdealism.\nA specifically different imagination must have a different sense as its ground; for imagination is only an inner determination of sense toward the same intuition which sense has as sense.\nOn the Critique of Pure Reason.\nThe two difficulties in it consist in this: that it must be shown that there is no contradiction in saying, I. that the soul knows itself theoretically only as *phaenomenon*, and so knows itself, but only as appearance. — The solution is this. It does not know itself through concepts, which are merely the simple acts of synthesis that belong to knowledge in general (that is, not through consciousness of these concepts, for that would be a contradiction, since it is supposed to know itself as object), but only by means of the application of these concepts to inner intuition. But it cannot determine time in itself without the representation of space, and the product in space through the imagination. Space, however, lies in its outer sense, which the imagination must affect in a certain way; and through this the inner sense also is affected with regard to the inherence of this representation, even the feeling of pleasure, etc. etc. But the empirical consciousness also of the representations of reason, or of the categories and of thinking in general, still belongs to appearance, because it is an occurrence; and nothing intellectual remains but the I — practically, however, freedom together with its law, as knowledge.\nII. How we can speak of the intelligible, e.g. God, through categories, although these hold only of *phaenomena* in order to yield knowledge, and so can be applied to a being that cannot be represented at all as *phaenomenon*.\nS. II:\nOf the soul in the birth, life and death of man. Of this we have no experience; we should therefore have to infer it either from experience, or prove it a priori from the mere faculty of thinking in life, or from freedom as presupposition for the practical [Verhalt] use of reason. But since the first would always be inferred from the sensible, and the latter solely from the supersensible, which is given to us — breaks off?\nThe identity of the person concerns the intelligible subject amid all diversity of empirical consciousness. The latter may be greatly altered. But so far as it remains connected, it is the knowledge of oneself as the same person, and is required for imputation.",[]],"reflm:6320":["S. I:\nOn the cosmological proof.\nIt rests on this, that (g the concept of) a necessary being is an individual concept, just like that of the *ens realissimum*; hence these concepts coincide, and must coincide, if it is true that the concept of a necessary being is not the concept of a species of things but of a single thing, and one thoroughly determined. The proof is supposed to be this: if there were several, then [sie auf] one necessary being would be determined in a certain way, another in another, i.e. opposite, way; so the first also could, without prejudice to its necessity, be de|termined\nin the opposite way, i.e. its opposite would be possible; so the thing would be contingent and yet necessary, which is self-contradictory. — But from the [other manner] possibility of being determined in another way, a contradiction with the concept of the necessary would follow only if [it is presupposed that thoroughgoing determination must follow from the concept, or that necessary existence (which at the same time contains thoroughgoing determination) is an existence which can already be inferred from the concept of a thing. But no such concept is possible from which existence can be inferred (hence the concept of a necessary being is also a merely problematic concept)] the very same being were thought as determinable in another way. For that one necessary being is determined in a certain way, and another necessary being in another way, proves only that the necessary being is not at all determined [sey] by this its concept as to what it is; for existence is no particular determination of it.\nS. II:\n1.\nïï [If] The most perfect being must exist necessarily (g i.e.) on account of this concept; for if it did not exist, a perfection, namely existence, would be lacking to it.\n2.\nïï The necessary being must* contain all perfection. For if it did not contain them all (already through its concept), then through this concept it would be undetermined in respect of one predicate or another; and consequently its being (g as) such [as] and not (g as) another would be contingent, i.e. it would not be necessary.\n*(g would already be known through this mere concept as the most perfect; |\nS. III: |\nfor if it were not determined as a being in general through all reality, [so wäre es in An] but undetermined even in respect of one, then [es seyn Gegentheil], just as it is, its opposite would be possible, and it would therefore also not be necessary. But this does not follow; for a necessary being with a, and also one with non-a, would be possible; i.e. what would be possible is not the opposite of the existence of this being, but the opposite of its predicates, without prejudice to the existence of the subject. — In the Cartesian proof existence was made a particular predicate; in the cosmological proof existence is made the subject. In both, the concept of an all of reality is always laid at the foundation, as the only concept that [represents] a thing [in sein] as thoroughly determined a priori, and hence as the only non-derivative concept.)\nS. II:\nEven the best thinker, when he hears this proof, may well fancy that he feels [a] conviction so long as he has it in thought; but once he has forgotten the words, he is quite empty, as if he had heard nothing. He must orient himself through sound reason.\nBoth must be analytic judgments, although the first is evidently synthetic. But if the second were analytic, the all of perfection would be derived from the concept of necessary existence. Since this, however, as mere being, contains no concept of the subject of the judgment, it follows that, just as in the first proposition the synthetic predicate is lacking to the subject, so here the concept of a subject as a thing of particular quality is lacking to the predicate, and mere being is taken in its place.\nS. IV:\nCosmological proof.\nThe proposition that the necessary being must have all reality is conducted synthetically, although it can be conducted only analytically. For it does not say: a necessary being has in its concept all reality; but: if we wish to form a concept of it, we must think it under the conditions under which only a single being can stand. Now the first (to form a concept of it) is impossible; for that could be nothing other than that a being of certain determinations would be so thought that, in accordance with these, its non-being would be impossible, i.e. self-contradictory.",[]],"reflm:6321":["*Ens necessarium* (necessary being).\n(g Its *conceptus* is not generic but *singularis*.)\nIt is said: The concept of an *ens necessarium* is only a *conceptus singularis** (because this being must be thought through its concept as thoroughly determined). But it does not follow that, if a thing also exists necessarily, this existence must also be derivable from its concept (rather, existence can never be inferred from the *omnitudo realitatis*); and the thing would not thereby become contingent, if besides it several further things, *partim realia, partim negativa* (partly real, partly negative), were assumed as existing necessarily.\n*(g i.e. there cannot be necessary beings internally different from one another.)\nA being that is already thoroughly determined through its concept (g identical proposition) (can exist in only one way, if it exists) contains all reality (as aggregate). Granted. But not: give me such a concept as carries absolute necessity with it. You can name only one of which it is problematic whether such a thing is even possible.",[]],"reflm:6322":["P. I:\n*a contingentia transscendentali* (from transcendental contingency) — in contrast to the *naturalis*.\nThat an absolutely necessary being must contain all reality would have to be an analytic judgment, since it has been drawn from the concept of the absolutely necessary. Thus the predicate is identical with the concept of the subject, but not conversely; for otherwise they would have to be *conceptus reciproci* (reciprocal concepts). [Now are] Thus it is not the concept of all the predicates that belong to the *necessarium*. Thus the concept of the object was not one that contained the thoroughgoing determination [because existence was not among them. — If the concepts were not wholly identical, some most real beings would not be necessary beings — Thus there would be more than one *realissimum*], which was yet demanded in the proposition [contained] that the necessary being can be determined in only one way; for according to this admis|sion the concept of the whole of reality does not contain the thoroughgoing *determinatio*. Thus the proposition that from absolute necessity one can indeed infer to thoroughgoing reality [of a being], but from the latter not conversely to the former, is a *contradictio in adiecto*.\nRather one must say: the concept of the *realissimum* is a transcendental original concept (*Urbegriff*), i.e. with transcendental concepts one can [by] invent no other things than by laying the *realissimum* at the foundation, [since] which contains, as it were, the material of all things, and now posits the manifoldness only in the form, and consequently thinks all other concepts as derived, but, be it well understood, through mere ontological concepts.\nThe analytic *oppositum* of a is = 0, the synthetic = a – a = 0; to designate these two kinds of removal (*remotio*) is the purpose.\n––––––––––\nThe proposition: an existing being must at the same time be represented as a thoroughgoingly determined being, i.e. it is thoroughgoingly determined, is an analytic proposition. — But the proposition: (g any) existing being must be able to be thoroughgoingly determined through the concept of its existence, is not only a synthetic proposition, because it goes [still] beyond the concept of existence, which contains the thoroughgoing existence, and says that the thoroughgoing determination of it as an individual being is possible through the mere concept of its (necessary) existence, but also a false proposition; for from the (g mere) concept of [mere] existence this concept of a thing cannot be determined at all, even if I assume it as necessary: rather the proposition means only this: if I were [to know] able to know its absolute necessity, I would have to know (g it) from the mere concept of it, which [schl] latter is utterly impossible. — The second proposition runs thus: [if I] I can know the thoroughgoing determination through the concept of it as an All of reality. [Das ist aber schon wiederl] But thereby I do not know the existence of this thing. [For] That is already refuted: for if I could do this, I would need no other proof than the Cartesian.\nThe proposition: an existing being must be represented as thoroughgoingly determined through the concept of its |\nexistence; but from this it does not follow that, if it is also thought as necessarily existing, it can be thoroughgoingly determined through this concept; rather [is] the concept of existence leaves the determination of the thing, i.e. with which predicates it exists, wholly undetermined, except only that something real is in it, since otherwise it would be no thing.\nThe concept of an *ens realissimum* (most real being) is a *conceptus singularis* (singular concept): it [is] contains nothing that can be common to different things.\nThe cosmological proof runs thus:\nThe necessary being must be a most real thing. For (g as a necessary being it must be determined through its concept with regard to existence, hence as most real) were it not such, it would not be thoroughgoingly determined through [the] its concept [of a thing] [(because the concept of the most real is the only one that at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination in itself)], and so would not be thought as existing. This inference is false; for through the concept of existence I think to myself the thoroughgoing determination, but not that through this concept the thing (g must) be able to be thoroughgoingly determined (as the concept of the *realissimum* carries with it). For the concept of the thing is not determined at all through (g the concept of) existence [even if it] (except that something real is thought in it), but only that it is [posited] given outside my concept.\n(g The proposition that the absolutely necessary being has all reality is just as much synthetic. For the concept says only that the non-being of it is impossible, whatever determination it may have. Thus the proposition goes beyond this concept, in that it determines it.)\nIf from the concept of a necessary being the *omnitudo realitatis* (totality of reality) of it followed, I should not [be allowed to prove] be permitted to ground the existence of a necessary thing on experience, |\nbut among everything possible there would also be an absolutely necessary, i.e. such a one exists.\nThe common proof runs thus: If [it not] a necessary being were not the most real, [it through] its concept would be alterable, hence this being itself alterable, hence contingent. But this does not follow.\nP. II:\nAccording to the Wolffian philosophy it is held that, even if all our actions, indeed every use of reason in time, were predetermined, yet, if through [the last] reason the concepts by which we determine ourselves to action were distinct, they would nevertheless be free. Critique has the peculiarity that it does not do this.\nAt right angles to the preceding:\nOn divine causality with regard to human beings.\nHow a *caussatum alterius* (something caused by another) can yet be a free being capable of imputation, since it is thoroughgoingly determined by another with regard to its own causality, cannot be comprehended. Thus neither can it be comprehended how God himself can know present free actions. — The ground of this incomprehensibility lies in this, that we have no concept of how a substance can yet at the same time exist and endure only as a *caussatum* of another being. For persistence in itself is the character of all substance as phenomenon.",[]],"reflm:6323":["p. I:\nCosmological proof of the existence of God.\nThe proposition runs thus: if [a necess] the necessity of the existence of a being consists in this, that [it is] it is thoroughly determined through a single concept, then it has all reality. —\n*idem aliter* (the same otherwise). If the necessity of the existence of a being can be known from concepts, then it must be known as the most real being [*ens realissimum*]. — But the necessity [through] of a being can never be known through concepts of it.\nOr conversely: If the most real being is to be known as a necessary being, then its existence must be known from concepts. Now the latter is false, therefore the former also. — For if in the antecedent [text breaks off].\nOr if a necessary being [is to be known as such] exists, then its thorough determination must follow from a concept of it [kn] (but the inference is not to be reversed). Here it must be remarked that this thorough determination would have to follow from the concept of a necessary being and not from other concepts, which is false.\n|| Or: If a being is absolutely necessary, then it must be thoroughly determined through its concept. — [The consequence is not evident. But if one were to say that it must be thoroughly determined (though not through its concept), then it would not have to be the most real being.] It should read thus: if it is to be known as such. For even if it is [as] necessary, yet if this absolute necessity admits of no knowledge of the being as such, then one can have no concept of it that would determine [it] that problematic [concept].\nIf one concedes that from (g the concept of a being of) the highest reality it cannot be inferred that it exists [necessarily] on that account (from such concepts), but that nevertheless, if a necessary being is assumed (which is already a kind of contradiction), the highest reality can be inferred, then the concept of a *realissimum* must be a wider concept, which contains under it not merely the concept of the *necessarium* [in], but still more things besides. But then the being is not thoroughly determined, in respect of its constitution (according to reality), through the concept of |\nnecessity, which yet was supposed to have been done in the concept of reality.\n(g The concept of a necessary (g modality) being is wholly indifferent to every possible determination that does not merely contradict itself.)\nA necessary being must have all reality; for if it did not have all reality [in its concept], it would not be thoroughly determined through its concept, and consequently would not be a being constituted necessarily as it is.\nSince, then, we can find no concept from which the necessity of its object could be known, the *omnitudo realitatis* (the sum total of reality) will only be the *conditio sine qua non* (indispensable condition) of the concept of a necessary being, without which a thing indeed cannot [be] be, but through which it does not yet become all that it is, that is, through which we cannot know its existence, although we think through it everything that it is.\np. II, to the left of Motherby's communication, written across it:\nThe real is opposed to the\nnegative,\nalso — — —\nideal,\n— — — — —\nformal.\nThe merely formal ideality of the objects of the senses is proved in the transcendental aesthetic.\nThe material ideality of the objects of outer senses, namely that no outer object whatever corresponding to them exists, is refu|ted\nby this: that otherwise we should ourselves have no inner sense, and no empirical consciousness in time, since time as a magnitude can be known only in outer [alterations] objects.\nOnly space and time have a formality of which the properties can be stated synthetically a priori (not so with colours).\nOn the alleged necessity of specifying something real (an underlying pleasure or displeasure) as the incentive of the will in the moral law.\n––––––––––\nIdealism.\nThe impossibility of determining one's existence in the succession of time through the succession of representations in us, and yet the actuality of this determination of one's existence, is an immediate consciousness of something outside me which corresponds to these representations*, and this intuition cannot be mere illusion. — The possibility of this consciousness of an object as lying outside us lies in the simultaneity [*Zugleichseyn*] of the manifold of intuition, because I can carry out the successive taking-together forwards and backwards, which cannot be done in the representation of the manifold in time without the limit of space.\n*(g and of what exists not merely in my representation (g but (g as thing) in itself), because otherwise no determination of time of my existence would be possible from this representation itself.\nThe thought of whatever it may be can indeed be possible, e.g. a relation of things outside us according to (g more than) three dimensions, but it is not on that account yet clear that such an object is also possible; at least it would not be space. One then says: that thought lacks the proof of its objective reality, although, logically considered, it is possible.)\nTo the right of the letter, written across it:\nOn God as the greatest aggregate of reality: *ens maximum* (the greatest being), or as the highest ground: *ens summum* (the highest being), *ens entium* (being of beings).\nComplaints about the mischief which metaphysics causes in matters of the state |\nand of the church: introducing anarchy into both. So much restricting of the supreme power that at last none is valid any more, or altering religion so long and so often that none is left. The importance which one thereby gives to metaphysics.\n1. Reality of the empirical way of thinking as against the ideality of the rational. Glossing over of [the latter through] the former by the latter.\n2) Reality according to matter, as distinguished from mere form, as filled space, and space not as a property of things but merely of the mode of representation.",[]],"reflm:6324":["P. I:\nCosmological proof.\nIf anyone were to ask whether we can prove from concepts a priori (g of any being whatever) that it is necessary, we should have to admit that this cannot be done. For, whether the concept contains much or little, provided only that it does not contain alteration [Veränderung] in itself, it would be just as easy to cancel it (g without contradiction), [it may] even if it contained all reality. — But suppose someone says: experience teaches that something is, and reason that something or other is necessary (s One could therefore perhaps contrive [herauskünsteln] from being-necessary the concept of such a being, e.g. all reality). Then reason would have to teach also that everything is necessary; for a thing is necessary either through itself or through a supreme cause. Thus no proof has been given of any being that has on that account something special about it; rather, everything that is, is necessary.\nIf that alone is necessary whose concept can be determined in only one way, then nothing is necessary. For a thing can be determined as the most real [allerrealeste] or as the less real, etc. etc. But if it is said that, when a thing is the most real, it can as such (not as the determinable concept of a thing in general) be determined in only one |\nway, then we must assume what is to be proved: that the necessary being is the most real. — On orientation [Vom Orientiren].\nIf I cannot prove from the mere concept of a thing that such a thing exists, I can never know its [necessary] existence as necessary; for it is precisely knowledge a priori that proves [that] the necessity.\n(s Proposition. A necessary being must have all reality. For if it does not have it, one reality would be lacking to it which yet could belong to it; the opposite of its determinations would therefore be possible; it would be possible that a being with the given determinations should not be, i.e. it would be contingent. — But if I have once assumed it as a necessarily existing being, just as it is, I cannot say that a reality which could be in it is lacking to it, but only that a certain reality is necessarily lacking in it (or that one which is in it cannot exist). Had I not added this, I could not say either that it is contingent, since it cannot exist otherwise than with this lack. — But according to my concept of an existing being in general, it cannot be thought as thoroughly determined [durchgängig determinirt] otherwise than through that of the *realissimum*. But from this thoroughgoing determinability existence does not follow at all, and if something exists I cannot, by the mere concept of its existence, determine [whether and how] how much reality it has.)\nIf one extracts from the Critique of Pure Reason the (g theoretical) propositions to which it leads, and compares them (g by themselves) alone with their currently prevailing, dogmatically [herausge] assumed opposites, without first setting out the derivation of the former from their grounds, one can, as Rector Ludwig of Schlottheim thought, set up palpable absurdities.\nP. II:\nBeing and not-being are the simplest concepts when they express the logical relation of the subject to the predicate in a categorical judgment. But the concept of a thing which in itself contains the representation of a being, and the opposite, the concept of that which in itself is [only] the representation of not-being, are the simplest representations of things.\n1.) *Realitas* (g as the material) in the object, as distinguished from the mere form, which represents only the manner of combination and separation. E.g. when one thinks that in the moral law the determining ground [Bestimmungsgrund] lies merely in the possibility of serving as law and in the form of the law — others, however, in some object of choice [Willkühr] as incentive [Triebfeder].\n2. *Realitas* as opposed to ideality. For even the form can lie in the object itself or merely in the representation of the subject.\n3. *Realitas*: in opposition to the nullity of a thought which can indeed be thought but has no object (g for knowledge).\n4. *Realitas* [in opposition] as ground, although its consequence is a nega|tion.\nThus –a is by itself something real, but [only a ground]; in combination with a, a–a is a negation = 0.\n5. The real to be regarded as the good (metaphysical perfection), and the evil merely as the formal. Consequently, by taking together all reality in general in the idea of one being, one forms for oneself, merely through limitation, the concepts metaphysically of all possible things. [De] Thus one is not to make for oneself a God out of what we know as real [as real] through moral incentives, but only to seek supporting instances [Belege] for the concept of reality. Existence of God inferred from this. (g The really opposed [das realiter entgegengesetzte]. — The aggregate of all reality as sum-total [Inbegrif]: God, or as ground.)\nAccording to Aristippus, pleasure and displeasure alone are true realities; everything else is only representations of relations.\nNow follows a preliminary work for the Gemeinspruch. Quite at the bottom of the page stands the following s-addition, from the same time (ω2) as the earlier ones:\n(s *Realitas*: that whose concept already contains a being signifies (in opposition to negation as *aliqvid oppositum nihilo*) that whose concept is existence, and there I can distinguish the possibility, actuality and necessity of existence. In objects of the senses, *realitas* (*phaenomenon*) is that which corresponds to sensation. *Sensibile qvoad materiam* (*qvoad formam* it is space and time, which are indeed something actual, but nothing existing). All appearances, i.e. objects of possible experience, contain in themselves space, time, and the sensible [Empfindbare] in general. — The latter one might also call *apprehensibile*, the graspable [Ergreifliche] of sensible intuition, which would otherwise be empty.)",[]],"reflm:6325":["P. I.:\nA necessary being must have all reality.\nFor if one were lacking to it, it would still be able to have this (g as a thing in general); it was therefore not necessary just as it is. — From the concept a priori of no thing in general can we obtain its existence, and from the [Voraus] a priori given [concept of] existence we can obtain no concept of the thing. A concept (thoroughly determined) gives no existence, and existence given a priori gives no concept.",[]],"reflm:6326":["P. IV:\n(Ontotheology.) The thoroughgoing determination of a thing in general through a concept consists in assuming it either as thoroughly negatively determined or as really determined. The first gives no thing, therefore only the second does. The most real [Allerrealeste] is the concept of an individual, not of a genus of things, which could still be A or non-A in respect of some predicate.",[]],"reflm:6327":["If I say: a [resting] body (g which is at rest) is moved, the predicate 'moved' can refer to rest or also to body. It is thereby indicated that it is meant here as a synthetic predicate, and indeed in respect of the concept of body. But if it says: a resting body is moved, that means: in so far as I think it as at rest, it is moved, and the judgment would be analytic and false.\nThe two propositions: a body which is at rest is not at rest,\nand: a resting body is not at rest,\nare indeed logically, i.e. *formaliter* (in respect of relation), the same, but not *materialiter* (in respect of content). — For in the latter [case] it is a relation according to concepts, in the former a relation [to] expressed in time. A body that exists as at rest (in [the] one time) exists in another as not at rest. Rest is here set in relation not with the concept of the body but with its existence, which is indicated by the separation of the predicate from the subject. There the first judgment is posited as an assertoric judgment in the relation of two judgments: A body is at rest, and the very same body is not at rest. If therefore the body is not at rest, while it remains the same body, it can yet also be at rest, but at another time.",[]],"reflm:6328":["P. I:\nThat a thing can be another than a certain given thing, although it is internally determined in precisely the same way — is again the *principium identitatis indiscernibilium*.\nBut that a thing can be precisely the same, although it is internally determined in wholly opposite fashion, is again the *principium diuersitatis discernibilium*. — According to the latter, the proposition: Caius, who is (was) unlearned, is (now) learned, is a proposition that expresses the identity of the subject with predicates of it that are (g mutually) opposed in time, hence a proposition of the possibility of alteration. In it the predicate (learned) holds neither of the thing nor of the concept of it, but according to the state in which I [sic] it Sätze, which (g in itself) is no concept of the thing.",[]],"reflm:6329":["In the proposition: Cajus, who is learned, is unlearned, there is no contradiction, because the »is« is only the copula in the relation of two representations, and I connect the »unlearned« not with the [Cajus] learned, but with the [who is learned] Cajus. But if I said: the learned Cajus is unlearned, then [das em] the subject makes up only one concept, and the predicate conflicts with it. In the first case the »est« signifies an existence in time, yet in such a way that time itself remains undetermined, whether it be the past or the present time.\nIf I say, then: Cajus, who is learned, (g is) unlearned, these are two opposed predicates connected in one thing (g namely Cajus, in respect of his existence as changeable), but not in one concept. If I say, however: the learned Cajus is unlearned, I connect both in one concept, and there they contradict each other. — This has only the use of showing* how little we have insight into the possibility of objects, e.g. of changes: a and non a in one thing.\n*(g That time is absolutely not to be brought under concepts of the understanding; because (therein) *conjunctio praedicatorum oppositorum in eodem subjecto* (conjunction of opposed predicates in the same subject) would occur in the concept of change, the possibility of which can be thought only on the presupposition of time.)\n(g In the proposition:) A man who is learned is unlearned, the concept of the learned is not a partial concept of the subject; for the »is« here signifies not the copula in a judgment, but merely the existence of the man, represented in a determinate way.",[]],"reflm:6330":["1793–Jan. 1794). Loose leaf F 19. p. I. R II 357.\nThe infinite divisibility of matter proves that it is mere appearance. For according to concepts of the understanding a whole cannot contain the ground of the possibility of the parts, but the reverse; the first ground of the whole can therefore lie only in the simple.",[]],"reflm:6331":["1793–Jan. 1794). Loose leaf C 1. pp. I–III. R I 121–124.\np. I:\nCausality as ground of existence\ncan also be used of the supersensible, like all categories, because it concerns merely synthetic unity (here, of the relation), but does not determine the way in which such a unity is given in experience, e.g. not as that upon which something else necessarily follows. Whether anything of this kind is possible or not is not settled theoretically, but only assumed practically. — To regard God not as sum-total (*Inbegriff*) but as ground of all reality guards, again, against the otherwise unavoidable anthropomorphism, if we wish to take this as our example.\nIn the right margin:\nThat metaphysics be recommended to all through the supersensible.\nWhether there is probability in metaphysics.\nAbove this marginal note Kant has noted two book titles:\nRayd. F. F. C. de aeqvitate etc etc. Lingae apud F.A. Julicher.\nWild: de rotatione annuli Saturni. 4. Hannover. apud fratres Hahn.\np. II:\nChange.\nContingency is supposed to be known from the changeability of a thing, hence also [wenn es] its necessity from unchangeability, which is said to be found only in the concept of the highest reality. But that is only the logical unchangeability of a concept in respect of its determinations in thought, not the real unchangeability of the thing itself.\nChange in general.\nThat a *realissimum* is necessary, [keine objective] i.e. [aus] that existence does not lie in its concept; so too that a *necessarium ens realissimum* is cannot lie in the concept of the *necessarium*. For in the first case the concept of the *realissimum* would be a *conceptus latior* (wider concept) than that of a necessary being, the latter therefore *angustior* (narrower); in the second case the former would be |\n*angustior* than that of a necessary being and contained under it. They must be *conceptus reciproci* (reciprocal concepts), because they concern thoroughgoing determination, which is identical on both sides.\np. III:\nAlthough one can form no concept at all of a necessary being, i.e. although no concept of a being suffices to enable us to infer from it that such a being must necessarily exist, yet one believes that, once the necessity of some thing of which one has no concept at all is assumed, the concept of what constitution this thing has can well be developed from it. Now the concept of a necessary existence [ganz einfach] is the concept of no object at all, but merely of the existence of an object and of the impossibility of its not being. But existence and non-being, likewise possibility and impossibility, are concepts of mere modality, which leave the object itself entirely undetermined as regards content. It is therefore vain to hope to find the properties of a necessary being in this way. It is as with a point, which can be represented only as the boundary of a line.\nThe proposition: a sick man is healthy, is a logical judgment, because the »is« signifies merely the copula and by means of it contains a contradiction. The proposition: a man who is sick is healthy, would say just the same, and so would contain a contradiction, if the »is«, which occurs twice, though belonging once to the subject and the other time to the copula, were regarded in both cases as copula. But the first time it is not the copula (of the categorical judgment), but a (synthetic) determination of the concept of the subject, and the judgment is regarded as synthetic: namely, that when I have affirmed something of the subject, I deny of it not the very same thing but something other. Namely, he is [gesund] sick at one time and healthy at an- |\nother. This is expressed thus: a sick man becomes healthy. This »becomes«, however, is not the mere copula, but a representation of the existence of an object in time.",[]],"reflm:6332":["Astrology, Cabbala, alchemy, treasure-digging, animal (g magnetism) electricity, incantation formulas.",[]],"reflm:6333":["The consciousness of the presence of an object is perception. — The subjective [element] of perception is sensation, the objective, i.e. the concept of what is sensed, is reality.",[]],"reflm:6334":["But not merely this negative ground of the deception with supposed insights that overfly all limits of sensibility, but the great good fortune which reason [a priori] has in extending its knowledge a priori, chiefly in mathematics, is [es] that which [Hofnung Muth macht auch mit] instils the courage, on the wings of mere [Vernun Verstan] concepts, without needing the construction of concepts, [welche de zu dürfen] which gives mathematics its whole firmness, to venture on its own part (g es auf) an adventure [von derselben] of a similar kind. For if mathematics, without borrowing anything from experience, yet gives the rule to experience itself a priori, why should this not [glücken] succeed just as well for philosophy, in that part of it which is entitled metaphysics? And it does in fact succeed for it in various principles belonging to natural science. If it succeeds |\nindependently of experience, why should it not succeed beyond all limits of possible experience (within which mathematics still keeps) as far as the attainment of that El Dorado (g i.e.) of the supersensible? — Before the appearance of the Crit. d. r. V. everyone thought so, hence also so many metaphysical systems and in fact how — the Organon breaks off.\n|––––––––––\nIt seems that if one grants that the soul is substance, permanence too must be granted to it, as to bodies. We cannot, however, know anything permanent at all [in] an the soul, such as, e.g., in body, weight or impenetrability. — Thus the concept of the soul as substance is only the concept of a mere category of the subject, in distinction from the inhering accident, a concept [dessen] of an object whose possibility is not at all knowable a priori.",[]],"reflm:6335":["P. I:\nOutline of Metaphysics. The investigation of the laws of nature, which constitutes the beginning of all philosophy, could not proceed without men's becoming aware, in the case of many of these laws, of their necessity as well, over and above their actuality and empirical universality. Yet men did not carefully separate these laws, as modes of knowledge *a priori*, from the others, which are of empirical origin, nor set them out ordered together in a system of their own. Why should this indeed have been needed, if one could get along with those laws *in concreto*, as still happens in the investigation of nature, without examining and appraising them in their abstract form? So Newton proceeded with the proposition of the equality of action and reaction. It is like the orators who long followed grammatical rules before they thought of a grammar; by their practice they instructed the gram|marians rather than learned their art of speaking from them. — Such a science of nature, united with mathematics, could also make great progress with consciousness of the necessity of its propositions, without subtilising over the possibility of this progress, since its certainty lay immediately before its eyes. — What, then, can have moved an Aristotle to separate from judgments of experience the concepts that are in constant use in common life and in experience, and likewise principles of the same kind, and so far as possible to measure out, as it were, all our knowledge, [which] of which we are masters *a priori*, unless he had intended something other than natural science, namely something that reaches beyond it? This science, which reaches beyond nature, rests on mere concepts *a priori*, and is therefore entitled metaphysics, is as it were the India that promises man far greater and more splendid possessions than the meagre fatherland of the senses. In that fatherland he is despotised by nature, and at last, after [long vain labour to] constant deception [with] through the feigned attainment of an end, sees nothing before him but death.\nFrom the inner nature of human reason there now develops a need for a final end which no nature grants, indeed does not even promise, since it lies outside the field of sensibility; and toward this land he looks out.\nP. II:\nThe addition (to this, [all is] there is no freedom, and everything is natural necessity) brings it about that in the opposition actually less is said than is required for contradictory opposition. For natural necessity means [a] the causality of a thing as a being of sense. This can always take place, because freedom is the causality of a being of understanding.",[]],"reflm:6336":["(2nd half of June–beginning of August 1795) L Bl. F 4. P. I. R II 281.\nAffinity according to laws of vitality (g of attraction and aversion, in which however I fear Doctor Akakia), not according to chemical laws, produces bastards. — Laws of animal affinity in the generation of bastards prove the sentient principle to be an organised or self-organising fluid, for otherwise they could not interpenetrate.",[]],"reflm:6337":["One could not have fallen upon the [dyna] mathematical antinomies if one had not taken things in space and time for things in themselves instead of appearances. For how could one assume a world-whole whose parts, as conditions, were yet always to be [un]conditioned? In space, however, it is so. Of appearances there is indeed nothing unconditioned, since they are mere representations. (Besides, given a whole, one would also have to abstain from the unconditioned, which would be infinite.) — But if we assume a boundary of the world, we have a place of the world in empty space, i.e. a relation and change of place without objects to which it is the relatum. Both are false, because space is not a thing in itself but a form of intuition, which signifies space only in so far as we draw it, hence merely as a process in our representation. — Likewise with infinite elapsed time, or time that has a beginning. — Infinite division, too, is possible only under the presupposition of the ideality of space, since bodies or changes in time are only appearances, and consequently in themselves undetermined in respect of the multitude of their parts, so that the *regressus* goes on to infinity. — But both [propositions] are false, because they contain not what is logically opposed but *realiter opposita*. *Contraria* therefore say more than etc. etc.\nThat everything which happens in the world stands under the law of *praedetermination* is true, because these are *phaenomena*, hence objects of possible experience, which without those laws could not be so. — Yet this very fact also proves that an intelligible ground of them, in so far as human beings could be regarded as *noumena* (which the moral law must subsequently establish), can at the same time be thought, a ground which determines these same events, although they are not [not de] dependent on the chain of causes and effects in the sensible world, and this chain therefore does not predetermine them. Thus both can be true, because they contain less than is required for opposition. — The same holds of the possibility of a necessary being, although all *phaenomena* connected with the world as cause and effect are contingent. — For the dynamical categories permit the conditions to be of a different kind from the conditioned; not so the mathematical, which assume only the homogeneous as condition.\n(g The I that composes and separates. — 2. The I as the composite of inner intuition.)\nWe can have a (g dynamical) knowledge of a *noumenon*, but only in a practical respect, if we recognise for human beings a practical law which lays at its ground a supersensible condition [thereof].\n|––––––––––\nThe proposition: all [effects] events in space and time stand under the law of natural necessity, is true. — The other: they do not stand under this law, because their ground can also be supersensible, since in the former case the relation of ground to consequences is only a sensible one and [...] concerns the possibility of experience. — Likewise: there is no necessary being in the world of sense, but there can yet be one in the intelligible world.",[]],"reflm:6338":["P. II:\nOne proof of the ideality of space is that it is a magnitude which can only ever be represented as a part of a still greater one |\n(i.e. is infinite). — A second is this: however small a space we may represent to ourselves, I can think all given things in a still smaller space, without the things in the one being required to differ in the least, considered internally, from those in the other. — Space is therefore not the sum-total of things in themselves. — Likewise with time.",[]],"reflm:6339":["A great ground for adopting the distinction of objects as *noumena* and *phaenomena* [as] also as a necessary hypothesis is that without it freedom cannot be defended at all, and without presupposing freedom there is no morality.",[]],"reflm:6340":["P. II:\nOn the utility of the principle of making every advance of knowledge by going back to principles, as if they were now to be discovered for the first time. Thereby one avoids |\nthe one-sidedness (*fallacia systematis*) of holding an accepted system itself to be faultless, on account of the plausibility of the consequences drawn from it. If one examines each proposition as if the centre of the system had not yet been found, one can often discover errors in it, which make a fresh examination of the system, and often its overthrow, necessary.\nP. I:\nDeutschland, 7th number, 1796. Berlin. p. 10: »On the concept of republicanism« by Friedrich Schlegel.",[]],"reflm:6341":["g-addition in the margin at upper right beside lines 15–21:\n(g On the identity of the person amid manifold changes. The I as that which does not arise, pass away, or even merely persist.)\nWhether, if a thing (a simple substance, or also a composite of such) has ceased to be, it could be entitled the very same thing (*per palingenesiam*) if something perfectly equal and similar followed upon it? Or whether, as in the cycle of the Platonic year, the previous state must always be the cause of the following, so that, should this causal nexus once be broken, the restoration (*apocatastasis*) would in fact be an entirely new generation, and likewise with the future resurrection. — A contradiction lies hidden in the question itself. For change of the very same thing is thought, where only exchange for another should be thought.\n––––––––––\nHe who philosophises not in the manner of the schools but in the manner of genius manages from full stock, which then lets a near bankruptcy be foretold |\n(*noris quam sit tibi curta supellex*). Critical philosophy is that [which] economy of reason which first examines its state of assets, in order to know how far it can go in expenditure, and it looks like a simpleton [*Pinsel*] beside the spirited head, who [so], like a certain minister boasting of his administration, says: the more debts he makes, the richer he becomes.",[]],"reflm:6342":["We could not know things a priori, [were] did not the subjective element of our faculty of representation, and hence the way in which things appear to us, lie a priori at the basis, as the condition under which alone they can present themselves to us thus and not otherwise.\nWe can know things as they are in themselves only through perception. But then w — breaks off. Were space and time the forms of things in themselves, we should know them only through perception, and so not as necessary.\nWe can know things according to what they are in themselves (*noumena*), and in general can know anything a priori, only in so far as we ourselves make them.",[]],"reflm:6343":["P. I:\nThe final aim of all metaphysics is to ascend from the knowledge of the sensible to that of the supersensible. The Critique of Pure Reason now proves that this can never be accomplished in a theoretical, but can be accomplished in a moral-practical re|spect,\nby means of the transcendental concept of freedom. In [respect] regard to the theoretical faculty of knowledge this concept is [wholly] transcendent and [wholly] absolutely inexplicable and indemonstrable; but in regard to the pure practical faculty (determinable through pure reason alone), by means of the categorical imperative, it has [wholly] indubitable reality. — The reality of the concept of freedom, however, unavoidably carries with it the doctrine of the ideality of objects [in so far as they] as objects [of their] of intuition in space and time. For if these intuitions were not merely subjective forms of sensibility, but forms of objects in themselves, the practical use of them, that is, actions, would depend absolutely on the mechanism of nature alone, and freedom, together with (g its consequence) morality, would be annihilated.\nP. II:\nAccording to the letter, not the supposed spirit.\nIn the reverse direction:\nwhat the very same man says in context.\nAn uprising of the dogmatists en masse.",[]],"reflm:6344":["P. III:\nAll objects are: 1. the *sensibile* (sensible), 2. the *aspectabile* (observable), 3. the *intelligibile* (intelligible).\nIn the margin:\n(g On style of writing: Niemand, ein Mal, Muse, concludiert.)\nThere are two cardinal principles of the whole of metaphysics: the ideality of space and time, and the reality of the concept of freedom. If the former is not admitted, there are no synthetic propositions a priori for theoretical knowledge; if the second does not hold, there are no unconditionally practical propositions of that kind, that is, no laws of duty. But if there are none of the latter, there is no ground for thinking the concepts of God, freedom and immortality as [concept] ideas of the supersensible. — Mathematical and dynamical powers. — Between the two, that of the power of judgment concerning purposiveness in objects, which is [merely] subjective and thereby an objective principle.\nA *quantum* in comparison with which every other given one (*dabile*) can be thought only as a part of a still greater *quantum* is infinite. But the *quantum* which, in comparison with every other assignable *quantum*, can be regarded only as a part is infinitely small. That all extended beings in the world can be brought into a drop of water, or into a space still infinitely smaller, proves the ideality of space, if everything is always regarded as relatively, never as absolutely, great or small.\nP. IV:\nIn order that others also, who have united for the same purpose, |\nI here also add the notice for them that the man whom I regard as the one who understands me best etc. etc.",[]],"reflm:6345":["P. I [also: XXIII 357,3-8 and XXIII 359,8-11 and 25-27]:\nNature and freedom. In both, knowledge a priori. Mathematical and dynamical faculty of reason in the sensible and the supersensible.\n(Against idealism.) Whether we could think things as existing simultaneously, if they merely represented what [is] is in us and in our mind. The thoughts in me are not simultaneous.\n(Schletwein and Hufeland.)\nP. II:\nHow are synthetic propositions* of the supersensible possible? As regulative principles of practical reason. Those of the sensible, as constitutive concepts of the theoretical.\n*(g possible a priori, the former as theoretical, objectively determining objects as appearances. The second as practical, merely subjectively determining objects as things in themselves.)",[]],"reflm:6346":["Two times are not simultaneous, and two spaces are not successive. But since there are nevertheless two different series (g of existence) in one time, and likewise different aggregates in one space, we cannot regard the two as properties inhering in the objects of the senses, but only as forms of the composition of the manifold in a sensible intuition.\nSince the conditions of space and time, which lie at the basis of all representations of experience, carry necessity with them, and hence lie a priori in the faculty of representation of the senses, this can [be possible] come about only in such a way that they lie in the subject and its sensible form of intuition; for this alone is given prior to all experience.\nSuppose we knew the objects of the senses as they are in themselves, when we are immediately conscious of them: this would still be no knowledge a priori, but mere perception, which carries no necessity with it, and would contain only that it is so, not that it must necessarily be so.\nThis ideality of the intuitions a priori (g in the representation of the senses), the (g form of the) composition of the manifold of intuition in one apperception [and a] through the understanding, together with the schematism of the power of judgment — and finally that through reason in the practical sphere.\nThe infinity of actual space proves that it is merely the form of appearance. For that is infinite which is [wholly given] given, but which cannot exist otherwise than as a part of a whole. Now the *quantum* which can exist only as a part of another *quantum* is not a thing that is objectively given.\nFurther, however great the given world may be, it can [in] yet be contained in the space of a drop of water, the head of a pin, etc. etc., because of infinite divisibility. Hence it cannot be composed at all of things in themselves.",[]],"reflm:6347":["On something and nothing, things and non-things — members of the division; the concept of an object is what is divided. — I cannot live to see my own death, for that is a contradiction; but I can live to see the death of another.",[]],"reflm:6348":["(Summer 1797.) Loose leaf E 21, p. I. R II 89f.\nThe form of intuition (not the form of thought) of objects in space and time, because it is represented a priori and as necessary, proves their subjectivity: namely, that they do not belong to the constitution of the objects, but must lie a priori in the sensibility of the subject. In the case of such objects one can indeed know a priori [how] in what form they will be intuited by us, and so how they will appear to us. But at the same time it must also be thought, at least, that there is a kind of representation, namely that of how things are.\nSecondly, freedom is a property of which we are not immediately conscious through inner sense, but it is proved apodictically (namely negatively) through the concept of duty. This property represents to us a being such as man, not as he [as] appears, but as he is. — This principle of knowledge a priori, however, is merely practical.\nBoth together are the cardinal points (*cardines*) of the Critical philosophy, and all metaphysics has them as its end. These, together with the doctrine of taste, make up the whole of the principles [of] breaks off?\nThe forms which we know through experience cannot be known a priori, since otherwise they would lack the character of necessity.\nTranscendental concepts may be called sense-free. Transcendental philosophy likewise sense-free. Everything in which there is nothing empirical, but which is nevertheless universally valid. [Transcendent] e.g. the categories. Transcendent: what is no item of knowledge at all.",[]],"reflm:6349":["P. I:\nIf the appearances of the senses were things in themselves, they would all stand under necessity in the chain of effects and causes, and there would be no freedom, and hence also no categorical imperative, which yet commands unconditionally and necessarily through reason.\nWere the object of inner sense, the soul, not mere appearance of itself but a pure consciousness of its thinking, we could not have in |\nus the togetherness of representations in time, and so in a form that is thought synthetically a priori [in us], and so could have no (g experiential) knowledge of ourselves. For whatever contains representations combined in relations of space and time is mere appearance.\nOf Aenesidemus and the Dialele. — To be answered by Tieftrunk. — How can a subject, intuited by itself, yet know itself merely as appearance?\nOne cannot [for oneself] have any knowledge of an object through the mere category: 1. that many together make up one; 2. that reality has a degree; 3. that something is so constituted that something else is its necessary consequence; 4. that the existence of a thing is in itself absolutely necessary.\nLikewise one can have no knowledge through intuitions alone, and, if they are empirical, no knowledge a priori, unless the rule already precedes.\nThe ideality of space and time (and consequently the concept of all objects of experience as appearances) and the [R] practical reality of the rational concept of freedom are the two cardinal points of metaphysics.\nOf the spectacle that is made over the proposition that in inner intuition one is to oneself only appearance, not the thing itself. It means only this: »inner experience is nothing more than [Erk] empirical knowledge of oneself, only according to the subjective aspect of the kind of our intuition of ourselves in time (of the apprehension of inner representations), as we represent ourselves inwardly, not as the subject is in itself; as it is given to us inwardly, not as it is thought by us.« The difficulty concerns only how the subject in itself can carry out an experience. It must not merely perceive sensations in itself, |\nbut arouse them and combine them synthetically, and hence affect itself. Thus it is not a thinking but an intuiting of itself.\nTo Herr Collegienrath and Director Euler at the Imperial Russian Academy of Sciences in St. Petersburg.\nThat space and time yield synthetic propositions a priori. That this is not possible otherwise than only if they are subjective forms of sensibility; for otherwise they would be synthetic empirical propositions about the object.\nP. II:\nThat synthetic propositions a priori are actual, and hence also possible, is proved by mathematics. That this [possibility] is not, however, possible through perception of the objects of intuition as things in themselves, can be seen from the fact that otherwise they would be empirical and would contain no necessity, which is proper only to modes of knowledge a priori. That [therefore] they point only [through] to the subjective constitution of our sensibility, which, prior to everything empirical, (g therefore) a priori, supplies the form of intuition, and hence can contain the knowledge (g objects) of the senses merely as appearances, follows from this inevitably. That it must also be so, however, follows if we start from the practical-supersensible of freedom. [For the categorical imperative could not hold if actions were determined by natural causes, and no freedom would be possible if determining nature represented things in themselves. Man (g as object in) himself (g merely) would think himself (g in conformity with) determinable by natural laws. So far as he is to be active in himself, he must think himself, regarded as phenomenon, solely under natural laws; for only so can he think himself as noumenon, determining himself independently of the condition of time:] Conversely, if our actions are free, inner sense can give us only appearances, not knowledge of our substance as thing in itself. For were the latter the case, all |\ninner determinations of the soul would* be physically necessary, like everything that happens according to the law of cause and effect, and there would be no freedom.\n*(g The consciousness of all determinations of the soul (of inner sense) would be empirical, and so no practical principle of them a priori, on which all unconditional necessity, and hence also that of actions of duty, rests, would be possible.)\nThat the concept of freedom and its reality is a sufficient ground for assuming the existence of God and a future life. — The moral imperative contains an ought, and for that very reason a capacity, though in conflict with the whole of nature on account of its causality, nevertheless to overcome it (to resist it). This capacity refers necessarily to the capacity of a being to make these [decret] decrees of practical reason accord with nature, or conversely. Such a capacity, however, is in God.",[]],"reflm:6350":["P. I:\nOn the presupposition of the [outer] subjective form of inner and outer intuition, synthetic propositions a priori alone are possible, for the objects must conform to these. This is the principle of the ideality of space and time.\nIf the subject [in the] affects itself to representations of inner sense, these are only [Appear] modes in which the subject appears to itself, and experience is only the composition, ordered according to the categories, of the representations of inner sense.\nMyinda, blind man's buff, is merrier than the brawl of the *andabatae* with bandaged eyes.\nThings [in themselves], as they are in themselves, not considered as appearances, do not qualify for any theoretical knowledge, [but] for they are mere ideas. But to conduct oneself in conformity with these ideas [goes] |\nis a real concept of practical reason. Myinda metaphysica, or anti-critique of pure reason.\nOf a supersensible nature (God, freedom, spiritual being) one can never have an experience; for between the two an action and a reaction would have to take place.\nWhat is an object? That whose representation is a sum of several predicates belonging to it. The plate is round, warm, pewter, etc. etc. Warm, round, pewter, etc. etc. is no object, but the warmth, the pewter, etc. certainly is.\nObject is that in whose representation various others can be thought (g as) synthetically combined.\nIn every judgment there are subject and predicate [and and object which]. The subject of the judgment, so far as it can contain various [pred] possible predicates, is the object. The predicates all depend on the subject, as warm on warmth.\nWarm, square, deep are predicates. — The warmth, the square, the depth are objects. — Just so rational and reason. What is determinable in a judgment, the logical subject, is at the same time the [object] real object.\nWhat is virtuous I can explain analytically; but virtue itself is afterwards composed synthetically [thereby] from it.\n(g Remark. Principle of synthetic universality, so far as it flows from the analytic. — 2 parallel lines, cut by a third, breaks off?)\nP. II:\nThe subject of a judgment, whose representation contains the ground of the synthetic unity of a manifold of predicates, is object.\nThat which contains the synthetic (real) universality of a concept is the object. — What contains merely the analytic [contains is] (logical) is the logical (subject). Reinhold.\nThe concept that contains the synthetic unity of the apperception of the mani- |\nfold (of what might be added to it) is the concept of an object. It is also the subject of a judgment that has many predicates.\n[What is represented in] What is thought in a judgment is the object (g matter); the thinking of it through the predicate is the form in which I think it. [A square] Thus the concept [virtuous happiness] wise is the predicate in a judgment, e.g. [This] The virtuous is wise.\nAt the bottom of the page, separated by a space of about 1 cm from what immediately precedes:\nTo the Society of Artists for the mechanical reproduction of paintings in Duisburg.\nOn the words ein Mahl [once], Niemand [nobody], Jemandt [somebody].",[]],"reflm:6351":["1797). L Bl. Berliner Staatsbibliothek. Nr. 21. S. I, II.\nS. I:\nBerl. Zeitung, 30 September 1797.\n»The Royal Academy of Sciences is not of the opinion of those who regard it as mathematically proved that there are pure subjective representations. It is rather convinced that there are essential counter-arguments to which no satisfactory answer has yet been given, and that there is no lack of strong grounds for the universal empirical origin of all our knowledge, grounds which perhaps have only not yet been placed in their full light.«\nNB. The prize question was set by the Philosophical Class of the Academy of Sciences up to 1 June 1799. The director is Herr Selle.\nSuch a ground of proof is a contradiction in itself. For, |\nif it is to be universally valid, it must also contain unconditioned necessity, and consequently be conducted a priori [gefüh], and therefore also be capable of being conducted independently of everything empirical. For (g only) it is by necessity that universality is strictly recognised.\nS. II:\nThere is no doubt that if a physician had been commissioned by the Academy to draw up the formulation of this problem [gehabt], he could have adduced for the universal [Urs] empirical origin of all his (medical) knowledge not merely strong but incontestable grounds. But how such a problem [für die philosophische] could then have passed into the philosophical field and not into the medical, is not to be comprehended.\nOf synthetic practical knowledge a priori it is also said that it is merely subjective; (of the will) freedom is the first, and the transcendental concepts of God and immortality bear only on the principles of my actions. I ought to act as if there were a God and a future life.\nHow are synthetic propositions of the supersensible possible? As regulative principles of the practical, not as constitutive principles of theoretical knowledge.\n(g The supersensible that is given is the concept of freedom; consequently no synthetic-theoretical proposition, and so none that would be objectively transcendent, is possible.)\nOf objects as things in themselves. That there must be such in our reason is to be seen from the opposite of the sensible in general.",[]],"reflm:6352":["S. I:\nOf the analogy between the parallels and the principle of the universal empiricism of Selle.",[]],"reflm:6353":["S. I:\nWhat if Herr Beck were to begin with the categories, which have no meaning by themselves yet are nevertheless concepts a priori, then proceed to intuitions a priori which correspond to them, and so arrive at space, time and reality.\nThe system of the Critique of Pure Reason turns on two cardinal points: as system of nature and of freedom, of which the one leads to the necessity of the other. The ideality of space and time and the reality of the concept of freedom, from the one of which one is unavoidably led analytically to the other. According to the one, to synthetic-theoretical knowledge a priori; according to the other, to synthetic-practical knowledge, likewise wholly a priori. The nature of man in himself cannot be determined a priori without this determination (to actions) presupposing freedom; for otherwise this determination would not take place a priori. Something supersensible must therefore be assumed, under which the sensible can be regarded as determinable by it, and conversely something sensible a priori, [unter] according to which the supersensible [empi zur Thätigkeit] determines sensibility in deeds. The (g\n1st) principle: all objects of our knowledge, if they are to yield [werden] experience, can only be regarded as appearances. The 2nd principle is the categorical imperative: all actions from freedom can only be thought according to the principle |\nof agreement with the universal validity of their maxims for universal legislation.",[]],"reflm:6354":["S. I:\nInner sense is not yet [das Bewustseyn wod] knowledge of myself, but [nur Vorstellung der inneren Erschei] we must first have appearances through it, and only afterwards, by reflection upon them, form for ourselves a concept of ourselves, which then has as its consequence empirical knowledge of myself, that is, [Erfahr] inner experience. But that even this experience, in another relation, allows us to be judged once more as mere appearance, and not as we are absolutely in ourselves, amounts to this, that the form of inner sensible intuition may also derive wholly and entirely from the specific constitution of the subject, because we must after all be affected by something [auss], at most through attention, by ourselves, and so all inner intuition is passive. We also meet with such a form in our sensible intuition (the inner), namely time, which could never be thought a priori (as it actually is) and so as necessarily proper to the subject [vorgestellt wer]. This representation of time also cannot be derived from concepts, but only as belonging to the appearance of the subject in respect of myself. This now constitutes a double I, but not of consciousness (I appear to myself; even in this empirical consciousness I am still the observed and at the same time the observer; it breaks off.",[]],"reflm:6355":["S. II:\nThat synthetic [sätze] propositions a priori are possible only through the subjective form |\nof sensibility, and consequently can represent their objects only as appearances, is to be seen from this, that they carry necessity with them, yet not from the concept through analysis. For suppose we could perceive things in themselves: such propositions would then lack necessity and universality. But if they are mere appearances, we can know a priori how they must appear to us; for they can have no other intuitions than the subjective constitution of our sense permits. This, however, has no kinship with the fact that, as regards colour, each of us may have his own mode of sense. For this is sensation, and consequently not objective but merely subjective, and carries with it no universality [für jeden] and necessity.",[]],"reflm:6356":["S. III:\n1. How are synthetic propositions a priori possible about things which can be objects of experience? 2. How are synthetic propositions a priori possible about things which cannot be objects of experience, that is, about [bloße idealische Wesen] objects of [blo] pure reason? The former only in that the objects (outer as well as the inner object of inner sense) can in theoretical knowledge be known merely as appearances; the latter merely as objects which correspond to the final end of reason [bestimmen] for (g pure) practical knowledge [in uns s] of ourselves.",[]],"reflm:6357":["S. III:\nBecause only the subjectivity of the form of sensible intuition can make possible synthetic propositions a priori (as going merely to objects in appearance), |\nit can also be understood why they contradict the principles of the pure understanding and the concepts formed in accordance with them; for example, that space consists neither of infinitely many parts nor of a finite number of them; why given space and elapsed time are neither finite nor infinite; because here it is not the object in itself but the making of it, by the composition of the manifold in appearance, that underlies or is laid at the basis of the concept [zu] dem Begriff.\nIs it one and the same to say that space is infinitely divisible, or to say that it consists of infinitely many parts? Likewise in regard to the infinity of space and of time, which are represented as given.\n––––––––––\n[Auch:XXIII 462,18-19]\nOnly through and for the moral law do the theoretical ideas of God and immortality obtain their (practical) reality.\nS. IV:\nHow are synthetic propositions a priori possible in respect of the faculty of knowledge, of the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and of the faculty of desire, objectively in respect of the sensible, subjectively in respect of the supersensible? Of the centring of the power of representation, in that the [Subj] objective is thought on the one hand as appearance, on the other as in itself.",[]],"reflm:6358":["P. II:\nThe whole of critical philosophy.\nFirst, the main division according to the categories.\na. Mathematical and dynamical powers [*Potenzen*]. Constitutive and regulative principles: the former of the faculty of knowledge, the latter of the faculty of desire. — A. All representations given [in] intuition, as appearances, |\nB. as things in themselves. (Appearances not empirical, e.g. of colours, but intuitive representations given a priori.) — The sensible, considered universally as such, points to a supersensible.\nPrinciple: Synthetic propositions a priori (g the theoretical) present all objects to me only [Vor] as appearances. — Things in themselves, thought a priori, relate to the supersensible.\nb. Dynamical powers. 1. Freedom: basis of the constitutive principle of the supersensible. 2. Necessity: basis of the regulative principle. Theoretically transcendent — practically immanent. α. God, β. Immortality of the soul. — Freedom: ideality as regards the subject, and reality in respect of its causality. Concepts of reason and ideas. — Apparent antinomy of reason in respect of the totality of conditions, which are all contingent and conditioned when compared with the unconditioned, which is found solely in pure reason, that is, in the idea. The practical idea as regulative principle: to act as if there were a God and another world. — The scholastic is systematic (*simplex et unum*), the popular fragmentary*.\nP. III:\n* I do not see why one should not begin, with Herr Beck, from the categories, provided it is at the same time admitted that these pure concepts of the understanding, unless sensibility is laid beneath them as *materia circa quam* (the matter with which they are concerned), can produce no knowledge whatever. E.g. quantity (how one is thought as contained in many), quality (how many [are thought] in One); relation: how, when something is given in perception, something really different from it is also given (g a priori) as its consequence; modality: how the form of the connection of all perceptions (in one experience) makes the actuality of this manifold in one experience cognizable a priori [best]. — Here |\nHerr Beck will merely remark that these categories lack objective reality, that is, that one does not see whether such a thing is possible or not. He would then apply the latter to appearances in general in space and time as intuitions, and so follow the synthetic method; but this attempt would involve a different standpoint.\nP. IV:\nTo empirical knowledge (experience) belong: (g 1.) intuition, that is, representation through which an object is given; 2. concept, through which this object is thought; 3. composition of the manifold of intuition; 4. unity in the consciousness of it.\nThe empirical in the consciousness of an intuition is entitled perception (*animadversio*), and has a degree, that is, intensive magnitude; pure intuition has only extensive magnitude (space and time). Space and time in which nothing is perceived (in which no empirical representation of the object is met with) are empty.\nTo the composition of the manifold of intuition in accordance with the rule contained in the concept of it there belong the synthesis of the manifold of intuition into a concept (category), and then the synthesis of perceptions for the possibility of an experience.\n(g To ascend from the morally practical to ideas, and indeed to those of the theoretical: God and immortality, and so in turn downward to the sensibly practical: religion, public and private happiness. Here too the subjective of practical reason is the determining ground of the objective in appearance, upon which synthetic practical propositions a priori can be grounded.)\nOf the knowledge of the sensible and the supersensible.\nSynthetic propositions a priori can make objects known to us only as appearances, not as they are in themselves. — For since by such a synthetic proposition I say more of the object than is contained in my representation of it, I assert of it something that does not belong to it, that is, it is without truth. (Spontaneity of an empty judgment.) — It is not possible in any other way to go a priori beyond my given concept than through a judgment which contains the relation of the object to the constitution |\nof the subject and of its faculty of representation [sub], or rather, in the first place, of its receptivity to being affected in a certain way by the object; such a judgment therefore says only how the object appears to me, not how it is. [With] With this limitation I can say a priori how the object will and must appear to me.\nP. III:\nOne cannot think any mode of representation as limited in respect of a certain principle without setting over against it another which is universal in respect of the same principle. That is, if I distinguish a knowledge by its being limited to the sensibility of the subject, I must think in opposition to it a knowledge of the supersensible, and can afterwards investigate whether and how (in theoretical or practical use) reality can be procured for it.\nTo investigate the supersensible [is] is therefore an unavoidable task of reason: what, outside me or in me, must be assumed [necessarily] as necessarily existing, if not in the theoretical, yet in the morally practical respect. God and immortality, which through the law of freedom (morality). All philosophy culminates in these 3 points, namely, in striving from the sensible to the supersensible (metaphysics).",[]],"reflm:6359":["and 11 Dec. 1797). Loose leaf C 2. P. I, II. R I 128-131.\nP. I:\n1. A note which (for the treatise on the false subtlety of the 4 syllogistic figures) would state in the briefest compass the essential difference between the procedure of the understanding in its logical function and that in its transcendental function; 2. a note which (for the treatise on the use of teleological principles in philosophy — at the end) would briefly state the difference between the exposition and the deduction of the categories. At the same time it might be remarked how the proposition in the Critique of Pure Reason, p. 177, is to be understood: that through the transcendental determination of time (because it is homogeneous with the appearances and with the categories) the application of the categories to appearances is mediated and made possible. The difficulty seems to lie in this, that the transcendental determination of time is itself already a product of apperception in relation to the form of intuition, and so itself raises the question how the application of the category to the form of intuition is possible, since categories and form of intuition are heterogeneous. Altogether the schematism is one of the most difficult points. — Even Herr Beck cannot find his way into it. — I hold this chapter to be one of the most important. |\nNB. The intuition of time is not homogeneous with the categories, but the determination of time, but the unity of the representations in the synthesis (composition) of the given intuition — breaks off?\n(g The desire to rule is as a rule found in the faculty of law.)\nNB. 1.) That the categories in themselves and by themselves alone have no object (g or sense) (because they are mere forms of thought), and that the possibility, e.g., of a composite (in that many together make One) cannot be explained.\nNB. 2) When philosophical principles of knowledge a priori come as far as metaphysics, judgment can no longer be fragmentary, |\nbut must be systematic; a critical-dogmatic philosophy is an absurdity.\nP. II:\nThe categories [are] functions (subjective possibility) of the composition of the manifold, so far as the many in the representation of something (*entis*) makes One: a) of magnitude in (g pure) intuition; b) of sensation, quality of the empirical, or of perception [c.)] of the homogeneous; c) of the real ground and consequence of sensations, that is, of causality; d) of experience, empirical knowledge, consciousness of the existence of an object of given perception.\nThe composite [can] as such cannot be given (g be) in intuition, but can be known as composite only through the composition of the manifold, hence through the concept of synthesis.\nThe merely subjective in intuition [is], as representation of an object, appearance. The form of an appearance, so far as it is representation a priori and not empirical, is entitled pure intuition. Thus [light and] representations of coloured objects are not pure intuitions.\nThe (g re-)finding of what has been thought is the (g selective) memory. The holding together of it, in a backward look at what has been thought and what is still prospected, is the capacity of comprehension [*Fassungskraft*]. The power of judgment (namely the negative) lasts longest in growing old. If this fails, old age makes one childish.\nN.B. That time is expressed by a line (which is yet a space) and space by a time (an hour's walk) is a schematism of the concepts of the understanding. *Compositio*.",[]],"reflm:6360":["—\nP. I:\n1.) The doctrine of the objects of the senses, both outer and inner, as appearances. 2. The doctrine of the supersensible [als] (the unconditioned) as a regulative principle, not a constitutive one, and so as a practical matter in itself; that is, one must proceed not from the concepts of God and immortality to morality, but from morality to those concepts.\nA quantum of which every magnitude that can be specified can always be thought only as a part of another homogeneous magnitude (g can [be thought]) is infinite. — That an object can exist only as a part proves that it is no true thing but only a thing of thought [*Gedankending*]. We do not intuit it as composite; we are conscious only [der Z uns] of the act of our composition in accordance with a certain form of intuition. What is the infinitely small? — Through categories alone, because they contain merely thinking, we know no object (matter). [sie] One cannot even see the possibility of an object in accordance with them: for instance, how many things of the same kind together make up one; or how something positive can yet, in respect of the same predicate, also be determined negatively; or how, when something actual is given, another actual thing must necessarily follow upon it; or finally how the existence of a thing can be absolutely necessary. Only intuition, space and time, attached to the real (the sensible), gives us modes of knowledge which in the end (even pure mathematics) [ih] can prove the reality of their concepts merely through their agreement with the possibility of experience. In this the intuitions can yield modes of knowledge a priori only if they are taken, not as things in themselves, but always only as appearances, that is, as the subjective form of our sensibility, because one can know this form by itself alone, whatever the objects may be, as regards its constitution, and consequently those intuitions are given a priori. |\n––––––––––––––––––\nThat a human being can also, on one side, be represented to himself as appearance is no harder to see than that this holds of outer objects, if one takes as ground the possibility of representing oneself as object, which admits of no further proof. For he is object to himself partly of his sense, partly of his faculty of thought. In the former case he is conscious of how he affects himself empirically, and places these impressions in the form of time. In the second case he is conscious of how he affects the subject, and to that extent is in the act of spontaneity.\nOne cannot become immediately conscious of the intuition of a composite as such, but only of the composition (*synthesis*), that is, of the self-activity of composing. Hence the categories.\nOf pressure, pull, and both together at the beginning of reciprocal action: impact.\nP. III:\nPhilosophy (as doctrine of wisdom) is the doctrine of the vocation [*Bestimmung*] of the human being, with regard to [sein] the final end that proceeds from his own reason.\nA. To this belongs, as science of the object (g theoretical) of this vocation in general, the task of finding through reason, for the conditioned in general, the supreme condition (the totality of conditions); and, since this, when the representation of the objects is sensible, always stands in contradiction with itself (antinomy), while yet an unconditioned must nevertheless be assumed, the task of arriving at the conclusion that knowledge of things through the senses never gives anything more than mere appearance, never things in themselves.\nB. That practical knowledge from reason can never command unconditionally (and consequently only through the concept of duty), which grounds the concept of freedom, a concept that can be known only through this imperative. — On this are then grounded the doctrine of right (together with the doctrine of the right of states and of nations to be found in it) and the doctrine of virtue, both as pure knowledge of reason from concepts.\nC. That from this non-sensible a step to the supersensible still remains open, namely to the concepts of God and immortality. Taught dogmatically, these contain only transcendent attempts; critically, however, [mit] after a preceding critique of one's own faculty of reason, they do not give a knowledge of those objects (as being unattainable by us), yet their idea furnishes a regulative principle (not a constitutive one), that we should act as if those ideas at the same time had objective theoretical reality.\nNow there is still the doctrine of taste, as belonging to the power of judgment according to principles. — —",[]],"reflm:6361":["P. I:\nHow are synthetic modes of knowledge (judgments) a priori possible, according to the three categories of modality, of possibility, actuality and necessity of judgments about the object (the problematic, assertoric [*categorischen*] and apodictic)? The faculty of knowledge, the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and the faculty of desire. Understanding, power of judgment and reason. Logic, aesthetic and morals. — Knowledge a priori indicates necessity. Necessity *simpliciter* [absolute] or *secundum quid* [relative]. Skill, prudence, wisdom. Philosophical or historical history of philosophy. — Ascending from the sensible of intuition, through concepts of reflection (of purpose), to the supersensible.",[]],"reflm:6362":["P. I:\nXenophanes. Empedocles, Parmenides, Pythagoras. Thales: whether they were the first is uncertain.\nNB. The composite as such I cannot (g im|mediately) intuit, but can only become conscious of it through the composition in [space and] time [folglich] and so obtain a concept of the object intuited, so that inner sense to this extent also contains the consciousness of outer intuition.",[]],"reflm:6363":["P. II:\nOn the metaphysical (g first principles) of natural science. The (g empirical) progress in space goes on to infinity, without my being thereby entitled to assume an infinite space: [denn] because I can experience no boundary; for that would have to happen through perception of empty space, which is impossible. But where there is no boundary [ich] for me, there my progress goes on to infinity.",[]],"reflm:6364":["P. I:\nAllgemeine Geographische Ephemeriden, March 1799, p. 276, where the difference between theoretical and practical astronomy is discussed and it is said: »What would astronomy be without observation? A Kantian astronomy a priori, perhaps? Theory and practice must go hand in hand« (but which of them goes first in mathematics?) — In the review of the theoretical astronomy of Friedrich Theodor Schubert, member of the Imperial Russian Academy of Sciences at St. Petersburg.",[]],"reflm:6365":["11. P. II R.-Sch. XI 2 p. 164.\nOn the method of teaching which treats the mode of thought of human beings like an *ars veterinaria* (g cattle-medicine), for instance by frightening them with purgatory and hell, and in general works on the senses, not on reason. — One can also treat mathematics philosophically, |\nbut not the reverse: namely, in order to determine the limits of its possible use and its purpose.",[]],"reflm:6366":["P. III:\nThere is a difference between philosophical knowledge and philosophy: being able to philosophize, and being a philosopher.",[]],"reflm:6367":["1800). L Bl. K 7.\nP. II:\nConsciousness of oneself as a being residing in the body, touching the matter on which it acts, and perceiving itself therein, is self-contradictory; for it would then be an atomistic principle that so acted, which, however, is entirely cancelled by the dynamical principle.",[]],"reflm:6368":["1800-1801). L Bl. Kemke 2. P. II.\nP. IIa:\nWhat are the *reagentia* [reagents], as tests by experiment, in morals, and what are the mixtures in chemistry that cannot be resolved any further?\nMathematics is the most reliable of all sciences, because it itself makes everything that it offers for knowing, and exhibits pure intuitions in space and time.\nP. IIb:\nTo make the subjective in our mode of representation objective.\nP. IIc:\nTheologians and jurists are the two scholars of reason upon whom the highest principles, that is, those of the use of reason in respect of morality, rest.",[]],"reflm:6369":["Remarks of Kant in his own copy, interleaved with octavo leaves, of Volume II of the Vermischte Schriften of G. Chr. Lichtenberg, edited by L. Chr. Lichtenberg and Fr. Kries (1801; preface dated August 1800; announced as finished in the catalogue of the Michaelmas Fair of 1800).\nOn p. 13 »It always seems to me as if the concept of being were something borrowed from our thinking, and if there are no more sentient and thinking creatures, then there is nothing any more«, Kant remarks on p. 13':\npure idealism.\nPp. 28/9. »In the instincts of insects there is much that must make us believe that they are guided more by the future than by the past.« On »insects«, p. 28':\nThey make their bed for the winter sleep, although beforehand they did not know that they would rise again in the future.\nPp. 32/3. Beside the beginning of the sentence: »I believe from the ground of my soul and after the most mature reflection that the doctrine of Christ, cleansed of priestly scribbling and properly understood according to our manner of expressing ourselves, is the most perfect system that I, at least, can conceive for promoting peace and happiness in the world most quickly, most powerfully, most surely and most universally«, there stand, written by Kant in red pencil, the words:\nI too.\nP. 33: »Christ at the same time accommodated himself to the material, and this wrings admiration even from the atheist. (In what sense I take the word atheist here, every thinker will feel.)« On the underlined word »atheist«, p. 33':\nGodlessness and denial of God.\nP. 74: »To provide for the future must, for creatures that do not know the future, suffer curious limitations.« Opposite, on p. 74':\nTo provide for the future (to be anxious) is commonly that which does not let the intended happiness arise.\nP. 76 at the bottom:\n*verte* (turn over)\nP. 92: »it is impossible that one being can receive the effects of another without the principal effect appearing mixed«. After »can receive« Kant makes the addition in the margin:\nwithout for its part taking them up.\nP. 137: »To make man as religion would have him is like the undertaking of the Stoics; it is only another degree of the impossible.« »religion would have« underlined in red pencil; besides this a stroke in red pencil in the margin beside the whole sentence, and beside the beginning (up to »undertaking«) a large question mark (also red pencil).\nP. 234: »The saddest thing that the French Revolution has brought about for us is indisputably this, that every reasonable demand, one that is to be made by God's and by rightful law, will be regarded as a seed of rebellion.« Beside the last words, in red pencil:\nHas not happened!\nP. 269: »I have never been able to find a kind that had so much in common with the tinker as the people who, under the appearance of practising a useful trade, go about to deceive and rob people.« Beside it, in red pencil:\nLavater.\nP. 415: »Something very good could certainly be written about superstition, namely in its defence. Everyone is superstitious. I with my lights: I do not believe in these things, but it is nevertheless pleasant to me if they do not turn out adversely.« Beside the words »namely — lights« a stroke and question mark in the margin (red pencil).\nOn the last blank leaf there stand, written in red pencil, the following words, of which the last is quite uncertain and of all of which it is doubtful whether they come from Kant:\nPatria Fr. II Patri.",[]],"reflm:6370":["Above and \nHypothetical possibility is problematic. If certain conditions are present, e.g. the equality of the angles of a triangle. Possibility *in hypothesi* (in the hypothesis) is categorical. The conditions are [given].",[]],"reflm:6371":["*Conditio est, qvo non posito non ponitur aliud* (a condition is that, which not being posited, nothing else is posited). It is therefore not the ground. The *hypothesis* can be negative.",[]],"reflm:6372":["Something is possible *in hypothesi*: this means more than internally possible.\n—\n—\nonly in it\n—\nWhat is possible only under a *hypothesis* is not absolute.",[]],"reflm:6373":["Something can be possible *in thesi* (in itself) and yet not be so *in hypothesi*. Hypothetical possibility is at all times restrictive, *sub conditione suspensiva* (under a suspensive condition).",[]],"reflm:6374":["I cannot say: it is absolutely possible (i.e. without any restric|tive condition) that the square of one side of the triangle be equal to the squares of the remaining sides; but in itself it is nevertheless possible.",[]],"reflm:6375":["The absolute is opposed not to the hypothetical, but to the restrictive and the conditioned.",[]],"reflm:6376":["One can say: something is possible not merely in itself (internally) but also hypothetically. But also: something is only hypothetically, not absolutely, possible. What is possible in every hypothesis is actual; for it is absolutely possible.",[]],"reflm:6377":["According to the *principium rationis* (principle of reason), one and the same state is always accompanied by one and the same consequence; since we can determine the state only [through] the consequence, the state determines the consequence. We cannot see how something must follow upon something else; but that one and the same *antecedens* must have one and the same *consequens* follows from this, that the state itself is determined by the consequence, and that otherwise there would be no *continuum* of the *phaenomena* (appearances), without connection of the members, and it is only thereby that they are represented in a series.",[]],"reflm:6378":["The consequence is posited in time after the ground, although this |\ntime is evanescent. But if there is a series of consequences, and one that is infinite, the succession becomes noticeable. The immediate ground is the starting point of a series of consequences.",[]],"reflm:6379":["In and \nThat everything contingent, or whatever arises, has its ground follows from this, that without a *prius* (antecedent) there would be no *continuitas* (continuity) of the *phaenomena*, and without rule no identity of them.",[]],"reflm:6380":["In, under and \nThe ground determines the consequence, but not conversely.",[]],"reflm:6381":["14. E II 1662.\nExistence contains at the same time the concept of a thoroughgoing determination in itself; but through that (g alone) the concept of the thing is not determined at all. Now we have a concept which at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination of a thing: the concept of the *realissimum* (the most real being); and these determinations (so it seems to us) we can also specify *in concreto* [gege], e.g. wisdom, power, etc., etc. Only the existence of the thing (because this is no determination) is not thereby given as following necessarily from this concept. But even if the necessary existence of some thing is assumed, I cannot say that the thoroughgoing determination with regard to its quality is given through the concept of necessity; it is given only through that of existence, in which the predicates remain undetermined. There could be several necessary beings differing from one another; but then, to be sure, none of them [Existenz] would be determinable from the concept of necessity (as to what it is like). One could only say: |\nM 14: |\nwe have no concept for its thoroughgoing determination except that of the *realissimum*, but not: this concept is con|tained in that of its necessity.\nIt is only a subjective [Nothwend] logical necessity of the condition, to form for ourselves a concept of its determinations; but that, if from the concept of it (of a *non realissimum*) this idea is determinable *duplici modo* (in a twofold manner), this means only: we can think many kinds of beings as necessary, not: the same being could also be otherwise determined, for here we have only concepts.",[]],"reflm:6382":["Through existence no predicate is added to the thing; rather the thing, with all its predicates, is posited outside the concept (in intuition) [added].",[]],"reflm:6383":["If I posit something only with respect to my concept, it is in many ways undetermined; but if it is posited absolutely, it is thoroughly determined according to the *principium exclusi medii* (principle of the excluded middle); but I cannot infer conversely.",[]],"reflm:6384":["In and \nI can indeed say: everything actual is thoroughly determined, but not: everything thoroughly determined is actual. — It is only a thought.",[]],"reflm:6385":["The objective reality of the concept of a *quantum*, namely that many make up one, is not clear of itself, but only in space and time, since many times cannot exist, as it were, separated from one another (nor many moments), but only so that they make up one time. Likewise many spaces. — Thereby the concept of |\n*quantum* becomes a principle of possible experience, namely of perceptions so connected that many together represent one (homogeneous) object, which represents not one as contained in many, but many as contained in one. *Praedicabilia quantitatis* (predicables of quantity).",[]],"reflm:6386":["The transcendental propositions a priori are all analytic. They are drawn from the concept of a thing in general, from the essential constituent, and from the thoroughgoing determination belonging to its existence: the unity of the concept; the truth in the combination of the manifold that belongs to it, i.e. that everything belonging to it is grounded, either sufficiently for existence or at least for its possibility. And since every common ground of the manifold [combined] composite is properly a ground [is according to] for me to compose it, this composition stands, in respect of its kind, under a rule, according to which the manifold is coordinated (essentialia) or subordinated ([attribu] affectiones). — Perfection is the [Zus] conformity of this manifold to its concept — *totalitas*. *Vnum, plura conjuncta, totum.*",[]],"reflm:6387":["Transcendental perfection consists in this, that all the constituents belonging to the essence together make up the whole essence.",[]],"reflm:6388":["That all combination, as composition, must not be perceived as (g outside me), but must be thought as brought about by me.",[]],"reflm:6389":["26. 25'. E II 1670II. 1669.\nM 26':\nAssume a necessary being. You then represent to yourselves a being whose non-existence is impossible. But of such a being you have no concept at all; for impossibility you can recognise only through a contradiction, whereas the non-being of a thing never contradicts itself, since that a thing is, is not an analytic proposition following from its concept, but a synthetic one. — Now you may well say that, since everything existing must be thoroughly determined, if you are to give any concept of the necessary being [from which its existence] that determines it thoroughly a priori, it can only be the concept of the *realissimum* (the most real being). — But I cannot say that if I did not think the [*realissimum*] *necessarium* (the necessary) under the concept of the *realissimum*, (g substances we never represent as necessary in respect of their existence, but only the *accidentia*), a contradiction with the concept of the *necessarium* would then arise. For if I presuppose a *realissimum*, the non-existence of this thing |\nM 26: |\ncontains no contradiction. (Such a relation of concepts is possible only if the concept of the *realissimum* contains merely a partial concept of the *necessarium*; for then |\nI can well infer from the *necessarium* to the *realissimum*, but not from the latter to that necessity. But the concept of the *realissimum*, according to the presupposition, itself constitutes the whole concept of the *necessarium*, since it is assumed [for that reason necessarily] precisely in order to represent the *necessarium* in its thoroughgoing determination.) For necessity can no more be inferred from a concept of a being determinable in two ways (g its contingency) than from one determinable in only one way. The reason is that the existential proposition is always synthetic, and from concepts, whether from one that contains thoroughgoing determination or from one that leaves it undetermined in many respects, it cannot be concluded whether the thing can be or not be. Consequently its objective reality can be neither affirmed nor denied.\nIt is not that the necessary being is determinable in only one way, for it could be so even if a non-A were among its determinations alongside A. Rather, if the existence of a thing is to be known from its concept, this concept must already contain the thoroughgoing determination in itself. E.g. [If] from the concept of the *realissimum* this alone could come about, if in general the existence of a thing could be known from its concept; for this is the only concept which [alle da] represents an *ens singulare* (an individual being).\nBut from the concept of no thing can absolutely necessary [being] existence be derived. Therefore [it is also] if it is thought by us as a necessary being, which is the modality of its position, it is wholly undetermined, in respect of this concept, which predicates it contains.\nThere is no ground why that which is *partim negativum* (in part negative) should not exist just as necessarily.\nM 25': |\nThe concept of a necessary being is (g 1.) the concept of a thing which is undetermined in respect of none of all possible predicates A or non-A, i.e. of an *individuum*. But through this con|cept\nit is 2.) undetermined; consequently it is logically contingent whether I attribute a or non-A to it. 3. From the necessity of its existence it does not follow that it is thoroughly determined through its concept, i.e. that it is an *ens realissimum*.\nThe proof of the (g necessary) existence of a *realissimum* is this: if it did not [alle Vollkommenheit ha → have all perfection] exist, it would not have all perfection, for existence (as real perfection) would be lacking to it. — The proof of the highest reality as predicate of the necessary being is this: if it did not have all perfection, then it would be (g in itself) (through its concept) undetermined whether it has a certain perfection or not. Consequently, however it may exist, it would be possible that it nevertheless did not exist as such, and hence [a contingent being] its existence (the thoroughgoing determination) would be contingent.\nIt is held here that, since the first proposition would have to be synthetic and a priori, the second is merely analytic. For there I proceed from possibility (but is even this given?) to actuality, hence beyond the concept; here, on the other hand, I proceed from actuality, [which however its] which is at the same time determined as necessary, to the [sole] concept which alone contains thoroughgoing determination (which must always be thought in existence) and seems to be identical with the previous concept. Hence by an analytic proposition I go not beyond the concept, but to what is contained in it. — But the proposition is nevertheless synthetic. For I go beyond every concept which I may have of a necessary being as such, if I make the concept which for me is the only one containing thoroughgoing determination into the condition of the possibility of the thoroughgoing determination of the object itself (in intuition), or infer to this. For in intuition the object can, without prejudice to its necessity, [contain] contain many negative predicates in its thoroughgoing determination; only I cannot derive them from a single concept or comprehend them together in it.",[]],"reflm:6390":["»A necessary being can exist only [with] as one that is already thoroughly determined through its concept. For if it were not determined through this concept, it could (g *salvo hoc conceptu*, this concept remaining intact) be A, but also non-A, i.e. its opposite would be (g objectively, i.e. in itself) possible, i.e. it would be contingent.« — That is the *argumentum cosmologicum* (cosmological argument) for proving the existence of an *ens realissimum*. For this concept is the only one that at the same time contains in itself the thoroughgoing determination of a thing as a thing in general. But through the concept of a necessary being no thing is determined as to what it is [denn wenn → for if], i.e. it remains wholly undetermined whether, in respect of any synthetic predicate, it is A or non-A, without thereby being contingent. For to the concept of a body it is contingent to be [warm or] heavy or [to be cold] not; but it does not follow that what that concept leaves undetermined does not belong necessarily to the body itself.",[]],"reflm:6391":["We cannot think any existing thing as contingent in every respect. (For then it would be something as an object of experience, but for reason nothing.)\nBut in relation to our concept of a thing all things are contingent.\nWe must think at least one thing as necessary, but we can form no concept of this thing as such; i.e. in respect of our concept everything is contingent.",[]],"reflm:6392":["Everything determined is necessary, the undetermined contingent.",[]],"reflm:6393":["Above, beside and\nAbsolute *Necessarium Ens* (absolutely necessary being). That something is possible we cannot know through the mere non-contradiction of the thought, but that something is impossible we can know from the contradiction.\nThat a thing is necessary we can see only through the contradiction of its opposite (either where the concept contradicts itself, or where the existence contradicts other given laws). Where, then, nothing of the kind can be demanded, e.g. that the non-being of a thing contradicts itself, there we cannot see the impossibility of non-being, and hence cannot see the necessity. If we knew the possibility of all things intuitively, we would also know the impossibility of something, even without contradiction, through our not finding the thing at all in the field of possibility. Absolute necessity would be nothing but the intuition of a thing a priori.\nWe are compelled to assume all things as necessary (as absolute or hypothetical). The ground is that we must represent everything true at the same time logically as necessary (*principium exclusi medii*, the principle of the excluded middle), since we determine things only through judgments, and judgments a posteriori, unless confirmed by judgments a priori, are not objective.",[]],"reflm:6394":["From the concept of an object I can never know the necessity or the contingency of its existence.",[]],"reflm:6395":["If there is no ground for a thing, it is contingent for me.",[]],"reflm:6396":["We can think of an occurrence [Begebenheit] that is not an alteration of a thing, e.g. the arising or perishing of substance. Of such an occurrence I do not say that it has a cause, but that such an arising is not an occurrence at all, i.e. cannot take place.",[]],"reflm:6397":["(g *Qvantum*) *Absolute minimum non foret quantum; ergo est im|possibile.* [An absolute minimum would not be a quantum; therefore it is im|possible.]\n*Absolute maximum non foret dabile qvoad quantitatem; ergo vt obiectum intuitus est impossibile.* [An absolute maximum would not be givable as regards quantity; therefore, as an object of intuition, it is impossible.]",[]],"reflm:6398":["All magnitude in appearances is merely comparative; in things in themselves it is absolute. The whole solar system could be contained in a drop of water, and so in neither of the two is there a determinate number of parts. But in a *mundus noumenon* [noumenal world] there must be a determinate multitude of parts. One drop of water cannot be distinguished from another in itself by anything, but only by place. In the *substantia noumenon* [noumenal substance], however, the difference is real. So also with the successive Platonic revolutions of the year. In the soul a minute can contain what a century contains in another. But in itself there must nevertheless be more reality in the one than in the other. This proves *animam vt phaenomenon* [the soul as appearance].",[]],"reflm:6399":["The magnitude of a thing as aggregate is extensive; as ground it is intensive.",[]],"reflm:6400":["Magnitude of space and degree of extension.",[]],"reflm:6401":["Contingency is either (g merely) internal or (g also) external. The latter is either in a particular respect or in every respect [respectu] possibility of the opposite. We represent everything that is actual also as necessary.",[]],"reflm:6402":["Possibility not merely *in thesi* but also *in hypothesi* is greater; but that *sub hypothesi* is smaller.",[]],"reflm:6403":["In every alteration substance persists, because alteration is the succession of determinations of one and the same thing. This is a merely logical proposition, according to the rule of identity. It does not say, however, that substance in general does not arise or perish, but only that it remains during the alteration.\nThat proposition (g moreover) holds only of (g corporeal) substances in space. For there arising or perishing is always an alteration of what is not substantial, namely of space, which remains but is not an object of experience at all; consequently that alteration also would be no object of experience, and so would be no alteration. The persistence of matter through all change of relations [is] is proved by this, that matter itself is a mere sum-total of relations, which cannot drop out without all other relations also dropping out, and hence all possible outer experience.",[]],"reflm:6404":["Because the reality which is met with in all things in part is thought together in God, there arises from this Spinozism: that all things inhere in God, because their essence is possible only as part of his essence. *Non indiget existentia alterius.* [It does not need the existence of another.]\nInherence, dependence, and real whole. In so many ways do realities belong together as regards existence.",[]],"reflm:6405":["Spinoza's proposition, that that whose idea requires no other is substance, is the same as the proposition that the *realissimum* alone is substance, and hence alone is the necessary being, since everything else inheres in it.",[]],"reflm:6406":["Above and \nEvery alteration has its cause; substances neither arise nor perish.",[]],"reflm:6407":["*Principia* [principles]:\n1. Of inherence. No substance perishes.\n2. Of causality, of alteration.\n3. Of commerce.",[]],"reflm:6408":["Contingent is that which is possible only conditionally (hypothetically), whose non-being, therefore, is possible in itself.\nOne cannot begin from this, that the opposite is possible, i.e. that it does not exist. For since we know impossibility only through a contradiction, but the non-being of a thing never contradicts itself, everything would be contingent. Everything that happens is contingent, i.e. possible only conditionally: *principium caussalitatis* [principle of causality]. Hence substances, which persist, are not to be regarded as contingent, although their state is contingent; and so the alterations of substances do not prove their contingency.",[]],"reflm:6409":["What happens is possible only conditionally. For a time precedes it, which contains the condition of the existence of the occurrence.",[]],"reflm:6410":["Contingent is not that whose non-being does not contradict itself, but that which is ungrounded.\nNow everything that exists is grounded; consequently nothing existing is contingent. Being grounded here refers to experience, not to objective principles.",[]],"reflm:6411":["Necessity and contingency are mere concepts of reason, and so they can of course be known in no object.",[]],"reflm:6412":["Of the contingency that is attributed to a connection on account of its purposiveness without a purpose.",[]],"reflm:6413":["94. 95'. E II 1089.\nEvery synthetic predicate, i.e. determination, has its ground, namely something else through which it is connected a priori with the concept of a thing. For otherwise the determination would not be objective. [Therefore] But the reality of an *ens realissimum* [most real being] is no determination. The existence of a thing, however, is a synthetic predicate of our representation of things, and one cannot say that it has a ground [but] (*rationem existendi* [ground of existing]), but only a *rationem cognoscendi* [ground of knowing], and this not merely *a posteriori* but also *a priori*.\nM 94:\nThe concept of cause holds only of objects of experience; for that something exists can be known only by means of experience, and of that which is not an object of experience it can only be thought [according to the] so far as it is represented according to the analogy of experience. That something is contingent in such objects, however, cannot be known from concepts and from the possibility of the opposite, i.e. from the agreement of the opposite with concepts; for contingency here concerns existence as the predicate of a synthetic judgment. Thus only the opposite of that which belongs to existence is possible which is determined according to [the] (g no) law of experience and according to no analogy thereof. Hence nothing is contingent except (g what) happens, |\nbut without cause, or relatively without external cause. Motion inheres in a body *accidentaliter* [accidentally] [or]. It arises *accidentaliter*. It is a *contingentia*, i.e. an occurrence [Eräugnis], but not according to concepts, i.e. absolute |\nM 94': |\npossibility of the opposite. For everything that is determinable through experience is necessary, because otherwise the connection of perceptions would not be objective. Logical contingency is nevertheless physical necessity.\nM 95':\n*Omne contingens, h.e. qvodcunque contingit, est rationatum alterius* [everything contingent, i.e. whatever happens, is the consequent of another]; every occurrence is [an] effect of a cause. This can be understood in two ways: either that by it is understood merely the determination of a thing, or the existence of the thing as substance. In the first meaning it is a possible concept of experience, and the proposition is a principle of nature; in the second it is a problematic concept which is wholly empty, since the existence of the subject itself is supposed to be an occurrence, and the existence itself is supposed to follow upon non-existence — empty —. This contingency of the thing can no more be understood than its necessity. *Contingentia absoluta* [absolute contingency] is just like *necessitas absoluta* [absolute necessity] unknowable.",[]],"reflm:6414":["Metaphysics proper is the application of transcendental philosophy to [that which] concepts given in reason (which are necessary to it), to which, however, [in the] no corresponding objects can be given in experience (consequently to the supersensible).\nThis can therefore only be the unconditioned, for that is the sole theoretical idea of reason. Metaphysics therefore goes 1. to that of which only |\nthe whole is to be represented as absolutely unconditioned; 2. to things so far as they are in themselves sensibly unconditioned.\nThe first part is therefore cosmology; the second, rational doctrine of the soul [and] as pneumatology, and theology.",[]],"reflm:6415":["Metaphysics proper: the application of transcendental philosophy to things that cannot become objects of experience, either to a whole of the objects of the senses or to supersensible things.",[]],"reflm:6416":["Cosmology. It is transcendent. For it goes to the whole of all conditions, which is unconditioned.",[]],"reflm:6417":["The world of sense is a mere *totum ideale* [ideal whole], because space is a mere form of intuition; hence the synthesis is never total. Time does not furnish a measure of the magnitude of the world, but [of] the whole of its states.",[]],"reflm:6418":["All antinomies arise from this, that one seeks the unconditioned in the world of sense.",[]],"reflm:6419":["That both opposites in the first two antinomies can be false comes from this, that they stand opposed not *contradictorie* [contradictorily] but also *contrarie* [contrarily] |\nto one another. The world has a beginning — It has not — It is infinite.",[]],"reflm:6420":["If space and time [as infinite] were something given in itself, they would have to be regarded as infinite magnitudes. Now they are nothing but forms for enlarging or diminishing to infinity.",[]],"reflm:6421":["In the mathematical antinomies both propositions are false, because the unconditioned is supposed to be a part of appearances, and yet as such a part can never be unconditioned. In the dynamical antinomies both can be true, because the unconditioned is laid at the basis of appearances but is not a part of them, and the one proposition holds of things in the appearance, the other of their relation to the intelligible ground.\nThe first two antinomies rest on the unconditioned totality of the conditions; the other two on the unconditioned ground of the existence of the conditioned. Hence the first two are false; the others can be true.",[]],"reflm:6422":["Freedom is a (g sensibly unconditioned) power [Vermögen] of a substance to determine itself to act.",[]],"reflm:6423":["*Non datur fatum* [there is no fate]. Everything in the world is composite, and therefore contingent.",[]],"reflm:6424":["Idea. A pure concept of reason must be a concept [durch] subjectively necessary for reason, and this is the concept of the unconditioned, and consequently of the absolute totality of the conditions (since reason is a faculty of deriving the particular from the universal). Now the world, as a *quantum*, is composite, and the possibility of a *quantum* which is itself still a part is always still conditioned. Hence only the concept |\nof the absolute totality of the whole of substances accords with the concept of reason of a world. But contradictions arise, whether one says that the world of sense, as an absolute whole of things, is infinite (given), or that it is given as finite. The reason is that it is contradictory that a world in space and time should be given [ein] as a whole in itself, since it is not a thing in itself. The statement 'The world of sense is infinite' means [only] not that it is actual as such. For since it exists merely in representation, and not in itself as we think it, it is given only so far as the progress extends, and hence never as a whole; that is, the progress goes on to infinity.",[]],"reflm:6425":["One can say that matter is divisible to infinity, but not that it consists of infinitely many parts. It is much as with Euclid: the proposition that two parallel lines [are], produced to infinity, unable to meet is not the same as the proposition that they meet at an infinite distance. For the latter would yield a triangle whose angles contained more than 2 right angles. It is just so with the proposition that (g world) space can be pursued to infinity, and the proposition that it is infinite (the latter we can say of pure space, but only as an idea). One can also give a proof that, if [the] (g a given) space consists of infinitely many parts, the space of the world is infinite.",[]],"reflm:6426":["The nature of the things of the world stands either under laws of the determination of their existence in space, or merely in time: corporeal and thinking nature.",[]],"reflm:6427":["Beside and \nBecause good conduct in life makes (g him) worthy of an enlargement of his natural predispositions, progress in conformity to his duty must also bring man in the future to a higher stage of his natural rank. But because this in turn draws greater duties after it, progress in the fulfilment of these will also continue, and so in eternity.",[]],"reflm:6428":["The concept of freedom is a concept of reason whose reality cannot be known theoretically-dogmatically. But it is also the only one that is not merely capable of a practically-dogmatic [nicht: not] proof, but is also necessarily bound up with it. — From it, however, God and immortality also follow. These too are therefore merely practically-dogmatic.\n(s To prove κατ' ανθρωπον a priori means: to prove sufficiently for the subject, but only in a practical respect.)",[]],"reflm:6429":["If space were the form of outer relations in themselves, there could be more than one *ens realissimum* [most real being].",[]],"reflm:6430":["*immensum*, [infinit] *incommensurabile*; what is analogy without similarity.\nThat there is no probability of the existence of God.\nThe divine nature is unattainable for our reason, transcendent. The knowledge of God as a moral being is immanent. The categories in the moral concept are concepts that remain over |\nwhen I leave out all theoretical relation of them — of a thing in general.\n*natura divina est imperscrutabilis* [the divine nature is inscrutable].",[]],"reflm:6431":["2) God not merely as first cause, but also as author.\nProof of a living God:\n1. as *primus motor* [first mover],\n2 — as architect,\n3 — as creator.",[]],"reflm:6432":["Of God and immortality.\nIf the moral law, in order to bind us, needed God and a future life, it would be absurd to ground upon such a need the belief in the actuality of that which can satisfy it.\nBut the moral law stands firm for itself, independently of every theoretical presupposition. Indeed, in place of such presuppositions it has as its ground something supersensible, which surpasses all our insight, namely freedom (which itself lies beyond nature, in another field). This law, as a principle of wisdom, leads our reason in its practical use to the final end of a highest wisdom: the greatest happiness combined with virtue, as the final end of all things. This end, however, so far as we can see, makes necessary an eternity [in] of our existence, and a moral author of the world to bring about the constitution of the world [geho] required for it.\nEven if one could [annehmen: assume] believe neither in immortality nor in the existence of God, the moral law and the end of a will conformable to it (the highest good) would nonetheless have just as mighty an influence on the will. We should not, in a practical respect, take such a scandal into our principles, but, as a (g permitted) hypothesis [unsere], should promote thereby contentment with our existence and with the world which we have in prospect.\nThe *argumentum a tuto* [argument from safety] is good for actions, but very bad (g itself) for the [Bestimmungsgr] (g inner) confession of faith.",[]],"reflm:6433":["340. E II 1676. 873.\nAll rational theology is either that of deism (g transcendental) or of theism (g psychologically determined). The first is either ontotheology or cosmotheology. — That of theism is either physico-theology or ethico-theology.\nThe cosmotheological proof seeks to infer from a given absolutely necessary existence (without being able to derive this itself from any [bestimmten] concept of a thing, but merely because it is unconditioned) the highest reality [Begrif] of this being. For, it is said, if we do not think it as *realissimum*, we have no concept through which it would at the same time be known in its thoroughgoing determination. Consequently [sie] we could think its concept through a or also through non-a; that is, the concept which we made of it would be only contingent, and hence the thing itself contingent.\nM 340:\nBut is the concept of a necessary or a contingent substance thinkable at all? At the least, changes of substances do not [sind] prove their contingency. These concepts merely seek to indicate the possibility of the knowledge of the existence of things entirely a priori, or the impossibility thereof, and so they hold not of objects but of the relation of our concepts of them to our faculty of knowledge. The former [that such knowledge is possible] is impossible. Instead of these concepts one should make use of those of unconditioned [erkennbaren: knowable] possible existence.",[]],"reflm:6434":["Irrational concepts (g *conceptus surdi* [deaf concepts]) are to be distinguished from ideas. They are, namely, such concepts of the understanding from which one nevertheless withdraws everything required for example and for application *in concreto*, so that they can have no meaning, although they are free of contradiction. |\nE.g. (g 1.) God is eternal, that is, his existence has a magnitude, but not one of time. Now we cannot think any magnitude of existence, that is, a duration, except in time. 2. His presence has a magnitude in respect of the totality of things outside one another (in space). But it is not to be taken as in space, for then God would be outside himself. 3. He is cause of substance. But the kind of causality [Art Ursache zu seyn] must differ from that which we know; for in the latter nothing can be the cause of a substance.",[]],"reflm:6435":["Concepts of irrational relations are those which can be exhausted by no approximation. They are transcendent, however, if in this relation an object has no standard of measure.",[]],"reflm:6436":["1. A (g most perfect) being that contains all reality (whether as ground or as aggregate) is (g necessarily) actual. For were it not actual, a perfection (metaphysical), namely existence, would be lacking to it.\n2. A being that exists necessarily must contain all reality (that is, if its existence can be inferred from its concept, for only by this can necessity be recognised). For if it did not contain all reality, it would not be thoroughly determined through its concept, and this thoroughgoing determination must be [noth] ascribed to every existing thing. But though one can indeed think a necessary being, one cannot know it in anything at all, since every thing can be removed in thought, even all reality, unless one were to take existence [al] as a particular reality, which contradicts the first argument.",[]],"reflm:6437":["That no [theoretical] knowledge (g in speculative intent) of God is possible for man, because from our concepts of things we must abstract everything that makes things knowable to us through them, so that nothing remains by which we could give them reality. The categories\n|\nare concepts of things whose objective reality is problematic. Through them we can think a being, but cannot know it.",[]],"reflm:6438":["Belief in the existence of God is not probability. Knowledge of God in a practical respect is merely a representation of him according to analogy with what holds in the theoretical, whereby he is thought only by analogy with an object of the senses. Anthropomorphism.\nUnderstanding, but not human in its thinking; feeling of pleasure and displeasure, but not akin to the human; faculty of desire or will, but not human. Yet according to analogy.",[]],"reflm:6439":["In and \nThe psychological concept of God as *summa intelligentia* (highest intelligence) is that of a living God with understanding and will. The proof of such a being is teleology, not the concept of reality. But ontotheology as well as physicotheology gives no knowledge of God without anthropomorphisms.",[]],"reflm:6440":["From the existence of the world we can indeed infer, according to laws of causality, supreme causes, but not their absolute necessity, unity and infinity.\nInference, according to the analogy of nature, from a contingent effect to a free cause.",[]],"reflm:6441":["NB. Inference according to the analogy of the cause of alteration, the first of which is always a free action.",[]],"reflm:6442":["We have reason to represent God to ourselves by analogy with an understanding |\n, that is, as a watch, a ship, etc. etc. is related to its cause (an intelligent being), so the world is related to the unknown, which we call an infinite understanding, although it has nothing in itself similar to this. But from the causality of things through understanding we cannot infer a cause of the world (g of the same kind, that is,) which has understanding, any more than we can infer from the artefacts of bees that they have understanding. For, just as in the latter case the causality is of a far lower species, so in the former it may be of a far higher kind than that which we indicate by understanding.\nNor is it in any way probable that such a cause exists. For everything probable must lie on the way to certainty, in so far as certainty is reached merely by supplying what is lacking in the progress. Here, however, the data are of such a kind that they do not lead to certainty at all.",[]],"reflm:6443":["That our idea of a highest being as an intelligent being has objective reality can be inferred only from the (g objective) reality of the concept of freedom. This concept makes it necessary for us to direct our actions towards [the] a highest [G] good in the world, possible through our cooperation. For in so doing we assume its possibility, or at least must proceed as if we assumed it (since we must not only act in agreement with it, as is required for a best world, but must also make it our incentive). From this it follows that, since our power alone does not suffice for this, we must assume a highest original good; and consequently the concept of the latter has objective practical reality, although in the theoretical relation it is transcendent, and hence only an object of belief.\nThe mere categories (of substance, cause, community) give merely ways of forming for ourselves concepts of things in general, that is, of the synthetic unity, in one consciousness, of a manifold that may be given to us. This unity must be in us a priori, because a *compositum* as such cannot be perceived by us but must always be made, and the act of making it, that is, of giving to the |\nmanifold unity of consciousness in one representation, must be inherent in us a priori. These categories, however, have of themselves no objective reality, because they do not contain the way in which a manifold may be given to us. Since this cannot be given to them before the theoretical faculty of knowledge, that is, as belonging (g to) a knowledge of the nature of objects, there remains only their practical reality in respect of the concept of freedom (which presupposes merely the form of unity, not intuition itself), as immanent for our reason, in order to think the highest being by analogy with those beings which we know in nature in respect of their spontaneity.",[]],"reflm:6444":["Physicotheology is the inference from experience of this world to an intelligent supreme cause:\n1. From the motion of matter, as something lifeless, to a first mover, as a living being.\n2. From the art in this world to an [first] artificer who has understanding.\nMoral theology:\n1. From moral wisdom, as a [rule] legislation in rational beings for this world, although nothing conformable to this wisdom can be shown in experience, to infer an intelligible world in which this is found.\n2. From the idea of such [to a] as the highest good, possible only through a moral author, to the existence of such an author.",[]],"reflm:6445":["We can indeed unite divine freedom with reason, but not the coming about of the effect from this freedom.\nThe transition from the intellectual, as cause, to what is merely appearance cannot be comprehended. So even if we wished to abolish the freedom of man, we could still not resolve the difficulty of how the determining causes of the will could first have begun.",[]],"reflm:6446":["Human actions cannot be regarded merely as *phaenomena* [according to] and hence as determined according to empirical laws; they are at the same time *noumena*, and have a relation to the causality of the (g pure) understanding and of pure [understanding] reason. What springs from this as a cause is good. The possibility of acting from reason, that is, from the motive of what is good in itself, is freedom. This possibility is (g to be met with) in all actions, because the incentives of sensibility never determine, not even in the case of evil actions; and no action is empirically certain according to laws of the *phaenomena*. The higher power of choice (*Willkühr*) is therefore at all times free (*a stimulis*, from stimuli), and between its motives and the empirically determined power of choice there is a connection, which can be represented only by analogy with the connection of appearances, and which does not constitute a series. Hence it remains incomprehensible how the very same reason which produces certain actions could have produced others in their stead. In regard to these actions as *phaenomena*, freedom is, for the human understanding, which does not comprehend the transition from the intellectual to the sensible nor the first ground of coming to be, a necessary hypothesis. Among the *noumena*, however, the *effectus* (effects) of freedom are to be judged not according to what happens in time but in themselves; in the soul they are to be judged otherwise, and what was evil there may be good here on the whole.",[]],"reflm:6447":["Theodicy, or the justification of God in respect of ills, is easy, because these can be a partial good. But in respect of evil (the moral), because it absolutely ought not to be (and cannot even belong to the negations |\nas necessary limits of creatures), neither accusation nor justification can take place. For all evil in the world occurs in it only as *phaenomenon*, and as regards the actual world as *noumenon*, we cannot see at all the possibility of it, whether in conformity with the moral laws or contrary to them.",[]],"reflm:6448":["The transcendental ideas of reason relate to the totality of the series of conditions *a parte priori*, and consequently to the absolutely unconditioned in the synthesis. They are: the absolutely entire elapsed time (the beginning of the world); the absolutely entire division (the simple); the absolutely entire series of determining causes (freedom); and the postulate of what is presupposed (g or absolute presupposition) (necessity).\nAccording to the schema of sensibility this is impossible (because the synthesis of it cannot be entire, nor even empirically bounded); but intellectually it is possible. These transcendental ideas cannot be used for the explanation of appearances, but have their significance only in the pure system of reason.",[]],"reflm:6449":["M 381':\n*Ens necessarium* (necessary being).\nWhoever does not know how to derive the existence of a thing (g as necessary) from its concept also does not know what determinations a thing must have in order [from it] to be absolutely necessary, if he assumes such an existence.* For, as regards men, this would have to be done from concepts. Now if existence is not to be derived from concepts, nothing further can be known of it than that its concept is thoroughly determined, without knowing in what these determinations consist. He could then only say that it is represented as also |\nposited outside the understanding, for (g only) on that account is a thing entitled thoroughly determined. So he has said nothing — breaks off.\n*(s The cosmological proof demands that the thoroughgoing determination of a *ens necessarium* must lie in its concept, which, however, is true only of the concept of the highest reality. Consequently the properties of the *ens necessarium* can be derived from the mere necessity of existence. Yet it admits that, conversely, the existence of a thing cannot be derived [from] any concept. It must therefore |\nM 381:\n[say] not say: the *ens necessarium* has, by virtue of its concept, the highest reality; but: we cannot determine it from concepts otherwise than through pure reality (for as (g necessarily) existing it must be represented a priori as thoroughly determined). If, however, the inference from the object itself is to hold, the concepts of necessity and of the *realissimum* must also be *conceptus reciproci* (reciprocal concepts). For here we have to do only with concepts, not with intuitions, in which more can be contained than in the concept of them.)",[]],"reflm:6450":["On creation in time or from eternity.\nAn existence upon which the whole duration of a given thing follows (the beginning of it) is quite thinkable, and such is the birth of an animal. But an existence upon which all time whatever follows (g as definition of the absolutely first beginning) is a contradiction; for time can follow only in so far as something precedes (which, therefore, was not in it). [Hence the beginning belongs to the Ver] But the preceding itself presupposes a time. Therefore not all time can follow upon a given existence; for that upon which a certain whole time (which contains the beginning of the thing) follows is a preceding time.\nThe definition of the absolutely first beginning is the existence before which a time preceded in which as yet no thing at all was.\nIf we assume that a beginning can be thought before which no time at all precedes, then an end too will (g be able to) be thought upon which no time at all follows. But one would then have to say that a world has been and is now no more; and since this nevertheless assumes a time which follows upon the duration of the world, it would be a contradiction.",[]],"reflm:6451":["On moral theology.\nThe final end of all things can be known, because it is moral, namely the highest (g contingent) good. This yields the concept of a highest original good, that is, of a moral author of the world; and this concept determines him as the most perfect of all, whereas the physico-theologian could prove only a being of great perfection. Strictly speaking, morality yields objectively no proof, but only, subjectively, a morally necessary assumption of the conditions under which alone the object of morality, the highest good, is possible on the side on which it is not in our power (namely, happiness).\nFirst, the representation of the world as a system [of the caus] of the *nexus finalis physicus* (physical final nexus) (of the *causae finales physicae*, among which man too must be). Thus an intelligent original being, but not yet God, since for this the concept of the perfection of the world, derived from experience, does not suffice. Now comes the representation of the world as a *systema causarum finalium moralium* (system of moral final causes) directed to the highest good. For man, who is a member in the *nexus finalis physicus* but finds in himself a principle of a higher *nexus finalis*, will [himself] relate his existence, in respect of this higher nexus also, to [an Ve] the same intelligent author; but the concept of the author is now that of a being who is author of the highest good, because this alone is adequate to the reference to ends of the moral man. Thereby the assumption of a living God as a moral being, and hereby also as an all-sufficient being, is adopted.\nThis is no objectively sufficient theoretical proof, but a subjectively and practically sufficient moral proof. The assumption of God is not necessary in order to fulfil a duty in single actions, |\nbut in order to work towards the object of the extended moral disposition, the highest good; without this the moral disposition indeed has incentives to action (from the form of morality), but no final end (in respect of the matter, that is, of the objects of rational choice). Technical wisdom we see in corporeal things; moral wisdom we find in ourselves as a rule.",[]],"reflm:6452":["An end is (g either) the object of the will or the incentive to the action for the production of this object. The final end is the [selb] immediate end.\nThe highest good is the final end, but not as an incentive lying outside God.\nThe honour of God consists in respect for his command. This respect, however, is the supreme condition of the highest good, and consequently is the final end.",[]],"reflm:6453":["If the world were regarded as created only for the end of men, that is, for their own happiness, it would have been created as a proof of God as a being whom one could love; but that is only a relative perfection, relative to the wish of men. Only the absolute perfection, if it is met with in the world, enables us to honour the author, namely in that we recognise him not merely as good but as wise. Wisdom, however, is the benevolence [Gütigkeit] which subordinates its exercise to the condition that the objects be worthy of it. Thus God created the world for his honour, not in order to be loved.",[]],"reflm:6454":["Moral theology.\nThe moral need to assume a highest moral good is not one conditioned (g pathologically), as though [on account of] we required, for the good actions which the moral law commands, still further incentives of self-|love\n; for it is a moral need to assume a just judge, hence not a being from whose goodness we hope, but one whose holiness we must fear. Even the thought of this, given the uncertainty of the purity of his [man's] actions, is more fearful than [einschm] alluring. — But for our legislative reason, if we represent ourselves not even as standing under laws, but as projecting the highest good for the world in accordance with moral laws, everyone will wish that virtue be rewarded with happiness and vice be punished. This wish alone is purely moral, not in the least selfish, and unavoidable for the rational man; and it makes it a necessity to assume a living God as moral author and ruler of the world, in so far as we relate our idea of the world not to theory but to our practical vocation [Bestimmung]. Hence the wavering when one weighs this matter theoretically and then looks back again to the satisfaction of our practical incentives.\nIt is no passive interest but an active one, in the idea of a merely rational being which regards itself as morally legislative.\n1. On the end of creation,\n2. the best world,\n3. God's end in respect of this object.",[]],"reflm:6455":["The *potestas legislatoria diuina* (divine legislative power) rests on benevolence [Gütigkeit]. For only he can obligate whose will agrees with what is the essential property of the will of others. This is only the desire of a finite being for happiness. Hence he must be benevolent, but also omnipotent, omniscient, etc., etc. Only benevolence is the moral condition, but under the supreme condition of his holy will. Benevolence is a consequence of blessedness. Wisdom, because it is merely restrictive [in regard] to benevolence, is not rewarding; thus it is justice.",[]]};
